// data/book_polity_p2.js
// Polity book, Units 2–3: Making of the Constitution; the Union, its territory and citizenship.
addBookTopic("polity", "p2-1", `
<p>India's Constitution was written by Indians in a Constituent Assembly, but it built on nearly a century of British constitutional Acts, above all the Government of India Act, 1935.</p>

<h3>Colonial constitutional history</h3>
<table><tr><th>Act</th><th>What it did</th></tr>
<tr><td>Regulating Act 1773, Charter Acts (1813, 1833, 1853)</td><td>Centralised Company rule; the Governor-General of India (1833)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Government of India Act 1858</td><td>Crown rule; the Secretary of State for India</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indian Councils Acts 1861, 1892</td><td>Legislative councils with Indian members</td></tr>
<tr><td>Morley–Minto Reforms 1909</td><td>Enlarged councils; <b>separate electorates</b> for Muslims. No division of powers.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Montagu–Chelmsford (GoI Act) 1919</td><td>Dyarchy in the provinces; central and provincial subjects separated; bicameral central legislature</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Government of India Act 1935</b></td><td>An all-India federation, with <b>three lists (Federal, Provincial, Concurrent)</b>; provincial autonomy; the Federal Court (1937); Public Service Commissions; the Governor's office; emergency powers. The largest single source of the Constitution.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indian Independence Act 1947</td><td>Created two dominions and ended British paramountcy. It did not design federal lists.</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>distribution of powers</b> between the Centre and the States (the Seventh Schedule's Union, State and Concurrent Lists) follows the <b>1935 Act's scheme</b>. Residuary powers now lie with the Union (Article 248), whereas the 1935 Act gave them to the Governor-General.</li>
<li>The <b>Instrument of Instructions</b> issued to the Governor-General and Governors under the 1935 Act reappeared in 1950 as the <b>Directive Principles of State Policy</b>. Ambedkar compared the two; the difference is that the DPSPs are instructions to the legislature and executive and are not enforceable in court (see 4.4).</li>
<li>Fundamental Rights came from the US Bill of Rights. The executive powers in Articles 73 and 162 follow the federal scheme, and the conduct of business (Article 77) is governed by the Rules of Business.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Constituent Assembly</h3>
<ul>
<li>It was set up under the <b>Cabinet Mission Plan (1946)</b>.</li>
<li>Its members were <b>elected by the provincial legislative assemblies</b> in July–August 1946, by single transferable vote, in separate General, Muslim and Sikh seats. The princely states' members were <b>nominated</b> by their rulers. Members were not nominated by the British Parliament or the Governor-General, nor elected by the Congress and Muslim League.</li>
<li>389 seats were planned (296 for British India, 93 for princely states). After Partition there were 299.</li>
<li>The <b>Muslim League boycotted</b> it, so <b>Jinnah was never a member</b>. Nehru and Patel were.</li>
<li>It had 15 women members, including Sarojini Naidu, Hansa Mehta, Durgabai Deshmukh, Rajkumari Amrit Kaur, Ammu Swaminathan and Dakshayani Velayudhan (the only Dalit woman).</li>
<li>B.N. Rau was the Constitutional Adviser and prepared the first draft.</li>
<li>It also acted as the <b>Provisional Parliament</b> until 1952. G.V. Mavalankar presided over its legislative sessions.</li>
<li>Drafting took 2 years, 11 months and 18 days.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Presidents of the Assembly</h4>
<ul>
<li>At the first meeting on <b>9 December 1946</b>, <b>Dr Sachchidananda Sinha</b>, the oldest member, was the <b>provisional (temporary) President</b>, following French practice. It was not C. Rajagopalachari, Ambedkar or T.T. Krishnamachari.</li>
<li><b>Dr Rajendra Prasad</b> was elected permanent President on <b>11 December 1946</b>. H.C. Mookherjee and V.T. Krishnamachari were Vice-Presidents.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Committees</h4>
<table><tr><th>Committee</th><th>Chairman</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Drafting Committee</b> (set up 29 August 1947)</td><td><b>B.R. Ambedkar</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Union Constitution Committee</b>, Union Powers Committee, States Committee</td><td><b>Jawaharlal Nehru</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Provincial Constitution Committee; Advisory Committee on Fundamental Rights, Minorities, Tribal and Excluded Areas</td><td>Vallabhbhai Patel</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fundamental Rights Sub-committee</td><td>J.B. Kripalani</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minorities Sub-committee</td><td>H.C. Mookherjee</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rules of Procedure, Steering, Finance and Staff Committees</td><td>Rajendra Prasad</td></tr></table>
<p>The Drafting Committee's seven members were Ambedkar, Alladi Krishnaswamy Ayyar, N. Gopalaswami Ayyangar, K.M. Munshi, Mohammad Saadulla, B.L. Mitter (replaced by N. Madhava Rau) and D.P. Khaitan (replaced by T.T. Krishnamachari).</p>

<h3>Key dates</h3>
<table><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr>
<tr><td>9 December 1946</td><td>First meeting (Sachchidananda Sinha presiding). Not January 1947.</td></tr>
<tr><td>11 December 1946</td><td>Rajendra Prasad elected President</td></tr>
<tr><td>13 December 1946</td><td>Nehru moves the <b>Objectives Resolution</b>, the basis of the Preamble (adopted 22 January 1947)</td></tr>
<tr><td>22 July 1947</td><td>National Flag adopted</td></tr>
<tr><td>29 August 1947</td><td>Drafting Committee set up</td></tr>
<tr><td>February 1948</td><td>Draft Constitution published</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>26 November 1949</b></td><td>Constitution <b>adopted</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>24 January 1950</td><td>Members sign; the National Anthem and Song are adopted; Prasad is elected first President</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>26 January 1950</b></td><td>Constitution <b>comes into force</b> (Republic Day)</td></tr></table>
<div class="key"><b>Constitution Day</b><b>Constitution Day</b> (Samvidhan Divas), <b>26 November</b>, has been observed since <b>2015</b> (Ambedkar's 125th birth anniversary year) to promote constitutional values. It marks the <b>adoption</b> of the Constitution in 1949, not the setting up of the Drafting Committee, which happened on 29 August 1947.</div>
<ul>
<li>2024–25 was the Constitution's 75th year, marked by the "Samvidhan Hamara Swabhiman" campaign.</li>
<li>Since 2024, 25 June has been observed as "Samvidhan Hatya Diwas" (the start of the Emergency).</li>
<li>26 January was chosen because the Congress had celebrated it as <b>Purna Swaraj Day</b> in 1930, following the Lahore resolution of December 1929.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p2-2", `
<p>India's Constitution is the longest written constitution in the world. It borrows from many sources but adapts them, and it is organised into Parts, Articles and Schedules that UPSC expects you to navigate.</p>

<h3>Sources of the Constitution</h3>
<table><tr><th>Source</th><th>Features borrowed</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Government of India Act 1935</b></td><td>The <b>distribution of powers</b>: its Federal, Provincial and Concurrent lists became the Seventh Schedule. This did not come from the Morley-Minto Reforms (1909), the Montagu-Chelmsford Act (1919) or the Indian Independence Act (1947). Also the federal scheme, office of Governor, judiciary, Public Service Commissions, emergency provisions, administrative details</td></tr>
<tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>Parliamentary government, rule of law, single citizenship, cabinet system, bicameralism, prerogative writs</td></tr>
<tr><td>USA</td><td><b>Fundamental Rights</b> (Bill of Rights), judicial review, independent judiciary, impeachment of the President, the Vice-President's office, removal of judges</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Ireland</b></td><td><b>Directive Principles</b> (which Ireland took from Spain), nomination to the Rajya Sabha, method of electing the President</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Canada</b></td><td>A <b>federation with a strong Centre</b> ("Union of States"), residuary powers with the Centre, Centre appoints Governors, Supreme Court's advisory jurisdiction</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Australia</b></td><td><b>Concurrent List</b>, joint sitting, freedom of trade and commerce</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weimar Germany</td><td>Suspension of Fundamental Rights during emergencies</td></tr>
<tr><td>USSR</td><td>Fundamental Duties; the ideal of justice in the Preamble</td></tr>
<tr><td>France</td><td>Republic; liberty, equality, fraternity</td></tr>
<tr><td>South Africa</td><td>Amendment procedure; election of Rajya Sabha members</td></tr>
<tr><td>Japan</td><td>"Procedure established by law"</td></tr></table>

<h3>Parts, Articles and Schedules</h3>
<div class="key"><b>Original and current size</b><ul>
<li><b>Original Constitution (1950):</b> 22 Parts, 395 Articles, 8 Schedules. So "20 parts" and "390 articles" are both wrong.</li>
<li><b>Now:</b> 25 Parts, about 470 Articles (the numbering still ends at 395, with lettered insertions) and 12 Schedules.</li>
<li>The <b>9th, 10th, 11th and 12th Schedules</b> were all added by amendments.</li></ul></div>
<table><tr><th>Part</th><th>Subject</th><th>Articles</th></tr>
<tr><td>I</td><td>Union and its territory</td><td>1–4</td></tr>
<tr><td>II</td><td>Citizenship</td><td>5–11</td></tr>
<tr><td>III</td><td>Fundamental Rights</td><td>12–35</td></tr>
<tr><td>IV</td><td>Directive Principles</td><td>36–51</td></tr>
<tr><td>IVA</td><td>Fundamental Duties</td><td>51A</td></tr>
<tr><td>V</td><td>The Union</td><td>52–151</td></tr>
<tr><td>VI</td><td>The States</td><td>152–237</td></tr>
<tr><td>VIII</td><td>Union Territories</td><td>239–242</td></tr>
<tr><td>IX</td><td>Panchayats</td><td>243–243O</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>IXA</b></td><td><b>Municipalities</b> (powers in Article 243W)</td><td>243P–243ZG</td></tr>
<tr><td>IXB</td><td>Co-operative societies (97th Amendment)</td><td>243ZH–243ZT</td></tr>
<tr><td>X</td><td>Scheduled and Tribal Areas</td><td>244–244A</td></tr>
<tr><td>XI</td><td>Union–State relations</td><td>245–263</td></tr>
<tr><td>XII</td><td>Finance, property, contracts, suits</td><td>264–300A</td></tr>
<tr><td>XIII</td><td>Trade and commerce within India</td><td>301–307</td></tr>
<tr><td>XIV</td><td>Services</td><td>308–323</td></tr>
<tr><td>XIVA</td><td>Tribunals</td><td>323A–323B</td></tr>
<tr><td>XV</td><td>Elections</td><td>324–329A</td></tr>
<tr><td>XVI</td><td>Special provisions for certain classes</td><td>330–342A</td></tr>
<tr><td>XVII</td><td>Official language</td><td>343–351</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>XVIII</b></td><td><b>Emergency</b></td><td>352–360</td></tr>
<tr><td>XIX</td><td>Miscellaneous</td><td>361–367</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>XX</b></td><td><b>Amendment</b></td><td>368</td></tr>
<tr><td>XXI</td><td>Temporary, transitional and special provisions</td><td>369–392</td></tr>
<tr><td>XXII</td><td>Short title, commencement, Hindi text, repeals</td><td>393–395</td></tr></table>
<p>Part VII (Part B States) was repealed by the 7th Amendment (1956).</p>
<table><tr><th>Schedule</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
<tr><td>First</td><td>States and UTs and their territories</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second</td><td>Salaries and allowances of the President, Governors, Speakers, judges, CAG and others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third</td><td>Forms of oaths and affirmations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fourth</td><td>Allocation of Rajya Sabha seats</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fifth</td><td>Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sixth</td><td>Tribal areas of Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seventh</td><td>Union, State and Concurrent Lists</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eighth</td><td>Languages (22)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ninth</td><td>Laws protected from judicial review (1st Amendment, 1951)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tenth</td><td>Anti-defection (52nd Amendment, 1985)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eleventh</td><td>Panchayats, 29 subjects (73rd Amendment, 1992)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Twelfth</td><td>Municipalities, 18 subjects (74th Amendment, 1992)</td></tr></table>

<h3>The final Part: short title, commencement and repeals</h3>
<p>Part XXII states all three things a 2026 question claimed were missing, so <b>none</b> of those statements was correct:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 393 (short title):</b> "This Constitution may be called the <b>Constitution of India</b>."</li>
<li><b>Article 394 (commencement):</b> certain articles came into force at once on <b>26 November 1949</b>: Articles 5–9 (citizenship), 60 (President's oath), 324 (elections), 366–367, 379–380 (provisional Parliament), 388, 391–393 and 394 itself. The rest came into force on <b>26 January 1950</b>, which the Constitution calls "the commencement of this Constitution".</li>
<li><b>Article 394A</b> (58th Amendment, 1987) provides for an authoritative Hindi text.</li>
<li><b>Article 395 (repeals):</b> the <b>Indian Independence Act 1947</b> and the <b>Government of India Act 1935</b> are repealed, except the Abolition of Privy Council Jurisdiction Act 1949. This cut legal continuity with the British Parliament, so the Constitution is "autochthonous": its authority comes from the people.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Salient features</h3>
<ul>
<li>It is written and the longest in the world.</li>
<li>It is partly rigid and partly flexible.</li>
<li>It is federal with a unitary bias (see 1.3).</li>
<li>It sets up parliamentary government (see 1.3).</li>
<li>It blends parliamentary sovereignty with judicial supremacy.</li>
<li>It has an integrated, independent judiciary.</li>
<li>It guarantees Fundamental Rights, sets out Directive Principles and lists Fundamental Duties.</li>
<li>It establishes a secular State.</li>
<li>It provides universal adult franchise and single citizenship.</li>
<li>It has emergency provisions, three tiers of government, and independent constitutional bodies.</li>
</ul>
<p>There have been <b>106 amendments</b>, the latest being the Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam (2023). The Constitution (130th Amendment) Bill, 2025, which would remove ministers held in custody for 30 days, was referred to a joint committee. Amendment procedure is covered in 13.1.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p2-3", `
<p>The Preamble is the introduction to the Constitution. In a few lines it states where the Constitution's authority comes from, what kind of State India is, what the State aims to achieve, and when the Constitution was adopted.</p>

<h3>Text and structure</h3>
<table><tr><th>Element</th><th>What the Preamble says</th></tr>
<tr><td>Source of authority</td><td>"<b>We, the people of India</b>"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nature of the State</td><td><b>Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic, Republic</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Objectives</td><td><b>Justice</b> (social, economic and political); <b>Liberty</b> (of thought, expression, belief, faith and worship); <b>Equality</b> (of status and of opportunity); <b>Fraternity</b> (assuring the dignity of the individual and the unity and integrity of the Nation)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Date of adoption</td><td>26 November 1949</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>It is based on Nehru's <b>Objectives Resolution</b> (moved 13 December 1946, adopted 22 January 1947).</li>
<li>The ideal of <b>justice</b> (social, economic, political) comes from the <b>Russian Revolution</b>. <b>Liberty, equality and fraternity</b> come from the French Revolution.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>The original Preamble</b>On <b>26 January 1950</b> India was a "<b>Sovereign Democratic Republic</b>". The words "<b>Socialist</b>" and "<b>Secular</b>", and "<b>Integrity</b>" (in "unity and integrity of the Nation"), were added by the <b>42nd Amendment (1976)</b>, during the Emergency. This is the only time the Preamble has been amended.<br><br>In <b>Dr Balram Singh v. Union of India</b> (November 2024) the Supreme Court dismissed petitions challenging these additions.</div>
<div class="trap"><b>Common traps</b><ul>
<li>The Preamble promises liberty of <b>thought, expression, belief, faith and worship</b>. It does <b>not</b> mention "economic liberty".</li>
<li>It does promise <b>economic justice</b>.</li></ul></div>
<p><b>Economic justice</b> appears in two places: the <b>Preamble</b> and the <b>Directive Principles</b>. Article 38 asks the State to secure a social order based on social, economic and political justice, and Article 39 spells out economic justice: livelihood, fair distribution of resources and equal pay. The Fundamental Rights mainly secure civil and political liberty.</p>

<h3>Legal status</h3>
<table><tr><th>Case</th><th>Held</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Berubari Union</b> (1960)</td><td>The Preamble is <b>not</b> part of the Constitution, but it is "<b>a key to open the mind of the makers</b>"</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kesavananda Bharati</b> (1973)</td><td>It <b>is</b> part of the Constitution and can be amended under Article 368, but not its basic features</td></tr>
<tr><td>LIC of India (1995)</td><td>Reaffirmed that it is part of the Constitution</td></tr></table>
<div class="key"><b>Status in one line</b>The Preamble is <b>part of the Constitution but has no legal effect independently of other parts</b>.<ul>
<li>It is <b>not enforceable</b> in court on its own.</li>
<li>It is neither a <b>source of power</b> nor a <b>limitation</b> on the legislature.</li>
<li>It is used to <b>interpret</b> ambiguous provisions.</li></ul>
"No legal effect at all" goes too far. "Not a part of the Constitution" is the old Berubari view. "Same legal effect as any other part" is wrong because the Preamble cannot be enforced on its own.</div>

<h3>Descriptions of the Preamble</h3>
<p>The "mind of the makers" of the Constitution is reflected in the <b>Preamble</b>, not in the Fundamental Rights, the Directive Principles, or the Fundamental Duties (which were added only in 1976).</p>
<table><tr><th>Who</th><th>Called it</th></tr>
<tr><td>Supreme Court (Berubari)</td><td>"Key to open the mind of the makers"</td></tr>
<tr><td>K.M. Munshi</td><td>"Horoscope of our sovereign democratic republic"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ernest Barker</td><td>"Key-note to the Constitution"</td></tr>
<tr><td>N.A. Palkhivala</td><td>"Identity card of the Constitution"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thakurdas Bhargava</td><td>"Soul of the Constitution"</td></tr>
<tr><td>M. Hidayatullah</td><td>Resembles the US Declaration of Independence, but is more than a declaration</td></tr></table>
<p>The Preamble's idea of liberty is <b>positive</b> liberty: the conditions for developing one's personality fully (see 1.1). It is protected through Articles 19–22 and 25–28.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p3-1", `
<p>Part I (Articles 1–4) describes India as a <b>Union of States</b>, and lets Parliament reshape that Union by ordinary law. The map of States has been redrawn many times since 1950, especially in the North-East.</p>

<h3>Articles 1–4</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 1:</b> India, that is Bharat, is a Union of States. Its territory comprises the States, the Union Territories and any acquired territory.</li>
<li><b>Article 2:</b> admission or establishment of new States, such as Sikkim.</li>
<li><b>Article 3:</b> Parliament can form new States, change boundaries or rename States by <b>simple majority</b>.
  <ul><li>The bill needs the <b>President's recommendation</b>.</li>
  <li>The affected State legislature's <b>views</b> must be sought, but its <b>consent</b> is not needed.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Article 4:</b> such laws are <b>not</b> amendments under Article 368, even though they amend the <b>First</b> and <b>Fourth</b> Schedules.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>The First Schedule</b>The <b>First Schedule</b> lists the names of the States and UTs and their territories. So creating a new State requires amending the <b>First Schedule</b>, and also the Fourth, which sets Rajya Sabha seats.<ul>
<li>The Second Schedule covers salaries, the Third oaths, and the Fifth Scheduled Areas.</li></ul></div>
<p>India now has <b>28 States and 8 UTs</b>, after Jammu and Kashmir was split into two UTs (2019) and Dadra and Nagar Haveli merged with Daman and Diu (2020).</p>

<h3>Reorganisation of States</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1950:</b> the original Constitution had four categories.
  <ul><li>Part A: the former Governors' provinces.</li>
  <li>Part B: former princely states, under a Rajpramukh.</li>
  <li>Part C: Chief Commissioners' provinces and some princely states.</li>
  <li>Part D: the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.</li></ul></li>
<li>Andhra State was created in 1953 after Potti Sriramulu's death. The States Reorganisation Commission (Fazl Ali, 1953–55) followed.</li>
<li>The <b>States Reorganisation Act and the 7th Amendment (1956)</b> abolished the categories, creating 14 States and 6 UTs on a linguistic basis.</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>State</th><th>Full statehood</th><th>How</th></tr>
<tr><td>Gujarat, Maharashtra</td><td>1960</td><td>Bombay State split</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Nagaland</b></td><td><b>1 December 1963</b></td><td>State of Nagaland Act 1962 and the <b>13th Amendment</b>, which inserted Article 371A. Carved from Assam as the 16th State.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Haryana</b></td><td><b>1 November 1966</b></td><td>Punjab Reorganisation Act (Shah Commission)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Himachal Pradesh</td><td>1971</td><td>From a UT</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Manipur, Tripura, Meghalaya</b></td><td><b>21 January 1972</b></td><td>North-Eastern Areas (Reorganisation) Act 1971. Meghalaya had been an autonomous State within Assam since 1970 (22nd Amendment).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Karnataka (renamed)</td><td>1 November 1973</td><td>Mysore renamed</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sikkim</b></td><td><b>16 May 1975</b></td><td>A protectorate, then an associate State (35th Amendment, 1974), then a full State by the <b>36th Amendment</b> (Article 371F)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mizoram</td><td>20 February 1987</td><td>53rd Amendment, after the Mizo Accord (1986); a UT since 1972</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Arunachal Pradesh</b></td><td><b>20 February 1987</b></td><td>55th Amendment. NEFA, then a UT (1972), then a State.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Goa</b></td><td><b>30 May 1987</b></td><td>Goa, Daman and Diu Reorganisation Act and the <b>56th Amendment</b>, which made Daman and Diu a separate UT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chhattisgarh, Uttarakhand, Jharkhand</td><td>2000</td><td>Split from MP, UP and Bihar</td></tr>
<tr><td>Telangana</td><td>2 June 2014</td><td>Split from Andhra Pradesh (the 28th State)</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li><b>Order asked:</b>
  <ul><li>Nagaland (1963) → Haryana (1966) → Sikkim (1975) → Arunachal Pradesh (1987).</li>
  <li>Haryana formed (November 1966) → fourth general elections (February 1967, which ended Congress dominance in several States) → Meghalaya and Tripura become States (1972) → Mysore renamed Karnataka (1973).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Arunachal Pradesh:</b> its capital, Itanagar, is named after the <b>Ita Fort</b> ("fort of bricks", 14th–15th century). It has two national parks, <b>Namdapha</b> (also a tiger reserve, home to four big cats) and <b>Mouling</b>.</li>
<li><b>Nagaland</b> came into being under a constitutional amendment (the 13th).</li>
<li><b>Tripura</b> was a princely state that merged with India in 1949, became a Part C State in 1950, a UT in 1956, and a full State in 1972.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Goa and the former colonies</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Portuguese territories:</b> Goa, Daman and Diu (liberated 1961), and <b>Dadra and Nagar Haveli</b>. Local volunteers freed Dadra and Nagar Haveli in <b>1954</b>, and it joined India by the 10th Amendment (1961).
  <ul><li>It was <b>Portuguese</b>, not French, so "French rule till 1954" is the incorrect statement.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Diu</b> is an island at the mouth of the Gulf of Khambhat.</li>
<li><b>French territories:</b> Pondicherry, Karaikal, Mahe, Yanam and Chandernagore.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Union Territories</h3>
<ul>
<li>UTs are administered by the President through an <b>Administrator</b> (Article 239).</li>
<li>Under Article 239(2), the Governor of a neighbouring State may be appointed Administrator. Since 1984 the <b>Governor of Punjab</b> has been the <b>Administrator of Chandigarh</b>.
  <ul><li>A 2025 proposal to bring Chandigarh under Article 240 was dropped after Punjab objected.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Lakshadweep</b> has its own Administrator; the Governor of Kerala does not hold the post.</li>
<li><b>Delhi</b> (Article 239AA) and <b>Puducherry</b> have legislatures. So does the J&amp;K UT (since 2019).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Special provisions (Articles 371–371J)</h3>
<p>Articles <b>371A to 371I</b> were inserted to meet regional demands of Nagaland, Assam, Manipur, Andhra Pradesh, Sikkim, Mizoram, Arunachal Pradesh and Goa.</p>
<table><tr><th>Article</th><th>State</th><th>Key feature</th></tr>
<tr><td>371</td><td>Maharashtra, Gujarat</td><td>Development boards (Vidarbha, Marathwada, Saurashtra, Kutch)</td></tr>
<tr><td>371A</td><td>Nagaland</td><td>Acts of Parliament on Naga religious and social practices, customary law, and ownership of land and resources apply only if the Assembly so resolves</td></tr>
<tr><td>371B</td><td>Assam</td><td>Committee of tribal-area members</td></tr>
<tr><td>371C</td><td>Manipur</td><td>Hill Areas committee</td></tr>
<tr><td>371D, 371E</td><td>Andhra Pradesh, Telangana</td><td>Equal opportunities in jobs and education; a central university</td></tr>
<tr><td>371F</td><td>Sikkim</td><td>Protection of existing laws; reserved seats</td></tr>
<tr><td>371G</td><td>Mizoram</td><td>Like Nagaland</td></tr>
<tr><td>371H</td><td>Arunachal Pradesh</td><td>Governor's special responsibility for law and order</td></tr>
<tr><td>371I</td><td>Goa</td><td>Assembly of at least 30 members</td></tr>
<tr><td>371J</td><td>Karnataka (2012)</td><td>Hyderabad–Karnataka (Kalyana Karnataka) region</td></tr></table>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p3-2", `
<p>Citizenship decides who belongs fully to the political community: who can vote, hold constitutional office and claim certain Fundamental Rights. The Constitution's Part II (Articles 5–11) dealt only with the position in 1950. Parliament regulates everything since then, through the <b>Citizenship Act, 1955</b>.</p>

<h3>Single citizenship</h3>
<ul>
<li>India has <b>single citizenship</b> under a dual polity (Union and States). The <b>USA</b> has <b>dual</b> citizenship (federal and state). So the statement "both India and the USA have single citizenship" is wrong.</li>
<li>UPSC's key also accepted "<b>one domicile</b>". Courts (e.g. Pradeep Jain, 1984) observed that there is only one domicile, that of India, under Article 5.
  <ul><li>States still use residence for job and education quotas.</li>
  <li>Article 16(3) lets Parliament allow residence requirements for State jobs.</li></ul></li>
<li>India does not allow dual citizenship. <b>OCI cardholders</b> are not citizens: they cannot vote or hold constitutional posts.</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Common traps</b><ul>
<li>India's <b>Head of State need not be a citizen by birth</b>. The President need only be a citizen of India (Article 58), so a naturalised citizen is eligible. The US President must be a natural-born citizen.</li>
<li>A naturalised or registered citizen <b>can</b> be deprived of citizenship (see below).</li></ul></div>

<h3>Citizenship Act, 1955</h3>
<table><tr><th>Acquisition</th><th>Loss</th></tr>
<tr><td>Birth, descent, registration, naturalisation, incorporation of territory</td><td><b>Renunciation</b> (voluntary); <b>termination</b> (automatic, on voluntarily acquiring another country's citizenship); <b>deprivation</b> (by the Centre under Section 10)</td></tr></table>
<p>Grounds for deprivation under Section 10, which applies to registered or naturalised citizens:</p>
<ul>
<li>citizenship obtained by <b>fraud</b>;</li>
<li><b>disloyalty</b> to the Constitution;</li>
<li><b>trading or communicating with the enemy</b> during a war;</li>
<li><b>imprisonment for two years</b> within five years of registration or naturalisation;</li>
<li><b>continuous residence abroad for seven years</b>.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><b>Citizenship (Amendment) Act, 2019:</b> a faster route to citizenship for Hindus, Sikhs, Buddhists, Jains, Parsis and Christians from Afghanistan, Bangladesh and Pakistan who entered India by 31 December 2014. Its rules were notified in <b>March 2024</b>.</li>
<li>In <b>October 2024</b> the Supreme Court upheld <b>Section 6A</b>, the Assam Accord cut-off for migrants.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Aadhaar is not citizenship</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Aadhaar is not proof of citizenship or domicile</b> (Section 9 of the Aadhaar Act, 2016). Any <b>resident</b> who has lived in India for 182 days in the previous year can get one, including foreigners.</li>
<li>UIDAI <b>can omit or deactivate</b> an Aadhaar number, for example for duplicate enrolment, fake documents, or a child's biometrics not being updated. So "once issued it cannot be deactivated" is wrong.</li>
<li>An Aadhaar number is a 12-digit random number linked to demographic and biometric data (fingerprints, iris, photograph).</li>
<li>UIDAI has been statutory since July 2016 and comes under MeitY.</li>
<li>Children under five get a "<b>Baal Aadhaar</b>" without biometrics, which must be updated at ages 5 and 15.</li>
<li>During Bihar's 2025 Special Intensive Revision of electoral rolls, the Election Commission and the courts reiterated that Aadhaar is an identity document, not proof of citizenship.</li>
<li>The Aadhaar judgment on privacy is covered in 4.2.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p4.js
// Polity book, Unit 4: Fundamental Rights, Directive Principles and Fundamental Duties.
addBookTopic("polity", "p4-1", `
<p>Part III (Articles 12–35) is the Constitution's bill of rights. Fundamental Rights are <b>justiciable</b>: a person can go straight to the Supreme Court (Article 32) or a High Court (Article 226) to enforce them. They mostly limit the <b>State</b>, and they secure political democracy.</p>

<h3>The six groups of Fundamental Rights</h3>
<table><tr><th>Group</th><th>Articles</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Right to Equality</b></td><td><b>14–18</b>: 14 equality before law; 15 no discrimination; 16 equal opportunity in public employment; <b>17 abolition of untouchability</b>; 18 abolition of titles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Right to Freedom</td><td>19–22 (see 4.2)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Right against Exploitation</td><td>23–24 (see 4.3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Freedom of Religion</td><td>25–28</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cultural and Educational Rights</td><td>29–30</td></tr>
<tr><td>Right to Constitutional Remedies</td><td>32</td></tr></table>
<p>There were originally seven groups; the right to property was removed in 1978 (see 4.3).</p>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b>The Right to Equality is granted by five Articles: <b>14 to 18</b>, not 13–17 or 15–19.<ul>
<li>Article 13 makes laws inconsistent with Fundamental Rights void.</li>
<li>Articles 19–20 belong to the Right to Freedom.</li></ul></div>

<h3>Article 12: who is the "State"?</h3>
<p>For Part III, the "State" includes the Government and Parliament of India, State governments and legislatures, and all local and other authorities under government control. Courts have extended this to bodies such as public sector companies.</p>
<p>A few rights bind private persons too:</p>
<ul>
<li>Article 15(2): access to shops and public places.</li>
<li>Article 17: untouchability.</li>
<li>Article 23: trafficking and forced labour.</li>
<li>Article 24: child labour.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Article 13: laws inconsistent with Fundamental Rights</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>13(1):</b> pre-Constitution laws are void to the extent they are inconsistent with Fundamental Rights.
  <ul><li>Under the <b>doctrine of eclipse</b> (Bhikaji Narain Dhakras, 1955), such a law becomes dormant rather than dead, and revives if the barrier is removed.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>13(2):</b> the State shall not make laws that take away or abridge Fundamental Rights.
  <ul><li>Under the <b>doctrine of severability</b> (R.M.D.C., 1957), only the offending part of a law is struck down.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>13(4)</b> (24th Amendment, 1971): Article 13 does not apply to constitutional amendments. Amendments remain subject to the <b>basic structure</b> doctrine (Kesavananda Bharati, 1973).</li>
<li>Article 13, read with Articles 32 and 226, is the textual basis of <b>judicial review</b> (see 7.2).</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>What "law" means: Article 13(3)(a)</b>"Law" includes any <b>Ordinance, order, bye-law, rule, regulation, notification, custom or usage</b> having the force of law in India.<br><br>So someone who says "law" includes ordinances, orders, rules and regulations but <b>not custom</b> is wrong, and someone who says it <b>includes custom or usage</b> is right.</div>
<p>On <b>personal law</b>: Narasu Appa Mali (1952) held that uncodified personal law is not "law" under Article 13. The question was revisited in Shayara Bano (2017) and the Sabarimala case (2018).</p>

<h3>Articles 14–18 one by one</h3>
<table><tr><th>Article</th><th>Provision</th><th>Available to</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>14</b></td><td>The State shall not deny to any person <b>equality before the law</b> or the <b>equal protection of the laws</b></td><td>All persons</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>15</b></td><td><b>No discrimination</b> on grounds only of religion, race, caste, sex or place of birth. Clauses 15(3)–(6) allow special provisions for women and children, backward classes, SCs and STs, and the economically weaker sections (EWS).</td><td>Citizens</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>16</b></td><td><b>Equality of opportunity in public employment</b>. 16(2): no discrimination on grounds of religion, race, caste, sex, descent, place of birth or residence. Clauses 16(4), (4A), (4B) and (6) allow reservations.</td><td>Citizens</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>17</b></td><td><b>Untouchability is abolished</b> and its practice in any form is forbidden; enforcing any disability arising from it is an offence</td><td>All, including against private persons</td></tr>
<tr><td>18</td><td>Abolition of titles, except military and academic distinctions. Bharat Ratna and Padma awards are <b>not</b> titles (Balaji Raghavan, 1996).</td><td>Citizens and foreigners in State service</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 14</b> combines a British idea (equality before law, the negative concept) with an American one (equal protection of laws, the positive concept). Its tests, <b>reasonable classification</b> and <b>non-arbitrariness</b>, are covered in 1.2.</li>
<li><b>Untouchability</b> is not defined in the Constitution or in any Act.
  <ul><li>The Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955 (originally the Untouchability (Offences) Act) enforces Article 17.</li>
  <li>So does the SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act, 1989. Its 2018 amendment restored the bar on anticipatory bail.</li>
  <li>Protection against untouchability belongs to the <b>Right to Equality</b>, not the Right against Exploitation or the Right to Freedom.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Reservation cases:</b>
  <ul><li>EWS reservation (Articles 15(6) and 16(6), 103rd Amendment, 2019) was upheld in <b>Janhit Abhiyan (2022)</b>.</li>
  <li>Sub-classification of SCs was allowed in <b>State of Punjab v. Davinder Singh (2024)</b>.</li>
  <li>More in 11.2.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Exceptions to equality:</b> Article 361 (immunity of the President and Governors), foreign diplomats, and the saving clauses in Articles 31A, 31B and 31C.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p4-2", `
<p>Articles 19–22 protect personal freedom. <b>Article 21</b>, "No person shall be deprived of his life or personal liberty except according to procedure established by law", has grown through judicial interpretation into the widest right in the Constitution.</p>

<h3>Articles 19–22</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 19</b> gives citizens six freedoms: speech and expression; peaceful assembly without arms; forming associations, unions and co-operatives; moving freely; residing anywhere in India; and any profession, trade or business.
  <ul><li>Each is subject to "reasonable restrictions" in Articles 19(2)–(6).</li>
  <li>The original seventh, property (19(1)(f)), was removed in 1978.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Article 20:</b> protection in respect of conviction. There is no ex post facto criminal law, no double jeopardy, and no compulsion to be a witness against oneself.</li>
<li><b>Article 21:</b> life and personal liberty. <b>Article 21A:</b> education.</li>
<li><b>Article 22:</b> protection against arbitrary arrest and detention, including preventive detention safeguards.</li>
<li>Articles <b>20 and 21 cannot be suspended</b> even during an emergency (44th Amendment).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The expansion of Article 21</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A.K. Gopalan (1950)</b> read "procedure established by law" narrowly.</li>
<li><b>Maneka Gandhi (1978)</b> required the procedure to be just, fair and reasonable, which brought in due process (see 1.2).</li>
<li>Since then, the courts have read many rights into Article 21.</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Right read into Article 21</th><th>Case</th></tr>
<tr><td>Livelihood</td><td>Olga Tellis (1985)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clean environment</td><td>Subhash Kumar (1991); M.C. Mehta cases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speedy trial; free legal aid</td><td>Hussainara Khatoon (1979)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Against custodial torture and handcuffing</td><td>D.K. Basu (1997)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Education (later Article 21A)</td><td>Mohini Jain (1992), Unni Krishnan (1993)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Privacy</b></td><td><b>K.S. Puttaswamy (2017)</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Marrying a person of one's choice</b></td><td><b>Shafin Jahan (the Hadiya case, 2018)</b>; Lata Singh (2006) on inter-caste marriage; Shakti Vahini (2018) against honour killings and khap interference</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dying with dignity (passive euthanasia)</td><td>Common Cause (2018)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sexual orientation</td><td>Navtej Johar (2018)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Against the adverse effects of climate change</td><td>M.K. Ranjitsinh (2024)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shelter, health, dignity, sleep, reputation</td><td>Various</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The right to marry is protected by <b>Article 21</b>, not Article 19, 25 (religion) or 29 (minority culture).
  <ul><li>In Supriyo Chakraborty (October 2023), the Supreme Court declined to recognise same-sex marriage, leaving it to Parliament, but affirmed queer persons' right to choose a partner.</li>
  <li>Inter-faith civil marriage is governed by the Special Marriage Act, 1954, whose 30-day notice period has been challenged. Several States have anti-conversion laws that cover marriage.</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>The right to privacy</h3>
<div class="key"><b>Puttaswamy (2017)</b>A nine-judge bench <b>unanimously</b> held that the right to privacy "is protected as an intrinsic part of the right to life and personal liberty under <b>Article 21</b> and as a part of the <b>freedoms guaranteed by Part III</b>."<ul>
<li>It overruled M.P. Sharma (1954) and Kharak Singh (1962) on this point.</li>
<li>Privacy is <b>not</b> placed in Article 15, 16 or 29, nor in Article 14 with the 42nd Amendment, Article 17 with the DPSPs, or Article 24 with the 44th Amendment.</li></ul></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Facets of privacy:</b> bodily integrity, informational privacy, and decisional autonomy (choices about marriage, sexuality, food).</li>
<li><b>Restrictions must pass a test:</b> legality (there must be a law), a legitimate aim, proportionality, and procedural safeguards.</li>
<li><b>Follow-ups:</b> the Aadhaar judgment (2018), Navtej Johar and Joseph Shine (2018), the Pegasus inquiry (2021), and the <b>Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023</b>. The Act sets up a Data Protection Board and consent-based processing; its Rules were notified in November 2025.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The Aadhaar judgment (Puttaswamy II, September 2018)</h4>
<p>A five-judge bench decided 4:1, with Justice Chandrachud dissenting.</p>
<ul>
<li>It upheld Aadhaar, and its passage as a <b>Money Bill</b>.</li>
<li>Aadhaar <b>can be mandatory for benefits funded from the Consolidated Fund of India</b> (Section 7) and for PAN linking (Section 139AA of the Income Tax Act, upheld in Binoy Viswam, 2017).</li>
<li>The Court <b>struck down Section 57</b>, so the State cannot contract with private corporations to share Aadhaar authentication. Aadhaar cannot be made mandatory for <b>bank accounts, mobile SIMs, insurance, school admissions or exams</b> (CBSE, NEET, UGC).</li>
<li>Retention of authentication metadata was cut from five years to <b>six months</b>, not three.</li>
<li>The Aadhaar and Other Laws (Amendment) Act, 2019 allowed <b>voluntary</b> Aadhaar KYC by banks and telecom companies and offline verification. Children can cancel their Aadhaar at 18.</li>
<li>Linking Aadhaar with voter ID is voluntary (2021). Aadhaar is not proof of citizenship (see 3.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The right to education</h3>
<div class="key"><b>The 86th Amendment (2002)</b>Introduced as the <b>93rd Constitution Amendment Bill</b> (2001), it made three changes:<ul>
<li>It inserted <b>Article 21A</b>: free and compulsory education for children aged <b>6–14</b> as a Fundamental Right.</li>
<li>It changed <b>Article 45</b> (a DPSP) to early childhood care and education for children <b>below six</b>.</li>
<li>It added <b>Article 51A(k)</b>, the duty of parents to provide educational opportunities.</li></ul>
A Bill's number differs from the Act's number once passed. The <b>93rd Amendment Act</b> (2005) was about OBC reservation in private educational institutions (Article 15(5)), and the <b>76th Amendment</b> (1994) placed Tamil Nadu's 69% reservation law in the Ninth Schedule. So "education was made a Fundamental Right by the 76th Amendment" is wrong.</div>
<ul>
<li>The <b>42nd Amendment (1976)</b> moved education from the State List to the <b>Concurrent List</b> (Entry 25).</li>
<li><b>Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan</b> (2001) included computer-aided learning, even in rural schools. It was merged with RMSA and teacher education into <b>Samagra Shiksha</b> (2018).</li>
<li><b>RTE Act 2009</b> (in force 1 April 2010):
  <ul><li>25% of seats in private schools are reserved for disadvantaged children.</li>
  <li>Its "no detention" policy was amended in 2019 and scrapped for classes 5 and 8 in central schools in 2024.</li>
  <li>Teacher qualifications under it are covered in 14.2.</li></ul></li>
<li>Other initiatives: NEP 2020 (a 5+3+3+4 structure) and PM SHRI schools (2022).</li>
<li>Education provisions are spread across the Constitution (see 4.4).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p4-3", `
<p>The remaining Fundamental Rights protect people against exploitation, protect religious and cultural freedom, and provide the remedy that makes all the others real. This topic also covers the right to property, which was once fundamental and is now a constitutional right.</p>

<h3>Right against Exploitation (Articles 23–24)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 23:</b> prohibits <b>traffic in human beings</b>, <b>begar</b> and other forms of <b>forced labour</b>. The State may still impose compulsory service for public purposes, such as military service, without discrimination.</li>
<li><b>Article 24:</b> "No child below the age of <b>fourteen</b> years shall be employed to work in any <b>factory or mine</b> or engaged in any other <b>hazardous employment</b>."</li>
<li>Both apply against private persons as well.</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b>The Right against Exploitation covers only <b>trafficking and forced labour</b> (23) and <b>child labour</b> (24).<ul>
<li>Abolition of untouchability is Article 17, under the Right to Equality.</li>
<li>Protection of minorities is Articles 29–30, under Cultural and Educational Rights.</li>
<li>Prohibition of intoxicants is Article 47, a Directive Principle.</li></ul></div>
<p>Laws and cases:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Immoral Traffic (Prevention) Act, 1956.</b> Trafficking is now also an offence under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (Section 143).</li>
<li><b>Bonded Labour System (Abolition) Act, 1976.</b></li>
<li><b>Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act, 1986</b> (amended 2016): a complete ban on employing children under 14, and on adolescents aged 14–18 in hazardous work.</li>
<li><b>PUDR (the Asiad workers case, 1982):</b> paying less than the minimum wage amounts to forced labour.</li>
<li><b>Bandhua Mukti Morcha (1984)</b> on bonded labour; <b>M.C. Mehta (1996)</b> on child labour in Sivakasi.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Child-related provisions across the Constitution</h4>
<table><tr><th>Article</th><th>Part</th><th>Content</th></tr>
<tr><td>21A</td><td>Fundamental Right</td><td>Free and compulsory education, 6–14 years</td></tr>
<tr><td>24</td><td>Fundamental Right</td><td>No child under 14 in factories, mines or hazardous work</td></tr>
<tr><td>39(e), (f)</td><td>DPSP</td><td>Children's tender age not abused; opportunities to develop in freedom and dignity</td></tr>
<tr><td>45</td><td>DPSP</td><td>Early childhood care and education below six</td></tr>
<tr><td>51A(k)</td><td>Fundamental Duty</td><td>Parents to provide educational opportunities</td></tr></table>

<h3>Religious and cultural rights (Articles 25–30)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 25:</b> freedom of conscience and the right to profess, practise and propagate religion.</li>
<li><b>Article 26:</b> freedom to manage religious affairs.</li>
<li><b>Article 27:</b> no taxes for promoting any religion.</li>
<li><b>Article 28:</b> no religious instruction in wholly State-funded institutions.</li>
<li><b>Article 29:</b> protection of the language, script and culture of minorities.
  <ul><li><b>29(2):</b> no citizen may be denied <b>admission</b> to a State-run or State-aided institution on grounds of religion, race, caste or language.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Article 30(1):</b> all <b>minorities, religious or linguistic</b>, have the right to establish and administer educational institutions of their choice.
  <ul><li>In the <b>AMU case</b> (November 2024), a seven-judge bench overruled Azeez Basha (1967) on how minority status is decided.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Clause</th><th>Provision (2002 match)</th></tr>
<tr><td>16(2)</td><td>No discrimination in public appointments on grounds of religion, race, caste and others</td></tr>
<tr><td>29(2)</td><td>No denial of admission to State or State-aided institutions</td></tr>
<tr><td>30(1)</td><td>Minorities may establish and run educational institutions</td></tr>
<tr><td>31(1)</td><td>No deprivation of property save by authority of law (repealed in 1978; now Article 300A)</td></tr></table>

<h3>The right to property</h3>
<div class="key"><b>Property: from Fundamental Right to legal right</b><ul>
<li>The <b>44th Amendment (1978)</b>, passed by the <b>Janata government</b> (Morarji Desai), not the Congress, removed Articles 19(1)(f) and 31.</li>
<li>Property is now a <b>constitutional or legal right</b> under <b>Article 300A</b> (Part XII): "No person shall be deprived of his property save by authority of law."</li>
<li>It is available to <b>any person</b>, including non-citizens and companies.</li>
<li>Its remedy lies in a High Court (Article 226) or an ordinary suit, <b>not Article 32</b>.</li></ul>
<b>Article 301</b> is <b>not</b> about property; it guarantees freedom of trade, commerce and intercourse throughout India (Part XIII).</div>
<ul>
<li>The courts call Article 300A a human right, and compensation must be just (Vidya Devi, 2020).</li>
<li>The <b>Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Act, 2013</b> provides:
  <ul><li>compensation up to four times market value in rural areas;</li>
  <li>a social impact assessment;</li>
  <li>consent of 70–80% of affected families for PPP and private projects.</li></ul></li>
<li>Property survives inside Part III in two places: Article 30(1A), compensation when minority institutions' property is acquired, and the second proviso to Article 31A, land under personal cultivation within the ceiling.</li>
<li><b>Saving clauses:</b> Articles 31A, 31B (the Ninth Schedule) and 31C protect land reform laws and laws giving effect to Articles 39(b) and (c).
  <ul><li>In <b>Property Owners Association</b> (November 2024), a nine-judge bench held that not all private property is a "material resource of the community" under Article 39(b).</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Right to Constitutional Remedies (Article 32)</h3>
<p>Ambedkar called <b>Article 32</b> "the very soul of the Constitution and the very heart of it", because without a remedy the other rights would be empty. It was not freedom of religion, property or equality that he described this way.</p>
<ul>
<li>The Supreme Court can issue five writs: habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, certiorari and quo warranto (see 7.2).</li>
<li>High Courts have wider writ powers under Article 226: they can also enforce legal rights.</li>
<li>Article 32 can be suspended only during an Emergency (Article 359), and never for Articles 20 and 21.</li>
<li>Article 32 is part of the <b>basic structure</b> (L. Chandra Kumar, 1997).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p4-4", `
<p>Part IV (Articles 36–51) contains the <b>Directive Principles of State Policy</b>: positive goals the State must pursue to build a welfare state. They cannot be enforced in court, but the Constitution calls them fundamental to governance. UPSC tests them more than almost any other part.</p>

<h3>Nature of the Directive Principles</h3>
<div class="key"><b>Article 37</b>The provisions of Part IV "shall <b>not be enforceable by any court</b>, but the principles therein laid down are nevertheless <b>fundamental in the governance of the country</b> and it shall be the duty of the State to apply these principles in making laws."<br><br>So "they shall be enforceable by courts" is wrong. "They shall not be enforceable by any court" and "they are to influence the making of laws" are both right.</div>
<ul>
<li><b>"Fundamental in the governance of the country"</b> is said only of the Directive Principles, not of Fundamental Rights or Duties.</li>
<li>The DPSPs <b>do not limit</b> either legislative or executive power. They are positive directions: "thou shalt". It is the Fundamental Rights that limit the State ("thou shalt not", Article 13). A law cannot be struck down for violating a DPSP.</li>
<li>Their <b>purpose</b> is to establish <b>social and economic democracy</b> and a <b>welfare state</b>; Fundamental Rights secure political democracy.</li>
<li>The ideal of a <b>Welfare State</b> is declared in the <b>Directive Principles</b>, not the Preamble, Fundamental Rights or Seventh Schedule. Article 38 asks the State to promote the people's welfare through a social order of social, economic and political justice.</li>
<li><b>Sources and descriptions:</b>
  <ul><li>They were borrowed from the <b>Irish Constitution</b> (1937), which took them from Spain.</li>
  <li>They resemble the <b>Instrument of Instructions</b> under the 1935 Act (see 2.1).</li>
  <li>Ambedkar called them "novel features", whose sanction is the electorate.</li>
  <li>Granville Austin called the Fundamental Rights and DPSPs together the "conscience of the Constitution".</li></ul></li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Fundamental Rights</th><th>Directive Principles</th></tr>
<tr><td>Justiciable</td><td>Non-justiciable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mostly negative: they limit the State</td><td>Positive: they direct the State</td></tr>
<tr><td>Political democracy</td><td>Social and economic democracy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Laws violating them are void</td><td>Laws cannot be struck down for violating them</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can be suspended in an emergency, except Articles 20 and 21</td><td>–</td></tr></table>

<h3>The Directive Principles, Article by Article</h3>
<table><tr><th>Article</th><th>Directive</th></tr>
<tr><td>38</td><td>A social order with social, economic and political justice. 38(2), added by the 44th Amendment: minimise inequalities of income, status and opportunity.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>39</b></td><td>(a) adequate livelihood; (b) ownership and control of material resources to serve the common good; <b>(c) the economic system must not lead to concentration of wealth</b> and means of production to the common detriment; <b>(d) equal pay for equal work for men and women</b>; (e) health of workers and children; (f) healthy development of children (42nd Amendment)</td></tr>
<tr><td>39A</td><td>Equal justice and <b>free legal aid</b> (42nd Amendment)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>40</b></td><td>Organise <b>village panchayats</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>41</b></td><td>Right to work, education and <b>public assistance</b> in unemployment, old age, sickness and disablement</td></tr>
<tr><td>42</td><td>Just and humane conditions of work; maternity relief</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>43</b></td><td>Living wage, decent conditions, leisure and cultural opportunities for workers; <b>cottage industries</b> in rural areas</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>43A</b></td><td><b>Workers' participation in the management of industries</b> (42nd Amendment)</td></tr>
<tr><td>43B</td><td>Promote co-operative societies (97th Amendment, 2011)</td></tr>
<tr><td>44</td><td><b>Uniform Civil Code</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>45</td><td>Early childhood care and education below six (86th Amendment)</td></tr>
<tr><td>46</td><td>Educational and economic interests of SCs, STs and <b>weaker sections</b>; protect them from social injustice</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>47</b></td><td>Nutrition and public health; <b>prohibition of intoxicating drinks and harmful drugs</b> except for medicinal purposes</td></tr>
<tr><td>48</td><td>Organise agriculture and animal husbandry on scientific lines; ban cow slaughter</td></tr>
<tr><td>48A</td><td>Protect the environment, forests and wildlife (42nd Amendment)</td></tr>
<tr><td>49</td><td>Protect monuments of national importance</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>50</b></td><td><b>Separate the judiciary from the executive</b> in the public services of the State</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>51</b></td><td><b>Promote international peace and security</b>; just and honourable relations between nations; respect international law and treaty obligations; settle disputes by arbitration</td></tr></table>

<h4>Common traps</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Concentration of wealth</b> violates the <b>Directive Principles</b> (Article 39(c)), not the Right to Equality, the Right to Freedom or a vague "concept of welfare". <b>Article 31C</b> shields laws giving effect to Articles 39(b) and (c) from challenge under Articles 14 and 19.</li>
<li><b>Separation of the judiciary</b> is directed by a DPSP (Article 50), not the Preamble, the Seventh Schedule or convention. The CrPC 1973 (now the BNSS 2023) put it into effect by separating judicial from executive magistrates.</li>
<li><b>International peace and security</b> is in Article 51 (a DPSP), not the Preamble, the Fundamental Duties or the Ninth Schedule. Do not confuse Article 51 with Article 51A (Fundamental Duties). Article 48A is the environment, 43A workers' participation, and 41 the right to work.</li>
<li><b>Prohibition of intoxicants</b> (Article 47) is a DPSP. <b>Trafficking and forced labour</b> (Article 23) is a Fundamental Right.
  <ul><li>States and UTs with prohibition include Gujarat, Bihar (2016), Mizoram, Nagaland and Lakshadweep.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Equal pay for equal work</b> <b>is</b> in the Constitution, as Article 39(d). It was given effect by the Equal Remuneration Act, 1976 (now part of the Code on Wages, 2019), and read with Articles 14 and 16 in Randhir Singh (1982).</li>
<li>The Constitution does <b>not define "backward classes"</b>. It uses "socially and educationally backward classes" in Articles 15(4), 16(4) and 340 (see 11.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Added by amendments</h3>
<table><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>Change</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>42nd (1976)</b></td><td>39(f) (children); <b>39A</b> (free legal aid); <b>43A</b> (workers' participation in management); <b>48A</b> (environment)</td></tr>
<tr><td>44th (1978)</td><td>38(2) (minimise inequalities)</td></tr>
<tr><td>86th (2002)</td><td>Article 45 changed to early childhood care</td></tr>
<tr><td>97th (2011)</td><td>43B (co-operatives)</td></tr></table>
<p>Equal pay (39(d)), the right to work, education and public assistance (41), and a living wage and humane conditions of work (42, 43) were in the <b>original</b> Constitution. Of the options asked, only <b>workers' participation in management</b> came with the 42nd Amendment.</p>

<h3>Classification (not in the Constitution itself)</h3>
<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Articles</th></tr>
<tr><td>Socialist</td><td>38, 39, 39A, 41, 42, 43 (living wage, leisure), 43A, 47</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Gandhian</b></td><td><b>40 (village panchayats)</b>, <b>43 (cottage industries)</b>, 43B (co-operatives), 46 (weaker sections), 47 (prohibition), 48 (cow slaughter)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Liberal-intellectual</td><td>44 (Uniform Civil Code), 45, 48 (scientific agriculture), 48A, 49, 50, 51</td></tr></table>
<div class="trap"><b>Gandhian or not?</b><ul>
<li>The <b>Uniform Civil Code</b> is liberal-intellectual, not Gandhian.</li>
<li>Reasonable <b>leisure and cultural opportunities</b> for workers is socialist.</li>
<li>Only panchayats and cottage industries were Gandhian among the options asked.</li></ul>
Uniform Civil Codes are in force in Uttarakhand (January 2025), and have been passed in Gujarat (March 2026) and Assam (May 2026).</div>

<h3>Conflict and harmony with Fundamental Rights</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Champakam Dorairajan (1951):</b> Fundamental Rights prevail over DPSPs. This prompted the First Amendment.</li>
<li><b>Golaknath (1967); the 25th Amendment</b> (Article 31C, 1971); <b>Kesavananda Bharati (1973)</b>; and the 42nd Amendment (1976), which tried to give all DPSPs priority.</li>
<li><b>Minerva Mills (1980):</b> <b>harmony and balance</b> between Fundamental Rights and DPSPs is part of the basic structure.</li>
<li>Courts use DPSPs to give content to Fundamental Rights. Article 21 has been expanded using Articles 39, 41 and 47, and the ban on cow slaughter was upheld using Article 48 (Mirzapur Moti Kureshi, 2005).</li>
</ul>

<h3>DPSPs in action</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>National Social Assistance Programme</b> (1995, Ministry of Rural Development) fulfils a <b>Directive Principle</b> (Article 41, public assistance), not a Fundamental Right or Duty.
  <ul><li>It covers old-age, widow and disability pensions, the National Family Benefit Scheme and Annapurna.</li></ul></li>
<li>Other DPSP-linked laws and schemes:
  <ul><li>MGNREGA (Article 41), now replaced by <b>VB-G RAM G</b>;</li>
  <li>mid-day meals and the National Food Security Act (Article 47);</li>
  <li>RTE (Articles 45 and 21A);</li>
  <li>the Maternity Benefit Act (Article 42);</li>
  <li>panchayats (Article 40, via the 73rd Amendment).</li></ul></li>
</ul>
<h4>Education across the Constitution</h4>
<p>All five of these have a bearing on education:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>DPSPs:</b> Articles 41, 45 and 46.</li>
<li><b>Rural and urban local bodies:</b> the 11th and 12th Schedules list education, libraries and cultural matters.</li>
<li><b>Fifth Schedule:</b> the Governor's regulations and Tribes Advisory Councils cover the welfare and education of STs.</li>
<li><b>Sixth Schedule:</b> Autonomous District Councils can establish primary schools.</li>
<li><b>Seventh Schedule:</b> education is Concurrent List Entry 25, while Union List Entries 63–66 cover central universities and standards.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Viksit Bharat Shiksha Adhishthan Bill (introduced December 2025, now with a joint committee) would replace the UGC, AICTE and NCTE with a single higher-education regulator.</p>

<h3>The UDHR and the Constitution</h3>
<p>The <b>Universal Declaration of Human Rights</b> was adopted by the UN General Assembly on <b>10 December 1948</b>, now Human Rights Day.</p>
<ul>
<li>Its drafting committee was chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt. India's <b>Hansa Mehta</b> changed "all men are born free" to "all human beings".</li>
<li>It has 30 articles and is not itself binding. The ICCPR and ICESCR (1966) are binding on their parties, and the three together form the International Bill of Human Rights.</li>
<li>It includes:
  <ul><li>the <b>right to education</b> (Article 26);</li>
  <li><b>equal access to public service</b> (Article 21(2));</li>
  <li>an adequate standard of living including <b>food</b>, clothing, housing and medical care (Article 25).</li></ul></li>
</ul>
<p>Beyond the Fundamental Rights, its ideas are reflected in all three of the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>the <b>Preamble</b> (liberty, equality, dignity, fraternity, echoing UDHR Article 1);</li>
<li>the <b>Directive Principles</b> (work, education, public assistance, living wage, echoing UDHR Articles 22–26);</li>
<li>the <b>Fundamental Duties</b> (UDHR Article 29 speaks of duties to the community).</li>
</ul>
<p>India's Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993 created the NHRC (see 12.2). The UN Human Rights Council (Geneva, 2006) replaced the Commission on Human Rights.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p4-5", `
<p>The Fundamental Duties remind citizens that rights come with obligations. They are listed in a single Article, 51A, in Part IVA.</p>

<h3>Origin and nature</h3>
<ul>
<li>They were added by the <b>42nd Amendment (1976)</b> on the recommendation of the <b>Swaran Singh Committee</b>, with <b>ten</b> duties. They were inspired by the <b>USSR</b> Constitution.</li>
<li>The <b>eleventh</b>, 51A(k), was added by the <b>86th Amendment (2002)</b>: parents or guardians must provide opportunities for education to children aged 6–14.</li>
<li>They apply <b>only to citizens</b>.</li>
<li>They are <b>non-justiciable</b>:
  <ul><li>The Constitution provides <b>no legislative process to enforce them</b>, though Parliament may enforce particular duties through ordinary laws.</li>
  <li>They are moral and civic duties, <b>not correlative to legal duties</b>.</li></ul></li>
<li>Courts use them to judge whether a law is reasonable (AIIMS Students' Union, 2001).</li>
<li>The <b>Verma Committee (1999)</b> identified laws that give effect to some duties: the Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act, 1971; the Wild Life Act; and the Environment Protection Act.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The eleven duties (Article 51A)</h3>
<table><tr><th>Clause</th><th>Duty</th></tr>
<tr><td>(a)</td><td>Abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals and institutions, the National Flag and the National Anthem</td></tr>
<tr><td>(b)</td><td>Cherish and follow the noble ideals of the freedom struggle</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>(c)</b></td><td><b>Uphold and protect the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>(d)</td><td>Defend the country and render national service when called upon</td></tr>
<tr><td>(e)</td><td>Promote harmony and brotherhood; renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>(f)</b></td><td><b>Value and preserve the rich heritage of our composite culture</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>(g)</td><td>Protect and improve the natural environment; have compassion for living creatures</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>(h)</b></td><td><b>Develop the scientific temper</b>, humanism and the spirit of inquiry and reform</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>(i)</b></td><td><b>Safeguard public property</b> and abjure violence</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>(j)</b></td><td><b>Strive towards excellence</b> in all spheres of individual and collective activity</td></tr>
<tr><td>(k)</td><td>Parents to provide educational opportunities to children aged 6–14 (86th Amendment)</td></tr></table>

<div class="trap"><b>Common traps</b><ul>
<li>"<b>To vote in public elections</b>" is <b>not</b> a Fundamental Duty. Voting is a right (see 1.1). Gujarat once proposed compulsory voting in local elections (a 2009 bill), but it is not national law.</li>
<li>"<b>To protect the weaker sections from social injustice</b>" is a duty of the <b>State</b> under Article 46 (a DPSP), not of citizens.</li>
<li>"Uphold sovereignty, unity and integrity" is a Fundamental Duty (51A(c)).
  <ul><li>The Preamble speaks of the "unity and integrity of the Nation".</li>
  <li>Article 19(2) allows restrictions "in the interests of the sovereignty and integrity of India".</li>
  <li>The oaths in the Third Schedule also mention it.</li>
  <li>The duty itself sits in Article 51A.</li></ul></li></ul></div>

<h3>Rights, Directives and Duties together</h3>
<p>All three of these 2025 pairs are correctly matched:</p>
<ul>
<li>separating the judiciary from the executive: a <b>DPSP</b> (Article 50);</li>
<li>valuing our composite culture: a <b>Fundamental Duty</b> (51A(f));</li>
<li>no employment of children under 14 in factories: a <b>Fundamental Right</b> (Article 24).</li>
</ul>
<p>In political theory rights and duties are correlative (see 1.1), but in Indian law the Fundamental Duties are not enforceable counterparts of the Fundamental Rights.</p>
`);
;
