// Polity book, Unit 5: The Union Executive.
addBookTopic("polity", "p5-1", `
<p>The President is the head of State and the formal head of the Union executive. Article 53 vests the executive power of the Union in the President. In a parliamentary system, however, the President acts on the advice of the Council of Ministers headed by the Prime Minister (see 1.3, 5.3).</p>

<h3>Key Articles</h3>
<table><tr><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>52–53</b></td><td>There shall be a President; the <b>executive power of the Union is vested in the President</b>, not the Prime Minister</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>54</b></td><td><b>Election</b> of the President (electoral college)</td></tr>
<tr><td>55</td><td>Manner of election and value of votes</td></tr>
<tr><td>56–58</td><td>Term (five years, eligible for re-election); qualifications (a <b>citizen</b> aged 35 or more, qualified for the Lok Sabha)</td></tr>
<tr><td>61</td><td>Impeachment for "violation of the Constitution"</td></tr>
<tr><td>62</td><td>Elections must be held on time</td></tr>
<tr><td>71</td><td>Election disputes go only to the Supreme Court</td></tr>
<tr><td>72</td><td>Pardoning power</td></tr>
<tr><td>74</td><td>Council of Ministers to aid and advise the President</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>75</b></td><td>Appointment of the <b>Prime Minister and other ministers</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>77</td><td>Conduct of business (see 5.3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>111</td><td>Assent to bills</td></tr>
<tr><td>123</td><td>Ordinances</td></tr>
<tr><td>143</td><td>Reference to the Supreme Court (see 7.1)</td></tr></table>
<p>The matching State Articles are <b>155</b> (appointment of the Governor), <b>164</b> (appointment of the Chief Minister and ministers) and 170 (composition of Legislative Assemblies).</p>

<h3>Election of the President</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Electoral college:</b> the <b>elected</b> members of both Houses of Parliament and of the State Legislative Assemblies, including those of <b>Delhi and Puducherry</b> (70th Amendment, 1992).</li>
<li><b>Nominated members</b> of Parliament or of Assemblies, and members of <b>Legislative Councils</b>, do <b>not</b> vote.</li>
<li><b>Method:</b> proportional representation by means of the single transferable vote, by secret ballot.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Value of votes</b><ul>
<li><b>Each MLA:</b> State population (1971 Census) ÷ number of elected MLAs ÷ 1,000. It <b>varies from State to State</b>.
  <ul><li>For the same population, <b>more seats means a lower value</b> per MLA, because seats are the denominator.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Each MP:</b> the total value of all MLAs' votes ÷ the total number of elected MPs. It is the <b>same for Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha MPs</b>: 708 in 2002 and <b>700</b> in 2022 (lower because J&amp;K had no Assembly).</li>
<li>The population base stays frozen at <b>1971</b> until after the first Census after 2026 (84th Amendment).</li></ul></div>
<table><tr><th>State (2022)</th><th>Value of each MLA's vote</th></tr>
<tr><td>Uttar Pradesh</td><td>208 (highest)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jharkhand, Tamil Nadu</td><td>176</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maharashtra</td><td>175</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bihar</td><td>173</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kerala</b></td><td><b>152</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Madhya Pradesh</b></td><td><b>131</b>, lower than Kerala's</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Puducherry</b></td><td><b>16</b>, higher than Arunachal Pradesh, because its population per seat is higher</td></tr>
<tr><td>Arunachal Pradesh, Mizoram</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sikkim</td><td>7 (lowest)</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The 2002 electoral college had about <b>4,896 members</b> (776 elected MPs and 4,120 elected MLAs), fewer than 5,000.</li>
<li><b>Droupadi Murmu</b> became the 15th President in 2022, the first tribal woman to hold the office. The next election is due in 2027.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Election disputes and timing (Article 71)</h4>
<ul>
<li>If the Supreme Court declares a President's election <b>void</b>, acts done before that decision are <b>not invalidated</b>.</li>
<li>The election <b>cannot be postponed</b> because some Assemblies are dissolved. Article 71(4), added by the 11th Amendment (1961), bars any challenge on the ground of a vacancy in the electoral college.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Veto and assent</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Constitution sets <b>no time limit</b> for the President's assent to bills (Article 111). This makes a <b>pocket veto</b> possible: Zail Singh used it on the Indian Post Office (Amendment) Bill, 1986.</li>
<li>In its 2025 opinion on a Presidential reference, the Supreme Court held that courts <b>cannot impose timelines</b> either.</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Veto</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Absolute</td><td>Withhold assent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suspensive</td><td>Return the bill once for reconsideration. Not available for Money Bills.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pocket</td><td>Keep the bill pending indefinitely</td></tr></table>
<p>There is <b>no veto over constitutional amendment bills</b>: the President must assent (24th Amendment).</p>
<h4>Returning advice for reconsideration</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>42nd Amendment (1976)</b> made the Council of Ministers' advice binding on the President.</li>
<li>The <b>44th Amendment (1978)</b> added a proviso to Article 74(1): the President may <b>send advice back once for reconsideration</b>, but must then act on the advice given after reconsideration. The 39th and 40th Amendments did not do this.</li>
<li><b>2002:</b> President Kalam returned the ordinance diluting candidates' disclosure of criminal records. When the Cabinet sent it back unchanged, he signed it under <b>Article 123</b>. The replacement law was struck down in PUCL (2003).</li>
<li><b>2006:</b> Kalam also returned the Office of Profit Bill.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ordinances (Article 123)</h3>
<p>The President can issue ordinances <b>only when either House is not in session</b>, on being "satisfied that circumstances exist which render it necessary to take immediate action". This is done on the Council of Ministers' advice. An ordinance has "the same force and effect as an Act of Parliament".</p>
<div class="key"><b>What an ordinance can and cannot do</b><ul>
<li>It <b>can amend or repeal any Central Act</b>.</li>
<li>It <b>can be retrospective</b>, though it cannot create retrospective criminal liability (Article 20(1)).</li>
<li>It <b>cannot abridge a Fundamental Right</b>. An ordinance is "law" under Article 13(3)(a), so it is void to that extent.</li>
<li>It <b>cannot amend the Constitution</b>, which requires the Article 368 procedure.</li>
<li>It can be issued only on subjects on which Parliament can legislate.</li></ul></div>
<table><tr><th></th><th>President (Article 123)</th><th>Governor (Article 213)</th></tr>
<tr><td>When</td><td>Either House not in session</td><td>Legislature not in session</td></tr>
<tr><td>Advice</td><td>Council of Ministers</td><td>Council of Ministers; needs the President's instructions in some cases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Life</td><td>Lapses six weeks after Parliament reassembles (maximum about six months plus six weeks)</td><td>Six weeks after the legislature reassembles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Withdrawal</td><td>Any time</td><td>Any time</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The 38th Amendment (1975) made the President's satisfaction final, but the 44th Amendment (1978) removed that, so it can be reviewed for mala fides (R.C. Cooper, 1970; A.K. Roy, 1982).</li>
<li><b>D.C. Wadhwa (1987):</b> repeated re-promulgation is a "fraud on the Constitution".</li>
<li><b>Krishna Kumar Singh (2017, seven judges):</b> laying an ordinance before the legislature is mandatory, ordinances are open to judicial review, and rights created by one do not automatically survive its lapse.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pardoning power (Article 72)</h3>
<ul>
<li>The President exercises it on the <b>advice of the Union Council of Ministers</b> (Maru Ram 1980; Dhananjoy Chatterjee 1994). It is <b>not</b> a personal discretion.</li>
<li>It is subject to <b>limited judicial review</b> for non-application of mind, mala fides, irrelevant considerations, or discrimination and arbitrariness (Epuru Sudhakar, 2006).</li>
<li>In Kehar Singh (1989) the Court said the President may examine the evidence afresh, but courts will not review the merits.</li>
<li>In Shatrughan Chauhan (2014), unexplained delay in deciding mercy petitions became a ground to commute a death sentence.</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pardon</td><td>Removes both sentence and conviction</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commutation</td><td>Substitutes a lighter form of punishment, such as death to life imprisonment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Remission</td><td>Reduces the period of the sentence without changing its nature</td></tr>
<tr><td>Respite</td><td>A lesser sentence because of special facts, such as pregnancy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reprieve</td><td>A temporary stay of execution</td></tr></table>
<table><tr><th></th><th>President (Article 72)</th><th>Governor (Article 161)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Offences against</td><td>Union laws</td><td>State laws</td></tr>
<tr><td>Court-martial</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Death sentence</td><td>Can pardon</td><td>Cannot pardon (can suspend, remit or commute)</td></tr></table>
<p>In <b>A.G. Perarivalan</b> (2022) the Court held that the Governor is bound by the State cabinet's advice under Article 161.</p>

<h3>Reports the President lays before Parliament</h3>
<p>The President must cause the following to be laid before Parliament:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Finance Commission</b> recommendations, with an explanatory memorandum (Article 281);</li>
<li>the <b>CAG</b>'s reports on Union accounts (Article 151);</li>
<li>reports of the <b>National Commission for Scheduled Castes</b> (Article 338), and of the NCST (338A) and NCBC (338B);</li>
<li><b>UPSC</b> reports (Article 323);</li>
<li>reports of the Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities (Article 350B);</li>
<li>the Annual Financial Statement (Article 112).</li>
</ul>
<p>The <b>Public Accounts Committee</b> is a committee of Parliament and presents its own reports; the President does <b>not</b> lay them.</p>

<h3>Executive power and civil services</h3>
<ul>
<li>The executive power is vested in the <b>President</b> (Article 53), not the PM. The PM heads the Council of Ministers that advises the President.</li>
<li>The <b>Civil Services Board</b>, which handles transfers and postings of senior officers, is chaired by the <b>Cabinet Secretary</b> at the Centre and by the <b>Chief Secretary</b> in States (T.S.R. Subramanian, 2013). The PM does not chair it.</li>
<li>Senior appointments are approved by the Appointments Committee of the Cabinet (the PM and the Home Minister).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p5-2", `
<p>The Vice-President is the second-highest constitutional office and is also the <b>ex officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha</b>. The office was borrowed from the USA.</p>

<h3>Election and removal</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Election (Article 66):</b> by an electoral college of <b>all members of both Houses</b>, including <b>nominated</b> members. State legislators do not vote.
  <ul><li>Voting is by proportional representation by single transferable vote, with a secret ballot.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Removal (Article 67(b)):</b> by a resolution of the <b>Rajya Sabha alone</b>, passed by an effective majority (a majority of all its then members), <b>and agreed to by the Lok Sabha</b> by simple majority.
  <ul><li>At least 14 days' notice is needed, and the Constitution specifies no grounds.</li>
  <li>The Lok Sabha only agrees; it cannot initiate.</li>
  <li>The resolution cannot be moved in either House, or at a joint sitting.</li></ul></li>
<li>The President, by contrast, is removed by <b>impeachment</b> (Article 61), which can begin in either House.</li>
<li>The term is five years. The Vice-President acts as President in a vacancy for up to six months.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Who has held the office</h3>
<table><tr><th>Person</th><th>Vice-President?</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>M. Hidayatullah</b></td><td><b>Yes</b> (1979–84). As Chief Justice he had also acted as President in 1969, the only person to have held all three roles.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fakhruddin Ali Ahmed</td><td>No. He was President (1974–77) and died in office.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Neelam Sanjiva Reddy</td><td>No. He was Lok Sabha Speaker and President (1977–82, elected unopposed).</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Shankar Dayal Sharma</b></td><td><b>Yes</b> (1987–92), then President (1992–97)</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>Vice-Presidents who went on to become President: S. Radhakrishnan, Zakir Husain, V.V. Giri, R. Venkataraman, S.D. Sharma and K.R. Narayanan.</li>
<li><b>December 2024:</b> opposition members gave notice of the first-ever removal motion, against Jagdeep Dhankhar. The Deputy Chairman rejected it on procedural grounds.</li>
<li><b>2025:</b> Dhankhar resigned in July on health grounds, and <b>C.P. Radhakrishnan</b> was elected the 15th Vice-President in September.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p5-3", `
<p>Real executive power lies with the <b>Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers</b>, collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha. This is the heart of the parliamentary system (see 1.3).</p>

<h3>Key Articles</h3>
<table><tr><th>Article</th><th>Provision</th></tr>
<tr><td>74</td><td>A Council of Ministers headed by the PM aids and advises the President; the advice is binding (subject to one reconsideration; see 5.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>75(1)</td><td>The President appoints the PM, and other ministers on the PM's advice</td></tr>
<tr><td>75(1A)</td><td>Size cap of 15% (91st Amendment)</td></tr>
<tr><td>75(2)</td><td>Ministers hold office <b>during the pleasure of the President</b> (individual responsibility)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>75(3)</b></td><td>The Council of Ministers is <b>collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>75(5)</td><td>A minister who is not an MP for six consecutive months ceases to be a minister</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>77</b></td><td>All executive action is expressed in the <b>name of the President</b>; the President makes rules for the transaction and allocation of business</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>78</b></td><td>The PM must <b>communicate</b> to the President all Cabinet decisions on administration and <b>proposals for legislation</b>, and furnish information the President asks for</td></tr></table>
<p>The State counterparts are Articles 154, 163, 164, 166 and 167.</p>
<div class="trap"><b>Common traps</b><ul>
<li>The Council of Ministers is responsible to the <b>Lok Sabha</b>, not to Parliament as a whole and not to both Houses. Only the Lok Sabha can pass a no-confidence motion.</li>
<li>Members of <b>both Houses</b> can be ministers, so the reason given in that assertion is true while the assertion itself is false. Indira Gandhi (1966) and Manmohan Singh (2004–14) were PMs from the Rajya Sabha.</li>
<li>Executive action is taken in the President's name, not the PM's.</li>
<li>Cabinet Ministers need <b>not</b> be Lok Sabha members.</li></ul></div>

<h3>Appointment of the PM and ministers</h3>
<ul>
<li>At the time of appointment, the PM <b>need not be a member of either House</b>, but must become a member of <b>either House</b> within six months. It does not have to be the Lok Sabha.</li>
<li>The President appoints the leader of the majority party or coalition. In a hung House the President uses discretion and may ask for a floor test.</li>
<li>A non-member can be a <b>State minister</b> for six months only if <b>qualified to be a member</b> of the legislature (Article 173: a citizen aged 25 or more for the Assembly). Being eligible to vote (18) is not enough.</li>
<li>In <b>S.R. Chaudhuri (2001)</b> the Court barred re-appointing a non-member as minister without election.</li>
<li>A minister may speak in either House but can vote only in their own House (Article 88).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The 91st Amendment (2003)</h3>
<div class="key"><b>A 15% cap on the Council of Ministers</b><ul>
<li>The total number of ministers, <b>including the PM</b>, may not exceed <b>15% of the Lok Sabha's total strength</b> (Article 75(1A)), a maximum of 81.</li>
<li>In States the cap is <b>15% of the Assembly, with a minimum of 12</b> (Article 164(1A)). The minimum binds small States such as Sikkim, Goa and Mizoram. Delhi is capped at 10% under Article 239AA.</li>
<li>A legislator <b>disqualified for defection</b> cannot become a minister or hold a remunerative political post until re-elected (Articles 75(1B), 164(1B), 361B).</li>
<li>It deleted the "split" (one-third) exception from the Tenth Schedule (see 6.1).</li></ul>
It was the 91st Amendment, not the 90th, 92nd, 93rd, 95th or 97th.</div>
<p>The Constitution does <b>not</b> classify ministers into ranks. Cabinet ministers, Ministers of State (Independent Charge), Ministers of State and Deputy Ministers come from convention and the Business Rules.</p>

<h3>The Cabinet system</h3>
<p>The principle underlying Cabinet government is <b>collective responsibility of the government to the people</b>, exercised through Parliament. Ministers "sink or swim together": a no-confidence vote removes the whole ministry, and every minister must publicly support Cabinet decisions or resign.</p>
<ul>
<li>It is <b>not</b> a device to minimise criticism, speed up work, or prop up a weak leader.</li>
<li>Its other principles are individual responsibility, secrecy (under oath) and the PM's leadership.</li>
<li>The Cabinet is the inner body of senior ministers. The word "Cabinet" appears in the Constitution only in <b>Article 352</b>, added by the 44th Amendment (1978).</li>
<li>Walter Bagehot called the Cabinet "the hyphen that joins, the buckle that fastens" the legislature to the executive.</li>
<li><b>Cabinet committees:</b> the Appointments Committee (PM and Home Minister), the Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs, the Cabinet Committee on Security, Political Affairs, and Parliamentary Affairs.</li>
<li><b>Confidence votes:</b> no no-confidence motion has directly toppled a government. Morarji Desai resigned in 1979 before the vote. The V.P. Singh (1990), Deve Gowda (1997) and Vajpayee (1999, by one vote) governments lost confidence votes. The 2023 no-confidence motion was defeated.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Cabinet Secretariat</h3>
<ul>
<li>It works <b>directly under the Prime Minister</b>, not under the Ministry of Parliamentary Affairs. It is headed by the <b>Cabinet Secretary</b>, the senior-most civil servant (T.V. Somanathan since August 2024).</li>
<li><b>Its functions:</b>
  <ul><li>preparing the <b>agenda for Cabinet meetings</b> and keeping records;</li>
  <li><b>secretarial help to Cabinet committees</b>;</li>
  <li>monitoring decisions and coordinating between ministries;</li>
  <li>crisis management (the National Crisis Management Committee);</li>
  <li>administering the Business Rules.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Allocating funds to ministries</b> is <b>not</b> its job; that belongs to the Finance Ministry through the Budget.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ministries and allocation of business</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ministries and departments are <b>created by the President on the PM's advice</b> under the Government of India (Allocation of Business) Rules, 1961, made under Article 77(3). The Cabinet Secretary does not advise on this.</li>
<li>Portfolios are <b>assigned to ministers by the President on the PM's advice</b>.</li>
<li>The Union began with <b>18 ministries</b> on 15 August 1947. There were already far more than 36 by 2009, and there are over 50 today. Recent additions are Jal Shakti (2019) and Cooperation (2021).</li>
<li>There is no cap on the number of ministries, only on the number of ministers. Departments and their parent ministries are covered in 14.1.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Disqualification of legislators</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under Section 8(3) of the RPA 1951, conviction with a sentence of <b>two years or more</b> disqualifies a person from the date of conviction <b>and for six years after release</b>. It is not permanent, so "a five-year sentence means a permanent ban" is wrong.</li>
<li>Section 8(1) offences (such as promoting enmity, bribery, rape, untouchability and terrorism) disqualify whatever the sentence.</li>
<li>The Election Commission can reduce the period (Section 11).</li>
<li><b>Lily Thomas (2013)</b> struck down Section 8(4), so disqualification is immediate on conviction.</li>
<li>Petitions seeking a life ban for convicted politicians are pending (the Ashwini Upadhyay case).</li>
<li>The Constitution (130th Amendment) Bill, 2025, which would remove ministers held in custody for 30 days, was sent to a joint committee.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p5-4", `
<p>The <b>Attorney General</b> (Article 76) is the highest law officer of the Union. The State counterpart is the Advocate General (Article 165; see 8.2).</p>

<h3>Appointment and tenure</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Attorney General is <b>appointed by the President</b> and must be <b>qualified to be a Supreme Court judge</b>.</li>
<li>They hold office <b>during the pleasure of the President</b>. The Constitution fixes <b>no term</b> and <b>no removal procedure</b>, so there is <b>no impeachment</b>.</li>
<li>Resigning when the government changes is only a <b>convention</b>; the Constitution does not require it.</li>
<li>Remuneration is fixed by the President.</li>
<li>The Attorney General need <b>not be a member of Parliament</b> and is <b>not</b> part of the Cabinet.</li>
<li>They are not a full-time government servant and may take private practice, but not against the government.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Rights in Parliament</h3>
<div class="key"><b>Article 88</b>Every minister and the Attorney General may <b>speak in</b> and <b>take part in the proceedings</b> of either House, a joint sitting, and any <b>parliamentary committee</b> of which they are named a member. They <b>cannot vote</b>.<ul>
<li>The <b>Solicitor General</b> has <b>no</b> such constitutional right; that post, and the Additional Solicitors General, are statutory or administrative, not constitutional.</li>
<li>So "the AG and SG are the only officers allowed to take part in Parliament" is wrong, because ministers also have this right.</li></ul></div>

<h3>Duties and privileges</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Duties:</b> advising the Government of India on legal matters, appearing for it in the Supreme Court and High Courts, and representing it in Presidential references under Article 143.</li>
<li><b>Privileges:</b> the right of audience in <b>all courts in India</b>, and the privileges of an MP (Article 105(4)).</li>
<li>The first Attorney General was <b>M.C. Setalvad</b>, also the longest-serving. The current one is <b>R. Venkataramani</b> (since October 2022, extended in 2025).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p6.js
// Polity book, Unit 6 (part 1): Parliament: composition, presiding officers, procedure.
addBookTopic("polity", "p6-1", `
<p><b>Article 79:</b> Parliament consists of the <b>President and the two Houses</b>, the Council of States (Rajya Sabha) and the House of the People (Lok Sabha). The President is part of Parliament even though not a member of either House, because no bill becomes law without the President's assent.</p>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b>"Parliament consists of the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha only" is wrong. Parliament also includes the President.</div>

<h3>The two Houses</h3>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Lok Sabha</th><th>Rajya Sabha</th></tr>
<tr><td>Maximum strength</td><td>550 elected (530 from States, 20 from UTs). Two Anglo-Indian nominations ended in 2020 (104th Amendment).</td><td>250: 238 elected from States and UTs, plus 12 nominated by the President</td></tr>
<tr><td>Present strength</td><td>543</td><td>245 (233 + 12)</td></tr>
<tr><td>How elected</td><td>Directly, first-past-the-post</td><td>By elected MLAs, using proportional representation by single transferable vote</td></tr>
<tr><td>Term</td><td>Five years, unless dissolved sooner</td><td>Permanent; one-third of members retire every two years, each with a six-year term</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum age</td><td>25</td><td>30</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li><b>Fourth Schedule:</b> allocates Rajya Sabha seats to States and UTs roughly by population. UP has the most (31), then Maharashtra (19) and Tamil Nadu (18).
  <ul><li>It does <b>not</b> distribute powers (Seventh Schedule), list languages (Eighth) or cover tribal areas (Fifth and Sixth).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>UTs in the Rajya Sabha:</b> UTs <b>with legislatures</b> are represented: <b>Delhi (3)</b>, <b>Puducherry (1)</b> and <b>J&amp;K (4)</b>. Ladakh, with no legislature, has no seat. So "UTs are not represented in the Rajya Sabha" is wrong.</li>
<li><b>Nominated members:</b> both Houses could have them (the Lok Sabha had Anglo-Indians until 2020). The Rajya Sabha's 12 come from literature, science, art and social service.
  <ul><li>There was <b>never</b> a provision to nominate Anglo-Indians to the Rajya Sabha.</li>
  <li>A nominated member <b>can be made a Union minister</b>; there is no constitutional bar.</li>
  <li>Nominated members vote in the <b>Vice-President's</b> election but <b>not the President's</b> (see 5.1).</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Size and term of the Lok Sabha</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Amendments raising the seats:</b>
  <ul><li>The <b>7th Amendment (1956)</b> rewrote Article 81 after States were reorganised, setting 500 seats from the States.</li>
  <li>The <b>31st Amendment (1973)</b> raised the maximum from the States to 525.</li></ul></li>
<li>The <b>84th Amendment (2001)</b> froze seats until after the first Census after 2026. Delimitation and the women's reservation (106th Amendment, 2023) both follow that Census.</li>
<li>The new Parliament building (2023) can seat 888 in the Lok Sabha chamber.</li>
<li><b>Term (Article 83(2)):</b> five years from the first meeting, after which the House is dissolved automatically.
  <ul><li>During a <b>National Emergency</b>, Parliament may extend it by law <b>by up to one year at a time</b>, and not beyond six months after the Emergency ends.</li>
  <li>It was extended once, during 1975–77; the 42nd Amendment had made the term six years, and the 44th restored five. State Assemblies follow the same rule (Article 172).</li></ul></li>
<li>The "One Nation, One Election" Bill (129th Amendment) proposes a new Article 82A to align terms.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Disqualification and office of profit</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 102</b> (Article 191 for States) disqualifies a member who:
  <ul><li>holds an <b>office of profit</b> under the government, unless the office is exempted by law;</li>
  <li>is of unsound mind or an undischarged insolvent;</li>
  <li>is not a citizen;</li>
  <li>is disqualified by a law of Parliament, such as the RPA 1951 (see 5.3);</li>
  <li>is disqualified for defection under the Tenth Schedule.</li></ul></li>
<li>Questions under Article 102(1) are decided by the <b>President on the Election Commission's opinion</b>, which is binding (Article 103). For MLAs, the Governor decides on the EC's opinion (Article 192).</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Office of profit</b><ul>
<li>The <b>Constitution does not define</b> "office of profit". Courts apply tests: who appoints and removes the holder, whether pay comes from public funds, the functions involved, and government control (<b>Jaya Bachchan, 2006</b>).</li>
<li>The <b>Parliament (Prevention of Disqualification) Act, 1959</b> exempts many posts. It has been <b>amended five times</b>, for example in 2006, retrospectively, after Jaya Bachchan's disqualification and the controversy over Sonia Gandhi's NAC chairmanship.</li>
<li>The rule protects legislators' independence from the executive.</li>
<li>In 2018 the EC recommended disqualifying 20 AAP MLAs who were parliamentary secretaries; the Delhi High Court set this aside.</li>
<li>A Joint Committee on Offices of Profit examines posts, and a new bill to replace the 1959 Act was proposed in 2024.</li></ul></div>

<h3>Anti-defection: the Tenth Schedule</h3>
<p>The anti-defection provisions are in the <b>Tenth Schedule</b>, added by the <b>52nd Amendment (1985)</b> under the Rajiv Gandhi government. That amendment also brought the words "political party" into the Constitution, so "the Constitution does not mention political parties" is wrong.</p>
<table><tr><th>Ground for disqualification</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
<tr><td>Voluntarily giving up party membership</td><td>Conduct counts too (Ravi Naik, 1994)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voting or abstaining against the party whip</td><td>Without permission, unless condoned within 15 days</td></tr>
<tr><td>An independent joining a party</td><td>After election</td></tr>
<tr><td>A <b>nominated member</b> joining a party</td><td>Only if after <b>six months</b>. Joining within six months of taking the seat is allowed.</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li><b>Exception:</b> a <b>merger</b> backed by two-thirds of the legislature party (Paragraph 4).</li>
<li>The <b>91st Amendment (2003)</b> deleted the "one-third split" exception and barred defectors from ministerial posts (see 5.3).</li>
<li>A Speaker who leaves their party on election is not disqualified (Paragraph 5).</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Who decides, and how fast</b><ul>
<li>Defection cases are decided by the <b>Speaker</b> (Lok Sabha) or <b>Chairman</b> (Rajya Sabha). The President, acting on the Council of Ministers' advice, does <b>not</b> decide them.</li>
<li><b>Kihoto Hollohan (1992):</b> the presiding officer acts as a <b>tribunal</b>, so the decision is open to <b>judicial review</b>. Paragraph 7, which barred courts, was struck down.</li>
<li>The law sets <b>no time limit</b> for deciding a case.
  <ul><li><b>Keisham Meghachandra Singh (2020):</b> Speakers should normally decide within three months. The Court repeated this deadline in the 2025 Telangana BRS case.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Subhash Desai (2023, Shiv Sena):</b> the decision must turn on the political party, not just the legislature party.</li>
<li><b>Nabam Rebia (2016):</b> a Speaker facing a removal notice cannot decide disqualifications. This has been referred to a larger bench.</li>
<li><b>Reform proposals</b> to have the President or Governor decide on the EC's advice: the Dinesh Goswami Committee (1990) and the Law Commission's 170th report (1999).</li></ul></div>

<h3>Election disputes</h3>
<p>Disputes over parliamentary and assembly elections are decided by an <b>election petition in the High Court</b>, filed within 45 days of the result, with an appeal to the Supreme Court. Disputes over the President's and Vice-President's elections go only to the Supreme Court (Article 71). The Chief Election Commissioner conducts elections but does <b>not</b> adjudicate disputes.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p6-2", `
<p>Each House has presiding officers who run its business impartially. In the Lok Sabha these are the Speaker and Deputy Speaker. In the Rajya Sabha they are the Chairman (the Vice-President) and the Deputy Chairman. The Leader of the Opposition leads the largest party not in government.</p>

<h3>The Speaker</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Speaker is elected by the Lok Sabha <b>from among its members</b> (Article 93), so must already be a member. The President fixes the date of the election.
  <ul><li>A <b>pro tem Speaker</b>, usually the senior-most member and appointed by the President, swears in a new House.</li></ul></li>
<li>The Speaker does <b>not</b> hold office at the President's pleasure. The office ends on ceasing to be a member, on resignation, or on removal.</li>
<li><b>Resignation:</b> the Speaker writes to the <b>Deputy Speaker</b>, and the Deputy Speaker to the Speaker (Article 94(b)).</li>
<li><b>On dissolution</b>, the Speaker stays in office until immediately before the first meeting of the new House (Article 94 proviso).</li>
<li>Nothing in the Constitution requires the Speaker to <b>resign from their party</b>. In Britain the Speaker traditionally leaves the party; in India it is only a suggested convention.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Removal of the Speaker</b><ul>
<li>A resolution of the Lok Sabha passed by <b>a majority of all its then members</b> (an effective majority), after <b>at least 14 days' notice</b> (Article 94(c)).</li>
<li>While it is under consideration, the Speaker <b>shall not preside</b>, but <b>can speak</b> and take part in the proceedings, and <b>can vote in the first instance</b>. The Speaker cannot cast a deciding vote in a tie (Article 96).</li>
<li>The same rules apply to the Deputy Speaker.</li>
<li>No Speaker has ever been removed. Motions against Mavalankar (1951), Hukam Singh (1966) and Balram Jakhar (1987) all failed.</li></ul></div>
<h4>Powers of the Speaker</h4>
<ul>
<li>Final interpreter of the Constitution and the Rules inside the House.</li>
<li>Certifies whether a bill is a <b>Money Bill</b>, and that decision is final (Article 110(3)).</li>
<li>Presides over <b>joint sittings</b> (Article 108(4)).</li>
<li>Decides disqualification for <b>defection</b> (see 6.1).</li>
<li>Has a <b>casting vote</b> in a tie only (Article 100).</li>
<li>Adjourns the House, including for lack of quorum (one-tenth of members).</li>
<li>Chairs the Business Advisory, Rules and General Purposes Committees, appoints the chairs of all committees, and heads the Indian Parliamentary Group.</li>
<li>Ranks seventh in the order of precedence, with the Chief Justice.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Speakers of the Lok Sabha</h4>
<ul>
<li>In order: <b>G.V. Mavalankar</b> (the first, 1952–56; earlier President of the Central Legislative Assembly), M.A. Ayyangar, Hukam Singh, N. Sanjiva Reddy, <b>G.S. Dhillon</b>, <b>Bali Ram Bhagat</b>, K.S. Hegde, Balram Jakhar, Rabi Ray, Shivraj Patil, P.A. Sangma, G.M.C. Balayogi (the first Dalit Speaker), Manohar Joshi, Somnath Chatterjee, <b>Meira Kumar</b> (the first woman, 2009), Sumitra Mahajan, and <b>Om Birla</b> (2019, re-elected 2024).</li>
<li><b>K.V.K. Sundaram</b> was <b>never</b> Speaker. He was the second Chief Election Commissioner (1958–67); the first was Sukumar Sen.</li>
<li>K.M. Munshi and U.N. Dhebar were never Speaker either.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Deputy Speaker</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under the Lok Sabha Rules (Rule 8), the Deputy Speaker's election is held on a date <b>fixed by the Speaker</b>. The Speaker's election date is fixed by the President.</li>
<li>When presiding, the Deputy Speaker has <b>all the powers of the Speaker</b>, and <b>no appeal</b> lies against their rulings.</li>
<li>There is <b>no mandatory rule</b> that the Deputy Speaker must come from the main opposition party or the ruling party; giving the post to the opposition is only a convention. Nor does any practice require the Speaker to move the motion and the PM to second it; any member may move it.</li>
<li>The Deputy Speaker is not subordinate to the Speaker; both are responsible to the House. Appointed to a committee, the Deputy Speaker automatically becomes its chair.</li>
<li>The first Deputy Speaker was M.A. Ayyangar. A Panel of Chairpersons (up to 10 members) presides when both are absent.</li>
<li>The post has been <b>vacant since June 2019</b>, through the whole of the 17th Lok Sabha and into the 18th. This has been criticised because Article 93 says the House "shall" choose one.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Rajya Sabha presiding officers</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Chairman</b> is the Vice-President, ex officio, and is <b>not a member</b> of the Rajya Sabha.</li>
<li>The <b>Deputy Chairman</b> is elected by the Rajya Sabha <b>from among its members</b> (Article 89). So "neither the Chairman nor the Deputy Chairman is a member" is wrong.</li>
<li>The Chairman is removed under Article 67(b) (see 5.2). While a removal resolution is under consideration, the Chairman cannot preside and <b>cannot vote at all</b> (Article 92).</li>
<li>The Vice-President is <b>C.P. Radhakrishnan</b> (since September 2025). The Deputy Chairman, <b>Harivansh</b>, was re-elected unopposed for a third term in April 2026, after being re-nominated to the House.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Leader of the Opposition</h3>
<ul>
<li>In the <b>first Lok Sabha</b> (1952), the largest opposition party was the <b>Communist Party of India</b> (16 seats, led by A.K. Gopalan). The <b>Swatantra Party</b> was founded only in 1959, by C. Rajagopalachari.</li>
<li>The <b>first recognised Leader of the Opposition</b> was <b>Ram Subhag Singh</b> of the Congress (O), in <b>1969</b>, after the Congress split.</li>
<li>The convention, known as the Mavalankar rule, requires a party to have at least <b>10% of the House: 55 members</b>, not 75.</li>
<li>The Salary and Allowances of Leaders of Opposition in Parliament Act, 1977 gives the post statutory recognition and Cabinet rank.</li>
<li>There was <b>no Leader of the Opposition</b> in the 16th and 17th Lok Sabhas (2014–24). <b>Rahul Gandhi</b> took the post in the 18th (June 2024).</li>
<li>The Leader of the Opposition sits on selection panels for the CVC, CIC, Lokpal, CBI Director and NHRC. Where there is none, the leader of the largest opposition party takes the seat.</li>
<li>In Britain the Leader of the Opposition heads a formal shadow cabinet.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p6-3", `
<p>This topic covers how Parliament meets, how bills become law, and the devices members use to hold the government to account.</p>

<h3>Summoning, prorogation and dissolution</h3>
<ul>
<li>The President <b>summons</b> each House "to meet at such <b>time and place as he thinks fit</b>" (Article 85(1)), on the Cabinet's advice.</li>
<li>The gap between two sessions <b>cannot exceed six months</b>, so Parliament must meet at least twice a year.</li>
<li>The <b>three sessions</b> (Budget, Monsoon, Winter) are a <b>convention</b>; the Constitution does not mention them.</li>
<li>There is <b>no minimum number of sitting days</b>. The NCRWC (2002) suggested 120 days a year for the Lok Sabha and 100 for the Rajya Sabha.</li>
<li><b>Quorum</b> is one-tenth of the House's total membership (Article 100(3)).</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Adjournment</th><th>Adjournment sine die</th><th>Prorogation</th><th>Dissolution</th></tr>
<tr><td>By</td><td>Presiding officer</td><td>Presiding officer</td><td>President</td><td>President (Lok Sabha only)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Effect</td><td>Suspends a sitting for hours or days</td><td>Ends a sitting with no date to reconvene</td><td><b>Ends a session</b></td><td>Ends the life of the Lok Sabha</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pending bills</td><td>Unaffected</td><td>Unaffected</td><td><b>Unaffected</b>; other notices lapse</td><td>Some lapse (see below)</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The President prorogues a House <b>on the advice of the Council of Ministers</b>; there is no discretion here.</li>
<li>Prorogation usually follows an adjournment sine die, but nothing stops the President proroguing a House <b>while it is sitting</b>.</li>
<li>The President dissolves the Lok Sabha on the Council of Ministers' advice, except in <b>exceptional</b> cases, such as a PM who has lost majority seeking dissolution.</li>
<li>The Speaker can adjourn sine die; only the President can summon the House after prorogation.</li>
<li>The Lok Sabha also <b>dissolves automatically</b> at the end of five years even without a formal order (see 6.1).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Lapse of bills</h3>
<div class="key"><b>What lapses when the Lok Sabha is dissolved</b><table><tr><th>Lapses</th><th>Does not lapse</th></tr>
<tr><td>A bill <b>pending in the Lok Sabha</b>, whether it began there or came from the Rajya Sabha</td><td>A bill pending in the <b>Rajya Sabha</b> that the Lok Sabha has <b>not</b> passed. The Rajya Sabha is permanent.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A bill <b>passed by the Lok Sabha and pending in the Rajya Sabha</b></td><td>A bill on which the President <b>notified a joint sitting</b> before dissolution (Article 108(5))</td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td>A bill passed by both Houses and awaiting assent, or returned by the President for reconsideration</td></tr></table>
Bills do <b>not</b> lapse on <b>prorogation</b> (Article 107(3)).</div>
<p>The Women's Reservation Bill, passed by the Rajya Sabha in 2010 and then pending in the Lok Sabha, lapsed when the 15th Lok Sabha was dissolved in 2014.</p>

<h3>Kinds of bills</h3>
<table><tr><th>Government bill</th><th>Private member's bill</th></tr>
<tr><td>Introduced by a minister; reflects government policy</td><td>Introduced by <b>any MP who is not a minister</b>, elected or nominated</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can be introduced any day, with 7 days' notice</td><td>Only on Fridays (afternoon), with one month's notice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drafted by the ministry with the Law Ministry</td><td>Drafted by the member</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rejection may suggest loss of confidence</td><td>Rejection has no such effect</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>Fourteen private members' bills have become law, the <b>last in 1970</b> (the Supreme Court (Enlargement of Criminal Appellate Jurisdiction) Act).</li>
<li>In 2015 the Rajya Sabha passed Tiruchi Siva's Rights of Transgender Persons Bill, the first private member's bill passed by either House in 45 years, but it never became law. So "a private member's bill was recently passed by Parliament for the first time" is wrong.</li>
<li>In the Lok Sabha, the Committee on Private Members' Bills and Resolutions allots time.</li>
<li>Money Bills and Financial Bills are covered in 6.4, and Constitution amendment bills in 13.1.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Joint sitting (Article 108)</h3>
<ul>
<li>The President may summon a joint sitting when the Houses <b>disagree on an ordinary bill</b>: one House rejects it, disagrees on amendments, or sits on it for six months.</li>
<li>There is <b>no joint sitting for Money Bills</b> (the Rajya Sabha can only recommend) or <b>Constitution amendment bills</b> (each House must pass them separately by special majority).</li>
<li>A bill at a joint sitting is passed by a <b>simple majority of the members of both Houses present and voting</b>.</li>
<li>The <b>Speaker presides</b>; in the Speaker's absence the Deputy Speaker, then the Rajya Sabha's Deputy Chairman.</li>
<li>Joint sittings have been held only <b>three times</b>:
  <ul><li><b>1961:</b> the Dowry Prohibition Bill (the first);</li>
  <li><b>1978:</b> the Banking Service Commission (Repeal) Bill (the second);</li>
  <li><b>2002:</b> the Prevention of Terrorism Bill (POTA).</li></ul></li>
<li>The provision was borrowed from Australia.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Devices of parliamentary proceedings</h3>
<h4>Questions</h4>
<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Answer</th><th>Supplementary questions</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Starred</b> (marked with an asterisk)</td><td><b>Oral</b>, in Question Hour</td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Unstarred</b></td><td><b>Written</b>, laid on the table</td><td><b>Not allowed</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Short notice</td><td>Oral; under 10 days' notice, on an urgent matter, with the minister's consent</td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Question to a private member</td><td>Answered by that member, about their bill or resolution</td><td>–</td></tr></table>
<p>A 2026 statement swapped the definitions of starred and unstarred questions, so only "no supplementary on an unstarred question" was correct.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Question Hour</b> is the first hour of a sitting (11–12 in the Lok Sabha; the Rajya Sabha moved its own to 12–1). Up to 20 starred and 230 unstarred questions are listed each day.</li>
<li><b>Zero Hour</b> (from 12 noon) is an Indian innovation from 1962 and is not in the Rules.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Motions</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Adjournment motion:</b> sets aside the House's normal business <b>to discuss a definite matter of urgent public importance</b>.
  <ul><li>It needs 50 members' support, is <b>available only in the Lok Sabha</b>, and is debated for at least 2.5 hours.</li>
  <li>It carries an element of censure on the government.</li>
  <li>The matter must be specific, recent and not sub judice.</li>
  <li>It is not for gathering information, cutting a grant (that is a cut motion; see 6.4) or disciplining members.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Calling attention motion</b> (an Indian innovation, 1954), half-an-hour discussions, and short-duration discussions (Rule 193).</li>
<li><b>No-confidence motion</b> (Rule 198 of the Lok Sabha Rules):
  <ul><li>It is <b>not mentioned in the Constitution</b>. Article 75(3) only lays down collective responsibility.</li>
  <li>It can be moved <b>only in the Lok Sabha</b> and needs 50 members' support. The Rajya Sabha cannot entertain one.</li>
  <li>It need <b>not set out grounds</b>, and there are no other conditions of admissibility. Once admitted, it must be taken up within 10 days.</li>
  <li>A <b>censure motion</b>, by contrast, must state its reasons and is aimed at particular policies or ministers.</li>
  <li>The first no-confidence motion was against Nehru (1963, moved by J.B. Kripalani). The latest (August 2023, on Manipur) was defeated.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
<h4>Terms</h4>
<table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Yielding the floor</b></td><td>A member stops speaking so that another (often a minister) may speak, at the Speaker's request</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crossing the floor</td><td>Changing sides or parties (see anti-defection, 6.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interpellation</td><td>A formal question challenging a minister, as in the French system</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decorum</td><td>Proper conduct in the House</td></tr>
<tr><td>Point of order</td><td>Raises a breach of the rules</td></tr>
<tr><td>Guillotine</td><td>Putting all remaining demands for grants to the vote together at the end of the time allotted</td></tr></table>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p6b.js
// Polity book, Unit 6 (part 2): Parliament: finance; Rajya Sabha, committees and control.
addBookTopic("polity", "p6-4", `
<p>Control of the purse is Parliament's oldest power. <b>No tax without law</b> (Article 265) and <b>no spending without appropriation</b> (Article 114) are the two pillars. The Lok Sabha dominates money matters, because the government is responsible to it.</p>

<h3>The three funds</h3>
<table><tr><th>Fund</th><th>Article</th><th>What goes in</th><th>Withdrawal</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Consolidated Fund of India</b></td><td>266(1)</td><td><b>All revenues</b> (taxes and other receipts), loans raised, and repayments of loans</td><td><b>Only under an appropriation law passed by Parliament</b> (Articles 114, 266(3))</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Public Account</b></td><td>266(2)</td><td>Money the government holds as banker or trustee: provident funds, small savings, deposits</td><td>By <b>executive action</b>; no vote of Parliament needed</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Contingency Fund</b></td><td>267</td><td>A corpus of ₹30,000 crore (since 2021), at the President's disposal and operated by the Finance Secretary</td><td>Advances for unforeseen expenditure, recouped later by Parliament through appropriation</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The Constitution provides a Consolidated Fund and a Public Account for <b>each State</b> too, and lets each State legislature create a Contingency Fund (Article 267(2)).</li>
<li>Authority to withdraw from the Consolidated Fund must come from <b>Parliament</b>, not the President, the PM or the Finance Minister.</li>
<li><b>Charged expenditure</b> (Article 112(3)) is <b>discussed but not voted</b>. It includes:
  <ul><li>the President's emoluments;</li>
  <li>the salaries of the Speaker, the Chairman and Deputy Chairman, Supreme Court judges, the CAG and the UPSC;</li>
  <li>debt charges;</li>
  <li>court decrees.</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>The Budget</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Constitution never uses the word "budget". It speaks of the "<b>Annual Financial Statement</b>" (Article 112), which <b>the President causes to be laid</b> before both Houses. In practice the Finance Minister presents it.
  <ul><li>UPSC's 2024 key accepted "the Finance Minister lays it on behalf of the PM", though the text says the President.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>No demand for a grant can be made except on the President's recommendation</b> (Article 113(3)).</li>
<li>The Budget is <b>prepared</b> by the <b>Budget Division of the Department of Economic Affairs</b>, Ministry of Finance, not the Department of Revenue (which handles taxes through the CBDT and CBIC) or the Departments of Expenditure or Financial Services.</li>
<li>The Finance Ministry has six departments: Economic Affairs, Expenditure, Revenue, DIPAM, Financial Services and Public Enterprises.</li>
<li><b>Since 2017</b> the Budget has been presented on <b>1 February</b>. The <b>Railway Budget was merged</b> into it that year, on the Bibek Debroy committee's advice; it had been separate since 1924, after the Acworth Committee. The plan/non-plan distinction also ended.</li>
<li>Printing begins with the "halwa ceremony", and the Budget has been paperless since 2021.</li>
<li>Railway appropriations always went through the same parliamentary process as other spending.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Stages</h4>
<ol>
<li>Presentation.</li>
<li>General discussion, in both Houses.</li>
<li>Scrutiny by the Departmentally Related Standing Committees.</li>
<li><b>Voting on demands for grants</b>, in the <b>Lok Sabha only</b>. Remaining demands are "guillotined" on the last day.</li>
<li><b>Appropriation Bill</b> (Article 114).</li>
<li><b>Finance Bill</b>, carrying the tax proposals. It is usually a Money Bill and must pass within 75 days under the Provisional Collection of Taxes Act, 1931.</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li><b>Cut motions</b> are moved only in the Lok Sabha:
  <ul><li><b>policy cut</b>: reduce the demand to ₹1, signalling disapproval of a policy;</li>
  <li><b>economy cut</b>: reduce it by a specific amount;</li>
  <li><b>token cut</b>: reduce it by ₹100, to air a grievance.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Other grants:</b> supplementary and additional (Article 115), excess (which needs the PAC's approval), and exceptional and token grants (Article 116).</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Vote on account and interim budget</b><ul>
<li>A <b>vote on account</b> (Article 116) lets the government withdraw money for <b>expenditure only</b>, for part of the year, until the full Budget passes.</li>
<li>An <b>interim budget</b> is a full statement of <b>both expenditure and receipts</b> for the interim period. It is usual in an <b>election year</b> (February 2019, February 2024, followed by full Budgets in July).</li>
<li>Either can be used by <b>any</b> government; neither is reserved for caretaker governments.</li></ul></div>
<p><b>If the Lok Sabha does not pass the Budget</b>, the government has lost confidence, so the <b>PM submits the resignation of the Council of Ministers</b> (collective responsibility, Article 75(3)). The Budget is not simply modified, sent to the Rajya Sabha, or met by the Finance Minister's resignation alone.</p>

<h3>Money Bills (Article 110)</h3>
<p>A bill is a Money Bill if it contains <b>only</b> provisions on the following matters (Article 110(1)):</p>
<ul>
<li>(a) imposing, abolishing, remitting, altering or regulating any <b>tax</b>;</li>
<li>(b) <b>borrowing</b>, or giving guarantees, by the Government of India;</li>
<li>(c) <b>custody of the Consolidated or Contingency Fund</b>, and payments into or withdrawals from them;</li>
<li>(d) <b>appropriation from the Consolidated Fund</b>;</li>
<li>(e) declaring expenditure charged on the Consolidated Fund;</li>
<li>(f) receipts to the Consolidated Fund or Public Account, and their audit;</li>
<li>(g) matters incidental to these.</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b>"Appropriation of moneys out of the <b>Contingency Fund</b>" is <b>not</b> a Money Bill matter. The Contingency Fund is at the President's disposal, and advances from it are recouped by appropriation from the Consolidated Fund.</div>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Money Bill (Article 110)</th><th>Financial Bill I (Article 117(1))</th><th>Financial Bill II (Article 117(3))</th></tr>
<tr><td>Content</td><td>Only Article 110 matters</td><td>Article 110 matters plus others</td><td>Spending from the Consolidated Fund, but no Article 110 matters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Introduced</td><td><b>Lok Sabha only</b>, on the President's recommendation</td><td>Lok Sabha only, on the President's recommendation</td><td>Either House; the President's recommendation is needed at the consideration stage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rajya Sabha</td><td><b>Only recommends, within 14 days</b>; cannot reject or amend</td><td>Full powers to amend or reject</td><td>Full powers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Joint sitting</td><td>Not possible</td><td>Possible (not "necessary")</td><td>Possible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Returned by the President?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 109</b> lays down the special procedure for Money Bills. If the Rajya Sabha does not return the bill within 14 days, it is <b>deemed passed</b> by both Houses.</li>
<li>If the Rajya Sabha "amends" a Money Bill, the changes are only <b>recommendations</b>. The <b>Lok Sabha may proceed with the bill, accepting or rejecting them</b>; it is not obliged to accept.</li>
<li>Even the <b>Appropriation Bill</b> does not strictly need active passage by both Houses, because the Rajya Sabha cannot reject it. UPSC's 2004 key treated "it must be passed by both Houses" as the false statement.</li>
<li>The <b>Speaker's certificate</b> that a bill is a Money Bill is final (Article 110(3)).
  <ul><li>Rojer Mathew (2019) said the certificate can be judicially reviewed.</li>
  <li>The Aadhaar Act's passage as a Money Bill (upheld 4:1 in Puttaswamy, 2018) is before a seven-judge bench.</li>
  <li>Unrelated changes passed through Finance Acts, such as tribunals and electoral bonds in 2017, drew criticism.</li></ul></li>
<li>Every Money Bill is a Financial Bill, but not every Financial Bill is a Money Bill.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Parliamentary control over public finance</h3>
<p>Parliament controls public money through:</p>
<ul>
<li>the <b>Annual Financial Statement</b> laid before it;</li>
<li><b>no withdrawal without an Appropriation Act</b>;</li>
<li><b>supplementary grants and votes on account</b>;</li>
<li>the <b>Finance Bill</b> for taxes;</li>
<li>post-audit through the CAG and the PAC (see 6.5).</li>
</ul>
<p>India has <b>no Parliamentary Budget Office</b>; one has been proposed, on the model of the US Congressional Budget Office. The mid-year review under the FRBM Act is laid by the government.</p>
<h4>FRBM documents</h4>
<p>The <b>Macro-Economic Framework Statement</b> is laid with the Budget because the <b>FRBM Act, 2003</b> requires it, not because of convention or Articles 110, 112 or 113. The Act's other documents are the Medium-Term Fiscal Policy cum Fiscal Policy Strategy Statement, and the Medium-Term Expenditure Framework Statement, laid after the Budget session.</p>
<ul>
<li>The N.K. Singh Committee (2017) set targets of a fiscal deficit of 3% of GDP and debt of 60% of GDP (40% Centre, 20% States).</li>
<li>From 2026–27 the Centre targets a falling debt-to-GDP ratio, about 50% (±1) by 2031.</li>
<li>Other Budget documents: Budget at a Glance, the Receipt and Expenditure Budgets, the Outcome Budget, the Gender Budget statement, and the Memorandum explaining the Finance Bill.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p6-5", `
<p>Parliament does much of its detailed work through committees, and it controls the executive through debate, questions and its financial committees. The Rajya Sabha, as the Council of States, has a few powers the Lok Sabha lacks.</p>

<h3>Powers of the two Houses compared</h3>
<table><tr><th>Equal powers</th><th>Lok Sabha stronger or exclusive</th><th>Rajya Sabha exclusive</th></tr>
<tr><td>Ordinary bills (with a joint sitting if deadlocked); <b>constitutional amendments</b> (each House by special majority, with no joint sitting, so the Rajya Sabha can block them); electing and impeaching the President; removing judges; approving emergencies and ordinances</td><td>Money Bills (the Rajya Sabha only recommends, within 14 days); voting demands for grants; cut motions; the <b>no-confidence motion</b>; disapproving a National Emergency (44th Amendment)</td><td><b>Article 249:</b> a resolution letting Parliament legislate on a <b>State List</b> subject in the national interest. <b>Article 312:</b> creating <b>new All India Services</b>. Approving a proclamation while the Lok Sabha is dissolved. Initiating the Vice-President's removal.</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The Rajya Sabha has <b>equal power</b> with the Lok Sabha in <b>amending the Constitution</b>, but not in creating All India Services (where it alone acts), removing the government, or moving cut motions.</li>
<li>The resolutions under Articles 249 and 312 need <b>two-thirds of members present and voting</b>. An Article 249 resolution lasts one year and can be renewed. Article 312 was used to create the Indian Forest Service (1966).</li>
<li><b>Emergency approval</b> needs <b>both Houses</b> (Article 352(4)), so "only the Lok Sabha approves an emergency" is wrong. Only the Lok Sabha can pass a resolution <b>disapproving</b> a National Emergency, by simple majority.</li>
<li><b>Impeachment</b> of the President needs both Houses (see 5.1).</li>
<li>Changing a State's territory or name (Article 3), amending the President's election procedure, and the EC's functions are <b>not</b> Rajya Sabha powers.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Parliamentary control over the executive</h3>
<p>Parliament exercises control <b>mainly through its committees</b>, together with:</p>
<ul>
<li>the <b>adjournment motion</b>;</li>
<li><b>Question Hour</b>;</li>
<li><b>supplementary questions</b>;</li>
<li>Zero Hour, calling attention, no-confidence and censure motions, and cut motions (see 6.3 and 6.4).</li>
</ul>
<p>Consultative committees are informal; administrators' periodic reports are not the main mechanism; and Parliament does not issue writs, which courts do.</p>

<h3>The financial committees</h3>
<table><tr><th>Committee</th><th>Members</th><th>Chair</th><th>Role</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Public Accounts Committee</b> (1921)</td><td><b>22</b>: 15 from the Lok Sabha and 7 from the Rajya Sabha, elected each year by proportional representation. <b>No outsiders.</b></td><td><b>Appointed by the Speaker</b>; from the <b>opposition</b> by convention since 1967 (K.C. Venugopal for 2024–26)</td><td>Scrutinises the <b>appropriation and finance accounts</b> and examines the <b>CAG's reports</b>; a "post-mortem" that cannot question policy</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Estimates Committee</b></td><td><b>30</b>, <b>all from the Lok Sabha</b></td><td>Appointed by the Speaker</td><td>The "<b>continuous economy committee</b>"; suggests economies; the <b>largest</b> committee</td></tr>
<tr><td>Committee on Public Undertakings</td><td>22 (15 + 7)</td><td>Appointed by the Speaker</td><td>Examines public sector undertakings and CAG reports on them</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The PAC does <b>not</b> have "up to 25 Lok Sabha members". It examines all CAG reports except those on PSUs, which go to the Committee on Public Undertakings. The CAG is its "friend, philosopher and guide".</li>
<li><b>Ministers cannot be members</b> of the financial committees.</li>
<li>Rajya Sabha members sit on the PAC and the Committee on Public Undertakings, but <b>not on the Estimates Committee</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Other committees</h3>
<table><tr><th>Committee</th><th>Facts</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Committee on Subordinate Legislation</b> (Lok Sabha from 1953; 15 members in each House)</td><td>Checks whether <b>rules, regulations and bye-laws made by the executive under delegated power</b> stay within the scope of the delegation. Often chaired by an opposition member.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Committee on Government Assurances</td><td>Checks whether ministers' promises made in the House are kept</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rules Committee; Business Advisory Committee</td><td>The House's procedure; allotting time for business (both chaired by the Speaker)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Departmentally Related Standing Committees</b> (since 1993)</td><td>24 committees of 31 members each (21 from the Lok Sabha, 10 from the Rajya Sabha); 16 under the Lok Sabha and 8 under the Rajya Sabha. They examine demands for grants, bills and annual reports, and <b>review independent regulators</b> (TRAI, IRDAI, CERC, SEBI).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ad hoc committees</td><td>Select and Joint Committees on bills (for example on the Waqf Bill 2024 and the One Nation One Election Bills); inquiry committees. These can also review regulators.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Welfare of SCs and STs</b> (1968)</td><td><b>30 members: 20 from the Lok Sabha and 10 from the Rajya Sabha</b>. The chair is <b>appointed by the Lok Sabha Speaker</b>. <b>No minister</b> can be a member. Term of <b>one year</b>. Examines NCSC and NCST reports.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Ethics Committee</b></td><td>Rajya Sabha (1997, under K.R. Narayanan; Code of Conduct 2005); Lok Sabha (set up ad hoc in 2000 by Speaker Balayogi, made permanent in 2015), with 15 members</td></tr>
<tr><td>Committees on Petitions, Privileges, and Papers Laid</td><td>–</td></tr></table>
<p>All four 2026 statements on the SC/ST welfare committee were wrong: the Speaker appoints the chair, the split is 20 from the Lok Sabha and 10 from the Rajya Sabha, no minister can be a member, and the term is one year.</p>
<div class="key"><b>The Lok Sabha Ethics Committee</b><ul>
<li>It <b>began as an ad hoc committee</b>.</li>
<li><b>Any person</b> can complain about a member's unethical conduct, through a Lok Sabha member, with evidence and an affidavit. An MP can complain directly, and the committee can act suo motu. So "only an MP can complain" is wrong.</li>
<li>It <b>does not take up sub judice matters</b>, or matters based only on media reports.</li>
<li>Mahua Moitra was expelled in December 2023 on its report ("cash for query"). In 2005, 11 MPs were expelled in an earlier cash-for-query case, which was upheld in Raja Ram Pal (2007).</li></ul></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Ministry of Parliamentary Affairs:</b>
  <ul><li>It works under the direction of the <b>Cabinet Committee on Parliamentary Affairs</b>.</li>
  <li>It nominates MPs to government committees, councils, boards and commissions.</li>
  <li>It constitutes the <b>Consultative Committees</b> of MPs attached to ministries, including those for <b>Railway Zones</b>; the Railway Ministry only services them.</li>
  <li>The minister since June 2024 is Kiren Rijiju.</li></ul></li>
<li>Consultative committees are informal and chaired by the minister. The DRSCs are formal parliamentary committees.</li>
</ul>

<h3>MPLADS</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Members of Parliament Local Area Development Scheme was launched in <b>December 1993</b>. It is a Central Sector Scheme run by the <b>Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation</b>.</li>
<li>Each MP is entitled to <b>₹5 crore a year</b> (since 2011–12). The scheme was suspended for 2020–22 during COVID and restored from November 2021.</li>
<li>Funds must create <b>durable community assets</b> (drinking water, education, health, roads, sanitation).</li>
<li>At least <b>15% must go to SC areas and 7.5% to ST areas</b>.</li>
<li>Funds are <b>non-lapsable</b>: unspent money carries forward, so "unused funds cannot be carried forward" is wrong.</li>
<li>The District Authority must <b>inspect at least 10%</b> of works every year.</li>
<li>Where funds can be used:
  <ul><li>Lok Sabha MPs: in their constituency.</li>
  <li>Rajya Sabha MPs: anywhere in their State.</li>
  <li>Nominated MPs: anywhere in India.</li></ul></li>
<li>The 2023 guidelines introduced the e-SAKSHI portal.</li>
<li>The Supreme Court upheld the scheme in Bhim Singh (2010). The Second ARC had recommended abolishing it.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p7.js
// Polity book, Unit 7: The Judiciary.
addBookTopic("polity", "p7-1", `
<p>India has a single integrated judiciary with the <b>Supreme Court</b> at the top, High Courts in the States, and subordinate courts below them. The Supreme Court is the final interpreter of the Constitution and the guardian of Fundamental Rights.</p>

<h3>Composition</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 124(1):</b> the Supreme Court consists of a Chief Justice and as many other judges as <b>Parliament</b> prescribes by law. So the power to increase the number of judges lies with <b>Parliament</b> (the Supreme Court (Number of Judges) Act, 1956), not the President, the CJI or the Law Commission.</li>
<li>High Court strength is fixed by the President (Article 216).</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Year</th><th>Strength (including the CJI)</th></tr>
<tr><td>1950</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>1956</td><td>11</td></tr>
<tr><td>1986</td><td>26</td></tr>
<tr><td>2008</td><td>31</td></tr>
<tr><td>2019</td><td>34</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li><b>Qualifications (Article 124(3)):</b> five years as a High Court judge, or ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President's opinion.</li>
<li>Judges <b>retire at 65</b>.</li>
<li><b>Appointment:</b> by the President after consulting the CJI. In practice this means the <b>collegium</b> system, from the Second (1993) and Third (1998) Judges cases.
  <ul><li>The <b>98th Amendment Bill (2003)</b> proposed a <b>National Judicial Commission</b>, but it lapsed in 2004. (Service tax came through the 88th Amendment, introduced as the 95th Bill.)</li>
  <li>The <b>99th Amendment (2014)</b> created the National Judicial Appointments Commission, which was <b>struck down</b> in the Fourth Judges case (2015).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Chief Justice:</b> Justice <b>Surya Kant</b>, the 53rd CJI, since 24 November 2025.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Ad hoc and retired judges</h4>
<table><tr><th>Article</th><th>Provision</th></tr>
<tr><td>127</td><td><b>Ad hoc</b> judges: a High Court judge appointed by the CJI, with the President's consent, when the Supreme Court lacks a quorum</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>128</b></td><td>The CJI may, <b>with the President's previous consent</b>, ask a <b>retired Supreme Court judge</b> to sit and act as a Supreme Court judge. The retired judge must also consent.</td></tr>
<tr><td>224A</td><td>Retired High Court judges may sit in High Courts. The Supreme Court encouraged this in 2021 and 2025 to clear backlogs.</td></tr>
<tr><td>129 / 215</td><td>The Supreme Court and High Courts are <b>courts of record</b>, with power to punish contempt</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>137</b></td><td>The Supreme Court may <b>review its own judgments</b>. A curative petition is also available (Rupa Hurra, 2002).</td></tr></table>
<p>There is no matching Article giving High Courts review power. UPSC's key therefore treated "a High Court can review its own judgment as the Supreme Court does" as wrong, though High Courts do have inherent review power as courts of record.</p>

<h3>Independence of the judiciary</h3>
<ul>
<li>The President must <b>consult the CJI</b> when appointing judges.</li>
<li><b>Salaries</b> are <b>charged on the Consolidated Fund of India</b>, so they are not voted, and cannot be reduced during a judge's term except in a financial emergency.</li>
<li>Security of tenure: removal only by the special procedure below.</li>
<li>No discussion of a judge's conduct in Parliament except during a removal motion (Article 121).</li>
<li>The power to punish contempt (Article 129).</li>
<li>Retired Supreme Court judges cannot plead in any court in India (Article 124(7)).</li>
<li>The CJI appoints the Court's own officers and staff (Article 146). The government does not.</li>
<li>The Court's administrative expenses are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India. A High Court's expenses are charged on the State's fund.</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b>The CJI <b>cannot remove</b> judges, and the government does not appoint the Supreme Court's staff.</div>

<h3>Removal of judges</h3>
<p>Under Article 124(4), a judge is removed by the President on an address by each House, on grounds of "<b>proved misbehaviour or incapacity</b>". The Constitution <b>does not define</b> these terms.</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Notice:</b> signed by 100 Lok Sabha or 50 Rajya Sabha members.</li>
<li><b>Admission:</b> the Speaker or Chairman <b>may admit or refuse</b> the motion. In 2018 Venkaiah Naidu rejected the motion against CJI Dipak Misra.</li>
<li><b>Inquiry:</b> if admitted, a three-member committee of a Supreme Court judge, a High Court Chief Justice and a distinguished jurist investigates.</li>
<li><b>Vote:</b> if the judge is found guilty, each House must pass the motion by a <b>special majority</b> (a majority of total membership and two-thirds of those present and voting) in the same session.</li>
<li>The President then orders removal.</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>The details are in the <b>Judges (Inquiry) Act, 1968</b>.</li>
<li><b>No judge has ever been removed.</b>
  <ul><li>The motion against V. Ramaswami (1993) failed in the Lok Sabha.</li>
  <li>Soumitra Sen of the Calcutta High Court resigned in 2011 after the Rajya Sabha passed the motion.</li>
  <li>A motion against Justice <b>Yashwant Varma</b> (2025, after cash was found at his residence) was admitted and an inquiry committee formed.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Reform attempts:</b> the Judges (Inquiry) Bill, 2006 proposed a <b>National Judicial Council</b> to receive complaints against Supreme Court and High Court judges, including Chief Justices. It lapsed, as did the Judicial Standards and Accountability Bill (2010).</li>
<li>The in-house procedure (1999) still handles complaints internally.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Jurisdiction</h3>
<table><tr><th>Jurisdiction</th><th>Article</th><th>Scope</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Original (exclusive)</b></td><td><b>131</b></td><td>Disputes between the <b>Centre and one or more States</b>; the Centre and States against other States; or <b>two or more States</b>, involving a legal right. It excludes pre-Constitution treaties and inter-State water disputes (Article 262), and is not open to private parties.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Writ</td><td>32</td><td>Enforcing Fundamental Rights; original but not exclusive (High Courts have Article 226)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appellate</td><td>132–136</td><td>Constitutional, civil and criminal appeals; Special Leave Petitions (136)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Advisory</b></td><td><b>143</b></td><td>See below</td></tr>
<tr><td>Review</td><td>137</td><td>Of its own judgments</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li><b>Not original jurisdiction:</b> election disputes about Parliament or State legislatures (these are election petitions in High Courts) and disputes between the Union and a UT (a UT is administered by the Union itself).</li>
<li>Recent Article 131 suits: Kerala on borrowing limits, and West Bengal on CBI consent (2024).</li>
<li>Parliament <b>can enlarge</b> the Court's jurisdiction:
  <ul><li>Article 138(1): on <b>Union List</b> matters;</li>
  <li>Article 138(2): on other matters if the Centre and a State agree;</li>
  <li>Article 139: writ powers for purposes beyond Article 32.</li></ul>
  So "Parliament cannot enlarge the jurisdiction" is wrong.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Advisory jurisdiction (Article 143)</h4>
<ul>
<li>The Court advises the President on a question of law or fact <b>only if the President seeks it</b>. It does not act on its own initiative, and references are not limited to Fundamental Rights or to threats to unity.</li>
<li>The advice is <b>not binding</b>, and the Court may decline to answer under Article 143(1); it returned the Ayodhya reference in 1993. Under Article 143(2), on pre-Constitution treaty disputes, it must answer.</li>
<li><b>References:</b>
  <ul><li>Kerala Education Bill (1958), Berubari (1960), Cauvery (1991), collegium (1998);</li>
  <li><b>Gujarat polls (2002):</b> the Court held that Article 174's six-month rule does not apply to a dissolved Assembly, and that the EC decides poll timing;</li>
  <li><b>2025:</b> on timelines for Governors and the President to act on State bills. In November 2025 the Court said courts cannot fix such timelines or deem assent, though prolonged unexplained inaction can be examined.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
<h4>Article 142: complete justice</h4>
<p>Article 142 lets the Court pass any order necessary for "complete justice". The statement that ordinary laws cannot limit Article 142 means the <b>Supreme Court is not constrained by laws made by Parliament</b> in exercising it. It still cannot override express statutory provisions on substantive rights (Supreme Court Bar Association, 1998).</p>
<ul>
<li>Uses:
  <ul><li>the Bhopal gas settlement (1989);</li>
  <li>the ban on liquor shops near highways (2016);</li>
  <li>the Ayodhya verdict (2019);</li>
  <li>divorce on irretrievable breakdown of marriage (Shilpa Sailesh, 2023);</li>
  <li>Perarivalan's release (2022);</li>
  <li>deeming Tamil Nadu bills assented (April 2025).</li></ul></li>
<li>Critics call it "judicial overreach".</li>
<li>Related Articles: 141 (the Supreme Court's law binds all courts) and 144 (all authorities must act in aid of the Court).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p7-2", `
<p>Judicial review is the courts' power to test laws and executive action against the Constitution. Together with the basic structure doctrine and the writs, it makes the Supreme Court the <b>custodian of the Constitution</b>.</p>

<h3>Judicial review</h3>
<ul>
<li>Judicial review means the power of the judiciary to <b>pronounce on the constitutionality of laws and executive orders</b>, and to declare them void if they are unconstitutional. It does <b>not</b> mean questioning the wisdom of laws, pre-screening bills before assent, or reviewing the courts' own past judgments (which is Article 137).</li>
<li>The doctrine began in <b>Marbury v. Madison</b> (USA, 1803).</li>
<li>The term "judicial review" does <b>not appear</b> in the Constitution. The power is inferred from Articles 13, 32, 131–136, 141–143, 226, 227, 245 and 246.</li>
<li>Courts review three things: legislation, executive action, and constitutional amendments (against the basic structure).</li>
<li>Judicial review by the Supreme Court and High Courts is itself <b>part of the basic structure</b> (Minerva Mills 1980; L. Chandra Kumar 1997).</li>
<li>Laws placed in the Ninth Schedule after 24 April 1973 can be reviewed (I.R. Coelho, 2007).</li>
<li>The <b>custodian</b> of the Constitution is the <b>Supreme Court</b>, not the President, the PM or the Lok Sabha Secretariat.</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Common traps</b><ul>
<li><b>High Courts can declare central laws unconstitutional</b> under Article 226. The 42nd Amendment's Article 226A, which barred this, was repealed by the 43rd Amendment (1977).</li>
<li><b>Constitutional amendments can be questioned</b> if they violate the basic structure. The 99th Amendment (NJAC) was struck down in 2015.</li>
<li>The Constitution <b>does not define the "basic structure"</b>. The Court built it case by case, so a statement that the Constitution defines it in terms of federalism, secularism and so on is wrong.</li></ul></div>

<h3>Basic structure: how it developed</h3>
<table><tr><th>Case or amendment</th><th>Holding</th></tr>
<tr><td>Shankari Prasad (1951), Sajjan Singh (1965)</td><td>Parliament can amend Fundamental Rights</td></tr>
<tr><td>Golaknath (1967)</td><td>It cannot</td></tr>
<tr><td>24th Amendment (1971)</td><td>Restored the power to amend Fundamental Rights</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kesavananda Bharati (1973)</b></td><td>Parliament can amend any part, but <b>not the basic structure</b>: supremacy of the Constitution, republican and democratic government, secularism, separation of powers, federalism</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indira Nehru Gandhi (1975)</td><td>Free and fair elections, rule of law and judicial review are part of it</td></tr>
<tr><td>42nd Amendment (1976)</td><td>Article 368(4)–(5) tried to make amendments immune from courts</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Minerva Mills (1980)</b></td><td>Struck down those clauses; limited amending power and the balance between FRs and DPSPs are basic features</td></tr>
<tr><td>S.R. Bommai (1994)</td><td>Federalism and secularism</td></tr>
<tr><td>L. Chandra Kumar (1997)</td><td>Judicial review by High Courts and the Supreme Court</td></tr>
<tr><td>NJAC case (2015)</td><td>Independence of the judiciary</td></tr></table>

<h3>Writs (Articles 32 and 226)</h3>
<table><tr><th>Writ</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Lies against</th><th>Does not lie against</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Habeas corpus</b></td><td>"To have the body": produce a detained person and show lawful cause</td><td>Public authorities <b>and private persons</b></td><td>Lawful detention, detention for contempt, detention outside the court's jurisdiction</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mandamus</b></td><td>"We command": perform a public duty</td><td>Public officials and bodies, government, lower courts and tribunals</td><td><b>Private bodies, unless entrusted with a public duty</b>; <b>companies, even government companies</b> (the textbook rule from Praga Tools, 1969, which UPSC accepted); discretionary duties; the President and Governors; non-statutory contracts</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Prohibition</b></td><td>"To forbid": a higher court <b>stops a lower court or tribunal from continuing proceedings</b> beyond its jurisdiction. Preventive.</td><td>Judicial and quasi-judicial bodies only</td><td>Administrative bodies, legislatures, private bodies</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Certiorari</b></td><td>"To be certified": quashes an order already passed. Curative.</td><td>Judicial and quasi-judicial bodies; administrative bodies affecting rights (since 1991)</td><td>Legislatures, private bodies</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Quo warranto</b></td><td>"By what authority": tests the legality of a claim to public office</td><td>Substantive public offices created by statute or the Constitution. <b>Any public-minded person can apply</b>; they need not be personally aggrieved.</td><td>Ministerial or private offices</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>Prohibition is <b>not</b> an order to a government officer, to a legislature to pass a prohibition law, or to stop an unconstitutional policy.</li>
<li><b>Article 32</b> covers only Fundamental Rights; Ambedkar called it the "heart and soul" of the Constitution (see 4.3).</li>
<li><b>Article 226</b> is wider: Fundamental Rights "and for any other purpose".</li>
<li>Parliament may empower other courts to issue writs (Article 32(3)), but has not done so.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Contempt of court</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Contempt of Courts Act, 1971</b> followed the <b>H.N. Sanyal Committee</b> (1961–63).</li>
<li>The <b>Constitution</b> empowers the Supreme Court (Article 129) and High Courts (Article 215) to punish contempt of themselves.</li>
<li><b>Parliament</b> can legislate on contempt: Union List Entry 77 for the Supreme Court and Concurrent List Entry 14 generally.</li>
<li>The Constitution does <b>not define</b> civil and criminal contempt; the 1971 Act does.
  <ul><li><b>Civil contempt:</b> wilful disobedience of a court's order.</li>
  <li><b>Criminal contempt:</b> scandalising the court, prejudicing proceedings, or obstructing justice.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Punishment:</b> up to six months' simple imprisonment and/or a ₹2,000 fine. An apology may end the matter.</li>
<li><b>Truth</b> has been a defence since 2006, if pleaded in the public interest and in good faith.</li>
<li>Contempt is a ground for restricting speech under Article 19(2).</li>
<li><b>Cases:</b> Prashant Bhushan (2020, fined ₹1) and Vijay Mallya (2017).</li>
<li>The Law Commission's 274th report (2018) advised keeping "scandalising the court" as an offence.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Public Interest Litigation</h3>
<ul>
<li>PIL relaxed <b>locus standi</b> (who may sue) and introduced <b>epistolary jurisdiction</b>, under which letters are treated as petitions.</li>
<li>Its pioneers were Justices <b>P.N. Bhagwati</b> (CJI 1985–86, the answer UPSC wanted) and <b>V.R. Krishna Iyer</b>.
  <ul><li>PIL actually began in the late 1970s and early 1980s, when Y.V. Chandrachud was CJI; Bhagwati drove it as a judge.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Krishna Iyer</b> was a Supreme Court judge (1973–80) but <b>never Chief Justice</b>.
  <ul><li>He had earlier been a minister in Kerala's first Communist government (1957).</li>
  <li>His judgments: Sunil Batra (1978, prisoners' rights) and Balchand (1977: "bail is the rule, jail the exception").</li>
  <li>He received the Padma Vibhushan (1999) and died in 2014, aged 100.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Landmark PILs:</b>
  <ul><li>Hussainara Khatoon (1979, undertrials and speedy trial);</li>
  <li>S.P. Gupta (1981, the First Judges case);</li>
  <li>Bandhua Mukti Morcha (1984);</li>
  <li>Olga Tellis (1985);</li>
  <li>the M.C. Mehta cases (the Ganga, Delhi vehicles, and absolute liability after the oleum gas leak);</li>
  <li>Vishaka (1997).</li></ul></li>
<li>Bhagwati was criticised for joining the majority in ADM Jabalpur (1976).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p7-3", `
<p>Each State is meant to have a High Court, which supervises the subordinate courts below it. High Courts have wide writ powers and are the main constitutional courts for most citizens.</p>

<h3>High Courts: number and jurisdiction</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 214</b> says there shall be a High Court for each State. <b>Article 231</b> lets Parliament set up a <b>common High Court</b> for two or more States, or a State and a UT. Article 230 extends a High Court's jurisdiction to UTs.</li>
<li>So "every State has a High Court in its territory" is false (Nagaland, Mizoram, Arunachal and Goa share one), while "the Constitution provides for a High Court in each State" is true.</li>
<li>India now has <b>25 High Courts</b>.
  <ul><li>There were 18 before November 2000, and 21 after the High Courts of Chhattisgarh, Uttarakhand and Jharkhand were created.</li>
  <li>Meghalaya, Manipur and Tripura got theirs in 2013.</li>
  <li>Telangana and Andhra Pradesh got separate ones in 2019.</li></ul></li>
<li>The oldest are <b>Calcutta</b>, Bombay and Madras (all 1862), then Allahabad (1866). Allahabad has the largest sanctioned strength (160 judges).</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>High Court covering more than one State</th><th>Jurisdiction</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bombay</b></td><td>Maharashtra, Goa, and the UT of Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Gauhati</b></td><td>Assam, Nagaland, Mizoram, Arunachal. Before 2013 it also covered Meghalaya, Manipur and Tripura.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Punjab and Haryana</b></td><td>Punjab, Haryana and the UT of Chandigarh</td></tr></table>
<p>So <b>three</b> High Courts cover more than one <b>State</b>, both in 2008 and today. The circulated key said four, which counts UTs.</p>
<table><tr><th>UT</th><th>High Court</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Andaman and Nicobar</b></td><td><b>Calcutta</b> (circuit bench at Port Blair)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lakshadweep</td><td>Kerala</td></tr>
<tr><td>Puducherry</td><td>Madras</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chandigarh</td><td>Punjab and Haryana</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Delhi</b></td><td><b>Its own High Court</b> (1966)</td></tr>
<tr><td>J&amp;K and Ladakh</td><td>A common High Court</td></tr></table>
<p>"No UT has a High Court of its own" is wrong because of Delhi.</p>

<h3>High Court judges</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Qualifications (Article 217(2)):</b> a citizen who has held judicial office for <b>ten years</b> or been a High Court advocate for <b>ten years</b>. Not five.</li>
<li>Judges <b>retire at 62</b>; Supreme Court judges at 65. A 2010 bill to raise the age to 65 (the 114th Amendment Bill) lapsed.</li>
<li><b>Removal</b> follows the <b>same procedure as for Supreme Court judges</b> (Article 217(1)(b) applies Article 124(4)).</li>
<li><b>After retirement (Article 220):</b> a permanent High Court judge <b>cannot plead in the High Court where they served or in courts below it</b>, but <b>can practise in the Supreme Court and other High Courts</b>.
  <ul><li>So "cannot plead in any court" and "can plead only in the Supreme Court" are both wrong.</li>
  <li>Retired Supreme Court judges cannot plead anywhere in India.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Salaries</b> are charged on the <b>Consolidated Fund of the State</b> (Article 202(3)(d)), but <b>pensions</b> on the Consolidated Fund of India.</li>
<li>Pay is set by the High Court and Supreme Court Judges (Salaries and Conditions of Service) Act, 1954.</li>
<li>In 2025 the Supreme Court ruled for "One Rank One Pension" for all retired High Court judges.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Subordinate courts (Articles 233–237)</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>District and Sessions Judge</b> is the highest court in the district. As District Judge it hears civil matters, and as Sessions Judge it is the <b>highest criminal court</b>.</li>
<li>District Judges are <b>appointed by the Governor in consultation with the High Court</b> (Article 233(1)).</li>
<li>A direct recruit must be an <b>advocate or pleader of seven years' standing</b>. Serving judicial officers can also be appointed.</li>
<li>A <b>death sentence</b> passed by a Sessions Court must be <b>confirmed by the High Court</b> (Section 366 CrPC, now Section 407 BNSS).</li>
<li>Below the District Judge: civil judges (senior and junior division) and judicial magistrates. In 2025 the Supreme Court restored a minimum of three years' practice for entry-level civil judges.</li>
<li>An <b>All-India Judicial Service</b> has been debated for decades.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p7-4", `
<p>Courts mean little if the poor cannot reach them. Article 39A (free legal aid) and Article 21 (fair procedure) underpin a network of legal services, Lok Adalats, Gram Nyayalayas, tribunals and consumer commissions. This topic also covers criminal procedure, rewritten by the new criminal laws of 2024.</p>

<h3>Free legal aid</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Basis:</b> Article 39A (42nd Amendment, 1976) and Article 21 (M.H. Hoskot 1978; Hussainara Khatoon 1979).</li>
<li><b>The Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987</b> set up a tiered structure:
  <ul><li><b>NALSA</b>, functioning since 1995. Its Patron-in-Chief is the <b>CJI</b> (Surya Kant), and its Executive Chairman the next senior-most judge (Justice Vikram Nath since November 2025).</li>
  <li>State, District and Taluk Legal Services Authorities and Committees.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>NALSA's role:</b>
  <ul><li>It provides <b>free and competent legal services to weaker sections</b> on the basis of equal opportunity.</li>
  <li>It <b>issues guidelines</b> to State authorities to implement schemes.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Who gets free legal services (Section 12)</b><ul>
<li>SCs and STs, regardless of income.</li>
<li>Victims of trafficking or begar.</li>
<li>Women and children.</li>
<li>Persons with disabilities.</li>
<li>Victims of mass disaster, ethnic violence or caste atrocity.</li>
<li>Industrial workers.</li>
<li>Persons in custody.</li>
<li>Persons <b>below an income ceiling</b>: ₹1 lakh for Supreme Court cases at the time; States set their own limits, for example ₹2 lakh for <b>transgender persons</b> in Delhi.</li></ul>
<b>OBC status</b> is not a category, and <b>not all senior citizens</b> qualify; only those within State rules and income limits do.</div>
<p>Other initiatives: the Legal Aid Defence Counsel System (2022), the Tele-Law scheme, and National Legal Services Day (9 November).</p>

<h3>Lok Adalats</h3>
<ul>
<li>Lok Adalats have statutory status under the 1987 Act. They settle disputes by <b>compromise</b>.</li>
<li>An award is <b>deemed a civil court decree</b> and is <b>final</b>: <b>no appeal</b> lies against it (Section 21), and only a writ petition is possible. No court fee is charged.</li>
<li>They handle matters at the <b>pre-litigation stage and cases pending in courts</b>, both <b>civil and compoundable criminal</b>. Benches can include social workers and lawyers, not only judicial officers. So every limiting statement in the 2010 question was wrong.</li>
<li><b>Matrimonial and family disputes</b> (maintenance, custody, reconciliation) <b>are</b> covered. Non-compoundable offences are not.</li>
<li>Common cases: <b>motor accident claims</b> (the mainstay), <b>pension</b> cases, and disputes with <b>mobile and telecom companies</b>, which count as public utility services.</li>
<li><b>Permanent Lok Adalats</b> (2002 amendment) handle public utility services and can decide on the merits, up to ₹1 crore, if compromise fails.</li>
<li>NALSA holds <b>National Lok Adalats</b> every quarter, and e-Lok Adalats have run since 2020.</li>
<li>The <b>Mediation Act, 2023</b> adds another form of alternative dispute resolution.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Gram Nyayalayas</h3>
<ul>
<li>Set up under the <b>Gram Nyayalayas Act, 2008</b>, following the Law Commission's 114th report (1986). They are courts at the intermediate panchayat level.</li>
<li>Each is presided over by a <b>Nyayadhikari</b> (a first-class judicial magistrate), who also holds mobile courts in villages.</li>
<li>They have <b>both civil and criminal jurisdiction</b> over specified matters, including offences punishable with up to two years.</li>
<li>Local <b>social workers</b> can be appointed as <b>conciliators</b>.</li>
<li>They follow summary procedure and are not strictly bound by the Evidence Act.</li>
<li>States are not obliged to set them up, so uptake is poor: about 300 function, mainly in MP, Rajasthan, Kerala and Maharashtra.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tribunals and consumer commissions</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Central Administrative Tribunal</b> was set up in <b>1985</b> under <b>Rajiv Gandhi</b>, by the Administrative Tribunals Act. Its basis is Article 323A (42nd Amendment). It was <b>not</b> set up under Shastri.
  <ul><li>Its members come from both <b>judicial and administrative</b> streams.</li>
  <li>Its decisions can be challenged in High Courts (L. Chandra Kumar, 1997).</li>
  <li>Tribunal-reform laws, including the Tribunals Reforms Act 2021, were repeatedly struck down in the Madras Bar Association cases.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Consumer forums:</b>
  <ul><li>Under the 1986 Act (as amended in 2002), the <b>District Forum</b>'s limit was <b>₹20 lakh</b>, so "₹50 lakh" was the incorrect statement in 2010.</li>
  <li>The State could set up more than one forum per district, one member had to be a woman, and the State government could file complaints on behalf of consumers.</li>
  <li>The <b>Consumer Protection Act, 2019</b> renamed them <b>Commissions</b>. Since December 2021 the limits are: District up to ₹50 lakh, State ₹50 lakh–₹2 crore, National above ₹2 crore.</li>
  <li>Complaints can be filed online through e-Daakhil.</li>
  <li>The Central Consumer Protection Authority (2020) issues guidelines on dark patterns and misleading advertisements. Consumer rights are covered in 14.2.</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>The legal profession</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under the <b>Advocates Act, 1961</b>, only <b>individuals enrolled with a State Bar Council</b> are advocates.
  <ul><li>A <b>law firm</b> is not an advocate.</li>
  <li>Government law officers enrol as advocates.</li>
  <li>Full-time salaried employees, such as in-house corporate lawyers, cannot practise while employed (BCI Rule 49).</li>
  <li>Patent agents register separately under the Patents Act.</li>
  <li>So the 2022 statement that "law firms are advocates while corporate lawyers and patent attorneys are excluded" was wrong.</li></ul></li>
<li>The <b>Bar Council of India</b> <b>sets standards of legal education and recognises law colleges</b>. It also governs professional conduct and runs the All India Bar Examination (since 2010). The Attorney General and Solicitor General are ex officio members.</li>
<li>Rules of 2022, amended in 2025, let <b>foreign lawyers</b> practise foreign and international law in India on a reciprocal basis, but not Indian law.</li>
<li>Senior Advocates are designated under Section 16 (Indira Jaising case, 2017 and 2023).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Criminal procedure</h3>
<p>Three new criminal laws came into force on <b>1 July 2024</b>:</p>
<table><tr><th>Old law</th><th>New law</th><th>Key changes</th></tr>
<tr><td>IPC 1860</td><td><b>Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita</b> (BNS) 2023</td><td>358 sections. New offences of terrorism, organised crime, mob lynching and snatching. Sedition replaced by acts endangering sovereignty (Section 152). Community service as a punishment.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CrPC 1973</td><td><b>Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita</b> (BNSS) 2023</td><td>Zero FIR and e-FIR; videography of searches; forensic visits for offences punishable with 7+ years; trial in absentia; timelines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Evidence Act 1872</td><td><b>Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam</b> 2023</td><td>Electronic records treated as primary evidence</td></tr></table>
<div class="key"><b>Zero FIR under BNSS</b><ul>
<li>Section 173(1): information about a <b>cognizable</b> offence can be given at any police station, <b>wherever the offence occurred</b>, and is then transferred to the right station.
  <ul><li>This applies only to cognizable offences. Non-cognizable offences go under Section 174 and need a magistrate's order, so a statement covering "cognizable/non-cognizable" offences was wrong.</li></ul></li>
<li>Section 173(3): for offences punishable with <b>3 to 7 years</b>, the officer-in-charge may, <b>with the permission of a DSP-rank officer</b>, conduct a <b>preliminary enquiry</b> within 14 days. This was the only correct statement.</li>
<li>Information may be given <b>orally or electronically</b>; electronic filing is an option, not an obligation. An e-FIR must be signed within three days.</li>
<li><b>Lalita Kumari (2013)</b> held that FIR registration is mandatory for cognizable offences.</li></ul></div>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Police custody</th><th>Judicial custody</th></tr>
<tr><td>Where held</td><td><b>Police lock-up</b></td><td><b>Jail</b>, under the magistrate's control</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interrogation</td><td>By the police</td><td><b>Only with the court's permission</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Limit</td><td>15 days in total. BNSS Section 187 lets these be taken in parts within the first 40 or 60 days of remand.</td><td>60 or 90 days before a chargesheet, after which <b>default bail</b> applies</td></tr></table>
<p>An arrested person must be produced before a magistrate within 24 hours (Article 22(2)). D.K. Basu (1997) laid down arrest guidelines.</p>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Parole</th><th>Furlough</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>A specific need: illness or death in the family, a marriage, a harvest</td><td>A break from long imprisonment, to keep family ties</td></tr>
<tr><td>Counted as sentence served?</td><td>Generally no</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Granted by</td><td>Divisional Commissioner or State government</td><td>Prison authorities</td></tr>
<tr><td>A right?</td><td><b>No</b>; it can be refused even if a case is made out</td><td>Not an absolute right (Supreme Court, 2022)</td></tr></table>
<p>"Prisons" is a <b>State List</b> subject (Entry 4), so <b>each State has its own parole rules</b>, made under the Prisons Act 1894 and the Prisoners Act 1900. The Model Prisons and Correctional Services Act, 2023 is for States to adopt.</p>

<h3>Offences under special laws</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Official Secrets Act, 1923:</b>
  <ul><li>Section 6 penalises the <b>unauthorised wearing of police or military uniforms</b>.</li>
  <li>Section 7 penalises <b>misleading or interfering with police or military officers</b>. This is in the OSA, not the Evidence Act.</li>
  <li>Section 3 covers espionage and Section 5 wrongful communication.</li>
  <li>RTI Act Section 22 overrides the OSA, and the Second ARC recommended repealing it.</li>
  <li>Cases: the ISRO espionage case (Nambi Narayanan, later exonerated) and the Rafale documents case (2019).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Arms (Amendment) Act, 2019:</b> <b>celebratory gunfire</b> that endangers life or personal safety is punishable with up to two years and/or a ₹1 lakh fine. The number of licensed firearms per person was cut from three to two.</li>
<li><b>Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005:</b>
  <ul><li>A woman can petition a <b>first-class Judicial Magistrate</b> or a Metropolitan Magistrate for protection, residence, monetary or custody orders.</li>
  <li>It covers physical, sexual, verbal, emotional and economic abuse.</li>
  <li>It covers live-in relationships "in the nature of marriage" (Indra Sarma, 2013).</li>
  <li>Cruelty by a husband or relatives is now Sections 85–86 of the BNS (formerly IPC 498A).</li></ul></li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p8.js
// Polity book, Unit 8: State Government.
addBookTopic("polity", "p8-1", `
<p>The Governor is the constitutional head of a State and also the Centre's link to it. Like the President, the Governor mostly acts on the advice of the Council of Ministers. Unlike the President, however, the Governor has <b>explicit discretionary powers</b>.</p>

<h3>Appointment, tenure and removal</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Governor is <b>appointed by the President</b> by warrant (Article 155) and must be a citizen aged at least 35.</li>
<li>The Governor holds office <b>during the pleasure of the President</b> (Article 156), normally for five years.</li>
<li><b>No procedure for removal</b> is laid down in the Constitution: there is no impeachment and no listed grounds. In <b>B.P. Singhal (2010)</b> the Supreme Court held that removal cannot be arbitrary or simply because the government at the Centre has changed, and is open to limited judicial review.</li>
<li>The <b>same person can be Governor of two or more States</b> (7th Amendment, 1956). The emoluments are then shared as the President decides.</li>
<li>Emoluments are charged on the State's Consolidated Fund and <b>cannot be reduced during the term</b> (Article 158(4); for the President, Article 59(4)).</li>
<li><b>High Court judges</b> are appointed by the <b>President</b>, not the Governor. The Governor is only consulted.</li>
<li>In a UT with a legislature, the CM is appointed by the <b>President</b>, not the Lieutenant Governor.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>The Sarkaria Commission on Governors</b>The Sarkaria Commission (1983–88) said the Governor should be:<ul>
<li>an <b>eminent person</b>;</li>
<li><b>from outside the State</b>;</li>
<li>a <b>detached figure</b> without intense political links;</li>
<li>someone who has <b>not taken part in active politics in the recent past</b>;</li>
<li>appointed after consulting the CM.</li></ul></div>
<table><tr><th>Body</th><th>Key ideas</th></tr>
<tr><td>First Administrative Reforms Commission (1966)</td><td>An Inter-State Council; non-partisan Governors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rajamannar Committee (1969, Tamil Nadu)</td><td>State autonomy; abolish Articles 356 and 357</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anandpur Sahib Resolution (1973)</td><td>Centre limited to defence, foreign affairs, currency and communications</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sarkaria Commission (1988)</b></td><td>247 recommendations; Article 356 only as a last resort; a permanent Inter-State Council (set up in 1990)</td></tr>
<tr><td>NCRWC (Venkatachaliah, 2002)</td><td>Appoint the Governor after consulting the CM; a fixed tenure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Punchhi Commission (2010)</td><td>A fixed five-year tenure; removal only by an impeachment-like process; "localised emergency"</td></tr></table>
<p>Tamil Nadu set up the Kurian Joseph committee on State autonomy in 2025. Centre–State relations are covered in Unit 9.</p>

<h3>Discretionary powers</h3>
<p>Article 163(1): the Council of Ministers aids and advises the Governor "except in so far as he is by or under this Constitution required to exercise his functions … in his discretion". So the Constitution <b>explicitly</b> gives the Governor discretion in certain matters. Under Article 163(2), the Governor's decision on what counts as discretionary is final.</p>
<table><tr><th>Constitutional discretion</th><th>Situational discretion</th></tr>
<tr><td>
<ul>
<li><b>Reserving a bill for the President</b> (Article 200). This is mandatory if the bill endangers the High Court's position.</li>
<li><b>Reporting to the President to recommend President's Rule</b> (Article 356).</li>
<li>Acting as Administrator of an adjoining UT.</li>
<li>Sixth Schedule matters, such as mining royalties for District Councils in Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram.</li>
<li>Special responsibilities under Article 371 (Nagaland, Arunachal).</li>
<li>Seeking information from the CM (Article 167).</li>
</ul>
</td><td>
<ul>
<li>Choosing a CM when no party has a majority.</li>
<li>Dismissing a ministry that has lost confidence.</li>
<li>Dissolving the Assembly.</li>
</ul>
</td></tr></table>
<div class="trap"><b>Not discretionary</b><ul>
<li><b>Appointing ministers</b>: done on the CM's advice (Article 164).</li>
<li><b>Making the rules of business</b>: done on the Council of Ministers' advice (Article 166(3)).</li>
<li>Summoning the Assembly: must follow the cabinet's advice (Nabam Rebia, 2016).</li></ul>
<b>Shamsher Singh (1974):</b> the President and Governors are constitutional heads who act on ministerial advice.</div>

<h3>Bills passed by the State legislature</h3>
<p>Under Article 200 the Governor may:</p>
<ul>
<li>give assent;</li>
<li>withhold assent;</li>
<li>return a bill (other than a Money Bill) for reconsideration, after which the Governor must assent if it is passed again;</li>
<li>reserve the bill for the President.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>Only the <b>Governor</b> can reserve a State bill. The <b>President cannot, on their own, call for</b> a State bill without the Governor forwarding it.</li>
<li>Article 201 sets out what the President may do with a reserved bill: assent, withhold, or return it (if not a Money Bill) within six months.</li>
<li><b>April 2025 (State of Tamil Nadu v. Governor):</b> the Supreme Court set timelines of one to three months for acting on bills, held indefinite delay unconstitutional, and used Article 142 to deem ten bills assented.</li>
<li><b>November 2025:</b> on a Presidential reference (Article 143), a Constitution Bench held that courts <b>cannot impose fixed timelines or "deemed assent"</b>, though prolonged unexplained inaction can be reviewed in limited ways.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Immunities (Article 361)</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Governor (and the President) is <b>not answerable to any court</b> for exercising the powers and duties of office. Their acts can still be challenged, with the government as defendant.</li>
<li><b>No criminal proceedings</b> can be started or continued against them during their term (361(2)).</li>
<li>No arrest or imprisonment process can be issued (361(3)).</li>
<li>Civil proceedings for <b>personal</b> acts need <b>two months' notice</b> (361(4)).</li>
<li>The immunity is personal: acts done mala fide can still be challenged (Rameshwar Prasad, 2006).</li>
<li>In 2024 the Supreme Court agreed to examine the scope of Article 361, in a case involving a West Bengal Raj Bhavan employee.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Legislative privileges</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Members of a State Legislature</b> cannot be sued in any court for <b>anything said or any vote given in the House</b> or its committees (Article 194(2)). Article 105(2) gives MPs the same protection.</li>
<li><b>Individual privileges:</b>
  <ul><li>freedom of speech in the House, subject to the rules; judges' conduct cannot be discussed (Articles 121, 211);</li>
  <li>freedom from arrest in <b>civil</b> cases for 40 days before and after a session (not in criminal cases or preventive detention);</li>
  <li>exemption from jury service and from giving evidence during a session.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Collective privileges:</b> publishing reports, excluding strangers, regulating internal affairs, punishing for contempt, and barring courts from inquiring into proceedings (Articles 122, 212).</li>
<li>Privileges have <b>not been codified</b>.</li>
<li><b>Sita Soren (2024, seven judges)</b> overruled P.V. Narasimha Rao (1998): legislators who take bribes to vote or speak are <b>not</b> protected.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p8-2", `
<p>States have a parliamentary system like the Centre's. The Chief Minister and Council of Ministers are responsible to the Legislative Assembly (Articles 163–164). Each State also has its own officers: the Advocate General, the Chief Secretary and others.</p>

<h3>The State legislature</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Legislative Assembly (Article 170):</b> <b>not more than 500 and not less than 60</b> members, directly elected. "Not more than 450" is wrong.
  <ul><li>Small States are exceptions by law: Sikkim 32, Goa 40, Mizoram 40.</li>
  <li>The largest is Uttar Pradesh, with 403.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Minimum age (Article 173):</b> 25 for the Assembly and 30 for the Council.</li>
<li><b>Governor's address (Article 176):</b> the Governor addresses the House at the start of the <b>first session after each general election</b> and of the <b>first session each year</b>. It is popularly called the "customary address".
  <ul><li>The address is drafted by the government. Governors skipping or altering parts of it caused rows in Kerala and Tamil Nadu (2023–25).</li>
  <li>The President's equivalent is Article 87.</li></ul></li>
<li>Where a State legislature's own <b>rules are silent</b>, it follows the Lok Sabha's rules and practice.</li>
<li>Each House makes its own rules (Article 208), and courts cannot inquire into its proceedings (Article 212).</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Office</th><th>Minimum age</th><th>Article</th></tr>
<tr><td>Lok Sabha; Legislative Assembly</td><td>25</td><td>84; 173</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rajya Sabha; Legislative Council</td><td>30</td><td>84; 173</td></tr>
<tr><td>President; Vice-President; Governor</td><td>35</td><td>58; 66; 157</td></tr></table>

<h4>Legislative Councils</h4>
<ul>
<li>Six States have them: <b>Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Bihar and Uttar Pradesh</b>. Jammu and Kashmir's was abolished in 2019.</li>
<li>A Council is <b>created or abolished by Parliament</b> on the Assembly's resolution, passed by special majority (Article 169). This is not treated as an amendment under Article 368.</li>
<li><b>Size (Article 171):</b> at most <b>one-third</b> of the Assembly's strength, and at least 40. So it can never be larger than half the Assembly.</li>
<li><b>Composition:</b>
  <ul><li>one-third elected by local bodies;</li>
  <li>one-twelfth by graduates;</li>
  <li>one-twelfth by teachers;</li>
  <li>one-third by MLAs;</li>
  <li>about one-sixth <b>nominated by the Governor</b> (literature, science, art, the co-operative movement and social service).</li></ul></li>
<li>The <b>Chairman is elected by the Council</b> from among its members (Article 182), <b>not nominated</b> by the Governor.</li>
<li>Members serve six years, with one-third retiring every two years.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The Speaker of the Assembly</h4>
<ul>
<li>The Speaker <b>vacates office on ceasing to be a member</b> (Article 179(a)).</li>
<li>On <b>dissolution</b>, the Speaker does <b>not</b> vacate office immediately, but stays until just before the new Assembly's first meeting.</li>
<li>Removal is by an effective majority with 14 days' notice. During it, the Speaker cannot preside but can speak and vote in the first instance.</li>
<li>Powers: certifying Money Bills (Article 199), a casting vote in a tie, and deciding defection cases (see 6.1). In July 2025 the Supreme Court set a three-month deadline for the Telangana Speaker.</li>
</ul>

<h3>State officers</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Advocate General (Article 165):</b>
  <ul><li><b>Appointed by the Governor</b>, not the President, and holds office at the Governor's pleasure.</li>
  <li>Must be qualified to be a High Court judge.</li>
  <li>May speak in the State legislature (Article 177).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Chief Secretary:</b>
  <ul><li>Chosen by the <b>Chief Minister</b> (the State government) from senior IAS officers, not appointed by the Governor.</li>
  <li>Has <b>no fixed tenure</b>. The Second ARC suggested one, and T.S.R. Subramanian (2013) urged fixed tenures for civil servants.</li>
  <li>Heads the secretariat and the State Civil Services Board, and acts as secretary to the Council of Ministers.</li>
  <li>The central counterpart is the Cabinet Secretary (see 5.3).</li></ul></li>
<li>The State <b>Director General of Police</b> has a minimum two-year tenure (Prakash Singh, 2006).</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>High Courts have no advisory jurisdiction</b>High Courts have <b>original, appellate, writ (Article 226) and supervisory (Article 227)</b> jurisdiction. Only the Supreme Court has advisory jurisdiction (Article 143). Their jurisdiction comes from the Constitution and statutes, not mainly from the Civil Procedure Code. Bombay, Calcutta, Madras and Delhi have ordinary original civil jurisdiction.</div>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p9.js
// Polity book, Unit 9: Centre–State relations and emergencies.
addBookTopic("polity", "p9-1", `
<p>Part XI (Articles 245–263) divides legislative and executive power between the Union and the States. The division leans towards the Centre, but each side has its own field. Federal features in general are covered in 1.3.</p>

<h3>The Seventh Schedule (Article 246)</h3>
<table><tr><th>List</th><th>Subjects</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Union List</b> (List I)</td><td>98 (originally 97)</td><td>Defence, foreign affairs, railways, banking, currency, atomic energy, census, citizenship, income tax (other than agricultural), customs, <b>corporation tax</b>, <b>inter-State trade and commerce</b>, <b>inter-State migration and inter-State quarantine</b>, <b>stock exchanges and futures markets</b> (Entry 48), <b>Post Office Savings Bank</b>, <b>regulation of labour and safety in mines and oilfields</b> (Entry 55), regulation of mines and mineral development (Entry 54)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>State List</b> (List II)</td><td>59 (originally 66)</td><td>Police, public order, <b>prisons</b> (Entry 4), <b>public health and sanitation</b> (Entry 6), <b>agriculture</b>, <b>fisheries</b>, land, local government, liquor, betting, mines and minerals subject to List I (Entry 23), taxes on mineral rights (Entry 50), agricultural income tax, land revenue</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Concurrent List</b> (List III)</td><td>52 (originally 47)</td><td>Criminal law and procedure, marriage and divorce, <b>forests</b>, <b>education</b>, population control, trade unions, labour welfare, electricity, contempt of court, medical education</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>42nd Amendment (1976)</b> moved five subjects from the State List to the Concurrent List: <b>education, forests, weights and measures, protection of wild animals and birds, and administration of justice</b>.</li>
<li><b>Residuary powers</b> lie with Parliament (Article 248; Union List Entry 97), an idea borrowed from Canada.</li>
<li>In a conflict on a Concurrent subject, the <b>Union law prevails</b> (Article 254), unless the State law was reserved for the President and received assent.</li>
<li>The list scheme comes from the Government of India Act 1935 (see 2.1).</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Matching traps</b><ul>
<li>Inter-State trade and commerce, and inter-State migration, are <b>Union</b> subjects, not State subjects.</li>
<li><b>Inter-State quarantine</b> is a <b>Union</b> subject.</li>
<li>Corporation tax is a <b>Union</b> subject.</li>
<li><b>Stock exchanges</b> are in the <b>Union</b> List, not the Concurrent List, which is why SEBI is a central regulator. Forests are Concurrent, the Post Office Savings Bank is Union, and public health is State.</li>
<li>Of agriculture, fisheries, public health and mine labour safety, only <b>mine labour safety</b> is a Union subject.</li></ul></div>

<h3>Federalism in practice: prisons, health and minerals</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Prisons</b> are a State subject, so <b>States run them under their own rules</b>. The colonial <b>Prisons Act, 1894</b> placed prisons under Provincial Governments, which explains State control.
  <ul><li>The <b>Model Prisons and Correctional Services Act, 2023</b> (MHA) is meant to replace the 1894 Act in States that adopt it.</li>
  <li>About 76% of prisoners are undertrials, and occupancy is about 131% (NCRB 2022).</li>
  <li>BNSS Section 479 lets first-time undertrials who have served a third of the maximum sentence be released on bail.</li>
  <li>Sunil Batra (1978) held that prisoners keep their Fundamental Rights. <b>Sukanya Shantha (2024)</b> struck down caste-based allocation of work in jail manuals.</li>
  <li>Committees: Mulla (1980–83), Krishna Iyer (1987, women prisoners), Amitava Roy (2018).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Health:</b> India's public health system is <b>largely curative</b>, and <b>States are primarily responsible</b> for health services (State List Entry 6). Both are true, but State responsibility does <b>not explain</b> the curative bias, which comes from historical underinvestment.
  <ul><li>Public health spending is about 1.9% of GDP; NHP 2017 targeted 2.5%.</li>
  <li>Out-of-pocket spending fell from 64% (2013–14) to about 39% (2021–22).</li>
  <li>Schemes: NHM (2013) and Ayushman Bharat (2018). PM-JAY's ₹5 lakh cover was extended to everyone aged 70+ in 2024, and Health and Wellness Centres became Ayushman Arogya Mandirs.</li>
  <li>During COVID the Centre acted through the Epidemic Diseases Act 1897 and the Disaster Management Act 2005.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Minor minerals:</b> States <b>do</b> make rules for minor mineral concessions and collect royalty (Section 15 of the MMDR Act, 1957). The <b>Centre notifies</b> which minerals are minor (Section 3(e)); in 2015, 31 minerals were reclassified as minor.
  <ul><li>In <b>Mineral Area Development Authority (July 2024, nine judges, 8:1)</b> the Court held that royalty is not a tax and that States can tax mineral rights. This overruled India Cement (1989).</li>
  <li>The District Mineral Foundation and National Mineral Exploration Trust are covered in 8.5 of the Geography book.</li></ul></li>
<li>The three <b>farm laws</b> (2020) were repealed in 2021, partly over federalism concerns, since agriculture is a State subject.</li>
<li>The four <b>labour codes</b>, in force from November 2025, consolidate 29 central laws on a Concurrent subject.</li>
</ul>

<h3>When Parliament can legislate on State subjects</h3>
<table><tr><th>Article</th><th>Situation</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>249</b></td><td>The <b>Rajya Sabha</b> passes a resolution by <b>two-thirds of members present and voting</b> that it is necessary in the national interest. The resolution lasts one year and is renewable; the law lapses six months after the resolution ends.</td></tr>
<tr><td>250</td><td>During a National Emergency</td></tr>
<tr><td>252</td><td>Two or more States request it by resolution</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>253</b></td><td>To <b>implement international treaties</b>, agreements or decisions of international conferences, <b>without the consent of any State</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>356–357</td><td>During President's Rule</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>Laws under <b>Article 253</b> include the Environment (Protection) Act 1986 and Air Act 1981 (after Stockholm 1972), the Biological Diversity Act 2002, the Protection of Human Rights Act 1993, and the Geographical Indications Act 1999 (TRIPS).</li>
<li>Treaty-making is an executive power (Article 73). India is <b>dualist</b>: treaties need a law before they take effect at home.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Administrative relations</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 256:</b> States must comply with Union laws.</li>
<li><b>Article 257:</b> the executive power of every State shall be exercised so as <b>not to impede or prejudice the executive power of the Union</b>. The Union may give directions for this, and on communications of military importance and protecting railways.</li>
<li><b>Article 258:</b> the Union may entrust functions to States.</li>
<li><b>Article 365:</b> failure to follow Union directions allows President's Rule.</li>
<li><b>Commissions on Centre–State relations:</b> Rajamannar (1969), Sarkaria (1988), Punchhi (2010), and Tamil Nadu's Kurian Joseph committee (2025) (see 8.1).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p9-2", `
<p>Taxing power and spending needs do not match. The Centre raises most revenue, while States carry much of the spending. The Constitution bridges the gap through tax-sharing and grants, guided by the <b>Finance Commission</b>.</p>

<h3>Distribution of taxes</h3>
<table><tr><th>Article</th><th>Arrangement</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>268</b></td><td><b>Levied by the Union, collected and kept by the States</b></td><td><b>Stamp duties</b> on bills of exchange, cheques, promissory notes and share transfers</td></tr>
<tr><td>269</td><td>Levied and collected by the Union, assigned to States</td><td>Taxes on inter-State sale of goods (before GST)</td></tr>
<tr><td>269A</td><td>GST on inter-State supplies (IGST)</td><td>Apportioned between the Union and States</td></tr>
<tr><td>270</td><td>Levied and collected by the Union, shared with States</td><td>Income tax and corporation tax: the divisible pool</td></tr>
<tr><td>Union only</td><td>Levied, collected and kept by the Union</td><td><b>Taxes on stock-exchange and futures transactions</b> (Union List Entry 90): Securities Transaction Tax and Commodities Transaction Tax</td></tr></table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common traps</b><ul>
<li><b>Stamp duties on financial instruments</b> are <b>levied by the Union</b> but <b>collected and appropriated by the States</b>. So both "levied and collected by States" and "appropriated by the Union" are wrong.</li>
<li><b>Taxes on stock-exchange transactions</b> (other than stamp duty) are <b>levied and collected by the Union</b>. States do not collect them.</li></ul></div>
<ul>
<li>Stamp duty on other documents, such as property sale deeds, is levied and kept by States (State List Entry 63).</li>
<li>Since a 2020 amendment to the Indian Stamp Act, stamp duty on securities is collected centrally by exchanges and depositories and passed on to the States.</li>
<li>STT, introduced in 2004, was raised in Budget 2024–25 to 0.02% on futures and 0.1% on options.</li>
<li>GST and the GST Council (Article 279A) are covered in the Economy book.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Finance Commission (Article 280)</h3>
<ul>
<li>A constitutional body set up by the President every five years.</li>
<li><b>Its primary function</b> is to <b>distribute revenue between the Centre and the States</b>: the vertical share of the divisible pool, and the horizontal share among States.</li>
<li><b>Its functions:</b>
  <ul><li>to recommend how the <b>net proceeds of taxes are divided</b> between the Union and the States, and <b>allocated among the States</b> (Article 280(3)(a));</li>
  <li>to recommend the <b>principles governing grants-in-aid</b> to States from the Consolidated Fund of India (Articles 280(3)(b) and 275), which involves considering States' needs;</li>
  <li>measures to supplement the resources of panchayats and municipalities;</li>
  <li>any other matter the President refers.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Not its functions:</b> authorising withdrawals from the Consolidated Fund (Parliament does that by law), supervising whether taxes match the Budget, preparing the Budget, or allocating funds to ministries.</li>
<li>The <b>President</b> causes its recommendations to be laid before each House, with an explanatory memorandum (Article 281), not the Speaker, the PM or the Finance Minister.</li>
<li>The recommending body for grants-in-aid principles is the <b>Finance Commission</b>, not the Inter-State Council, the Finance Ministry or the PAC.</li>
<li>Discretionary grants under <b>Article 282</b> are a separate channel, formerly used for Planning Commission transfers.</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Commission</th><th>Period</th><th>States' share</th></tr>
<tr><td>15th (N.K. Singh)</td><td>2021–26</td><td>41% of the divisible pool</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>16th (Arvind Panagariya)</b></td><td>2026–31</td><td>41%. The report was submitted in November 2025 and laid on 1 February 2026; it added GDP contribution as a criterion.</td></tr></table>
<p>Finance Commission grants include revenue-deficit grants, local-body grants and disaster grants. The 15th FC also gave ₹70,051 crore in health grants through local bodies. State Finance Commissions are covered in 10.1.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p9-3", `
<p>Part XVIII (Articles 352–360) lets the Union take over extraordinary powers in a crisis. These provisions turn the federation into an almost unitary system. Their misuse in 1975–77 led the 44th Amendment (1978) to add safeguards.</p>

<h3>The Union's duty (Article 355)</h3>
<p>Article 355: "It shall be the <b>duty of the Union to protect every State against external aggression and internal disturbance</b>" and to ensure that every State is governed according to the Constitution. It is the foundation of President's Rule.</p>
<div class="trap"><b>2023: two of three statements correct</b><ul>
<li>The Centre's duty to protect States from internal disturbance (Article 355): <b>correct</b>.</li>
<li>Under Article 22(3), the right to consult a lawyer does <b>not apply</b> to preventive detainees, so the Constitution does exempt States from providing counsel: <b>correct</b>.</li>
<li>Under <b>POTA 2002</b> (Section 32), confessions to a police officer of SP rank or above <b>were admissible</b>, unlike the normal rule in Evidence Act Section 25, so "cannot be used as evidence" was <b>wrong</b>. POTA was repealed in 2004, and TADA had a similar provision.</li></ul></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Preventive detention (Article 22(4)–(7)):</b> detention beyond three months needs an Advisory Board report; grounds must be communicated, and the detainee may make a representation.
  <ul><li>Laws: the NSA 1980, COFEPOSA 1974, PIT-NDPS 1988, and State Goonda Acts.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Anti-terror laws:</b> TADA (1985–95), POTA (2002–04), and the UAPA 1967 (amended 2019 to let individuals be designated terrorists).
  <ul><li>UAPA bail is very hard to get (Watali, 2019), though K.A. Najeeb (2021) stressed speedy trial.</li>
  <li>The NIA Act dates from 2008, and BNS Section 113 defines a "terrorist act".</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Three kinds of emergency</h3>
<table><tr><th></th><th>National (Article 352)</th><th>President's Rule (Article 356)</th><th>Financial (Article 360)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Grounds</td><td>War, external aggression or <b>armed rebellion</b> ("internal disturbance" was replaced by the 44th Amendment)</td><td>Failure of constitutional machinery in a State</td><td>A threat to the financial stability or credit of India</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parliamentary approval</td><td><b>Within one month</b>, by <b>special majority</b> in both Houses</td><td><b>Within two months</b>, by simple majority</td><td><b>Within two months</b>, by simple majority</td></tr>
<tr><td>Duration</td><td>Six months at a time, renewable</td><td>Six months at a time, <b>up to three years</b></td><td><b>Indefinite</b> until revoked; no renewal needed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Used</td><td>1962, 1971, 1975</td><td>Over 125 times</td><td><b>Never</b></td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>Only the Lok Sabha can pass a resolution <b>disapproving</b> a National Emergency, by simple majority.</li>
<li>During a National Emergency, <b>Articles 20 and 21 cannot be suspended</b>. The Lok Sabha's term may be extended a year at a time (see 6.1).</li>
<li>The 38th Amendment barred judicial review of the President's satisfaction; the 44th Amendment removed that bar.</li>
</ul>

<h3>President's Rule (Article 356)</h3>
<div class="key"><b>What a proclamation does</b><ul>
<li>The President takes over the State's executive functions, so the <b>Council of Ministers is dismissed</b>. The Governor runs the State on the President's behalf, with advisers.</li>
<li>The <b>powers of the State legislature</b> become <b>exercisable by or under the authority of Parliament</b>. Parliament may delegate law-making to the President (Article 357), and the State Budget is passed by Parliament.</li>
<li>The <b>Assembly is not necessarily dissolved</b>; it may be kept in suspended animation. Bommai held that it should not be dissolved before Parliament approves the proclamation.</li>
<li><b>Local bodies are not dissolved</b>. Panchayats and municipalities have their own constitutional five-year terms.</li>
<li><b>Article 19 is not suspended</b>; that happens only in a National Emergency on grounds of war or external aggression.</li>
<li>The High Court is unaffected.</li>
<li>Laws made during President's Rule continue afterwards until the State legislature changes them.</li></ul>
So of the 2017 options, dissolution of the Assembly and of local bodies are "not necessarily" consequences, while removal of the ministry is.</div>
<ul>
<li>Extending it <b>beyond one year</b> needs a National Emergency to be in force and the EC to certify that elections cannot be held (44th Amendment).</li>
<li><b>S.R. Bommai (1994):</b> majority must be tested on the floor of the House, the proclamation is open to judicial review, and secularism is part of the basic structure.</li>
<li>The first use was in Punjab (1951). Ambedkar had hoped Article 356 would remain a "dead letter". The Sarkaria and Punchhi Commissions urged it be used only as a last resort.</li>
<li><b>Manipur:</b> 13 February 2025 to 4 February 2026, revoked when a new government under Yumnam Khemchand Singh took office.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Financial Emergency (Article 360)</h3>
<ul>
<li>A proclamation <b>ceases after two months</b> unless both Houses approve it. Once approved, it continues until revoked.</li>
<li>The President may direct <b>salary cuts for Union employees, including Supreme Court and High Court judges</b>. So "excluding judges" is wrong.</li>
<li>States can be asked to <b>reserve Money Bills</b> for the President.</li>
<li>It has <b>never</b> been declared.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p9-4", `
<p>Beyond the lists and emergencies, India has forums for cooperation between States and with the Centre. Some are constitutional, some statutory and some purely executive.</p>

<h3>Inter-State Council and Zonal Councils</h3>
<table><tr><th>Body</th><th>Basis</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Inter-State Council</b></td><td><b>Constitutional</b> (Article 263). Actually set up by Presidential order in 1990, on the Sarkaria Commission's advice.</td><td>Chaired by the <b>PM</b>. Members: CMs of all States and of UTs with legislatures, Administrators of other UTs, Governors of States under President's Rule, and six Union Cabinet ministers. It advises on inter-State disputes and common interests. It should meet three times a year, but the last full meeting was in 2016. The Home Minister chairs its Standing Committee.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Zonal Councils</b> (5)</td><td><b>Statutory</b>: States Reorganisation Act 1956, an idea of Nehru's</td><td>Northern, Central, Eastern, Western and Southern. Chaired by the <b>Union Home Minister</b>, with CMs as vice-chairs by rotation.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>National Security Council</b></td><td><b>Executive</b> (1998, Vajpayee government)</td><td>Chaired by the PM, with the NSA as secretary. Under it sit the Strategic Policy Group (chaired by the Cabinet Secretary), the National Security Advisory Board, and the Joint Intelligence Committee.</td></tr></table>
<p>So of these three, <b>only one</b>, the Inter-State Council, rests on a constitutional provision. Constitutional and statutory bodies in general are covered in 12.2.</p>
<ul>
<li>Inter-State <b>water disputes</b> go to tribunals under Article 262 and the Inter-State River Water Disputes Act, 1956. The Supreme Court's jurisdiction over them can be excluded.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The North Eastern Council</h3>
<div class="key"><b>NEC membership</b>The NEC was created by the <b>North Eastern Council Act, 1971</b> (operational 1972), with headquarters at <b>Shillong</b>. After the <b>2002 amendment</b> it comprises:<ul>
<li>the <b>Governors</b> of the eight constituent States;</li>
<li>their <b>Chief Ministers</b>;</li>
<li><b>three members nominated by the President</b>.</li></ul>
The eight States are Assam, Arunachal, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Tripura and <b>Sikkim</b> (added in 2002). The <b>Union Home Minister is not a member under the Act</b>. Since a 2018 Cabinet decision the Home Minister is the ex officio Chairman, and the DoNER Minister Vice-Chairman, but that is an executive arrangement.</div>
<ul>
<li>The 2002 amendment turned the NEC from an advisory body into a <b>regional planning body</b>.</li>
<li>The <b>Ministry of DoNER</b> was created in 2004 (a department since 2001).</li>
<li>Central ministries must spend 10% of their gross budgetary support in the North-East.</li>
<li>Schemes: PM-DevINE (2022) and NESIDS.</li>
<li>"Ashtalakshmi" is the PM's term for the eight north-eastern States (Ashtalakshmi Mahotsav, 2024).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p10.js
// Polity book, Unit 10: Local Government.
addBookTopic("polity", "p10-1", `
<p>Local self-government is best described as <b>democratic decentralisation</b>, a term the Balwant Rai Mehta Committee (1957) used. Power, functions and funds pass to <b>elected</b> local bodies so that people govern themselves at the grassroots.</p>
<ul>
<li>It is not federalism, which is the constitutional division between the Centre and the States.</li>
<li>It is not administrative delegation, which transfers work to subordinate officials rather than to elected bodies.</li>
<li>It is not direct democracy, though the Gram Sabha comes close.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <b>fundamental objects</b> of Panchayati Raj are <b>people's participation in development</b> and <b>democratic decentralisation</b>. UPSC's key did not count political accountability or financial mobilisation as fundamental objects.</p>

<h3>Committees and evolution</h3>
<table><tr><th>Committee</th><th>Key recommendation</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Balwant Rai Mehta (1957)</b></td><td>A three-tier system. The first Panchayati Raj institutions were set up at <b>Nagaur, Rajasthan, on 2 October 1959</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ashok Mehta (1977)</td><td>Two tiers; participation by political parties</td></tr>
<tr><td>G.V.K. Rao (1985)</td><td>The district as the key unit of planning</td></tr>
<tr><td>L.M. Singhvi (1986)</td><td>Constitutional status for panchayats</td></tr></table>
<p>The <b>73rd Amendment (1992)</b> laid the foundation for strong Panchayati Raj institutions. It came into force on <b>24 April 1993</b>, now <b>National Panchayati Raj Day</b>. It is not about rural employment or about rights.</p>
<p>Before the 73rd Amendment, local bodies varied by State. In 1999, for example:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Assam</b> had Zila Parishads at the sub-divisional level (Mahkuma Parishads).</li>
<li><b>Andhra Pradesh</b> had Mandal Praja Parishads (NTR's 1986 reform).</li>
<li><b>Mizoram</b> had tribal Village Councils and Autonomous District Councils.</li>
<li><b>Meghalaya</b> had no village panchayats; traditional Dorbar Shnong bodies serve instead.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Part IX: key provisions</h3>
<table><tr><th>Provision</th><th>Article</th></tr>
<tr><td>Gram Sabha</td><td>243A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Three tiers: village, intermediate and district. The <b>intermediate tier is optional in States with a population of 20 lakh or less</b> (Goa, Sikkim and some north-eastern States have two tiers).</td><td>243B</td></tr>
<tr><td>Direct election at all levels. Chairpersons of intermediate and district panchayats are elected indirectly.</td><td>243C</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reservation: SCs and STs in proportion to population; <b>at least one-third for women</b> (about 21 States give 50%); OBC reservation optional</td><td>243D</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Five-year term</b>. After dissolution, <b>elections within six months</b>. A body reconstituted after early dissolution serves only <b>the remainder</b> of the term; if under six months remain, no election is held.</td><td>243E</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disqualification: anyone qualified for the State legislature, except that the <b>minimum age is 21</b>, not 25 or 30</td><td>243F</td></tr>
<tr><td>Powers and functions: <b>29 subjects</b> in the <b>Eleventh Schedule</b></td><td>243G</td></tr>
<tr><td>Taxes and funds</td><td>243H</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>State Finance Commission</b>, set up by the <b>Governor</b> every five years</td><td>243I</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>State Election Commission</b>: appointed by the Governor, and removable only like a High Court judge</td><td>243K</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exempted areas: <b>Nagaland, Meghalaya, Mizoram</b>, Sixth Schedule areas, Manipur's hill areas, Darjeeling</td><td>243M</td></tr>
<tr><td>Courts barred from electoral matters</td><td>243O</td></tr></table>
<div class="trap"><b>2025: all three statements were incorrect</b><ul>
<li>Intermediate panchayats do <b>not</b> exist in all States.</li>
<li>The minimum age is <b>21</b>, not 30.</li>
<li>The <b>Governor</b>, not the Chief Minister, sets up the State Finance Commission.</li></ul></div>
<ul>
<li><b>State Finance Commission:</b> it recommends to the Governor the <b>principles for determining the taxes and duties that panchayats may levy</b>, the sharing of State taxes, and grants-in-aid. That is its job, not that of District Planning Committees or State ministries.
  <ul><li>The Union Finance Commission (Article 280(3)(bb)) recommends measures to supplement local bodies' resources on the basis of SFC reports.</li>
  <li>The 16th Finance Commission (2026–31) continued grants to local bodies.</li></ul></li>
<li>UPSC's key credited the 73rd Amendment with <b>District Planning Committees, State Election Commissions and State Finance Commissions</b>. Strictly, District Planning Committees (Article 243ZD) came from the 74th Amendment.</li>
<li><b>State control:</b> States control local bodies through legislation, finance and grievance redress. UPSC's key named <b>personnel</b> as the area where they do not.</li>
<li>Devolution of the "3 Fs" (<b>funds, functions and functionaries</b>) varies widely between States.</li>
<li>The <b>"Sarpanch Pati"</b> problem, where husbands act as proxies for elected women, was the subject of a 2024–25 advisory committee report recommending penalties.</li>
<li>In 2025 the Supreme Court ordered Maharashtra to hold long-delayed local elections. OBC quotas in local bodies must pass the "triple test" (2021).</li>
</ul>

<h3>PESA, 1996</h3>
<p>The <b>Panchayats (Extension to the Scheduled Areas) Act, 1996</b>, based on the <b>Bhuria Committee</b> (1995), extends Part IX to <b>Fifth Schedule areas</b> with modifications that empower the <b>Gram Sabha</b>.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Objectives:</b> self-governance, recognising traditional rights, and freeing tribal people from exploitation.</li>
<li>Creating <b>autonomous regions</b> is <b>not</b> a PESA objective; that is a Sixth Schedule feature (see 11.1).</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Gram Sabha powers under PESA</b><ul>
<li>It can <b>prevent alienation of land</b> and restore land unlawfully alienated.</li>
<li>It owns <b>minor forest produce</b>: tendu leaves, bamboo, mahua, lac.</li>
<li>Its recommendation, or that of the Panchayat at the appropriate level, is mandatory for prospecting licences or mining leases for <b>minor minerals</b>. It is not needed for "any mineral", and not the Gram Sabha's alone.</li>
<li>It approves development plans, identifies beneficiaries, controls moneylending and village markets, and must be consulted before land acquisition.</li></ul></div>
<ul>
<li>At least half the seats are reserved for STs, and chairpersons at all levels are STs.</li>
<li>PESA applies in <b>ten States</b>: Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Chhattisgarh, Gujarat, Himachal, Jharkhand, MP, Maharashtra, Odisha and Rajasthan.</li>
<li>Jharkhand notified its PESA rules only in January 2026, leaving Odisha as the last State without final rules.</li>
<li>Related: the Forest Rights Act (see 11.1) and Niyamgiri (2013).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Schemes for panchayats</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Revamped Rashtriya Gram Swaraj Abhiyan:</b> approved in April 2022 for <b>2022–23 to 2025–26</b> (1 April 2022 to 31 March 2026), not from 2021.
  <ul><li>Its key aim is to build the <b>governance capabilities of panchayats to deliver the Sustainable Development Goals</b> (Localised SDGs, under nine themes).</li>
  <li>Central funding is <b>not 100% for all</b>. The State component is shared 60:40 (90:10 for north-eastern and Himalayan States and J&amp;K), and is fully central only for UTs without legislatures.</li>
  <li>RGSA was first launched in 2018.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Other tools:</b> e-Gram Swaraj, <b>SVAMITVA</b> (2020; drone-based property cards, over 2 crore issued), Gram Panchayat Development Plans ("Sabki Yojana Sabka Vikas"), the Panchayat Development Index (2024), and the Meri Panchayat app.</li>
<li>National Panchayat Awards are given on 24 April.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p10-2", `
<p>Urban local bodies received constitutional status from the <b>74th Amendment (1992)</b>, which inserted <b>Part IXA</b> (Articles 243P–243ZG) and the <b>Twelfth Schedule</b> (18 subjects).</p>

<h3>History</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>first Municipal Corporation</b> was set up in <b>Madras in 1688</b>, by royal charter under the East India Company. It is now the Greater Chennai Corporation.</li>
<li><b>Lord Ripon's resolution (1882)</b> is called the "Magna Carta of local self-government".</li>
<li>Delhi's three municipal corporations were reunified in 2022.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Three types of municipality (Article 243Q)</h3>
<table><tr><th>Body</th><th>For</th></tr>
<tr><td>Nagar Panchayat</td><td>A transitional area, changing from rural to urban</td></tr>
<tr><td>Municipal Council</td><td>A smaller urban area</td></tr>
<tr><td>Municipal Corporation</td><td>A larger urban area</td></tr></table>
<p>So "Article 243Q envisages two types" is wrong; it envisages three.</p>

<h3>Key provisions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Reservation:</b> SCs and STs, plus at least one-third of seats for women (many States give 50%).</li>
<li><b>Term:</b> five years, with fresh elections within six months of dissolution (Article 243U).</li>
<li><b>Elections</b> are run by the State Election Commission (Article 243ZA); <b>finances</b> are reviewed by the State Finance Commission (Article 243Y).</li>
<li><b>Powers:</b> the 18 subjects of the Twelfth Schedule (Article 243W), including urban planning, water supply, public health, slum improvement, fire services, and cultural and educational aspects.</li>
<li><b>Ward Committees</b> (Article 243S) are required in municipalities with a population of three lakh or more.</li>
<li><b>District Planning Committees</b> (Article 243ZD) consolidate the plans of both panchayats and municipalities.</li>
<li><b>Contesting:</b> a person's eligibility depends on <b>being on the voters' list</b> of the municipal area and being at least 21 (Article 243V and State laws). Party membership, college permission or a declaration of allegiance is not required.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Metropolitan Planning Committee (Article 243ZE)</b><ul>
<li>It is <b>constituted under the Constitution</b> for every metropolitan area, meaning one with a population of 10 lakh or more.</li>
<li>It <b>prepares the draft development plan</b> for the whole metropolitan area.</li>
<li>It does <b>not</b> have sole responsibility for implementing schemes; municipalities, development authorities and State departments implement them.</li>
<li>At least two-thirds of its members are elected by municipal councillors and panchayat chairpersons in the area.</li></ul>
Many States have not set up working MPCs. A 2024 CAG compilation found weak devolution of funds, functions and functionaries to urban bodies.</div>
<p>Urban schemes (Smart Cities, AMRUT 2.0, PMAY-Urban) and the "Right to the City" idea are covered in 1.1 and the Geography book.</p>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p11.js
// Polity book, Unit 11: Scheduled areas, tribes and special provisions for groups.
addBookTopic("polity", "p11-1", `
<p>The Fifth and Sixth Schedules exist to <b>protect the interests of Scheduled Tribes</b>. They do not fix State boundaries, define panchayat powers or protect border States. They are backed by laws such as PESA (1996) and the Forest Rights Act (2006).</p>

<h3>Fifth Schedule and Sixth Schedule</h3>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Fifth Schedule (Article 244(1))</th><th>Sixth Schedule (Articles 244(2), 275(1))</th></tr>
<tr><td>Where</td><td><b>Scheduled Areas</b> in ten States: Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Chhattisgarh, Gujarat, Himachal, Jharkhand, MP, Maharashtra, Odisha, Rajasthan</td><td><b>Tribal areas of Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Declared by</td><td>The <b>President, by order</b>, after consulting the Governor. The President can also enlarge, reduce or alter the areas.</td><td>Listed in the Schedule; the Governor can reorganise districts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Governing bodies</td><td>The <b>State government</b> keeps executive power, overseen by the Governor; a <b>Tribes Advisory Council</b> (up to 20 members, three-quarters of them ST MLAs)</td><td><b>Ten Autonomous District Councils</b> (up to 30 members: 26 elected and 4 nominated by the Governor), with legislative, executive and judicial powers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Special powers</td><td>The Governor can direct that an Act of Parliament or of the State <b>does not apply, or applies with changes</b>. The Governor can make regulations to <b>prohibit or restrict the transfer of tribal land</b>, regulate land allotment and regulate money-lending, with the President's assent and after consulting the TAC.</td><td>The councils make laws on land, forests, jhum, inheritance, marriage and similar matters, with the Governor's assent; they run village courts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reporting</td><td>The <b>Governor</b> reports <b>annually to the President</b>; the Union can then direct the State</td><td>–</td></tr></table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common traps on the Fifth Schedule</b><ul>
<li>Declaring a Scheduled Area does <b>not</b> take away the State's executive power or hand total administration to a local body; that kind of autonomy belongs to the Sixth Schedule.</li>
<li>The <b>Union does not take over</b> administration on the Governor's recommendation. It can only give directions.</li>
<li>The annual report is sent by the <b>Governor to the President</b>, not by the Chief Minister to the Home Ministry.</li>
<li>Including an area does <b>not</b> make it a UT or a Special Category State.</li>
<li>The tribal areas of Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram are under the <b>Sixth</b> Schedule, not the Fifth.</li></ul></div>
<ul>
<li>The largest unit notified as a Scheduled Area is a <b>district</b>; the smallest is a <b>cluster of villages in a block</b>.</li>
<li>The <b>Dhebar Commission</b> (1960–61) gave the criteria for Scheduled Areas: a preponderance of tribal population, compact and reasonable size, under-development, and marked economic disparity with neighbouring areas. The Bhuria Commission followed in 2002–04.</li>
<li><b>Bringing an area under the Fifth Schedule</b> mainly <b>prevents tribal land from passing to non-tribals</b>.
  <ul><li>In <b>Samatha v. State of AP (1997)</b> the Court held that leasing tribal land in Scheduled Areas to private, non-tribal companies for <b>mining</b> is <b>void under the Fifth Schedule</b>. The Third, Ninth and Twelfth Schedules have nothing to do with this.</li>
  <li>The Andhra Pradesh Scheduled Areas Land Transfer Regulation 1959 is one such regulation.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>The ten Sixth Schedule councils:</b>
  <ul><li>Assam: Bodoland, Karbi Anglong, Dima Hasao.</li>
  <li>Meghalaya: Garo, Khasi, Jaintia Hills.</li>
  <li>Tripura: TTAADC.</li>
  <li>Mizoram: Chakma, Lai, Mara.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Ladakh</b> has demanded Sixth Schedule status. Four people were killed in Leh on 24 September 2025; Sonam Wangchuk was detained under the NSA and released in March 2026. Talks continue, with Article 371-type safeguards under discussion.</li>
<li>PESA extends panchayats to Fifth Schedule areas (see 10.1).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Scheduled Tribes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 342(1):</b> the <b>President</b>, after consulting the Governor, notifies the STs of each State or UT. After that, <b>only Parliament</b> can add or remove communities. So the Governor does <b>not</b> declare STs.</li>
<li><b>ST status is State-specific:</b> a community can be an ST in one State and not in another. For example, the Lambadas are ST in Telangana but OBC or SC elsewhere. A migrant does not carry ST status to another State (Marri Chandra Shekhar Rao, 1990).</li>
<li>The Constitution does <b>not define</b> a Scheduled Tribe.</li>
<li><b>Lokur Committee (1965) criteria:</b> primitive traits, distinctive culture, geographical isolation, shyness of contact with the wider community, and backwardness.</li>
<li><b>Inclusion process:</b> State proposal → Registrar General of India → National Commission for STs → Cabinet → Parliament amends the Constitution (ST) Order 1950.</li>
<li>STs can belong to any religion. SCs must be Hindu, Sikh or Buddhist.</li>
<li><b>Numbers (Census 2011):</b> about 705 communities and 10.4 crore people (8.6% of the population).
  <ul><li>The <b>North-East has only about 12%</b> of India's STs, not over half.</li>
  <li><b>MP</b> has the most (about 14.7%), then Maharashtra and Odisha.</li>
  <li>STs make up over 85% of the population in Mizoram, Lakshadweep, Meghalaya and Nagaland.</li>
  <li>Punjab, Haryana, Delhi, Chandigarh and Puducherry have no STs.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Droupadi Murmu</b> (2022) is India's first tribal President.</li>
<li><b>Tax exemptions:</b> some STs pay no income tax on certain incomes. Section 10(26) covers STs living in specified north-eastern areas, on income from sources there; Section 10(26A) covers Ladakh.</li>
<li>Peoples to remember: the <b>Todas</b> of the Nilgiris, and <b>Lotha</b>, a language of Nagaland.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs)</h3>
<ul>
<li>There are <b>75</b> PVTGs (not 95), in <b>18 States and one UT</b> (the Andaman and Nicobar Islands). Odisha has the most (13).</li>
<li><b>Criteria:</b> pre-agricultural technology, low literacy, economic backwardness, and a <b>stagnant or declining population</b>.</li>
<li>The category was created in 1975 as "Primitive Tribal Groups" and renamed in 2006.</li>
<li><b>Examples:</b>
  <ul><li>Andaman and Nicobar: Jarawa, <b>Onge</b> (Little Andaman, about 100 people), Great Andamanese, Sentinelese, Shompen.</li>
  <li>MP: Baiga, Bharia, <b>Sahariya</b> (also <b>Rajasthan</b>'s only PVTG, in Baran, where starvation deaths in 2002–04 made the news).</li>
  <li>Odisha: Bonda, Dongria Kondh, Juang.</li>
  <li>Tamil Nadu: Toda, Kota, Kurumba, Paniyan, <b>Irular</b>.</li>
  <li>Andhra Pradesh and Telangana: <b>Konda Reddi</b>.</li>
  <li>Kerala: Cholanaikan.</li>
  <li>Jharkhand: Birhor, Asur.</li>
  <li>Maharashtra: Katkari.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>PM-JANMAN</b> (November 2023, ₹24,000 crore) funds housing, roads, water, health and education in PVTG habitations. The Dharti Aaba Janjatiya Gram Utkarsh Abhiyan (2024) covers tribal villages.</li>
<li>Kuno National Park was created after relocating Sahariya villages.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Forest Rights Act, 2006</h3>
<ul>
<li>Full name: the Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act.</li>
<li>The <b>nodal ministry</b> is the <b>Ministry of Tribal Affairs</b> (Section 11), not the Environment, Panchayati Raj or Rural Development ministries.</li>
<li><b>Eligibility:</b> forest-dwelling STs in occupation before <b>13 December 2005</b>, and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers who have lived there for <b>three generations (75 years)</b>.</li>
<li><b>Rights:</b>
  <ul><li>individual rights (up to 4 ha under cultivation);</li>
  <li>community rights (grazing, fishing);</li>
  <li>community forest resource rights (to protect and manage the forest);</li>
  <li><b>ownership of minor forest produce</b>;</li>
  <li>habitat rights for PVTGs;</li>
  <li>conversion of forest villages into revenue villages.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Process:</b> the <b>Gram Sabha initiates</b> it, receiving and verifying claims through its Forest Rights Committee. The Forest Department, the Collector and the Tahsildar do not. Claims then go to the Sub-Divisional Committee and finally the District Level Committee.</li>
<li>Gram Sabha consent is needed for diverting forest land. In <b>Niyamgiri (2013)</b>, the Dongria Kondh gram sabhas rejected Vedanta's bauxite mining.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Bamboo and minor forest produce</b><ul>
<li>Under the FRA, <b>bamboo is a minor forest produce</b>, along with honey, lac, tendu leaves, cane, cocoons and medicinal plants. The Act gives forest dwellers <b>ownership</b> of it.</li>
<li>The <b>Indian Forest (Amendment) Act, 2017</b> freed bamboo grown in <b>non-forest</b> areas from the definition of "tree". It did <b>not</b> give forest dwellers the right to fell bamboo in forest areas.</li>
<li>Bamboo is botanically a grass. India has the second-largest bamboo resource after China, and the North-East holds two-thirds of the stock.</li>
<li><b>Mendha Lekha</b> (Gadchiroli) was the first village to win community rights over bamboo (2009).</li></ul></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Critical wildlife habitat</b> is defined in the FRA (Section 2(b)). It covers parts of national parks and sanctuaries that must be kept inviolate, determined scientifically in consultation with the Gram Sabha. Critical tiger habitat is defined separately, under the Wild Life Act.</li>
<li><b>Habitat rights:</b> the <b>Baiga</b> of Dindori, MP, became the first community to receive them, in 2016. The Bharia of Patalkot and the Kamar followed.
  <ul><li>Habitat rights come through the FRA process, with the Ministry of Tribal Affairs as nodal ministry. <b>MoEFCC does not declare them</b>.</li></ul></li>
<li>The Forest (Conservation) Amendment Act 2023 (the Van Sanrakshan Evam Samvardhan Adhiniyam) has raised concerns about FRA consent.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p11-2", `
<p>The Constitution protects groups that have historically been excluded: SCs, STs, backward classes, minorities, women and persons with disabilities. It does this through reservation, special commissions and specific rights.</p>

<h3>Safeguards for SCs and STs</h3>
<table><tr><th>Article</th><th>Provision</th></tr>
<tr><td>15(4), 16(4), 16(4A), 16(4B)</td><td>Special provisions; reservation in jobs, promotions, and carry-forward of backlog vacancies</td></tr>
<tr><td>17</td><td>Abolition of untouchability (see 4.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>46</td><td>Promote the educational and economic interests of SCs, STs and weaker sections (a DPSP)</td></tr>
<tr><td>243D, 243T</td><td>Reservation in panchayats and municipalities. <b>Not less than one-third of the seats reserved for SCs and STs are for SC and ST women</b> (243D(2)).</td></tr>
<tr><td>244; Fifth and Sixth Schedules</td><td>Scheduled and tribal areas (see 11.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>275(1)</td><td>Grants for tribal welfare</td></tr>
<tr><td>330, 332, 334</td><td>Reserved seats in the Lok Sabha and Assemblies, extended to 2030 by the 104th Amendment, which also ended the Anglo-Indian nomination</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>335</b></td><td>SC and ST claims to services must be consistent with the <b>efficiency of administration</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>338, 338A</td><td>National Commissions for SCs and for STs (the NCST since the 89th Amendment, 2003)</td></tr>
<tr><td>341, 342</td><td>The President notifies the SC and ST lists; Parliament can modify them</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>Since the 2008 delimitation there are <b>47 ST seats</b> in the Lok Sabha: <b>MP has the most (6)</b>, then Odisha and Jharkhand (5 each). In 1999 undivided MP also led.</li>
<li><b>State of Punjab v. Davinder Singh (2024, seven judges):</b> States may sub-classify SCs for reservation, and the Court suggested a creamy-layer test for SCs and STs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Reservation in services</h3>
<div class="key"><b>Article 335 and efficiency</b>The Supreme Court has held that reservation under Article 16(4) is limited by Article 335, the maintenance of efficiency of administration (Indra Sawhney 1992; M. Nagaraj 2006). But Article 335 does <b>not define</b> "efficiency of administration"; the courts have interpreted it. B.K. Pavitra II (2019) held that inclusion itself improves administration.</div>
<table><tr><th>Amendment or case</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr><td>Indra Sawhney (1992)</td><td>Upheld the 27% OBC quota from the Mandal Commission; excluded the creamy layer; set a 50% ceiling; barred reservation in promotions</td></tr>
<tr><td>77th Amendment (1995)</td><td>Article 16(4A): reservation in promotions for SCs and STs. The 85th (2001) added consequential seniority.</td></tr>
<tr><td>81st Amendment (2000)</td><td>Article 16(4B): carried-forward vacancies sit outside the 50% cap</td></tr>
<tr><td>82nd Amendment (2000)</td><td>Proviso to Article 335: qualifying marks can be relaxed for SCs and STs in promotions</td></tr>
<tr><td>M. Nagaraj (2006); Jarnail Singh (2018)</td><td>Promotion quotas need data on inadequate representation. Backwardness of SCs and STs need not be proved, and the creamy layer applies.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>93rd Amendment (2005)</b> (introduced as the <b>104th Amendment Bill</b>)</td><td>Article 15(5): quotas for SEBCs, SCs and STs in admissions to <b>private educational institutions</b>, except minority institutions. Upheld in Ashoka Kumar Thakur (2008) and Pramati (2014).</td></tr>
<tr><td>103rd Amendment (2019)</td><td>10% EWS quota (Articles 15(6), 16(6)), upheld in Janhit Abhiyan (2022)</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The Central Educational Institutions (Reservation in Admission) Act 2006 gives a 27% OBC quota in IITs, IIMs and central universities.</li>
<li><b>Backward classes commissions:</b> Kaka Kalelkar (1953) and B.P. Mandal (1979).
  <ul><li>The NCBC became constitutional under Article 338B (102nd Amendment, 2018).</li>
  <li>The 105th Amendment (2021) restored States' power to make their own OBC lists.</li>
  <li>The Rohini Commission on OBC sub-categorisation reported in 2023.</li>
  <li>The Constitution does not define backward classes (see 4.4).</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Women's reservation: the Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam</h3>
<p>The <b>106th Amendment (2023)</b> reserves <b>one-third of seats for women</b> in the Lok Sabha, the State Assemblies and the Delhi Assembly.</p>
<ul>
<li>It takes effect <b>only after the first Census after the Act and the delimitation based on it</b>, so <b>not</b> from the 18th Lok Sabha. The Census is under way (house-listing in 2026, enumeration in February 2027).</li>
<li>It lasts <b>15 years</b> from commencement, and Parliament can extend it.</li>
<li><b>One-third of SC and ST seats</b> are reserved for SC and ST women, within those quotas. There is no OBC sub-quota.</li>
<li>It does <b>not</b> cover the Rajya Sabha or Legislative Councils.</li>
<li>New articles: 330A, 332A, 239AA(2)(ba) and 334A. Reserved seats rotate after each delimitation.</li>
<li>It was passed in September 2023 (Lok Sabha 454–2; Rajya Sabha unanimous).</li>
<li>Earlier attempts: the 81st Amendment Bill (1996) and the 108th Amendment Bill (passed by the Rajya Sabha in 2010, then lapsed).</li>
<li>There are 74 women MPs (13.6%) in the 18th Lok Sabha.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Minorities</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Constitution protects both <b>religious and linguistic minorities</b> (Articles 29 and 30).</li>
<li>The <b>National Commission for Minorities</b> became statutory under the NCM Act 1992 and was constituted in 1993.</li>
<li><b>Notified minorities:</b> five in 1993 (Muslims, Christians, Sikhs, Buddhists and Parsis or Zoroastrians), plus <b>Jains in 2014</b>. Parsis (about 57,000 in 2011) are the smallest notified minority, and the Jiyo Parsi scheme (2013) addresses their decline.</li>
<li><b>National minority status gives:</b>
  <ul><li>the right to <b>establish and administer educational institutions</b> (Article 30);</li>
  <li>benefits under the <b>PM's New 15-Point Programme</b> (2006).</li></ul>
  It does <b>not</b> mean the President nominates a member to the Lok Sabha. That applied only to Anglo-Indians (Article 331) and ended in 2020.</li>
<li>Minority status is decided <b>State by State</b> (T.M.A. Pai, 2002). The AMU case (November 2024) laid down tests for minority institutions.</li>
<li>In September 2025 the Supreme Court stayed parts of the Waqf (Amendment) Act 2025, renamed the UMEED Act.</li>
<li><b>Linguistic minorities:</b>
  <ul><li><b>Article 350A</b> (7th Amendment, 1956): instruction in the <b>mother tongue at the primary stage</b> for children of linguistic minorities.</li>
  <li><b>Article 350B</b>: a Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities.</li>
  <li>Article 349 concerns language laws and Article 351 the development of Hindi (see 13.2).</li>
  <li>NEP 2020 recommends mother-tongue teaching at least up to Class 5. The three-language policy became a Tamil Nadu–Centre dispute in 2025.</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Persons with disabilities</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016</b> (in force April 2017) replaced the 1995 Act and is based on the UN CRPD (ratified by India in 2007). It was passed in 2016, not 2018.</li>
<li><b>Recognised disabilities:</b> 21, up from 7. New ones include acid attack, Parkinson's, thalassaemia, autism, dwarfism and specific learning disabilities.</li>
<li><b>Benchmark disability</b> means 40% or more.</li>
<li><b>Reservation:</b> at least 4% in government jobs and 5% in higher education.</li>
<li><b>Free education</b> from 6 to 18 for children with benchmark disabilities.</li>
<li><b>Other entitlements:</b> preferential allotment of land for business and housing, and barrier-free access such as ramps in public buildings.</li>
<li><b>Enforcement:</b> a Chief Commissioner, special courts, and penalties of up to five years in jail.</li>
<li><b>Sugamya Bharat Abhiyan</b> (the Accessible India Campaign, launched 3 December 2015) targets the built environment, transport and ICT.</li>
<li><b>NDFDC</b> (1997) gives concessional loans to persons with disabilities. It is a not-for-profit company under the <b>Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment</b>, not Corporate Affairs.</li>
<li>Other schemes: UDID cards, ADIP (aids and appliances) and DDRS.</li>
<li>Article 41 (a DPSP) and State List Entry 9 cover relief of the disabled.</li>
<li><b>Cases:</b> Vikash Kumar (2021) on reasonable accommodation, and <b>Rajive Raturi (November 2024)</b>, which made accessibility rules mandatory, not optional.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p12.js
// Polity book, Unit 12: Elections and constitutional, statutory and other bodies.
addBookTopic("polity", "p12-1", `
<p>Free and fair elections are part of the basic structure. Part XV (Articles 324–329A) sets up the Election Commission and the framework, and the Representation of the People Acts of 1950 and 1951 fill in the detail.</p>

<h3>The Election Commission of India (Article 324)</h3>
<ul>
<li>The ECI has <b>superintendence, direction and control</b> of elections to Parliament, State legislatures, and the offices of President and Vice-President.
  <ul><li>Panchayat and municipal elections are run by <b>State Election Commissions</b> (Articles 243K, 243ZA).</li></ul></li>
<li>It is a <b>three-member</b> body: the Chief Election Commissioner and two Election Commissioners. It has been multi-member since October 1993, and the President can fix the number. It is not a five-member body.</li>
<li>The <b>Commission itself</b> decides the schedule of general elections and bye-elections; the Home Ministry does not.</li>
<li><b>Gyanesh Kumar</b> has been CEC since February 2025.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>CEC and ECs: service conditions</b><ul>
<li>The CEC and ECs have <b>equal powers</b> and <b>equal salaries</b>, equal to a <b>Supreme Court judge's</b>. The 2023 Act dropped a proposal to equate them with the Cabinet Secretary.</li>
<li>The <b>CEC</b> can be removed only <b>like a Supreme Court judge</b> (Article 324(5)). An EC can be removed only on the CEC's recommendation.</li>
<li>The term is <b>six years or until age 65</b>, not five years or 62.</li>
<li><b>Appointment:</b> under the CEC and Other ECs Act, 2023, by a selection committee of the <b>PM, the Leader of the Opposition and a Union Cabinet Minister</b>. This replaced the CJI, who had been placed on the panel by Anoop Baranwal (March 2023). Challenges are pending.</li></ul></div>
<h4>Functions</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Superintendence, direction and conduct</b> of elections.</li>
<li><b>Preparing electoral rolls</b>. The Bihar Special Intensive Revision (2025), later extended to more States, was challenged in the Supreme Court.</li>
<li><b>Recognising parties and allotting symbols</b> under the Election Symbols (Reservation and Allotment) Order, 1968.</li>
<li>Deciding <b>disputes over splits and mergers</b> of recognised parties (Paragraph 15 of the Symbols Order), as with Shiv Sena and NCP in 2023–24.</li>
<li>The Model Code of Conduct.</li>
<li>Advising the President or Governor on disqualifying legislators (Articles 103, 192).</li>
</ul>
<p>The ECI does <b>not</b> give the final verdict on <b>election disputes</b>. Election petitions go to the <b>High Court</b>, with an appeal to the Supreme Court. Presidential and Vice-Presidential disputes go straight to the Supreme Court (Article 71).</p>

<h3>The electoral system</h3>
<table><tr><th>System</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>First-past-the-post</b> (simple plurality)</td><td>Lok Sabha, State Assemblies, local bodies. The winner needs only <b>the most votes</b>, <b>not 50%</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proportional representation by single transferable vote</td><td>President, Vice-President, Rajya Sabha, Legislative Councils</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The Constitution does <b>not</b> say the Speaker's post goes to the majority party and the Deputy Speaker's to the opposition. That is a convention (see 6.2).</li>
<li>The right to vote and NOTA are covered in 1.1.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Candidates</h3>
<ul>
<li>Any <b>citizen whose name appears on the electoral roll of any constituency</b> can file nomination papers for the Lok Sabha (Section 4(d) of RPA 1951). They need not live in the constituency, but for reserved seats they must belong to the SC or ST.</li>
<li><b>Article 84:</b> a citizen, who takes the oath, and is at least 25 for the Lok Sabha or 30 for the Rajya Sabha.
  <ul><li>The domicile requirement for the Rajya Sabha was removed in 2003; this, and open ballot for Rajya Sabha elections, were upheld in Kuldip Nayar (2006).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Proposers:</b> one for a recognised party's candidate, ten for others.</li>
<li>Candidates must file affidavits disclosing criminal records, assets and education (ADR 2002; PUCL 2003).</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Security deposit</th><th>General</th><th>SC/ST</th></tr>
<tr><td>Lok Sabha in 2002</td><td><b>₹10,000</b></td><td><b>₹5,000</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Lok Sabha since 2009</td><td>₹25,000</td><td>₹12,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assembly since 2009</td><td>₹10,000</td><td>₹5,000</td></tr></table>
<p>A candidate who wins less than one-sixth of the valid votes forfeits the deposit; about 86% of Lok Sabha candidates did so in 2024.</p>
<h4>Contesting from more than one seat</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Section 33(7) of the RPA</b> (1996) limits a candidate to <b>two</b> constituencies. In 2023 the Supreme Court refused to strike it down.</li>
<li><b>Devi Lal</b> contested three seats in <b>1989</b>, not 1991.</li>
<li>No rule makes the party pay for bye-elections. The EC has proposed a cost deposit (₹5 lakh for an Assembly seat, ₹10 lakh for the Lok Sabha).</li>
<li>The 2021 question was dropped from scoring because all its statements were wrong.</li>
<li><b>Vacating seats:</b>
  <ul><li>A person elected to both Houses must choose within 10 days, or the Rajya Sabha seat falls vacant.</li>
  <li>Someone who wins two Lok Sabha seats must resign one within 14 days.</li>
  <li>Someone elected to both Parliament and a State legislature must resign the State seat within 14 days.</li>
  <li>Examples: Modi in 2014 (Varanasi, Vadodara); Rahul Gandhi in 2019 and 2024.</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Delimitation</h3>
<ul>
<li>Delimitation is the <b>redrawing of constituency boundaries</b> after a Census, under Articles 82 and 170.</li>
<li><b>Four Delimitation Commissions</b> have been set up: <b>1952, 1963, 1973 and 2002</b> (the last chaired by Justice Kuldip Singh, using the 2001 Census, which changed boundaries but not seat numbers).</li>
<li><b>Composition:</b> a retired Supreme Court judge, the CEC or an EC, and State Election Commissioners.</li>
<li>Its orders have the force of law and <b>cannot be challenged in court</b> (Article 329(a); Meghraj Kothari, 1967).
  <ul><li>In Kishorchandra Rathod (July 2024) the Court held that manifestly arbitrary orders can still face limited review.</li>
  <li>When the orders are laid before the Lok Sabha or an Assembly, those Houses <b>cannot modify</b> them.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>The seat freeze:</b> the 42nd Amendment froze seat numbers on the 1971 Census until 2001, and the <b>84th Amendment</b> extended this until the first Census after 2026.</li>
<li><b>Census 2027</b> opens the way for a fresh delimitation and the start of women's reservation (see 11.2). Southern States fear losing seats.</li>
<li>Separate exercises: Jammu and Kashmir (2020–22, Justice Ranjana Desai, 90 seats) and Assam (2023, by the EC).</li>
<li><b>Largest constituency by area: Ladakh</b> (about 1.7 lakh sq km including occupied areas), larger than Barmer or Kachchh. The smallest is Chandni Chowk. Malkajgiri has the largest electorate, and Lakshadweep the smallest.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Political parties</h3>
<ul>
<li>Parties are <b>registered</b> by the <b>Election Commission</b> under <b>Section 29A of the RPA 1951</b> (added 1989).</li>
<li><b>National party criteria today:</b> any one of
  <ul><li>6% of votes in four States plus four Lok Sabha seats;</li>
  <li>2% of Lok Sabha seats from three States;</li>
  <li>State-party status in four States.</li></ul>
  Earlier, recognition in four or more States was enough.</li>
<li><b>National parties (2025):</b> BJP, INC, BSP, CPI(M), AAP and NPP.</li>
<li>Electoral bonds were struck down in February 2024.</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Party</th><th>Founded</th><th>Founder or leader</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bharatiya Jana Sangh</b></td><td>1951</td><td><b>Syama Prasad Mookerjee</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Socialist Party (from the Congress Socialist Party, 1934)</td><td>1948</td><td><b>Acharya Narendra Dev</b>, Jayaprakash Narayan, Ram Manohar Lohia. Not Rajagopalachari.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kisan Mazdoor Praja Party</td><td>1951</td><td>J.B. Kripalani; it merged into the Praja Socialist Party (1952)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Swatantra Party</b></td><td>1959</td><td><b>C. Rajagopalachari</b> (with Minoo Masani, N.G. Ranga and K.M. Munshi); pro-market. Not Narendra Dev.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CPI(M)</td><td>1964</td><td>Split from the CPI</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Congress for Democracy</b></td><td>February 1977</td><td><b>Jagjivan Ram</b> (with H.N. Bahuguna and Nandini Satpathy); merged into the Janata Party</td></tr>
<tr><td>Janata Party</td><td>1977</td><td>A merger; PM Morarji Desai</td></tr>
<tr><td>BJP</td><td>1980</td><td>A.B. Vajpayee (its first president)</td></tr></table>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p12-2", `
<p>India has many independent bodies. Some are created by the <b>Constitution</b>, some by <b>statute</b>, and some only by <b>executive resolution</b>. UPSC repeatedly asks which is which.</p>

<h3>Classifying the bodies</h3>
<table><tr><th>Constitutional</th><th>Statutory</th><th>Executive (non-constitutional, non-statutory)</th></tr>
<tr><td>
<ul>
<li><b>Election Commission</b> (324)</li>
<li>UPSC and SPSCs (315)</li>
<li>Finance Commission (280)</li>
<li>CAG (148)</li>
<li>Attorney General (76)</li>
<li>GST Council (279A)</li>
<li>NCSC (338), NCST (338A), <b>NCBC (338B, since 2018)</b></li>
<li>Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities (350B)</li>
<li><b>Inter-State Council</b> (263)</li>
<li>State Election Commissions (243K)</li>
</ul>
</td><td>
<ul>
<li><b>NHRC</b> (1993 Act)</li>
<li>CVC (2003 Act)</li>
<li><b>Lokpal</b> (2013 Act)</li>
<li>NGT (2010)</li>
<li>NCW (1990)</li>
<li>National Commission for Minorities (1992)</li>
<li>NCPCR (2005)</li>
<li>NDMA (2005)</li>
<li><b>Zonal Councils</b> (1956)</li>
<li>North Eastern Council (1971)</li>
<li>NIA (2008)</li>
<li>UGC (1956 Act)</li>
<li><b>NCDRC</b> (Consumer Protection Act)</li>
</ul>
</td><td>
<ul>
<li><b>NITI Aayog</b> (2015)</li>
<li>the former <b>Planning Commission</b> (1950)</li>
<li><b>National Development Council</b> (1952)</li>
<li><b>National Security Council</b> (1998)</li>
<li><b>Law Commission</b></li>
<li>CBI (1963 resolution; it draws its powers from the DSPE Act 1946)</li>
<li>National Commission for Safai Karamcharis (non-statutory since 2004)</li>
<li>National Water Resources Council</li>
</ul>
</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>Of the NCBC, NHRC, Law Commission and NCDRC, <b>only the NCBC</b> is constitutional.</li>
<li>Of the UGC, NHRC, Election Commission and CVC, <b>only the Election Commission</b> was set up under an Article of the Constitution.</li>
<li>The <b>National Development Council, Planning Commission and Zonal Councils</b> are <b>not mentioned</b> in the Constitution.</li>
<li>Of the Inter-State Council, National Security Council and Zonal Councils, only the Inter-State Council is constitutional (see 9.4).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Comptroller and Auditor General (Articles 148–151)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Appointed by the <b>President</b>, for six years or until age 65, and removed like a Supreme Court judge.</li>
<li>The current CAG is <b>K. Sanjay Murthy</b> (since 21 November 2024).</li>
<li>Ambedkar called the CAG "probably the most important officer in the Constitution". Duties are set by the DPC Act 1971.</li>
<li>Reports on Union accounts go to the <b>President</b>, who lays them before Parliament (Article 151); State reports go to the Governor. The PAC examines them (see 6.5).</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>The CAG is only an auditor</b><ul>
<li>The CAG <b>audits and reports</b> on all expenditure from the Consolidated Fund, the Contingency Funds and Public Accounts, and on trading, manufacturing and profit-and-loss accounts.</li>
<li>The CAG does <b>not control the receipt and issue of public money</b>; the UK Comptroller does. There is <b>no exchequer control</b>, even during an emergency.</li>
<li>The CAG has <b>no judicial or prosecuting powers</b>.</li>
<li>CAG reports <b>are discussed by the PAC</b>, and investigating agencies <b>can use their findings</b>, as in the 2G, coal-block and Commonwealth Games cases.</li></ul></div>

<h3>The NHRC</h3>
<ul>
<li>Set up under the <b>Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993</b>.</li>
<li>Under the original Act, the Chairperson had to be <b>only a retired Chief Justice of India</b>, not a serving judge or a High Court chief justice.</li>
<li>The <b>2019 amendment</b> allows a retired CJI <b>or a retired Supreme Court judge</b>, for three years or until age 70, with re-appointment allowed.</li>
<li>Appointment is by the President on a committee chaired by the PM.</li>
<li>The chairperson since December 2024 is <b>Justice V. Ramasubramanian</b>.</li>
<li>Its "A" status with GANHRI was deferred in 2023–24.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Lokpal</h3>
<p>The Lokpal was set up under the <b>Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act, 2013</b>, in force from 16 January 2014. The first appointment was made in 2019.</p>
<div class="key"><b>2025: only statement III was correct</b><ul>
<li>Its jurisdiction covers public servants <b>in India and outside India</b> (Section 14).</li>
<li>MPs and MLAs cannot be members. But the Chairperson may be a <b>former CJI, a former Supreme Court judge, or an eminent person</b> with 25 years' relevant expertise, not only the CJI.
  <ul><li>The first chairperson was Justice Pinaki Chandra Ghose (2019), and the current one is Justice A.M. Khanwilkar (2024).</li></ul></li>
<li>Members must be <b>at least 45 years old</b> on taking office, and serve five years or until age 70.</li>
<li>The <b>PM is covered</b>, with safeguards:
  <ul><li>no inquiry into international relations, security, public order, atomic energy or space;</li>
  <li>an inquiry needs a full bench and two-thirds approval, held in camera.</li></ul></li></ul></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Composition:</b> a Chairperson and up to eight members. Half must be judicial, and half from SCs, STs, OBCs, minorities and women.</li>
<li><b>Selection committee:</b> the PM, the Lok Sabha Speaker, the Leader of the Opposition, the CJI or a judge nominated by the CJI, and an eminent jurist.</li>
<li><b>Jurisdiction</b> covers ministers, MPs, Group A–D central officers, and NGOs receiving over ₹10 lakh of foreign donations. It has inquiry and prosecution wings.</li>
<li><b>History:</b> the term was coined by L.M. Singhvi (1963), the first bill came in 1968, the First ARC recommended it, and the Anna Hazare movement (2011) pushed it through.</li>
<li><b>Lokayuktas:</b> Odisha passed the first law (1970) and Maharashtra set up the first office (1971).</li>
<li>In 2025 the Supreme Court stayed a Lokpal order bringing High Court judges within its reach.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Planning bodies</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>NITI Aayog</b> (National Institution for Transforming India) was set up on <b>1 January 2015</b> by Cabinet resolution, <b>replacing the Planning Commission</b> (1950). It did not replace the Finance Commission, the Law Commission or the Human Rights Commission.
  <ul><li>It is a think tank with no power to allocate funds.</li>
  <li>The PM is its Chairperson, its Governing Council includes all CMs and LGs, and a Vice-Chairperson and CEO run it day to day.</li>
  <li>Its work includes the SDG India Index, the Aspirational Districts and Blocks programme, the Atal Innovation Mission and the Fiscal Health Index.</li>
  <li>The Governing Council held its 10th meeting in May 2025 and its 11th in June 2026, on "Viksit Bharat @2047".</li></ul></li>
<li><b>National Development Council</b> (1952, by executive resolution) approved the Five-Year Plans and was called a "super cabinet".
  <ul><li><b>Members:</b> the <b>PM</b>, all <b>Union Cabinet ministers</b>, all <b>Chief Ministers</b>, and Planning Commission members.</li>
  <li>The <b>Finance Commission chairman</b> was <b>not</b> a member, and it was <b>not</b> made up only of the Finance Minister and CMs.</li>
  <li>It was <b>not an organ of the Planning Commission</b>; the Commission served as its secretariat.</li>
  <li>It has not met since 2012, and NITI's Governing Council has effectively replaced it.</li></ul></li>
<li>"<b>Economic and social planning</b>" is a <b>Concurrent List</b> subject (Entry 20). The Constitution lets States entrust <b>panchayats</b> with preparing plans for economic development and social justice (Article 243G).</li>
<li>The Planning Commission was <b>never accountable to Parliament</b>.</li>
<li>Five-Year Plans ended with the 12th (2012–17).</li>
<li>The <b>National Water Resources Council</b> (1983) is chaired by the <b>Prime Minister</b>. It adopted the National Water Policies of 1987, 2002 and 2012.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Other bodies at a glance</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Law Commission:</b> executive, set up for fixed terms. The first after independence was in 1955 under M.C. Setalvad, and the 23rd runs 2024–27.</li>
<li><b>NCDRC:</b> statutory and quasi-judicial (see 7.4).</li>
<li><b>National Commissions for SCs and STs:</b> separated by the 89th Amendment (2003). The NCBC has been constitutional since the 102nd Amendment (2018).</li>
<li>The <b>minimum age for Supreme Court judges</b> is not prescribed (see 7.1). <b>Ordinances</b> can be issued when either House is not in session (see 5.1).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p13.js
// Polity book, Unit 13: Amending the Constitution and key schedules.
addBookTopic("polity", "p13-1", `
<p>India's Constitution is <b>partly rigid and partly flexible</b>. Article 368 sets the amendment procedure, the basic structure doctrine limits what amendments can do, and over 100 amendments show how the document has changed.</p>

<h3>Article 368</h3>
<p>Article 368(1), added by the 24th Amendment (1971), says Parliament may, in exercise of its constituent power, "amend by way of <b>addition, variation or repeal</b> any provision of this Constitution". All three methods are allowed.</p>
<div class="key"><b>Procedure</b><ul>
<li>An amendment bill can be introduced <b>in either House</b>, by a minister or a <b>private member</b>, so not only in the Lok Sabha.</li>
<li>It needs <b>no prior recommendation of the President</b>.</li>
<li>Each House must pass it <b>separately by special majority</b>: a majority of the total membership <b>and</b> two-thirds of members present and voting.</li>
<li>There is <b>no joint sitting</b> if the Houses disagree.</li>
<li>The President <b>must give assent</b> and cannot withhold it or return the bill (24th Amendment).</li>
<li>There is no time limit for State ratification, and no prior State permission is needed.</li></ul>
A Constitution Amendment Bill is the only kind that must pass each House separately by special majority. Ordinary, Money and Finance bills do not.</div>

<h3>Three ways to amend</h3>
<table><tr><th>Method</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Simple majority</b> of Parliament (outside Article 368)</td><td>Admission or creation of States (Articles 2–4), quorum, salaries and the Second Schedule, citizenship (Article 11), delimitation, use of English in Parliament, the Fifth and Sixth Schedules, Legislative Councils (Article 169), UTs</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Special majority</b></td><td>Most provisions, including Fundamental Rights and DPSPs</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Special majority plus ratification by at least half the State legislatures</b>, by simple majority (proviso to Article 368(2))</td><td>Federal provisions: the President's election (Articles 54, 55); the <b>extent of Union and State executive power (Articles 73, 162)</b>; the Supreme Court and High Courts; High Courts for UTs (Article 241); the distribution of legislative powers; <b>any List in the Seventh Schedule</b>; the GST Council (279A); States' representation in Parliament; Article 368 itself</td></tr></table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common traps</b><ul>
<li>Federal amendments need ratification by <b>half</b> the States, not <b>all</b> of them.</li>
<li>Conditions of the <b>Governor's office</b> (Article 158) do <b>not</b> need State ratification. So of the Union List, the extent of State executive power and the Governor's office, only the first two do.</li></ul></div>
<p>Amendments that were ratified by the States include the <b>101st</b> (GST, 2016) and the <b>99th</b> (NJAC, 2014), which was later struck down.</p>

<h3>Limits: the basic structure</h3>
<table><tr><th>Case or amendment</th><th>Holding</th></tr>
<tr><td>Shankari Prasad (1951), Sajjan Singh (1965)</td><td>Parliament can amend Fundamental Rights</td></tr>
<tr><td>Golaknath (1967)</td><td>It cannot</td></tr>
<tr><td>24th Amendment (1971)</td><td>Restored the power</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kesavananda Bharati (1973, 13 judges, 7:6)</b></td><td>Parliament can amend any part, but <b>not the basic structure</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Minerva Mills (1980)</td><td>Struck down the 42nd Amendment's clauses 368(4)–(5), which tried to make amendments immune</td></tr>
<tr><td>Waman Rao (1981)</td><td>The basic structure test applies to amendments after 24 April 1973</td></tr>
<tr><td>I.R. Coelho (2007)</td><td>Ninth Schedule laws added after that date can be reviewed (see 13.2)</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Elements of the basic structure:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>supremacy of the Constitution;</li>
<li>republican, democratic and parliamentary government;</li>
<li>secularism and federalism;</li>
<li>separation of powers and judicial review;</li>
<li>the rule of law;</li>
<li>free and fair elections;</li>
<li>judicial independence;</li>
<li>limited amending power;</li>
<li>harmony between Fundamental Rights and DPSPs;</li>
<li>the welfare state;</li>
<li>unity and integrity.</li>
</ul>
<p>See 7.2 for the judicial review side.</p>

<h3>Amendments that overrode court rulings</h3>
<p>The <b>1st Amendment (1951)</b> is the classic case of an amendment enacted to overcome judicial interpretations of Fundamental Rights. UPSC dropped the 2023 question on this, because the 42nd also fits. The 1st Amendment:</p>
<ul>
<li>added "public order", "friendly relations with foreign States", "incitement to an offence" and the word "<b>reasonable</b>" to Article 19(2), answering Romesh Thappar and Brij Bhushan (1950);</li>
<li>added <b>Article 15(4)</b>, answering Champakam Dorairajan (1951);</li>
<li>added Articles 31A and 31B and the <b>Ninth Schedule</b> to save zamindari abolition laws.</li>
</ul>
<p>It was passed by the Provisional Parliament under Nehru.</p>
<table><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>Overrode</th></tr>
<tr><td>4th (1955)</td><td>Bela Banerjee (compensation)</td></tr>
<tr><td>17th (1964)</td><td>Karimbil Kunhikoman</td></tr>
<tr><td>24th, 25th (1971)</td><td>Golaknath; R.C. Cooper (bank nationalisation)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>39th (1975)</b></td><td>The Allahabad High Court's verdict on Indira Gandhi's election. It inserted <b>Article 329A</b>, putting the PM's and Speaker's elections beyond courts. Struck down in part in Indira Gandhi v. Raj Narain; repealed by the 44th.</td></tr>
<tr><td>42nd (1976)</td><td>Kesavananda; it tried to bar review of amendments, and was struck down in Minerva Mills</td></tr>
<tr><td>77th, 81st, 82nd, 85th</td><td>Indra Sawhney (reservation in promotions, backlog, relaxations)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>99th (2014)</b></td><td>The collegium (the NJAC), <b>struck down in October 2015</b> as violating judicial independence</td></tr>
<tr><td>102nd, 105th</td><td>The Maratha reservation verdict; States' power to identify SEBCs was restored</td></tr></table>
<p>The <b>44th Amendment</b> did <b>not</b> put the PM's election beyond judicial review; the 39th did, and the 44th repealed it.</p>
<p>The four Judges cases: S.P. Gupta (1981); the Second (1993), which created the collegium; the Third (1998), which expanded it to the CJI plus four judges; and the Fourth (2015), which struck down the NJAC.</p>

<h3>Landmark amendments</h3>
<table><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>Change</th></tr>
<tr><td>1st (1951)</td><td>Ninth Schedule, Articles 15(4), 31A, 31B; reasonable restrictions</td></tr>
<tr><td>7th (1956)</td><td>Reorganised States; abolished Part A/B/C/D States</td></tr>
<tr><td>24th (1971)</td><td>Parliament can amend any part; Presidential assent made compulsory</td></tr>
<tr><td>42nd (1976)</td><td>The "mini-Constitution": socialist, secular, integrity; Fundamental Duties; tribunals; new DPSPs</td></tr>
<tr><td>44th (1978)</td><td>Property removed from Part III; "armed rebellion"; Articles 20–21 cannot be suspended</td></tr>
<tr><td>52nd (1985)</td><td>Anti-defection (Tenth Schedule)</td></tr>
<tr><td>61st (1988)</td><td>Voting age lowered to 18</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>69th (1991)</b></td><td><b>Delhi made the National Capital Territory</b>, with a legislature (Articles 239AA, 239AB)</td></tr>
<tr><td>73rd, 74th (1992)</td><td>Panchayats and municipalities</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>75th (1994)</b></td><td><b>State-level rent tribunals</b> (Article 323B)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>80th (2000)</b></td><td>Accepted the <b>Tenth Finance Commission's</b> scheme of tax devolution: all central taxes in a divisible pool</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>83rd (2000)</b></td><td><b>No SC reservation in Arunachal Pradesh panchayats</b> (there is no SC population)</td></tr>
<tr><td>86th (2002)</td><td>Article 21A, the right to education</td></tr>
<tr><td>91st (2003)</td><td>Ministry size capped at 15%</td></tr>
<tr><td>101st (2016)</td><td>GST</td></tr>
<tr><td>103rd (2019)</td><td>10% EWS quota</td></tr>
<tr><td>106th (2023)</td><td>Women's reservation, the latest amendment</td></tr></table>
<p>Panchayats as such came through the 73rd Amendment, not the 75th or 80th. The GNCTD (Amendment) Act 2023 gave the Centre control over Delhi's services after the Supreme Court's May 2023 ruling.</p>
<p><b>Pending bills:</b> the Constitution (129th Amendment) Bill on "One Nation, One Election" and the 130th Amendment Bill on removing ministers held in custody for 30 days. Both went to joint committees, and no amendment has been passed since the 106th, as of October 2026.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p13-2", `
<p>Two Schedules come up again and again: the <b>Eighth</b> (languages) and the <b>Ninth</b> (laws protected from challenge). This topic also covers the official-language provisions of Part XVII.</p>

<h3>The Eighth Schedule: languages</h3>
<table><tr><th>When</th><th>Languages added</th><th>Total</th></tr>
<tr><td>Original (1950)</td><td>Assamese, Bengali, Gujarati, Hindi, Kannada, Kashmiri, Malayalam, Marathi, Oriya, Punjabi, Sanskrit, Tamil, Telugu, Urdu</td><td>14</td></tr>
<tr><td>21st Amendment (1967)</td><td>Sindhi</td><td>15</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>71st Amendment (1992)</b></td><td><b>Konkani, Manipuri, Nepali</b></td><td>18</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>92nd Amendment (2003)</b></td><td><b>Bodo, Dogri, Maithili, Santhali</b></td><td><b>22</b></td></tr></table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b><b>Maithili</b> came with the <b>92nd</b> Amendment, not the 71st. It was the 92nd, not the 90th, 91st or 93rd, that raised the number to 22.</div>
<ul>
<li>The <b>96th Amendment (2011)</b> changed "Oriya" to "Odia".</li>
<li>About 38 languages are seeking inclusion, among them Bhojpuri, Rajasthani, Tulu, Ho and Kodava.</li>
<li>The Constitution was released in Santhali (in the Ol Chiki script) in 2025.</li>
<li><b>Classical languages</b> (designated by the Ministry of Culture): Tamil (2004, the first), Sanskrit (2005), Telugu and Kannada (2008), Malayalam (2013), Odia (2014), and in October 2024 Marathi, Pali, Prakrit, Assamese and Bengali, making 11 in all.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Official language (Part XVII)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 343:</b> the official language of the Union is <b>Hindi in Devanagari</b>. English continues alongside it under the <b>Official Languages Act, 1963</b>.</li>
<li><b>Article 344:</b> an Official Language Commission and Committee.</li>
<li>Articles 345–347: official languages of the States.</li>
<li>Article 348: English in the higher courts and in laws.</li>
<li><b>Article 349:</b> special procedure for language laws.</li>
<li><b>Article 350:</b> the right to submit representations in any language.</li>
<li><b>Article 350A:</b> instruction in the <b>mother tongue at the primary stage</b> (see 11.2).</li>
<li>Article 350B: the Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities.</li>
<li><b>Article 351:</b> the directive for developing Hindi.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Ninth Schedule</h3>
<ul>
<li>It was added by the <b>First Amendment (1951)</b>, together with Article 31B, during the prime ministership of <b>Jawaharlal Nehru</b>, not by the 8th, 9th or 42nd Amendment, and not under Shastri, Indira Gandhi or Morarji Desai.</li>
<li>Its purpose was to shield <b>land reform laws</b> (zamindari abolition) from challenge for violating Fundamental Rights.</li>
<li>It began with 13 laws and now has about 284.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Is the Ninth Schedule immune?</b><ul>
<li><b>Parliament can place a law in the Ninth Schedule</b> by a constitutional amendment, a true statement.</li>
<li>Such laws are <b>not</b> fully beyond the courts. In <b>I.R. Coelho (2007, nine judges)</b> the Court held that laws added <b>after 24 April 1973</b>, the date of the Kesavananda judgment, can be <b>reviewed if they damage the basic structure</b>, including the rights in Articles 14, 19 and 21.</li>
<li><b>Waman Rao (1981):</b> laws added before that date stay protected.</li></ul></div>
<ul>
<li>Example: Tamil Nadu's 69% reservation law, placed in the Schedule by the <b>76th Amendment (1994)</b>.</li>
<li>Related saving articles: <b>31A</b> (agrarian reform and acquisition) and <b>31C</b> (laws giving effect to Article 39(b) and (c)).</li>
<li>Other Schedules: the Tenth (anti-defection, 1985), Eleventh (panchayats, 29 subjects) and Twelfth (municipalities, 18 subjects). All twelve Schedules are listed in 2.2.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_polity_p14.js
// Polity book, Unit 14: Governance, welfare and general polity.
addBookTopic("polity", "p14-1", `
<p>Governance is about how the State actually works day to day: its ministries and departments, its civil services, its investigating and vigilance bodies, and the ethics expected of officials. Recent papers (2026) add case-study questions in the style of GS-IV.</p>

<h3>The bureaucracy</h3>
<ul>
<li>The defining characteristic of the bureaucracy in India is that it is <b>an agency for implementing public policy</b>. It carries out the laws and policies made by the political executive and the legislature, advises ministers, and provides continuity.
  <ul><li>Widening democracy, strengthening federalism, or delivering stability and growth are effects or ideals, not its defining role.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Max Weber's ideal bureaucracy:</b> hierarchy, rules, impersonality, merit, specialisation and written records.</li>
<li><b>In the Constitution:</b> Part XIV (Articles 308–323).
  <ul><li>Article 311: safeguards against dismissal without inquiry.</li>
  <li>Article 312: All India Services, which can be created by a Rajya Sabha resolution (see 6.5).</li>
  <li>Article 315: Public Service Commissions.</li></ul></li>
<li>Sardar Patel called the All India Services the "<b>steel frame</b>" of India.</li>
<li><b>Reforms:</b> Mission Karmayogi (2020), lateral entry (paused in 2024), and the Jan Vishwas Acts (2023, with a second bill in 2025) decriminalising minor offences.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Administrative reform bodies</h3>
<table><tr><th>Body</th><th>Year</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
<tr><td>Gopalaswamy Ayyangar report</td><td>1949</td><td>Reorganising the machinery of government; it led to the Organisation and Methods division (1954). It did <b>not</b> propose a dedicated minister and secretary for administrative reform.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A.D. Gorwala report</td><td>1951</td><td>Public administration</td></tr>
<tr><td>Paul Appleby reports</td><td>1953, 1956</td><td>The O&amp;M division; the IIPA (1954)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Santhanam Committee</td><td>1962–64</td><td>Corruption; led to the CVC (1964) and the CBI</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>First Administrative Reforms Commission</b></td><td>1966</td><td>Chaired by Morarji Desai, then K. Hanumanthaiya; 20 reports; proposed the Lokpal and Lokayukta. On its advice the <b>Department of Personnel</b> was created in <b>1970</b> in the Cabinet Secretariat, <b>under the Prime Minister</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Second Administrative Reforms Commission</b></td><td>2005</td><td>Chaired by <b>M. Veerappa Moily</b>, later V. Ramachandran; 15 reports, including on RTI, <b>ethics in governance</b>, public order and e-governance. It was not about police, tax or technical-education reform.</td></tr></table>
<p>Today the Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions (DoPT, DARPG, Pensions) is held by the PM.</p>

<h3>Departments and their ministries</h3>
<table><tr><th>Department or body</th><th>Ministry</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Department of Atomic Energy</b> (1954), also Space and Personnel</td><td><b>Under the Prime Minister</b>. The Chairman of the Atomic Energy Commission is the DAE Secretary.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Department of Border Management</b> (2004, after the Group of Ministers' report)</td><td><b>Home Affairs</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Department of Official Language</td><td>Home Affairs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Department of Women and Child Development (in 2004)</td><td>HRD; a separate Ministry from January 2006</td></tr>
<tr><td>Department of Drinking Water Supply (in 2004)</td><td>Rural Development; now part of Jal Shakti (2019)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Food and Nutrition Board</b></td><td>The Department of WCD, part of the <b>Ministry of HRD</b> in 2005; now under the Ministry of WCD</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>National Automotive Board</b></td><td><b>Heavy Industries</b> (it runs FAME, PM E-DRIVE and PLI-Auto)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Coir Board</b> (Kochi), KVIC</td><td><b>MSME</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>National Centre for Trade Information</b></td><td><b>Commerce and Industry</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Tea, Coffee, Rubber, Spices and Tobacco Boards; APEDA, MPEDA; National Turmeric Board</td><td>Commerce and Industry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Central Silk Board, National Jute Board</td><td>Textiles</td></tr>
<tr><td>NDDB (Anand)</td><td>Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bureau of Energy Efficiency</td><td>Power</td></tr></table>
<p>None of the 2004 department–ministry pairs and none of the 2025 board–ministry pairs were correct.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>DAE units:</b> BARC, NPCIL, BHAVINI (the PFBR), the Heavy Water Board and UCIL.</li>
<li>The SHANTI Act 2025 opened nuclear power to private firms, and the Nuclear Energy Mission targets 100 GW by 2047.</li>
<li>The <b>Department of Border Management</b> handles fencing, border roads, Integrated Check Posts (run by the Land Ports Authority, 2012) and border-area development.
  <ul><li>The Vibrant Villages Programme began in 2023, with a second phase (VVP-II) in 2025.</li>
  <li>The border guarding forces are the BSF, ITBP, SSB and Assam Rifles.</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Investigating and economic agencies</h3>
<table><tr><th>Agency</th><th>Ministry</th><th>Function</th></tr>
<tr><td>Central Economic Intelligence Bureau (1985)</td><td><b>Finance</b> (Department of Revenue), <b>not Home Affairs</b></td><td>Coordinates economic intelligence among the DRI, ED, CBDT, CBIC and others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Serious Fraud Investigation Office (2003; statutory under Section 211 of the Companies Act 2013)</td><td><b>Corporate Affairs</b>, <b>not Finance</b></td><td>Complex corporate frauds</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>CBI</b> (1963)</td><td><b>Personnel</b> (DoPT)</td><td>Its mission includes "to preserve values in public life and to ensure the health of the national economy". This was the only correct row in 2026.</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>CBI</b> was set up by a Home Ministry resolution (1 April 1963) on the Santhanam Committee's advice. It draws its powers from the <b>DSPE Act 1946</b> and is <b>not a statutory body</b>.
  <ul><li>It needs <b>State consent</b> (Section 6) to investigate in a State, and several States have withdrawn general consent. Courts can order CBI probes without consent.</li>
  <li>Its Director is chosen by the PM, the Leader of the Opposition and the CJI, for two years, extendable to five.</li>
  <li>Vineet Narain (1997) gave the CVC superintendence over it.</li>
  <li>The CBI is India's INTERPOL nodal agency.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Home Guards</b> are raised under <b>State</b> Home Guards Acts and Rules, not a central Act.
  <ul><li>They serve as an <b>auxiliary to the police</b> in internal security and emergencies.</li>
  <li><b>Border Wing Home Guards</b> battalions in border States support the BSF against infiltration.</li>
  <li>They were first raised in Bombay in 1946 and formalised after 1962.</li>
  <li>Civil Defence comes under the Civil Defence Act 1968; nationwide mock drills were held in May 2025.</li>
  <li>Police is a State subject. The Central Armed Police Forces (CRPF, BSF, ITBP, SSB, CISF, Assam Rifles, NSG) come under the MHA.</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Governance ethics: the 2026 case studies</h3>
<div class="key"><b>Accountability</b>An officer who finds a private vaccine distributor cutting quality for profit, reports it to vigilance and halts the contract despite pressure is demonstrating <b>accountability</b>: being answerable for public resources to superiors, the law and citizens.<ul>
<li><b>Esprit de corps</b> (team spirit; one of Fayol's 14 principles) does not fit, and he went against internal pressure anyway.</li>
<li><b>Equity</b> (fairness) and <b>delegation</b> (passing authority downward) are not the point.</li></ul></div>
<p><b>Accountability mechanisms in India:</b></p>
<ul>
<li><b>Political:</b> collective responsibility, Question Hour, and committees.</li>
<li><b>Financial:</b> the CAG.</li>
<li><b>Vigilance:</b> the CVC (CVC Act 2003) and its Chief Vigilance Officers, the Lokpal and Lokayuktas, and the CBI.</li>
<li><b>Legal:</b> the Prevention of Corruption Act 1988. Its 2018 amendment made bribe-giving an offence and added Section 17A, requiring prior sanction to investigate officials.</li>
<li><b>Citizen-centric:</b> the RTI Act 2005, the Whistle Blowers Protection Act 2014 (not yet operational), Citizen's Charters, social audits and CPGRAMS.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Confidential, unproven information</b>Ms X learns, in confidence, of unproven allegations against a shortlisted contractor, and her superior tells her to stay silent. The right course is <b>limited disclosure to an oversight committee</b>, keeping it from the public for now.<ul>
<li><b>Immediate public disclosure</b> could defame the contractor and breach confidence.</li>
<li><b>Removing the contractor</b> on unproven allegations violates due process (<i>audi alteram partem</i>).</li></ul>
The principles in play: transparency versus confidentiality, the presumption of innocence, integrity (not following an unethical instruction) and prudence. The Nolan principles of public life are selflessness, integrity, objectivity, accountability, openness, honesty and leadership. In India the All India Services (Conduct) Rules 1968 apply, and the CVC's PIDPI resolution (2004) provides a whistle-blowing channel.</div>
<div class="key"><b>Conflict resolution: the land dispute</b>A tribal community opposes a waste facility near land it holds sacred, while urban residents support it. Steps that <b>help</b>:<ul>
<li><b>acknowledge</b> the tribal community's cultural concerns first;</li>
<li>create a <b>multi-stakeholder dialogue</b> platform;</li>
<li>conduct an <b>independent Environmental and Social Impact Assessment</b> and share it transparently.</li></ul>
Pushing ahead because the majority's needs "outweigh" a small group's is majoritarian and would inflame the conflict.<br><br>The legal framework: the FRA and PESA (Gram Sabha consent; Niyamgiri), the 2013 land acquisition law (social impact assessment, consent), the Solid Waste Management Rules 2016, and Articles 21 and 47. A model approach is to de-escalate, listen, gather evidence, consider alternatives (another site, decentralised processing, benefit-sharing), and reach a monitored agreement.</div>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p14-2", `
<p>Welfare schemes and social laws turn the Directive Principles into practice (see 4.4). UPSC asks about their ministries, target groups and key features.</p>

<h3>Food and nutrition</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>National Food Security Act, 2013:</b>
  <ul><li>It covers up to <b>75% of the rural and 50% of the urban population</b> (Priority Households and Antyodaya households), <b>not BPL families only</b>.</li>
  <li><b>The eldest woman aged 18 or over</b> is the head of the household for ration cards (Section 13).</li>
  <li>Pregnant and lactating women get a <b>meal of about 600 calories</b> (not 1,600) through anganwadis, plus a maternity benefit of at least ₹6,000.</li>
  <li>Entitlements: 5 kg per person a month, or 35 kg per Antyodaya household. Grain has been <b>free since January 2023</b> (PMGKAY merged, extended to 2028).</li>
  <li>Children get mid-day meals (PM POSHAN) and ICDS meals.</li>
  <li>One Nation One Ration Card has given nationwide portability since 2022.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>POSHAN Abhiyaan</b> (the National Nutrition Mission, launched 8 March 2018 at Jhunjhunu; Ministry of WCD):
  <ul><li>Objectives: <b>awareness of malnutrition among pregnant and lactating women</b>, and <b>reducing anaemia among children, adolescent girls and women</b>, along with stunting and low birth weight.</li>
  <li>Promoting <b>millets</b> or <b>eggs</b> was <b>not</b> among its stated objectives.</li>
  <li>It was merged into Mission Poshan 2.0 (2021). September is Poshan Maah.</li>
  <li>NFHS-5: stunting 35.5%, wasting 19.3%.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Anaemia Mukt Bharat</b> (2018), the "6×6×6" strategy:
  <ul><li>Prophylactic <b>iron and folic acid</b> supplements, <b>not calcium</b>.</li>
  <li><b>Delayed cord clamping</b> at birth.</li>
  <li><b>Periodic deworming</b> (National Deworming Day: 10 February and 10 August).</li>
  <li>Testing and treatment, and fortified foods.</li>
  <li>Addressing <b>non-nutritional causes</b> (malaria, haemoglobinopathies, fluorosis).</li>
  <li>NFHS-5: 67% of children and 57% of women are anaemic.</li>
  <li>Rice fortification became universal in 2024, and the Sickle Cell Mission began in 2023.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>FSSAI:</b> the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 <b>replaced the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954</b> and seven other orders.
  <ul><li>FSSAI is a statutory body under the Health Ministry, headed by a Chairperson and a CEO, <b>not by the Director General of Health Services</b>.</li>
  <li>Its initiatives: Eat Right India, a 2% trans-fat limit, and the "+F" logo for fortified foods.</li></ul></li>
<li>The <b>Food and Nutrition Board</b> is covered in 14.1.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Maternal and child health</h3>
<table><tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Key feature</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Janani Suraksha Yojana</b> (12 April 2005)</td><td>A safe-motherhood intervention under the <b>National (Rural) Health Mission</b>, a centrally sponsored scheme, not a State health department scheme. Aims: <b>reduce maternal and neonatal deaths</b> and <b>promote institutional delivery</b> among poor women, through cash incentives, with ASHAs as link workers.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (2011)</td><td>Free delivery, drugs, transport and diet. Free care for <b>sick infants up to one year</b> belongs here, not to JSY.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>PM Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan</b> (2016)</td><td>Free antenatal check-ups in the <b>second and third trimesters</b> on the 9th of every month. It does <b>not</b> cover six months of post-delivery care. <b>Private specialists volunteer</b> at government facilities.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PM Matru Vandana Yojana (2017)</td><td>₹5,000 for the first child, plus ₹6,000 for a second child if a girl (under Mission Shakti)</td></tr>
<tr><td>LaQshya (2017); SUMAN (2019)</td><td>Quality of labour rooms; zero tolerance for denial of maternal services</td></tr></table>
<p>The Maternal Mortality Ratio is 93 per lakh live births (SRS 2019–21), against an SDG target below 70. Institutional deliveries have reached 89% (NFHS-5).</p>
<div class="key"><b>Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, 2017</b><ul>
<li>Paid leave rose from 12 to <b>26 weeks</b>, of which <b>no more than 8 weeks before delivery</b>. "Three months before and three months after" is wrong.</li>
<li>Establishments with 50 or more employees must have a <b>crèche</b>, and mothers get <b>four visits a day</b>, not six.</li>
<li>Women with <b>two or more surviving children</b> get only <b>12 weeks</b>: a reduced entitlement.</li>
<li>Adopting and commissioning mothers get 12 weeks, and work from home is possible.</li>
<li>It applies to establishments with 10 or more employees and is now part of the Code on Social Security 2020.</li></ul></div>

<h3>Social security</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>PM Shram Yogi Maan-dhan</b> (2019, Ministry of Labour): a pension scheme for unorganised workers earning up to ₹15,000 a month.
  <ul><li>The entry age is <b>18–40</b>, not 21–40.</li>
  <li>The worker pays an <b>age-specific contribution</b> (₹55–200 a month), which the Centre matches.</li>
  <li>It gives a <b>minimum pension of ₹3,000</b> a month from age 60.</li>
  <li>The family pension goes to the <b>spouse only</b>.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Other schemes:</b> PM Kisan Maan-dhan; Atal Pension Yojana (2015); NPS (2004); and the Unified Pension Scheme (April 2025), which offers 50% of last pay.</li>
<li>The <b>e-Shram</b> portal (2021) has over 30 crore registrations. The Code on Social Security (in force November 2025) covers gig and platform workers.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Child protection and education</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Child labour:</b>
  <ul><li>The National Child Labour Project (1988) was run by the <b>Ministry of Labour</b>, not Social Justice, and was later merged into Samagra Shiksha.</li>
  <li>The <b>Gurupadaswamy Committee</b> (1979) studied child labour and led to the 1986 Act, amended in 2016.</li>
  <li>India ratified ILO Conventions 138 and 182 in 2017. 12 June is World Day Against Child Labour.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>UN Convention on the Rights of the Child</b> (adopted 20 November 1989; ratified by India in 1992). It covers the rights to <b>development</b> (Article 6), <b>expression</b> (Article 13) and <b>recreation</b> (Article 31).
  <ul><li>Its four core rights are survival, development, protection and participation.</li>
  <li>It is the most widely ratified human-rights treaty; the US has not ratified it.</li>
  <li>Indian laws in this area: POCSO 2012, the Juvenile Justice Act 2015, and the NCPCR.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>RTE and teachers:</b>
  <ul><li>Minimum teacher qualifications are set by the <b>NCTE</b>, the academic authority named by the Centre under Section 23, not by State councils.</li>
  <li>A <b>Teacher Eligibility Test</b>, following NCTE guidelines, is mandatory for Classes 1–8. In September 2025 the Supreme Court held it mandatory even for serving teachers, with conditions.</li>
  <li><b>Over 90%</b> of teacher education institutions are <b>private</b>, not State-run (Justice Verma Commission, 2012).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Vidyanjali</b> (2016): volunteers from the community and private sector help <b>improve the quality of education in government schools</b>. It was not about foreign campuses or, in 2017, monetary donations; Vidyanjali 2.0 (2021) now also accepts contributions in kind.</li>
<li><b>Unnat Bharat Abhiyan</b> (2014, Ministry of Education): <b>connects higher education institutions with local communities</b> to solve development problems with appropriate technology. Each institution adopts five villages, and IIT Delhi coordinates.</li>
<li><b>Central universities</b> are created by Acts of Parliament; there are about 56, including Pondicherry, Maulana Azad National Urdu University and Visva-Bharati. The <b>University of Madras</b> (1857) is a <b>State</b> university. Admission is through CUET since 2022.</li>
<li><b>Institutions of National Importance</b> are declared by Act of Parliament:
  <ul><li>the <b>Dakshina Bharat Hindi Prachar Sabha</b> (1964 Act);</li>
  <li><b>NIPER, Mohali</b> (1998);</li>
  <li><b>SCTIMST, Thiruvananthapuram</b> (1980);</li>
  <li>RGNIYD (2012);</li>
  <li>IITs, NITs, IIMs, AIIMS and IISERs.</li></ul>
  <b>LNIPE Gwalior</b> is a deemed university, not an INI.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Rural development, sanitation and land</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Central Rural Sanitation Programme (1986)</b> was the start of rural sanitation programmes. Sanitation is a <b>State List</b> subject (Entry 6), not Concurrent, so the reason given in that assertion was false.
  <ul><li>Its successors: the Total Sanitation Campaign (1999), Nirmal Bharat Abhiyan (2012), and Swachh Bharat Mission (2014). Rural areas were declared ODF in 2019, and SBM-Urban 2.0 runs 2021–26.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Sanitation and urban bodies:</b>
  <ul><li>The <b>India Sanitation Coalition</b> (2015) is a FICCI-hosted, member-funded platform. It is not funded by the government and the WHO.</li>
  <li>The <b>National Institute of Urban Affairs</b> (1976) is an autonomous think tank supported by the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs, not its "apex body".</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Rashtriya Garima Abhiyan</b> (2001) campaigns to <b>end manual scavenging</b> and rehabilitate those freed from it.
  <ul><li>Laws: the 1993 Act and the 2013 Prohibition of Employment as Manual Scavengers Act.</li>
  <li>Safai Karamchari Andolan (2014) set compensation for sewer deaths, raised to ₹30 lakh in 2023.</li>
  <li>The NAMASTE scheme followed in 2023.</li>
  <li>Other options in that question belong to different schemes: rehabilitating the homeless and beggars is <b>SMILE</b> (2022); rescuing trafficking victims and sex workers is <b>Ujjawala</b>; freeing bonded labourers is the Central Sector Scheme for Rehabilitation of Bonded Labourers (2016).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>National Rehabilitation and Resettlement Policy, 2007:</b>
  <ul><li>It covered <b>involuntary displacement from other causes too</b>, not only land acquisition.</li>
  <li>It was framed by the <b>Ministry of Rural Development</b>, not Social Justice.</li>
  <li>It was replaced by the 2013 land acquisition law (see 4.3).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Digital India Land Records Modernisation Programme:</b>
  <ul><li><b>100% central funding</b> since 2016.</li>
  <li><b>Digitised cadastral maps</b>.</li>
  <li><b>Transliteration</b> of land records into any of the 22 Eighth Schedule languages.</li>
  <li>The ULPIN or "Bhu-Aadhaar" (14 digits), NGDRS, SVAMITVA and NAKSHA (2025).</li>
  <li>Land is a State subject.</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Consumer rights</h3>
<ul>
<li>A consumer <b>can have food samples tested</b> by a food analyst, on paying a fee (FSSA Section 40).</li>
<li>A <b>legal heir</b> can file a complaint for a deceased consumer.</li>
<li>Complaints are not always free; fees depend on the claim, and only claims up to ₹5 lakh are free.</li>
<li>The <b>Consumer Protection Act, 2019</b> and its commissions are covered in 7.4. National Consumer Day is 24 December and World Consumer Rights Day 15 March.</li>
</ul>
`);

