addBookTopic("economy", "e1-3", `
<p>Growth and development are often used loosely as synonyms, but UPSC separates them sharply. This topic also covers what drives growth (savings, investment, the capital-output ratio) and how economies move through booms and recessions.</p>

<h3>Growth vs development</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Economic growth</b> is a quantitative rise in output: real GDP or GNP, ideally per head.</li>
<li><b>Economic development</b> is broader: better living standards, less poverty, unemployment and inequality, better health and education, and structural change (people moving out of low-productivity farming).</li>
<li>If real GNP rises in total and per head but <b>poverty and unemployment rise</b>, there is "growth without development" (jobless growth). An imbalance between agriculture and industry, or imports outgrowing exports, does not by itself rule out development.</li>
<li>Amartya Sen's <i>Development as Freedom</i> (1999) sees development as expanding people's capabilities.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Measure of development</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
<tr><td>Human Development Index (UNDP, 1990; Mahbub ul Haq and Amartya Sen)</td><td>Life expectancy, education (mean and expected years of schooling), GNI per capita (PPP). India ranked 130 of 193 in HDR 2025 (see 3.4)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Multidimensional Poverty Index (UNDP–OPHI)</td><td>Health, education and living standards through 10 indicators (see 3.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Physical Quality of Life Index (Morris D. Morris, 1979)</td><td>Literacy, infant mortality, life expectancy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gross National Happiness</td><td>Bhutan's approach</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inequality-adjusted HDI, Gender Development Index, Gender Inequality Index</td><td>Distribution-sensitive variants</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>What drives growth: saving, investment and the capital-output ratio</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Capital formation</b> (investment in machinery, infrastructure, buildings) raises productive capacity. Among the usual options it is the factor that most directly produces growth in a country. Population growth, world technical progress or world trade do not "necessarily" cause growth in a given country.</li>
<li>The <b>capital-output ratio</b> is the capital needed to produce one extra unit of output. The <b>incremental capital-output ratio (ICOR)</b> is the extra capital per extra unit of output.</li>
<li><b>Harrod–Domar model</b>: growth rate = savings rate ÷ capital-output ratio. With savings of 30% of GDP and an ICOR of 6, growth is 5%. With an ICOR of 4, it is 7.5%.</li>
<li>So a high-saving economy can still grow slowly if its <b>capital-output ratio is high</b>, meaning its investment is inefficient. Causes: project delays, idle capacity, old technology, inefficient public enterprises, infrastructure bottlenecks.</li>
<li>India's ICOR has usually been about 4–5, rising above 6 in the slowdowns around 2012–14 and 2019–20. Gross domestic saving is about 30% of GDP and GFCF about 30–31% (2024–25). Government capital expenditure is budgeted at about ₹12.2 lakh crore for 2026–27.</li>
<li><b>Solow model</b>: capital faces diminishing returns, so long-run growth per head comes from technological progress. The "residual" growth not explained by labour and capital is total factor productivity.</li>
<li><b>Human capital</b> (education, health, skills) is another driver, stressed in endogenous-growth theory (see 3.4).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The business cycle</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Phase</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
<tr><td>Boom (prosperity)</td><td>Business activity at a high level; income, output and employment rising; may overheat into inflation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recession</td><td>Gradual fall in income, output and employment; business in low gear</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depression (trough)</td><td>Severe fall in income and output, mass unemployment and under-employment (the 1930s Great Depression)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recovery</td><td>Steady revival of prices, income, output and employment</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Correct meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Meltdown</td><td>A steep, sudden <b>fall in stock and asset prices</b> (2008)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Slowdown</td><td>A <b>fall in the growth rate</b>: GDP still grows, only more slowly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recession</td><td>A <b>fall in GDP itself</b>; commonly "two consecutive quarters of negative growth" (technical recession)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depression</td><td>A deep, prolonged recession</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stagflation</td><td>Stagnant growth plus high inflation (1970s oil shocks; feared again during the 2026 oil-price shock)</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b>Paired questions often swap "slowdown" and "recession". A slowdown is a fall in the growth rate; a recession is a fall in GDP.</div>
<ul>
<li>India had a technical recession in 2020 (COVID-19); GDP fell about 6% in 2020–21.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Policy over the cycle</h3>
<p>In a recession aggregate demand is weak. Following Keynes, the state uses <b>expansionary (counter-cyclical) policy</b>. The most direct step is to <b>increase spending on public projects</b>, which creates jobs and incomes and works through the <b>multiplier</b>.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Phase</th><th>Fiscal policy</th><th>Monetary policy</th></tr>
<tr><td>Recession</td><td>Raise spending, cut taxes, accept a wider deficit</td><td>Cut repo rate and CRR, buy bonds (OMO purchases), add liquidity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overheating / inflation</td><td>Cut spending, raise taxes</td><td>Raise rates, absorb liquidity</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Mixed signals, such as cutting taxes while raising interest rates, or raising taxes while cutting rates, are not the textbook response. Cutting public spending in a recession deepens it.</li>
<li><b>Automatic stabilisers</b> (progressive taxes, unemployment benefits) act without new decisions.</li>
<li>India's responses: the 2008–09 fiscal stimulus (excise and service-tax cuts, extra spending; see 7.2) and the 2020 Atmanirbhar Bharat packages, which combined government guarantees and spending with RBI liquidity measures.</li>
</ul>
`);
