addBookTopic("economy", "e2-2", `
<p>In 1991 India moved from a licence-and-control economy to a more open, market-based one. The shorthand is <b>LPG: liberalisation, privatisation and globalisation</b>. UPSC asks what began the reforms, what they achieved and how the government sells its stakes in public enterprises.</p>

<h3>The 1991 crisis and the reforms</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Trigger</b>: a balance of payments crisis. The Gulf War raised oil prices and cut remittances from the Gulf, there was political instability, and forex reserves fell to about $1 billion in mid-1991, barely three weeks of imports. India pledged gold with the Bank of England and took an IMF loan.</li>
<li>Leaders: PM P.V. Narasimha Rao and Finance Minister Manmohan Singh. Some licensing had already been eased in 1985 under Rajiv Gandhi.</li>
<li>Liberalisation <b>started with substantial changes in industrial licensing policy</b>. The <b>New Industrial Policy (24 July 1991)</b> abolished licensing for all but 18 industries (now only a handful), removed the MRTP asset limits, cut the list of industries reserved for the public sector, and allowed automatic approval of FDI up to 51% in priority industries. Rupee convertibility, simpler FDI procedures and big cuts in tax rates came later, so none of them is the starting point.</li>
<li>External: the rupee was devalued (July 1991), LERMS dual exchange rate (1992), unified market rate (1993), current account convertibility (August 1994; see 8.2), tariffs cut.</li>
<li>Reform committees: Narasimham (banking, 1991 and 1998), Raja Chelliah (tax, 1991), Malhotra (insurance, 1994), Rangarajan (disinvestment, 1993).</li>
</ul>

<h3>What changed after 1991</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Indicator</th><th>Around 1991</th><th>2020s</th><th>Verdict</th></tr>
<tr><td>Agriculture's share of GDP</td><td>About 30%</td><td>About 16–18%</td><td>Fell; it did not rise</td></tr>
<tr><td>India's share of world merchandise exports</td><td>About 0.5%</td><td>About 1.8% (2024); services about 4.3%</td><td>Rose</td></tr>
<tr><td>FDI inflows</td><td>Under $100 million a year</td><td>$70–80 billion gross a year (about $81 billion in 2024–25)</td><td>Rose</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forex reserves</td><td>About $1 billion (mid-1991)</td><td>About $600–700 billion</td><td>Rose enormously</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Supporting institutions of the reform years</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>National Renewal Fund (February 1992)</b>: a social safety net that paid for voluntary retirement compensation and for <b>counselling, retraining and redeployment</b> of workers hit by restructuring of sick public units. It was wound up in 2000; the Department of Public Enterprises then ran a counselling, retraining and redeployment scheme. Today the Industrial Relations Code (in force November 2025) creates a <b>Worker Re-skilling Fund</b>.</li>
<li><b>Investment Commission (December 2004)</b>: advised on attracting domestic and foreign investment. Chaired by <b>Ratan Tata</b>, with Deepak Parekh (HDFC) and Ashok Ganguly (ex-Unilever). Kumar Mangalam Birla was not a member; he had chaired SEBI's corporate governance committee (1999).</li>
<li>Investment promotion now: Invest India (2009), the National Single Window System, and the Production-Linked Incentive (PLI) schemes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Disinvestment and privatisation</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Disinvestment</b>: the government selling part of its shares in public enterprises. <b>Strategic sale (privatisation)</b>: selling a controlling stake along with management control.</li>
<li>In 2011 the policy was minority stake sales with the government keeping at least 51% and management control. Proceeds were not meant mainly to repay external debt.</li>
<li>The Department of Disinvestment (1999) became <b>DIPAM</b> (Department of Investment and Public Asset Management) in 2016. It now stresses asset monetisation and dividends too.</li>
<li><b>New Public Sector Enterprise Policy (2021)</b>: keep a minimal presence in four broad strategic sectors and privatise or close the rest. Done: Air India to the Tata group (January 2022), Neelachal Ispat Nigam (2022).</li>
<li>In October 2026 the Cabinet approved selling a 60.72% stake in IDBI Bank (held by the government and LIC) to a Fairfax group company for about ₹52,878 crore; completion awaits RBI clearance.</li>
<li><b>BPCL</b>: in 2003 the government held about 66% (between 60% and 70%). That year the Supreme Court ruled that companies nationalised by Acts of Parliament (BPCL, HPCL) could be privatised only after amending those Acts. A 2020–22 attempt to sell the 52.98% stake was called off for lack of bidders. HPCL has been an ONGC subsidiary since 2018.</li>
</ul>
<h4>National Investment Fund (NIF)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Set up in 2005 to receive disinvestment proceeds. It was kept <b>outside the Consolidated Fund of India</b> (in the Public Account), not run directly by the Finance Ministry.</li>
<li>It was managed by public-sector <b>asset management companies</b>: UTI AMC, SBI Funds and LIC Mutual Fund.</li>
<li><b>75% of its annual income financed selected social-sector schemes</b> (education, health, employment); 25% went to profitable and revivable public enterprises.</li>
<li>From 2013 proceeds could fund capital spending. Since 2015–16 disinvestment receipts go into the Consolidated Fund and the NIF is only a book account.</li>
<li>Do not confuse it with the <b>National Investment and Infrastructure Fund (NIIF, 2015)</b>, a quasi-sovereign infrastructure fund (see 11.3).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e2-3", `
<p>"Inclusive growth" means growth whose gains reach the poor, women, socially weaker groups and lagging regions. It was the central idea of the Eleventh and Twelfth Plans and remains the frame for welfare policy.</p>

<h3>The Plan themes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Eleventh Plan (2007–12): "<b>Towards Faster and More Inclusive Growth</b>". Its targets covered poverty reduction, employment, education, health, women and children, and narrowing regional and gender gaps. <b>Strengthening the capital market</b> is a financial-sector goal and was not part of its idea of inclusion.</li>
<li>Twelfth Plan (2012–17): "Faster, More Inclusive and Sustainable Growth" (see 2.1). The long-term vision now is Viksit Bharat @2047.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What advances inclusive growth</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Why it is inclusive</th></tr>
<tr><td>Self-Help Groups</td><td>Credit, savings and livelihoods for poor women. Under DAY-NRLM about 10 crore women are in over 90 lakh SHGs (see 4.6, 12.1); "Lakhpati Didi" aims at a household income of ₹1 lakh a year</td></tr>
<tr><td>MSMEs</td><td>Labour-intensive and widely spread: about 30% of GDP and 45% of exports. Definitions were raised in Budget 2025–26 (micro: investment up to ₹2.5 crore, turnover up to ₹10 crore; see 11.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Right to Education Act 2009 (Article 21A)</td><td>Free and compulsory schooling for 6–14-year-olds; 25% of seats in private schools for weaker sections</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public health spending</td><td>Widens access for the poor. Government health spending is about 1.9% of GDP against the National Health Policy 2017 target of 2.5%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mid-day Meal, now PM POSHAN (2021)</td><td>Hot cooked meals for about 11 crore children in Classes 1–8; improves nutrition and attendance; millets encouraged</td></tr>
<tr><td>Effective District Planning Committees (Article 243ZD)</td><td>Let local people take part in planning: participatory governance</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Inclusive governance</h3>
<ul>
<li>Inclusive governance means participation, accountability and fair access to public services for all groups.</li>
<li>Strong DPCs, more public health spending and a stronger Mid-day Meal Scheme are part of it. <b>Allowing NBFCs to do banking</b> is a financial-sector reform, not inclusive governance (NBFCs cannot accept demand deposits; see 4.4).</li>
<li>Results so far: multidimensional poverty fell from about 29% (2013–14) to about 11% (2022–23) by NITI Aayog's estimate (see 3.1).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e3-1", `
<p>Poverty can be measured by income or consumption (can a household afford a minimum basket?) or by deprivations (does it lack schooling, nutrition, clean fuel or sanitation?). India uses both kinds of measure, and UPSC asks about each.</p>

<h3>Consumption-based poverty lines</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>poverty line</b> is the spending needed to buy a minimum basket of goods and services. The <b>headcount ratio</b> is the share of people below it.</li>
<li>Official poverty lines differ by State because <b>price levels vary from State to State</b>: the same basket costs different amounts. The Tendulkar and Rangarajan methods adjust State lines with State-specific price indices, so a State with higher prices has a higher poverty line.</li>
<li>The Planning Commission estimated poverty from NSSO consumption surveys; NITI Aayog has not adopted a new official line since.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Expert group</th><th>Basis</th><th>Poverty in 2011–12</th></tr>
<tr><td>Y.K. Alagh (1979)</td><td>Calorie norm: 2,400 kcal rural, 2,100 urban</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Lakdawala (1993)</td><td>Calorie norm, State-specific lines, separate price indices</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Suresh Tendulkar (2009)</td><td>Broader basket including health and education; mixed reference period; about ₹27 rural / ₹33 urban per person per day</td><td>21.9%</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. Rangarajan (2014)</td><td>About ₹32 rural / ₹47 urban per day</td><td>29.5%</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The World Bank raised its international extreme-poverty line to <b>$3 a day (2021 PPP)</b> in 2025. On that line India's extreme poverty was about 5.3% in 2022–23.</li>
<li>The Household Consumption Expenditure Surveys of 2022–23 and 2023–24 showed rising rural consumption and a narrowing rural–urban gap.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Developed in 2010 by the <b>Oxford Poverty and Human Development Initiative (OPHI)</b> with the <b>UNDP</b>, and published in the Human Development Report and the annual Global MPI.</li>
<li>It measures overlapping <b>deprivations at the household level</b> in health, education and living standards. It does <b>not</b> use PPP income, budget deficits or GDP growth.</li>
<li>Method: the <b>Alkire–Foster method</b> (Sabina Alkire and James Foster). A person is multidimensionally poor if deprived in at least one-third of the weighted indicators. <b>MPI = H × A</b>: headcount ratio times intensity (average share of deprivations among the poor).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Dimension (weight)</th><th>Global MPI: 10 indicators</th><th>NITI Aayog's National MPI: 12 indicators</th></tr>
<tr><td>Health (1/3)</td><td>Nutrition, child mortality</td><td>Nutrition, child and adolescent mortality, <b>maternal health</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Education (1/3)</td><td>Years of schooling, school attendance</td><td>Same</td></tr>
<tr><td>Standard of living (1/3)</td><td>Cooking fuel, sanitation, drinking water, electricity, housing, assets</td><td>Same, plus <b>bank account</b></td></tr>
</table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b>Maternal health and bank account are the two indicators India added. They are not in the global MPI, so they are not "common" to both.</div>
<h4>Key numbers</h4>
<ul>
<li>NITI Aayog (January 2024 discussion paper): multidimensional poverty fell from 29.17% (2013–14) to 11.28% (2022–23); about 24.82 crore people escaped it. The earlier review showed 24.85% (NFHS-4, 2015–16) to 14.96% (NFHS-5, 2019–21).</li>
<li>Global MPI (UNDP–OPHI): India's headcount fell from 55.1% (2005–06) to about 16.4% (2019–21), about 41 crore people, the largest absolute fall of any country.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e3-2", `
<p>India's employment problem is less open unemployment than under-employment, low productivity and informality. UPSC asks about types of unemployment, who compiles labour data, and the new Labour Codes.</p>

<h3>Types of unemployment</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Disguised</b></td><td>More people work than needed, so removing some would not reduce output: <b>marginal productivity of labour is zero</b> (or near zero). Typical of family farms in India. Arthur Lewis's model sees this surplus labour moving to industry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seasonal</td><td>Work only in sowing and harvest seasons</td></tr>
<tr><td>Structural</td><td>Mismatch between skills and available jobs, or too little capital</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frictional</td><td>Short spells between jobs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cyclical</td><td>Caused by a recession</td></tr>
<tr><td>Educated</td><td>Educated people unable to find suitable jobs</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Measuring employment</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS)</b> of MoSPI (since 2017–18) gives annual and quarterly estimates; monthly bulletins began in 2025. It uses <b>Usual Status</b> (reference year) and <b>Current Weekly Status</b> (reference week).</li>
<li>Agriculture employs about 46% of workers (PLFS 2023–24) but produces only about 17–18% of GVA. Female labour force participation rose to about 41.7% (2023–24, usual status), mainly through rural self-employment.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Data</th><th>Compiled by</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Industrial disputes, strikes, lockouts, closures, retrenchments and lay-offs</b></td><td><b>Labour Bureau</b> (1946; attached office of the Ministry of Labour, at Shimla and Chandigarh), from State labour departments</td></tr>
<tr><td>CPI for Industrial Workers, Agricultural Labourers and Rural Labourers</td><td>Labour Bureau (see 5.2)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quarterly Employment Survey, surveys of migrant and domestic workers</td><td>Labour Bureau</td></tr>
<tr><td>CPI (Rural, Urban, Combined), IIP, GDP, PLFS, Annual Survey of Industries</td><td>NSO, MoSPI (CSO and NSSO merged in 2019)</td></tr>
<tr><td>WPI, Index of Eight Core Industries</td><td>Office of the Economic Adviser, DPIIT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coal statistics</td><td>Coal Controller's Organisation (see 11.2)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Rural employment after 1991</h3>
<p>NITI Aayog's paper "Changing Structure of Rural Economy of India" (2017) found:</p>
<ul>
<li>Worker productivity rose in <b>both</b> rural and urban areas (urban remains much higher). It did not fall in rural areas.</li>
<li>The rural share of the workforce <b>fell</b> (about 77% in the early 1990s to about 70% by 2011–12) as people moved to towns.</li>
<li>The <b>rural non-farm economy grew</b> (construction, manufacturing, services); the rural economy became less agricultural.</li>
<li><b>Growth of rural employment slowed</b>: "jobless growth" in the 2000s.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Labour laws and the four Labour Codes</h3>
<p>Labour is in the Concurrent List (Entries 22–24). Four Codes replaced 29 central laws and came into force on <b>21 November 2025</b>.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Code</th><th>Laws merged and key features</th></tr>
<tr><td>Code on Wages, 2019</td><td>Minimum Wages, Payment of Wages, Payment of Bonus, Equal Remuneration Acts; a national floor wage. The government can by notification require an establishment to <b>pay wages only by cheque or bank transfer</b> (a power first added to the Payment of Wages Act in 2017)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Industrial Relations Code, 2020</td><td>Trade Unions, Industrial Disputes and Industrial Employment (Standing Orders) Acts. Government permission for lay-off, retrenchment and closure needed only in units with <b>300 or more workers</b> (earlier 100). Fixed-term employment. Worker Re-skilling Fund</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code on Social Security, 2020</td><td>EPF, ESI, Maternity Benefit, Gratuity Acts; covers <b>gig and platform workers</b> for the first time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020</td><td>Factories, Mines, Contract Labour, Inter-State Migrant Workmen Acts</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Casual workers</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>EPF does not cover all casual workers</b>: it applies to establishments with 20 or more employees, so casual workers in small units are left out.</li>
<li>Labour law gives every worker, casual ones included, limits on working hours and overtime at twice the ordinary rate. UPSC accepted this statement, though in practice many informal workers do not get it.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Fixed-term employment</h4>
<ul>
<li>The Industrial Employment (Standing Orders) Central (Amendment) Rules, 2018 extended <b>fixed-term employment</b> to all sectors (earlier only apparel). Employment ends with the contract term, so <b>firms can shed workers more easily</b>, without retrenchment notice or compensation. Fixed-term workers get the same wages, hours and benefits as permanent ones, pro rata.</li>
<li>The rules say <b>no notice of termination is needed for a temporary workman</b> (monthly, weekly or piece-rated) or a probationer.</li>
<li>Under the IR Code, fixed-term workers get gratuity after one year of service instead of five. Standing orders are now needed only in establishments with 300 or more workers (earlier 100).</li>
</ul>
<p>Related schemes: MGNREGA (see 12.1), DAY-NRLM, PMEGP, PM Vishwakarma (2023), National Career Service and skilling schemes (see 12.2).</p>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e3-3", `
<p>Population size, age structure and fertility shape the labour supply, savings and public spending. India's young population is its "demographic dividend", but only if it is educated, healthy and employed.</p>

<h3>Key demographic measures</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Measure</th><th>Definition</th><th>India (latest)</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Total Fertility Rate (TFR)</b></td><td>Average number of children a woman would have over her reproductive span (15–49) if she experienced the current age-specific fertility rates throughout</td><td>2.0 (NFHS-5, 2019–21; NFHS-4: 2.2); SRS 2023: 1.9. Urban 1.6, rural 2.1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Replacement-level TFR</td><td>TFR at which a population just replaces itself</td><td>2.1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Net Reproduction Rate</td><td>Counts only daughters who survive to child-bearing age</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)</b></td><td>Deaths of infants <b>under one year</b> per <b>1,000 live births</b> in a year</td><td>25 (SRS 2023; 40 in 2013). Kerala 5; MP, Chhattisgarh, UP about 37</td></tr>
<tr><td>Neonatal Mortality Rate</td><td>Deaths within the first <b>28 days</b> per 1,000 live births</td><td>SDG 3.2 target: 12 or less by 2030</td></tr>
<tr><td>Under-5 Mortality Rate</td><td>Deaths before age five per 1,000 live births</td><td>29 (SRS 2023); SDG target 25 or less</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maternal Mortality Ratio</td><td>Maternal deaths per 1 lakh live births</td><td>93 (2019–21)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependency ratio</td><td>(Under-15 + over-64) ÷ working-age (15–64)</td><td>Falling</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sex ratio</td><td>Women per 1,000 men</td><td>1,020 overall; 929 at birth (NFHS-5)</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b>IMR covers the first year of life, not the first month (that is neonatal mortality), and it is per 1,000 live births, not per 100.</div>
<ul>
<li>Highest TFR: Bihar (about 3.0), Meghalaya, UP, Jharkhand, Manipur. Lowest: Sikkim (about 1.1), Goa, Andaman and Nicobar.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Population size, age and ageing</h3>
<ul>
<li>India became the world's <b>most populous country in April 2023</b> (UN World Population Prospects); about 146 crore in 2025.</li>
<li>Median age, lowest to highest: <b>India &lt; China &lt; USA &lt; UK</b>. Around 2008: India about 25, China about 33–34, USA about 36–37, UK about 39–40. Now: India about 28–29, China about 39–40 (ageing fast after the one-child policy, 1980–2015; its population has been falling since 2022), USA about 38–39, UK about 40–41. Oldest countries: Monaco, Japan (about 49), Italy; youngest: Niger (about 15).</li>
<li>People aged <b>65 and above</b> were about <b>5–6%</b> of India's population in the 2000s (Census 2011: about 5.5%; 60+ was 8.6%). Now 65+ is about 7%.</li>
<li>India Ageing Report 2023 (UNFPA–IIPS): the 60+ share was about 10.5% in 2022 and will be about 20.8% (347 million) by 2050; the elderly will outnumber children by about 2046. Kerala and Tamil Nadu are the oldest States; Bihar and UP the youngest.</li>
<li>Elderly policy: National Policy on Older Persons (1999), Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act (2007), IGNOAPS pensions (see 12.2), Ayushman Vay Vandana card (PM-JAY for everyone aged 70+, 2024).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The demographic dividend</h3>
<ul>
<li>It is the growth boost available when the share of the <b>working-age population (15–64)</b> is high and the dependency ratio falls.</li>
<li>India's working-age share will keep rising until about 2041; the window may last until 2041–2055. About 65% of Indians are under 35; nearly 70 crore are under 25. Southern States are ageing faster.</li>
<li>To reap it, India must <b>promote skill development</b>, education, health and jobs. Skill schemes: Skill India Mission, PMKVY 4.0, National Apprenticeship Promotion Scheme, PM Internship Scheme (2024), Skill India Digital Hub, NSQF (see 12.2).</li>
<li>Risks: low female labour force participation (about 42%), jobless growth, informality.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Population policy</h3>
<ul>
<li>India was the <b>first country in the world</b> to launch a national family planning programme (1952). Forced sterilisation during the Emergency (1975–77) made it unpopular; it was renamed "Family Welfare".</li>
<li><b>Kerala</b> was the first State to reach replacement-level fertility (late 1980s), followed by Tamil Nadu.</li>
<li><b>National Population Policy 2000</b>:
 <ul>
 <li>Immediate objective: meet the unmet need for contraception and health infrastructure.</li>
 <li>Medium-term: bring TFR to replacement level (2.1) by 2010, when the population was projected at about 111 crore.</li>
 <li>Long-term: a <b>stable population by 2045</b>.</li>
 <li>It extended the freeze on Lok Sabha seat allocation based on the 1971 Census until 2026 (42nd and 84th Amendments).</li>
 </ul></li>
<li>National Commission on Population (2000) is chaired by the PM. Mission Parivar Vikas (2016) targets high-TFR districts.</li>
<li>India reached replacement fertility much later than NPP 2000 planned (TFR 2.0 in NFHS-5).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Census 2027</h3>
<ul>
<li>The 16th Census, the first since 2011, will include <b>caste enumeration</b> (the first full caste count since 1931).</li>
<li>House-listing: April–September 2026. Population enumeration: February 2027, with reference date <b>1 March 2027</b> (1 October 2026 for snow-bound areas).</li>
<li>It will be the basis for the next delimitation and for women's reservation in legislatures.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e3-4", `
<p>Development is about people, not only output. This topic covers human capital and the main indices that rank countries on human development, gender and hunger.</p>

<h3>Human capital</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Human capital formation</b> is the process of <b>increasing the knowledge, skills, health and capacities</b> of people. Because these are embodied in people, it is the accumulation of <b>intangible wealth</b>. Accumulating capital in general, or tangible wealth, describes physical or financial capital.</li>
<li>Sources (NCERT): spending on education, health, on-the-job training, migration and information.</li>
<li>Human capital treats people as a means to higher productivity; <b>human development</b> treats people's well-being as the end in itself.</li>
<li>World Bank <b>Human Capital Index</b> (2018): India scored 0.49 (2020), meaning a child born today will be 49% as productive as with full education and health.</li>
<li>Schemes: Samagra Shiksha, NEP 2020, Skill India, Ayushman Bharat, POSHAN Abhiyaan.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Human Development Index (HDI)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Published by the <b>UNDP</b> in its Human Development Report since 1990. Designed by <b>Mahbub ul Haq</b> with <b>Amartya Sen</b> (Nobel 1998, Bharat Ratna 1999).</li>
<li>Three dimensions: <b>health</b> (life expectancy at birth), <b>education</b> (expected and mean years of schooling) and <b>income</b> (GNI per capita, PPP).</li>
<li>HDR 2025 (2023 data; theme: artificial intelligence): India ranked <b>130 of 193</b> with HDI 0.685 (medium human development).</li>
<li>Variants: Inequality-adjusted HDI, Gender Development Index, Gender Inequality Index, Planetary-pressures adjusted HDI.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>South Asian HDI order</th><th>HDR 2002</th><th>HDR 2025</th></tr>
<tr><td>Sri Lanka</td><td>89</td><td>89</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maldives</td><td>77</td><td>93</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bhutan</td><td></td><td>125</td></tr>
<tr><td>India</td><td>124</td><td>130 (tied with Bangladesh)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nepal</td><td></td><td>145</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pakistan</td><td>138</td><td>168</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>In 2002 the order was <b>Maldives, Sri Lanka, India, Pakistan</b>; now Sri Lanka is ahead of the Maldives.</li>
<li>In Asia, <b>South Korea</b> has long done best among the developing economies: about 30th in HDR 1999, ahead of Malaysia (about 56th), the Philippines (about 77th) and China (about 98th). In HDR 2025 South Korea is about 20th and Japan about 23rd; only Singapore (about 13th) and Hong Kong rank higher in Asia. China is about 78th.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Human development reports in India</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Madhya Pradesh</b> released India's first State Human Development Report in 1995 (CM Digvijaya Singh); Amartya Sen released it in Delhi. State HDRs were backed by UNDP and the Planning Commission.</li>
<li>NCAER's <b>India Human Development Report</b> (1999) surveyed about 1,700 villages and built village-level indices of infrastructure and amenities, education and health. It did <b>not</b> build an unemployment index; unemployment data come from the NSSO and now PLFS.</li>
<li>The national Human Development Reports of India were published by the Planning Commission (2002, 2011).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Gender and hunger indices</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Index</th><th>Publisher</th><th>Notes and India's rank</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Global Gender Gap Index</b></td><td><b>World Economic Forum</b> (since 2006)</td><td>Four sub-indices: economic participation and opportunity, educational attainment, health and survival, political empowerment. India 131 of 148 (2025; 129 in 2024); weakest in economic participation and political empowerment. Iceland tops</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gender Development Index, Gender Inequality Index</td><td>UNDP</td><td>Part of the HDR</td></tr>
<tr><td>Women, Business and the Law</td><td>World Bank</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Women, Peace and Security Index</td><td>Georgetown Institute (GIWPS)</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>SDG Gender Index</td><td>Equal Measures 2030</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Global Hunger Index</b></td><td>Concern Worldwide and Welthungerhilfe (IFPRI co-published until 2017)</td><td>India 105 of 127 (2024), 102 of 123 (2025), "serious"</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Global Hunger Index indicators</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Undernourishment</b> (share of population with insufficient calories), weight 1/3.</li>
<li><b>Child stunting</b> (low height for age), 1/6.</li>
<li><b>Child wasting</b> (low weight for height), 1/6.</li>
<li><b>Under-five mortality</b>, 1/3.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The Indian government disputes the method: three of the four indicators concern children, and undernourishment is estimated from a small poll.</li>
<li>India's own data: NFHS-5 shows stunting at 35.5% and wasting at 19.3%. POSHAN Abhiyaan and the free-grain PMGKAY address these.</li>
<li>Other food reports: FAO's State of Food Security and Nutrition in the World (SOFI), the Global Nutrition Report.</li>
<li>India's gender measures: Beti Bachao Beti Padhao (2015), Mission Shakti (2022), Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam (2023), Lakhpati Didi, Stand-Up India.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_economy_e4.js
// Economy book, Unit 4: Money, banking and monetary policy.
addBookTopic("economy", "e4-1", `
<p>Money is whatever is generally accepted in exchange. This topic covers what money does, why some money is "legal tender", how the RBI measures money supply, and how banks multiply the RBI's base money. These ideas are needed for monetary policy (4.3) and inflation (5.1).</p>

<h3>Functions and kinds of money</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Functions</b>: medium of exchange, unit of account (measure of value), store of value, standard of deferred payment. Barter fails for lack of a "double coincidence of wants".</li>
<li><b>Fiat money</b> has value because the government says so, not because it is backed by a commodity. India has used the <b>minimum reserve system</b> since 1957: the RBI must hold at least ₹200 crore of gold and foreign exchange, of which at least ₹115 crore is gold.</li>
<li><b>Legal tender</b> is money that a <b>creditor is legally bound to accept</b> in settlement of a debt. In India, RBI notes (Section 26 of the RBI Act) and coins (Coinage Act 2011, up to set limits) are legal tender. A cheque or card payment is not legal tender.</li>
<li>One-rupee notes and all coins are issued by the <b>Government of India</b> (Ministry of Finance); all other notes by the RBI.</li>
<li>Demonetisation (8 November 2016) removed the legal tender status of the old ₹500 and ₹1,000 notes. ₹2,000 notes were withdrawn from circulation in May 2023 but remain legal tender.</li>
<li>The <b>e-rupee</b> (the RBI's central bank digital currency, pilots from 2022) is also legal tender (see 4.6).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Measures of money supply</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Measure</th><th>Components</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>M0 (reserve money, base money, high-powered money)</b></td><td>Currency in circulation + bankers' deposits with the RBI + other deposits with the RBI</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>M1 (narrow money)</b></td><td>Currency with the public + demand deposits with banks + other deposits with the RBI</td></tr>
<tr><td>M2</td><td>M1 + savings deposits with post office savings banks</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>M3 (broad money)</b></td><td><b>M1 + time deposits with banks</b>; the most used measure</td></tr>
<tr><td>M4</td><td>M3 + all post office deposits (excluding National Savings Certificates)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>So <b>currency with the public, demand deposits and time deposits are all in M3</b>.</li>
<li>Liquidity falls from M1 to M4. Currency held by banks is not part of money supply; only currency with the public is.</li>
<li>The RBI also publishes New Monetary Aggregates (NM0–NM3) and liquidity aggregates (L1–L3), following the Y.V. Reddy working group (1998). Broad money grew about 9–10% in 2024–25.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Liquidity of assets</h4>
<p>From most to least liquid: <b>currency</b> (money itself) → <b>demand deposits</b> (withdrawable on demand by cheque) → <b>savings deposits</b> (withdrawable, with some limits) → <b>time (fixed) deposits</b> (locked for a term; early withdrawal is penalised).</p>

<h3>What changes the money supply</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Action</th><th>Effect on money supply</th></tr>
<tr><td>RBI <b>buys government securities</b> from the public (OMO purchase)</td><td>Rises: the RBI pays with new money</td></tr>
<tr><td>Government <b>borrows from the RBI</b> (monetisation of deficit)</td><td>Rises: new money created (see 7.2)</td></tr>
<tr><td>RBI buys foreign currency</td><td>Rises, unless sterilised (see 4.3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>RBI <b>sells</b> government securities</td><td>Falls: money is absorbed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public <b>deposits currency in banks</b></td><td>No immediate change: currency becomes deposits</td></tr>
<tr><td>You <b>withdraw ₹1 lakh cash</b> from your demand deposit</td><td><b>No immediate change</b>: deposits fall by ₹1 lakh, currency with the public rises by ₹1 lakh. Later, banks' lower reserves may reduce credit creation</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The money multiplier</h3>
<ul>
<li>Banks keep only part of deposits as reserves and lend the rest; each loan creates a new deposit. So each rupee of reserve money supports several rupees of broad money.</li>
<li><b>Money multiplier = M3 ÷ M0</b> (about 5 in India). Simple form: 1 ÷ reserve ratio. With a currency–deposit ratio c and reserve ratio r: (1 + c) ÷ (c + r).</li>
<li>It <b>rises with the banking habit of the people</b>: when people hold more as deposits and less as cash (a lower currency–deposit ratio), banks can lend more. It falls when reserve ratios (CRR) rise or people hoard cash. Population size has no direct effect.</li>
<li>Jan Dhan, UPI and digital payments raise the banking habit. Demonetisation (2016) briefly raised deposits and the multiplier.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Demand for money and the rate of interest</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keynes's <b>liquidity preference</b> theory: people hold money for <b>transactions, precautionary and speculative</b> motives. The interest rate is the price of parting with liquidity.</li>
<li>If money demand rises while supply is fixed, people sell bonds to get cash; bond prices fall and <b>interest rates rise</b> until demand equals supply again.</li>
<li><b>Liquidity trap</b>: at very low rates, extra money is simply held, so monetary policy loses power (Japan in the 1990s).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e4-2", `
<p>The Reserve Bank of India is the country's central bank: it issues currency, runs monetary policy, regulates banks and payment systems, and manages the foreign exchange reserves and the government's debt.</p>

<h3>History and governance</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Item</th><th>Fact</th></tr>
<tr><td>Set up</td><td><b>1 April 1935</b> under the RBI Act 1934, on the Hilton Young Commission's advice; headquarters moved from Calcutta to Mumbai in 1937</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nationalised</td><td><b>1 January 1949</b> (not 26 January 1950)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Governor and Deputy Governors</td><td><b>Appointed by the Central Government</b> (Section 8; in practice the Appointments Committee of the Cabinet). Governor's term up to five years, reappointment allowed; the Centre can remove him (Section 11). Governor since December 2024: Sanjay Malhotra</td></tr>
<tr><td>Central Board</td><td>Governor, up to four Deputy Governors, nominated directors and two government officials</td></tr>
<tr><td>Source of the Governor's power</td><td>The <b>RBI Act</b>: the Governor has general superintendence and direction of the Bank's affairs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Government directions</td><td><b>Section 7 of the RBI Act</b> (a statute, not the Constitution) lets the Centre, after consulting the Governor, give directions "in the public interest". It was never formally used; its possible use was discussed in 2018</td></tr>
<tr><td>Financial year</td><td>April–March since 2020–21 (earlier July–June)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Functions of the RBI</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Monetary authority</b>: frames and runs monetary policy through the Monetary Policy Committee (see 4.3).</li>
<li><b>Issuer of currency</b> (except one-rupee notes and coins).</li>
<li><b>Banker to the government</b>: banker to the Centre by statute and to States by agreement (Section 21A). It lends to governments through Ways and Means Advances and overdrafts.</li>
<li><b>Debt manager</b>: conducts the government's <b>borrowing programme</b> (auctions of government securities) and earns a commission. The Finance Ministry's <b>Department of Economic Affairs</b> (Budget Division) decides the borrowing plan, not the Department of Expenditure. A separate Public Debt Management Agency has been proposed but not created.</li>
<li><b>Government's agent for IMF membership</b>: it holds the IMF's accounts in India. The Finance Minister is India's Governor on the IMF Board; the RBI Governor is the Alternate Governor.</li>
<li><b>Bankers' bank</b>: banks keep part of their deposits with it (CRR and current accounts for clearing), it <b>lends to banks</b> in need, and it <b>advises and guides them</b> on monetary and credit matters.</li>
<li><b>Lender of last resort</b>: provides emergency liquidity to <b>solvent banks in a temporary crisis</b>, to stop runs and contagion. Walter Bagehot's rule (<i>Lombard Street</i>, 1873): lend freely, against good collateral, at a penalty rate. India's tools: the Marginal Standing Facility and special windows (for mutual funds and TLTROs in 2020, for health care in 2021). Lending to trade and industry, or to governments for deficits, is not this function.</li>
<li><b>Regulator and supervisor</b> of banks, NBFCs and payment systems (Payment and Settlement Systems Act 2007).</li>
<li><b>Manager of foreign exchange</b> under FEMA 1999; custodian of reserves (record of about $741 billion in August 2026).</li>
<li><b>Developmental role</b>: financial inclusion and rural credit (see 4.6).</li>
</ul>

<h3>How the RBI regulates banks</h3>
<p>The RBI regulates commercial banks in all of these matters:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Liquidity of assets</b>: CRR, SLR and the Liquidity Coverage Ratio.</li>
<li><b>Branch expansion</b> (Banking Regulation Act, Section 23): now general permission, with a rule that 25% of new branches be in unbanked rural centres.</li>
<li><b>Mergers</b>: amalgamation needs RBI approval (Section 44A). The RBI can frame compulsory amalgamation schemes (Section 45), as with Lakshmi Vilas Bank and DBS India (2020).</li>
<li><b>Winding up</b>: the RBI can apply for winding up (Section 38), impose moratoriums and cancel licences.</li>
<li>Also licensing, approving directors and CEOs, inspection, superseding boards (Yes Bank, 2020) and the Prompt Corrective Action framework.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Banking Laws (Amendment) Act 2025 allows up to four nominees per account (from 1 November 2025) and raised the "substantial interest" threshold from ₹5 lakh to ₹2 crore. Deposit insurance by DICGC covers ₹5 lakh per depositor per bank.</p>

<h3>The RBI's income and surplus</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Income</th><th>Not income</th></tr>
<tr><td>Interest on domestic government securities, and gains from <b>buying and selling government bonds</b> (OMOs)</td><td><b>Pension fund management</b>: done by PFRDA-regulated fund managers under NPS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interest on foreign securities and gains from <b>buying and selling foreign currency</b>; this was the largest source of the record surpluses in years of heavy dollar sales</td><td><b>Lending to private companies</b>: the RBI lends only to banks and some financial institutions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interest on lending to banks (repo, LAF)</td><td><b>Printing currency</b>: an expense (presses of BRBNMPL, owned by the RBI, and SPMCIL, owned by the government)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commission for managing public debt</td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Expenses: printing notes, staff, interest paid on the Standing Deposit Facility, and provisions to the Contingency Fund.</li>
<li>Under <b>Section 47</b> of the RBI Act the surplus after provisions goes to the Centre. The <b>Bimal Jalan Committee (2019)</b> set the Economic Capital Framework: a Contingent Risk Buffer of 5.5–6.5% of the balance sheet, widened to 4.5–7.5% in 2025.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e4-3", `
<p>Monetary policy is the RBI's management of interest rates and liquidity to keep prices stable while supporting growth. Fiscal policy (taxes, spending, public debt) belongs to the government (see 7.2).</p>

<h3>Objective and framework: flexible inflation targeting</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>RBI</b> is responsible for price stability. The RBI Act, amended in 2016, makes "price stability while keeping in mind the objective of growth" the main objective. It followed the Urjit Patel Committee (2014) and the Monetary Policy Framework Agreement (February 2015).</li>
<li>Target: <b>CPI inflation of 4% ± 2%</b> (band 2–6%), set by the Centre in consultation with the RBI every five years. It was retained for 2021–26 and again for <b>2026–31 (notified 25 March 2026)</b>.</li>
<li>Failure: if inflation stays outside the band for <b>three consecutive quarters</b>, the RBI must write to the government explaining why and what it will do. This first happened in November 2022.</li>
<li>Other bodies are not responsible for inflation targeting. The Department of Consumer Affairs monitors essential prices and runs the Price Stabilisation Fund. The Expenditure Management Commission (2014, Bimal Jalan) reviewed spending. The FSDC handles financial stability (see 6.3).</li>
</ul>
<h4>The Monetary Policy Committee (MPC)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Section 45ZB of the RBI Act (2016); first meeting October 2016. It <b>decides the policy repo rate</b>.</li>
<li><b>Six members</b>: the RBI Governor (chair, casting vote in a tie), the Deputy Governor in charge of monetary policy, one RBI officer, and three external members appointed by the Centre (search committee headed by the Cabinet Secretary) for four years, not reappointable. It is not a 12-member body, is not reconstituted every year, and is not chaired by the Finance Minister.</li>
<li>Meets at least four times a year (in practice six); quorum four; decisions by majority; minutes published on the 14th day.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The policy rates and the corridor (October 2026)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Rate</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Level</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Marginal Standing Facility (MSF) rate</b> = <b>Bank Rate</b></td><td>Ceiling of the corridor: repo + 0.25%. Banks borrow overnight at a penal rate, even dipping into their SLR holdings (a small share of NDTL). Introduced 2011</td><td>5.75%</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Repo rate</b></td><td>Policy rate: the rate at which banks borrow from the RBI against government securities ("sell and repurchase")</td><td><b>5.50%</b> (raised from 5.25% on 7 October 2026)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Standing Deposit Facility (SDF)</b></td><td>Floor of the corridor since April 2022: repo − 0.25%; banks park surplus funds without collateral. It replaced the fixed reverse repo as the floor</td><td>5.25%</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)</b></td><td>Share of NDTL kept as cash with the RBI; earns no interest (RBI Act, Section 42)</td><td>3% (cut in stages from 4% in September–November 2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR)</b></td><td>Share of NDTL held in cash, gold or approved government securities (Banking Regulation Act, Section 24; maximum 40%)</td><td>18% (38.5% in 1990)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>NDTL (Net Demand and Time Liabilities)</b>: a bank's demand and time deposits and other liabilities, net of inter-bank claims. CRR and SLR are worked out on NDTL. MSF and NDTL are thus <b>banking-operations</b> terms.</li>
<li>The <b>Liquidity Adjustment Facility (LAF)</b> covers repo and reverse repo operations, plus variable-rate auctions.</li>
<li>Rate path: repo raised from 4% to 6.5% (May 2022–February 2023) to fight post-pandemic inflation; cut from 6.5% to 5.25% (February–December 2025); raised to <b>5.50% on 7 October 2026</b>, the first hike since February 2023, as oil-driven inflation rose. The MPC shifted its stance to "calibrated tightening".</li>
<li>A basis point is 0.01 percentage point.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Instruments of monetary policy</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Quantitative (general)</th><th>Qualitative (selective)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Repo, SDF, MSF, <b>Bank Rate</b>, CRR, SLR, <b>Open Market Operations</b>, LAF, Market Stabilisation Scheme, forex swaps</td><td>Margin requirements, credit rationing, moral suasion, selective credit controls, direct action</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b>Public debt and public revenue are parts of <b>fiscal</b> policy, not monetary policy.</div>
<h4>Bank Rate</h4>
<ul>
<li>The rate at which the RBI lends long-term funds to banks without collateral (RBI Act, Section 49). Since 2012 it has been aligned with the MSF rate. Penal interest on CRR and SLR shortfalls is linked to it.</li>
<li><b>Raising</b> it signals a <b>tight (dear) money policy</b>. <b>Lowering</b> it makes borrowing cheaper and gives <b>more liquidity</b> in the market. The repo rate is now the main signal.</li>
</ul>
<h4>CRR and SLR</h4>
<ul>
<li>A <b>higher CRR</b> means banks have <b>less money to lend</b>.</li>
<li>A <b>cut in SLR</b> (say by 50 basis points) frees funds for lending, so banks <b>may cut lending rates</b>.</li>
<li>SLR creates a captive market for government bonds, so it is the mechanism by which <b>banks provide credit to the government</b>. Banks hold more than the SLR because of Basel III liquidity rules.</li>
<li>The purpose of statutory reserve requirements, per UPSC, is to <b>let the central bank control the amount of credit banks can create</b>. Deposit safety is mainly the job of prudential rules and deposit insurance (the key was debated). They are not meant to curb bank profits, and they are not vault cash: cash in hand does not count towards CRR.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Open Market Operations (OMOs)</h4>
<ul>
<li>The RBI's <b>purchase and sale of government securities</b> to manage liquidity. Buying injects money (expansionary); selling absorbs it (contractionary).</li>
<li>"Operation Twist": buying long-term and selling short-term bonds at the same time to lower long-term yields (used in 2019–20).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Using the tools: expansionary and contractionary policy</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Situation</th><th>What the RBI does</th></tr>
<tr><td>Expansionary (easy) policy, e.g. in a slowdown</td><td>Cuts repo and Bank Rate, cuts CRR and SLR, buys bonds. It would <b>not raise the MSF rate</b>, which is contractionary</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Inflation too high</b></td><td>Raises repo; <b>sells</b> government securities to absorb liquidity. Buying them would add to inflation</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Rupee depreciating fast</b></td><td><b>Sells dollars</b> from reserves (about $100 billion sold in 2022 as the rupee crossed 80 per dollar)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Interest rates in the USA or EU fall</b></td><td>Capital flows into India and the rupee tends to rise, so the RBI <b>buys dollars</b> to absorb inflows and build reserves</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Transmission of monetary policy</h3>
<ul>
<li>A higher policy rate leads to higher lending and deposit rates, less borrowing and spending, more saving, lower demand and lower inflation. It also works through a stronger currency (cheaper imports) and expectations. Lags are 12–18 months.</li>
<li>A <b>cut in interest rates</b> lowers the cost of borrowing, so it <b>increases investment spending</b>.</li>
<li>Monetary policy is weaker against supply shocks such as food or oil prices.</li>
<li>After the pandemic, supply disruptions, pent-up demand, stimulus and the 2022 energy and food shock pushed inflation to multi-decade highs. Central banks raised rates sharply in 2022–23 (the US Fed from about 0% to 5.25–5.5%, plus the ECB, Bank of England and RBI). They did so because they believe tightening can curb demand and anchor expectations.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Lending-rate systems</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>System</th><th>Period</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>Prime Lending Rate / Benchmark PLR</td><td>1994–2010</td><td>Bank's own prime rate; opaque</td></tr>
<tr><td>Base Rate</td><td>2010–2016</td><td>Average cost of funds</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>MCLR</b> (Marginal Cost of Funds based Lending Rate)</td><td>April 2016–2019</td><td>Marginal (latest) cost of funds, operating cost, CRR cost and tenor premium. Aims: <b>transparency</b> in how banks set loan rates, and credit at rates <b>fair to borrowers and banks</b>, with faster transmission</td></tr>
<tr><td>External Benchmark Lending Rate</td><td>October 2019 onwards</td><td>Floating retail and MSME loans linked to an external benchmark (repo rate or T-bill yield), reset at least every three months; fastest transmission</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Interest rates were deregulated in stages: lending rates (1994 onwards), term deposits (1997) and <b>savings deposits (October 2011)</b>. Before that the savings rate (3.5% in 2010) was fixed by the RBI, not the Finance Ministry, the Finance Commission or the Indian Banks' Association; now each bank sets its own.</li>
<li>Small savings rates (PPF 7.1%, Sukanya Samriddhi 8.2%) are set by the government using the Shyamala Gopinath formula.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Sterilisation and the impossible trinity</h3>
<ul>
<li>When the RBI buys dollars to stop the rupee rising, it releases rupees that could fuel inflation. <b>Sterilisation</b> neutralises this, mainly by <b>Open Market Operations</b> (selling government securities).</li>
<li>Other sterilisation tools: the <b>Market Stabilisation Scheme</b> (2004; special T-bills and bonds whose proceeds sit unused in a separate account with the RBI; used heavily in 2004–08 and briefly in 2016), the SDF and reverse repo, CRR changes, and forex buy/sell swaps (since 2019).</li>
<li><b>Impossible trinity (trilemma)</b>: a country cannot have a fixed exchange rate, free capital movement and an independent monetary policy all at once.</li>
<li>Forex reserves: about $705 billion in September 2024 and a record of about $741 billion in August 2026 (see 8.1).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Inequality: the Gini coefficient</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Gini coefficient comes from the Lorenz curve and ranges from <b>0 (perfect equality)</b> to <b>1 (perfect inequality</b>, one person has everything).</li>
<li>India's consumption Gini is about 0.25–0.26 (HCES 2023–24), but income and wealth inequality are much higher (World Inequality Lab).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e4-4", `
<p>India's banking system has many layers: scheduled commercial banks (public, private, foreign, small finance, payments and regional rural banks), cooperative banks, non-bank finance companies and development finance institutions. UPSC asks how each differs and what it may or may not do.</p>

<h3>Commercial banks</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Primary functions</b>: accepting deposits and lending. <b>Agency functions</b>: collecting cheques and dividends, paying bills, <b>buying and selling shares and securities for customers</b>, acting as <b>executors and trustees of wills</b> (both allowed by Section 6 of the Banking Regulation Act 1949; banks use trustee subsidiaries). <b>Utility functions</b>: lockers, letters of credit, guarantees. Banks may not trade in goods (Section 8).</li>
<li><b>Nationalisation</b>: 14 banks in July 1969 and 6 in April 1980. Mergers in 2019–20 cut public sector banks from 27 (2017) to <b>12</b>.</li>
<li><b>Federal Bank</b> (Aluva, Kerala; founded 1931 as Travancore Federal Bank) is a <b>private</b> bank and was never nationalised. Coastal Karnataka gave birth to Canara, Syndicate, Corporation, Vijaya and Karnataka Bank.</li>
<li>Private bank ownership: in 2001–03 FDI up to <b>49% from all sources</b> was allowed on the automatic route, and there was no 49% cap on Indian promoters (new private banks' promoters had to hold at least 40%, locked in for five years). Now FDI up to <b>74%</b> (49% automatic) in private banks and 20% in public sector banks. Promoter holding must fall to 26% after 15 years (2021 norms).</li>
<li>Recent foreign entries: Japan's SMBC took about 25% of Yes Bank (2025); Emirates NBD agreed to buy about 60% of RBL Bank (October 2025).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Foreign banks</h4>
<ul>
<li>About 44–45 foreign banks operate in India, mostly as branches. Only SBM Bank (Mauritius) and DBS Bank (Singapore) have become <b>wholly owned subsidiaries (WOS)</b>. DBS India took over Lakshmi Vilas Bank (2020).</li>
<li>RBI's WOS framework (2013): a <b>minimum capital</b> of ₹500 crore (so "no minimum capital" is wrong) and capital adequacy of at least 10%. Board: <b>not less than 50% Indian nationals, NRIs or PIOs</b>, with at least one-third Indian nationals resident in India, and at least one-third independent directors. UPSC treated "at least 50% Indian nationals" as incorrect (the key was debated).</li>
<li>WOS get near "national treatment" but must meet the 40% priority sector target.</li>
<li>Foreign bank origins: <b>ABN AMRO</b> is Dutch, <b>Barclays</b> British, <b>Kookmin</b> South Korean. ABN AMRO's Indian business passed to RBS (2007). Japan's big banks: MUFG, SMBC, Mizuho.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Differentiated banks: payments banks and small finance banks</h3>
<p>Both came from the <b>Nachiket Mor Committee</b> on financial inclusion; RBI guidelines in November 2014.</p>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Payments Bank</th><th>Small Finance Bank</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Small savings accounts and payments for migrant workers, low-income households, small businesses</td><td>Savings vehicles, and <b>credit to small business units, small and marginal farmers</b>, micro and small industries and the unorganised sector, through low-cost technology</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum capital</td><td>₹100 crore</td><td>₹200 crore (on-tap licensing, 2019)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lending</td><td><b>Cannot lend</b>; must invest at least 75% of demand deposits in SLR government securities</td><td>Can lend; 75% to the priority sector; 50% of loans up to ₹25 lakh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deposits</td><td>Demand deposits only, up to ₹2 lakh per customer (₹1 lakh until 2021)</td><td>All kinds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cards</td><td>ATM/debit cards, <b>not credit cards</b></td><td>All</td></tr>
<tr><td>Promoters</td><td>Prepaid instrument issuers, NBFCs, business correspondents, <b>mobile telephone companies, supermarket chains</b>, companies, cooperatives, owned and controlled by residents; public sector entities</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Airtel, India Post Payments Bank, Fino, Jio, NSDL; Paytm barred from new deposits (2024)</td><td>AU, Equitas, Ujjivan, Jana, ESAF, Suryoday</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Payments banks may sell mutual funds, insurance and pensions and act as business correspondents of other banks.</li>
<li>Encouraging young rural entrepreneurs is not a stated purpose of SFBs. SFBs may convert to universal banks after five years with a good record (April 2024 guidelines).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Rural and cooperative credit</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Institution</th><th>Facts</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Regional Rural Banks</b></td><td>1975 (Narasimham Working Group), RRB Act 1976. Ownership: Centre 50%, sponsor bank 35%, State 15%. <b>Lend directly</b> to small farmers, artisans and rural households. "One State, One RRB" amalgamation (1 May 2025) left 28</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>NABARD</b></td><td>1982 (Sivaraman Committee). Apex <b>refinancing</b> and development body: refinances banks, cooperatives and RRBs, runs the Rural Infrastructure Development Fund. It does <b>not lend directly</b> to households. Supervises StCBs and DCCBs</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Land Development Banks</b> (now SCARDBs / PCARDBs)</td><td>Long-term cooperative credit <b>directly</b> to farmers for land improvement, wells, tractors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Short-term cooperative structure</td><td>PACS (village, about 1 lakh) → <b>DCCB</b> (district) → StCB (State)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>One of the most important functions of <b>DCCBs is to fund the PACS</b>, which lend to farmers.</li>
<li>In short-term farm credit, <b>scheduled commercial banks lend the most</b> (about three-quarters); cooperatives' share has fallen to about 10–15% and RRBs' to about 10–12%. DCCBs do not lend more than commercial banks.</li>
<li>PACS are being computerised (2022) and turned into multipurpose societies (model bye-laws 2023), with the "world's largest grain storage plan". PACS are not banks under the BR Act.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Urban Cooperative Banks (UCBs)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Under <b>dual control</b>: the RBI regulates and supervises banking functions; the State Registrar of Cooperative Societies (or the Central Registrar for multi-State banks) handles registration, management and audit. There are no "local boards" set up by States.</li>
<li>Cooperative banks came under the <b>Banking Regulation Act from 1 March 1966</b> (Section 56).</li>
<li>The Banking Regulation (Amendment) Act 2020, after the PMC Bank crisis (2019), lets them <b>issue equity, preference and special shares</b> and debentures with RBI approval, and lets the RBI supersede boards and order mergers.</li>
<li>Four-tier regulation of UCBs by deposit size (2022); umbrella organisation NUCFDC (2024); Ministry of Cooperation (July 2021). The 97th Amendment (2011) added cooperatives to Article 19(1)(c), Article 43B and Part IXB; the Supreme Court (2021) struck down the Part IXB provisions on State cooperatives.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Directing credit: Lead Bank Scheme and priority sector lending</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Lead Bank Scheme</b> (1969, Gadgil Study Group and Nariman Committee): <b>each district is assigned a bank</b> that takes the lead in intensive development and credit planning with other banks and agencies. Forums: District Consultative Committee, District Level Review Committee, State Level Bankers' Committee, Block Level Bankers' Committee. Annual Credit Plans; NABARD prepares Potential Linked Credit Plans. Revised in 2018 and 2024.</li>
<li><b>Service Area Approach</b> (April 1989) worked <b>within the Lead Bank Scheme</b>: each rural and semi-urban branch got a group of villages to serve. Made non-mandatory in 2004 except for government-sponsored schemes.</li>
<li><b>Priority Sector Lending</b>: banks must lend a share of credit to sectors that otherwise get too little: agriculture, MSMEs, export credit, education, housing, social infrastructure, renewable energy and weaker sections.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>PSL target (domestic banks)</th><th>Share of adjusted net bank credit</th></tr>
<tr><td>Total</td><td>40% (RRBs and SFBs 75%; UCBs 60%)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agriculture</td><td>18% (small and marginal farmers 10%)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Micro enterprises</td><td>7.5%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weaker sections</td><td>12%</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Shortfalls go to RIDF (NABARD) and similar funds. Surpluses can be traded as Priority Sector Lending Certificates (2016). Directions revised in 2025 raised housing and education loan limits.</p>

<h3>Non-Banking Financial Companies (NBFCs)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Regulated under Chapter III-B of the RBI Act. An NBFC's principal business is financial: financial assets above 50% of total assets and financial income above 50% of gross income (the "50-50 test").</li>
<li>Differences from banks:
 <ul>
 <li>NBFCs <b>cannot accept demand deposits</b> (savings or current accounts). Only some can take term deposits (12–60 months).</li>
 <li>They are <b>not part of the payment and settlement system</b> and cannot issue cheques drawn on themselves.</li>
 <li><b>DICGC deposit insurance is not available</b> to depositors of deposit-taking NBFCs.</li>
 </ul></li>
<li><b>Not all NBFCs register with the RBI</b>: those regulated by others are exempt, such as insurance companies (IRDAI), merchant bankers and stock brokers (SEBI), chit funds (State governments) and Nidhi companies (MCA). Housing finance companies moved from NHB to RBI regulation in 2019.</li>
<li>NBFCs <b>can</b> buy shares, bonds and government securities; that is a core activity (investment companies, primary dealers).</li>
<li><b>Scale-Based Regulation (2021)</b>: Base, Middle, Upper (the top 15 by size; bank-like rules; must list within three years) and Top layers. Types include investment and credit companies, NBFC-MFIs, factors, infrastructure finance companies, core investment companies, P2P lenders and account aggregators. Minimum net owned fund rising to ₹10 crore by 2027.</li>
<li>Crises: IL&FS (2018), DHFL (2019).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Development finance institutions (DFIs)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Institution</th><th>Year and facts</th></tr>
<tr><td>IFCI (Industrial Finance Corporation of India)</td><td><b>1948</b>: India's first DFI</td></tr>
<tr><td>ICICI</td><td><b>1955</b>; became a bank in 2002</td></tr>
<tr><td>IDBI</td><td><b>1964</b>, as an RBI subsidiary; became a bank in 2004. LIC took control (2019); a 60.72% stake sale to a Fairfax company was approved in October 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>NABARD</td><td><b>1982</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>EXIM Bank</td><td>1982</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>National Housing Bank</b></td><td>NHB Act 1987, set up July 1988 as a <b>wholly owned subsidiary of the RBI</b>; RBI's stake moved to the government in 2019. Now supervises and refinances housing finance companies; runs RESIDEX</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>SIDBI</b></td><td>SIDBI Act 1989, set up 1990 as a <b>wholly owned subsidiary of IDBI</b>; delinked in 2000. Runs the Fund of Funds for Startups, MUDRA refinance and (with the government) CGTMSE</td></tr>
<tr><td>NaBFID</td><td>2021: new infrastructure DFI</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The all-India financial institutions regulated by the RBI are NABARD, SIDBI, NHB, EXIM Bank and NaBFID. DICGC remains wholly owned by the RBI.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e4-5", `
<p>Banks lend other people's money, so regulators insist on enough capital, careful lending and quick handling of bad loans. This topic covers the bank balance sheet, capital adequacy and the Basel norms, stressed-asset schemes, and how public sector banks are governed.</p>

<h3>The bank balance sheet</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Liabilities</th><th>Assets</th></tr>
<tr><td>Capital, reserves and surplus</td><td>Cash in hand and balances with the RBI (CRR)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Deposits</b> (demand, savings, time): money the bank owes</td><td>Balances with banks; money at call and short notice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Borrowings (from the RBI and other banks)</td><td>Investments (government securities for SLR, bonds, shares)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other liabilities and provisions</td><td>Advances (loans, cash credit, overdrafts, bills discounted)</td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td>Fixed and other assets</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A loan is an asset for the bank and a liability for the borrower; a deposit is the reverse. So <b>deposits are not among a bank's assets</b>.</p>

<h3>Capital adequacy and the Basel norms</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Capital Adequacy Ratio (CAR or CRAR)</b> = bank's own capital ÷ risk-weighted assets. Own funds <b>absorb losses if borrowers fail to repay</b>, protecting depositors.</li>
<li>The minimum is <b>set by the RBI</b> following the Basel norms, not decided by each bank.</li>
<li>The norms are framed by the <b>Basel Committee on Banking Supervision</b>, housed at the Bank for International Settlements (BIS), Basel, Switzerland.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Accord</th><th>Key points</th></tr>
<tr><td>Basel I (1988)</td><td>Credit risk; minimum capital 8% of risk-weighted assets</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Basel II</b> (2004)</td><td><b>International standards for measuring the adequacy of a bank's capital</b>. Three pillars: minimum capital (for credit, market and operational risk), supervisory review, market discipline (disclosure). India adopted it from 2008–09</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Basel III</b> (2010, after the 2008 crisis)</td><td>Aims to <b>improve banks' ability to absorb financial and economic stress</b> and improve risk management. Higher-quality capital: CET1 at least 4.5%, Tier 1 6%, total 8%, plus a 2.5% capital conservation buffer and a 0–2.5% countercyclical buffer. Liquidity Coverage Ratio (30-day stress), Net Stable Funding Ratio (one year), leverage ratio (3%; RBI 4% for systemically important banks)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>India requires a CRAR of <b>9%</b> (stricter than Basel's 8%) plus the 2.5% buffer, so 11.5% in all. Domestic Systemically Important Banks (SBI, HDFC Bank, ICICI Bank) must hold extra CET1.</li>
<li>Indian banks' CRAR was about 17% in 2025; gross NPAs fell to about 2.3%. The RBI's final Basel III credit-risk rules are due to take effect from 2027.</li>
<li><b>Large Exposure Framework</b>: a bank's exposure to one counterparty is capped at 20% of Tier-1 capital (25% with board approval), and to a group at 25%.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Assessing borrowers and risky lending</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Interest Coverage Ratio (ICR)</b> = EBIT ÷ interest expense: how many times operating profit covers interest. Lenders use the current ICR and its trend to judge a firm's <b>present and emerging risk</b>. A <b>higher</b> ICR means a <b>better</b> ability to service debt; below 1, the firm cannot pay interest from operating earnings ("zombie firms").</li>
<li>Related ratios: debt–equity (leverage), Debt Service Coverage Ratio, current ratio (liquidity).</li>
<li><b>Teaser loans</b>: low fixed rates for the first few years, then higher. They are a form of <b>sub-prime</b> lending: borrowers who qualify only at the teaser rate may default later, as in the US crisis of 2007–08. In India they were mainly home loans (2009–10), not loans to entrepreneurs. The RBI raised provisioning (2% on standard teaser loans, 2010).</li>
<li>The RBI raised risk weights on unsecured consumer credit in November 2023 and partly eased them in 2025.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Syndicated and consortium lending</h4>
<ul>
<li>A <b>syndicated loan</b> is one large loan from a group of lenders to one borrower, arranged by a lead bank. It <b>spreads default risk</b> across lenders and keeps each within its exposure limits. It can be a lump-sum term loan, a <b>revolving credit line</b>, or both. (UPSC dropped the 2024 question.)</li>
<li>Consortium lending is the common Indian form: several banks lend under one agreement with a lead bank. In multiple banking, each lends separately. Co-lending by banks and NBFCs for the priority sector began in 2020.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Resolving stressed assets</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Year</th><th>Feature</th></tr>
<tr><td>Corporate Debt Restructuring</td><td>2001</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Joint Lenders' Forum, Corrective Action Plan; 5/25 flexible structuring</td><td>2014</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Strategic Debt Restructuring</td><td>2015</td><td>Lenders convert debt to equity and take control</td></tr>
<tr><td>Asset Quality Review</td><td>2015</td><td>Exposed hidden NPAs</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>S4A</b> (Scheme for Sustainable Structuring of Stressed Assets)</td><td>June 2016</td><td>For large viable accounts (debt above ₹500 crore) of firms facing genuine difficulty: debt split into a <b>sustainable part</b> (at least 50%, serviceable from current cash flow) and an unsustainable part converted into equity or quasi-equity; promoters could stay</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code</td><td>2016</td><td>Resolution through the NCLT within 330 days; Committee of Creditors decides by 66% vote</td></tr>
<tr><td>12 February 2018 circular</td><td>2018</td><td>Withdrew all earlier schemes; struck down by the Supreme Court in 2019</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Inter-Creditor Agreement</b></td><td>July 2018</td><td>Under Project Sashakt (Sunil Mehta Committee): for <b>faster resolution of stressed loans of ₹50 crore or more under consortium lending</b>. Once lenders with 66% of the debt approve a plan, it binds all; a lead bank implements it within 180 days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prudential Framework for Resolution of Stressed Assets</td><td>June 2019</td><td>Made the ICA mandatory</td></tr>
<tr><td>National Asset Reconstruction Company (NARCL, "bad bank")</td><td>2021</td><td>Government guarantee on security receipts</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Project Sashakt's five tracks: bank-level resolution for loans up to ₹50 crore; bank-led resolution through the ICA for ₹50–500 crore; AMC or AIF-led resolution above ₹500 crore; the NCLT route; and an asset-trading platform.</p>

<h3>Governance of public sector banks</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Banks Board Bureau (BBB)</b>: set up in 2016 under the <b>Indradhanush</b> plan, on the P.J. Nayak Committee's advice (2014). It <b>recommended the chairmen, MDs and whole-time directors</b> of public sector banks and financial institutions, and helped them with <b>strategy and capital-raising plans</b>. The final appointment is made by the government (Appointments Committee of the Cabinet). It was chaired by an eminent outsider appointed by the Centre (first chairman: former CAG Vinod Rai), <b>not the RBI Governor</b>.</li>
<li>In 2022 it was replaced by the <b>Financial Services Institutions Bureau (FSIB)</b>, covering banks, public insurers and financial institutions, after the Delhi High Court held the BBB could not select general insurance heads.</li>
<li><b>Indradhanush (2015)</b>, seven elements: appointments, BBB, capitalisation, de-stressing, empowerment, framework of accountability, governance reforms. P.J. Nayak also proposed a Bank Investment Company and cutting the government's stake below 50%.</li>
<li>Government <b>capital infusion fluctuated</b>; it did not rise steadily (large under Indradhanush and the ₹2.11 lakh crore recapitalisation plan of 2017, smaller in other years).</li>
<li>On <b>1 April 2017 SBI absorbed its five associate banks</b> (State Bank of Bikaner and Jaipur, Hyderabad, Mysore, Patiala, Travancore) and Bharatiya Mahila Bank.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>Merger</th></tr>
<tr><td>2008, 2010</td><td>State Bank of Saurashtra and State Bank of Indore into SBI</td></tr>
<tr><td>2017</td><td>Five associates and Bharatiya Mahila Bank into SBI</td></tr>
<tr><td>2019</td><td>Vijaya Bank and Dena Bank into Bank of Baroda</td></tr>
<tr><td>2020</td><td>OBC and United Bank into PNB; Syndicate into Canara; Andhra and Corporation into Union Bank; Allahabad into Indian Bank</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Public sector banks earned record profits (over ₹1.4 lakh crore in 2023–24), and gross NPAs fell below 3%.</p>
<h4>Bank failures and rescues</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Global Trust Bank</b> was placed under moratorium and merged with <b>Oriental Bank of Commerce</b> (August 2004), not PNB. OBC itself merged into PNB in 2020.</li>
<li>Yes Bank (2020, SBI-led rescue); Lakshmi Vilas Bank (2020, merged with DBS India); PMC Bank (2019, later absorbed into Unity Small Finance Bank).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Customer grievances: the Ombudsman</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Banking Ombudsman Scheme (1995) was run by the RBI. An aggrieved party could <b>appeal</b> against the Ombudsman's award to an Appellate Authority (a Deputy Governor), so awards were not final. The service was <b>free of any fee</b>, and NRIs with accounts in India could also complain.</li>
<li><b>RBI Integrated Ombudsman Scheme (November 2021)</b>, "One Nation, One Ombudsman": merged the banking, NBFC and digital payments ombudsmen; appeals go to an Executive Director of the RBI. Complaints at cms.rbi.org.in; compensation up to ₹20 lakh plus ₹1 lakh for mental agony.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e4-6", `
<p>Financial inclusion means affordable access to savings, credit, insurance, pensions and payments for everyone, especially the poor. India's path ran from bank nationalisation to Jan Dhan accounts and UPI, and now the digital rupee.</p>

<h3>Steps towards financial inclusion</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Step</th><th>Year</th></tr>
<tr><td>Bank nationalisation</td><td>1969, 1980</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lead Bank Scheme; <b>adoption of villages by bank branches</b> (Service Area Approach)</td><td>1969, 1989</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Regional Rural Banks</b></td><td>1975</td></tr>
<tr><td>SHG–Bank Linkage Programme (NABARD)</td><td>1992</td></tr>
<tr><td>No-frills accounts</td><td>2005</td></tr>
<tr><td>Business correspondents</td><td>2006</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pradhan Mantri Jan-Dhan Yojana</td><td>2014</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payments banks and small finance banks; UPI</td><td>2015–16</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>The RBI's Financial Inclusion Index</h4>
<ul>
<li>Launched August 2021, published each July. Three sub-indices: <b>Access (35%), Usage (45%), Quality (20%)</b>. It uses 97 indicators across banking, investment, insurance, postal and pension services, on a scale of 0 to 100.</li>
<li>Values: 43.4 (March 2017), 53.9 (2021), 60.1 (2023), 64.2 (2024), <b>67.0 (2025)</b>. Usage drives the gains; Quality (financial literacy, consumer protection, gaps in services) improves slowest.</li>
<li>National Strategy for Financial Inclusion 2019–24; National Strategy for Financial Education 2020–25. World Bank Global Findex 2025: about 89% of Indian adults have an account, though many are inactive.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pradhan Mantri Jan-Dhan Yojana (PMJDY)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Launched <b>28 August 2014</b> as the National Mission for <b>Financial Inclusion</b>: a basic account for every unbanked household.</li>
<li>Features: RuPay debit card; accident cover of ₹1 lakh (₹2 lakh for accounts opened after August 2018); overdraft up to ₹10,000.</li>
<li>About 59 crore accounts with ₹3.17 lakh crore in deposits (August 2026); about 56% held by women.</li>
<li><b>JAM trinity</b>: Jan Dhan, Aadhaar and Mobile, the base for Direct Benefit Transfer.</li>
<li>Companion schemes (2015): PM Jeevan Jyoti Bima Yojana (life cover, ₹436 a year), PM Suraksha Bima Yojana (accident cover, ₹20 a year), Atal Pension Yojana (see 6.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Business correspondents</h3>
<ul>
<li>Allowed by the RBI in 2006 (H.R. Khan's recommendations) to take banking to branchless areas. In villages they let people <b>draw subsidies and social-security payments</b> (DBT, MGNREGA wages, pensions, often via Aadhaar-enabled payments) and make <b>deposits and withdrawals</b>, open accounts and send remittances.</li>
<li>They can be retired bank staff, kirana shops, post offices, SHGs, NGOs or Common Service Centres ("Bank Mitra" under PMJDY). Over 15 lakh outlets.</li>
<li>Business facilitators only help (find customers, collect papers) and cannot handle cash.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Microfinance and Self-Help Groups</h3>
<ul>
<li>Microfinance gives low-income people <b>credit, savings, insurance and fund-transfer services</b>, all four.</li>
<li>An SHG has 10–20 members (usually women) who save regularly, lend internally and borrow from banks. Members take <b>joint responsibility</b> for loans to individual members; peer pressure replaces collateral.</li>
<li>India's <b>SHG–Bank Linkage Programme</b> was launched by <b>NABARD in 1992</b> (pilot of 500 SHGs), building on NGOs such as MYRADA and SEWA. It was not started by SBI. <b>Commercial banks, RRBs and cooperative banks</b> lend to SHGs, which counts as priority sector lending.</li>
<li>DAY-NRLM (2011, from SGSY 1999): about 10 crore women in over 90 lakh SHGs; collateral-free loans up to ₹20 lakh; Lakhpati Didi (₹1 lakh+ yearly income) and Namo Drone Didi. State models: Kudumbashree (Kerala, 1998), Jeevika (Bihar), SERP (Andhra Pradesh). See 12.1.</li>
<li>NBFC-MFIs are regulated by the RBI (harmonised framework, 2022): household income cap ₹3 lakh, repayments capped at 50% of income. The Andhra Pradesh crisis (2010) led to the Malegam Committee. Stress rose again in 2024–25; the industry now limits borrowers to three lenders.</li>
<li>Grameen Bank (Bangladesh; Muhammad Yunus, Nobel Peace Prize 2006) is the global model.</li>
<li><b>WaterCredit</b>: Water.org's programme (a US non-profit co-founded by Gary White and Matt Damon, not WHO or the World Bank). It puts <b>microfinance tools to work for water and sanitation</b>, giving small loans for toilets and tap connections so poor households can meet water needs <b>without depending on subsidies</b>. Related Indian programmes: Swachh Bharat Mission (Grameen) Phase II, Jal Jeevan Mission (2019), AMRUT 2.0 (2021), Atal Bhujal Yojana; SDG 6.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Credit for small entrepreneurs</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Features</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>PM MUDRA Yojana</b> (April 2015)</td><td>Brings small entrepreneurs into the <b>formal financial system</b>: collateral-free loans to non-corporate, non-farm micro and small enterprises through banks, NBFCs and MFIs. MUDRA (Micro Units Development and Refinance Agency) is a SIDBI subsidiary that refinances lenders. Categories: Shishu (up to ₹50,000), Kishore (to ₹5 lakh), Tarun (to ₹10 lakh), Tarun Plus (₹10–20 lakh, 2024). Over 50 crore loans, mostly to women</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Stand-Up India</b> (April 2016)</td><td>Promotes entrepreneurship among <b>SC/ST and women</b>: bank loans of ₹10 lakh to ₹1 crore to at least one SC/ST and one woman borrower per branch for greenfield enterprises (agri-allied added in 2019). <b>Refinance through SIDBI</b> (₹10,000 crore) and credit guarantee through NCGTC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Startup India (2016)</td><td>DPIIT recognition, tax holiday (Section 80-IAC), SIDBI Fund of Funds</td></tr>
<tr><td>PMEGP (2008)</td><td>Credit-linked subsidy for micro enterprises (KVIC)</td></tr>
<tr><td>PM SVANidhi (2020)</td><td>Loans for street vendors</td></tr>
<tr><td>PM Vishwakarma (2023)</td><td>Artisans and craftspeople</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Budget 2025–26 announced term loans up to ₹2 crore for 5 lakh first-time SC/ST and women entrepreneurs.</p>

<h3>Core banking and payment systems</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Core Banking Solutions (CBS)</b> link all of a bank's branches to a central server, so customers can operate their accounts <b>from any branch</b> ("anywhere banking"). It is the bank's own IT system: it is not a tool of RBI control, nor a takeover procedure for weak banks. CBS ("Centralised Online Real-time Exchange") enables NEFT, RTGS, UPI and DBT.</li>
<li>The Payment and Settlement Systems Act 2007 gives the RBI regulatory power; its Board for Regulation and Supervision of Payment and Settlement Systems oversees them.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>System</th><th>Operator</th><th>Limit</th><th>Settlement</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>RTGS</b></td><td>RBI</td><td>Minimum ₹2 lakh, no maximum</td><td><b>Real-time, gross</b> (each transaction individually)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>NEFT</b></td><td>RBI</td><td>No minimum or maximum</td><td>Half-hourly batches (48 a day), deferred net</td></tr>
<tr><td>IMPS</td><td>NPCI</td><td>Up to ₹5 lakh</td><td>Instant, 24×7</td></tr>
<tr><td>UPI</td><td>NPCI</td><td>Generally ₹1 lakh (₹5 lakh for taxes, hospitals, education)</td><td>Instant</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Both NEFT (since 16 December 2019) and RTGS (since 14 December 2020) run <b>24×7×365</b>, so neither has restricted days.</li>
<li><b>No charges for inward transactions</b> in either. The RBI waived its own charges on outward RTGS and NEFT (July 2019), and banks cannot charge savings customers for online NEFT (January 2020).</li>
<li>A Legal Entity Identifier is mandatory for RTGS/NEFT payments of ₹50 crore and above.</li>
</ul>

<h3>NPCI, RuPay and UPI</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI)</b>: set up in 2008 by the RBI and the Indian Banks' Association as a not-for-profit (Section 8) company owned by banks. It <b>promotes financial inclusion</b> through affordable retail payments.</li>
<li>It runs the <b>National Financial Switch</b>, which <b>links all ATMs in India</b>, plus UPI, IMPS, <b>RuPay</b> (India's own card network, 2012), AePS, NACH, FASTag (NETC), BHIM, Bharat BillPay, cheque truncation, *99# USSD, UPI Lite and UPI 123PAY (feature phones). NSDL is a securities depository and the IBA a bankers' association, not ATM networks.</li>
<li>RuPay debit cards go to Jan Dhan account holders; RuPay is accepted in Singapore, UAE, Bhutan, Nepal and the Maldives and via Discover and JCB. RuPay credit cards work on UPI (2022).</li>
<li><b>UPI</b> (April 2016): interoperable, real-time, 24×7, built on IMPS. Users pay straight from bank accounts with a virtual address and PIN, so <b>mobile wallets are no longer necessary</b> for online payments. UPI handles about 80% of India's digital payment volume (over 23 billion transactions a month; record 24.5 billion in August 2026). Features: AutoPay, UPI Lite, UPI 123PAY, credit lines, UPI Circle (2024), biometric authentication (2025). A 30% market-share cap per third-party app has been deferred to end-2026. UPI links abroad: UAE, Singapore (PayNow), France, Sri Lanka, Mauritius, Nepal, Bhutan, Qatar, Trinidad and Tobago.</li>
<li><b>BHIM</b> (Bharat Interface for Money, NPCI, December 2016) lets users send money to <b>anyone with a UPI-enabled bank account</b> (via UPI ID, mobile number, account + IFSC or QR). A chip-and-PIN card has <b>two</b> factors of authentication (the card and the PIN); BHIM/UPI has <b>three</b> (device binding, SIM/mobile number, UPI PIN). The 2018 statement had these the wrong way round.</li>
<li><b>e-RUPI</b> (2021): a prepaid digital voucher for targeted benefits.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Merchant Discount Rate and data localisation</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Merchant Discount Rate (MDR)</b>: the <b>fee a merchant pays its bank</b> for accepting card or digital payments, as a percentage of the transaction. It is shared by the acquiring bank, the issuing bank and the card network. The RBI rationalised debit-card MDR in December 2017. Since January 2020, MDR is zero on RuPay debit cards and BHIM-UPI (Section 10A of the PSS Act, via the Finance Act 2019); the government pays banks an incentive instead. Since 2023, an interchange fee of up to 1.1% applies on merchant UPI payments above ₹2,000 made from wallets.</li>
<li><b>Storage of Payment System Data</b> (RBI circular, April 2018): all payment system operators must store the <b>entire payment data only in India</b> (deadline 15 October 2018; a foreign copy is allowed for cross-border legs). There is no requirement of public-sector ownership, and the system audit report (by a CERT-In empanelled auditor) goes to the RBI, not the CAG. The RBI barred Mastercard, American Express and Diners Club from adding customers in 2021 for non-compliance, lifted later. The DPDP Act 2023 allows cross-border transfers except to restricted countries, but sector rules like the RBI's still apply.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Central Bank Digital Currency: the e-rupee</h3>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>e₹-W (wholesale)</th><th>e₹-R (retail)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pilot launched</td><td>1 November 2022</td><td>1 December 2022</td></tr>
<tr><td>Users</td><td>Banks, for interbank settlement of government securities and call money</td><td>Individuals and merchants (person-to-person, person-to-merchant)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Form</td><td>Account-based</td><td>Token-based, in the same denominations as notes and coins</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Announced in Budget 2022–23; the RBI Act was amended (Finance Act 2022) so that "bank note" includes digital form. RBI Concept Note on CBDC: October 2022.</li>
<li>The e-rupee is <b>sovereign currency issued by the RBI</b> in line with its monetary policy, a <b>liability on the RBI's balance sheet</b> like banknotes, <b>legal tender</b>, and <b>freely convertible one-to-one</b> with bank deposits and cash.</li>
<li>It is <b>not insured against inflation</b>: like cash it earns no interest (so that bank deposits are not drained) and loses real value when prices rise.</li>
</ul>
<h4>UPI vs the e-rupee</h4>
<ul>
<li>UPI is a <b>payment system</b> that moves bank deposits, which are liabilities of commercial banks. The e-rupee is <b>money itself</b>, a direct liability of the RBI; banks only distribute wallets. So the liability for e₹ does <b>not</b> lie with users' banks.</li>
<li>A UPI payment debits one account and credits another, with interbank settlement through the RBI. An e₹ payment is like handing over cash: the token moves wallet to wallet and is final in itself.</li>
<li>Wallet-to-wallet e₹ transfers do not show in bank statements, so they offer more cash-like privacy for small values.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Features and the global picture</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Programmability</b>: money can carry conditions such as an expiry date or a permitted use (subsidies, fertiliser). The RBI added programmability in 2024, along with offline payments and UPI QR interoperability. China tested expiring e-CNY "red packets".</li>
<li>CBDCs can be exchanged directly between central banks on shared platforms, so cross-border payments <b>need not use the US dollar or SWIFT</b>. Example: Project mBridge (China, Hong Kong, Thailand, UAE, Saudi Arabia; the BIS left in 2024).</li>
<li>SWIFT (Belgium) is a messaging network; Russian banks were cut off in 2022. Alternatives: Russia's SPFS, China's CIPS, India's SFMS. India's rupee trade settlement through Special Rupee Vostro Accounts (2022) and UPI links reduce dollar dependence.</li>
<li>Over 130 countries are exploring CBDCs. Launched: the Bahamas (Sand Dollar), Nigeria (eNaira), Jamaica (JAM-DEX). China's e-CNY is the largest pilot.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
