addBookTopic("economy", "e5-1", `
<p>Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level, which means a fall in the purchasing power of money. Mild inflation usually comes with growth; high or volatile inflation hurts the poor, savers and fixed-income earners.</p>

<h3>Kinds of price movement</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Inflation</td><td>Sustained rise in the general price level</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Deflation</b></td><td>A <b>persistent fall in the general price level</b> (negative inflation), as in Japan from the 1990s to the 2010s. Dangerous because people delay spending and the real burden of debt rises (Irving Fisher's "debt-deflation"). It is not a fall in a currency's external value (that is depreciation or devaluation; see 8.2), not a recession, and not a fall in the rate of inflation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disinflation</td><td>A fall in the <b>rate</b> of inflation: prices still rise, only more slowly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reflation</td><td>Deliberate policy to lift prices out of deflation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stagflation</td><td>High inflation with stagnant output (1970s oil shocks)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Skewflation</td><td>Sharp rise in the prices of a few goods (onion, tomato)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Core inflation</td><td>Headline inflation minus food and fuel; tracks underlying demand</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hyperinflation</td><td>Extremely rapid inflation (Zimbabwe 2008, Venezuela)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>India's WPI inflation turned negative in 2015–16 and parts of 2023–24. CPI inflation fell below 2% in several months of 2025.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Causes of inflation</h3>
<p>A rise in the general price level can come from <b>more money supply</b>, <b>lower aggregate output</b> or <b>higher effective demand</b>: all three. The quantity theory of money puts it as MV = PY.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Cause</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Demand-pull</b></td><td>Aggregate demand exceeds supply: "too much money chasing too few goods"</td><td><b>Expansionary monetary or fiscal policy</b>, <b>fiscal stimulus</b>, <b>higher purchasing power</b>; post-COVID stimulus</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Cost-push</b></td><td>Rising costs of production</td><td>Crude oil shocks, higher wages, <b>wage indexation</b> (feeds a wage–price spiral)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Structural</td><td>Supply bottlenecks</td><td>Weak storage and farm supply chains</td></tr>
<tr><td>Imported</td><td>Costlier imports</td><td>Oil prices, a weaker rupee</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b><b>Rising interest rates</b> reduce demand; they are a cure for inflation, not a cause. Wage indexation is a cost-push factor, not demand-pull.</div>

<h4>Financing a deficit and inflation</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>most inflationary</b> way to finance a budget deficit is <b>creating new money</b> (borrowing from the central bank, "monetisation"). It adds directly to reserve money, which banks then multiply, without reducing anyone's spending elsewhere. Borrowing from the public or raising taxes takes existing money and is less inflationary.</li>
<li>India ended automatic monetisation through ad hoc Treasury Bills by the 1994 and 1997 agreements; <b>Ways and Means Advances</b> (temporary loans for cash mismatches) replaced them from April 1997.</li>
<li>The FRBM Act 2003 barred the RBI from buying primary issues of government securities from April 2006, except in emergencies. The escape clause was used in 2020.</li>
<li>Monetised deficit = the rise in the RBI's net credit to the Centre (see 7.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Who gains and who loses</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Gainers</th><th>Losers</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Debtors</b> (they repay in money worth less), including governments; holders of real assets (land, gold); producers whose prices rise</td><td><b>Creditors and bondholders</b> (fixed interest and principal lose real value, unless the bonds are inflation-indexed), fixed-income earners, pensioners, savers</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Inflation thus redistributes wealth from creditors to debtors. India issued Capital Indexed Bonds (1997) and Inflation-Indexed Bonds (2013) to protect investors (see 6.1).</p>

<h3>Growth and inflation</h3>
<ul>
<li>Economic growth is usually <b>coupled with inflation</b>: rising incomes and demand bring moderate price rises. The short-run <b>Phillips curve</b> shows a trade-off between inflation and unemployment.</li>
<li>"Goldilocks": high growth with low inflation, as India had in 2025.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Food inflation</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reasons for persistent food inflation around 2010–11 (RBI and Economic Survey):
 <ul>
 <li><b>Rising incomes changed consumption patterns</b> towards protein-rich and high-value foods (milk, eggs, meat, pulses, fruit, vegetables) whose supply did not keep pace.</li>
 <li><b>Structural constraints in the food supply chain</b>: poor cold storage and warehousing, many intermediaries, APMC restrictions, post-harvest losses.</li>
 </ul></li>
<li>Foodgrain area did <b>not</b> fall by anything like 30%; it stayed around 120–125 million hectares.</li>
<li>Tools: releasing buffer stocks (Open Market Sale Scheme), export bans or duties, stock limits, import duty cuts, Bharat-brand atta and pulses, Operation Greens for tomato, onion and potato (see 10.1).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Base effect</h3>
<ul>
<li>Year-on-year inflation compares this month's index with the same month last year. The <b>base effect</b> is the <b>impact of last year's price level on the current inflation rate</b>.</li>
<li>If prices were unusually low a year ago (a low, "unfavourable" base), this year's rate looks high even with small monthly rises. A high base makes current inflation look low.</li>
<li>Example: very low inflation in 2025 created an unfavourable base for 2026.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Controlling inflation</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Monetary</b>: <b>decreasing money in circulation</b> controls inflation: raise the repo rate and CRR, sell bonds (OMO sales). The RBI targets CPI inflation of 4% ± 2% (see 4.3).</li>
<li><b>Fiscal</b>: cut government spending, raise taxes, lower the deficit.</li>
<li><b>Supply-side</b>: better storage and logistics, imports, buffer-stock releases.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Inflation in 2026</h4>
<ul>
<li>After an El Niño-hit, weak 2026 monsoon and higher oil prices (conflict involving Iran disrupted Hormuz shipping), CPI inflation (new series, base 2024) rose to <b>5.54% in September 2026</b>, with food inflation at 7.74%.</li>
<li>WPI inflation was 9.68% in May 2026, mainly because of fuel.</li>
<li>The RBI raised the repo rate to 5.50% on 7 October 2026, its first hike since February 2023.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e5-2", `
<p>Inflation is measured by price indices that track the cost of a fixed basket over time. India has several, and UPSC asks who compiles each one, what it covers and its base year.</p>

<h3>India's price indices</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Index</th><th>Compiled by</th><th>Base year</th><th>Coverage</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Wholesale Price Index (WPI)</b></td><td><b>Office of the Economic Adviser, DPIIT</b> (Ministry of Commerce and Industry)</td><td><b>2022–23</b> (new series from 15 June 2026; earlier 2011–12)</td><td><b>Goods only</b>: primary articles, fuel and power, manufactured products. No services; excludes taxes. 957 items in the new series (697 earlier)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>CPI (Rural, Urban, Combined)</b></td><td><b>NSO, MoSPI</b></td><td>2024 (new series from 2026; earlier 2012)</td><td>Goods <b>and services</b> (housing, health, education, transport). CPI-Combined is the RBI's inflation target</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>CPI for Industrial Workers (CPI-IW)</b></td><td><b>Labour Bureau</b> (Shimla)</td><td>2016 (revised in 2020)</td><td>Monthly; used to fix dearness allowance of government and many industrial employees</td></tr>
<tr><td>CPI for Agricultural Labourers / Rural Labourers</td><td>Labour Bureau</td><td>1986–87</td><td>Used for minimum wages in agriculture</td></tr>
<tr><td>Producer Price Indices (output, input, services)</td><td>Office of the Economic Adviser</td><td>2022–23</td><td>Launched with the new WPI (June 2026); WPI to run alongside for about five years before it is discontinued</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>GDP deflator</b> is the broadest price measure, covering all goods and services produced (see 1.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>CPI vs WPI</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Food weight is much higher in CPI</b> (about 46% in CPI-Combined, 2012 series) than in WPI (about 24% for food articles plus food products, 2011–12 series). In the older series, food articles were about 14–15% of the WPI (2004–05 base) against about 46% in CPI-IW.</li>
<li><b>WPI does not capture services</b>; CPI does.</li>
<li>The RBI <b>does not use WPI</b> as its key measure. Since 2014 (Urjit Patel Committee), and formally under the 2016 framework, it uses <b>CPI-Combined</b> as the nominal anchor.</li>
<li>WPI measures prices at the wholesale (first-sale) stage; CPI measures what consumers pay at retail.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Frequency and base-year history of the WPI</h3>
<ul>
<li>From <b>October 2009</b> the headline (all-commodities) WPI moved from weekly to <b>monthly</b> release. Weekly figures for primary articles and fuel continued until 2012, so "monthly only" was debated, but UPSC accepted it.</li>
<li>WPI base years: 1981–82 → <b>1993–94</b> (series launched in <b>April 2000</b>, not April 1998) → 2004–05 (from 2010) → 2011–12 (from May 2017) → <b>2022–23</b> (from 15 June 2026).</li>
<li>Each new base updates the basket and weights. The 1993–94 series, for instance, gave <b>electricity</b> (fuel, power, light and lubricants) a higher weight than before. The 2022–23 series added solar, wind and nuclear power to the electricity group and moved crude petroleum and natural gas into fuel and power.</li>
<li>Why revise the base: an old base over-weights items that matter less today and misses new ones.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e6-1", `
<p>Financial markets are divided by the maturity of what is traded. The <b>money market</b> deals in short-term funds (up to one year); the <b>capital market</b> deals in long-term funds (over one year). Government securities span both.</p>

<h3>Money market vs capital market</h3>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Money market</th><th>Capital market</th></tr>
<tr><td>Maturity</td><td>Up to 1 year</td><td>Over 1 year</td></tr>
<tr><td>Instruments</td><td>Call, notice and term money; <b>Treasury bills</b>; cash management bills; commercial paper; certificates of deposit; repo; TREPS (formerly CBLO); commercial bills</td><td>Shares, debentures, bonds, <b>dated government securities</b>, mutual fund units</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Liquidity and working capital</td><td>Long-term investment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Regulator</td><td>RBI</td><td>SEBI (the government securities market: RBI)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So of the government bond market, call money market, Treasury bill market and stock market, <b>only the bond market and the stock market</b> are capital markets.</p>

<h3>Money market instruments</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Features</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Call money</b></td><td><b>Overnight interbank</b> borrowing and lending, unsecured. Notice money: 2–14 days; term money: 15 days to 1 year. The Weighted Average Call Rate is the operating target of monetary policy</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Treasury bills</b></td><td>Issued <b>only by the Central Government</b> (States use Ways and Means Advances and the Special Drawing Facility instead); 91, 182 and 364 days; <b>zero-coupon, issued at a discount</b> and redeemed at face value</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cash Management Bills</td><td>Central government, under 91 days, for temporary mismatches (since 2010)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Commercial Paper</b></td><td><b>Short-term unsecured promissory note</b> issued by highly rated companies, NBFCs and financial institutions; 7 days to 1 year; at a discount</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Certificate of Deposit</b></td><td>Short-term negotiable deposit issued by <b>banks</b> (7 days to 1 year) and financial institutions (1–3 years) to investors. Not issued by the RBI and not long-term</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>CBLO</b> (Collateralised Borrowing and Lending Obligation)</td><td>Money market instrument of the Clearing Corporation of India (2003): short-term borrowing against government securities, for entities without call-market access (mutual funds, insurers). Replaced by <b>TREPS</b> (Tri-party Repo) in November 2018</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repo / reverse repo</td><td>Collateralised borrowing (see 4.3)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Zero-coupon bonds</b> pay no interest: they are issued at a discount and redeemed at face value. They can be long-term and are issued by governments and companies, not only by banks.</li>
<li>Benchmarks: MIBOR (published by FBIL), SORR, MROR. The Clearing Corporation of India (2001) settles government securities, forex and money market trades.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Government securities (G-secs)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Gilt-edged market</b> means the <b>market for government securities</b>: free of default risk, "as good as gold".</li>
<li>The <b>RBI manages the debt of both the Centre</b> (RBI Act, Section 21) <b>and the States</b> (by agreement, Section 21A). It auctions State Development Loans (SDLs).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Feature</th></tr>
<tr><td>Treasury bills</td><td>91, 182, 364 days; Centre only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cash Management Bills</td><td>Under 91 days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dated securities</td><td>5 to 40 years (a 50-year bond in 2023); fixed or floating coupon</td></tr>
<tr><td>State Development Loans</td><td>State government dated securities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Floating Rate Bonds, STRIPS</td><td>Coupon reset periodically; stripped coupons and principal traded separately</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sovereign Green Bonds</td><td>Since January 2023 (see 6.2)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sovereign Gold Bonds</td><td>2015–2024; scheme discontinued in 2025</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Banks hold G-secs for their SLR (18%), which creates steady demand (see 4.3).</li>
<li>Primary dealers (21, banks and standalone) underwrite G-sec auctions and make markets.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Who can access what</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Retail investors</b> can bid in primary auctions of T-bills and G-secs on a non-competitive basis through stock-exchange platforms (NSE goBID, BSE Direct) using demat accounts. Since November 2021, <b>RBI Retail Direct</b> lets them open a gilt account with the RBI.</li>
<li><b>NDS-OM (Negotiated Dealing System–Order Matching)</b> is the RBI's anonymous electronic trading platform for G-secs, run by CCIL.</li>
<li><b>Depositories</b> (SEBI-regulated, Depositories Act 1996): <b>NSDL</b> (1996, promoted by IDBI, UTI and NSE) and <b>CDSL</b> (1999, promoted by <b>BSE</b> with banks such as SBI, Bank of India, Bank of Baroda and HDFC Bank, not the RBI).</li>
<li><b>Foreign portfolio investors</b> (formerly FIIs) can hold G-secs within limits, and without limits in specified bonds under the <b>Fully Accessible Route</b> (2020). Indian G-secs entered the JP Morgan GBI-EM index (June 2024) and the Bloomberg emerging-market index (2025).</li>
<li>The <b>LAF</b> is mainly for banks, but <b>standalone primary dealers</b> (registered with the RBI as NBFCs) can also use it, so some NBFCs can access it (UPSC key).</li>
<li><b>Stock exchanges can run separate debt segments</b> (NSE and BSE debt platforms, RFQ platforms for corporate bonds).</li>
<li><b>Insurance companies, pension funds and retail investors</b> can all trade in both corporate bonds and G-secs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Bond yields</h3>
<ul>
<li>A bond's price and yield move <b>inversely</b>. When market rates rise, older bonds with lower coupons lose value.</li>
<li>Indian G-sec yields are influenced by:
 <ul>
 <li><b>US Federal Reserve actions</b>: a Fed hike pulls foreign money out of Indian bonds, raising yields (2013 "taper tantrum", 2022).</li>
 <li><b>RBI actions</b>: policy rates, OMOs, liquidity, and its role as debt manager (e.g. bond buying under G-SAP 1.0 in 2021).</li>
 <li><b>Inflation and short-term interest rates</b>: investors want a yield above expected inflation.</li>
 <li>Also: the fiscal deficit and borrowing size, crude prices, the rupee, inclusion in global indices.</li>
 </ul></li>
<li>Yield curve: normally upward-sloping; an <b>inverted</b> curve (short rates above long) often signals recession.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Inflation-indexed bonds and gold schemes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Inflation-Indexed Bonds (IIBs)</b>: the principal (and sometimes the coupon) is linked to an inflation index. Benefits: investors are <b>protected from inflation uncertainty</b>, and since they demand no inflation-risk premium, the <b>government can pay a lower coupon</b>. There is <b>no special tax exemption</b>: interest and gains are taxable.</li>
<li>India's issues: Capital Indexed Bonds (1997, principal only); IIBs linked to WPI (June 2013); Inflation Indexed National Savings Securities–Cumulative for retail, linked to CPI (December 2013). Demand was weak. Abroad: US TIPS, UK index-linked gilts.</li>
<li><b>Sovereign Gold Bond Scheme</b> and <b>Gold Monetisation Scheme</b> (both 2015): aim to <b>bring idle household gold into the economy</b> and <b>reduce gold imports</b> (and so the current account deficit). They had nothing to do with FDI in jewellery.
 <ul>
 <li>Gold Monetisation: households deposit gold with banks and earn interest; the gold is lent to jewellers or auctioned.</li>
 <li>SGBs: "paper gold" issued by the RBI for the government, linked to gold prices, 2.5% interest, 8-year term (exit from year 5), capital gains tax-free on redemption. No new tranches after February 2024; discontinued in 2025 because soaring gold prices made it costly.</li>
 </ul></li>
<li>Indian households hold about 25,000 tonnes of gold. India is the second-largest gold consumer after China. Other measures: Indian Gold Coin (2015), import duty cut to 6% (2024), India International Bullion Exchange at GIFT City (2022).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e6-2", `
<p>The capital market raises long-term money for companies and governments. It has a <b>primary market</b> (new issues, such as IPOs) and a <b>secondary market</b> (trading on stock exchanges). SEBI regulates it.</p>

<h3>Shares, bonds and debentures</h3>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Bond or debenture</th><th>Equity share</th></tr>
<tr><td>Holder is</td><td><b>Creditor (lender)</b></td><td><b>Owner</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Return</td><td>Fixed coupon, paid whether or not there is profit</td><td>Variable dividend (discretionary) plus capital gains</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voting rights</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claim on liquidation</td><td><b>Before</b> shareholders</td><td>Residual (last)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Risk and return</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Bondholders bear lower risk than stockholders because they are lenders and because they are <b>repaid first</b>; both facts explain the lower risk.</li>
<li>IBC 2016, Section 53 "waterfall": insolvency costs → secured creditors and workmen's dues → employees → unsecured financial creditors → government dues → other debts → preference shareholders → equity shareholders last.</li>
<li><b>Debenture holders are creditors</b>, not owners. Convertible debentures can become shares; non-convertible debentures (NCDs) are a popular retail product.</li>
<li>Preference shares sit in between: fixed dividend and priority over equity, but behind creditors.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Types of bonds</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Bond</th><th>Feature</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Convertible</b></td><td>Can be exchanged for shares at a set ratio. Because the option has value, it <b>pays a lower interest rate</b>. The option to convert gives the holder some <b>indexation to rising prices</b>, since share prices tend to rise with inflation. FCCBs are a route for external borrowing (see 8.3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zero-coupon</td><td>No interest; issued at a discount</td></tr>
<tr><td>Callable / puttable</td><td>Issuer may redeem early / holder may demand early repayment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Perpetual (e.g. AT1)</td><td>No maturity. Yes Bank's AT1 bonds were written off in 2020</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inflation-indexed</td><td>See 6.1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Junk / high-yield</td><td>Below investment grade</td></tr>
<tr><td>Municipal</td><td>Issued by urban local bodies (Pune, Indore, Ghaziabad green bond)</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>Labelled (sustainable) bonds</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Bond</th><th>Use of proceeds</th></tr>
<tr><td>Green bond</td><td>Environmental projects only: renewables, clean transport, efficiency, pollution control (ICMA Green Bond Principles, 2014)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Social bond</td><td>Social projects only: affordable housing, health, education, jobs</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sustainability bond</b></td><td>A <b>combination of green and social</b> projects</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sustainability-linked bond</td><td>General purpose; coupon changes if the issuer misses sustainability targets</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blue bond</td><td>Ocean and marine projects (Seychelles, 2018, first)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transition bond</td><td>Hard-to-abate sectors cutting emissions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sovereign bond</td><td>Simply any bond issued by a national government</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>India's <b>Sovereign Green Bonds</b>: first issued January 2023 (₹16,000 crore in 2022–23) under the framework of November 2022, aligned with ICMA principles. They fund public green projects and earn a small "greenium" (lower yield).</li>
<li>The world's first green bond was the European Investment Bank's Climate Awareness Bond (2007); Yes Bank issued India's first (2015). SEBI has frameworks for green debt (2023) and other ESG debt (2025).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Stock exchanges and indices</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>BSE</b> (1875) is Asia's oldest stock exchange. The world's oldest is <b>Amsterdam</b> (1602, VOC shares); the NYSE dates from 1792.</li>
<li><b>NSE</b> (1992; screen-based trading from 1994) overtook the BSE in turnover in the mid-1990s and had the <b>highest turnover in 2000–01</b> (about ₹13.4 lakh crore against BSE's ₹10 lakh crore). It is the world's largest derivatives exchange by contracts.</li>
<li><b>Sensex</b> (1986, base 1978–79 = 100): <b>30 stocks</b> on the BSE, weighted by <b>free-float market capitalisation</b> (since 2003), so stocks get proportional weight. A rise in the Sensex means an <b>overall rise in the prices of its group of companies</b>, not that every share rose. It crossed 85,000 in September 2024. The 50-stock index is NSE's <b>Nifty 50</b>.</li>
<li><b>MCX-SX</b> (MCX Stock Exchange) was a <b>stock exchange</b> that began currency futures in October 2008; renamed the Metropolitan Stock Exchange of India in 2015. MCX itself is the largest commodity exchange.</li>
<li><b>IndoNext</b> (January 2005): an alternative trading platform promoted by the <b>BSE and regional stock exchanges</b> for small and medium companies. It did not take off; regional exchanges were later derecognised. SME platforms now: BSE SME and NSE Emerge (2012), with stricter rules from 2025.</li>
<li>GIFT City has India INX and NSE IX (which hosts "GIFT Nifty").</li>
<li>India's market capitalisation crossed about $5 trillion in 2024.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Index</th><th>Market</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>S&P 500</b></td><td>About 500 large US companies (an index of large-company stocks)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dow Jones Industrial Average (30), Nasdaq Composite</td><td>USA</td></tr>
<tr><td>FTSE 100</td><td>UK</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nikkei 225</td><td>Japan</td></tr>
<tr><td>DAX</td><td>Germany</td></tr>
<tr><td>CAC 40</td><td>France</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hang Seng</td><td>Hong Kong</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>SHCOMP</b> (Shanghai Composite)</td><td><b>China</b>, not Singapore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Straits Times Index</td><td>Singapore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kospi</td><td>South Korea</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Market terms</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Bear</b>: an investor who <b>expects prices to fall</b> (sells or short-sells). Bear market: a fall of 20% or more. <b>Bull</b>: expects a rise. <b>Stag</b>: buys IPO shares to sell on listing. <b>Lame duck</b>: a bear unable to meet commitments. Correction: a 10% fall.</li>
<li><b>Beta</b>: how much a stock moves relative to the market. β = 1 moves with the market; above 1 is more volatile; below 1 less; negative moves the opposite way. It measures <b>systematic (market) risk</b> in the CAPM. <b>Alpha</b>: return above the benchmark. Unsystematic (company) risk can be reduced by diversification; systematic risk cannot. Related terms: Sharpe ratio (return per unit of risk), standard deviation (total volatility), arbitrage (profiting from price gaps between markets), asset allocation (spreading money across asset classes), smart-beta funds (weighting stocks by factors such as value or momentum).</li>
<li>SEBI tightened equity derivatives (F&O) rules in 2024–25 after finding about 90% of retail traders lost money.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Financial instruments and funds</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>financial instrument</b> is a contract that creates a financial asset for one party and a liability or equity for another (Ind AS 32). <b>ETFs</b> and <b>currency swaps</b> are financial instruments; a <b>motor vehicle</b> is a physical asset.</li>
<li>Classes: equity (shares, equity ETFs, mutual fund units), debt (bonds, T-bills, CP, CDs), derivatives (futures, options, forwards, swaps), hybrids (convertibles, preference shares).</li>
<li>Central-bank currency swaps: RBI's SAARC swap framework, India–Japan ($75 billion), support to Sri Lanka (2022).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs)</h4>
<ul>
<li>SEBI (AIF) Regulations 2012. Privately pooled funds (trust, company or LLP) from sophisticated investors; minimum ₹1 crore per investor. Not mutual funds.</li>
<li>Of bonds, hedge funds, stocks and venture capital, <b>only hedge funds and venture capital</b> are AIFs; bonds and stocks are the traditional investments AIFs are an "alternative" to.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th><th>Leverage</th></tr>
<tr><td>I: socially or economically desirable sectors</td><td><b>Venture capital funds</b> (including angel funds), SME funds, social venture funds, infrastructure funds</td><td>Not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td>II: everything else not in I or III</td><td>Private equity, debt funds, fund of funds</td><td>Not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td>III: complex trading strategies for short-term returns</td><td><b>Hedge funds</b>, PIPE funds</td><td>Allowed within limits</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Categories I and II get tax "pass-through". AIFs in GIFT City are regulated by IFSCA.</li>
<li><b>Venture capital</b>: <b>long-term start-up capital</b> (usually equity) for new, high-growth businesses that cannot get bank loans. Stages: seed and angel → early (Series A) → growth → pre-IPO. Private equity invests in mature firms. SIDBI runs the Fund of Funds for Startups (₹10,000 crore, 2016; a second fund announced in 2025). India is the third-largest start-up ecosystem.</li>
</ul>
<h4>InvITs and REITs</h4>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>InvIT</th><th>REIT</th></tr>
<tr><td>Holds</td><td>Revenue-generating infrastructure: roads, transmission lines, pipelines, towers</td><td>Income-generating real estate: offices, malls</td></tr>
<tr><td>Regulation</td><td colspan="2">SEBI regulations (2014); trust with sponsor, trustee and manager; must distribute at least 90% of net distributable cash flow</td></tr>
<tr><td>Examples</td><td>IndiGrid, PowerGrid InvIT, NHAI's National Highways Infra Trust</td><td>Embassy Office Parks (first, 2019), Mindspace, Brookfield, Nexus Select</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Tax: InvITs are pass-through. Interest distributed to unitholders is <b>taxable</b>; dividends are exempt if the SPV has not chosen the concessional corporate tax regime. The 2023 statement reversed this, so it was wrong.</li>
<li>The Finance Act 2021 amended the <b>SARFAESI Act</b> so that <b>InvITs and REITs are "borrowers"</b>; lenders can enforce security against them. SARFAESI (2002) lets banks seize and sell secured assets of defaulters without going to court, and created asset reconstruction companies.</li>
<li>InvITs are central to the National Monetisation Pipeline (2021, ₹6 lakh crore). SEBI's SM REIT framework (2024) covers fractional-ownership platforms (assets ₹50–500 crore).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Credit rating agencies</h3>
<ul>
<li>Regulated by <b>SEBI</b> (Credit Rating Agencies Regulations, 1999), not the RBI. The RBI only accredits some as External Credit Assessment Institutions for bank capital.</li>
<li>SEBI-registered agencies: CRISIL (1987, first; majority owned by S&P Global), <b>ICRA</b> (1991; a listed <b>public limited company</b>, Moody's is its largest shareholder), CARE Ratings (1993), India Ratings (Fitch), Acuité, Infomerics. <b>Brickwork Ratings</b> (Bengaluru, 2007) is Indian; SEBI cancelled its registration in October 2022.</li>
<li>Global "big three": S&P, Moody's, Fitch. India's sovereign rating: Moody's Baa3, Fitch BBB−; <b>S&P upgraded India to BBB in August 2025</b>, its first upgrade in 18 years. Investment grade is BBB− and above.</li>
<li>Criticisms: the issuer-pays model and late downgrades (IL&FS, 2018).</li>
</ul>

<h3>New ways of raising money</h3>
<h4>Crowdfunding</h4>
<ul>
<li>SEBI's 2014 consultation paper defined it as soliciting <b>small amounts from many investors through a web platform or social network</b> for a specific project, venture or cause, and said it lets SMEs raise early capital at <b>lower cost without the rigorous procedures</b> of an IPO. UPSC accepted both statements.</li>
<li>In practice, <b>equity crowdfunding is not permitted</b> in India (private placement is limited to 200 persons a year; SEBI warned against unauthorised platforms in 2017).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Investor gets</th><th>India</th></tr>
<tr><td>Donation-based</td><td>Nothing</td><td>Allowed (Ketto, Milaap)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reward-based</td><td>A product or perk</td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Debt / peer-to-peer</td><td>Interest</td><td>RBI's NBFC-P2P rules (2017, tightened 2024)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Equity-based</td><td>Shares</td><td>Not permitted</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Other SME routes: SME IPO platforms, AIFs and angel funds, the Social Stock Exchange (SEBI framework 2022; Zero Coupon Zero Principal instruments for non-profits). Abroad: the US JOBS Act (2012), the EU's crowdfunding regulation (2021).</p>
<h4>Tokenisation of real-world assets</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Tokenisation</b> turns ownership of real assets (property, bonds, gold, art, invoices, carbon credits) into <b>digital tokens on a blockchain</b>.</li>
<li>Tokens can be fractional, traded <b>24×7</b>, settled almost instantly and programmed. This widens access and supports <b>financial inclusion</b>, giving individuals access to <b>high-growth investments</b> once open only to large investors.</li>
<li>Risks: legal enforceability, custody, cyber risk, valuation, investor protection.</li>
<li>India: RBI pilots in tokenised government securities and wholesale CBDC; IFSCA sandbox at GIFT City. Card tokenisation (RBI, 2022) is a different idea: replacing card numbers with tokens for security. Global: BlackRock's BUIDL fund (2024), Singapore's Project Guardian, the BIS's Project Agorá (RBI participates).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Carbon markets</h3>
<ul>
<li>Carbon markets are expected to be <b>one of the most widespread climate tools</b> (World Bank: 75+ carbon pricing instruments cover about 24% of global emissions, 2024). Article 6 of the Paris Agreement allows international trading.</li>
<li>Where governments <b>auction emission allowances</b> (EU ETS, California), firms pay the State for the right to emit, which <b>transfers resources from the private sector to the State</b> (the EU ETS has raised over €175 billion). UPSC accepted this statement but held that it does <b>not explain</b> why carbon markets are widespread; they spread because they price pollution and let emissions be cut where cheapest.</li>
<li>Compliance markets: EU ETS (2005, first), China's national ETS (2021, largest by emissions covered), UK, Korea, California–Quebec, RGGI. Voluntary standards: Verra, Gold Standard.</li>
<li>India: <b>Carbon Credit Trading Scheme 2023</b> under the Energy Conservation (Amendment) Act 2022. Compliance mechanism with emission-intensity targets for sectors such as aluminium, cement, chlor-alkali, pulp and paper, refineries, petrochemicals, textiles and steel; an offset mechanism for voluntary projects. BEE administers it; CERC regulates trading on power exchanges. Earlier: PAT scheme and Renewable Energy Certificates. Green Credit Programme (2023) rewards actions such as tree planting.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e6-3", `
<p>Beyond banks and stock markets, the financial system includes insurance, pensions and commodity markets, each with its own regulator. This topic also gathers the committees that shaped financial-sector reform.</p>

<h3>Financial regulators</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Regulator</th><th>Sector</th></tr>
<tr><td>RBI</td><td>Banks, NBFCs, payment systems, money and G-sec markets, forex</td></tr>
<tr><td>SEBI (statutory 1992)</td><td>Securities markets, mutual funds, AIFs, credit rating agencies, and since 2015 commodity derivatives</td></tr>
<tr><td>IRDAI (1999)</td><td>Insurance</td></tr>
<tr><td>PFRDA (statutory 2013)</td><td>Pensions: NPS, Atal Pension Yojana</td></tr>
<tr><td>IBBI (2016)</td><td>Insolvency professionals and process</td></tr>
<tr><td>IFSCA (2020)</td><td>International Financial Services Centres (GIFT City)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Forward Markets Commission</b> (1953, under the Forward Contracts (Regulation) Act 1952) regulated <b>commodity futures trading</b> (MCX, NCDEX). After the NSEL scam (2013) it was <b>merged with SEBI in September 2015</b>.</li>
<li>Futures in seven farm commodities (non-basmati paddy, wheat, chana, mustard, soybean, crude palm oil, moong) have been suspended since December 2021, with repeated extensions.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Financial Stability and Development Council (FSDC)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Set up in 2010 on the Raghuram Rajan Committee's advice as an apex body under the <b>Ministry of Finance</b> (secretariat in the Department of Economic Affairs). It is <b>not an organ of NITI Aayog</b>.</li>
<li><b>Chaired by the Union Finance Minister</b>. Members: heads of the RBI, SEBI, IRDAI, PFRDA, IBBI and IFSCA; Finance Secretary; Secretaries of Economic Affairs, Financial Services, Revenue and MeitY; Chief Economic Adviser.</li>
<li>It <b>monitors macro-prudential supervision</b>, financial stability, inter-regulatory coordination, financial literacy and inclusion. It is a coordination body, not a regulator.</li>
<li>Its sub-committee is chaired by the RBI Governor; the RBI's half-yearly Financial Stability Report reflects its assessments.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Insurance</h3>
<ul>
<li>The first life insurer in India was the Oriental Life Insurance Company (Calcutta, 1818); the first Indian-owned was Bombay Mutual Life Assurance Society (1870). <b>LIC</b> was formed in <b>1956</b> by nationalising about 245 insurers, so it is not the oldest.</li>
<li>General insurance was nationalised in 1972. <b>National Insurance Company</b> (Kolkata, 1906) was nationalised in 1972 and made a subsidiary of GIC. The four public general insurers: New India Assurance (Mumbai), <b>United India (Chennai)</b>, Oriental (New Delhi), National (Kolkata). A 2002 amendment made them independent of GIC; GIC Re is the national reinsurer.</li>
<li>Reform: R.N. Malhotra Committee (1993–94) → IRDA Act 1999; FDI cap 26% (2000), 49% (2015), 74% (2021) and 100% (announced in Budget 2025–26 and enacted in 2025). Goal: "Insurance for All by 2047".</li>
</ul>
<h4>Cyber insurance</h4>
<p>Individual cyber policies usually cover: loss of funds from unauthorised digital transactions, phishing and identity theft; <b>restoring a computer system after malware</b>; <b>consultants' fees in cyber extortion</b> (ransomware); <b>legal defence</b> if a third party sues; and counselling for cyber-stalking victims. Buying a <b>new computer after physical damage</b> is property insurance, not cyber cover.</p>
<ul>
<li>IRDAI's working group (2021) proposed a model individual product. Cyber-crime reporting: National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal and helpline 1930; I4C (MHA). CERT-In directions (2022): report incidents within 6 hours. "Digital arrest" scams rose sharply in 2024.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Aviation insurance</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Hull insurance</b> covers the <b>physical aircraft</b>: body, engines, avionics and on-board equipment, on the ground or in flight. Hull war insurance separately covers war and terrorism.</li>
<li>Other aviation covers: passenger liability, third-party liability, cargo, airport operators' liability, loss of licence (pilots), product liability.</li>
<li><b>Montreal Convention (1999)</b>, about 140 parties: <b>strict liability</b> for passenger death or injury up to about 151,880 SDR (revised in December 2019; about ₹1.8 crore) <b>without proof of fault</b>; above that, liability is unlimited unless the airline proves it was not negligent. It also covers baggage and delay and requires advance payments for immediate needs. India acceded in 2009 (Carriage by Air (Amendment) Act 2009). It replaced the fault-based Warsaw Convention (1929).</li>
<li>Indian aviation bodies: DGCA, AAIB, BCAS, AERA (see 11.3). The Bharatiya Vayuyan Adhiniyam 2024 replaced the Aircraft Act 1934. The Air India AI171 crash (Ahmedabad, June 2025) was compensated under the Montreal regime plus ex gratia from the Tata group.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pensions</h3>
<h4>National Pension System (NPS)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Introduced for new central government employees from 1 January 2004; opened to all citizens in 2009. Regulated by PFRDA (PFRDA Act 2013).</li>
<li>Who can join: any Indian citizen (resident or non-resident) and OCI aged 18–70; <b>State government employees</b> who joined service <b>after the date of notification by their State</b> (most States adopted it, though some have returned to the old pension).</li>
<li>Defined-contribution: Tier I (pension, restricted withdrawal), Tier II (voluntary savings). At 60, at least 40% of the corpus must buy an annuity; up to 60% lump sum is tax-free.</li>
<li><b>Unified Pension Scheme</b> (approved August 2024, from April 2025): an option for central employees under NPS, giving an assured pension of 50% of average basic pay of the last 12 months for 25+ years of service. NPS Vatsalya (2024) is for minors.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Atal Pension Yojana (APY)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Launched May 2015, administered by PFRDA. A <b>minimum guaranteed pension</b> of ₹1,000–5,000 a month from age 60, aimed mainly at <b>unorganised-sector workers</b>.</li>
<li>Any citizen aged 18–40 with a bank or post office savings account can join; there is <b>no one-per-family limit</b>. Since October 2022 income-tax payers cannot join.</li>
<li>After the subscriber's death, the <b>spouse gets the same pension for life</b>; after both die, the corpus goes to the nominee.</li>
<li>The government co-contributed 50% (up to ₹1,000 a year) for 2015–20 for early non-taxpayer joiners. Over 7 crore subscribers, nearly half women.</li>
<li>Other pension schemes: PM Shram Yogi Maan-dhan (2019; ₹3,000 a month for unorganised workers, 50:50 matching), PM Kisan Maan-dhan, IGNOAPS (see 12.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Committees on the financial sector</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Committee</th><th>Subject</th><th>Set up by</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>M. Narasimham</b> (1991 and 1998)</td><td><b>Financial sector and banking reforms</b>: cut SLR and CRR, free interest rates, prudential norms, new private banks</td><td>Government</td></tr>
<tr><td>Raja Chelliah (1991)</td><td>Tax reform</td><td>Government</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. Rangarajan (1993)</td><td>Disinvestment</td><td>Government</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>R.N. Malhotra</b> (1993–94)</td><td>Insurance reform; recommended an independent regulator and private entry</td><td><b>Government of India</b>, not IRDAI (which did not yet exist)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>L.C. Gupta</b> (1996–98)</td><td>Framework for <b>derivatives trading</b>; index futures began June 2000</td><td><b>SEBI</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>S.S. Tarapore (1997, 2006)</td><td>Capital account convertibility (see 8.2)</td><td>RBI</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Y.H. Malegam</b> (2010–11)</td><td><b>Microfinance</b>, after the Andhra Pradesh crisis; led to the NBFC-MFI category, margin caps and income limits</td><td><b>RBI</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Urjit Patel</b> (2013–14)</td><td><b>Monetary policy framework</b>: CPI inflation targeting and the MPC. Not housing-sector lending</td><td>RBI</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nachiket Mor (2014)</td><td>Financial inclusion; payments banks</td><td>RBI</td></tr>
<tr><td>P.J. Nayak (2014)</td><td>Governance of bank boards</td><td>RBI</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kelkar (2002, 2015)</td><td>Tax reform; revisiting PPPs</td><td>Government</td></tr>
<tr><td>N.K. Singh (2017)</td><td>FRBM review (see 7.2)</td><td>Government</td></tr>
<tr><td>Usha Thorat (2017)</td><td>Offshore rupee markets</td><td>RBI</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bimal Jalan (2019)</td><td>RBI's economic capital framework</td><td>RBI</td></tr>
</table>
`);
;
