// Economy book, Unit 7: Public finance.
addBookTopic("economy", "e7-1", `
<p>The Union Budget (the "Annual Financial Statement", Article 112) records what the government expects to receive and spend in a year. Everything in it is sorted into a <b>revenue account</b> and a <b>capital account</b>, and that split decides which deficit you are looking at (see 7.2).</p>

<h3>Receipts</h3>
<ul>
<li>The test for a <b>capital receipt</b>: it either <b>creates a liability</b> (borrowing) or <b>reduces assets</b> (disinvestment, recovery of loans) (NCERT Macroeconomics, Chapter 5).</li>
<li>A <b>revenue receipt</b> does neither. <b>Interest received</b> on loans is non-tax revenue: it creates no liability. Recovery of the loan principal, however, is a capital receipt.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Revenue receipts</th><th>Capital receipts</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Tax</b>: income tax, corporation tax, GST, customs, excise<br><b>Non-tax</b>: interest receipts, dividends and profits (including the RBI surplus), fees, fines, spectrum charges, grants received</td><td><b>Debt-creating</b>: market borrowings, T-bills, small savings (NSSF), external loans (e.g. <b>loans from foreign governments</b>), provident funds<br><b>Non-debt</b>: recovery of loans, <b>disinvestment</b> and strategic sales, asset monetisation</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Expenditure</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Revenue expenditure (creates no asset)</th><th>Capital expenditure (creates assets or reduces liabilities)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Salaries, pensions, <b>interest payments</b>, subsidies, administration, <b>grants to States</b> (even for creating assets)</td><td><b>Roads, buildings, machinery</b>, railways, defence equipment, <b>loans and advances given to States and UTs</b>, repayment of debt</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>capital budget</b> therefore includes spending on acquiring assets, loans received from foreign governments, and loans given to States and UTs.</li>
<li>Grants to States for creating assets are revenue spending for the Centre, which is why "effective revenue deficit" exists (see 7.2). <b>Effective capital expenditure</b> = capital expenditure + grants for creating capital assets.</li>
<li>Central capital expenditure: about ₹11.2 lakh crore (2025–26, about 3.1% of GDP); about ₹12.2 lakh crore budgeted for 2026–27.</li>
<li>Interest payments are the <b>largest single item</b> of Union spending: about ₹12.8 lakh crore in 2025–26, roughly a quarter of total spending, a third of revenue spending and close to 40% of revenue receipts.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Capital vs revenue for a company</h4>
<ul>
<li>Buying new technology (machinery, patents, software licences) creates a long-term asset, so it is <b>capital expenditure</b>.</li>
<li><b>Debt and equity are sources of finance</b>, not kinds of expenditure. Interest on debt is a revenue expense; dividends are an appropriation of profit.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Plan and Non-Plan expenditure (until 2016–17)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Plan</b> expenditure funded the schemes of the Five-Year Plans. <b>Non-Plan</b> covered everything else: <b>defence, interest payments, salaries and pensions, and major subsidies</b> (food, fertiliser, petroleum). Interest was the largest Non-Plan item (about 40% of Non-Plan revenue spending around 2001–02).</li>
<li>The distinction was <b>abolished from Budget 2017–18</b> (as the Rangarajan Committee, 2011, had suggested). That year the Railway Budget was also merged and Budget day moved to 1 February.</li>
<li>The Budget now shows revenue vs capital spending, and "Centre's expenditure" (establishment, central sector schemes) vs "transfers" (centrally sponsored schemes, Finance Commission transfers).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Union finances around 2001–02</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>External liabilities</b> in the Budget are shown at <b>historical exchange rates</b> (book value); the Economic Survey and Debt Status Papers show they are much larger at current rates.</li>
<li>Continued heavy borrowing kept <b>real interest rates high</b>.</li>
<li>A rising fiscal deficit hurt <b>private investment</b> (crowding out; see 7.2).</li>
<li>All four statements of that question, including interest as the largest Non-Plan item, were correct.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Union's fiscal deficit was 4.4% of GDP in 2025–26 and is budgeted at 4.3% for 2026–27. From 2026–27 the main anchor is the debt-to-GDP ratio (target about 50% ± 1 by 2030–31).</p>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e7-2", `
<p>When a government spends more than it earns, it runs a deficit and must borrow. How big the deficit is, how it is financed and how fast debt grows shape interest rates, inflation and growth.</p>

<h3>The deficit formulas</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Deficit</th><th>Formula</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Revenue deficit</b></td><td>Revenue expenditure − revenue receipts</td><td>Borrowing to pay for consumption (government dissaving)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Effective revenue deficit</td><td>Revenue deficit − grants for creating capital assets</td><td>Introduced in Budget 2011–12 (Rangarajan Committee); dropped from FRBM targets in 2018</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Fiscal deficit</b></td><td>Total expenditure − (revenue receipts + non-debt capital receipts) = <b>total borrowing</b></td><td>The most watched number: the government's total borrowing need</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Primary deficit</b></td><td>Fiscal deficit − interest payments</td><td>Borrowing for current needs, leaving out the cost of past debt. If it is zero, the government borrows only to pay interest</td></tr>
<tr><td>Budget deficit (old)</td><td>Total expenditure − total receipts (including borrowing)</td><td>Dropped in 1997–98</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monetised deficit</td><td>Rise in the RBI's net credit to the government</td><td>Automatic monetisation ended in 1997 (see 5.1)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Matching the old definitions: fiscal deficit = total expenditure over total receipts <i>less borrowings</i>; budget deficit = total expenditure over total receipts; revenue deficit = revenue expenditure over revenue receipts; primary deficit = total expenditure over total receipts less borrowings and interest.</li>
<li>Net fiscal deficit = gross fiscal deficit − net lending by the government.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Worked examples</h4>
<ul>
<li>Revenue expenditure ₹80,000 crore, revenue receipts ₹60,000 crore, borrowings ₹10,000 crore, interest ₹6,000 crore: revenue deficit ₹20,000 crore; fiscal deficit = borrowings = ₹10,000 crore; primary deficit = 10,000 − 6,000 = ₹4,000 crore. (A fiscal deficit smaller than the revenue deficit just means a surplus on capital account, e.g. large disinvestment.)</li>
<li>Fiscal deficit ₹50,000 crore, non-debt capital receipts ₹10,000 crore, interest ₹1,500 crore: <b>gross primary deficit = 50,000 − 1,500 = ₹48,500 crore</b>. The non-debt receipts are already inside the fiscal deficit, so they are a distractor.</li>
</ul>
<h4>India's numbers</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>Centre's fiscal deficit (% of GDP)</th></tr>
<tr><td>2009–10</td><td>About 6.5 (global crisis stimulus)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2016–17</td><td>About 3.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>2020–21</td><td>9.2 (COVID)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2022–23 / 2023–24</td><td>6.4 / 5.6</td></tr>
<tr><td>2024–25</td><td>4.8</td></tr>
<tr><td>2025–26</td><td>4.4 (revenue deficit about 1.5, primary deficit about 0.8)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2026–27 (Budget)</td><td>4.3</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So the fiscal deficit did not rise "steadily" over 2007–17: it jumped in 2009–10 and then fell.</p>

<h3>The FRBM Act</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act 2003</b> (rules from July 2004). It concerned <b>both the fiscal and the revenue deficit</b>: cut the fiscal deficit to 3% of GDP and <b>eliminate the revenue deficit</b> by March 2008. It did <b>not</b> require eliminating the primary deficit. Medium-term fiscal policy statements are laid with the Budget.</li>
<li>It barred the RBI from buying primary issues of government securities from April 2006, except in emergencies.</li>
<li>Targets were paused after 2008 and postponed many times.</li>
<li><b>N.K. Singh FRBM Review Committee (2017)</b>: make <b>debt</b> the main anchor: general government debt of <b>60% of GDP by 2023</b> (Centre 40%, States 20%); fiscal deficit of 3% for the Centre; an "escape clause" for shocks. The 2018 amendment adopted the 40% central debt target.</li>
<li>From 2026–27 the Centre uses debt-to-GDP as its main anchor, aiming at about 50% (± 1) by March 2031. Its debt is about 56% of GDP (2025–26) and 55.6% budgeted for 2026–27.</li>
<li>States have their own FRBM laws (deficit limit 3% of GSDP, with extra borrowing allowed for reforms).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Public debt</h3>
<ul>
<li>Public debt is a Union subject (Entry 35, List I). <b>Article 292</b>: the Union borrows on the security of the Consolidated Fund of India within limits set by Parliament. <b>Article 293</b>: States borrow only within India; under <b>Article 293(3)</b> a State <b>must get the Centre's consent</b> for any loan if it still owes the Centre money (or a loan guaranteed by it).</li>
<li>The Centre's liabilities were about 49% of GDP and the States' about 21% in 2017; the question reversed them.</li>
<li>The Centre sets a Net Borrowing Ceiling for States (normally 3% of GSDP; an extra 0.5% for power reforms). Off-budget borrowings by State entities are counted within it since 2022. Kerala challenged the ceiling in the Supreme Court (referred to a Constitution Bench in 2024).</li>
<li>Debt levels: Centre about 56–57% of GDP; general government (Centre plus States) about 80–82%.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Internal debt</h4>
<ul>
<li>Components: <b>market borrowings</b> (dated securities, the largest part, about 80%+, auctioned at market-related rates), <b>Treasury bills</b>, cash management bills, <b>special securities issued to the RBI</b> (including converted ad hoc T-bills), securities against small savings (NSSF), and special securities such as oil bonds and bank recapitalisation bonds.</li>
<li><b>Household savings reach the government</b> directly (PPF, NSC, Sukanya Samriddhi via the NSSF) and indirectly (bank deposits, insurance and pension money invested in G-secs).</li>
<li>External debt is only about 5% of the Centre's debt, mostly from multilateral and bilateral lenders, which limits currency risk.</li>
<li>Indian G-secs joined global indices: JP Morgan (June 2024), Bloomberg (January 2025), FTSE Russell (September 2025).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Non-financial debt</h4>
<ul>
<li>Debt owed by the <b>non-financial sectors</b>: households, non-financial companies and government (as tracked by the BIS and IMF). So <b>housing loans, credit-card dues and Treasury bills</b> are all non-financial debt. Debt that banks and financial firms owe one another is excluded.</li>
<li>India's household debt rose to about 40–43% of GDP (2024), driven by unsecured loans and credit cards.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Debt trap</h4>
<ul>
<li>A country is in a <b>debt trap</b> when it must <b>borrow just to pay interest</b> on existing loans, so debt grows faster than its capacity to repay. Having to accept IMF conditions, being refused loans, or paying high interest to the World Bank do not by themselves define a debt trap.</li>
<li>"Debt-trap diplomacy" is a charge against China's BRI loans (Sri Lanka leased Hambantota port to China for 99 years in 2017). Sri Lanka defaulted in 2022 and restructured in 2024; Zambia and Ghana used the G20 Common Framework (see 9.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Deficit financing</h3>
<ul>
<li>Financing a deficit by borrowing or, historically, by printing money. In the Plan era it was used to raise resources for <b>economic development</b>: investment beyond tax revenues.</li>
<li>Risks: inflation (creating new money is the most inflationary option; see 5.1), crowding out, debt trap.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Reducing the deficit</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Lowers the deficit</th><th>Does not</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Reducing revenue expenditure</b>; <b>rationalising subsidies</b> (DBT, removing ghost beneficiaries, fuel price deregulation); <b>downsizing the bureaucracy</b>; <b>selling PSU shares</b> (disinvestment is a non-debt capital receipt); widening the tax base</td><td><b>New welfare schemes</b> (more spending); <b>cutting import duty</b> (less revenue); expanding industries (more spending now); encouraging FDI (goes to the private sector); privatising higher education</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Disinvestment receipts have often missed their targets; the IDBI Bank stake sale (about ₹52,878 crore, approved October 2026) is the biggest pending deal.</p>

<h3>Fiscal stimulus and crowding out</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>fiscal stimulus</b> is <b>intense affirmative action to boost economic activity</b>: more government spending and/or tax cuts in a slowdown. In a recession, <b>tax cuts and higher spending</b> are stimulus; <b>abolishing subsidies</b> is consolidation, the opposite.</li>
<li>India: 2008–09 packages (excise and service-tax cuts plus spending); Atmanirbhar Bharat packages (2020). Income-tax relief in Budget 2025–26 (no tax up to ₹12 lakh under the new regime) and the GST 2.0 rate cuts (September 2025) were described as demand stimulus.</li>
<li><b>Fiscal multiplier</b>: the change in GDP per rupee of government spending. Capital spending has a higher multiplier in India (RBI estimate about 2.45, against about 1 for revenue spending). The spending multiplier is usually larger than the tax-cut multiplier.</li>
<li><b>Crowding out</b>: heavy government borrowing competes with private borrowers for the same pool of savings, pushing up interest rates and <b>displacing private investment</b>. It weakens fiscal stimulus. It is minimal in a liquidity trap.</li>
<li>Related ideas: Ricardian equivalence (Robert Barro: people save more expecting future taxes, cancelling the stimulus); fiscal dominance (big deficits push the central bank to keep rates low or monetise debt); financial repression (SLR creates a captive market for G-secs).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e7-3", `
<p>Taxes are the government's main source of revenue. UPSC asks how taxes are classified, who levies what under the Constitution, how GST works, and the reforms against tax avoidance and black money.</p>

<h3>Direct and indirect taxes</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Direct (burden cannot be shifted)</th><th>Indirect (burden passed to the buyer)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Personal income tax, <b>corporation tax</b>, capital gains tax, <b>wealth tax</b> (abolished 2015), gift tax, <b>Securities Transaction Tax</b> (2004), <b>Fringe Benefit Tax</b> (2005–09), <b>Interest Tax</b> on banks (abolished 2000); all administered by the CBDT</td><td>GST, <b>customs duty</b>, <b>excise duty</b> (now only on petroleum and tobacco), the old service tax, VAT and sales tax</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Direct taxes now exceed indirect taxes in the Centre's gross tax revenue. Personal income tax has overtaken corporation tax; GST is the largest indirect tax.</li>
<li>Between 2005 and 2010 the share of <b>Union excise duty</b> in gross tax revenue fell sharply (rate cuts, the 2008–09 stimulus, rising direct taxes).</li>
<li><b>Service tax</b> (1994–95, on Raja Chelliah's advice, three services at first) was an <b>indirect tax levied by the Centre</b>; the 88th Amendment (2003) added Entry 92C to the Union List. It was subsumed into GST.</li>
<li>Before GST, <b>sales tax</b> on goods such as toothpaste was <b>imposed and collected by the State</b> (Entry 54, State List). States agreed on <b>uniform floor rates of sales tax</b> from January 2000 to stop undercutting each other; this paved the way for State <b>VAT</b> (from April 2005), designed by the Empowered Committee of State Finance Ministers (Asim Dasgupta). VAT is a tax on the value added at each stage of the production–distribution chain, with credit for tax paid at earlier stages; its burden finally falls on the consumer. In India it was <b>basically a State subject</b>, not a Central one with States as mere facilitators.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Goods and Services Tax (GST)</h3>
<ul>
<li>101st Amendment (2016); launched <b>1 July 2017</b>. <b>Article 246A</b> gives the Centre and States concurrent power to levy it; <b>Article 269A</b> covers IGST on inter-State supply; <b>Article 279A</b> creates the GST Council.</li>
<li>Structure: CGST plus SGST/UTGST on intra-State supply; IGST on inter-State supply and imports. It is a <b>destination-based</b> tax with input tax credit, which removes the cascading "tax on tax" effect.</li>
<li>It subsumed central taxes (central excise, service tax, CVD, SAD) and State taxes (VAT, entry tax, octroi, luxury tax, entertainment tax, purchase tax), <b>replacing multiple taxes by multiple authorities with a single national market</b>. It does not directly cut the current account deficit, and claims that it would let India overtake China soon were exaggerations.</li>
<li><b>GST Council</b>: Union Finance Minister (chair), Minister of State for Finance and State Finance Ministers. The Centre has one-third of the vote weight and the States two-thirds; decisions need a three-fourths majority. <i>Mohit Minerals</i> (2022): its recommendations are persuasive, not binding.</li>
<li>Outside GST: alcohol for human consumption, and five petroleum products (crude, petrol, diesel, ATF, natural gas) until the Council decides; electricity.</li>
<li>Compensation to States for five years (to June 2022) through a compensation cess, extended to March 2026 to repay COVID loans.</li>
<li><b>GST 2.0</b> (from 22 September 2025): two main slabs, <b>5% and 18%</b>, plus <b>40%</b> on sin and luxury goods; compensation cess wound up. Toothpaste, for example, moved from 18% to 5%.</li>
<li>GSTN is the IT backbone (now a government company). The GST Appellate Tribunal began work in 2025. Monthly gross collections crossed ₹2 lakh crore (April 2024 and April 2025).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Exempt and taxed items (as in 2018)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Exempt (nil rate): <b>hulled cereal grains</b> (unbranded), <b>eggs (fresh or cooked)</b>, <b>printed newspapers</b> (even with advertisements), fresh fruit and vegetables, milk, curd, salt, books, unbranded atta.</li>
<li>Taxed: <b>processed and canned fish</b>.</li>
<li>From July 2022, pre-packed and labelled cereals, curd and paneer came under 5%. Rates change often.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Income tax: key ideas</h3>
<h4>Agricultural income</h4>
<ul>
<li>Taxes on agricultural income are a <b>State subject</b> (Entry 46, List II); the Centre's income tax (Entry 82, List I) covers income "other than agricultural income". Hence agricultural income is exempt from central income tax (Section 10(1)). Some States (Kerala, Assam) tax plantation income.</li>
<li>"Agricultural income" (Section 2(1A)) means rent or revenue from agricultural land, income from farming it or processing the produce to make it marketable, and income from farm buildings. Courts require basic operations on land (tilling, sowing).</li>
<li>So income from <b>allied activities</b> such as <b>poultry, dairy, wool rearing</b>, fisheries and beekeeping is <b>taxable</b> business income.</li>
<li><b>Rural agricultural land is not a "capital asset"</b> (Section 2(14)), so gains from selling it are not taxed. "Rural" means outside municipal limits by set distances (e.g. beyond 2 km of a town of 10,000–1 lakh people, 6 km for 1–10 lakh, 8 km above 10 lakh). Urban farmland is a capital asset.</li>
<li>Partial integration: agricultural income above ₹5,000 is added to other income only to decide the tax rate. Composite incomes: tea 60% agricultural, 40% business; coffee and rubber have their own splits. Nursery saplings count as agricultural income.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Capital gains</h4>
<ul>
<li>A capital gain arises from a <b>rise in the value of a capital asset</b>: land or a house that naturally appreciates, or a painting that grows in value. Higher sales of a product raise business income, not capital gains.</li>
<li>Short or long term depends on holding period (listed shares 12 months; property 24 months). Budget 2024: short-term gains on listed equity 20%; long-term 12.5% above ₹1.25 lakh; indexation removed for most assets.</li>
</ul>
<h4>New law</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Income-tax Act 2025</b> replaced the 1961 Act from <b>1 April 2026</b>. Under the new (default) regime, income up to ₹12 lakh is effectively tax-free (Budget 2025–26, with rebate), with a standard deduction of ₹75,000. Housing-loan interest deduction survives only in the old regime.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tax reform committees and incentives</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Vijay Kelkar</b> chaired the 2002 <b>Task Forces on direct and indirect taxes</b> (so the "Kelkar proposals" were tax-reform proposals). Direct taxes: <b>abolish wealth tax</b>, raise the exemption limit to ₹1 lakh (₹1.5 lakh for seniors), remove most exemptions, set up the Tax Information Network. It also suggested dropping the standard deduction and exempting dividends and long-term gains on listed shares. The final report softened the draft, keeping housing-loan interest relief. Kelkar's 2004 FRBM task force proposed a national GST; he also chaired the 13th Finance Commission. Wealth tax was finally abolished in 2015–16.</li>
<li>Raja Chelliah Committee (1991): tax reform and the start of service tax.</li>
<li>After the <b>Bhuj earthquake</b> (26 January 2001), a five-year <b>excise duty holiday</b> was announced for new industries in <b>Kutch</b>; it drew investment to Mundra, Kandla and Anjar.</li>
<li>Budget 2000–01 gave a <b>10-year tax holiday</b> for new industries in the <b>North-East</b>. Later schemes: NEIIPP (2007), NEIDS (2017), UNNATI (2024). Ministry of DoNER since 2001.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tax-GDP ratio and buoyancy</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Tax-to-GDP ratio</b>: tax revenue as a share of GDP. India: Centre's gross tax about 11.7–12% (2024–25); Centre plus States about 17–18%; OECD average about 34%.</li>
<li>Tax collections fall faster than GDP in slowdowns, so a <b>falling ratio signals slowing growth</b> (UPSC key; debated, since tax cuts can also cause it). It says nothing directly about income distribution.</li>
<li>The ratio did not rise steadily over 2007–17: it fell from about 12% (2007–08) to about 10% (2009–10), then recovered to about 11%.</li>
<li><b>Tax buoyancy</b> = % change in tax revenue ÷ % change in GDP; above 1 means taxes grow faster than GDP.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Taxing multinationals and the digital economy</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS)</b>: multinational tax planning that shifts profits to low-tax places (transfer pricing, intangibles, treaty shopping). The OECD/G20 BEPS Project (2013–15) produced 15 actions to <b>curb tax avoidance by multinationals</b>.</li>
<li><b>Two-Pillar Solution</b> (2021): Pillar One reallocates taxing rights to market countries; Pillar Two sets a <b>15% global minimum corporate tax</b> (in force in the EU, UK, Japan and Korea from 2024).</li>
<li><b>Equalisation Levy</b>: introduced by the <b>Finance Act 2016</b> as a separate levy, <b>outside the Income Tax Act</b>. So it is not covered by tax treaties, and foreign firms <b>cannot claim credit</b> for it at home. 6% on online advertising payments to non-residents (abolished from 1 April 2025); 2% on e-commerce operators (2020, abolished from August 2024).</li>
<li>India's other measures: General Anti-Avoidance Rule (2017), Significant Economic Presence (2018, effective 2022), country-by-country reporting, the Multilateral Instrument (2017).</li>
<li><b>Indirect transfers</b>: shares of a <b>foreign company</b> change hands abroad, but they derive substantial value from <b>assets in India</b>; India taxes such gains (Section 9(1)(i), Explanation 5, inserted retrospectively in 2012). The Vodafone case: the Supreme Court (2012) ruled against taxing Vodafone's purchase of Hutchison's Indian business through a Cayman company; Parliament then amended the law back to 1962. Vodafone and Cairn won international arbitration (2020). The Taxation Laws (Amendment) Act 2021 dropped retrospective demands for deals before 28 May 2012.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Black money and benami property</h3>
<ul>
<li>Black money is income on which tax was not paid. The government's <b>main worry is the loss of revenue</b> to the exchequer through tax evasion.</li>
<li>Measures: SIT on black money (2014), Black Money (Undisclosed Foreign Income and Assets) Act 2015, Benami amendment (2016), demonetisation and the PM Garib Kalyan disclosure scheme (2016), GST (2017), Fugitive Economic Offenders Act (2018), automatic exchange of information (from 2017), PMLA 2002, cash limit of ₹2 lakh (Section 269ST), PAN for large deals. Electoral bonds (2018) were struck down in February 2024.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Prohibition of Benami Property Transactions Act</h4>
<ul>
<li>"Benami" property is held in another person's name while the real owner pays. The 1988 Act stayed largely unused; the <b>2016 amendment</b> (in force 1 November 2016) made it work and renamed it the PBPT Act.</li>
<li>A transaction is benami <b>even if the owner is not aware of it</b> or denies knowing.</li>
<li>Benami property is <b>liable to confiscation</b> by the Centre without compensation.</li>
<li>There are <b>four</b> authorities (Initiating Officer, Approving Authority, Administrator, Adjudicating Authority) and an <b>Appellate Tribunal</b>, with appeal to the High Court. So it does have an appeal mechanism.</li>
<li>Penalties: rigorous imprisonment of 1–7 years and a fine up to 25% of fair market value. Exceptions: property in the name of a spouse, child or sibling paid from known sources; trustees.</li>
<li><i>Ganpati Dealcom</i> (2022) held the 2016 penal provisions non-retrospective; the Supreme Court recalled that judgment in October 2024 for fresh hearing.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Revenue and enforcement agencies (Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Agency</th><th>Role</th><th>Under</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Enforcement Directorate</b></td><td>PMLA 2002, FEMA 1999, <b>Fugitive Economic Offenders Act 2018</b></td><td><b>Department of Revenue</b>, not the MHA</td></tr>
<tr><td>CBDT</td><td>Direct taxes (statutory, Central Boards of Revenue Act 1963)</td><td>Department of Revenue</td></tr>
<tr><td>CBIC (formerly CBEC)</td><td>Customs, GST, central excise</td><td>Department of Revenue</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Directorate of Revenue Intelligence</b></td><td>Apex anti-smuggling intelligence; enforces the <b>Customs Act 1962</b></td><td>CBIC, Department of Revenue</td></tr>
<tr><td>DG GST Intelligence</td><td>GST evasion</td><td>CBIC</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>DG Analytics and Risk Management</b> (2017)</td><td><b>Big-data analytics</b> to catch tax evaders in customs and GST</td><td>CBIC</td></tr>
<tr><td>DG Systems and Data Management</td><td>CBIC's IT systems (ICEGATE, customs EDI, GST back-end), not the analytics wing</td><td>CBIC</td></tr>
<tr><td>FIU-India</td><td>Receives suspicious transaction reports under PMLA</td><td>Department of Revenue</td></tr>
<tr><td>Central Economic Intelligence Bureau</td><td>Coordinates economic intelligence</td><td>Department of Revenue</td></tr>
<tr><td>Narcotics Control Bureau</td><td>NDPS Act</td><td>MHA</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A "fugitive economic offender" faces an arrest warrant for scheduled offences of ₹100 crore or more and has left India and refuses to return; their property can be confiscated even before conviction.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e7-4", `
<p>The Constitution gives the Centre the more buoyant taxes and the States most spending duties. The <b>Finance Commission</b> bridges this gap every five years by dividing tax revenue and recommending grants.</p>

<h3>The Finance Commission</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 280</b>: constituted by the President every five years. A chairman and four members, with qualifications set by the Finance Commission (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1951. A quasi-judicial body.</li>
<li>It recommends:
 <ul>
 <li>the division of the net proceeds of shareable central taxes between the Centre and States (<b>vertical devolution</b>) and among States (<b>horizontal devolution</b>);</li>
 <li>principles for <b>grants-in-aid</b> to States (Article 275);</li>
 <li>measures to augment State funds for <b>panchayats and municipalities</b>;</li>
 <li>any other matter the President refers.</li>
 </ul></li>
<li>It does not attract foreign capital, distribute funds among public sector undertakings or plan jobs; transparency in financial administration is the CAG's and Parliament's concern; and plan grants were never its job (that was the Planning Commission's; see 2.1). The divisible pool excludes cesses, surcharges and collection costs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Successive Commissions</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Commission</th><th>Chair</th><th>Period</th><th>States' share</th><th>Notable features</th></tr>
<tr><td>13th</td><td>Vijay Kelkar</td><td>2010–15</td><td>32%</td><td>Designed a <b>model GST</b> with a ₹50,000 crore <b>compensation package</b> for States adopting it; <b>grants to local bodies as a share of the divisible pool</b> (a first)</td></tr>
<tr><td>14th</td><td>Y.V. Reddy</td><td>2015–20</td><td><b>42%</b> (from 32%: the largest jump)</td><td><b>No sector-specific grants</b>; only local body, disaster and revenue deficit grants, leaving States more untied funds</td></tr>
<tr><td>15th</td><td>N.K. Singh</td><td>2020–26</td><td><b>41%</b> (1% adjusted for the new UTs of J&K and Ladakh)</td><td>Performance grants; tax effort criterion brought back</td></tr>
<tr><td>16th</td><td>Arvind Panagariya</td><td>2026–31</td><td>41% (unchanged)</td><td>Constituted December 2023; report submitted November 2025 and tabled on 1 February 2026; accepted by the government</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Horizontal devolution criteria</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Criterion</th><th>14th FC</th><th>15th FC</th><th>16th FC</th></tr>
<tr><td>Income distance</td><td>50%</td><td>45%</td><td>42.5%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Population 1971</td><td>17.5%</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Population 2011</td><td>10%</td><td>15%</td><td>17.5%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Area</td><td>15%</td><td>15%</td><td>10%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forest cover / forest and ecology</td><td>7.5%</td><td>10%</td><td>10%</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Demographic performance</b> (rewards lower fertility)</td><td>—</td><td>12.5%</td><td>10%</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Tax and fiscal efforts</b></td><td>—</td><td>2.5%</td><td>— (dropped)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contribution to GDP (new)</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>10%</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Besides population, area and income distance, the 15th FC used <b>three</b> criteria: <b>demographic performance, forest and ecology, and tax and fiscal efforts</b>. "Governance reforms" and "stable government" were not criteria.</li>
<li>Demographic performance was added because southern States argued that using the 2011 population penalised their success in controlling population. The 16th FC's "contribution to GDP" criterion also helps them.</li>
<li>Tax effort had been used by the 11th and 12th Commissions, dropped by the 14th and reintroduced by the 15th.</li>
<li>Largest shares (15th FC): Uttar Pradesh (about 17.9%), Bihar (about 10%), Madhya Pradesh, West Bengal, Maharashtra.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The 15th Finance Commission's grants</h3>
<ul>
<li>Revenue deficit grants (about ₹2.95 lakh crore to 17 States), local body grants (₹4.36 lakh crore), disaster management grants (SDRMF/NDRMF), health grants (₹70,051 crore).</li>
<li>Performance grants: <b>₹4,800 crore</b> (2022–23 to 2025–26) for better <b>educational outcomes</b>; <b>₹45,000 crore</b> as an incentive for <b>agricultural reforms</b> (land leasing, efficient water use, exports).</li>
<li>Vertical share was <b>41%, not 45%</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The 16th Finance Commission (2026–31)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Kept the States' share at 41%. Added <b>contribution to GDP</b> (10%) as a horizontal criterion and dropped tax effort.</li>
<li>Stressed fiscal discipline: a fiscal deficit norm of 3% of GSDP for States, an end to off-budget borrowing, and subsidy rationalisation. It did not recommend revenue deficit grants and declined to bring cesses and surcharges into the divisible pool.</li>
</ul>
`);
