// Economy book, Unit 8: External sector.
addBookTopic("economy", "e8-1", `
<p>The external sector records how an economy deals with the rest of the world: what it buys and sells, what it borrows and invests, and what reserves it keeps for bad times. The organising framework is the balance of payments.</p>

<h3>The balance of payments (BoP)</h3>
<ul>
<li>The BoP is a <b>systematic record of all economic transactions</b> between a country's residents and the rest of the world over a period (normally a year). Among the old options, "all import and export transactions" was the closest description.</li>
<li>It always balances in accounting terms: current account + capital account + errors and omissions = change in reserves.</li>
<li>The <b>balance of trade</b> covers only <b>visible</b> merchandise exports and imports. The BoP covers both visibles and <b>invisibles</b>, so it gives the better picture.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Current account (no asset or liability created)</th><th>Capital and financial account (creates assets or liabilities)</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Balance of trade</b> (goods) + <b>balance of invisibles</b>: services (IT, tourism, shipping), primary income (interest, dividends, profits), secondary income (transfers, <b>private remittances</b>)</td><td><b>FDI</b>, <b>portfolio investment</b>, <b>foreign loans</b> (ECBs, external assistance, trade credit), NRI deposits, banking capital; changes in reserves (including <b>SDRs</b>)</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b>Remittances are current-account transfers, not capital. Foreign assets and SDRs are not current-account items.</div>
<ul>
<li>When foreign tourists spend in India, for example visitors to the 2010 Commonwealth Games in Delhi, it counts as an <b>export</b> of services (travel receipts). Ahmedabad will host the 2030 Commonwealth Games.</li>
</ul>

<h3>India's current account</h3>
<ul>
<li>India runs a large <b>merchandise trade deficit</b> (about $280–340 billion), offset by big invisible surpluses: services exports (net about $217 billion in 2025–26, led by IT) and <b>remittances</b> (India is the world's largest recipient, about $125–135 billion).</li>
<li>Current account deficit: about 0.6% of GDP ($25 billion) in 2025–26, with a surplus in January–March 2026. India had current account surpluses in 2001–04 and in 2020–21 (COVID year). A CAD of about 2.5% of GDP is considered sustainable (C. Rangarajan); above 3% is risky.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Reducing the current account deficit</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Devaluing the currency</b> makes exports cheaper and imports dearer, improving the trade balance after a lag (the J-curve).</li>
<li><b>Policies attracting FDI and FII funds</b>: UPSC accepted this, reasoning that FDI raises export capacity. Strictly, capital inflows finance the CAD rather than reduce it, so the key was debated.</li>
<li><b>Cutting export subsidies</b> makes exports less competitive and tends to widen the deficit.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Foreign exchange reserves</h3>
<ul>
<li>Four components: <b>foreign currency assets</b> (largest), <b>gold</b>, <b>SDRs</b>, and the <b>Reserve Tranche Position</b> in the IMF.</li>
<li>Managed by the RBI under the RBI Act; invested in high-rated sovereign bonds and deposits with other central banks and the BIS.</li>
<li>Path: about $1 billion (mid-1991) → crossed $125 billion in late 2004 (about $131 billion by December 2004) → about $705 billion (September 2024) → record of about <b>$741 billion</b> (August 2026), among the world's top five.</li>
<li><b>Import cover</b>: the <b>number of months of imports that reserves could pay for</b> = reserves ÷ average monthly imports. At least three months is considered adequate. India: about two to three weeks in mid-1991; about 11 months now.</li>
<li>Other adequacy measures: reserves to short-term debt (Greenspan–Guidotti rule: at least 100%), reserves to broad money, the IMF's ARA metric.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Gold</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Rank</th><th>Country</th><th>Official gold reserves (approx. tonnes, 2025)</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>USA</td><td>8,133</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Germany</td><td>3,350</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Italy</td><td>2,450</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>France</td><td>2,437</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Russia</td><td>2,330</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>China</td><td>2,300</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Switzerland</td><td>1,040</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>India</td><td>880</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Switzerland</b> is a <b>leading exporter of gold</b> by value because it refines about two-thirds of the world's gold (Valcambi, PAMP, Argor-Heraeus, Metalor) and re-exports it. Its reserves rank about seventh, not second. The IMF holds about 2,800 tonnes.</li>
<li>Central banks have bought record amounts since 2022 (de-dollarisation, sanctions risk). Gold crossed $4,000 an ounce in 2025 and $5,000 in January 2026. The RBI has repatriated gold from the Bank of England.</li>
<li>Largest gold miners: China, Russia, Australia, Canada, USA. India's Kolar Gold Fields closed in 2001; the Hutti mine (Karnataka) still works.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Guarding against currency crises</h3>
<ul>
<li>A currency crisis is a sharp fall in a currency, often with reserves running out and capital fleeing (India 1991, East Asia 1997, Sri Lanka 2022).</li>
<li>What reduces the risk for India: <b>foreign-currency earnings of the IT sector</b> (about $200 billion a year) and <b>remittances</b>. <b>Higher government spending</b> does not; it can widen the "twin deficits" and raise imports and inflation.</li>
<li>What gives immunity in a global financial crisis: <b>not depending on short-term foreign borrowing</b>, which can flee suddenly ("sudden stop"). <b>Opening to more foreign banks</b> and <b>full capital account convertibility</b> increase exposure to global shocks. India's cautious capital-account opening was credited with shielding it in 1997 and 2008.</li>
<li>Other buffers: large reserves, swap lines (SAARC, BRICS Contingent Reserve Arrangement, Japan $75 billion), a flexible exchange rate.</li>
</ul>

<h3>External debt</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's external debt: about $736–750 billion at end-March 2025, about 19% of GDP.</li>
<li><b>Most of it is not owed by government</b>: sovereign debt is only about 20%. Largest parts: commercial borrowings (about a third), NRI deposits (about 22%), short-term trade credit (about 18%).</li>
<li><b>Not all is in US dollars</b>: the dollar's share is about 54%, the rupee about 30% (masala bonds, FPI in rupee bonds), plus yen, SDR and euro.</li>
<li>Short-term debt is about 18–20%; reserves cover over 90% of external debt. India is among the less vulnerable emerging economies.</li>
<li>Budget figures show external liabilities at historical exchange rates (see 7.1).</li>
<li>Between March 2000 and March 2001 external debt rose (about $98.2 billion to $100.2 billion) because of <b>commercial borrowings and NRI deposits</b> (the India Millennium Deposits raised about $5.5 billion), while multilateral debt fell.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e8-2", `
<p>The exchange rate is the price of one currency in terms of another. This topic covers how it is set, how it is measured against many currencies at once, what devaluation does, and how freely the rupee can be converted.</p>

<h3>What determines a currency's price</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under a float, the market sets it through demand and supply of the currency: <b>demand for the country's goods and services</b> (exports), <b>political stability</b> (which draws investment), interest-rate differences, inflation, the current account, capital flows, central bank intervention and speculation.</li>
<li>The <b>World Bank does not fix exchange rates</b>; the IMF oversees exchange arrangements. UPSC's key left out "economic potential" as less direct (debated).</li>
<li><b>India's regime</b>: LERMS dual rate (1992), unified market rate (March 1993), and a <b>managed float</b> since. The RBI intervenes to curb volatility, not to target a level. The IMF reclassified India's arrangement as "stabilised" (2023). The rupee hit record lows in 2025–26 amid US tariffs and the 2026 oil shock.</li>
</ul>

<h3>NEER and REER</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>NEER (Nominal Effective Exchange Rate)</b>: a trade-weighted average of the rupee against a basket of currencies (the RBI uses 40- and 6-currency baskets). In the RBI's index, a <b>rise means the rupee has appreciated</b>.</li>
<li><b>REER (Real Effective Exchange Rate)</b>: NEER adjusted for inflation at home relative to trading partners. REER ≈ NEER × (domestic prices ÷ foreign prices).</li>
<li>A <b>rise in REER</b> means Indian goods have become relatively dearer, so trade competitiveness <b>worsens</b>; it does not improve.</li>
<li>If India's inflation runs above its partners', REER rises even when NEER is flat, so the two <b>diverge more and more</b>.</li>
<li>REER above 100 suggests overvaluation. In late 2024 the 40-currency REER crossed about 108, a reason cited for the RBI letting the rupee weaken. The RBI publishes both monthly.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Devaluation and depreciation</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Fall in value</th><th>Rise in value</th><th>How</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Devaluation</b></td><td>Revaluation</td><td>Official decision under a fixed regime</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Depreciation</b></td><td>Appreciation</td><td>Market-driven under a float</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>India devalued in <b>1949</b>, <b>1966</b> (36.5%) and <b>July 1991</b> (about 18–19% in two steps).</li>
<li>Devaluation <b>necessarily makes exports more price-competitive</b>. It <b>reduces</b> (not increases) the currency's foreign value. It does <b>not necessarily improve the trade balance</b>: that depends on the <b>Marshall–Lerner condition</b> (export and import price elasticities summing to more than 1), and in the short run the balance often worsens first (the <b>J-curve</b>). Costly essential imports such as oil can keep the deficit wide.</li>
<li>Side effects: imported inflation, a heavier burden of foreign-currency debt (ECBs), gains for exporters and remittance receivers.</li>
<li>After 1991 export growth did rise (about 20% a year in dollar terms in 1993–96), and the rupee was devalued, but the rise came from the whole reform package, not devaluation alone.</li>
<li>Competitive devaluations ("currency wars") invite retaliation. The US Treasury keeps a currency-monitoring list (India was on it at times between 2018 and 2023).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Defending the rupee</h3>
<ul>
<li>To stop a slide the RBI <b>tightens</b>: raises rates, sells dollars from reserves, eases rules to draw in foreign money. The government helps by <b>curbing non-essential imports and promoting exports</b>, encouraging <b>rupee-denominated masala bonds</b> and <b>easing ECB conditions</b> to bring in dollars. They would <b>not follow an expansionary monetary policy</b>, which lowers rates, drives capital out and weakens the rupee further.</li>
<li>2013 "taper tantrum": an FCNR(B) deposit swap window raised about $34 billion; gold imports were curbed (the 80:20 rule).</li>
<li>2022: higher automatic ECB limit ($1.5 billion), eased FPI debt limits, new NRI deposits exempted from CRR and SLR, rupee trade settlement through Special Rupee Vostro Accounts (July 2022).</li>
<li>Intervention works through spot, forward and offshore non-deliverable forward markets (see also 4.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Convertibility of the rupee</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Convertibility</b> means <b>freely converting the rupee into other currencies and back</b> at market rates, for permitted transactions.</li>
<li><b>Full convertibility</b> would mean a free float, direct exchange with any currency at any place inside or outside the country, and the rupee acting like any other international currency.</li>
<li>India has <b>full current account convertibility</b> (since <b>August 1994</b>, accepting IMF Article VIII) but only <b>partial capital account convertibility</b> (limits on ECBs, FPI and residents' investment abroad).</li>
<li>The rupee is <b>not convertible into gold</b>; currencies have been fiat money since the end of dollar–gold convertibility in 1971.</li>
<li><b>S.S. Tarapore Committees</b> (RBI, 1997 and 2006): roadmaps for capital account and fuller capital account convertibility, with preconditions such as a low fiscal deficit, low inflation, low bank NPAs and adequate reserves.</li>
<li><b>Liberalised Remittance Scheme</b> (2004): residents may send up to $250,000 a year abroad. Under the FEMA Current Account Transactions Rules (May 2000) limits for some current heads (travel, gifts) were reset, some more tightly; UPSC treated this as true but not caused by the dip in reserves that year.</li>
<li>Rupee internationalisation: RBI working group report (2023), rupee trade settlement (2022), local-currency deals with the UAE, Indonesia and the Maldives, wider rupee accounts for non-residents (2025).</li>
</ul>

<h3>From FERA to FEMA</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Foreign Exchange Regulation Act 1973</b> treated violations as criminal offences. The <b>Foreign Exchange Management Act 1999</b> (in force June 2000) made them <b>civil offences</b> with penalties: it aims to "manage" rather than "regulate" foreign exchange.</li>
<li>The Enforcement Directorate (Ministry of Finance) enforces FEMA, PMLA and the Fugitive Economic Offenders Act (see 7.3). The Supreme Court upheld the ED's PMLA powers in <i>Vijay Madanlal Choudhary</i> (2022).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e8-3", `
<p>Foreign capital comes in two broad forms: <b>direct investment</b>, where the investor takes a lasting stake and some control, and <b>portfolio investment</b>, which buys shares and bonds without control. A third channel is borrowing abroad.</p>

<h3>FDI and FPI</h3>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>FDI</th><th>FPI (formerly FII)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Stake</td><td><b>10% or more</b> of a listed company's equity; any stake in an unlisted company</td><td>Below 10% of a listed company</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nature</td><td>Long-term, lasting interest, often management control; targets <b>specific enterprises and sectors</b>; stable in crises</td><td>Passive, liquid, "hot money"; increases capital availability <b>in general</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Policy and regulator</td><td>DPIIT policy; RBI under FEMA</td><td>SEBI registration (FPI Regulations 2014, 2019)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The 10% line came from the <b>Arvind Mayaram Committee</b> (2013–14) and is in the FEMA (Non-Debt Instruments) Rules 2019. An FPI holding of 10% or more is reclassified as FDI.</li>
<li>FDI includes <b>subsidiaries of foreign companies</b>, <b>majority foreign holdings</b> in Indian companies, and <b>companies wholly financed by foreign companies</b>. Portfolio investment is separate.</li>
<li>Instruments counted in FDI: <b>FCCBs</b> (when converted), <b>GDRs/ADRs</b>, and FII holdings above the threshold. <b>NRE deposits</b> are bank deposits (debt-like), not FDI.</li>
<li>FDI's main characteristic: it is <b>largely non-debt-creating</b>. There is no repayment or interest burden; dividends are paid only out of profits. It also brings technology, management skills, jobs and export links.</li>
<li>After the East Asian (1997) and Mexican (1994) crises, <b>FDI</b> was seen as the best kind of inflow for a host country, since short-term debt and portfolio money flee fast.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Non-debt-creating flows</th><th>Debt-creating flows</th></tr>
<tr><td>FDI, FPI in equity</td><td>ECBs, external assistance, NRI deposits, short-term trade credit, FPI in debt</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Routes: <b>automatic</b> and <b>government approval</b>. Prohibited: lottery, gambling, chit funds, Nidhi companies, real estate business, tobacco cigars and cigarettes.</li>
<li><b>Press Note 3 (2020)</b>: FDI from countries sharing a land border with India (mainly China) needs government approval.</li>
<li>Gross FDI inflows about $81 billion (2024–25), but net FDI fell sharply (about $0.4 billion) because of repatriation and outward investment. FPIs sold heavily in 2025 and during the 2026 oil shock.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Where FDI comes from and goes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Top sources: <b>Singapore</b> (top since 2018–19), <b>Mauritius</b>, USA.</li>
<li>Mauritius long led because of the <b>India–Mauritius Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement</b> (1983): capital gains on Indian shares were taxable only in Mauritius, which had no capital gains tax. This invited treaty shopping and round-tripping. The 2016 protocol made gains taxable in India (for shares bought after April 2017); GAAR came in 2017; a 2024 protocol added a principal purpose test. In <i>Tiger Global</i> (January 2026) the Supreme Court denied treaty relief on the Flipkart exit to a structure lacking substance.</li>
<li>Sectors: cumulatively (2000–2025) <b>services</b> lead, then computer software and hardware, trading, telecom, automobiles. In 1991–2003 <b>telecommunications</b> was among the largest recipients (UPSC key); for approvals in 1997–2000 the key gave <b>engineering</b>. Telecom now allows 100% FDI automatically (2021).</li>
<li>2002 rules: 100% FDI in <b>tea plantations</b> with 26% to be divested to Indians within five years (not 33% in four); <b>print media</b> news FDI capped at 26%, with the largest Indian shareholder holding at least 51%. Now: tea 100% automatic with no divestment; news print and digital news 26% (approval); non-news publications 100%.</li>
<li>Insurance FDI cap raised to 100% (Budget 2025–26; see 6.3).</li>
</ul>
<h4>E-commerce</h4>
<ul>
<li>100% FDI (automatic) is allowed only in the <b>marketplace model</b> (a platform linking buyers and sellers). FDI is <b>not allowed in the inventory model</b>, so a foreign-owned platform <b>cannot sell its own goods</b>.</li>
<li>Press Note 2 (2018, effective February 2019) <b>bars</b> sellers in which the marketplace or its group holds equity from selling on it, and bans exclusive deals (Amazon-linked Cloudtail and Appario were wound down). The older 2016 rule had capped any vendor at 25% of sales. UPSC treated "ownership of big sellers is limited" as incorrect because such sellers are barred outright.</li>
<li>Exceptions: single-brand retailers may sell online; food products made in India may use the inventory model with 100% FDI (approval route).</li>
<li>Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules 2020; dark-pattern guidelines (2023); ONDC (see 11.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Participatory Notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>P-Notes are <b>offshore derivative instruments</b> issued by <b>SEBI-registered foreign portfolio investors</b> (earlier, FIIs) to overseas investors who want exposure to Indian shares <b>without registering</b> with SEBI.</li>
<li>Concerns: anonymity, round-tripping of black money, money laundering. SEBI requires KYC of ultimate beneficial owners and monthly reporting, and since 2017 bars P-Notes with derivatives as underlying except for hedging. Their share peaked at over 50% of FII assets in 2007 and is now about 2%.</li>
<li>FIIs and qualified foreign investors were merged into FPIs in 2014.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Borrowing abroad</h3>
<h4>External Commercial Borrowings (ECBs)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Loans raised by Indian entities from non-residents: bank loans, bonds, FCCBs, trade credit beyond three years, financial leases. Regulated by the RBI under FEMA.</li>
<li>Automatic route up to $1.5 billion a year per borrower; limits on all-in cost, minimum average maturity (generally three years) and end-use (no real estate or capital market).</li>
<li><b>US Fed tightening can cause capital flight</b> from emerging markets (2013 taper tantrum; 2022). Capital flight weakens the rupee, raising the rupee cost of servicing dollar ECBs. <b>Devaluation increases</b> (not decreases) the currency risk on ECBs. UPSC dropped the 2022 question because the second statement was ambiguous.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Masala bonds</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Rupee-denominated bonds issued abroad</b>; the <b>foreign investor bears the currency risk</b>. A source of debt finance for public and private entities, regulated under the ECB framework (minimum maturity three years).</li>
<li>First issued by the <b>International Finance Corporation</b> (the World Bank Group's private-sector arm) in 2014 on the London Stock Exchange. HDFC was the first Indian corporate issuer (2016); NTPC issued the first green masala bond (2016); Kerala's KIIFB issued them (2019).</li>
<li>Similar names: Dim Sum (yuan, issued outside China), Panda (yuan, issued in China by foreigners), Samurai (yen, in Japan by foreigners), Kangaroo (Australia), Uridashi.</li>
</ul>
<h4>NRI bond issues</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Resurgent India Bonds</b> (SBI, August 1998, after the Pokhran-II sanctions): issued in <b>US dollars, pounds sterling and Deutsche marks</b>; raised about $4.2 billion.</li>
<li><b>India Millennium Deposits</b> (2000): about $5.5 billion.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e8-4", `
<p>Trade connects India's producers to world markets. UPSC asks about trade policy, India's trade numbers over the years, its partners, and recent trade deals and disputes.</p>

<h3>Trade policy</h3>
<ul>
<li>Import substitution ruled until 1991 (see 2.1). The 1991 reforms cut tariffs and licensing; the rupee moved to a market-set rate.</li>
<li>The <b>EXIM Policy 2002–07</b> was liberal, market-oriented and pro-trade: it removed quantitative restrictions and promoted SEZs. <b>GATT</b> (1947–94) and the WTO did drive global liberalisation, but India's liberal policy came from its own 1991 reforms, so GATT does not explain it. India removed its last import controls in 2001 after losing a WTO case brought by the USA.</li>
<li><b>Foreign Trade Policy 2023</b> has no end date (DGFT runs it): target <b>$2 trillion</b> of exports by 2030; Districts as Export Hubs; RoDTEP refunds of embedded taxes.</li>
<li><b>Production-Linked Incentive schemes</b> (2020–21, about ₹1.97 lakh crore across 14 sectors) drew both Indian and foreign firms (Foxconn, Pegatron/Tata, Samsung). Sectors: mobiles and electronics, pharmaceuticals, medical devices, autos and components, advanced chemistry cell batteries, telecom, food processing, white goods, solar PV modules, textiles (MMF), specialty steel, IT hardware, drones.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Special Economic Zones</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>SEZ Act 2005</b> (in force February 2006). Objectives: additional economic activity, <b>promotion of exports of goods and services</b> (not services only), <b>promotion of domestic and foreign investment</b>, employment, and <b>development of infrastructure</b>.</li>
<li>SEZs are treated as foreign territory for trade and customs. About 270+ operate. A replacement law (DESH: Development of Enterprise and Service Hubs) was proposed in 2022 but not passed. 2025 rule changes cut minimum land for semiconductor and electronics SEZs to 10 hectares and allowed some domestic sales.</li>
</ul>

<h3>India's trade over the years</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>Facts</th></tr>
<tr><td>2001–02</td><td>Exports <b>fell</b> slightly (about −1.6% in dollar terms; UPSC treated the "−2.17%" claim as false) during the post-9/11 slowdown. Exports of <b>iron and steel, coffee, textiles and marine products</b> fell. Crude and petroleum products were <b>about 27%</b> of imports. After oil and gold, the biggest import was <b>pearls, precious and semi-precious stones</b> (rough diamonds cut and re-exported from Surat)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2002–03</td><td>Exports crossed <b>$50 billion</b> for the first time (about $52.7 billion). India still had a <b>trade deficit</b>; what it had in 2001–04 was a <b>current account surplus</b>. India has had merchandise trade surpluses only in 1972–73 and 1976–77</td></tr>
<tr><td>2004–05</td><td>Exports grew about 28% in rupee terms (about 30% in dollars, crossing $80 billion), not below 10%</td></tr>
<tr><td>2005</td><td>India's share of world merchandise exports was about 1%, not above 2%</td></tr>
<tr><td>2024–25</td><td>Merchandise exports about $437 billion, imports about $720 billion; total exports including services about $825 billion</td></tr>
<tr><td>2025–26</td><td>Record total exports of about $863 billion: merchandise $441.8 billion, services a record $421.3 billion (about 49% of the total)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>India's share of world <b>merchandise</b> exports is about <b>1.8%</b> (ranked 17th–18th), not 3.2%. Its share of commercial <b>services</b> exports is about 4.3–4.5% (seventh).</li>
<li>Top merchandise exports now: petroleum products, electronics (smartphones became the top item in 2024–25), engineering goods, gems and jewellery, pharmaceuticals, chemicals, rice. Top imports: crude oil, electronics, gold, coal, edible oil. Crude import dependence is about 88%; Russia became the top crude supplier after 2022.</li>
<li>India cuts and polishes about 90% of the world's diamonds.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Statements about "the present" (2020)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Merchandise exports are less than merchandise imports (true); services exports exceed services imports (true); imports of iron and steel, chemicals, fertilisers and machinery had not clearly fallen. India usually has a current account deficit, but it had a surplus in 2020–21, which is likely why UPSC dropped the question.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Trading partners and trade deals</h3>
<ul>
<li>Around 2000–02 the <b>USA</b> was India's largest trading partner. In 2024–25 it was again the largest (about $132 billion), then China (about $128 billion, with a deficit near $100 billion) and the UAE.</li>
<li>Free trade agreements: UAE (CEPA, 2022), Australia (ECTA, 2022), EFTA (TEPA, in force 1 October 2025), UK (CETA, signed July 2025, in force 15 July 2026), Oman (CEPA, signed December 2025, in force 1 June 2026). India–EU FTA negotiations were concluded at the summit of 27 January 2026; signing and ratification are pending.</li>
<li>US tariffs: raised to 50% on many Indian goods in August 2025 (25% "reciprocal" plus 25% over Russian oil purchases), hitting textiles, gems and shrimp. An interim framework in February 2026 cut them to 18%. After the US Supreme Court struck down tariffs imposed under IEEPA (February 2026), a temporary 10% global tariff under Section 122 applied.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Apples, GM food and India–US trade</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>India does import apples from the USA</b> (Washington apples). In 2023 it removed the 20% retaliatory duty imposed in 2019 over US steel and aluminium tariffs, as part of settling WTO disputes; imports rose. The 2023 deal also covered walnuts, almonds, chickpeas and lentils.</li>
<li><b>GM food cannot be imported without approval</b> of the competent authority: FSSAI under Section 22 of the Food Safety and Standards Act 2006 (and GEAC for living modified organisms). Since 2021 a GM-free certificate is needed for imports of 24 food crops. US apples are not GM, so the two statements are unrelated.</li>
<li>GM crops in India: <b>Bt cotton</b> (2002) is the only one approved for commercial cultivation. Bt brinjal: moratorium since 2010. GM mustard (DMH-11): environmental release approved in 2022; split Supreme Court verdict (July 2024) asked for a national GM policy. Genome-edited crops without foreign DNA (SDN-1, SDN-2) were exempted in 2022; India released the world's first genome-edited rice varieties (DRR Dhan 100 Kamala, Pusa DST Rice 1) in May 2025. GEAC sits under the Environment Ministry (Rules 1989 under the Environment Protection Act 1986).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_economy_e9.js
// Economy book, Unit 9: International economic institutions and reports.
addBookTopic("economy", "e9-1", `
<p>In July 1944, 44 nations (India among them) met at Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, to design the post-war economic order. The two "Bretton Woods twins" it created, the IMF and the World Bank, still sit at the centre of global finance.</p>

<h3>The Bretton Woods Conference</h3>
<ul>
<li>Officially the <b>United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference</b> (July 1944). It set up the <b>IMF</b> and the <b>IBRD</b>. India's delegation was led by Sir Jeremy Raisman and included C.D. Deshmukh.</li>
<li>GATT was not actually signed there: it came from separate talks in Geneva (1947) after the planned International Trade Organization failed. UPSC's intended answer for "the conference that set up IBRD, GATT and IMF" was still Bretton Woods.</li>
<li><b>Bretton Woods system</b>: fixed exchange rates pegged to the dollar, and the dollar convertible to gold at $35 an ounce. Nixon ended gold convertibility in 1971.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The International Monetary Fund (IMF)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Headquarters Washington DC; 191 members; India a founder member. Its Managing Director is traditionally European (Kristalina Georgieva).</li>
<li>It lends <b>only to member countries</b>, usually for balance-of-payments problems and usually with conditions.</li>
<li><b>Quotas</b> decide a member's subscription, votes and access to funds. India's share is about 2.75%. The 16th General Review (2023) raised quotas by 50% without changing shares.</li>
<li>Governance: Board of Governors (one per member; for India, the Finance Minister, with the RBI Governor as alternate), Executive Board of 25 directors (India's constituency includes Bangladesh, Sri Lanka and Bhutan), Managing Director.</li>
<li>The <b>International Monetary and Financial Committee (IMFC)</b> (25 members, replaced the Interim Committee in 1999) <b>discusses global economic concerns and advises the IMF</b> on its work; it meets twice a year. The <b>World Bank participates as an observer</b>. The Development Committee is the joint World Bank–IMF ministerial body on development.</li>
<li>Training: IMF–Singapore Regional Training Institute (1998) trains officials in macroeconomic analysis; South Asia Regional Training and Technical Assistance Center (New Delhi, 2017).</li>
<li>Chief Economists: <b>Raghuram Rajan</b> (2003–06, the youngest and the first from an emerging economy; RBI Governor 2013–16), Gita Gopinath (2019–22; then First Deputy MD to 2025), now Pierre-Olivier Gourinchas. At the World Bank: Kaushik Basu (2012–16), now Indermit Gill. India's Chief Economic Adviser: V. Anantha Nageswaran (since 2022).</li>
<li>Reports: World Economic Outlook, Global Financial Stability Report, Fiscal Monitor, External Sector Report (see 9.3).</li>
</ul>
<h4>IMF lending facilities</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Facility</th><th>Use</th></tr>
<tr><td>Stand-By Arrangement</td><td>Short-term BoP problems (12–24 months); India used it in 1981 and 1991</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extended Fund Facility</td><td>Medium-term structural BoP problems (Pakistan $7 billion, 2024; Sri Lanka $3 billion, 2023)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extended Credit Facility</td><td>Concessional, for low-income countries</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flexible Credit Line</td><td>Precautionary, for very strong economies (Mexico, Chile)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Precautionary and Liquidity Line</td><td>Sound economies with some vulnerabilities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Resilience and Sustainability Facility (2022)</td><td>Long-term finance for climate change and pandemic preparedness</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Rapid Financing Instrument</b> (all members) and <b>Rapid Credit Facility</b> (concessional, low-income)</td><td><b>IMF emergency windows</b> for urgent needs (disasters, pandemics, commodity shocks), without a full programme; heavily used in 2020–21</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Poverty Reduction and Growth Facility</b> (1999) was an <b>IMF</b> concessional facility (successor of ESAF), not an IDA one; replaced in 2010 by the Poverty Reduction and Growth Trust.</li>
<li>Debt relief: HIPC Initiative (1996), MDRI (2005), G20 Common Framework (2020; see 9.3).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Reserve (gold) tranche</h4>
<ul>
<li>Each member pays its quota, 25% of it originally in gold (hence "gold tranche"), now in SDRs or hard currencies. This <b>reserve tranche</b> is a member's own money at the IMF: it can be drawn at any time <b>without conditions or interest</b>. So it is a <b>credit facility granted by the IMF to its members</b> and part of their forex reserves.</li>
<li>Drawings beyond it (credit tranches) carry conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Special Drawing Rights (SDR) and international liquidity</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>International liquidity</b> is the supply of internationally accepted means of payment: <b>dollars and other hard currencies</b>, gold, SDRs and IMF reserve positions. A shortage makes it hard to pay for imports and debts (India 1991).</li>
<li>The IMF created the <b>SDR in 1969</b> to add to world liquidity (the Triffin dilemma). It is an international reserve asset, "paper gold", an <b>artificial currency</b>; not a currency in circulation.</li>
<li>Allocated in proportion to quotas. The largest allocation was about $650 billion in August 2021; India received about $17.9 billion.</li>
<li>Its value is based on a basket reviewed every five years. The <b>Chinese renminbi</b> joined from <b>1 October 2016</b> (decided November 2015). The SDR interest rate tracks short-term rates of the basket currencies.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Currency</th><th>SDR weight (2022 review)</th></tr>
<tr><td>US dollar</td><td>43.38%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Euro</td><td>29.31%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chinese renminbi</td><td>12.28%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Japanese yen</td><td>7.59%</td></tr>
<tr><td>British pound</td><td>7.44%</td></tr>
</table>
<p>India's reserves include SDRs and the reserve tranche alongside foreign currency assets and gold (record about $741 billion in August 2026; see 8.1).</p>

<h3>The World Bank Group</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Institution</th><th>Year</th><th>Role</th><th>India a member?</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>IBRD</b> (International Bank for Reconstruction and Development)</td><td>1944</td><td>Set up to <b>help Europe rebuild after World War II</b> (first loan to France, 1947); now gives <b>loans and guarantees to middle-income</b> and creditworthy low-income governments</td><td>Yes (founder)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>IDA</b> (International Development Association)</td><td>1960</td><td>Concessional loans and grants to the poorest countries; <b>administered by the IBRD</b> (same staff, headquarters and President). India was its largest borrower, "graduated" in 2014 and is now a donor. IDA21 replenishment about $100 billion (December 2024)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>IFC</b> (International Finance Corporation)</td><td>1956</td><td>Lends to the private sector; issued the first masala bond (see 8.3)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>MIGA</td><td>1988</td><td>Political-risk insurance for investors</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>ICSID</td><td>1966</td><td>Investor–state dispute arbitration</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>"The World Bank" usually means IBRD plus IDA. The IBRD does <b>not</b> work "single-handedly": it works with the other Group institutions, other development banks, the IMF and governments.</li>
<li>Its President is traditionally American (Ajay Banga since 2023).</li>
<li>Reports: World Development Report, Global Economic Prospects, the former Doing Business report and its successor B-READY, Human Capital Index, Logistics Performance Index (see 9.3).</li>
<li>Other development banks: Asian Development Bank (Manila, 1966), New Development Bank (BRICS, Shanghai, 2015), AIIB (Beijing, 2016).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e9-2", `
<p>The World Trade Organization (WTO) came into being on 1 January 1995 (Marrakesh Agreement), at the end of the Uruguay Round (1986–94), and replaced GATT. Its agreements cover goods, services, intellectual property and investment measures.</p>

<h3>WTO agreements</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Agreement</th><th>Subject</th></tr>
<tr><td>GATT 1994</td><td>Trade in goods</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Agreement on Agriculture</b></td><td>Market access, domestic support (the "boxes"), export competition</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>SPS Agreement</b></td><td>Sanitary and phytosanitary measures: food safety, animal and plant health must be science-based</td></tr>
<tr><td>TBT Agreement</td><td>Other technical standards</td></tr>
<tr><td>GATS</td><td>Trade in services (four modes)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>TRIPS</b></td><td>Trade-related intellectual property rights</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>TRIMs</b></td><td>Trade-related investment measures (goods only)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The Appellate Body has been non-functional since December 2019 because the USA blocked appointments.</li>
<li>Ministerial Conferences: MC12 (Geneva, 2022); MC13 (Abu Dhabi, 2024) failed to settle public stockholding; MC14 (Yaoundé, Cameroon, March 2026) ended without a declaration, the e-commerce duty moratorium lapsed and public stockholding stayed unresolved.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Agriculture: the boxes and the Peace Clause</h3>
<p>The terms "Agreement on Agriculture", "SPS Agreement" and "Peace Clause" all belong to the <b>WTO</b>. Domestic farm support is sorted into "boxes" by how much it distorts trade.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Box</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Limit</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Amber</b></td><td>Trade-distorting support: market price support (e.g. MSP procurement), input subsidies</td><td>De minimis: 10% of value of production for developing countries, 5% for developed</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Blue</b></td><td>Amber support tied to production-limiting conditions</td><td>No limit</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Green</b></td><td>Minimal distortion: research, extension, food stocks, income support not linked to production</td><td>No limit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Development box (Article 6.2)</td><td>Investment and input subsidies for low-income farmers in developing countries</td><td>Exempt</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Export subsidies on farm goods were to be eliminated (Nairobi, 2015).</li>
<li><b>Peace Clause</b> (Bali Ministerial, 2013): developing countries' <b>public stockholding for food security</b> cannot be legally challenged even if it breaches the 10% limit; extended indefinitely in 2014 until a permanent solution. India invoked it first in 2020 for rice (support above 10% from 2018–19). India seeks a permanent solution.</li>
</ul>

<h3>TRIMs</h3>
<ul>
<li>The TRIMs Agreement bans investment measures that break GATT Article III (national treatment) or Article XI (quantitative restrictions): <b>local content rules</b>, trade-balancing rules, and <b>import restrictions on foreign investors</b> linked to their exports.</li>
<li>It applies <b>only to trade in goods</b> (services fall under GATS).</li>
<li>It does <b>not regulate foreign investment as such</b> (entry, ownership caps); only trade-distorting conditions attached to it.</li>
<li>India opposes the WTO's Investment Facilitation for Development agreement.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Domestic Content Requirement in solar power</h4>
<ul>
<li>Under the Jawaharlal Nehru National Solar Mission (2010) some projects had to use Indian-made cells and modules: the <b>Domestic Content Requirement</b>, to develop <b>solar power production</b> at home.</li>
<li>The USA challenged it (2013); the WTO panel (2016) and Appellate Body ruled it violated national treatment and TRIMs. India then limited DCR to government projects (allowed under the government-procurement exception).</li>
<li>DCR today: CPSU Scheme Phase II, PM-KUSUM, PM Surya Ghar rooftop scheme. Other support: the Approved List of Models and Manufacturers, basic customs duty (40% on modules, 25% on cells from 2022), PLI for high-efficiency modules. India has its own WTO cases against US State-level local-content rules.</li>
</ul>

<h3>TRIPS and India's IP laws</h3>
<ul>
<li>India enacted the <b>Geographical Indications of Goods (Registration and Protection) Act 1999</b> (in force September 2003) to meet its <b>WTO (TRIPS)</b> obligations (Articles 22–24).</li>
<li>Other TRIPS-related laws: Patents (Amendment) Acts 1999, 2002 and 2005 (product patents), Trade Marks Act 1999, Designs Act 2000, Semiconductor Integrated Circuits Layout-Design Act 2000, Protection of Plant Varieties and Farmers' Rights Act 2001.</li>
<li>Doha Declaration on TRIPS and Public Health (2001) affirms compulsory licensing. India's first compulsory licence: Natco for Bayer's Nexavar (2012).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Geographical Indications</h4>
<ul>
<li>A GI identifies goods whose quality or reputation comes from their place of origin. The <b>GI Registry is in Chennai</b> (under the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks, DPIIT). Registration lasts 10 years and is renewable indefinitely.</li>
<li>First Indian GI: <b>Darjeeling tea</b> (2004). India has over 650 GIs; Uttar Pradesh and Tamil Nadu have the most.</li>
<li>GIs cover <b>agricultural, natural and manufactured goods, handicrafts and foodstuffs</b> (Basmati, Alphonso, Kanchipuram silk, Pashmina, Channapatna toys, Mysore sandal soap, Feni, Kolhapuri chappal, Rasgulla, Tirupati laddu), not only farm goods and crafts.</li>
<li>TRIPS Article 23 gives extra protection only to wines and spirits; India wants it for all products.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Trade mark</th><th>Geographical Indication</th></tr>
<tr><td>Right of an <b>individual or company</b></td><td><b>Collective right</b> of a region's producers</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Can be licensed</b> or assigned</td><td><b>Cannot be assigned, licensed or mortgaged</b> (Section 24, GI Act)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Goods and services</td><td>Agricultural, natural, manufactured goods, handicrafts, foodstuffs</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>National IPR Policy (May 2016)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Slogan: "Creative India; Innovative India". It <b>reiterates India's commitment to the Doha Development Agenda and TRIPS</b>, while using TRIPS flexibilities (public health).</li>
<li><b>DIPP (now DPIIT)</b> was made the <b>nodal department for all IPRs</b>; copyright (from the Culture Ministry) and semiconductor layout designs (from MeitY) moved to it in 2016.</li>
<li>Seven objectives: awareness and promotion; generation of IPRs; legal framework; administration and management; commercialisation; enforcement and adjudication; human capital. CIPAM (Cell for IPR Promotion and Management) works under DPIIT.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e9-3", `
<p>Many UPSC questions simply ask who publishes a report or index. This topic gathers those, together with country groupings and the global debt architecture.</p>

<h3>Who publishes what</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Publisher</th><th>Reports and indices</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>IMF</b></td><td><b>World Economic Outlook</b> (April and October, with January and July updates), <b>Global Financial Stability Report</b> (twice a year), Fiscal Monitor, External Sector Report</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>World Bank</b> (IBRD)</td><td><b>World Development Report</b> (since 1978), <b>Global Economic Prospects</b> (January and June), <b>Doing Business</b> (2003–2020) and its successor B-READY (2024), Human Capital Index, Logistics Performance Index</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>World Economic Forum</b> (Geneva, 1971, Klaus Schwab; Davos meeting)</td><td><b>Global Competitiveness Report</b> (12 pillars; India 68th in 2019; paused after 2019), Global Gender Gap, Global Risks, Energy Transition Index, Travel and Tourism Development Index, Future of Jobs</td></tr>
<tr><td>UNDP</td><td>Human Development Report (HDI; MPI with OPHI) (see 3.4)</td></tr>
<tr><td>UNCTAD (renamed UN Trade and Development, 2024)</td><td>World Investment Report, Trade and Development Report, Digital Economy Report</td></tr>
<tr><td>WIPO</td><td>Global Innovation Index (India 38th in 2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td>BIS</td><td>Annual Economic Report</td></tr>
<tr><td>RBI</td><td>Financial Stability Report (half-yearly), <b>Report on Currency and Finance</b>, Annual Report, Handbook of Statistics</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ministry of Finance</td><td><b>Economic Survey</b> (day before the Budget)</td></tr>
<tr><td>CSO (now NSO)</td><td><b>National Accounts Statistics</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Ministry of Industry (Office of the Economic Adviser)</td><td><b>Wholesale Price Index</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>NCAER</td><td>India Human Development Report (1999)</td></tr>
<tr><td>IGIDR (Mumbai)</td><td>India Development Report (since 1997)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The Asian Development Bank publishes the Asian Development Outlook; the OECD publishes the OECD Economic Outlook.</li>
<li>World Development Report themes: 1996 "From Plan to Market", 1997 "The State in a Changing World", 1998/99 "Knowledge for Development", <b>2000/2001 "Attacking Poverty"</b> (three pillars: opportunity, empowerment, security); 2024 "The Middle-Income Trap".</li>
<li>IMF October 2026 WEO: India's 2026 growth forecast 7.0%; India (about $4.5 trillion) projected to overtake Japan as the fourth-largest economy in 2027 (see 1.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ease of Doing Business</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>World Bank's</b> Doing Business report ranked economies on 10 indicators: starting a business, construction permits, getting electricity, registering property, getting credit, protecting minority investors, paying taxes, trading across borders, enforcing contracts, resolving insolvency. <b>Law and order was not one of them.</b></li>
<li>India rose from 142nd (2015) to 63rd (Doing Business 2020).</li>
<li>The report was discontinued in September 2021 after data irregularities. Its successor, <b>Business Ready (B-READY)</b>, published its first edition in October 2024 (50 economies; India to be added later).</li>
<li>India's own steps: Business Reform Action Plan ranking of States (DPIIT), IBC 2016, GST, National Single Window System (2021), Jan Vishwas Act 2023 (decriminalised 183 provisions), Jan Vishwas 2.0 Bill (2025).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Infrastructure finance initiatives</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Global Infrastructure Facility</b> (2014): a <b>World Bank-hosted partnership</b> of governments, development banks and private investors that helps prepare and structure complex <b>infrastructure PPPs</b> so that private and institutional capital can be mobilised. Endorsed by the G20.</li>
<li>Global Infrastructure Hub (G20, 2014); Partnership for Global Infrastructure and Investment (G7, 2022, a counter to China's BRI); Blue Dot Network (USA, Japan, Australia).</li>
<li>India's PPP models: BOT, BOOT, Hybrid Annuity Model (roads, 2016), Toll-Operate-Transfer, Swiss challenge. Vijay Kelkar Committee (2015) on revisiting PPPs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Groupings of economies</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>UNCTAD</b> classes <b>Brazil, Mexico and South Africa</b> all as emerging economies.</li>
<li><b>BRIC</b> was coined by Jim O'Neill (Goldman Sachs, 2001); South Africa joined in 2010 (BRICS). New members: Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, UAE (2024), Indonesia (2025). New Development Bank (Shanghai, 2015). India chairs BRICS in 2026.</li>
<li>In 2009–10 <b>China's GDP</b> (about $5–6 trillion) exceeded that of Brazil, Russia and India combined, and still does (China about $19 trillion now). But China's population was <b>not</b> larger than any two others combined: India plus Brazil exceeded it. India has been more populous than China since 2023.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Sovereign debt</h3>
<h4>US Treasuries</h4>
<ul>
<li>US government debt is backed by the "<b>full faith and credit</b>" of the government (its power to tax and borrow), <b>not by hard assets</b>. A sovereign cannot be taken to bankruptcy court or have its assets seized, so if the USA defaulted, holders <b>could not enforce their claims</b>. Statement II explains Statement I.</li>
<li>Debt-ceiling standoffs led to downgrades: S&P (2011), Fitch (2023), Moody's (May 2025); all three now rate the USA below AAA. The 2025 "One Big Beautiful Bill Act" raised the ceiling by $5 trillion. US debt exceeds $36 trillion (above 120% of GDP). Japan is the largest foreign holder; India holds about $230 billion+.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The G20 Common Framework</h4>
<ul>
<li>Agreed in November 2020 by the <b>G20 together with the Paris Club</b>, to give coordinated, case-by-case debt restructuring to <b>low-income countries with unsustainable debt</b> (the 73 DSSI-eligible countries). It brings in non-Paris Club lenders such as China and India and requires comparable treatment from private creditors.</li>
<li>Before it: DSSI (May 2020–December 2021) suspended official debt payments for the poorest during COVID.</li>
<li>Users: Chad, Zambia (took about three years), Ethiopia, Ghana. Under India's G20 presidency (2023) the Global Sovereign Debt Roundtable tried to speed things up.</li>
<li><b>Paris Club</b>: informal group of 22 major creditor countries (since 1956). India is not a member (an "ad hoc participant"); nor is China, the biggest bilateral lender to developing countries.</li>
<li>Sri Lanka, a middle-income country, restructured outside the framework (2023–24); India gave about $4 billion of support and was the first creditor to give financing assurances to the IMF. Other defaults: Argentina (repeatedly), Greece (2012).</li>
<li>India's own debt is mostly domestic and in rupees, which limits default risk (see 7.2).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_economy_e10.js
// Economy book, Unit 10: Agriculture.
addBookTopic("economy", "e10-1", `
<p>Agricultural prices are not left entirely to the market in India. The government announces support prices, buys grain, stores it and distributes it cheaply or free. This topic covers that chain from minimum support price to the ration shop, and how farm produce is marketed.</p>

<h3>Minimum Support Price (MSP)</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP)</b> (1965, as the Agricultural Prices Commission; an attached office of the Agriculture Ministry) recommends MSPs. The <b>Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA)</b>, chaired by the PM, approves and announces them.</li>
<li>MSP is announced for <b>22 mandated crops</b>, not 32 and not only cereals.</li>
<li>MSP is a <b>floor</b> price; market prices often rise above it (wheat in 2022, pulses). It has no statutory backing; a legal MSP was a demand of the 2020–21 farmer protests.</li>
<li>Since 2018–19 MSP is at least <b>1.5 times the A2+FL cost</b>. Cost concepts: A2 (paid-out costs), A2+FL (plus imputed family labour), C2 (comprehensive, including rent and interest on owned land and capital). The Swaminathan Commission recommended C2 + 50%.</li>
<li>Example: MSP for common paddy, kharif 2025–26: ₹2,369 per quintal.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Season</th><th>MSP crops</th></tr>
<tr><td>Kharif (14)</td><td>Paddy, jowar, bajra, maize, <b>ragi (finger millet)</b>, tur (arhar), moong, urad, <b>groundnut</b>, soybean, sunflower seed, <b>sesamum</b>, <b>nigerseed</b>, cotton</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rabi (6)</td><td>Wheat, <b>barley</b>, gram, masoor (lentil), rapeseed-mustard, safflower</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commercial (2)</td><td>Jute, copra</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Toria is covered with mustard and de-husked coconut with copra. <b>Areca nut, coffee and turmeric have no MSP.</b></li>
<li><b>Niger seed</b> (<i>Guizotia abyssinica</i>) has an MSP, is mainly a <b>kharif</b> crop on poor hill soils in tribal areas (Odisha, MP, Chhattisgarh, Maharashtra, Jharkhand), and <b>tribal people use its oil for cooking</b>. It is also used in soaps and paints, and exported as bird feed.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Sugarcane pricing</h4>
<ul>
<li>Until 2009 the Centre fixed a <b>Statutory Minimum Price</b>; from the 2009–10 season it was replaced by the <b>Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP)</b>, the minimum mills must pay. Both are fixed under the <b>Sugarcane (Control) Order 1966</b>, made under the <b>Essential Commodities Act</b>; <b>sugar and sugarcane are essential commodities</b>. (UPSC's 2010 key accepted the Statutory Minimum Price statement, though by then FRP had replaced it.)</li>
<li>The FRP is recommended by the CACP and <b>approved by the CCEA</b>. It is linked to a basic recovery rate of 10.25%, with premiums above it. For 2026–27 it is ₹365 per quintal.</li>
<li>Some States (UP, Punjab, Haryana, Uttarakhand) add a higher <b>State Advised Price</b>.</li>
<li>Rangarajan Committee (2012–13) suggested sharing about 70–75% of sugar and by-product revenue with farmers and ended levy sugar. The Centre also fixes a minimum selling price of sugar; ethanol diversion helps mills pay cane dues (see 10.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Procurement</h3>
<ul>
<li>The price at which government buys grain for the PDS and buffer stocks is the <b>procurement price</b>; the MSP is the guaranteed floor. Since the 1990s the two have been the same for wheat and paddy.</li>
<li>Procurement of wheat and paddy is <b>open-ended</b>: FCI and State agencies buy all grain offered at MSP that meets quality norms, with no fixed target.</li>
<li>Procurement is <b>not unlimited for all cereals, pulses and oilseeds in every State</b>. Pulses and oilseeds are bought under <b>PM-AASHA</b> (2018) with a cap of about 25% of State output (Price Support Scheme, Price Deficiency Payment, private procurement pilot); coarse grains only in some States. In 2025 the government promised full procurement of tur, urad and masoor for four years.</li>
<li><b>Food Corporation of India</b> (1965, under the Food Corporations Act 1964). <b>Decentralised Procurement</b> (1997): States procure and distribute, the Centre reimburses.</li>
<li><b>Economic cost</b> of grain to FCI = <b>MSP + bonus + procurement incidentals + distribution cost</b>. Incidentals: mandi charges, taxes, arhatiya commission, gunny bags, labour, storage and interest during procurement. Distribution: freight, handling, storage, transit losses. Economic cost about ₹39–40/kg for rice and ₹27–28/kg for wheat (2024–25). The gap between economic cost and the Central Issue Price is the food subsidy.</li>
<li>FCI borrowed from the National Small Savings Fund off-budget until 2020–21; the 2021 Budget brought this on-budget.</li>
<li>Shanta Kumar Committee (2015): restructure FCI, limit NFSA coverage to about 40%, cash transfers, negotiable warehouse receipts.</li>
<li>Buffer norms are set for 1 April, 1 July, 1 October and 1 January.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Public distribution and food security</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under the Targeted PDS (1997), the Centre issued wheat and rice to all States at <b>uniform Central Issue Prices</b>. From April 2002 BPL families got 35 kg per family per month (not 50).</li>
<li><b>Annapurna Scheme</b> (2000): <b>10 kg of free food grains a month</b> to indigent senior citizens not covered by old-age pension; now merged into the National Social Assistance Programme.</li>
<li><b>National Food Security Act 2013</b>: covers up to 75% of the rural and 50% of the urban population; 5 kg per person a month for priority households, 35 kg per Antyodaya household. Free since January 2023 under <b>PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana</b> (extended to 2028), covering about 81 crore people.</li>
<li>Mid-day Meal is now PM POSHAN (2021).</li>
</ul>
<h4>What affects rice prices</h4>
<p>All four: <b>MSP</b> (a floor), <b>government trading</b> (export bans and duties, Open Market Sale Scheme sales), <b>stockpiling</b> (central pool stocks far above buffer norms reduce market supply) and <b>consumer subsidies</b> (free rice under NFSA/PMGKAY lowers market demand). India is the world's largest rice exporter (about 40% of world trade); export curbs of 2022–24 were lifted in late 2024. Bharat Atta and Bharat Rice (2023–24) were sold at subsidised prices.</p>

<h3>Agricultural marketing</h3>
<ul>
<li>Markets in farm produce are regulated under the <b>Agricultural Produce Market Committee (APMC) Acts enacted by States</b>; agricultural markets are a State subject (Entry 28, State List). Each Act sets up regulated mandis run by market committees that license traders and commission agents.</li>
<li>Model APMC Act (2003) and Model APLM Act (2017): private markets, direct marketing, a single licence, e-trading. Bihar abolished its APMC Act in 2006. The three farm laws of 2020 were repealed in November 2021.</li>
<li><b>e-NAM (National Agriculture Market)</b>, launched 14 April 2016, implemented by SFAC: a <b>pan-India electronic trading portal</b> linking APMC mandis, giving farmers <b>access to a nationwide market with quality-based prices</b> (assaying labs), online bidding and payment. About 1,400+ mandis in 23 States and 4 UTs are linked, along with FPOs and warehouse receipts. States must offer a single State-wide trading licence, single-point market fee and e-auction.</li>
<li><b>ITC's e-Choupal</b> (2000, starting with soybean farmers in MP): village internet kiosks run by a farmer "sanchalak" that give price and weather information and let ITC buy directly, cutting out middlemen. C.K. Prahalad cited it in <i>The Fortune at the Bottom of the Pyramid</i>. Do not confuse it with Hindustan Unilever's <b>Project Shakti</b>, which uses rural women entrepreneurs to sell its products in villages.</li>
<li>Digital agriculture now: Digital Agriculture Mission (2024, AgriStack farmer IDs), Kisan Call Centres, Gramin Agricultural Markets, Agriculture Infrastructure Fund, ONDC for farm produce.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e10-2", `
<p>Farm output depends on land, water, seed, fertiliser, credit and protection against risk. This topic covers land reforms and each of these inputs, with the main government schemes.</p>

<h3>Land reforms</h3>
<p>The aim, in UPSC's key, was <b>providing agricultural land to the landless</b>, broadly "land to the tiller" (the key was debated as overstated).</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Abolition of zamindari</b> and intermediaries (early 1950s; UP Zamindari Abolition Act 1950).</li>
<li><b>Tenancy reforms</b>: fair rent, security of tenure, ownership rights. Operation Barga (West Bengal, 1978) recorded sharecroppers.</li>
<li><b>Ceilings</b> on holdings and redistribution of surplus land.</li>
<li><b>Consolidation of holdings</b>: successful in Punjab, Haryana and western UP.</li>
<li>Voluntary movements: Bhoodan (Vinoba Bhave, 1951, Pochampalli) and Gramdan.</li>
<li>Land reform laws were placed in the <b>Ninth Schedule</b> to protect them from court challenge.</li>
<li>Ceiling laws allowed many <b>exemptions</b> (plantations, orchards, religious and charitable trusts, among others), which weakened them. Land reforms did not make cash crops the predominant form of cultivation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Who farms: the Situation Assessment Surveys</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>NSSO 70th Round</b> (2013): about 9 crore agricultural households, 57.8% of rural households.
 <ul>
 <li><b>Rajasthan</b> had the <b>highest share</b> of agricultural households among rural households (about 78%); Kerala the lowest (about 27%).</li>
 <li><b>OBCs</b> were about <b>45%</b> of agricultural households (not over 60%); SCs about 16%, STs about 13%.</li>
 <li>In <b>Kerala</b>, about <b>61%</b> of agricultural households got their main income from <b>non-agricultural sources</b>.</li>
 <li>Average monthly income ₹6,426; about 52% indebted.</li>
 </ul></li>
<li><b>77th Round</b> (2019): 9.3 crore agricultural households; average monthly income ₹10,218; <b>wages</b> overtook crop cultivation as the largest income source; about 50% indebted.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Farmer collectives</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Small Farmer Large Field (SFLF)</b>: neighbouring small farmers <b>keep ownership of their plots</b> but synchronise operations (same variety, sowing date, inputs, pest control, harvest and marketing), so together they gain the economies of scale of one large farm. It began with rice in Vietnam (2011) and has been tried in India.</li>
<li><b>Farmer Producer Organisations</b>: producer companies or cooperatives; scheme for 10,000 FPOs (2020) through SFAC, NABARD and NCDC.</li>
<li>Cooperatives (the Amul model); Ministry of Cooperation (2021); cooperative grain storage plan (2023). Model Contract Farming Act 2018. Kerala's group farming (padasekharam) and Kudumbashree groups.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Investment vs subsidy</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Public investment</b> creates lasting assets that raise future productivity: <b>computerising PACS</b>, <b>developing social capital</b> (farmer institutions, FPOs, training) and <b>government cold storage</b>; also irrigation, rural roads, research, markets.</li>
<li><b>Not investment</b>: fixing <b>MSP</b> (price support), <b>free electricity</b> (input subsidy), <b>loan waivers</b> (transfers). Such spending tends to crowd out investment.</li>
<li>Gross capital formation in agriculture is about 15–18% of agricultural GVA, mostly private. Agriculture Infrastructure Fund (2020, ₹1 lakh crore) gives interest subvention for post-harvest infrastructure. The Ashok Dalwai Committee on Doubling Farmers' Income (2017–18) stressed investment over subsidies.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Water</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Accelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme</b> (1996–97): central loans (later grants) to <b>State governments</b> to complete ongoing major and medium irrigation projects; it did not lend to farmers.</li>
<li><b>Command Area Development Programme</b> (1974–75): closes the gap between irrigation potential created and used, improving <b>water-use efficiency</b> through field channels, land levelling and warabandi (rotational supply).</li>
<li>Both are now under <b>PM Krishi Sinchayee Yojana</b> (2015): AIBP; Har Khet Ko Pani (command area development, surface minor irrigation, repair of water bodies); Per Drop More Crop (micro-irrigation); watershed development. Long Term Irrigation Fund with NABARD (2016–17) for 99 priority projects.</li>
<li>Gross irrigated area is about 55% of gross cropped area; groundwater supplies over 60% of irrigation.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Rainfed farming</h4>
<ul>
<li>About 51% of net sown area is rainfed and yields about 40% of food, including most pulses, oilseeds, coarse cereals and cotton.</li>
<li><b>National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture</b> (one of the eight NAPCC missions; operational 2014–15). Its <b>Rainfed Area Development</b> component aims at <b>higher productivity and lower climate risk through Integrated Farming Systems</b> (crops with horticulture, livestock, fisheries, agroforestry, beekeeping). It discourages monoculture and is not about irrigated rice. Other components: Soil Health Management (Soil Health Cards), on-farm water management, climate monitoring.</li>
<li>Related: Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (organic clusters), National Mission on Natural Farming (2024), ICAR's NICRA, National Rainfed Area Authority (2006). IFS models: rice–fish–duck, crop–dairy–biogas.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Seeds</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Seed Replacement Rate</b>: the share of area sown with certified or quality seed instead of farm-saved seed. Targets: 33% for self-pollinated crops, 50% for cross-pollinated, 100% for hybrids.</li>
<li>Constraint: a <b>demand–supply gap in quality seed of low-value, high-volume crops</b> (wheat, rice, pulses), where farmers use saved seed and private firms show little interest. India <b>has had a National Seeds Policy since 2002</b>, and <b>private companies dominate</b> vegetable seed and horticultural planting material.</li>
<li><b>Seed Village Programme</b>: farmers get foundation or certified seed and <b>training in quality seed production</b>, so the village supplies quality seed to itself and neighbours on time and at low cost.</li>
<li>Seed classes: nucleus → breeder → foundation → certified. Laws: Seeds Act 1966 (a new Seeds Bill was drafted in 2025), Protection of Plant Varieties and Farmers' Rights Act 2001 (farmers may save and sell unbranded farm-saved seed). Bharatiya Beej Sahakari Samiti (2023).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fertilisers</h3>
<ul>
<li>The retail price of <b>urea is fixed by the government</b> (about ₹242 per 45 kg bag); the gap from cost is paid as subsidy. P&K fertilisers (DAP, MOP, NPK) are under the <b>Nutrient Based Subsidy</b> scheme (2010): nominally decontrolled but guided in practice. So prices are not simply market-driven.</li>
<li><b>Ammonia</b>, the input for urea, is made from <b>natural gas</b> (hydrogen by steam reforming plus nitrogen from air, Haber–Bosch). Ammonia + CO2 → urea.</li>
<li><b>Sulphur</b>, a by-product of <b>oil refineries</b>, is burnt to make sulphuric acid, which treats rock phosphate to make phosphoric acid for DAP.</li>
<li>India is the second-largest fertiliser consumer and imports much urea, DAP, rock phosphate and all its potash. The ideal NPK ratio is 4:2:1; India over-uses nitrogen.</li>
<li>Policies: New Urea Policy 2015; 100% neem-coated urea (2015); "One Nation One Fertiliser" (Bharat brand, 2022); PM-PRANAM (2023) to cut chemical use; nano urea and nano DAP (IFFCO); revived plants at Gorakhpur, Sindri, Barauni, Ramagundam, Talcher (coal gasification).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Agricultural credit</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Commercial banks</b> have the highest share of institutional farm credit (about three-quarters); cooperatives and RRBs share the rest (see 4.4). Banks must lend 18% of adjusted net bank credit to agriculture (10% to small and marginal farmers). Agricultural credit flows exceed ₹25 lakh crore.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Kisan Credit Card (KCC)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Launched in 1998 (NABARD; R.V. Gupta Committee); extended to animal husbandry and fisheries in 2018–19.</li>
<li>Short-term credit covers: <b>cultivation expenses</b> and <b>post-harvest expenses</b>, produce marketing loans, <b>consumption needs of farm households</b> (up to 10% of the limit), and <b>working capital for maintaining farm assets</b> and allied activities (up to 20%).</li>
<li>Buying <b>tractors and combine harvesters</b>, or building <b>houses and cold storage</b>, need long-term investment loans, not short-term KCC credit.</li>
<li>Modified Interest Subvention Scheme: loans at 7%, with 3% extra for prompt repayment (effective 4%); the limit was raised from ₹3 lakh to ₹5 lakh (Budget 2025–26). Collateral-free farm loans up to ₹2 lakh (RBI, January 2025). KCC saturation drive linked to PM-KISAN (2020).</li>
<li>In 2001 KCC holders got <b>personal accident insurance</b> of ₹50,000 for death and ₹25,000 for permanent disability. KCC crop loans were covered under the National Agricultural Insurance Scheme run by GIC (later AIC), <b>not LIC</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Crop insurance</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Years</th></tr>
<tr><td>Comprehensive Crop Insurance Scheme</td><td>1985</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>National Agricultural Insurance Scheme</b> (Rashtriya Krishi Bima Yojana)</td><td>From rabi <b>1999</b>–2000; run by GIC, then the Agriculture Insurance Company (2002)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Modified NAIS</td><td>2010</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>PM Fasal Bima Yojana</b></td><td>2016</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>PMFBY farmer premiums are <b>not uniform</b>: <b>2%</b> for kharif food and oilseed crops, <b>1.5%</b> for rabi, <b>5%</b> for annual commercial and horticultural crops. The rest is shared 50:50 by Centre and State (90:10 in the North-East and Himalayan States).</li>
<li>It covers prevented sowing, standing crop losses, <b>post-harvest losses</b> for up to 14 days for crops drying in the field (cyclone, cyclonic and unseasonal rain, hailstorm) and localised calamities (hailstorm, landslide, inundation, cloudburst, natural fire).</li>
<li>Voluntary for all farmers since kharif 2020. Technology: YES-TECH (remote-sensing yields), WINDS (weather data), CROPIC (crop photos), Digiclaim. The Restructured Weather-Based Crop Insurance Scheme runs alongside. Some States (Bihar, Gujarat, West Bengal) run their own schemes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Crop and livestock missions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>National Food Security Mission</b> (2007–08): launched by the <b>Ministry of Agriculture</b> (not the Food Ministry) to raise output of <b>rice, wheat and pulses</b> through area expansion and productivity in identified districts (targets for 2011–12: +10 MT rice, +8 MT wheat, +2 MT pulses). Coarse cereals, nutri-cereals and commercial crops were added later.</li>
<li><b>Mission for Aatmanirbharta in Pulses</b> (2025–26 to 2030–31, ₹11,440 crore; launched 11 October 2025): 350 lakh tonnes from 310 lakh hectares by 2030–31. National Mission on Edible Oils for oilseeds and oil palm (see 10.3).</li>
<li><b>Rashtriya Gokul Mission</b> (December 2014): develops and conserves <b>indigenous cattle and buffalo breeds</b> scientifically. It matters for the rural poor because most low-yielding indigenous animals belong to small and marginal farmers and landless labourers. Now part of the Rashtriya Pashudhan Vikas Yojana (2021–26; outlay raised to about ₹3,400 crore in 2025).
 <ul>
 <li>Components: Gokul Grams, National Kamdhenu Breeding Centres (Itarsi, Chintaldevi), the nationwide artificial insemination programme, sex-sorted semen, IVF and embryo transfer, genomic chips ("Gau Chip", "Mahish Chip"), animal identification (Pashu Aadhaar), e-Pashu Haat, MAITRI technicians.</li>
 <li>Rashtriya Kamdhenu Aayog (2019). Gopal Ratna awards on National Milk Day, 26 November (Verghese Kurien's birthday).</li>
 </ul></li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Indigenous breeds</th></tr>
<tr><td>Milch cattle</td><td>Gir (Gujarat), Sahiwal (Punjab), Red Sindhi, Tharparkar (Rajasthan), Rathi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Draught cattle</td><td>Amritmahal, Hallikar, Khillari, Kangayam, Malvi, Nagori, Siri, Bargur</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dual purpose</td><td>Hariana, Ongole (AP), Kankrej (Gujarat), Deoni, Gaolao, Krishna Valley</td></tr>
<tr><td>Buffalo</td><td>Murrah (Haryana; highest yield), Jaffarabadi, Mehsana, Surti, Nili-Ravi, Bhadawari (high fat), Toda, Chilika</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Livestock diseases</h4>
<ul>
<li>UPSC treated <b>foot-and-mouth disease</b> (viral, extremely contagious in cloven-hoofed animals) and <b>cowpox</b> (viral, spreads by contact; the source of Jenner's 1796 smallpox vaccine) as the infectious (contagious) ones.</li>
<li><b>Anthrax</b> (<i>Bacillus anthracis</i>) and <b>Black Quarter</b> (<i>Clostridium chauvoei</i>) are bacterial, caught mainly from soil spores rather than directly from other animals. (Strictly they are infectious too, so the key was debated.) Anthrax can infect humans.</li>
<li>National Animal Disease Control Programme (2019) vaccinates against FMD and brucellosis, aiming to eradicate FMD by 2030. Lumpy skin disease (viral, insect-borne) killed lakhs of cattle in 2022; India developed the Lumpi-ProVacInd vaccine.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e10-3", `
<p>This topic covers what India grows and how much, the allied sectors (livestock, silk), agro-industries built on farm produce (sugar, food processing), and India's agricultural trade.</p>

<h3>Foodgrain production</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Crop</th><th>2005–06 (approx.)</th><th>2024–25 (record)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Rice</td><td>92 MT</td><td>About 149 MT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wheat</td><td>69 MT</td><td>About 117.5 MT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coarse cereals (maize, jowar, bajra, ragi)</td><td>34 MT</td><td>60+ MT (maize a record of about 42 MT)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pulses</td><td>13 MT</td><td>About 25 MT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Total foodgrains</td><td></td><td>About 354 MT</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Order of production: <b>rice > wheat > coarse cereals > pulses</b>.</li>
<li>India is the largest producer and consumer of pulses but still imports them. Millets ("Shree Anna"): International Year of Millets 2023, proposed by India.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The Green Revolution</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Norman Borlaug</b> (1914–2009), an <b>American</b> agronomist, bred high-yielding, disease-resistant dwarf wheat in Mexico (CIMMYT). Varieties such as Lerma Rojo and Sonora 64 came to India in the 1960s. He won the Nobel Peace Prize (1970), the Padma Vibhushan (2006) and founded the World Food Prize.</li>
<li>In India: M.S. Swaminathan (scientist; Bharat Ratna 2024) and C. Subramaniam (Agriculture Minister). Rice variety IR-8 came from IRRI (Philippines). Mainly wheat and rice in Punjab, Haryana and western UP.</li>
<li>Side effects: groundwater depletion, soil degradation, regional inequality, monoculture.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Oilseeds and edible oils</h3>
<ul>
<li>India is the world's <b>largest importer of edible oil</b>, importing about 55–60% of its needs (14–16 million tonnes a year). <b>Imports exceed domestic production</b> (about 10–12 MT). Vegetable oils are India's <b>largest agricultural import by value</b> (about $15–20 billion a year), well ahead of pulses ($3–5 billion), fruits, raw cashew and spices.</li>
<li>Mix: palm oil (Indonesia, Malaysia) about 55–60%, soybean oil (Argentina, Brazil), sunflower oil (Russia, Ukraine).</li>
<li><b>Customs duties are imposed</b> and changed often to balance farmers and consumers: raised to 30–44% on crude palm oil in 2018, cut to near zero in 2021–22, basic duty on crude oils raised to 20% (September 2024) and cut to 10% (May 2025).</li>
<li>Why India still depends on imports (UPSC key): farmers prefer foodgrains with <b>remunerative support prices and assured procurement</b>, and about 70% of oilseed area is <b>rainfed</b>, so yields are low and unstable. Under-used tree-borne oilseeds and rice bran are a minor factor, and it is not "far cheaper" to import oilseeds (India imports mostly oil, not seeds).</li>
<li>Domestic oilseeds: soybean, rapeseed-mustard and groundnut (about 85% of output), plus cottonseed and rice bran.</li>
<li>Missions: Technology Mission on Oilseeds (1986, the "Yellow Revolution"), National Mission on Edible Oils – Oil Palm (2021, North-East and Andaman focus), – Oilseeds (2024–31, ₹10,103 crore, target 69.7 MT of oilseeds by 2030–31).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Sugar and its by-products</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Molasses</b>: the thick syrup left after sugar crystallises; fermented to make <b>ethanol</b> (for liquor, chemicals and fuel blending). It is <b>not</b> a feedstock for synthetic chemical fertilisers (those come from natural gas, naphtha, rock phosphate). Press mud and spent wash can serve as organic manure.</li>
<li><b>Bagasse</b>: fibrous residue after crushing; burnt as <b>biomass fuel</b> in mill boilers for steam and power cogeneration, and used for paper and particle board.</li>
<li>Press mud can feed compressed biogas plants (SATAT, 2018).</li>
<li>Sugar is <b>not only from cane</b>: sugar beet gives about 20% of world output; palm and maple sap are other sources.</li>
<li>Brazil is the largest sugar producer; India is second (it topped Brazil in some years, such as 2021–22) and the largest consumer.</li>
<li>Ethanol blending reached the <b>20% (E20)</b> target in 2025, using cane juice, B-heavy and C-heavy molasses, maize and damaged grain (see 11.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Allied sectors</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Livestock</b>: India is usually described as having the <b>largest livestock population</b> in the world: about 536 million animals (20th Livestock Census, 2019), with the most cattle and buffaloes (about 303 million bovines) and the second most goats. (Counting pigs and poultry, China's numbers are larger.) The 21st Livestock Census (fieldwork October 2024–February 2025) covers 16 species and 219 indigenous breeds.</li>
<li><b>Milk</b>: India is the largest producer (about 24–25% of world output; about 239 MT in 2023–24), thanks to Operation Flood (Verghese Kurien, 1970).</li>
<li><b>Silk</b>: India is the <b>only country producing all five commercial silks</b>: mulberry, tropical tasar, oak tasar, eri and muga (golden silk, unique to Assam, GI-tagged). It is the second-largest silk producer (after China) and the largest consumer. Karnataka leads in mulberry silk. Central Silk Board (Bengaluru); Silk Samagra-2.</li>
<li><b>Natural rubber</b>: around 2004 India did export some natural rubber, while domestic output met about 97% of demand. Now India is the fourth-largest producer (about 8.5 lakh tonnes) but the second-largest consumer, importing about 40% of needs (tyres). Kerala produces most; Tripura and the North-East are a new focus (INROAD). Top producers: Thailand, Indonesia, Vietnam.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Commodity boards</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Board</th><th>Law</th><th>Headquarters</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Tea Board</b></td><td>Tea Act 1953 (formed 1954): a <b>statutory body</b></td><td><b>Kolkata</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Coffee Board</td><td>Coffee Act 1942</td><td>Bengaluru</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rubber Board</td><td>Rubber Act 1947</td><td>Kottayam</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spices Board (also covers cardamom)</td><td>Spices Board Act 1986</td><td>Kochi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tobacco Board</td><td>Tobacco Board Act 1975</td><td>Guntur</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>All five are under the <b>Ministry of Commerce and Industry</b> (plantation crops are export matters), not the Agriculture Ministry. The Tea Board had overseas offices in <b>Dubai and Moscow</b> (Russia and the UAE are big buyers).</li>
<li>Draft laws (2022) proposed replacing the old Tea, Coffee, Rubber, Spices and Tobacco Acts with promotion-and-development laws. Other bodies: Coir Board (Kochi, MSME Ministry), National Turmeric Board (Nizamabad, 2025).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Food processing</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Mega Food Parks</b> (2008, Ministry of Food Processing Industries): to give <b>good infrastructure for the food processing industry</b> (cold chain, warehousing, testing labs) and <b>raise processing of perishables and cut wastage</b> through a cluster, hub-and-spoke model linking farmers to processors. Supplying processing technologies to entrepreneurs was not an objective. The scheme was discontinued after March 2021 (24 of 41 approved parks operational); the smaller Agro Processing Cluster scheme replaced it.</li>
<li>Other schemes: PM Kisan SAMPADA, PLI for food processing, PM-FME (micro enterprises, One District One Product), Operation Greens. World Food India 2025 was held in New Delhi.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Agricultural trade</h3>
<ul>
<li>Exports: rice is the top agricultural export now (about $12 billion in 2024–25); marine products about $7.5 billion (mainly frozen shrimp to the USA, hit by 2025 US tariffs). In 1997–2000 <b>marine products</b> were the largest agricultural export by value (UPSC key; rice was close in some years). Agricultural exports total about $50 billion: rice, marine products, spices, sugar, buffalo meat.</li>
<li>Rice and wheat export curbs (2022–24) were eased in 2024–25.</li>
<li><b>International Grains Council</b> (London; from the International Wheat Council, 1949) administers the Grains Trade Convention (1995) and publishes the Grain Market Report. <b>India is a member</b>, but <b>membership is not needed to trade grains</b>; trade runs under WTO rules regardless.</li>
<li>Other bodies: FAO (Rome; Food Price Index), WFP, IFAD, AMIS (G20, 2011), International Sugar Organization (India chaired it in 2023), International Coffee Organization, International Cotton Advisory Committee.</li>
<li>WTO issues: public stockholding and the Peace Clause (see 9.2).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
