// Economy book, Unit 11: Industry, infrastructure and services.
addBookTopic("economy", "e11-1", `
<p>Industrial policy in India moved from licensing and public-sector dominance (1956–91) to liberalisation and, more recently, targeted incentives for manufacturing. This topic covers manufacturing policy, MSMEs, the core-industries index and the public and private companies that UPSC has asked about.</p>

<h3>Manufacturing policy</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>National Manufacturing Policy (2011)</b>: raise manufacturing to 25% of GDP and create 10 crore jobs by 2022. Its tools:
 <ul>
 <li><b>National Investment and Manufacturing Zones (NIMZs)</b>: integrated industrial townships of at least 5,000 hectares. India's first NIMZ was approved in Prakasam district, <b>Andhra Pradesh</b> (UPSC key; some sources name others).</li>
 <li><b>Single-window clearance</b> within NIMZs.</li>
 <li>A <b>Technology Acquisition and Development Fund</b> to help MSMEs acquire clean and green technology.</li>
 </ul></li>
<li>Later: Make in India (2014), PLI schemes in 14 sectors (2020–21; see 8.4), National Single Window System (2021), PM Gati Shakti (2021), National Industrial Corridor Programme (DMIC, CBIC, AKIC; 12 new industrial smart cities approved 2024), National Manufacturing Mission (Budget 2025–26).</li>
<li>Manufacturing's share of GVA has stayed around 15–17%.</li>
<li>India Semiconductor Mission (2021): fabs and assembly units at Dholera (Tata–PSMC), Sanand (Micron, CG Power, Kaynes) and Assam (Tata OSAT); first made-in-India chips in 2025.</li>
<li><b>Global Automotive Research Centre</b>: set up at Oragadam near <b>Chennai</b> ("the Detroit of Asia") under NATRiP (2005), for crash tests and certification. Bharat NCAP crash ratings began in 2023. India is the third-largest automobile market; EV schemes: PM E-DRIVE (2024), SPMEPCI (2025).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Industrial growth in the past</h4>
<ul>
<li>1999–2000: industrial production rose (IIP about 6.7% against about 4% in 1998–99), credited to a <b>stable exchange rate</b>, low inflation, lower interest rates and better business sentiment after the East Asian crisis.</li>
<li>2000–01: <b>steel</b> recorded the highest growth among core industries; cement output fell.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Measuring industry: IIP and the eight core industries</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Index of Industrial Production</b> (NSO, MoSPI; base 2011–12, a 2022–23 base is due): weights manufacturing about 77.6%, mining 14.4%, electricity 8%.</li>
<li>The <b>Index of Eight Core Industries</b> (Office of the Economic Adviser, DPIIT; monthly) covers about <b>40.27%</b> of the IIP (37.90% on the older 2004–05 base).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Core industry</th><th>Weight (base 2011–12)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Refinery products</td><td>28.04%</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Electricity</b></td><td>19.85%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steel</td><td>17.92%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coal</td><td>10.33%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crude oil</td><td>8.98%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Natural gas</td><td>6.88%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cement</td><td>5.37%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fertilisers</td><td>2.63%</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Memory aid: R-E-S-C-C-N-C-F. Refinery products have the highest weight overall; among the options asked (2015), <b>electricity</b> was highest. <b>Textiles are not a core industry.</b></li>
<li>Size of an industry is judged in India by <b>capital investment, turnover and power use</b> (the Factories Act distinguishes units with power, 10 workers, from those without, 20). UPSC's key left out labour force, though many countries use it.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs)</h3>
<ul>
<li>MSMEs give about 30% of GDP, about 45% of exports and over 11 crore jobs.</li>
<li>Definitions under the <b>MSMED Act 2006</b>: originally investment in plant and machinery (medium manufacturing ₹5–10 crore; services ₹2–5 crore), never "₹15–25 crore". Since 2020, composite criteria (investment and turnover), same for manufacturing and services. Udyam registration covers over 6 crore units.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Enterprise</th><th>2020 (investment / turnover)</th><th>2025 revision (investment / turnover)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Micro</td><td>≤ ₹1 crore / ≤ ₹5 crore</td><td>≤ ₹2.5 crore / ≤ ₹10 crore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Small</td><td>≤ ₹10 crore / ≤ ₹50 crore</td><td>≤ ₹25 crore / ≤ ₹100 crore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Medium</td><td>≤ ₹50 crore / ≤ ₹250 crore</td><td>≤ ₹125 crore / ≤ ₹500 crore</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>All bank loans to MSMEs qualify as priority sector lending</b>, without a cap (see 4.4).</li>
<li><b>Reservation</b> of products for the small-scale sector (over 800 items in the 1990s) was criticised by the <b>Abid Hussain Committee</b> (1997), which recommended its abolition; the last 20 items were de-reserved in 2015.</li>
</ul>
<h4>TReDS and MSME finance</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Trade Receivables Discounting System</b> (RBI guidelines 2014, under the PSS Act 2007): an MSME uploads an invoice on a large buyer; once accepted, banks and NBFC-factors bid to <b>discount the invoice or bill of exchange</b>, and the MSME is paid quickly, collateral-free and without recourse. The credit risk lies on the buyer.</li>
<li>Platforms: RXIL (SIDBI–NSE, first, 2017), Invoicemart, <b>M1xchange</b>, and newer ones. M1xchange is neither a collateral-based lender nor a rating agency.</li>
<li>Companies with turnover above ₹250 crore and all CPSEs must join TReDS (2024).</li>
<li>Payment discipline: MSMED Act (payment within 45 days); Section 43B(h) of the Income-tax Act (2023); MSME Samadhaan portal. Credit tools: CGTMSE, Mutual Credit Guarantee Scheme (2025). The Factoring Regulation (Amendment) Act 2021 let more NBFCs do factoring.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Public sector enterprises</h3>
<ul>
<li>CPSE categories: Maharatna (14 by 2025; recent additions REC in 2022 and HAL in 2024), Navratna, Miniratna I and II. Disinvestment and strategic sales: see 2.2.</li>
<li><b>Balmer Lawrie</b> (1867, Kolkata; Petroleum Ministry), <b>Dredging Corporation of India</b> (1976, <b>Visakhapatnam</b>; since 2019 owned by four major ports, so still public) and <b>EdCIL</b> (1981; Education Ministry) are all public sector undertakings.</li>
<li><b>Projects and Development India Ltd</b> (Noida; Department of Fertilizers): design, engineering, procurement and supervision for <b>fertiliser and allied chemical plants</b>.</li>
<li><b>Ordnance Factories</b> were the largest departmentally run undertaking (Ministry of Defence); the Ordnance Factory Board was split into seven defence PSUs in October 2021.</li>
<li><b>NTPC</b> is the largest power utility (see 11.2). ONGC did not produce half of India's LPG, and IOC does not run all refineries (BPCL, HPCL, Reliance, CPCL, NRL, MRPL also do; Reliance's Jamnagar is the world's largest refining complex).</li>
<li><b>Uranium Corporation of India</b> (1967, DAE) runs the <b>Narwapahar</b> mine (Jharkhand) along with Jaduguda, Bhatin, Turamdih, Bagjata and Tummalapalle (AP).</li>
<li>Petronet LNG's terminals: Dahej (2004, largest) and <b>Kochi</b> (2013); its Mangalore plan was shelved. A third terminal is planned at Gopalpur.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Trading PSUs</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>MMTC</b> (1963) was India's largest international trading organisation and largest non-oil importer (gold, fertilisers, metals). It promoted <b>Neelachal Ispat Nigam</b> (Kalinganagar, Odisha) with Odisha government bodies; NINL was sold to Tata Steel Long Products in July 2022.</li>
<li>PEC Ltd was under the <b>Ministry of Commerce</b> (not Industry). MMTC, STC and PEC are being wound down.</li>
<li><b>ECGC</b> provides export credit insurance (and runs NIRVIK); quality control and pre-shipment inspection are done by the <b>Export Inspection Council</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Steel</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Plant</th><th>Collaboration and facts</th></tr>
<tr><td>Bhilai (Chhattisgarh)</td><td>Soviet Union (Second Plan)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Rourkela</b> (Odisha, 1959)</td><td><b>West Germany</b> (Krupp-Demag), not the Soviet Union; first public-sector integrated plant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Durgapur (West Bengal)</td><td>Britain</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bokaro (Jharkhand)</td><td>Soviet Union (1964–72)</td></tr>
<tr><td>TISCO (Jamshedpur, 1907)</td><td>First private integrated plant</td></tr>
<tr><td>IISCO (Burnpur, 1918)</td><td><b>Merged with SAIL in February 2006</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>RINL</b> (Visakhapatnam Steel Plant)</td><td>India's first shore-based integrated plant; <b>Miniratna (2006)</b>, Navratna (2010); a ₹11,440 crore revival package (January 2025) replaced its planned sale</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kalinganagar, Jajpur (Odisha)</td><td><b>Tata Steel's</b> plant, not SAIL's (in the news after the January 2006 police firing on tribal protesters)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>India is the world's second-largest crude steel producer (about 150 MT in 2024–25). National Steel Policy 2017: 300 MT capacity by 2030–31. Safeguard duty on steel imports (2025); green steel taxonomy (2024). POSCO's Odisha project was abandoned (2017).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Private companies and groups</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Company</th><th>Business or owner</th></tr>
<tr><td>Chevron (USA)</td><td>Oil</td></tr>
<tr><td>AT&T (USA)</td><td>Telephone and internet</td></tr>
<tr><td>AMD (USA)</td><td>Microprocessors and graphics chips</td></tr>
<tr><td>Enercon (Germany)</td><td>Wind turbines (its Indian JV became Inox Wind)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Genpact</b></td><td>Business process management and IT services (spun off from GE in 2005), <b>not power generation</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Synthite</td><td>Spice extracts (Kerala)</td></tr>
<tr><td>VSNL</td><td>Sold to <b>Tata</b> (2002; now Tata Communications), not Bharti</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mundra Port and SEZ</td><td>Adani</td></tr>
<tr><td>CMC Ltd</td><td>Tata (2001; merged into TCS 2008)</td></tr>
<tr><td>IPCL</td><td>Reliance (2002; merged 2008). By 2002–03 Reliance, not IPCL, was the largest petrochemical company</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Voltas, Titan, Rallis India, Indian Hotels</b> (Taj)</td><td>All <b>Tata group</b></td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The 1999–2004 strategic disinvestment wave (Arun Shourie's Ministry of Disinvestment): BALCO, VSNL, CMC, IPCL, Modern Foods, Maruti stake sales.</li>
<li>Tata Sons is controlled by Tata Trusts; chairman N. Chandrasekaran; Noel Tata became chairman of Tata Trusts after Ratan Tata's death (October 2024). Tata Motors split into passenger and commercial vehicle companies (2025).</li>
<li>MTNL listed ADRs on the New York Stock Exchange (2001). BSNL launched CellOne nationwide in October 2002 and indigenous 4G in September 2025.</li>
<li>Heads of companies in 2005: V.R.S. Natarajan (BEML), A.K. Puri (BHEL), V. Thulasidas (Air India), Jagdish Khattar (Maruti Udyog). BEML now makes metro coaches and Vande Bharat sleeper coaches.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e11-2", `
<p>Energy is India's biggest import bill and the backbone of its industry. This topic covers electricity, coal, oil and gas, renewables and biofuels, and minerals, together with their regulators.</p>

<h3>Electricity</h3>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>2000–01</th><th>2007–08</th><th>2026</th></tr>
<tr><td>Installed capacity (utilities)</td><td>About 97,000–1,01,000 MW</td><td>About 1,43,000 MW</td><td>About 549 GW (June 2026, CEA); non-fossil about 54%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Generation</td><td>Thermal about 80%; hydro about 17–25%; nuclear about 3%</td><td>About 704 billion kWh (about 662 in 2006–07)</td><td>About 1,840 billion kWh (2025–26)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Largest source</td><td>Coal</td><td>Coal</td><td>Coal (about 224 GW), then solar (about 162 GW)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>In 2001 nuclear gave nowhere near 15% and hydro nowhere near 40% of generation. In 2008 installed capacity was well above 1,00,000 MW, and UPSC took generation as about 700+ billion kWh (the 660 figure was debated).</li>
<li>India reached <b>50% of installed capacity from non-fossil sources in June 2025</b>, five years ahead of its 2030 NDC target; the next target is 500 GW non-fossil by 2030. Coal still gives about 70–75% of generation; nuclear about 3% (8.8 GW).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Laws and regulators</h4>
<ul>
<li>The Draft Electricity Bill 2001 became the <b>Electricity Act 2003</b>, which <b>repealed</b> the Indian Electricity Act 1910, the Electricity (Supply) Act 1948 and the Electricity Regulatory Commissions Act 1998. State Electricity Boards are bodies, not laws: the Act made States unbundle them into generation, transmission and distribution companies. It brought open access and de-licensed generation.</li>
<li>Regulators: CERC (1998) and State ERCs. There is a <b>single Appellate Tribunal for Electricity</b> (APTEL, New Delhi, 2005, under Section 110), set up by the Centre, not one per State; appeals from it go to the Supreme Court. It also hears appeals against PNGRB.</li>
<li>The Electricity (Amendment) Bill (2022; 2025 draft) proposes several distribution licensees in one area and cost-reflective tariffs.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Distribution reform and access</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Year</th><th>Aim</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>APDRP</b></td><td>2002–03</td><td><b>Upgrading sub-transmission and distribution</b>; cutting losses</td></tr>
<tr><td>R-APDRP, IPDS</td><td>2008, 2014</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>UDAY</b> (Ujwal DISCOM Assurance Yojana)</td><td>November 2015</td><td><b>Financial turnaround of power distribution companies</b>: States took over 75% of DISCOM debt; DISCOMs to cut AT&C losses to 15% and close the cost–revenue gap by 2018–19</td></tr>
<tr><td>Revamped Distribution Sector Scheme</td><td>2021 (₹3.04 lakh crore)</td><td>Smart prepaid meters, loss reduction; AT&C losses fell from about 22% to about 16%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Late Payment Surcharge Rules</td><td>2022</td><td>Cleared DISCOM dues to generators</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Rural Electrification Policy</b> (August 2006): electricity access for all households by <b>2009</b>, and a lifeline of 1 unit per household per day by 2012. Delivery: RGGVY (2005) → DDUGJY (2014): all villages electrified by April 2018 (Leisang, Manipur, was the last); Saubhagya (2017): about 2.86 crore households connected. A village counts as electrified if 10% of households and public places have power.</li>
<li>In 2002 the Ministry of Power had signed reform MoUs with 14 States, and <b>100% FDI</b> was allowed in power (except atomic energy). UPSC treated rural electrification as not yet a "Basic Minimum Service" under the PM Gramodaya Yojana.</li>
<li><b>NTPC</b> diversified into <b>hydropower</b> (Koldam, Tapovan-Vishnugad), and <b>Power Grid</b> into <b>telecom</b> (optical fibre on its towers; POWERTEL). NTPC is the largest power generator (over 80 GW), plans 60 GW of renewables by 2032 and has a nuclear JV with NPCIL (ASHVINI).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Renewable energy</h3>
<ul>
<li>Solar capacity about 165 GW (July 2026). Schemes: PM Surya Ghar Muft Bijli Yojana (2024, rooftop solar for 1 crore homes), PM-KUSUM, solar parks (Bhadla, Rajasthan; Khavda, Gujarat), Green Energy Corridors. International Solar Alliance (India and France, 2015; Gurugram).</li>
<li>India was <b>not</b> the third-largest maker of silicon wafers: China makes over 95% of wafers and polysilicon; India mainly assembled modules from imported cells. PLI for high-efficiency modules (2021, ₹24,000 crore) is building the chain. ALMM lists for modules (2021) and cells (June 2026); customs duty on imported cells and modules.</li>
<li>Solar tariffs are discovered through <b>competitive reverse auctions</b> or set by regulators (CERC, SERCs); <b>SECI</b> runs auctions and acts as an intermediary buyer but does not determine tariffs.</li>
<li>Wind capacity about 50+ GW (Tamil Nadu, Gujarat); offshore tenders off Gujarat and Tamil Nadu.</li>
<li><b>IREDA</b> (1987, Ministry of New and Renewable Energy) is a <b>public limited government company</b> and an <b>NBFC</b> (infrastructure finance company) lending to renewable and efficiency projects. Listed in November 2023; Navratna in April 2024. Other energy financiers: PFC and REC (both Maharatnas).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Biofuels</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>USA</b> is the largest ethanol producer (about 55% of world output, from <b>corn</b>); <b>Brazil</b> is second (from <b>sugarcane</b>). Order: USA > Brazil > EU > India > China.</li>
<li>India's Ethanol Blended Petrol programme: E10 in June 2022; <b>E20 reached in 2025</b> (advanced from 2030). Feedstocks: molasses, cane juice, damaged grain, maize, surplus FCI rice. Concerns: food vs fuel, water use, older engines, mileage.</li>
<li><b>National Policy on Biofuels 2018</b> (amended 2022; nodal: Petroleum Ministry): 1G, 2G (crop residue; IOCL Panipat 2G refinery, 2022) and 3G (algae) fuels; 5% biodiesel in diesel by 2030. PM JI-VAN Yojana (2019) for 2G ethanol; SATAT for compressed biogas. Global Biofuels Alliance launched at the New Delhi G20 (2023). Sustainable aviation fuel targets: 1% in international flights by 2027, 2% by 2028.</li>
<li>The earlier <b>National Biodiesel Mission</b> (2003 Planning Commission committee) based on Jatropha on wasteland had the <b>Ministry of Rural Development</b> as nodal ministry; Jatropha largely failed.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Oil and gas</h3>
<ul>
<li>India is the third-largest crude consumer and imports about 85–88% of its crude; Russia became the top supplier after 2022.</li>
<li><b>Barrel</b>: 42 US gallons ≈ <b>159 litres</b>; about 7.3 barrels make a tonne.</li>
<li>Benchmarks: <b>Brent</b> (North Sea; a major classification used for about two-thirds of traded oil; <b>sweet</b>, about 0.4% sulphur, not sulphur-free), <b>WTI</b> (West Texas Intermediate: light, sweet US crude delivered at Cushing, Oklahoma; fell below zero in April 2020), Dubai/Oman (sour, for Asia). The <b>Indian Basket</b> is roughly 75:25 sour to sweet. Light/heavy refers to API gravity; sweet/sour to sulphur.</li>
<li>The 2026 war involving Iran and disruption in the Strait of Hormuz pushed Brent above $100 a barrel.</li>
<li><b>Strategic Petroleum Reserves</b> (ISPRL): Visakhapatnam, Mangaluru, Padur (5.33 MMT, about 9.5 days); new ones planned at Chandikhol and Padur-II.</li>
<li>Pricing: the <b>Oil Pool Account</b> was <b>dismantled on 1 April 2002</b> with the Administered Price Mechanism; subsidies on PDS kerosene and domestic LPG were then <b>borne by the Consolidated Fund</b>. Petrol (2010) and diesel (2014) prices were deregulated. LPG subsidy now goes by DBT (PAHAL), mainly to Ujjwala users (over 10 crore connections).</li>
<li><b>B.K. Chaturvedi Committee</b> (2008): <b>price reforms in the oil sector</b> (price increases, sharing under-recoveries, targeted subsidies). Kirit Parikh committees (2010, 2022) also dealt with fuel and gas pricing.</li>
<li><b>Mashelkar Committee</b> (auto fuel policy): Bharat Stage II nationwide by <b>1 April 2005</b> (not 2004). India jumped from BS-IV to BS-VI on 1 April 2020.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The oil and gas value chain and its regulators</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Segment</th><th>Activities</th><th>Regulator / policy</th></tr>
<tr><td>Upstream</td><td><b>Exploration and production of crude oil and natural gas</b></td><td>Petroleum Ministry and the <b>Directorate General of Hydrocarbons</b> (1993); HELP (2016) with Open Acreage Licensing; Oilfields (Regulation and Development) Amendment Act 2025</td></tr>
<tr><td>Midstream</td><td>Pipelines, storage, LNG terminals</td><td>PNGRB: authorises city gas networks and pipelines, sets tariffs (unified tariff 2023); "One Nation One Gas Grid"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Downstream</td><td><b>Refining, storage, distribution, marketing and sale</b></td><td>PNGRB (technical and safety standards, fair trade practices)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>PNGRB</b> (PNGRB Act 2006) regulates refining, processing, storage, transport, distribution, marketing and sale of petroleum products and natural gas, <b>excluding production of crude oil and natural gas</b>. One of its tasks is to <b>ensure competitive markets for gas</b>. Appeals go to <b>APTEL</b>. It was <b>not</b> India's first regulator: SEBI (1992), TRAI (1997), CERC (1998) and IRDA (1999) came earlier.</li>
<li>Natural gas is about 6% of India's energy mix; the target is 15% by 2030. About half of gas is imported as LNG (terminals at Dahej, Hazira, Kochi, Ennore, Mundra, Dhamra, Dabhol and others).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Coal</h3>
<ul>
<li>Coal was <b>nationalised under Indira Gandhi</b>: coking coal mines in 1971–72 and non-coking in 1973; Coal India Ltd was formed in 1975. Singareni Collieries is a Telangana–Centre company.</li>
<li>Blocks are <b>not</b> allotted by lottery: after the Supreme Court cancelled 204 blocks in 2014 ("Coalgate"), they are allotted by <b>e-auction</b> (and allotment to PSUs) under the Coal Mines (Special Provisions) Act 2015. Commercial mining opened to private firms in 2020; 100% FDI on the automatic route.</li>
<li>India is <b>not self-sufficient</b>: it still imports coking coal for steel and high-grade thermal coal (about 250 MT a year), though output crossed 1 billion tonnes in 2024–25. India is the second-largest producer and consumer after China.</li>
<li>Reserves: Jharkhand (largest), Odisha, Chhattisgarh, West Bengal, MP; about 98% Gondwana coal; Tertiary coal in the North-East. Coking coal at Jharia; lignite at Neyveli (Tamil Nadu), Rajasthan, Gujarat. Coal gasification mission: 100 MT by 2030.</li>
<li><b>Coal Controller's Organisation</b> (Ministry of Coal, Kolkata): the <b>major source of coal statistics</b>; <b>monitors development of captive and commercial coal and lignite blocks</b>; <b>hears objections</b> to notifications under the Coal Bearing Areas (Acquisition and Development) Act 1957; grades coal and issues mine opening and closing permissions. It does <b>not</b> enforce delivery timelines to end users (fuel supply agreements do).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Minerals</h3>
<ul>
<li>By value, mineral output in 2002–03 ranked <b>fuel minerals > metallic minerals > non-metallic minerals</b>; fuel minerals were about three-quarters. Coal is the single most valuable mineral. National Critical Mineral Mission (2025) covers lithium, rare earths and cobalt.</li>
<li><b>Minor minerals</b>: "ordinary sand" is a minor mineral (Section 3(e), MMDR Act 1957). <b>States both grant leases and make the rules</b> for minor minerals (Section 15), and <b>States can frame rules against illegal mining</b>, transport and storage (Section 23C). Environmental clearance is needed even for small sand leases (<i>Deepak Kumar</i>, 2012). Guidelines: Sustainable Sand Mining (2016), Enforcement and Monitoring (2020). Manufactured sand from crushed rock is an alternative.</li>
<li><b>District Mineral Foundations</b> (Section 9B, MMDR Act, 2015 amendment): non-profit trusts in mining districts working for the <b>interest and benefit of persons and areas affected by mining</b>. Funded by miners: 30% of royalty for leases before January 2015, 10% for auctioned leases. Spent under the PM Khanij Kshetra Kalyan Yojana (2015, revised 2024; at least 70% on drinking water, health, education, sanitation and similar). They do not fund exploration (that is the <b>National Mineral Exploration Trust</b>, 2% of royalty) or license exploration (States do). Over ₹1 lakh crore collected; Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand lead.</li>
<li>Energy statistics: the annual publication "<b>Energy Statistics</b>" is brought out by the <b>CSO</b> (now NSO, MoSPI).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e11-3", `
<p>Services make up more than half of India's output. This topic covers transport and communication infrastructure, infrastructure finance, the IT industry and India's digital public infrastructure.</p>

<h3>Infrastructure finance</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Deepak Parekh Committee</b> (2007): <b>financing infrastructure</b>: deepen the corporate bond market, take-out financing, larger roles for IIFCL and insurance funds.</li>
<li><b>National Investment and Infrastructure Fund (NIIF)</b>, 2015: India's first sovereign-anchored infrastructure fund, registered with SEBI as an AIF and run by NIIF Ltd under the <b>Department of Economic Affairs</b>, <b>not NITI Aayog</b>. Planned corpus <b>₹40,000 crore</b> (government 49%, the rest from investors such as ADIA, Temasek, AIIB and JBIC), not ₹4 lakh crore. Funds: Master Fund, Fund of Funds, Strategic Opportunities Fund, India–Japan Fund (2023).</li>
<li>Others: NaBFID (2021), IIFCL, InvITs and REITs (see 6.2), National Infrastructure Pipeline (₹111 lakh crore, 2019–25), National Monetisation Pipeline (₹6 lakh crore, 2021–25) and its second phase (2025–30, about ₹10 lakh crore), PM Gati Shakti (2021).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Transport</h3>
<h4>Railways</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>North Western Railway</b> (2002) has its headquarters at <b>Jaipur</b>; Jodhpur is a division.</li>
<li>The <b>Indrail Pass</b> was for <b>foreign tourists and NRIs</b> (paid in foreign currency), not freedom fighters or sportspersons; it was discontinued in 2017.</li>
<li>The <b>Fairy Queen</b> (built in England, 1855) is the world's oldest working steam locomotive (Guinness); it ran heritage trips from Delhi to Alwar and Sariska.</li>
<li>Tourist trains: Palace on Wheels, Deccan Odyssey, Maharajas' Express, Golden Chariot. Mountain railways (Darjeeling, Nilgiri, Kalka–Shimla) are World Heritage. Now: Vande Bharat (sleeper version from 2025–26), Kavach, Dedicated Freight Corridors, and the Chenab bridge (world's highest railway arch bridge, opened June 2025).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Aviation and shipping</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Cochin International Airport</b> (1999) was the first airport <b>owned by a public limited company</b> (CIAL, with thousands of NRI and other shareholders and the Kerala government); world's first fully solar-powered airport (2015); UNEP Champions of the Earth (2018). Navi Mumbai airport opened in 2025.</li>
<li><b>Alliance Air</b> (1996) was set up as a <b>wholly owned subsidiary of Indian Airlines</b>. It stayed with the government when Air India was sold to Tata (2022) and flies UDAN routes.</li>
<li>Division of work: the <b>Airports Authority of India</b> (1995) builds and manages airports, runways and terminals and provides air navigation services; the <b>DGCA</b> is the regulator that enforces civil air regulations and safety standards. Many big airports (Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru, Hyderabad) are now run by private operators.</li>
<li>Aviation bodies: DGCA, AAIB (accident investigation, e.g. Air India AI171, June 2025), BCAS, AERA. The Bharatiya Vayuyan Adhiniyam 2024 replaced the Aircraft Act 1934. India is the third-largest domestic aviation market.</li>
<li><b>Cochin Shipyard</b> (1972, a Miniratna) is India's <b>largest shipyard</b>; it built INS Vikrant (commissioned 2022) and opened India's largest dry dock (2024). India builds under 1% of world ships; a 2025 shipbuilding package of about ₹70,000 crore aims to change that.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Communication</h3>
<ul>
<li>India has the world's largest postal network: about 1.55 lakh post offices in 2005 (<b>over 1.3 lakh</b>) and about 1.64 lakh now, nearly 90% rural. The Post Office Act 2023 replaced the 1898 Act; India Post Payments Bank opened in 2018.</li>
<li><b>BSNL</b> was corporatised on <b>1 October 2000</b> (not 1997). <b>TRAI</b> was set up in <b>1997</b> (not 2000); TDSAT was added in 2000.</li>
<li>Telecommunications Act 2023 replaced the Telegraph Act 1885. BSNL launched indigenous 4G in 2025; MTNL's operations are being moved to BSNL.</li>
<li><b>Digital India</b> (1 July 2015, MeitY): three vision areas (digital infrastructure as a utility for every citizen; governance and services on demand; digital empowerment) and nine pillars. Its aims include <b>connecting villages to the internet and bringing Wi-Fi to schools, public places and tourist centres</b> (BharatNet, PM-WANI). Creating Chinese-style national internet companies or forcing foreign firms to build data centres in India were <b>not</b> its stated aims. Related: PMGDISHA, DigiLocker, UMANG, Common Service Centres; a Digital India Act is proposed to replace the IT Act 2000.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The IT industry</h3>
<ul>
<li>In the late 1990s software exports grew about <b>50% a year</b> (about ₹2,500 crore in 1995–96 to ₹28,000 crore in 2000–01), helped by Y2K work, because Indian firms were <b>cost-effective with international quality</b> (CMM Level 5 certifications, English-speaking engineers).</li>
<li>Over 1996–97 to 2001–02, exports grew at roughly 35–45% a year, not 60%; in 2001–02 the whole software and services industry grew about 22–23% in rupee terms (UPSC treated both "60%" and "28%" as wrong; the figures were debated).</li>
<li><b>TCS</b> was the largest IT exporter in 2002–03 (ahead of Infosys and Wipro) and remains the largest (revenue about $30 billion). IT-BPM revenue about $283 billion (2024–25), exports about $224 billion. About 1,700–1,800 Global Capability Centres. Challenges: AI disruption, US H-1B fee rises (2025).</li>
</ul>

<h3>E-commerce and digital public infrastructure</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>E-commerce revenue model</th><th>How it earns</th><th>Sets prices?</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dropshipping</b></td><td>Retail margin on goods it never stocks: the seller sets the price and forwards orders to a third-party supplier who ships directly to the customer</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Affiliate</td><td>Commission for referring buyers (e.g. Amazon Associates links)</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transaction fee</td><td>A cut on each transaction (marketplaces, payment gateways)</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agency</td><td>Commission as agent for a principal who sets prices</td><td>No</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Other models: subscription, freemium, advertising, quick commerce (dark stores). For FDI rules on marketplaces vs inventory, see 8.3. Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules 2020; dark-pattern guidelines (2023, 13 types).</p>
<h4>ONDC</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Open Network for Digital Commerce</b> (incorporated December 2021; a Section 8 not-for-profit company promoted by DPIIT) is an <b>open protocol</b>, not an app. Any buyer app can buy from any seller on any seller app, as UPI lets any bank app pay any other. Its key objective is to <b>break the dominance of large e-commerce platforms by enabling interoperability</b>, lowering barriers for small sellers and kiranas. Private platforms can join; it does not give the government control of transactions.</li>
<li>Built on the open-source Beckn protocol; it unbundles discovery, ordering, payment, logistics and fulfilment. Sectors: groceries, food delivery, mobility (Namma Yatri), fashion, financial services, B2B. Over 10 million transactions a month.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>India Stack layer</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Identity</td><td>Aadhaar (UIDAI, 2009)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payments</td><td>UPI (NPCI, 2016) (see 4.6)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data</td><td>DigiLocker, Account Aggregators (RBI NBFC-AA, 2021), DEPA</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commerce</td><td>ONDC, GeM (Government e-Marketplace)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Health</td><td>Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABHA ID)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Credit</td><td>OCEN, Unified Lending Interface (RBI, 2024)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>India's G20 presidency (2023) promoted digital public infrastructure globally (Global DPI Repository, One Future Alliance).</p>
`);
;
// data/book_economy_e12.js
// Economy book, Unit 12: Social sector, welfare and business regulation.
addBookTopic("economy", "e12-1", `
<p>India's rural development programmes evolved from the Community Development Programme of the 1950s, through wage-employment and self-employment schemes, to rights-based laws such as MGNREGA and its 2025 successor. UPSC asks about their aims, provisions and implementing ministries.</p>

<h3>The beginnings: Community Development</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Community Development Programme</b> (2 October 1952) took development to the block level with people's participation. Nehru described its aim as instilling "a sense of partnership and of cooperative performance" in millions of workers. The National Extension Service followed in 1953.</li>
<li>Weak public participation led to the Balwantrai Mehta Committee and three-tier Panchayati Raj (Nagaur, Rajasthan, 2 October 1959).</li>
</ul>

<h3>District Rural Development Agencies (DRDAs)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Set up in the 1980s to run IRDP; later handled SGSY, Indira Awaas Yojana and MGNREGA support. They are registered societies under the Ministry of Rural Development, ideally working under the Zila Parishad.</li>
<li>They <b>secure inter-sectoral and inter-departmental coordination</b> for anti-poverty programmes and <b>watch over the use of their funds</b>.</li>
<li>They are <b>not Panchayati Raj institutions</b> and do <b>not</b> do scientific research into the causes of poverty and malnutrition.</li>
<li>Several States (Kerala, Karnataka, West Bengal, MP; Odisha in 2022) have merged DRDAs into Zila Parishads to strengthen PRIs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Wage employment: from NREGA to VB-G RAM G</h3>
<h4>NREGA / MGNREGA (2005)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Guaranteed <b>100 days</b> of unskilled manual wage work a year to every rural household whose <b>adult members</b> volunteered: universal and demand-driven, so <b>adult members of any rural household</b> were eligible, not only SC/ST, BPL or backward-class households. Renamed MGNREGA in 2009.</li>
<li>It created a <b>legal (statutory) right</b>, not a Fundamental Right. If work was not given within 15 days, an unemployment allowance was due.</li>
<li>At least <b>one-third</b> of beneficiaries were to be women (not one-half); in practice women's share was about 55–59%.</li>
<li>Wages: under Section 6(2), until the Centre notified its own rate, the <b>State's statutory minimum wage for agricultural labourers</b> applied. The Centre later notified State-wise rates under Section 6(1), revised yearly by CPI-AL.</li>
<li>Other features: work within 5 km, worksite facilities (crèche, water, shade), social audits by Gram Sabhas (Section 17), payment into bank or post office accounts (Aadhaar-based since 2024).</li>
</ul>
<h4>The 2025 replacement</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Viksit Bharat – Guarantee for Rozgar and Ajeevika Mission (Gramin) Act 2025 (VB-G RAM G)</b>, passed in December 2025, replaced MGNREGA from <b>1 July 2026</b>, when MGNREGA stood repealed.</li>
<li>It guarantees <b>125 days</b> of wage employment a year to every rural household whose adult members volunteer for unskilled manual work.</li>
<li>Funding is shared <b>60:40</b> between the Centre and States (90:10 for north-eastern and Himalayan States; 100% central for UTs without legislatures).</li>
<li>States may pause works for up to 60 days during peak sowing and harvest seasons.</li>
<li>Critics questioned the shift of cost to States and the removal of Gandhi's name.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Earlier wage schemes</h4>
<ul>
<li>Jawahar Rozgar Yojana (1989) → <b>Jawahar Gram Samriddhi Yojana</b> (April 1999) → Sampoorna Grameen Rozgar Yojana (2001) → NREGA (2006).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Self-employment and livelihoods</h3>
<ul>
<li>IRDP (1978–80) → Swarnajayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana (1999) → <b>National Rural Livelihood Mission</b> (2011), renamed <b>DAY-NRLM</b> in 2016 (Ministry of Rural Development, World Bank support).</li>
<li>NRLM improves livelihoods by <b>strengthening self-help groups and providing skill development</b>: it organises poor women into SHGs and federations, gives revolving funds and bank linkage, and trains them (DDU-GKY for placement-linked skills; RSETIs for self-employment). It does <b>not</b> set up factories itself or give free seeds, fertiliser and pump-sets.</li>
<li>About 10 crore women in about 90 lakh SHGs (see 4.6). "Lakhpati Didi": women earning ₹1 lakh+ a year; the target of 3 crore has been reached. Other components: Start-up Village Entrepreneurship Programme, Mahila Kisan Sashaktikaran Pariyojana, Aajeevika Grameen Express Yojana.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Urban poverty</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Swarna Jayanti Shahari Rozgar Yojana</b> (1 December 1997) subsumed the Nehru Rozgar Yojana, the Urban Basic Services for the Poor programme and the PM's Integrated Urban Poverty Eradication Programme. It did <b>not include the PM's Rozgar Yojana</b> (1993, for educated unemployed youth), which stayed separate.</li>
<li>SJSRY → National Urban Livelihoods Mission (DAY-NULM, 2013). PMRY and REGP merged into the PM's Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP, 2008). PM SVANidhi (2020) for street vendors.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Schemes of around 2000</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Launch</th><th>Features</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Antyodaya Anna Yojana</b></td><td>25 December 2000</td><td>The poorest 1 crore families got grain at <b>₹2/kg wheat and ₹3/kg rice</b> (35 kg a month)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>PM Gram Sadak Yojana</b></td><td>25 December 2000</td><td>All-weather roads to unconnected villages; PMGSY-IV (2024–29)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sarvapriya</td><td>Announced 1999, piloted 2000</td><td>Essential goods through ration shops (UPSC treated it as not a 2000 scheme)</td></tr>
<tr><td>National Old Age Pension Scheme</td><td>1995</td><td><b>₹75 a month</b> from the Centre, topped up by States</td></tr>
<tr><td>BPL grain allocation</td><td>2000–02</td><td><b>25 kg</b> per family a month at about half the economic cost (35 kg from April 2002)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Today: PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana gives about 81 crore people 5 kg of free grain a month (AAY families 35 kg), extended to 2028 (see 10.1).</p>

<h3>Bharat Nirman (2005–09)</h3>
<p>Six components: irrigation, rural roads, rural housing, <b>rural drinking water</b>, rural electrification and rural telephony. Drinking water today: Jal Jeevan Mission (2019, tap water to every rural household; extended to 2028).</p>

<h3>Area development and watershed programmes</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Programme</th><th>Year</th><th>Ministry</th></tr>
<tr><td>Drought-Prone Areas Programme</td><td>1973–74</td><td><b>Rural Development</b> (Department of Land Resources), not Agriculture</td></tr>
<tr><td>Desert Development Programme</td><td>1977–78</td><td><b>Rural Development</b>, not Environment and Forests</td></tr>
<tr><td>National Watershed Development Project for Rainfed Areas</td><td>1990–91</td><td><b>Agriculture</b>, not Rural Development</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>DPAP, DDP and the Integrated Wastelands Development Programme merged into the Integrated Watershed Management Programme (2009–10), which became the Watershed Development Component of PMKSY (2015; WDC 2.0 for 2021–26).</li>
<li>Watershed approach: ridge-to-valley treatment, soil and water conservation, community participation (Hanumantha Rao Committee 1994, Parthasarathy Committee 2006).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Sanitation</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Nirmal Gram Puraskar</b> (2003–05): an <b>incentive scheme for Panchayati Raj institutions</b>, rewarding gram panchayats, blocks and districts that became fully sanitised and open-defecation free under the Total Sanitation Campaign.</li>
<li>Sequence: Central Rural Sanitation Programme (1986) → Total Sanitation Campaign (1999) → Nirmal Bharat Abhiyan (2012) → Swachh Bharat Mission (Gramin) (2014; India declared ODF on 2 October 2019) → SBM-G Phase II (ODF Plus, 2020–25).</li>
<li>Panchayat awards now: Deen Dayal Upadhyay Panchayat Satat Vikas Puraskar, Nanaji Deshmukh Sarvottam Panchayat Satat Vikas Puraskar.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e12-2", `
<p>The welfare state in India works through a large set of schemes for social security, health, women and skills. UPSC asks who runs each scheme, who is eligible and what exactly it pays for.</p>

<h3>Social security and pensions</h3>
<h4>National Social Assistance Programme (NSAP, 1995)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Rooted in Article 41 (DPSP); run by the Ministry of Rural Development. Components: Indira Gandhi National Old Age Pension Scheme (IGNOAPS), widow pensions (IGNWPS), disability pensions (IGNDPS), National Family Benefit Scheme (₹20,000 once on the death of the breadwinner) and Annapurna.</li>
<li><b>IGNOAPS</b> (November 2007): at launch, eligibility was <b>65 years and above</b> in <b>BPL households, rural and urban</b> (so "60+ rural only" was wrong), with central assistance of <b>₹200 a month</b> (not ₹300) and States urged to add at least as much. The age was lowered to 60 in 2011. Now ₹200 a month for ages 60–79 and ₹500 for 80+, unchanged since 2011.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Employees' State Insurance (ESI)</h4>
<ul>
<li>The ESI Act 1948 applies to factories and, by notification, to <b>shops, hotels and restaurants, cinemas, road motor transport undertakings, newspaper establishments, and private medical and educational institutions</b> employing 10 or more people. All four of those in the question were covered.</li>
<li>Benefits: medical care, sickness, maternity, disablement, dependants' benefit, unemployment allowance (Atal Bimit Vyakti Kalyan Yojana), funeral expenses. Wage ceiling ₹21,000 a month; contributions: employer 3.25%, employee 0.75%.</li>
<li>The Code on Social Security 2020 (in force 21 November 2025) subsumed the ESI Act and covers gig and platform workers (see 3.2).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Insurance for the poor</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Aam Admi Bima Yojana</b> (2007, LIC): insured the <b>head or an earning member of a rural landless household</b>, aged <b>18 to 59</b> (not 30–65). Its add-on <b>Shiksha Sahyog Yojana</b> gave a <b>scholarship</b> of ₹100 a month for up to two children in Classes 9–12. It was merged into PMJJBY and PMSBY from 2017–18.</li>
<li>PM Jeevan Jyoti Bima Yojana (life, ₹2 lakh for ₹436 a year, age 18–50) and PM Suraksha Bima Yojana (accident, ₹2 lakh for ₹20 a year, age 18–70).</li>
<li><b>Rashtriya Swasthya Bima Yojana</b> (2008): launched by the <b>Ministry of Labour and Employment</b> (not Health) for BPL families (₹30,000 cover, smart card); moved to Health in 2015; subsumed into <b>AB-PMJAY</b> (2018, ₹5 lakh per family a year; extended to everyone aged 70+ from October 2024 through the Vay Vandana card).</li>
<li><b>Rajiv Gandhi Shilpi Swasthya Bima Yojana</b> (2007) for handicraft artisans was run by the <b>Ministry of Textiles</b> (Development Commissioner, Handicrafts). Artisans now also have PM Vishwakarma (2023).</li>
<li>Pensions for workers: Atal Pension Yojana, PM Shram Yogi Maan-dhan, NPS (see 6.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Health</h3>
<h4>ASHA workers</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Accredited Social Health Activist</b> was created under the National Rural Health Mission (2005): about one per 1,000 people, usually a local woman aged 25–45; about 10 lakh in all; paid incentives plus a fixed honorarium.</li>
<li>ASHAs <b>accompany women to health facilities for antenatal check-ups</b> and institutional delivery, <b>use pregnancy test kits</b> (Nischay) for early detection, and <b>counsel on nutrition and immunisation</b>, breastfeeding, family planning and hygiene.</li>
<li>They do <b>not conduct deliveries</b>; skilled birth attendants (ANMs, nurses, doctors) do.</li>
<li>The ASHA, the ANM and the Anganwadi worker form the "AAA" frontline trio. Indian ASHAs received the WHO Director-General's Global Health Leaders Award (2022). NRHM and the Urban Health Mission (2013) form the National Health Mission.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Maternal health schemes</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Janani Suraksha Yojana</b> (2005, under NRHM): <b>promotes institutional deliveries</b> among poor women and gives <b>cash assistance to meet delivery costs</b> (for example ₹1,400 in rural low-performing States); ASHAs get escort incentives.</li>
<li><b>Wage-loss compensation</b> is not JSY's aim; that is the <b>PM Matru Vandana Yojana</b> (2017): ₹5,000 for the first child and ₹6,000 for a second girl child (under Mission Shakti).</li>
<li>Others: Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (2011; free delivery, drugs, transport), PM Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (check-ups on the 9th of every month), LaQshya (labour rooms), SUMAN (2019).</li>
<li>Results: institutional deliveries about 89% (NFHS-5); maternal mortality about 93 per lakh live births (2019–21) against the SDG target of 70 by 2030 (see 3.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Women's development schemes</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Implemented through</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Swadhar</b> (2001–02)</td><td>Women in <b>difficult circumstances</b>: survivors of disasters or violence, women released from prison, destitute women (shelter, food, counselling)</td><td>NGOs and voluntary organisations</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Swayam Siddha</b> (2001)</td><td><b>Holistic empowerment through SHGs</b></td><td><b>ICDS</b> blocks</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Swawlamban</b> (earlier NORAD, 1982–83)</td><td><b>Training and skills in traditional and non-traditional trades</b> for poor women; later merged into STEP</td><td>NGOs, women's development corporations</td></tr>
<tr><td>STEP (1986–87)</td><td>Training and employment in agriculture, dairy, handlooms and similar</td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The 2010 statements swapped Swadhar and Swayam Siddha, so both were wrong.</li>
<li>Other older schemes: <b>Kishori Shakti Yojana</b> (adolescent girls, through ICDS) and <b>Rashtriya Mahila Kosh</b> (1993, micro-credit for poor women through NGOs).</li>
<li>Today's umbrella is <b>Mission Shakti</b> (2022): "Sambal" (safety: One Stop Centres, 181 helpline, Beti Bachao Beti Padhao, Nari Adalat) and "Samarthya" (empowerment: Shakti Sadan, which absorbed Swadhar Greh and Ujjawala; PMMVY; crèches).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Skills and employment services</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>PM Kaushal Vikas Yojana</b> (July 2015, Skill India Mission): flagship scheme of the <b>Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship</b> (created November 2014), not the Labour Ministry; implemented by the NSDC. Besides job skills it trains in <b>soft skills, entrepreneurship, financial and digital literacy</b>, and aims to <b>align the competencies of the unregulated workforce to the NSQF</b>. Versions: 2.0 (2016–20), 3.0 (2021), 4.0 (2022–26; Industry 4.0, AI, drones, coding).</li>
<li><b>Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL)</b>, a PMKVY component, assesses and <b>certifies skills gained informally</b>, notably by <b>construction workers</b> through traditional channels, against the NSQF so they can earn more. Types: camp-based, at employer's premises, RPL centres, best-in-class employers. About 90% of India's workforce is informal.</li>
<li><b>National Skills Qualification Framework</b> (notified December 2013): 10 levels by knowledge, skill and responsibility. Certification can come through <b>formal, non-formal and informal learning</b> (not only formal). An expected outcome is <b>mobility between vocational and general education</b> and the job market. Anchored in NCVET (2018), the skill regulator; NSQF 2.0 (2023) aligned with the National Credit Framework.</li>
<li><b>National Career Service</b> (July 2015): a Mission Mode Project of the <b>Ministry of Labour and Employment</b> (not DoPT) serving <b>all job seekers</b> and employers, not specifically uneducated youth. The ncs.gov.in portal offers job matching, counselling and job fairs; employment exchanges became Model Career Centres. Linked with e-Shram (2021) and the Skill India Digital Hub.</li>
<li>Other: Skill India Digital Hub (2023), Jan Shikshan Sansthan, National Apprenticeship Promotion Scheme, PM Internship Scheme (2024), PM Viksit Bharat Rozgar Yojana (employment-linked incentive, 2025). World Youth Skills Day: 15 July.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Education</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>SWAYAM</b> (Study Webs of Active-Learning for Young Aspiring Minds, 2017, Ministry of Education): India's MOOC platform, <b>affordable, quality education for citizens for free</b>, from Class 9 to postgraduate level, taught by faculty of IITs, IIMs and central universities. Credits can be transferred (up to 40% of a semester's credits under UGC rules). National coordinators include NPTEL, UGC/CEC, NCERT, NIOS, IGNOU and IIM Bangalore.</li>
<li>Also: SWAYAM Prabha (200 DTH education channels), DIKSHA (school content), PM eVidya (2020), National Digital Library, Academic Bank of Credits (NEP 2020).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("economy", "e12-3", `
<p>Businesses in India operate under company law, securities rules, quality standards and consumer-protection rules. This topic covers the forms of business, corporate responsibility and disclosure, certification marks and standards bodies, and support for innovation.</p>

<h3>Forms of business and company law</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Limited Liability Partnership</b> (LLP Act 2008): needs a <b>minimum of two partners and has no maximum</b>, so "fewer than 20 partners" is <b>not</b> a feature. At least two designated partners, one resident in India; registered with the Ministry of Corporate Affairs. The LLP (Amendment) Act 2021 decriminalised many offences and created "small LLPs".</li>
<li><b>MCA21</b> (2006): the Ministry of Corporate Affairs' <b>e-governance</b> portal for online incorporation, annual filings and Registrar of Companies services; a Mission Mode Project under the National e-Governance Plan (2006). MCA21 Version 3 (2022–24) added AI-based analytics; SPICe+ is the single incorporation form.</li>
<li>Decriminalisation: Jan Vishwas (Amendment of Provisions) Act 2023 and the Jan Vishwas 2.0 Bill (2025).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR)</h3>
<ul>
<li>India was the <b>first country to make CSR spending mandatory by law</b> (Section 135, Companies Act 2013; from 1 April 2014).</li>
<li>Applies to companies with <b>net worth ≥ ₹500 crore, turnover ≥ ₹1,000 crore or net profit ≥ ₹5 crore</b> in the previous year.</li>
<li>They must spend <b>at least 2% of average net profit of the preceding three years</b>, so the rules <b>do specify minimum spending</b>.</li>
<li>The CSR Rules (2014, amended 2021) <b>exclude</b> activities that benefit only the company's employees and their families, activities in the normal course of business, contributions to political parties, most activities outside India, and sponsorships for marketing benefit.</li>
<li>Unspent money goes to a Schedule VII fund (such as PM CARES) within six months, or to an Unspent CSR Account for ongoing projects (to be spent within three years). Non-compliance is a civil penalty (decriminalised 2020). A board CSR committee is not needed if the obligation is ₹50 lakh or less.</li>
<li>Schedule VII activities: hunger and poverty, health, sanitation, education, gender equality, environment, heritage, armed forces veterans, sports, PM's funds, rural and slum development, disaster management, research incubators. Total CSR spending is over ₹30,000 crore a year; Maharashtra, Karnataka and Gujarat get the most.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report (BRSR)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mandated by <b>SEBI</b> (LODR Regulations), not the RBI, and only for the <b>top 1,000 listed companies by market capitalisation</b>, not all listed companies.</li>
<li>Its disclosures are <b>largely non-financial</b>: environmental, social and governance performance (energy and water use, emissions, waste, employee well-being, diversity, human rights, consumer responsibility, ethics).</li>
<li>History: Business Responsibility Report (2012, top 100 companies, based on the National Voluntary Guidelines 2011) → National Guidelines on Responsible Business Conduct (2019, nine principles) → BRSR (voluntary 2021–22, mandatory from 2022–23) → <b>BRSR Core</b> (2023, key ESG indicators needing reasonable assurance, phased in, plus value-chain disclosures).</li>
<li>Structure: general disclosures; management and process disclosures; principle-wise performance against the nine NGRBC principles, with "essential" (mandatory) and "leadership" (voluntary) indicators.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Standards, certification marks and quality bodies</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Mark</th><th>For</th><th>Body</th></tr>
<tr><td>ISI</td><td>Industrial products; <b>mandatory for automotive tyres and tubes</b>, cement, LPG cylinders, electrical appliances, packaged drinking water, helmets, toys (2021) under Quality Control Orders</td><td>Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS Act 2016)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hallmark</td><td>Gold and silver jewellery (mandatory for gold since 2021, with HUID)</td><td>BIS</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>AGMARK</b></td><td>Agricultural produce</td><td><b>Directorate of Marketing and Inspection</b> (Agriculture Ministry) under the Agricultural Produce (Grading and Marking) Act 1937; <b>not the FAO</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>FPO mark</td><td>Processed fruit products</td><td>FSSAI</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ecomark</td><td>Environment-friendly products (revamped 2024)</td><td>CPCB / BIS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Star label</td><td>Energy efficiency</td><td>Bureau of Energy Efficiency</td></tr>
<tr><td>India Organic, Jaivik Bharat</td><td>Organic food</td><td>APEDA, FSSAI</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>ISO</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>International Organization for Standardization</b> (1947) is headquartered in <b>Geneva</b>, not Rome (Rome hosts FAO, WFP and IFAD). BIS is a founder member.</li>
<li><b>ISO 9000</b>: quality management systems. <b>ISO 14000</b>: environmental management systems. Others: ISO 27001 (information security), ISO 45001 (occupational health and safety), ISO 22000 (food safety), ISO 50001 (energy), ISO 26000 (social responsibility).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Quality Council of India (QCI)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Set up in 1997 <b>jointly by the Government of India and Indian industry</b> (ASSOCHAM, CII, FICCI) as a non-profit society; nodal ministry DPIIT.</li>
<li>Its <b>Chairman is appointed by the Prime Minister on the industry's recommendation</b> to the government.</li>
<li>Boards: NABCB (certification and inspection bodies), NABL (testing and calibration labs), NABH (hospitals), NABET (education and training, including EIA consultants), NBQP (quality promotion). It also runs Swachh Survekshan assessments and ZED certification for MSMEs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Food labelling</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under the FSS (Packaging and Labelling) Regulations 2011, the main label of pre-packaged food had to show the <b>list of ingredients including additives</b> (in descending order of weight), <b>nutrition information</b> and the <b>vegetarian (green dot) or non-vegetarian (brown dot)</b> symbol, plus the name, net quantity, manufacturer, FSSAI licence number, dates, batch number and country of origin for imports.</li>
<li>Medical recommendations on allergic reactions were <b>not</b> mandatory then; the FSS (Labelling and Display) Regulations 2020 later required allergen declarations, set font sizes and added the "+F" fortification logo.</li>
<li>Front-of-pack labelling (an Indian Nutrition Rating) has been proposed; the Supreme Court asked FSSAI to decide (2024–25).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Innovation and entrepreneurship</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Atal Innovation Mission</b> (2016) is set up under <b>NITI Aayog</b>. AIM 2.0 (approved November 2024) runs to March 2028.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>AIM programme</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td>Atal Tinkering Labs</td><td>School students (Classes 6–12): DIY kits, 3D printers, robotics; Budget 2025–26 promised 50,000 more</td></tr>
<tr><td>Atal Incubation Centres</td><td>Start-ups in universities and industry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Atal New India Challenges</td><td>Grants for products solving national problems</td></tr>
<tr><td>Atal Community Innovation Centres</td><td>Tier 2–3 cities and rural areas</td></tr>
<tr><td>ARISE</td><td>Innovation in MSMEs</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Other innovation schemes: Startup India (DPIIT, 2016), SAMRIDH (MeitY), NIDHI (DST), BIRAC (DBT), Anusandhan National Research Foundation (ANRF Act 2023). For Stand-Up India and MUDRA, see 4.6.</p>
`);
;
