// Environment book, Unit 1: Ecology: how ecosystems work.
addBookTopic("environment", "v1-1", `
<p>Ecology is the study of how living things relate to each other and to their surroundings. Every later topic in this book (biodiversity, pollution, climate change) builds on a few ideas set out here: the ecosystem, the flow of energy through food chains, the roles species play, and how communities change over time.</p>

<h3>Levels of organisation and the ecosystem</h3>
<ul>
<li>Individual → <b>population</b> (one species in an area) → <b>community</b> (all species in an area) → <b>ecosystem</b> (community plus its physical environment) → <b>biome</b> (large regional ecosystems such as tropical rainforest or tundra) → <b>biosphere</b>.</li>
<li>An <b>ecosystem</b> (term coined by A.G. Tansley, 1935) is <b>a community of organisms together with the environment in which they live</b>: the biotic part (plants, animals, microbes) interacting with the abiotic part (soil, water, air, sunlight), with energy flowing through and nutrients cycling.</li>
<li><b>Habitat</b> is where a species lives. Its <b>ecological niche</b> is not only the physical space it occupies but also its <b>functional role</b> in the community: what it eats, when it is active, how it interacts with others and how it affects its environment (Grinnell, Elton, Hutchinson). By <b>Gause's competitive exclusion principle</b>, two species cannot share exactly the same niche for long.</li>
<li><b>Ecotone</b>: a transition zone between two ecosystems (mangroves between land and sea, grassland–forest edges). Ecotones often hold more species: the <b>edge effect</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Producers, consumers, detritivores and decomposers</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Role</th><th>What it does</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Producers (autotrophs)</td><td>Make food by photosynthesis</td><td>Green plants; in oceans, <b>phytoplankton</b>: <b>diatoms</b>, dinoflagellates, coccolithophores, <b>cyanobacteria</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Primary consumers</td><td>Eat producers</td><td>Grasshoppers, deer; in oceans <b>copepods</b>, krill (zooplankton)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Secondary and tertiary consumers</td><td>Eat other animals</td><td>Frogs, snakes, hawks, herrings</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Detritivores</b></td><td>Eat dead organic matter (detritus) and digest it internally, fragmenting it</td><td><b>Earthworms, millipedes, woodlice</b>, dung beetles, termites, sea cucumbers</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Decomposers</b></td><td>Break down dead matter by external digestion (enzymes) and absorb the nutrients</td><td><b>Bacteria and fungi</b> (fungi break down lignin and cellulose of wood)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scavengers</td><td>Eat dead animals</td><td>Vultures, hyenas, jackals</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Viruses are not decomposers</b>: they are obligate parasites inside living cells.</li>
<li><b>Jellyfish</b> (carnivores eating plankton and fish larvae) and <b>seahorses</b> (predators sucking in small crustaceans) are not detritivores.</li>
<li><b>Foraminifera</b> are amoeboid protists with calcium carbonate shells that feed on other organisms: consumers, not producers. Their fossil shells are used to reconstruct past climates.</li>
<li>Steps of decomposition: fragmentation → leaching → catabolism → humification → mineralisation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Food chains and food webs</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>food chain</b> shows the <b>order in which organisms feed on one another</b>: the path of energy from producers to consumers (grass → grasshopper → frog → snake → eagle). It links <b>different species across trophic levels</b>, not members of one population, and it does <b>not show numbers</b> (that is the pyramid of numbers).</li>
<li>A correct marine chain: <b>diatoms → crustaceans → herrings</b>.</li>
<li>A <b>food web</b> is a set of interlinked chains; it is more stable than a single chain.</li>
<li><b>Grazing food chain</b> starts with living plants; <b>detritus food chain</b> starts with dead matter and moves more energy in forests.</li>
<li><b>Lindeman's ten percent law</b>: only about 10% of energy passes from one trophic level to the next.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Ecological pyramid</th><th>Can it be inverted?</th></tr>
<tr><td>Energy</td><td>Never: always upright</td></tr>
<tr><td>Numbers</td><td>Yes: one tree supports many insects</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biomass</td><td>Yes in oceans: the small standing biomass of fast-reproducing phytoplankton supports a larger biomass of zooplankton</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>When a link is lost</h4>
<ul>
<li>If <b>butterflies</b> crashed, <b>pollination</b> of some plants would suffer, and predators that eat them (some <b>wasps, spiders and birds</b>) would decline. There is no link to fungal infections of crops. Butterflies are bio-indicators; India has about 1,300+ species.</li>
<li>If ocean <b>phytoplankton</b> were wiped out, the <b>ocean carbon sink</b> and <b>marine food chains</b> would collapse. Seawater density depends on temperature and salinity, so it would not change.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Biomagnification</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fat-soluble, persistent chemicals such as chlorinated hydrocarbons (DDT, aldrin, endosulfan) and mercury are not broken down or excreted. They <b>accumulate in tissues and concentrate at each higher trophic level</b>. In the chain crop → rat → snake → <b>hawk</b>, the top predator carries the highest load.</li>
<li>DDT thinned raptor eggshells (Rachel Carson, <i>Silent Spring</i>, 1962). The Stockholm Convention (2001) controls persistent organic pollutants; India still makes DDT for vector control under an exemption. Endosulfan was banned by the Supreme Court in 2011 after the Kasaragod tragedy. Mercury in fish caused Minamata disease (see 4.2).</li>
<li><b>Bioaccumulation</b> is build-up within one organism over time; <b>biomagnification</b> is the increase up the food chain.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ecological interactions</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Interaction</th><th>Effect on the two species</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Predation</td><td>+ / − (prey killed)</td><td>Tiger and deer; centipedes eating insects</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parasitism</td><td>+ / − (host usually survives)</td><td>Tapeworm, ticks, <i>Cuscuta</i></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Parasitoidism</b></td><td>+ / − (host eventually killed)</td><td>Larvae of <b>wasps</b> (ichneumonids, braconids, <i>Trichogramma</i>), <b>flies</b> (tachinids) and some <b>beetles</b> (certain carabid ground beetles) inside other insects</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mutualism</td><td>+ / +</td><td>Fig and fig wasp, lichens, mycorrhiza, <i>Rhizobium</i> in legumes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commensalism</td><td>+ / 0</td><td>Orchid on a mango branch, barnacles on whales, cattle egrets with cattle</td></tr>
<tr><td>Amensalism</td><td>− / 0</td><td><i>Penicillium</i> inhibiting bacteria</td></tr>
<tr><td>Competition</td><td>− / −</td><td>Species sharing a scarce resource</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Among carabid beetles, centipedes, flies, termites and wasps, parasitoids occur in <b>three</b>: beetles, flies and wasps. <b>Centipedes are predators</b>; <b>termites are detritivores</b>.</li>
<li>Parasitoids are used in biological pest control: <i>Trichogramma</i> egg parasitoids are released against sugarcane borers and cotton bollworms (ICAR–NBAIR, Bengaluru), part of Integrated Pest Management. See 2.2 for more interactions.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Keystone and other special species</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>keystone species</b> determines whether many other species can persist; its influence is far greater than its numbers. Robert Paine's starfish experiment (1969) showed that removing one predator collapsed diversity. Examples: tigers (top predators), elephants (ecosystem engineers), fig trees (year-round fruit), sea otters, beavers.</li>
<li>Not to be confused with <b>allopatric species</b> (living in separate areas, often after geographic isolation), <b>sympatric species</b> (sharing the same area) or <b>threatened species</b> (an IUCN status).</li>
<li><b>Flagship species</b> attract public support (tiger, panda). <b>Umbrella species</b> need large areas, so protecting them protects many others. <b>Indicator species</b> show environmental health (lichens for SO2; butterflies).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ecological succession</h3>
<ul>
<li>Succession is the gradual change in species composition of a community until a stable <b>climax community</b> is reached.</li>
<li><b>Primary succession</b> starts on surfaces with <b>no soil</b> (bare rock, lava, sand dunes). <b>Secondary succession</b> starts where soil remains after disturbance (abandoned farms, burnt forests) and is faster.</li>
<li>On rock (<b>lithosere/xerosere</b>): <b>lichens</b> (pioneers) → <b>mosses</b> → herbs → shrubs → trees. In water (<b>hydrosere</b>): phytoplankton → submerged and floating plants → reed swamp → marsh meadow → woodland.</li>
<li>On a surface without soil you will find <b>lichens and mosses</b>. <b>Ferns</b> need soil or humus for their roots; <b>mushrooms</b> need organic substrate (soil, wood, dung).</li>
<li>A <b>lichen</b> is a mutualistic association of an <b>alga (or cyanobacterium) and a fungus</b>: the fungus gives structure, absorbs water and secretes acids that weather rock; the alga makes food. Lichens are pollution indicators (absent where SO2 is high) and give litmus and dyes.</li>
<li>In <b>grasslands, trees do not replace grasses</b> because of <b>water limits and fire</b>: low, seasonal rainfall and frequent fires kill tree seedlings while grasses regrow from roots; grazing helps. Examples: savannas, Banni (Kutch), terai and shola grasslands. India's grasslands are often wrongly classed as "wastelands". 2026 is the UN International Year of Rangelands and Pastoralists.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Productivity</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Gross primary productivity</b> is total photosynthesis; <b>net primary productivity</b> (NPP) is what is left after the plants' own respiration.</li>
<li>Decreasing NPP per unit area: <b>mangroves</b> (with estuaries, coral reefs, swamps and tropical rainforests, about 2,000–2,500 g/m²/year) &gt; <b>grasslands</b> (600–900) &gt; <b>lakes</b> (250–400) &gt; <b>open oceans</b> (about 125, nutrient-poor).</li>
<li>Oceans have huge <b>total</b> productivity because of their area, but low productivity per square metre. Lowest: deserts, tundra, open ocean.</li>
<li>Mangroves also store "blue carbon" (see 5.2); the MISHTI scheme (2023) plants mangroves.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ecosystem services</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Category (Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005)</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Provisioning</td><td>Food, water, timber, fibre, medicines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Regulating</td><td>Climate regulation, flood control, water purification, disease control</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Supporting</b></td><td><b>Nutrient cycling</b>, soil formation, primary production, (and in the question's source) <b>crop pollination</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Cultural</td><td>Recreation, spiritual, aesthetic, educational values</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Biodiversity underpins human life through <b>soil formation, prevention of erosion, recycling of waste and pollination</b> (see 2.1). Valuing services: TEEB (see 6.2), natural capital accounting (India's EnviStats), payments for ecosystem services. IPBES (2012) now speaks of "nature's contributions to people".</p>
`);

addBookTopic("environment", "v1-2", `
<p>Nutrients such as carbon, nitrogen and phosphorus cycle between living things and the air, water and rocks. These cycles explain much of what follows in the book: climate change (carbon), fertiliser pollution (nitrogen and phosphorus) and dead zones (oxygen).</p>

<h3>Two kinds of cycle</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Main reservoir</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Gaseous</td><td>Atmosphere (and oceans)</td><td>Carbon, nitrogen, oxygen</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sedimentary</td><td>Rocks and sediments; entry mainly by <b>weathering of rocks</b></td><td><b>Phosphorus</b> (no significant gaseous phase)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mixed</td><td>Rocks, but with an atmospheric phase</td><td>Sulphur (SO2 from volcanoes and fuel burning, H2S, DMS from oceans)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The carbon cycle</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Adds CO2 to the atmosphere</th><th>Removes CO2</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Respiration</b> (plants, animals, microbes); <b>decay of organic matter</b> (also methane where oxygen is low); <b>volcanic action</b> (about 0.3–0.4 billion tonnes a year, about 1% of human emissions); burning fossil fuels; deforestation; cement making; forest fires</td><td><b>Photosynthesis</b>; ocean absorption; rock weathering; burial in sediments</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Sinks</b>: the <b>oceans</b> absorb about a quarter of human CO2 emissions, through dissolution and the <b>photosynthesis of phytoplankton</b>, which sinks carbon to the deep sea (the "biological pump"). Land (forests, soils) takes about another quarter. Sedimentary rocks are the largest store.</li>
<li>CO2 does <b>not</b> escape to the outer stratosphere or space, and air trapped in <b>polar ice</b> is a tiny archive used to study past climate, not a real sink.</li>
<li>Ocean uptake causes acidification, which became the seventh planetary boundary to be crossed (2025; see 5.1).</li>
<li>Atmospheric CO2: about 280 ppm before industrialisation, about 425 ppm in 2025 (Mauna Loa). Global fossil CO2 emissions hit a record of about 38 Gt in 2025 (Global Carbon Project).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The nitrogen cycle</h3>
<ul>
<li>N2 makes up 78% of air but plants cannot use it directly. It must be fixed.</li>
<li><b>Fixation</b>: biological (<i>Rhizobium</i> in legume root nodules, free-living cyanobacteria, <i>Azotobacter</i>), lightning, and industry (Haber–Bosch process for fertilisers).</li>
<li><b>Ammonification</b>: decomposers turn organic nitrogen in dead matter and excreta into ammonia.</li>
<li><b>Nitrification</b>: <i>Nitrosomonas</i> (ammonium → nitrite), <i>Nitrobacter</i> (nitrite → nitrate).</li>
<li><b>Denitrification</b>: <i>Pseudomonas</i>, <i>Thiobacillus</i> return N2 to the air, especially in waterlogged soils.</li>
<li>Nitrogen is added to the soil by <b>animal excretion of urea</b> and by the <b>death and decay of vegetation</b>. UPSC's key did not count <b>burning coal</b>, which mainly puts nitrogen oxides into the air (they can return as nitric acid in rain, so the key was debated).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Reactive nitrogen</h4>
<ul>
<li>Reactive nitrogen means every form except inert N2: ammonia (NH3), nitrogen oxides (NOx), nitrous oxide (N2O), nitrates.</li>
<li>The <b>Indian Nitrogen Assessment</b> (2017), the first national study, found that <b>agricultural soils release nitrogen oxides</b> (and N2O), <b>cattle release ammonia</b> (livestock are India's largest ammonia source), and the <b>poultry industry releases reactive nitrogen</b> compounds. All three statements were correct.</li>
<li>Urea is India's main fertiliser, and nitrogen-use efficiency is only about 33%. Effects: nitrate in groundwater, eutrophication, smog (NOx → ozone), PM2.5 (ammonium salts), climate change (N2O is about 270 times as potent as CO2).</li>
<li>Responses: neem-coated urea, soil health cards, nano urea, and the India-sponsored UNEA resolution on sustainable nitrogen management (2019). The planetary boundary for nitrogen and phosphorus flows is already crossed (see 4.2 on fertiliser overuse).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The phosphorus cycle</h3>
<ul>
<li>Phosphorus enters the cycle mainly through the <b>weathering of phosphate rock</b>; plants take up phosphate, animals eat plants, and it returns to sediments.</li>
<li>It is often the limiting nutrient in fresh water, so phosphate from fertilisers and detergents causes eutrophication and algal blooms.</li>
<li>India imports most of its rock phosphate and DAP; deposits in Rajasthan (Jhamarkotra) and Madhya Pradesh.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Oxygen: who produces it</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Marine phytoplankton and photosynthetic bacteria produce about 50%</b> (estimates 50–80%) of the world's oxygen. <i>Prochlorococcus</i>, the smallest photosynthetic organism, alone may produce up to 20%. Diatoms produce about 20%.</li>
<li>Tropical <b>rainforests</b> produce a lot of oxygen but consume almost all of it through respiration and decay, so they do <b>not</b> produce more net oxygen than the oceans.</li>
<li><b>Air holds far more oxygen than water</b>: air is about 21% oxygen (about 210,000 ppm), while even well-oxygenated surface water holds only about 8–10 mg/L. Oxygen dissolves poorly and less so as water warms, which is why fish need gills and warm polluted water causes fish kills.</li>
<li><b>Dissolved oxygen</b>: healthy water has more than 5 mg/L; below 2 mg/L is hypoxic. CPCB bathing-water criteria: DO ≥ 5 mg/L, BOD ≤ 3 mg/L (see 4.2).</li>
<li><b>Eutrophication</b>: excess nitrogen and phosphorus → algal bloom → decomposition uses up oxygen → <b>dead zones</b> (Gulf of Mexico, Baltic Sea; the Arabian Sea has a natural oxygen-minimum zone). Ocean deoxygenation is linked to warming (IUCN, 2019).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("environment", "v1-3", `
<p>A biome is a large region with a characteristic climate, vegetation and animal life. This topic covers how biomes differ in productivity, soils and reflectivity, how plants adapt to harsh conditions, and the carbon-rich peatlands.</p>

<h3>Major biomes</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Biome</th><th>Climate and vegetation</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Tropical rainforest (evergreen)</td><td>Hot, wet all year; tall, dense, multi-layered canopy</td><td>Highest land productivity and species richness; Amazon, Congo, South-East Asia; in India the Western Ghats, North-East, Andaman and Nicobar</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tropical deciduous (monsoon) forest</td><td>Seasonal rain; trees shed leaves in the dry season</td><td>Most of India's forests (sal, teak)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Savanna and grassland</td><td>Seasonal rain, fire, grazing</td><td>See 1.1 on why trees do not take over</td></tr>
<tr><td>Desert</td><td>Very low rainfall</td><td>Xerophytic plants (below)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temperate forest</td><td>Cool, seasonal</td><td>Brown forest soils rich in humus</td></tr>
<tr><td>Taiga (boreal forest)</td><td>Long cold winters; conifers</td><td>Largest land biome; large carbon stores in soils and peat</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tundra</td><td>Treeless; permafrost; snow-covered much of the year</td><td>Mosses, lichens; thawing permafrost releases methane</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Rainforest soils</h3>
<ul>
<li>Tropical rainforests have the <b>highest primary productivity</b> of any land biome (warmth, light and water all year).</li>
<li>But their <b>soils are poor</b> (laterite, oxisols): most nutrients are held in the living vegetation, and heavy rain leaches the soil. Once cleared, land <b>loses fertility within a few years</b> and cannot support intensive farming without fertiliser. That is why shifting cultivation (<b>jhum</b> in North-East India) keeps moving.</li>
<li>Temperate forest soils keep more organic matter and farm better once cleared. Laterite soils in India: Kerala, Karnataka, Odisha, Meghalaya.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Albedo</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Albedo</b> is the share of sunlight a surface reflects. Fresh snow reflects 80–90%; dark forest about 10–12%; Earth's average is about 30%.</li>
<li>Decreasing albedo: <b>tundra</b> (snow much of the year) &gt; <b>taiga</b> (snowy winters, but dark conifers) &gt; <b>tropical deciduous</b> (leaf-fall exposes lighter ground) &gt; <b>tropical evergreen</b> (dense dark canopy).</li>
<li>Melting Arctic ice lowers albedo and speeds warming: the <b>ice–albedo feedback</b> (see 5.1). Planting forests in snowy boreal zones can even warm the climate by lowering winter albedo.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Plant adaptations</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Group</th><th>Adaptations</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Xerophytes</b> (desert plants)</td><td><b>Hard, waxy leaves</b> with thick cuticle (oleander, <i>Calotropis</i>); <b>tiny leaves</b> (tamarisk, acacia, khejri); <b>thorns or spines instead of leaves</b> with green stems doing photosynthesis (cactus, <i>Opuntia</i>); sunken and fewer stomata; hairy or rolled leaves; deep roots (khejri); succulence (cactus, aloe); <b>CAM photosynthesis</b> with stomata open at night (cacti, pineapple); short-lived ephemerals</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hydrophytes (water plants)</td><td>Air spaces (aerenchyma), reduced roots, floating leaves</td></tr>
<tr><td>Halophytes (salt-tolerant)</td><td>Mangroves: <b>pneumatophores</b> (breathing roots), stilt roots, salt-excreting leaves, <b>vivipary</b> (seeds germinate on the tree) (see 3.2)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Peatlands</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Peat</b> is partly decomposed plant matter that builds up in waterlogged, oxygen-poor conditions (bogs, fens, peat swamp forests).</li>
<li>Peatlands cover only about 3% of land but store about a third of all soil carbon, twice as much as all the world's forests. Drained peat emits about 5% of human CO2.</li>
<li>The world's <b>largest tropical peatland</b> is the <b>Cuvette Centrale in the Congo Basin</b> (Republic of Congo and DR Congo), mapped in 2017: about 167,600 km², storing about 29 billion tonnes of carbon, about three years of global fossil-fuel emissions. Drainage, logging, oil exploration and drought threaten it. Brazzaville Declaration (2018) to protect it; Global Peatlands Initiative (UNEP, 2016).</li>
<li>Other peatlands: Canada and Russia (boreal); Indonesia and Malaysia (peat swamp forests drained for palm oil, causing haze from peat fires); the Amazon's Pastaza-Marañón basin (Peru). India: Himalayan peatlands (Ladakh, Sikkim), Kashmir valley, North-East.</li>
<li>Kikori Basin is in Papua New Guinea (rainforest); the Río de la Plata is an Argentina–Uruguay estuary.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
