addBookTopic("environment", "v3-2", `
<p>Wetlands, mangroves and coral reefs are among the most productive and most threatened ecosystems. They purify water, buffer coasts against storms, store carbon and support fisheries.</p>

<h3>Wetlands and the Ramsar Convention</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Ramsar Convention</b> (Ramsar, Iran, 1971) asks parties to designate wetlands of international importance and to make "<b>wise use</b>" of all wetlands: <b>conservation with simultaneous sustainable use</b>. It does <b>not</b> make it mandatory to protect and conserve <b>all</b> wetlands in a country.</li>
<li>India joined in 1982; its first sites were <b>Chilika</b> (Odisha) and <b>Keoladeo</b> (Rajasthan), 1981. India has <b>100 Ramsar sites</b> (June 2026; the 100th is Surha Tal, Ballia, UP), the most in Asia and third in the world after the UK and Mexico. Tamil Nadu has the most, then Uttar Pradesh. Largest: Sundarbans (2019). World Wetlands Day: 2 February. Ramsar COP15: Victoria Falls, Zimbabwe (July 2025).</li>
<li>The <b>Montreux Record</b> (1990) lists Ramsar sites where <b>changes in ecological character have occurred, are occurring or are likely to occur because of human interference</b>. Indian sites on it: <b>Keoladeo</b> and <b>Loktak</b>. Chilika was added in 1993 and removed in 2002 after restoration.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Wetland</th><th>Correct location</th></tr>
<tr><td>Hokersar</td><td><b>Jammu and Kashmir</b> (near Srinagar), not Punjab; Ramsar 2005</td></tr>
<tr><td>Renuka</td><td><b>Himachal Pradesh</b> (Sirmaur); largest natural lake in HP; among the smallest Ramsar sites</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rudrasagar</td><td><b>Tripura</b>; the Neermahal water palace stands in it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sasthamkotta</td><td><b>Kerala</b> (Kollam), not Tamil Nadu; Kerala's largest freshwater lake; <i>Chaoborus</i> larvae keep its water clean</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Other Kerala sites: Ashtamudi, Vembanad-Kol. J&K and Ladakh: Wular, Hokersar, Surinsar-Mansar, Shallbugh, Hygam, Tso Moriri, Tso Kar.</p>
<h4>Indian wetland facts</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Gujarat</b> has the largest wetland area of any State (about a fifth of India's), thanks to the Rann of Kutch, mudflats and the Gulfs of Kutch and Khambhat.</li>
<li><b>Inland wetlands</b> (rivers, lakes, reservoirs, ponds, floodplains) cover about two-thirds of India's wetland area, <b>more</b> than coastal wetlands.</li>
<li>National Wetland Decadal Change Atlas (ISRO SAC, 2022): about 2.3 lakh wetlands, about 16 million ha, about 4.9% of India's area. About 30% of natural wetlands were lost in four decades.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Wetlands as kidneys</h4>
<ul>
<li>"If forests are the lungs of the Earth, wetlands are its <b>kidneys</b>": like kidneys filtering blood, <b>wetland plants and microbes absorb heavy metals and excess nutrients</b>, trap sediment and break down pollutants.</li>
<li>Other services: flood control (sponges), groundwater recharge, shoreline protection, carbon storage (peatlands; see 1.3), bird habitat, fisheries.</li>
<li><b>East Kolkata Wetlands</b> (Ramsar 2002) treat about 750 million litres of sewage a day naturally while supporting fisheries and vegetable farming: a model of wise use. Constructed wetlands and phytoremediation plants (water hyacinth, duckweed, vetiver, <i>Typha</i>) treat wastewater (see 4.3).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Wetland rules</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Wetlands (Conservation and Management) Rules 2010</b> were made under the <b>Environment (Protection) Act 1986</b>, not on Ramsar's recommendation. They defined wetlands to <b>include their catchment or drainage areas</b> as determined by the Central Wetlands Regulatory Authority.</li>
<li>The <b>2017 Rules</b> replaced them: decentralised to State Wetland Authorities, banned encroachment, waste dumping and industrial conversion, and dropped the catchment from the definition.</li>
<li>Amrit Dharohar scheme (2023) for Ramsar sites; National Plan for Conservation of Aquatic Ecosystems.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Mangroves</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mangroves are salt-tolerant (halophytic) trees of tropical coasts and estuaries, with <b>pneumatophores</b> (breathing roots), <b>stilt and prop roots</b>, salt-excreting leaves and <b>vivipary</b>.</li>
<li>As a safety hedge against coastal calamities, their <b>extensive roots anchor them so they are not uprooted</b>, and they absorb wave energy. In the 2004 tsunami, villages behind mangroves at Pichavaram and Muthupet suffered less.</li>
<li>For climate resilience they act as <b>natural bio-shields against tidal surges and as livelihood bases</b> (fisheries, honey, fuelwood, tourism). Converting them to paddy or aquaculture is exactly what destroys them; they are saline, not freshwater stores.</li>
<li>India's mangrove cover: about 4,992 km² (ISFR 2023), about 0.15% of the country. West Bengal (Sundarbans, about 42%) leads, then Gujarat, then Andaman and Nicobar. Bhitarkanika (Odisha) is the second-largest mangrove ecosystem; also Pichavaram (Tamil Nadu), Coringa (Andhra).</li>
<li>The <b>Sundarbans</b> (India and Bangladesh) is the world's largest mangrove forest: national park, tiger reserve, biosphere reserve, Ramsar site and World Heritage Site; named after the sundari tree.</li>
<li>Mangroves store 3–5 times more carbon per hectare than tropical forests, mostly in soil ("blue carbon"; see 5.2).</li>
<li>Policies: MISHTI (Budget 2023), Mangrove Alliance for Climate (COP27, 2022; India joined), CRZ-I protection. Mangrove Day: 26 July.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Mangrove</th><th>State</th></tr>
<tr><td>Achra–Ratnagiri</td><td>Maharashtra</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coondapur (Kundapura)</td><td>Karnataka</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pichavaram</td><td>Tamil Nadu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vembanad</td><td>Kerala</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Coral reefs</h3>
<ul>
<li>Corals are animals (cnidarians) living with symbiotic algae (<b>zooxanthellae</b>) that give them food and colour (see 2.2). They need warm (about 20–29°C), clear, shallow, sunlit, salty water, so <b>most reefs are in tropical waters</b> (30°N–30°S).</li>
<li><b>Australia, Indonesia and the Philippines</b> together hold <b>more than a third</b> of the world's reefs (Indonesia alone about 18%; the Coral Triangle).</li>
<li>Reefs host <b>far more animal phyla</b> than tropical rainforests (about 32 of 34 phyla, against about 9), though rainforests have more species.</li>
<li>The world's largest reef is the <b>Great Barrier Reef</b> off Queensland, <b>Australia</b> (about 2,300 km; World Heritage Site 1981).</li>
<li>Types: fringing, barrier, atoll (Darwin's subsidence theory).</li>
<li><b>Bleaching</b>: heat stress makes corals expel their algae and turn white. The fourth global bleaching event began in 2023 and had affected about 84% of the world's reefs by 2025. Other threats: acidification, pollution, destructive fishing, crown-of-thorns starfish.</li>
<li>India's reefs: Gulf of Mannar, Gulf of Kutch, Andaman and Nicobar, Lakshadweep (atolls); patches at Malvan, Netrani, Gaveshani. All reef-building corals are in Schedule I.</li>
<li><b>Biorock technology</b> is used for <b>restoring damaged coral reefs</b>: a low-voltage current through a steel frame on the seabed makes calcium carbonate deposit on it, and coral fragments attached grow faster and resist bleaching. The Zoological Survey of India used it in the Gulf of Kutch (2020), a first in India. Other methods: coral gardening, 3D-printed and artificial reefs, larval seeding. India is a member of the International Coral Reef Initiative.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Turtle nesting</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Odisha's</b> coast hosts the world's largest mass nesting (<b>arribada</b>) of <b>Olive Ridley</b> turtles at <b>Gahirmatha</b> (Bhitarkanika; marine sanctuary 1997), <b>Rushikulya</b> and the <b>Devi</b> river mouth. Rushikulya saw a record of over 6 lakh nesting turtles in 2025.</li>
<li>Olive Ridleys are the smallest sea turtles (the question's "giant, from South America" was loose; Odisha's come from the Indian Ocean). Vulnerable; Schedule I. Trawlers must use Turtle Excluder Devices; fishing is banned during nesting; the Coast Guard runs Operation Olivia.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("environment", "v3-3", `
<p>India's environmental protection rests on the Constitution, a set of central laws and a network of regulators and research institutions. UPSC asks which law created which body, what each law allows, and how forests are measured.</p>

<h3>Constitutional basis</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 48A</b> (DPSP): the State shall protect and improve the environment and safeguard forests and wildlife. <b>Article 51A(g)</b> (Fundamental Duty): protect and improve the natural environment.</li>
<li>The 42nd Amendment (1976) moved <b>forests</b> and <b>protection of wild animals and birds</b> from the State List to the <b>Concurrent List</b> (Entries 17A, 17B).</li>
<li><b>Article 21</b>: the right to a healthy environment is part of the right to life. Article 253 lets Parliament implement international agreements (used for the EPA 1986).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Key laws</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Law</th><th>Key points</th></tr>
<tr><td>Indian Forest Act 1927</td><td>Reserved and protected forests</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act 1960</td><td>Set up the <b>Animal Welfare Board of India</b> (1962, Section 4), through Rukmini Devi Arundale's efforts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wild Life (Protection) Act 1972</td><td>Schedules, protected areas, NTCA (2006), Wildlife Crime Control Bureau (2006); amended 2022</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td>Created the <b>CPCB</b> and State boards</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forest (Conservation) Act 1980</td><td>Centre's approval to divert forest land; renamed Van (Sanrakshan Evam Samvardhan) Adhiniyam in 2023</td></tr>
<tr><td>Air Act 1981</td><td>CPCB's air powers</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Environment (Protection) Act 1986</b></td><td>Umbrella law after the Bhopal gas tragedy, under Article 253 (Stockholm 1972)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biological Diversity Act 2002</td><td>NBA, State boards, BMCs; amended 2023</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forest Rights Act 2006</td><td>Rights of forest dwellers</td></tr>
<tr><td>National Green Tribunal Act 2010</td><td>Environmental tribunal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compensatory Afforestation Fund Act 2016</td><td>CAMPA</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Laws with a <b>bearing on biodiversity</b> include not only the core laws but also the <b>Import and Export (Control) Act 1947</b> (wildlife trade; now the Foreign Trade Act 1992), the <b>Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act 1957</b> (habitats), the <b>Customs Act 1962</b> (seizure of smuggled wildlife) and the <b>Indian Forest Act 1927</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Environment (Protection) Act 1986</h3>
<ul>
<li>It empowers the Centre to take all measures to protect the environment, including stating the requirement and procedure for <b>public participation</b> (the EIA Notifications of 1994 and 2006 mandate public hearings) and <b>laying down standards for emission or discharge</b> of pollutants (Section 3(2)(iv), Section 6, Schedules of the EP Rules).</li>
<li>Bodies constituted under it (Section 3(3)): the <b>Central Ground Water Authority</b> (1997, on the Supreme Court's direction in M.C. Mehta), the National Ganga Council and NMCG as authority (2016), Taj Trapezium Zone Authority (1998), Coastal Zone Management Authorities, EPCA for Delhi (1998; replaced by the Commission for Air Quality Management, 2020–21). <b>GEAC</b> sits under its 1989 Rules.</li>
<li>Not under the EPA: the <b>Central Water Commission</b> (1945; surface water, flood forecasting, dam safety; Jal Shakti), the <b>Central Ground Water Board</b> (1970; groundwater surveys, aquifer mapping), the <b>National Water Development Agency</b> (1982; river-linking studies, Ken–Betwa first link).</li>
<li>Rules under the EPA: EIA Notification, CRZ, Hazardous Waste, Plastic, E-waste, Batteries, Bio-medical, Solid Waste, Ozone Depleting Substances, Noise, Wetlands Rules (see 3.2 and 4.3), Eco-Sensitive Zones (see 3.1).</li>
<li>The Jan Vishwas Act 2023 replaced imprisonment for many EPA violations with monetary penalties.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Wild Life (Protection) Act 1972</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Section 9</b> bans hunting of scheduled animals, except under <b>Section 11</b> (an animal dangerous to human life or diseased beyond recovery, with the Chief Wildlife Warden's written order) and <b>Section 12</b> (education, research, scientific management, zoos). <b>Gharial, Indian wild ass and wild buffalo</b> (all Schedule I) cannot be hunted except under such provisions.</li>
<li>Wild animals are <b>not</b> the sole property of the government: Section 39 makes animals that are hunted, killed, found dead or captured (and their parts) government property.</li>
<li>A protected animal gets the <b>same protection inside or outside</b> protected areas.</li>
<li>A <b>mere apprehension</b> that an animal may endanger human life is <b>not</b> enough to capture or kill it; it must actually have become dangerous, recorded in writing, and capture or translocation must be impossible. (Killing in good faith in self-defence is not an offence.)</li>
<li><b>Schedule I</b> gives the highest protection. A tortoise in Schedule I enjoys <b>the same protection as the tiger</b>; listing does not mean it is extinct or endemic. Penalties for Schedule I offences: 3–7 years and a fine of at least ₹25,000.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The 2022 amendment (in force April 2023)</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>New schedule</th><th>Content</th></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule I</td><td>Animals with the highest protection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule II</td><td>Animals with lesser protection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule III</td><td><b>Specified plants</b> (formerly Schedule VI)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule IV</td><td>Specimens listed in CITES appendices</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Six schedules became four. The separate <b>vermin</b> schedule (formerly Schedule V, which included the <b>Indian flying fox</b>, common crows and rats) was abolished, though the Centre can still declare a species vermin in an area for a period (Section 62). UPSC dropped a 2024 question on the flying fox as vermin for this reason. The flying fox eats fruit and nectar, not blood (only three Latin American vampire bats do).</li>
<li>Under the old <b>Schedule VI</b>, a <b>licence was required to cultivate</b> a specified plant (Section 17C). It listed beddomes cycad, blue vanda, kuth, lady's slipper orchids, pitcher plant (<i>Nepenthes khasiana</i>) and red vanda.</li>
<li>The amendment also implements CITES (Management and Scientific Authorities), lets the Centre regulate invasive species, allows transfer of captive elephants for religious purposes (controversial) and raises penalties.</li>
<li>Recent issues: human–wildlife conflict (Kerala declared it a State-specific disaster, 2025); requests to declare wild boar vermin.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Biodiversity governance</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Biological Diversity Act 2002</b> implements the <b>CBD</b> (1992; objectives: conservation, sustainable use, fair and equitable benefit sharing). Three tiers: <b>National Biodiversity Authority</b> (Chennai, 2003), <b>State Biodiversity Boards</b>, and <b>Biodiversity Management Committees</b> in every local body (about 2.7 lakh+).</li>
<li>The <b>NBA</b> regulates access to biological resources and traditional knowledge and <b>checks biopiracy</b> (it opposed neem- and turmeric-type patents; it took action over Bt brinjal biopiracy, 2011). Applying for IPR on inventions based on Indian biological resources needed <b>NBA approval</b> (Section 6). The 2023 amendment moved this to approval before the <b>grant</b> of the IPR. The NBA does <b>not</b> supervise GM crop research (that is GEAC and the DBT's Review Committee on Genetic Manipulation).</li>
<li><b>BMCs are key to the Nagoya Protocol's</b> access-and-benefit-sharing objectives: they prepare People's Biodiversity Registers, are consulted on access decisions and <b>may levy collection fees</b> on anyone collecting biological resources commercially in their area (Section 41(3)).</li>
<li>Biological Diversity (Amendment) Act 2023: decriminalised offences, exempted AYUSH practitioners and cultivated medicinal plants from benefit sharing, eased rules for Indian companies, and exempted "codified traditional knowledge" (criticised).</li>
<li>The Kani tribe's share in the Jeevani drug is the famous benefit-sharing case.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Biosafety and GM crops</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Cartagena Protocol on Biosafety</b> (adopted 2000, in force 2003) under the CBD deals with <b>genetically modified (living modified) organisms</b>: their safe transboundary movement, using the precautionary principle and advance informed agreement. In India it is implemented by the <b>Ministry of Environment</b>. India is a party to both the CBD and the protocol. Its first Meeting of the Parties was held in Kuala Lumpur (2004), not the Philippines. Nagoya–Kuala Lumpur Supplementary Protocol on Liability and Redress (in force 2018).</li>
<li>The <b>Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee</b> (renamed from "Approval" in 2010) sits under the <b>Environment Ministry</b> and was constituted under the 1989 Rules made under the <b>Environment (Protection) Act 1986</b>. It approves large-scale use and environmental release of GM organisms; it cleared Bt cotton in March 2002.</li>
<li>Status of GM crops: <b>Bt cotton</b> (2002) is the only GM crop approved for commercial cultivation (illegal herbicide-tolerant Bt cotton is widespread). Bt brinjal: approved by GEAC in 2009, then a moratorium (2010). GM mustard DMH-11: environmental release approved (2022); after a split Supreme Court verdict (2024) the Court asked for a national GM policy. Genome-edited crops without foreign DNA (SDN-1, SDN-2) were exempted from GM rules (2022), and India released two genome-edited rice varieties in May 2025.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Regulators and tribunals</h3>
<h4>National Green Tribunal (NGT)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Set up in October 2010 under the NGT Act 2010, enacted in consonance with the <b>right to a healthy environment under Article 21</b> (and India's Stockholm and Rio commitments). Articles 275(1) and 243A have nothing to do with it.</li>
<li>Principal bench in Delhi; zonal benches at Bhopal, Pune, Kolkata and Chennai. Chaired by a retired Supreme Court judge or High Court Chief Justice, with expert members.</li>
<li>It hears civil cases under <b>seven laws</b>: Water Act, Water Cess Act, Forest (Conservation) Act, Air Act, EPA, Public Liability Insurance Act and Biological Diversity Act. Not the Wild Life Act or the Forest Rights Act.</li>
<li>It applies sustainable development, precautionary and polluter-pays principles; cases are to be disposed of within six months; appeals go to the Supreme Court within 90 days. India was the third country with a specialised environmental tribunal (after Australia and New Zealand).</li>
</ul>
<h4>NGT vs CPCB</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Both are statutory</b>: the CPCB was constituted in 1974 under the Water Act (and given powers under the Air Act 1981), not by an executive order.</li>
<li>The NGT provides <b>environmental justice</b> and reduces litigation in higher courts; the CPCB <b>promotes cleanliness of streams and wells</b>, improves air quality, sets standards and coordinates State boards.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Other bodies</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>NTCA</b>: a <b>statutory</b> body (WPA amendment 2006, Section 38L), chaired by the Environment Minister.</li>
<li><b>Animal Welfare Board of India</b>: under the <b>Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act 1960</b>, not the EPA. Headquarters was <b>Chennai</b> until 2018; now Ballabhgarh (Haryana). Now under the Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying. In May 2023 the Supreme Court upheld the State laws permitting Jallikattu and similar sports.</li>
<li><b>National Ganga River Basin Authority</b> (2009, under the EPA): made the <b>river basin the unit of planning</b> and <b>spearheaded Ganga conservation nationally</b>; it was <b>chaired by the Prime Minister</b>, not by Chief Ministers in rotation. Ganga Action Plan (1985) → Ganga declared National River (2008) → NGRBA (2009) → Namami Gange (2014) → National Ganga Council chaired by the PM, with NMCG as authority (2016); Namami Gange 2.0 (2021–26).</li>
<li>Ganga Action Plan Phase II was <b>merged into the National River Conservation Plan</b> (1996). The <b>National Museum of Natural History</b> (New Delhi, 1978) gave non-formal environmental education (its building burned in 2016). <b>ENVIS</b> (1982) is a decentralised environmental information network (renamed EIACP in 2022). The <b>Gulf of Mannar</b> is a biosphere reserve (1989).</li>
</ul>
<h4>The Environment Ministry's institutions and NIRANTAR</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Institution</th><th>HQ</th><th>Role</th></tr>
<tr><td>Botanical Survey of India (1890)</td><td>Kolkata</td><td>Plant inventory</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zoological Survey of India (1916)</td><td>Kolkata</td><td>Faunal survey</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forest Survey of India (1981)</td><td>Dehradun</td><td>Biennial India State of Forest Report</td></tr>
<tr><td>ICFRE</td><td>Dehradun</td><td>Forestry research; administers the Green Credit Programme</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wildlife Institute of India</td><td>Dehradun</td><td>Wildlife research; tiger estimation with NTCA</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indian Institute of Forest Management</td><td>Bhopal</td><td>Forest management education</td></tr>
<tr><td>Central Zoo Authority (1992)</td><td>New Delhi</td><td>Statutory regulator of zoos</td></tr>
<tr><td>G.B. Pant National Institute of Himalayan Environment</td><td>Almora</td><td>Himalayan ecology</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>NIRANTAR</b> (formalised 2025) is the ministry's umbrella platform grouping these institutions into verticals with lead institutes: <b>Ecosystem Survey and Analysis</b> led by the <b>Botanical Survey of India</b>, and <b>Capacity Development Support</b> led by <b>IIFM Bhopal</b>. Research on ecosystem services is led by a research institution (ICFRE or WII), <b>not the Central Zoo Authority</b>.</p>

<h3>Forests</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>India State of Forest Report</b> (Forest Survey of India, biennial). ISFR 2023 (December 2024): forest cover about <b>21.76%</b> and tree cover 3.41%, together <b>25.17%</b> of India's area.</li>
<li>Classes by canopy density: very dense (70%+), moderately dense (40–70%), open (10–40%), scrub (below 10%).</li>
<li>Largest forest area: Madhya Pradesh, then Arunachal Pradesh, Chhattisgarh. Highest percentage: Mizoram (about 84–85%) and Lakshadweep. Lowest among States: <b>Haryana</b> and Punjab (about 3.6–4%; Haryana about 1,614 km²).</li>
<li>Forest cover as % of area (ascending): <b>Maharashtra</b> (about 16.5%) &lt; <b>Madhya Pradesh</b> (about 25%) &lt; <b>Odisha</b> (about 33%) &lt; <b>Chhattisgarh</b> (about 41%).</li>
<li>In 2003 forest cover was about 20.6% of India's area (UPSC accepted "around 20%, dense forest around 40% of it", though dense forest was actually nearer 58%). The <b>National Forest Policy 1988</b> and the <b>National Forestry Action Programme</b> (1999) aim at <b>one-third</b> of India's area under forest or tree cover (two-thirds in hills).</li>
<li>India's NDC: an extra carbon sink of 2.5–3 billion tonnes CO2e by 2030 through forests and trees.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Green India Mission and CAMPA</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>National Mission for a Green India</b> (one of eight NAPCC missions; launched 2014) aims to <b>restore and enhance forest cover and respond to climate change through adaptation and mitigation</b>; not green accounting of budgets or a second green revolution. Targets: more forest or tree cover on 5 million ha and better quality on another 5 million ha; livelihoods for about 3 million households. The 2025 revision focuses on the Aravallis, Western Ghats, Himalaya and mangroves.</li>
<li>NAPCC (2008) missions: Solar, Enhanced Energy Efficiency, Sustainable Habitat, Water, Himalayan Ecosystem, Green India, Sustainable Agriculture, Strategic Knowledge.</li>
<li>When forest land is diverted, the user pays for <b>compensatory afforestation</b> and the Net Present Value of the forest. The <b>Compensatory Afforestation Fund Act 2016</b> created the <b>National CAMPA and State CAMPAs</b> and funds in the public accounts (90% to States). It does <b>not</b> make people's participation or Gram Sabha consultation mandatory, a key criticism. The Supreme Court ordered CAMPA's creation in the T.N. Godavarman case (2002). CAF Rules 2018 bar spending on salaries and buildings.</li>
<li>Green Credit Programme (2023): tradable credits for planting on degraded land. Aravalli Green Wall (2023).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
