addBookTopic("environment", "v4-1", `
<p>Air pollution is India's largest environmental health risk. This topic covers the main pollutants and their sources, how they are measured, smog and acid rain, and the emissions of particular industries.</p>

<h3>Measuring air quality</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's <b>National Air Quality Index</b> (CPCB with IIT Kanpur, April 2015) uses <b>eight pollutants</b>: PM10, PM2.5, <b>NO2, SO2, CO</b>, O3, NH3 and lead. <b>Carbon dioxide and methane</b> are greenhouse gases, <b>not</b> part of the AQI.</li>
<li>The index takes the worst sub-index (at least three pollutants, one of them PM). Categories: Good (0–50), Satisfactory (51–100), Moderately polluted (101–200), Poor (201–300), Very poor (301–400), Severe (401–500). Delhi's Graded Response Action Plan (GRAP) acts by AQI stage.</li>
<li><b>National Ambient Air Quality Standards</b> (2009) cover 12 pollutants: the eight above plus <b>benzene</b>, benzo(a)pyrene, arsenic and nickel.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Pollutant</th><th>WHO guideline 2021</th><th>India NAAQS 2009</th></tr>
<tr><td>PM2.5 annual / 24-hour</td><td><b>5 / 15 µg/m³</b> (tightened from 10 / 25 in 2005)</td><td>40 / 60</td></tr>
<tr><td>PM10 annual / 24-hour</td><td>15 / 45</td><td>60 / 100</td></tr>
<tr><td>NO2 annual / 24-hour</td><td>10 / 25</td><td>40 / 80</td></tr>
<tr><td>SO2 24-hour</td><td>40</td><td>80</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ozone 8-hour</td><td>100 (60 in peak season)</td><td>100</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>PM10</b> lodges deep in the lungs; it is the finer <b>PM2.5</b> (and ultrafine particles under 0.1 µm) that can <b>cross into the bloodstream</b>.</li>
<li>Ground-level <b>ozone</b> forms when NOx and VOCs react in sunlight, so it peaks in <b>sunny, hot, calm weather</b>, not inclement weather. Excess ozone <b>can trigger asthma</b> and harm lungs.</li>
<li><b>National Clean Air Programme</b> (2019): a 40% cut in particulate levels by 2025–26 (from 2017) in 131 non-attainment cities. Commission for Air Quality Management for Delhi-NCR (Act of 2021).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Key pollutants and their sources</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Pollutant</th><th>Main sources</th><th>Effects</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sulphur dioxide</b></td><td>Largest source (US EPA): <b>power plants burning fossil fuels</b>, especially coal; also smelting of sulphide ores, high-sulphur fuels in ships and locomotives, volcanoes</td><td>Respiratory illness, sulphate PM2.5, acid rain</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nitrogen oxides</td><td>Vehicles, power plants, industry (high-temperature combustion)</td><td>Smog, ozone, acid rain</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carbon monoxide</td><td>Incomplete combustion</td><td>Binds haemoglobin; toxic</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Benzene</b> (Group 1 carcinogen; leukaemia)</td><td><b>Vehicle exhaust</b> and petrol evaporation, <b>tobacco smoke</b>, <b>wood burning</b>. UPSC did not count varnished furniture or polyurethane products (debated, since some paints and foams emit VOCs)</td><td>Bone-marrow damage. NAAQS annual limit 5 µg/m³; BS-VI petrol limits benzene to 1%</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Magnetite nanoparticles</b></td><td>Vehicle <b>brakes</b> and <b>engines</b>, and high-temperature combustion in <b>power plants</b>; not microwaves or telephone lines</td><td>Found in human brain tissue (2016 study); suspected link to Alzheimer's</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>India is the world's largest SO2 emitter, mostly from coal power plants. Indoor pollutants: formaldehyde (pressed wood, furniture), radon, CO from stoves, VOCs from paints.</li>
<li><b>Non-exhaust emissions</b> (brake, tyre and road wear) now exceed exhaust PM in many cities, which matters even for electric vehicles; Euro 7 norms will cover brake particles.</li>
<li><b>Furnace oil</b> is a heavy residue <b>from oil refineries</b>, <b>used by some industries to generate power</b> and heat, and it <b>emits sulphur</b>. The Supreme Court banned furnace oil and pet coke in Delhi-NCR States (2017). Refinery products from light to heavy: LPG, petrol, kerosene/ATF, diesel, lubricants, furnace oil, bitumen, pet coke.</li>
<li>Fuel and shipping norms: BS-VI (April 2020) limits sulphur in petrol and diesel to 10 ppm; IMO 2020 cut sulphur in ship fuel from 3.5% to 0.5%.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Smog</h3>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Photochemical (Los Angeles) smog</th><th>Classical (London) smog</th></tr>
<tr><td>Ingredients</td><td>NOx and volatile organic compounds from vehicles, reacting in <b>sunlight</b></td><td>Coal smoke, SO2 and fog</td></tr>
<tr><td>Products</td><td><b>Ozone</b>, <b>PAN</b> (peroxyacetyl nitrate), aldehydes, NO2</td><td>Sulphur compounds, soot</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conditions</td><td>Warm, sunny days</td><td>Cold, humid conditions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nature</td><td>Oxidising, brownish; irritates eyes and lungs</td><td>Reducing (Great Smog of London, 1952)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Photochemical smog thus results from the reaction among <b>NO2, ozone and PAN in sunlight</b>. Delhi's winter smog mixes both types, worsened by temperature inversion, low winds and stubble burning.</p>

<h3>Acid rain</h3>
<ul>
<li>Normal rain has a pH of about 5.6 because CO2 forms weak carbonic acid. Acid rain (pH below 5.6) is caused by <b>sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides</b>, which form <b>sulphuric and nitric acids</b>.</li>
<li><b>Not</b> causes: carbon monoxide (toxic, but forms no acid), ozone (an oxidant that helps convert SO2 and NOx, but is not itself a source), "oxides of hydrogen" (water). The 2013 option said "nitrous oxide"; strictly it is nitrogen oxides (NO, NO2), while nitrous oxide (N2O) is mainly a greenhouse gas.</li>
<li><b>Coal-based thermal power plants do contribute to acid rain</b> (through SO2 and NOx). That coal also emits oxides of carbon is true but does <b>not explain</b> acid rain.</li>
<li>Effects: acidified lakes (Scandinavia), forest damage (Black Forest), leaching of soil nutrients and release of toxic aluminium, corrosion of monuments. The Taj Mahal's yellowing ("marble cancer") from Mathura refinery emissions led to the Taj Trapezium Zone (M.C. Mehta, 1996).</li>
<li>Most Indian rain is not highly acidic because alkaline dust neutralises it. Monitoring: IMD's Global Atmosphere Watch stations.</li>
<li>Controls: flue gas desulphurisation, low-sulphur fuels, BS-VI fuels, catalytic converters.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Coal power and industry</h3>
<ul>
<li>Coal combustion at thermal plants emits <b>CO2, NOx and SOx</b>, plus fly ash, particulates and mercury. Coal power is India's largest single CO2 source.</li>
<li><b>Coal ash contains arsenic, lead and mercury</b> (and cadmium, chromium, selenium); ash-pond breaches pollute water. <b>Indian coal has high ash</b> (30–45%) but low sulphur. See 4.2 on fly ash uses.</li>
<li>Emission norms of December 2015 for SO2, NOx, particulates, mercury and water use required <b>flue gas desulphurisation (FGD)</b>. Deadlines slipped repeatedly, and in July 2025 the government exempted most plants (those away from million-plus cities and critically polluted areas) from FGD.</li>
<li>Thermal plants in India: about 215–224 GW of coal, about 45% of capacity but 70–75% of generation. NTPC's Vindhyachal (MP) is the largest plant. About 80 GW of new coal capacity is planned by 2032.</li>
<li>Thermal plant myths: some <b>do use seawater</b> (coastal plants at Mundra, Krishnapatnam, Tuticorin, Ennore, Udupi); many <b>are in water-stressed districts</b> (about 40% of capacity); many <b>are privately owned</b> (Adani, Tata, JSW, Reliance, Vedanta, Torrent: about 30%+ of capacity). So none of the three "none of them" statements was correct. 2015 norms capped water use (3.5 m³/MWh, 2.5 for new plants) and the 2016 tariff policy requires treated sewage within 50 km.</li>
<li><b>Steel industry</b> releases oxides of sulphur, oxides of nitrogen, <b>carbon monoxide</b> (from blast furnaces) and <b>CO2</b> (Indian steel emits about 2.5 t CO2 per tonne, above the global average of about 1.9); also particulates, PAHs and benzene from coke ovens. Steel is about 10–12% of India's CO2 emissions. Decarbonisation: scrap-based electric arc furnaces, green-hydrogen DRI, CCUS, Green Steel Taxonomy (2024). The EU's CBAM (definitive from 2026) affects Indian steel and aluminium exports.</li>
<li><b>Copper smelting</b>: copper ores are mostly sulphides (chalcopyrite), so smelting <b>releases SO2</b> (often captured to make sulphuric acid), and <b>copper slag can leach heavy metals</b> (arsenic, lead, cadmium). "Lethal quantities of carbon monoxide" is not a concern. The Sterlite smelter at Thoothukudi was shut in 2018 after protests and police firing; the Supreme Court upheld the closure (2024). Hindustan Copper: Khetri, Malanjkhand, Singhbhum.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Crop residue burning</h3>
<ul>
<li>Burning crop or biomass residue releases <b>carbon monoxide, methane and sulphur dioxide</b>, plus CO2, NOx, VOCs, PM2.5 and black carbon. <b>Ozone</b> forms in the smoke plume from NOx and VOCs in sunlight, so UPSC counted all four.</li>
<li>Mainly paddy straw in Punjab, Haryana and western UP in October–November. Causes: combine harvesters leave stubble; labour costs; the Punjab Preservation of Subsoil Water Act (2009) delayed transplanting and squeezed the window before wheat sowing.</li>
<li>Solutions: Happy Seeder, Super SMS, PUSA bio-decomposer, ex-situ use (5–7% biomass co-firing mandated in thermal plants, 2G ethanol, compressed biogas), crop diversification. The CAQM can levy environmental compensation from farmers who burn residue (raised in 2024).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("environment", "v4-2", `
<p>Beyond air, pollutants reach water, soil and food. This topic covers how water quality is measured, natural and man-made contaminants, persistent chemicals and the effects of fertilisers and pesticides.</p>

<h3>Measuring water pollution</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>BOD</b> (Biological/Biochemical Oxygen Demand)</td><td>Oxygen consumed by microbes to decompose organic matter (usually over 5 days at 20°C, in mg/L). A standard criterion for <b>pollution assay in aquatic ecosystems</b>: high BOD means heavy organic pollution. Clean water under 1–2 mg/L; raw sewage 200–600 mg/L; bathing water ≤ 3 mg/L (CPCB)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dissolved Oxygen</td><td>Bathing water ≥ 5 mg/L; fish need more than 4 mg/L (see 1.2)</td></tr>
<tr><td>COD (Chemical Oxygen Demand)</td><td>Oxygen needed to oxidise all organic matter chemically; always more than BOD</td></tr>
<tr><td>Faecal coliform</td><td>Sewage contamination; bathing limit 2,500 MPN/100 ml (desirable 500)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>CPCB (2022) identified 311 polluted river stretches by BOD.</p>

<h3>Drinking water contaminants in India</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Contaminant</th><th>Where and effects</th><th>BIS limit (IS 10500)</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Arsenic</b></td><td>Natural, in Ganga–Brahmaputra plains (West Bengal, Bihar, UP, Assam, Punjab); skin lesions, cancers</td><td>0.01 mg/L</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Fluoride</b></td><td>Rajasthan, Telangana, Andhra, Karnataka, Gujarat; dental and skeletal fluorosis</td><td>1.0 (permissible 1.5) mg/L</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Uranium</b></td><td>Groundwater of Punjab, Rajasthan, Haryana and others (2018 study; CGWB reports)</td><td>0.03 mg/L</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nitrate</td><td>From fertilisers; "blue baby syndrome"</td><td>45 mg/L</td></tr>
<tr><td>Iron, salinity, lead</td><td>Iron in the North-East</td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Sorbitol</b> (a sugar alcohol sweetener) and <b>formaldehyde</b> (an indoor air pollutant and preservative) are not drinking-water pollutants in India. Jal Jeevan Mission (2019): tap water to every rural household, about 80%+ covered by 2025, extended to 2028.</p>

<h3>Heavy metals</h3>
<h4>Lead</h4>
<ul>
<li>Even after leaded petrol was banned (India by 2000; worldwide by 2021), lead comes from <b>smelting units</b> (and informal battery recycling) and <b>paints</b> (Lead Contents in Paints Rules 2016 cap it at 90 ppm). Pencil "lead" is graphite and clay; UPSC did not count hair oils and cosmetics (some traditional cosmetics such as sindoor and surma do contain lead, so it was debated).</li>
<li>Lead damages the brain (IQ loss in children), kidneys and blood. The WHO says there is <b>no known safe level</b> of lead exposure. A UNICEF–Pure Earth report (2020) estimated about 27.5 crore Indian children have blood lead above 5 µg/dL. Other sources: turmeric adulterated with lead chromate, some ayurvedic preparations, cookware, e-waste.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Mercury</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Artisanal and small-scale gold mining</b> is the largest human source (about 38%): mercury forms an amalgam with gold and is burnt off as vapour. <b>Coal-fired power plants</b> are the second-largest. Others: cement, non-ferrous smelting, chlor-alkali, incineration, cremation.</li>
<li>"No known safe level of exposure" is the WHO's wording for <b>lead</b>, not mercury; for mercury, tolerable weekly intakes are set (methylmercury 1.6 µg/kg body weight). So only two statements were correct (UPSC key).</li>
<li>Microbes turn mercury into methylmercury, which bioaccumulates and biomagnifies in big fish (tuna, swordfish, shark) and damages the nervous system and developing foetus. <b>Minamata disease</b> (Japan, 1950s, Chisso Corporation). <b>Minamata Convention</b> (2013, in force 2017; India ratified 2018) phases out mercury in thermometers, batteries, lamps and processes. India: Kodaikanal thermometer factory contamination (2001); dental amalgam being phased down.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Persistent and endocrine-disrupting chemicals</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Chemical</th><th>Facts</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>PFAS</b> ("forever chemicals")</td><td>Over 10,000 synthetic chemicals with strong carbon–fluorine bonds, used for water, grease and stain resistance (non-stick cookware, waterproof clothing, food wrappers, firefighting foams). They are <b>widespread in drinking water, food and packaging</b>, <b>not easily degraded</b>, and <b>bioaccumulate</b>. Linked to kidney and testicular cancer, liver and thyroid disease. PFOA is a Group 1 carcinogen (IARC, 2023). PFOS (2009) and PFOA (2019) are listed under the Stockholm Convention (India has not ratified those amendments); the USA set drinking-water limits (2024); India has no specific standard</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Brominated flame retardants</b> (PBDEs, HBCD)</td><td>Used in mattresses, upholstery, electronics. They are <b>highly resistant to degradation</b> and <b>accumulate in humans and animals</b> (breast milk, house dust); linked to thyroid, neurodevelopmental and reproductive harm. Several are listed under the Stockholm Convention</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bisphenol A (BPA)</b></td><td>A chemical used to make polycarbonate plastics and epoxy linings for <b>food packaging</b> (bottles, containers, cans). An endocrine disruptor that mimics oestrogen. Canada moved to ban it in baby bottles (2008); BIS banned it in infant feeding bottles (2015); the EU banned it in food-contact materials (December 2024)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phthalates</td><td>Plasticisers; endocrine disruptors</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Persistent Organic Pollutants</b>: persistent, bioaccumulative, travel long distances, toxic. <b>Stockholm Convention</b> (2001, in force 2004; India ratified 2006). The original "dirty dozen" included DDT, aldrin, dieldrin, PCBs, dioxins and furans. India still uses DDT for vector control under an exemption.</li>
<li>Related conventions: <b>Basel</b> (hazardous waste trade), <b>Rotterdam</b> (prior informed consent for hazardous chemicals trade), <b>Minamata</b> (mercury).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Plastics and microplastics</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Microbeads</b>: tiny plastic particles (under 1 mm, often polyethylene) in face scrubs, toothpastes and gels. They pass through sewage treatment and are <b>harmful to marine ecosystems</b>: eaten by plankton, fish and shellfish, they absorb toxins and move up the food chain. Banned in the USA (2015) and UK (2018); BIS classed them unsafe in cosmetics (2020).</li>
<li>Primary microplastics are made small (microbeads, nurdles); secondary ones come from breakdown (tyres, synthetic textiles, bottles); nanoplastics are below 1 µm. Microplastics have been found in human blood, placenta, breast milk and brain.</li>
<li><b>Chewing gum</b> is a pollution source <b>because</b> many gums use plastic gum bases (polyisobutylene, polyvinyl acetate) that do not biodegrade; discarded gum litters pavements and sheds microplastics. Traditional gum used natural chicle. Singapore has banned gum sales since 1992.</li>
<li>India banned 19 single-use plastic items in 2022. Global plastics treaty talks (UNEA 5/14, 2022) failed to agree at Busan (2024) and Geneva (August 2025). See 4.3 on plastic waste rules.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Agrochemicals</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Carbofuran, methyl parathion, phorate and triazophos</b> are highly toxic <b>pesticides</b> used in agriculture (organophosphates and a carbamate; many in WHO Class I). They caused farmer poisonings (Yavatmal, 2017), food residues and harm to birds and pollinators. Sikkim bans all chemical pesticides.</li>
<li>Regulation: Insecticides Act 1968 (Central Insecticides Board and Registration Committee); the Pesticide Management Bill 2020 is pending. The Anupam Verma Committee (2015) reviewed 66 pesticides; 18 were banned in 2018 (including methyl parathion; phorate from 2020); three more in 2023. Endosulfan was banned by the Supreme Court in 2011 (Kasaragod).</li>
<li>Excessive <b>nitrogen fertiliser</b>:
 <ul>
 <li><b>Increases soil acidity</b> (ammonium fertilisers release H+ during nitrification).</li>
 <li>Causes <b>nitrate leaching into groundwater</b> (blue baby syndrome).</li>
 <li>Does <b>not</b> make nitrogen-fixing microbes multiply; abundant nitrogen suppresses biological fixation.</li>
 <li>Also eutrophication and nitrous oxide emissions (see 1.2). India's NPK use is skewed to nitrogen because urea is cheap; remedies include neem-coated urea, nano urea, soil health cards, PM-PRANAM, biofertilisers.</li>
 </ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Harmful algal blooms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Causes in Indian seas: <b>nutrients discharged from estuaries</b> (sewage, fertiliser), <b>monsoon run-off</b> from land, and <b>upwelling</b> of nutrient-rich deep water. All three.</li>
<li>Blooms (red tides) produce toxins and create low-oxygen dead zones. <i>Noctiluca scintillans</i> blooms have spread in the Arabian Sea in winter, linked to warming; its bioluminescent "blue tides" appear on Goa, Karnataka and Kerala beaches.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fly ash</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fly ash from coal plants can be used to make <b>bricks</b> (with lime and gypsum) and as a <b>partial replacement for Portland cement</b> in concrete (it is a pozzolan, replacing 15–35%; Portland Pozzolana Cement).</li>
<li>It is <b>not</b> just silica and lime: it contains silica, alumina, iron oxide and calcium oxide plus <b>toxic heavy metals</b> (arsenic, lead, mercury, cadmium, chromium) and traces of radioactive elements.</li>
<li>India produces about 270–300 million tonnes a year. The Fly Ash Utilisation Notification (2021) sets 100% utilisation targets with a ₹1,000-per-tonne penalty; fly ash products must be used in construction within 300 km of plants. Uses: cement, bricks, roads and embankments, mine filling, land reclamation. Problems: ash-pond breaches (Singrauli), dust, water contamination.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("environment", "v4-3", `
<p>Managing waste means reducing it, reusing and recycling materials, recovering energy and cleaning up pollution already done. This topic covers India's waste rules, the circular economy, waste-to-energy and bioremediation.</p>

<h3>The circular economy</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>linear economy</b> runs take → make → dispose. A <b>circular economy</b> keeps materials in use: design out waste, keep products and materials circulating, regenerate nature (Ellen MacArthur Foundation's three principles).</li>
<li>It <b>reduces greenhouse gas emissions</b> because it <b>reduces raw material inputs</b> (less mining and processing: recycled aluminium needs about 95% less energy, recycled steel 60–75% less) and <b>reduces waste in production</b> (less embedded and landfill emissions). Both explain the first.</li>
<li>The "R" ladder: Refuse → Rethink → Reduce → Reuse → Repair → Refurbish → Remanufacture → Repurpose → Recycle → Recover. Industrial symbiosis: Kalundborg, Denmark.</li>
<li>India: circular-economy action plans for 11 sectors; EPR rules for plastic packaging, e-waste, batteries and tyres (2022); Vehicle Scrappage Policy (2021); Mission LiFE.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR)</h3>
<ul>
<li>EPR makes producers responsible for their products at end of life (take-back, collection, recycling). The idea came from Sweden (Thomas Lindhqvist, 1990).</li>
<li>In India it was first introduced as an important feature in the <b>e-Waste (Management and Handling) Rules 2011</b> (in force 2012).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Rules</th><th>EPR feature</th></tr>
<tr><td>E-Waste Rules 2016 and <b>2022</b> (in force April 2023)</td><td>Collection targets rising to 80%; tradable EPR certificates on a CPCB portal; 106 items including solar panels; minimum EPR price (2024)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plastic Waste Management Rules 2016 (EPR guidelines 2022)</td><td>Collection, recycling and recycled-content targets for brand owners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Battery Waste Management Rules 2022</td><td>All battery types</td></tr>
<tr><td>Waste tyres (2022), used oil (2023)</td><td>EPR regimes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solid Waste Management Rules 2016</td><td>Packaging and sanitary products</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Solid waste management rules</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Solid Waste Management Rules 2016</b> (replacing the Municipal Solid Wastes Rules 2000) provide <b>exact and elaborate criteria for identifying sites</b> for landfills and processing facilities. They also brought user fees and spot fines, EPR for packaging, on-site processing of wet waste by bulk generators, integration of waste pickers, and a rule that non-recyclable waste of 1,500 kcal/kg or more should go to waste-to-energy or refuse-derived fuel instead of landfills.</li>
<li>Under the 2016 Rules the generator segregated waste into <b>three</b> streams (biodegradable/wet, non-biodegradable/dry, domestic hazardous), not five. The Rules applied <b>beyond municipal areas</b> (census towns, urban agglomerations, notified industrial townships, SEZs, railways, airports, places of pilgrimage) and did not bar moving waste from one district to another.</li>
<li>The <b>Solid Waste Management Rules 2026</b> (notified January 2026, in force 1 April 2026) replace them: four-stream segregation at source (wet, dry, sanitary, special care), bulk generators must process wet waste on site or obtain responsibility certificates, polluter-pays compensation, landfill only for non-recyclable waste.</li>
<li>Other rules: Bio-medical Waste (2016), Construction and Demolition Waste (2016; new rules 2025), Battery Waste (2022). Swachh Bharat Mission (Urban) 2.0 aims at "Garbage Free Cities" and legacy dumpsite clean-up.</li>
</ul>

<h3>E-waste</h3>
<ul>
<li>Improper disposal of old computers releases <b>beryllium</b> (motherboards, connectors), <b>cadmium</b> (batteries, chip resistors, old CRTs), <b>hexavalent chromium</b> (anti-corrosion coatings), <b>mercury</b> (switches, fluorescent backlights) and <b>lead</b> (CRT glass, solder), plus brominated flame retardants and PVC. <b>Heptachlor</b> (a pesticide) and <b>plutonium</b> (nuclear material) are not in computers.</li>
<li>India is the third-largest e-waste generator after China and the USA (about 14 lakh tonnes in 2024–25 by CPCB's EPR data); much still goes to the informal sector (Seelampur, Moradabad). Global E-waste Monitor 2024: 62 million tonnes in 2022, only about 22% recycled.</li>
<li>The <b>R2 (Responsible Recycling) Code of Practices</b>, run by SERI (USA), promotes <b>environmentally responsible practices in the electronics recycling industry</b> (environment, health, safety, data security). e-Stewards is another standard.</li>
<li>The <b>Basel Convention</b> (1989) controls transboundary movement of hazardous waste (Ban Amendment 2019; plastic waste amendments).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Reusing industrial waste</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Waste</th><th>Uses</th></tr>
<tr><td>Fly ash</td><td>Bricks, cement, road embankments, mine filling (see 4.2)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Steel slag</b></td><td><b>Road base</b> (CSIR-CRRI technology; Mumbai–Goa highway, Surat 2022), <b>improving acidic agricultural soil</b> (lime-rich "basic slag"), <b>cement</b> (ground granulated blast-furnace slag in Portland Slag Cement)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Copper slag</td><td>Road construction, abrasives, partial sand replacement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Red mud (alumina)</td><td>Bricks, cement (research)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phosphogypsum</td><td>Cement, soil amendment</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>For <b>green rural roads</b>, prefer <b>copper slag</b> (waste reused as aggregate), <b>cold-mix asphalt</b> (mixed at ambient temperature, saving fuel) and <b>geotextiles</b> (jute and coir geotextiles strengthen soil and save aggregate). <b>Hot-mix asphalt</b> (heated to about 150°C) and <b>Portland cement</b> (CO2-intensive) are not the green choices.</li>
<li>PMGSY guidelines require a share of roads with new and green technologies (waste plastic, fly ash, cold mix, cell-filled concrete, geotextiles, slags). Plastic roads use R. Vasudevan's technique (IRC guidelines). PMGSY-IV (2024) covers 25,000 newly eligible habitations.</li>
<li>Ministry of Steel's Steel Scrap Recycling Policy (2019).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Waste-to-energy</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pyrolysis</b> and <b>plasma gasification</b> are <b>waste-to-energy technologies</b>. Pyrolysis heats waste (plastics, biomass, tyres) without oxygen, giving oil, syngas and char. Plasma gasification uses plasma torches at thousands of degrees to turn waste into syngas (H2 and CO) and inert glassy slag, even hazardous waste.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Route</th><th>Feature</th></tr>
<tr><td>Incineration (mass burn)</td><td>Needs high-calorie waste; Indian waste is wet and low-calorie (the Okhla plant has been criticised)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gasification</td><td>Partial oxidation to syngas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pyrolysis</td><td>No oxygen; oil, gas, char</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anaerobic digestion / biomethanation</td><td>Wet organic waste to biogas and slurry (GOBARdhan, SATAT)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Refuse-derived fuel</td><td>Dry combustible fraction burnt in cement kilns</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Early industrial examples (2000): Gowthami Solvents (Andhra) used <b>rice husk</b>; K.M. Sugar Mills (UP) used <b>distillery spent wash</b>; Satia Paper Mills (Punjab) used <b>black liquor</b> from pulping; Al Kabeer (Andhra) used <b>slaughterhouse waste</b>. Bagasse cogeneration in sugar mills continues the idea.</p>

<h3>Bioremediation</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bioremediation cleans up pollution by <b>enhancing natural biodegradation</b> using microbes, fungi or plants (adding nutrients, oxygen or microbes).</li>
<li><b>Genetic engineering</b> can create microbes designed for it. The first patented GM organism was Ananda Chakrabarty's oil-eating <i>Pseudomonas</i> (Diamond v. Chakrabarty, 1980).</li>
<li>Heavy metals such as <b>cadmium and lead cannot be degraded</b>; microbes can only change their form or concentrate them. So they are <b>not readily and completely treated</b> by bioremediation.</li>
<li>Types: in-situ (bioventing, biosparging), ex-situ (landfarming, biopiles, bioreactors), <b>phytoremediation</b> (plants: sunflowers for radioactive caesium after Chernobyl; Indian mustard for heavy metals), mycoremediation (fungi), bioaugmentation (adding microbes) and biostimulation (adding nutrients).</li>
<li><b>Oilzapper</b> (TERI with IOCL): an <b>eco-friendly bacterial consortium for remediation of oily sludge and oil spills</b>, breaking hydrocarbons into CO2 and water; Oilivorous-S handles high-sulphur sludge. Oil-spill response in India is led by the Coast Guard (National Oil Spill Disaster Contingency Plan, 1993). Recent spills off Kerala: MSC Elsa 3 (May 2025), Wan Hai 503 fire (June 2025).</li>
<li>Bioremediation is also used on legacy dumpsites and drains (Namami Gange).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_environment_v5.js
// Environment book, Unit 5: Climate change.
addBookTopic("environment", "v5-1", `
<p>Climate change is driven by greenhouse gases trapping heat. This topic covers the greenhouse effect and its gases, the separate problem of ozone depletion, and what warming does to people and ecosystems.</p>

<h3>The greenhouse effect</h3>
<ul>
<li>CO2 is largely transparent to incoming visible sunlight but <b>absorbs infrared</b> (long-wave) radiation. Most of that is heat re-radiated by the Earth's surface; CO2 traps and re-emits it, warming the lower atmosphere. (UPSC's option said "infrared part of the solar radiation".)</li>
<li>CO2 about 425 ppm (2025) against 280 ppm before industrialisation. 2024 was the first calendar year about 1.5°C above pre-industrial (WMO), though the long-term average is about 1.3°C.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Warming agent</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Water vapour</b></td><td>Most abundant natural greenhouse gas; amplifies warming as a feedback</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carbon dioxide</td><td>Main long-lived gas</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Methane</b></td><td>About 28 times CO2 over 100 years, about 80 times over 20 years; lifetime about 9–12 years, after which it <b>oxidises to CO2</b> (mainly via hydroxyl radicals). So methane is <b>more harmful than CO2</b> molecule for molecule</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nitrous oxide</td><td>About 265–273 times CO2; also depletes ozone</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sulphur hexafluoride</b></td><td>Used in electrical switchgear; the most potent GHG (about 23,500 times CO2) and lasts thousands of years</td></tr>
<tr><td>HFCs</td><td>HFC-134a about 1,300 times; HFC-23 about 14,800 times</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Soot (black carbon)</b></td><td>Not a gas: absorbs sunlight and darkens snow; a short-lived climate forcer, by some estimates second only to CO2</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Kyoto basket: CO2, CH4, N2O, HFCs, PFCs, SF6 (NF3 added in 2012).</p>

<h3>Sources of methane and nitrous oxide</h3>
<ul>
<li>Methane sources: <b>rice fields</b> (anaerobic paddy soils where methanogenic archaea live), <b>coal mining</b> (coal-bed methane), <b>domestic animals</b> (enteric fermentation and manure; the largest source in India) and <b>wetlands</b> (the largest natural source); also landfills and fossil-fuel leaks.</li>
<li><b>Rice</b> is the most important crop source of <b>both methane and nitrous oxide</b>: flooding gives methane, and nitrogen fertiliser plus alternate wet and dry phases give N2O through nitrification and denitrification. Paddy produces about 10–12% of human methane.</li>
<li>Agriculture is about 13–14% of India's GHG emissions. Mitigation: alternate wetting and drying, direct-seeded rice, SRI (see 7.1), neem-coated and nano urea, cattle feed additives (ICAR's Harit Dhara), biogas (GOBARdhan).</li>
<li><b>Global Methane Pledge</b> (COP26, 2021): cut methane 30% from 2020 levels by 2030. India has not joined, citing farmers' livelihoods.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Methane hydrates</h4>
<ul>
<li>Ice-like solids of methane trapped in water molecules, stable only at low temperature and high pressure. <b>Large deposits lie in Arctic tundra (permafrost) and under the seafloor</b> on continental margins.</li>
<li><b>Warming could release methane</b> from them, a positive feedback (the "clathrate gun" hypothesis). Atmospheric methane <b>oxidises to CO2 after a decade or two</b>. All three statements were correct.</li>
<li>India has large gas hydrate reserves in the Krishna–Godavari basin, Mahanadi and Andaman (National Gas Hydrate Programme; NGHP-02 found sand-rich deposits, 2015).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Industrial emissions: cement</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cement causes about 7–8% of global CO2 emissions (more than 5%). If it were a country, it would be the third-largest emitter.</li>
<li>Cement-making mixes <b>limestone with silica-bearing clay</b> (true, but not the cause of emissions) and heats it to about 1,450°C. <b>Limestone is converted into lime</b> in the kiln: <b>calcination</b> (CaCO3 → CaO + CO2), which causes about 60% of cement's CO2; fuel burning causes about 40%. So only the calcination statement explains the emissions.</li>
<li>Decarbonising: blended cements (Portland Pozzolana with fly ash; Portland Slag with blast-furnace slag), <b>LC3</b> (Limestone Calcined Clay Cement, about 50% clinker, about 30% less CO2; developed with IITs and EPFL), alternative fuels, waste-heat recovery, CCUS. India is the second-largest cement producer; cement is among the sectors with GHG-intensity targets under the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (see 5.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>India's emissions profile</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's per capita CO2 emissions are about <b>1.9–2.0 tonnes</b>, not under 0.5 t (world average about 4.7; USA about 14; EU about 6; China 8–9).</li>
<li>In absolute fuel-combustion emissions India is the world's third-largest and <b>second in Asia-Pacific</b> after China.</li>
<li><b>Electricity and heat producers</b> (coal-dominated) are the <b>largest source</b>, about half of energy CO2, followed by industry and transport.</li>
<li>India produces about 7% of current global emissions but only about 3–4% of cumulative historical emissions, the basis of its "common but differentiated responsibilities" argument.</li>
<li>India's limitations in mitigating warming (2010): <b>appropriate alternative technologies were not sufficiently available</b>, and India <b>could not invest huge funds in R&D</b> (about 0.65% of GDP). The claim that developed countries had shifted many polluting industries to India was not accepted. Today: the ₹1 lakh crore Research Development and Innovation scheme (2025) and the Anusandhan National Research Foundation.</li>
<li>For India's reports (BUR-4, NDCs) see 5.3.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ozone depletion</h3>
<ul>
<li>Stratospheric ozone absorbs harmful UV-B. Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) release chlorine under UV; one chlorine atom can destroy thousands of ozone molecules.</li>
<li>CFCs were used <b>to make plastic foams</b> (blowing agents), <b>to clean electronic components</b> (CFC-113 solvent) and <b>as aerosol propellants</b>, as well as refrigerants. They were <b>not</b> used to make tubeless tyres.</li>
<li>The <b>Antarctic ozone hole</b> (discovered 1985) forms because a strong <b>polar vortex</b> isolates very cold air, <b>polar stratospheric clouds</b> form below about −78°C, and on their ice surfaces chlorine from <b>CFCs</b> is converted into reactive forms; when sunlight returns in spring (September–October) it destroys ozone rapidly. Arctic holes are rarer because its vortex is weaker and warmer.</li>
<li>The 2025 Antarctic hole was one of the smallest since 1992 and closed early. The layer is projected to recover to 1980 levels by about 2066 over Antarctica, 2045 over the Arctic and 2040 elsewhere. World Ozone Day: 16 September.</li>
<li><b>Montreal Protocol</b> (1987): the most successful environmental treaty, universally ratified; phased out CFCs (developing countries by 2010), halons, carbon tetrachloride, methyl chloroform; HCFCs are being phased out (India by 2030). Note that the <b>Kyoto Protocol</b> deals with greenhouse gases, not ozone.</li>
</ul>
<h4>HFCs and the Kigali Amendment</h4>
<ul>
<li>HFCs replaced CFCs and HCFCs. They are used in refrigeration and air-conditioning, <b>aerosols</b> (inhalers, sprays), <b>foam-blowing agents</b> and <b>fire retardants/suppressants</b> (HFC-227ea), and as solvents, but <b>not lubricants</b>.</li>
<li>HFCs do not harm ozone but are powerful greenhouse gases. The <b>Kigali Amendment</b> (2016) phases them down by 80–85% by the late 2040s, avoiding up to 0.4°C of warming by 2100. Developed countries started in 2019; China and most developing countries froze in 2024; <b>India</b> (with the Gulf states, Pakistan, Iran, Iraq) <b>freezes in 2028</b> and cuts 85% by 2047. India ratified Kigali in 2021.</li>
<li>Alternatives: HFOs, natural refrigerants (ammonia, CO2, propane, isobutane). <b>India Cooling Action Plan</b> (2019, the world's first): cut cooling demand 20–25% and refrigerant demand 25–30% by 2037–38.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Impacts of warming</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Carbon fertilisation</b>: <b>increased plant growth due to higher atmospheric CO2</b> (faster photosynthesis and better water-use efficiency), especially in C3 plants (wheat, rice, soybean, trees) more than C4 (maize, sugarcane, millets). Satellite data show "global greening", but gains are limited by nitrogen, water and heat, and crops grown at high CO2 have less protein, zinc and iron. CAM plants (pineapple, cacti) take in CO2 at night.</li>
<li>Beyond <b>3°C</b> of warming (IPCC AR4), the <b>terrestrial biosphere tends to become a net carbon source</b> (dieback, fires, permafrost thaw), and there is <b>widespread coral mortality</b>. "All wetlands disappearing" or "no cereals anywhere" are exaggerations. Current policies point to about 2.7–3°C by 2100 (IPCC AR6). Tipping points: Greenland and West Antarctic ice sheets, Amazon dieback, collapse of the Atlantic overturning circulation, permafrost, coral reefs (the first tipping point crossed, per the Global Tipping Points Report 2025).</li>
<li><b>Ocean acidification</b>: oceans absorb about a quarter of human CO2, forming carbonic acid; surface pH fell from about 8.2 to 8.1 (about 30% more acidic). Fewer carbonate ions harm <b>calcareous phytoplankton</b> (coccolithophores), <b>coral reefs</b> and <b>animals with planktonic larvae</b> (molluscs, sea urchins). UPSC did not accept effects on cloud formation. The Planetary Health Check 2025 declared it the seventh of nine planetary boundaries crossed (only ozone and aerosols remain safe). SDG 14.3 targets it.</li>
<li><b>Wet-bulb temperature</b>: the reading of a thermometer wrapped in a wet cloth; it combines heat and humidity. At about 35°C (e.g. 40°C air at 75% humidity) <b>sweat cannot evaporate</b>, so humans and animals cannot shed body heat; death can follow within hours (recent studies suggest a lower limit of about 31°C). The World Bank's 2022 report on India's cooling sector warned India could be among the first places to cross it routinely. UPSC's key also accepted the broader implication of more floods, cyclones and droughts in the peninsula. The report foresaw 160–200 million people facing lethal heat each year by 2030 and about 34 million jobs lost to heat stress.</li>
<li>Heat policy: Ahmedabad Heat Action Plan (2013, the first in South Asia); NDMA guidelines. IMD heat wave: plains ≥ 40°C (hills ≥ 30°C) with a departure of 4.5–6.4°C (severe ≥ 6.5°C), or actual ≥ 45°C. Heat waves are not a notified disaster under the DM Act.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("environment", "v5-2", `
<p>Mitigation means cutting emissions and removing CO2 already emitted. This topic covers carbon capture and removal, geoengineering, biofuels and clean energy measures.</p>

<h3>Carbon capture, storage and removal</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Carbon capture and storage (CCS)</b> captures CO2 at power plants and factories and stores it underground. <b>Carbon dioxide removal (CDR)</b> pulls CO2 already in the air (negative emissions); the IPCC says it is unavoidable for net zero.</li>
<li>Geological storage sites: <b>abandoned and uneconomic coal seams</b> (coal adsorbs CO2 and can release methane: enhanced coal-bed methane), <b>depleted oil and gas reservoirs</b> (proven traps; CO2 injection also boosts oil recovery) and <b>deep saline formations</b> (the largest capacity; Sleipner, Norway, since 1996). Basalt mineralisation (Iceland's Carbfix; India studies Deccan basalts).</li>
<li>Commonly discussed CDR methods: <b>spreading finely ground basalt</b> on farmland (enhanced rock weathering), <b>adding lime to raise ocean alkalinity</b>, and <b>pumping industrial CO2 underground</b> (into abandoned mines, as carbonated water to speed mineralisation). All three.</li>
<li><b>Direct Air Capture (DAC)</b> takes CO2 straight from ambient air. The CO2 can be <b>stored</b> (carbon sequestration; Climeworks' Orca and Mammoth plants in Iceland with Carbfix), used in <b>plastic production and food processing</b> (polymers, carbonated drinks, dry ice), or combined with green <b>hydrogen to make synthetic aviation fuel</b>. All three. It is energy-hungry and costly ($400–1,000 per tonne).</li>
<li>Other approaches: <b>BECCS</b> (bioenergy with CCS: negative emissions), biochar, afforestation, blue carbon.</li>
<li>Risks: leakage, induced earthquakes, ocean side-effects, cost, "moral hazard" (delaying emission cuts). Ocean iron fertilisation is restricted under the London Protocol.</li>
<li>India: NITI Aayog's CCUS policy report (2022), DST's CCUS programme, NTPC capture pilots.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Blue carbon and biochar</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Blue carbon</b> is <b>carbon captured by oceans and coastal ecosystems</b>, mainly mangroves, salt marshes and seagrass meadows, which store carbon several times faster per hectare than land forests, mostly in waterlogged sediments for centuries. Blue Carbon Initiative (Conservation International, IUCN, IOC-UNESCO). See 3.2.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Colour of carbon</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Green</td><td>Stored in plants and soils on land</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blue</td><td>Stored in ocean and coastal ecosystems</td></tr>
<tr><td>Black</td><td>Soot from incomplete combustion; a short-lived warming agent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brown</td><td>Light-absorbing organic aerosols from biomass burning</td></tr>
<tr><td>Teal</td><td>Carbon in non-tidal freshwater wetlands</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Biochar</b> is charcoal from pyrolysis of crop residues, wood or manure. It can be <b>part of the growing medium in vertical farming</b> (a peat substitute), <b>promotes nitrogen-fixing microbes</b> (its pores house them) and helps the medium <b>retain water longer</b>. All three. It locks carbon away for centuries, earns carbon credits and offers a use for paddy straw. Amazonian "terra preta" soils are charcoal-enriched.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Geoengineering</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Cirrus cloud thinning</b> and <b>injecting sulphate aerosols into the stratosphere</b> are proposals for <b>reducing global warming</b>.</li>
<li>Stratospheric aerosol injection mimics large volcanic eruptions (Pinatubo, 1991, cooled Earth by about 0.5°C) to reflect sunlight. Cirrus thinning seeds high cirrus clouds (which trap outgoing heat) so more heat escapes.</li>
<li><b>Solar radiation management</b>: aerosol injection, marine cloud brightening, space mirrors, white roofs, cirrus thinning. <b>Carbon dioxide removal</b>: afforestation, BECCS, DAC, ocean fertilisation, enhanced weathering, biochar, blue carbon.</li>
<li>Concerns: "termination shock" if stopped suddenly, effects on monsoons, governance. The CBD placed a de facto moratorium on climate geoengineering (2010). Cloud seeding (silver iodide) is for rain, not cooling.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Biofuels</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Generation</th><th>Feedstock</th></tr>
<tr><td>First (1G)</td><td>Food crops: sugarcane, maize, vegetable oil; biodiesel from non-edible oilseeds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second (2G)</td><td>Lignocellulosic waste: rice straw, bagasse (IOCL's Panipat 2G ethanol plant, 2022)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third (3G)</td><td>Algae</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fourth (4G)</td><td>Engineered organisms, carbon capture</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bio-CNG</td><td>Compressed biogas (SATAT, 2018)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>National Policy on Biofuels 2018</b> (amended 2022) lists ethanol feedstocks: sugarcane juice, <b>sugar beet</b>, sweet sorghum, starchy materials such as corn and <b>cassava</b>, <b>damaged food grains</b> (wheat, broken rice) and <b>rotten potatoes</b> unfit for eating; surplus grains with the National Biofuel Coordination Committee's approval. <b>Groundnut seeds</b> (edible oilseed) and <b>horse gram</b> (pulse) are not listed.</li>
<li>Ethanol blending: E20 (advanced from 2030 to 2025–26) was reached in 2025; E27/E30 and flex-fuel vehicles are next. Biodiesel target: 5% by 2030. PM JI-VAN Yojana (2019) supports 2G plants. The Global Biofuels Alliance was launched at the G20 in New Delhi (2023).</li>
<li><b>Maize</b> can be cultivated for ethanol (starch → sugar → fermentation); the USA makes most of its ethanol from corn. In India, <b>sugarcane</b> (molasses and juice) was long the main feedstock (as in Brazil); grain-based ethanol (maize, damaged and surplus rice) now exceeds it, and maize became the largest feedstock in 2024–25, turning India into a maize importer.</li>
<li><b>Algae biofuels</b>: algae can be grown <b>on land</b> too (open raceway ponds, closed photobioreactors, even on wasteland with saline or waste water), so "seas only" is wrong. Limitations for developing countries: setting up production <b>needs high expertise and technology</b>, and economic viability needs <b>large facilities that raise ecological and social concerns</b>. Advantages: high oil yield per hectare, no competition with food, absorbs CO2.</li>
<li><b><i>Pongamia pinnata</i></b> (karanja): besides jatropha, a good biodiesel option because it is hardy, drought-tolerant and nitrogen-fixing, growing across much of India including dry land (UPSC accepted "arid regions", though debated), and its seeds hold 30–40% oil, about half of it <b>oleic acid</b>. The 2003 Biodiesel Mission's jatropha plantations largely failed.</li>
<li><b>Sustainable Aviation Fuel</b> can be made from <b>agricultural residues, corn grain, wastewater treatment sludge and wood mill waste</b> (all four), as well as used cooking oil, animal fats, oilseeds, algae, municipal waste and CO2 plus green hydrogen (e-fuels). Pathways: HEFA (most common), alcohol-to-jet, Fischer–Tropsch, power-to-liquid. It can be blended up to 50% ("drop-in"). ICAO's CORSIA becomes mandatory from 2027; EU ReFuelEU: 2% SAF from 2025, 70% by 2050. India: 1% in international flights by 2027, 2% by 2028; first SAF-blended commercial flight (AirAsia India, 2023); IOCL's Panipat SAF plant certified (2025).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Clean energy measures</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Net metering</b> promotes <b>production and use of solar energy by households</b>: a bidirectional meter records electricity drawn from and fed into the grid, and the consumer pays only for net use. Compare gross metering (all output sold at a feed-in tariff) and net billing (different prices for import and export). Electricity (Rights of Consumers) Rules allow net metering up to 500 kW. PM Surya Ghar Muft Bijli Yojana (2024): rooftop solar for 1 crore homes, subsidy up to ₹78,000, 300 free units a month.</li>
<li><b>BEE star labels</b> (Bureau of Energy Efficiency, Energy Conservation Act 2001; Standards and Labelling programme, 2006) appear on <b>ceiling fans, electric geysers and tubular fluorescent lamps</b>, among others. Mandatory for refrigerators, ACs, TFLs (first, 2010), distribution transformers, TVs, storage water heaters, LED lamps and ceiling fans (2023); voluntary for washing machines, pumps and laptops. Other BEE schemes: PAT for industries, the Energy Conservation Building Code, UJALA LED bulbs (2015, EESL).</li>
<li><b>International Solar Alliance</b>: <b>launched at COP21 in Paris</b> (30 November 2015) by PM Modi and President Hollande. It does <b>not</b> include all UN members: it began for about 121 solar-rich countries between the Tropics, and a 2020 amendment opened it to all UN members, but only those that sign and ratify join (about 100+ members). HQ Gurugram: the first treaty-based international body headquartered in India (framework agreement in force December 2017). Goal: $1 trillion of solar investment by 2030. Initiatives: One Sun One World One Grid, Global Solar Facility. UN General Assembly observer (2021).</li>
<li>Other India-led bodies: CDRI (2019), Global Biofuels Alliance (2023), International Big Cat Alliance.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("environment", "v5-3", `
<p>Climate change is negotiated under the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change. This topic follows the treaties from Rio to Paris, the carbon markets they created, forest-carbon schemes, and India's own commitments.</p>

<h3>The UNFCCC and the Kyoto Protocol</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>UNFCCC</b> was opened for signature at the Earth Summit in <b>Rio de Janeiro, 1992</b> (in force 1994; 198 parties). Rio also produced the CBD, the UNCCD (opened 1994), Agenda 21 and the Rio Declaration with "common but differentiated responsibilities" (see 6.1).</li>
<li><b>Annex I</b> countries (industrialised and transition economies) is a UNFCCC term, not from the Cartagena Protocol.</li>
<li>The <b>Kyoto Protocol</b> (1997) came into force on <b>16 February 2005</b> after Russia ratified. It deals with <b>greenhouse gases</b>, not ozone depletion. Binding targets for developed (Annex B) countries: about 5% below 1990 levels in 2008–12; the Doha Amendment covered 2013–20.</li>
<li>The <b>concept of carbon credits originated from the Kyoto Protocol</b>, which created three flexibility mechanisms:
 <ul>
 <li><b>Clean Development Mechanism</b> (Article 12): a market device under which developed countries fund emission-cutting projects in <b>developing (non-Annex I)</b> countries and earn <b>Certified Emission Reductions</b> (1 CER = 1 tonne CO2e). CDM projects were <b>not limited to Annex I</b> countries. India and China were among the largest hosts.</li>
 <li><b>Joint Implementation</b>: projects in other Annex I countries (Emission Reduction Units).</li>
 <li><b>International Emissions Trading</b>.</li>
 </ul></li>
<li>CERs come from Kyoto's CDM, not the Nagoya Protocol (which concerns access and benefit sharing of genetic resources).</li>
<li>Carbon credit prices are <b>set by markets</b>, not fixed by UNEP; the CDM was run under the UNFCCC.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Carbon pricing</h3>
<ul>
<li>A carbon <b>tax</b> fixes the price; <b>cap-and-trade</b> fixes the quantity. <b>Finland</b> introduced the world's first carbon tax (1990), followed by Sweden, Norway and Denmark. <b>New Zealand's</b> 2005 proposal for a general carbon tax (UPSC's key) was dropped for an emissions trading scheme (2008).</li>
<li>EU ETS (2005) is the largest market; China's national ETS began in 2021. The EU's <b>CBAM</b> (carbon border tax) starts charging in 2026, affecting Indian steel and aluminium.</li>
<li>Voluntary market integrity: Integrity Council's Core Carbon Principles (2023).</li>
<li><b>Social cost of carbon</b>: the money value of the <b>long-term damage done by one tonne of CO2 emitted in a given year</b> (crop losses, health, flood damage, lost ecosystem services). US EPA (2023): about $190 per tonne; it depends heavily on the discount rate. A 2018 study put India's country-level SCC as the world's highest (about $86/t). Related: Pigouvian tax. India's coal cess (Clean Environment Cess, ₹400/t) was subsumed into the GST compensation cess in 2017.</li>
<li><b>India's Carbon Credit Trading Scheme</b> (2023, under the Energy Conservation (Amendment) Act 2022): a compliance mechanism with emission-intensity targets (about 490 units in seven sectors notified in October 2025 and January 2026) and an offset mechanism. BEE administers it; Grid-India runs the registry; trading on power exchanges under CERC. First compliance credits were planned for October 2026.</li>
<li>National climate laws: Canada's House of Commons passed the <b>Climate Change Accountability Bill</b> (C-377) in June 2008 (UPSC's answer for the "first Climate Act"); the UK's Climate Change Act (November 2008) is usually called the first legally binding national climate law. Canada's Net-Zero Emissions Accountability Act followed in 2021. The ICJ's advisory opinion (July 2025) said states have legal obligations to prevent significant climate harm.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Paris Agreement</h3>
<ul>
<li>Adopted at COP21 (December 2015), opened for signature in April 2016, and in force on <b>4 November 2016</b> (after 55 parties with 55% of emissions ratified). Not every UN member signed at once, and it did not take effect in 2017.</li>
<li>Article 2 aims to keep warming <b>well below 2°C</b> and pursue <b>1.5°C</b>.</li>
<li>Finance: developed countries were to mobilise <b>$100 billion a year</b> by 2020 (from Copenhagen 2009), not $1,000 billion; the Agreement contains no admission of historical responsibility. COP29 (Baku, 2024) agreed the New Collective Quantified Goal of $300 billion a year by 2035.</li>
<li>Structure: bottom-up NDCs every five years (Article 4); forests and REDD+ (Article 5); carbon markets (Article 6); adaptation (Article 7); loss and damage (Article 8); finance (Article 9); Enhanced Transparency Framework (Article 13); Global Stocktake every five years (Article 14; first at COP28, 2023).</li>
<li><b>INDCs</b> (Intended Nationally Determined Contributions) were countries' own climate action plans submitted before Paris; on ratification they became NDCs.</li>
<li>The USA withdrew (2020), rejoined (2021) and withdrew again (effective 27 January 2026); a January 2026 memorandum also ordered withdrawal from the UNFCCC and IPCC.</li>
<li>COP30 was held at Belém, Brazil (November 2025): the "Global Mutirão" decision and a call to triple adaptation finance by 2035. COP31 (November 2026) is in Antalya, Türkiye.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Article 6: carbon markets</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Provision</th><th>Content</th></tr>
<tr><td>6.1</td><td>Voluntary cooperation to implement NDCs with higher ambition</td></tr>
<tr><td>6.2</td><td>Cooperative approaches: bilateral transfers of ITMOs (internationally transferred mitigation outcomes) with corresponding adjustments to avoid double counting (Switzerland–Ghana; Japan's Joint Crediting Mechanism, which India joined in 2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td>6.4</td><td>Paris Agreement Crediting Mechanism, the <b>successor to the CDM</b>; a share of proceeds goes to the Adaptation Fund</td></tr>
<tr><td>6.8–6.9</td><td><b>Non-market approaches</b>: finance, technology, capacity building without trading credits</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Article 6 is much discussed because it <b>sets out the principles of carbon markets</b> and also <b>promotes non-market cooperation</b>; both explain it. Its rulebook was agreed at COP26 (Glasgow) and operationalised at COP29 (Baku).</p>

<h3>India's commitments</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Pledge</th><th>Emissions intensity of GDP (vs 2005)</th><th>Non-fossil power capacity</th><th>Forest sink</th></tr>
<tr><td>INDC (2015)</td><td>33–35% cut by 2030</td><td>40% by 2030</td><td>Extra 2.5–3 GtCO2e</td></tr>
<tr><td>Updated NDC (2022)</td><td>45% by 2030</td><td>50% by 2030 (reached in 2025)</td><td>Same</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>NDC 3.0</b> (2031–35; Cabinet March 2026, submitted 24 April 2026)</td><td><b>47% by 2035</b></td><td><b>60% by 2035</b> (about 52.6% in February 2026)</td><td>3.5–4 GtCO2e by 2035</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Panchamrit</b> (COP26, Glasgow 2021): 500 GW non-fossil capacity by 2030; 50% of energy needs from renewables by 2030; cut 1 billion tonnes of projected emissions by 2030; cut carbon intensity 45% by 2030; <b>net zero by 2070</b>.</li>
<li><b>Long-Term Low Emission Development Strategy</b> (COP27, November 2022) maps the path to net zero by 2070 through seven transitions (low-carbon power, transport, urban adaptation and green buildings, industry, CO2 removal and CCUS, forest sinks, finance), based on equity, CBDR-RC and Mission LiFE.</li>
<li><b>Fourth Biennial Update Report</b> (30 December 2024): total GHG emissions 2,959 MtCO2e in 2020 (about 2,437 Mt net with land use), a <b>7.93% fall from 2019</b> (largely due to COVID). Energy 75.66%, agriculture 13.72%, industrial processes 8.06%, waste 2.56%. Emissions intensity of GDP fell 36% between 2005 and 2020. From 2024, Biennial Transparency Reports replace BURs; India submitted its first in 2025.</li>
<li>In the 2026 "relationship" question: the LT-LEDS is a key tool for net zero by 2070, and BUR-4's fall in emissions gives it some empirical support; a claim that resilience "necessarily" needs quick short-term cuts contradicts the LT-LEDS's long-term, development-first approach.</li>
<li><b>National Action Plan on Climate Change</b> (2008): eight missions (see 3.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Forest carbon and REDD+</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>REDD+</b>: Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and forest Degradation, plus conservation, sustainable management of forests and enhancement of forest carbon stocks. REDD (COP11, 2005) became REDD+ (Bali Action Plan, COP13, 2007); Warsaw Framework (COP19, 2013); Article 5 of the Paris Agreement.</li>
<li>The <b>UN-REDD Programme</b> (2008; FAO, UNDP, UNEP), well designed, contributes to <b>biodiversity protection, resilience of forest ecosystems and poverty reduction</b>. All three.</li>
<li>India's <b>first Plan Vivo-certified REDD+ project</b> is the <b>Khasi Hills Community REDD+ Project</b> (Umiam sub-watershed, East Khasi Hills, Meghalaya), run by a federation of 10 traditional Khasi <i>hima</i> governments with Community Forestry International (validated 2012–13). It sells credits from protecting community forests and sacred groves such as Mawphlang. Plan Vivo (UK) certifies community-led projects; others are Verra and Gold Standard. Over 90% of Meghalaya's forests are community or privately owned.</li>
<li>India's National REDD+ Strategy (2018).</li>
<li>The World Bank's <b>Forest Carbon Partnership Facility</b> (2008, launched at Bali COP13) is a <b>global partnership of governments, businesses, civil society and indigenous peoples</b> that <b>helps countries with REDD+</b> through a Readiness Fund and a Carbon Fund. It does <b>not</b> fund universities or individual scientists. About 47 developing countries take part; India is not a REDD participant country.</li>
<li>The <b>BioCarbon Fund Initiative for Sustainable Forest Landscapes</b> (2013) is <b>managed by the World Bank</b> with donors such as Norway, the UK, the USA, Germany and Switzerland; it promotes lower land-sector emissions in places like Colombia, Ethiopia, Indonesia, Mexico and Zambia. The original BioCarbon Fund (2004) was the first carbon fund for forestry; the Prototype Carbon Fund dates from 1999.</li>
<li>Other forest finance: LEAF Coalition, Tropical Forest Forever Facility (COP30), Green Climate Fund REDD+ pilot. India's own: Green Credit Programme (2023), Nagar Van Yojana, "Ek Ped Maa Ke Naam" (2024).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_environment_v6.js
// Environment book, Units 6 and 7: Global environmental governance; sustainable resource use.
addBookTopic("environment", "v6-1", `
<p>International environmental law grew out of a series of UN summits starting at Stockholm in 1972. This topic covers the summits, the conventions they produced, the bodies behind them and the Sustainable Development Goals.</p>

<h3>The major summits</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>Summit</th><th>Outcomes</th></tr>
<tr><td>1972</td><td>Stockholm: UN Conference on the Human Environment</td><td>UNEP created (Nairobi); 5 June became World Environment Day</td></tr>
<tr><td>1987</td><td>Brundtland Report, <i>Our Common Future</i></td><td>Defined sustainable development (see 7.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1992</td><td><b>Earth Summit (UNCED), Rio de Janeiro</b></td><td><b>Agenda 21</b>; Rio Declaration (27 principles: precautionary principle, polluter pays, common but differentiated responsibilities); Forest Principles; UNFCCC and CBD opened for signature; UNCCD negotiations launched; Commission on Sustainable Development (1993)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2002</td><td><b>World Summit on Sustainable Development (Rio+10), Johannesburg</b></td><td>Johannesburg Plan of Implementation; "Type II" public–private partnerships; GIAHS launched</td></tr>
<tr><td>2012</td><td><b>Rio+20: UN Conference on Sustainable Development</b>, Rio</td><td>"The Future We Want": launched the SDG process, the High-Level Political Forum and green economy work (PAGE)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2015</td><td>UN Summit, New York</td><td>Agenda 2030 and the 17 SDGs</td></tr>
<tr><td>2022</td><td>Stockholm+50</td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Agenda 21</b> is a non-binding global action plan for sustainable development in the 21st century (with "Local Agenda 21" for local governments). It came from <b>Rio 1992</b>, not Johannesburg 2002.</li>
<li>The Johannesburg summit (August–September 2002) was on <b>sustainable development</b>. Johannesburg also hosted the first G20 summit in Africa (November 2025).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Sustainable Development Goals</h3>
<ul>
<li>17 goals with 169 targets, adopted by the UN General Assembly in September 2015 as <b>Agenda 2030</b>, to be <b>achieved by 2030</b>. They succeeded the eight Millennium Development Goals (2000–15) and apply to all countries.</li>
<li>The SDGs were first proposed by <b>Colombia and Guatemala</b> before Rio+20, not by the Club of Rome in 1972 (whose 1972 report was <i>The Limits to Growth</i>).</li>
<li>Reviewed at the High-Level Political Forum through Voluntary National Reviews (India: 2017, 2020). NITI Aayog publishes the SDG India Index (since 2018; Kerala tops); MoSPI runs the National Indicator Framework. The UN's 2025 report found only about a sixth of targets on track.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Biodiversity-related conventions</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Convention</th><th>Year</th><th>Subject</th></tr>
<tr><td>Ramsar</td><td>1971</td><td>Wetlands (see 3.2)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>World Heritage Convention</b> (UNESCO)</td><td>1972</td><td>Cultural and natural sites, including biodiversity-rich ones (Western Ghats, Kaziranga, Sundarbans)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>CITES</b></td><td>1973 (Washington; in force 1975)</td><td>Trade in endangered species</td></tr>
<tr><td>CMS (Bonn Convention)</td><td>1979</td><td>Migratory species; India hosted COP13 (Gandhinagar, 2020)</td></tr>
<tr><td>CBD</td><td>1992</td><td>Biodiversity (see 2.1, 3.3)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>ITPGRFA</b> (FAO)</td><td>2001 (in force 2004)</td><td>Plant genetic resources for food and agriculture: a Multilateral System of access and benefit sharing for 64 crops; farmers' rights</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Plant Protection Convention</td><td>1951</td><td>Plant pests</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The ITPGRFA, the <b>UNCCD</b> and the World Heritage Convention all <b>have a bearing on biodiversity</b>. A Liaison Group coordinates the biodiversity conventions.</li>
</ul>
<h4>IUCN and CITES</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>IUCN</b> (1948, Gland, Switzerland) is <b>not a UN organ</b>: it is a membership union of governments and NGOs (with UN General Assembly observer status). It <b>runs thousands of field projects</b>. World Conservation Congress: Abu Dhabi, October 2025.</li>
<li>Its <b>Red List</b> (Red Data Book, since 1964) lists <b>threatened species</b> worldwide by extinction risk, not endemics of hotspots or protected sites (the World Database on Protected Areas covers those). Categories: Extinct, Extinct in the Wild, Critically Endangered, Endangered, Vulnerable, Near Threatened, Least Concern, Data Deficient, Not Evaluated. Over 170,000 species assessed, over 48,000 threatened. The IUCN Green Status assesses recovery.</li>
<li><b>CITES</b> is an agreement between governments, <b>legally binding</b> on its parties, but it <b>does not replace national laws</b>; each party legislates (India: the WPA, with a CITES schedule since 2022). Secretariat Geneva (administered by UNEP); 185 parties; India joined 1976. CoP20: Samarkand, Uzbekistan (2025).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>CITES appendix</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>I</td><td>Threatened with extinction; commercial trade banned (tiger, snow leopard, pangolins)</td></tr>
<tr><td>II</td><td>Not now threatened, but trade must be controlled (red sanders, many orchids, rosewoods)</td></tr>
<tr><td>III</td><td>Listed by one country seeking help to control trade</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>UNCCD</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>UN Convention to Combat Desertification</b> (adopted Paris 1994, in force 1996; secretariat Bonn) is one of the three Rio Conventions.</li>
<li>It works through <b>national action programmes and international partnerships</b> and is committed to a <b>bottom-up approach</b> with local participation.</li>
<li>It does <b>not</b> focus specially on South Asia and North Africa, and its secretariat does not allocate most funds to them: its special regional annex is for <b>Africa</b> (plus annexes for Asia, Latin America, the Northern Mediterranean and Central and Eastern Europe); the Global Mechanism and GEF mobilise funds.</li>
<li>Land Degradation Neutrality (SDG 15.3) by 2030. India hosted COP14 (Greater Noida, 2019) and pledged to restore <b>26 million hectares</b> of degraded land by 2030. COP16: Riyadh (2024). ISRO's atlas: about 97.8 Mha (29.7% of India) degraded (2018–19). Day to Combat Desertification: 17 June.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ozone treaties</h3>
<p>The <b>Montreal Protocol</b> (1987, in force 1989), under the Vienna Convention (1985), controls and phases out ozone-depleting substances; it was the first UN treaty ratified by every country (198 parties). Kigali Amendment (2016) on HFCs. See 5.1.</p>

<h3>Environment days</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Day</th><th>Date</th></tr>
<tr><td>World Wildlife Day</td><td>3 March</td></tr>
<tr><td>World Forestry Day / International Day of Forests</td><td>21 March</td></tr>
<tr><td>World Water Day</td><td>22 March</td></tr>
<tr><td>Earth Day</td><td>22 April</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Day for Biological Diversity</td><td>22 May</td></tr>
<tr><td>World Environment Day</td><td>5 June (India hosted in 2018: "Beat Plastic Pollution"; 2025 host South Korea, on plastic pollution)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Day to Combat Desertification</td><td>17 June</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mangrove Day</td><td>26 July</td></tr>
<tr><td>World Ozone Day</td><td>16 September</td></tr>
<tr><td>World Habitat Day</td><td>First Monday of October</td></tr>
<tr><td>World Wetlands Day</td><td>2 February</td></tr>
</table>
<p>10 December is Human Rights Day.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("environment", "v6-2", `
<p>Beyond treaties, environmental action runs through funds, partnerships, NGOs and campaigns. UPSC asks who runs each one and what it does.</p>

<h3>Climate and environment funds</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Fund</th><th>Facts</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Global Environment Facility (GEF)</b></td><td>1991 (before Rio); HQ Washington DC; World Bank is trustee. Serves as the <b>financial mechanism for the CBD and UNFCCC</b>, and also the UNCCD, Stockholm and Minamata conventions; supports the Montreal Protocol in transition economies. Four-year cycles (GEF-8: 2022–26; GEF-9: July 2026–June 2030). Global Biodiversity Framework Fund (2023) sits under it. India is both donor and recipient; Small Grants Programme run by UNDP</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Green Climate Fund (GCF)</b></td><td>Set up under the <b>UNFCCC</b> at COP16, Cancún (2010), to <b>help developing countries with adaptation and mitigation</b> (aiming at a 50:50 balance). It was <b>not</b> founded by UNEP, OECD, ADB and the World Bank (the World Bank was only interim trustee). HQ Songdo, South Korea; the largest multilateral climate fund. NABARD and SIDBI are accredited Indian entities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adaptation Fund</td><td>Under Kyoto; financed by a 2% share of CDM proceeds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loss and Damage Fund</td><td>Agreed at COP27, operational from COP28; hosted by the World Bank; board in the Philippines</td></tr>
<tr><td>BioCarbon Fund, Forest Carbon Partnership Facility</td><td>World Bank forest-carbon funds (see 5.3)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Partnerships and alliances</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Initiative</th><th>Who and what</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Global Climate Change Alliance</b> (2007; GCCA+ since 2014)</td><td>An <b>EU initiative</b> giving <b>technical and financial support to vulnerable developing countries</b> (LDCs, small islands) to integrate climate change into their policies and budgets. It is run by the European Commission, <b>not</b> WRI and WBCSD</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Climate and Clean Air Coalition</b> (February 2012)</td><td>Launched by <b>UNEP and six countries</b> (Bangladesh, Canada, Ghana, Mexico, Sweden, USA), <b>not the G20</b>; secretariat hosted by UNEP in Paris; India joined in 2019. Focuses on short-lived climate pollutants: <b>methane, black carbon, HFCs</b> and tropospheric ozone. Cutting them could avoid about 0.6°C of warming by 2050</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Global Alliance for Climate-Smart Agriculture</b></td><td>Launched at the <b>UN Climate Summit in New York, 2014</b> (not Paris 2015); voluntary, <b>no binding obligations</b>; FAO hosts its facilitation unit. <b>India was not instrumental</b> and is not a member</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Partnership for Action on Green Economy (PAGE)</b></td><td>Emerged from <b>Rio+20</b> (2012), launched 2013: UNEP, ILO, UNDP, UNIDO and UNITAR help countries move to green, inclusive economies. India joined in 2018</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Climate Neutral Now</b> (2015)</td><td>A <b>UNFCCC Secretariat</b> initiative inviting individuals, companies and governments to measure, reduce and offset emissions; part of the UNFCCC's "Momentum for Change" (2011)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>New York Declaration on Forests</b></td><td>First endorsed at the <b>UN Climate Summit, New York, 2014</b>; aims to halve natural forest loss by 2020 and <b>end it by 2030</b>, and restore 350 Mha. <b>Non-binding</b>; endorsed by <b>governments, companies and indigenous groups</b>. <b>India did not sign</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>The Climate Group</b> (2004, London)</td><td>An international non-profit that <b>builds and runs large networks</b> for climate action. It is the secretariat of the <b>Under2 Coalition</b> of sub-national governments (not the IEA). Its initiatives: <b>EP100</b> (launched 2016 with the Alliance to Save Energy, not the IEA), which brings together companies <b>committed to energy-efficiency innovation and emission cuts</b>, with <b>Indian members</b> (Mahindra, Tech Mahindra, Godrej, Dalmia Cement, JSW Steel); RE100 (100% renewable power); EV100</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Climate Action Tracker</b></td><td>A <b>database by a coalition of research organisations</b> (Climate Analytics and NewClimate Institute) tracking pledges against 1.5°C since 2009; it rated India's 2035 NDC "highly insufficient"</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Forest pledges: Bonn Challenge (2011; restore 150 Mha by 2020, 350 Mha by 2030; India pledged 26 Mha); Glasgow Leaders' Declaration on Forests (COP26; India did not sign); UN Decade on Ecosystem Restoration (2021–30); Tropical Forest Forever Facility (COP30).</li>
<li>India-led bodies: International Solar Alliance (with France, 2015), Coalition for Disaster Resilient Infrastructure (2019), LeadIT (with Sweden, 2019), Global Biofuels Alliance (2023), Mission LiFE (2022). Other coalitions: Mission Innovation, Powering Past Coal Alliance.</li>
<li>The IEA (Paris, 1974) publishes the World Energy Outlook; India is an association country seeking full membership.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Climate report or index</th><th>Publisher</th></tr>
<tr><td>Emissions Gap Report, Adaptation Gap Report</td><td>UNEP</td></tr>
<tr><td>Climate Change Performance Index</td><td>Germanwatch, NewClimate Institute, CAN (India fell to 23rd in CCPI 2026, mainly over coal)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Global Climate Risk Index</td><td>Germanwatch</td></tr>
<tr><td>State of the Global Climate; Greenhouse Gas Bulletin</td><td>WMO</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assessment Reports (AR6, 2021–23)</td><td>IPCC (WMO and UNEP, 1988; Nobel Peace Prize 2007)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Environmental Performance Index</td><td>Yale and Columbia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Global Carbon Budget</td><td>Global Carbon Project</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Accounting and valuation tools</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Greenhouse Gas Protocol</b> (1998, WRI and WBCSD) is an <b>international accounting tool</b> for governments and businesses to measure and manage emissions. Scope 1: direct emissions from owned sources; Scope 2: purchased electricity, heat and steam; Scope 3: the rest of the value chain. India GHG Program (WRI India, CII, TERI); SEBI's BRSR Core requires listed companies to disclose emissions.</li>
<li><b>The Economics of Ecosystems and Biodiversity (TEEB)</b>, hosted by <b>UNEP</b> alone (not the IMF or WEF), began at a 2007 G8+5 meeting in Potsdam, led by Pavan Sukhdev. It <b>draws attention to the economic benefits of biodiversity</b> and helps decision-makers <b>recognise, demonstrate and capture the value of ecosystems</b>. TEEB India Initiative (2011). Related: UN SEEA accounting; India's EnviStats; IPBES (2012) as the IPCC-like body for biodiversity.</li>
<li>The UNEP-supported <b>Common Carbon Metric</b> (UNEP's Sustainable Buildings and Climate Initiative, 2009–10) assesses the <b>carbon footprint of building operations</b> worldwide (kgCO2e per m² per year). Buildings use about a third of global energy. India: Energy Conservation Building Code (2007, 2017), ECSBC 2024, Eco Niwas Samhita; rating systems GRIHA (TERI and MNRE), IGBC, LEED, EDGE.</li>
<li><b>Greenwashing</b> is making misleading claims that a company, product or investment is environmentally friendly (vague labels, selective disclosure); coined by Jay Westerveld (1986). India's CCPA issued guidelines against greenwashing (October 2024) under the Consumer Protection Act 2019; SEBI's ESG fund and green-debt rules also guard against it. Related: greenhushing, bluewashing.</li>
<li><b>Greendex</b> (National Geographic and GlobeScan, 2008–14) measured <b>environmentally sustainable consumer behaviour</b> (housing, transport, food, goods); India topped most editions.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Agriculture partnerships</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Climate-Smart Villages</b> in India were part of a project of <b>CCAFS</b> (Climate Change, Agriculture and Food Security), a research programme of <b>CGIAR</b>, whose System Organization is in <b>Montpellier, France</b>. <b>ICRISAT</b> (Patancheru, Hyderabad, 1972; sorghum, millets, chickpea, pigeonpea, groundnut) is a CGIAR centre. All three statements were correct.</li>
<li>Other CGIAR centres: IRRI (Philippines; South Asia centre at Varanasi), CIMMYT (Mexico), IFPRI (Washington), IWMI (Colombo), CIP (Lima; South Asia centre at Agra).</li>
<li>Climate-smart agriculture (FAO, 2010) has three pillars: raise productivity and incomes, adapt and build resilience, reduce emissions. India: ICAR's NICRA (2011), NMSA, PMFBY, soil health cards, micro-irrigation.</li>
<li>FAO's <b>Globally Important Agricultural Heritage Systems</b> (launched 2002 at Johannesburg) aims to <b>identify and safeguard traditional, eco-friendly farming systems</b> with their landscapes, agro-biodiversity and knowledge, not to modernise them or grant GI tags (which come under national law). India's sites: Pampore saffron (Kashmir, 2011), Koraput (Odisha, 2012), Kuttanad below-sea-level farming (Kerala, 2013). About 90 sites in 28 countries.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Conservation NGOs and campaigns</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>TRAFFIC</b> (1976, Cambridge, UK) is a <b>joint programme of WWF and IUCN</b>, not a UNEP bureau. Its mission is to <b>ensure that trade in wild plants and animals is not a threat to nature</b>; it supports CITES enforcement. India works with the Wildlife Crime Control Bureau (Operation Save Kurma, Operation Thunderbird). Most trafficked: pangolins, tortoises, red sanders, tiger parts, rhino horn, ivory, shark fins.</li>
<li><b>BirdLife International</b> (1922 as the International Council for Bird Preservation; Cambridge, UK) is a <b>global partnership of conservation organisations</b> in over 100 countries that identifies <b>Important Bird and Biodiversity Areas</b> (India has over 550). It is the IUCN Red List authority for birds. The <b>hotspot concept came from Norman Myers</b>, not BirdLife.</li>
<li><b>Bombay Natural History Society</b> (1883, Mumbai) is an <b>NGO</b>, not an autonomous body under the Environment Ministry. It conserves nature through <b>action-based research, education and awareness</b> and runs <b>nature trails and camps</b>. Associated with Salim Ali; BirdLife's Indian partner; runs vulture breeding centres; publishes the Journal of the BNHS (since 1886) and <i>Hornbill</i>.</li>
<li><b>Wetlands International</b> (HQ Ede, Netherlands) is a <b>non-profit NGO</b>, not an intergovernmental body of Ramsar parties. It <b>works at field level to develop knowledge and advocate better policies</b>, and is one of Ramsar's five International Organisation Partners (with BirdLife, IUCN, WWF, IWMI). Runs the Asian Waterbird Census in India with BNHS.</li>
<li><b>Earth Hour</b> is a <b>WWF</b> initiative (Sydney, 2007), not UNEP or UNESCO: people <b>switch off lights for one hour</b> (8:30–9:30 pm, usually the last Saturday of March) to <b>raise awareness about climate change</b>. WWF (1961, Gland) also publishes the Living Planet Report.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("environment", "v7-1", `
<p>Sustainable development means living within the planet's limits. This last topic brings together sustainable farming, water, land and the people and movements that showed how.</p>

<h3>Sustainable development</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Brundtland Report</b>, <i>Our Common Future</i> (1987), defined sustainable development as development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.</li>
<li>The idea is inherently tied to <b>carrying capacity</b>: the maximum population or resource use the environment can sustain indefinitely.</li>
<li>Related ideas: ecological footprint, planetary boundaries (seven of nine crossed by 2025), Earth Overshoot Day, Mission LiFE (Lifestyle for Environment, 2022). See 6.1 for the SDGs.</li>
<li><b>Wangari Maathai</b> (Kenya, 1940–2011) founded the <b>Green Belt Movement</b> (1977), which planted tens of millions of trees through rural women's groups. She won the 2004 Nobel Peace Prize for her <b>contribution to sustainable development</b>, democracy and peace: the first African woman laureate. Other environmental Peace laureates: Al Gore and the IPCC (2007).</li>
<li>Indian movements: Chipko (Sunderlal Bahuguna, Gaura Devi), Appiko (Karnataka), Save Silent Valley; "Tree Woman" Saalumarada Thimmakka (died 2025).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Sustainable agriculture</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>System of Rice Intensification (SRI)</b>, with alternate wetting and drying, results in <b>less seed</b> (single young seedlings at wide spacing: about 5–7 kg/ha instead of 50–60), <b>less methane</b> (fields moist but not flooded, so soil stays aerobic) and <b>less electricity</b> for pumping (25–50% less water). Developed in Madagascar in the 1980s by Fr. Henri de Laulanié and popularised by Norman Uphoff (Cornell). Practices: early transplanting, single seedlings, wide spacing, intermittent irrigation, mechanical weeding, organic manure. Downside: labour-intensive weeding. Related: direct-seeded rice, laser land levelling, System of Wheat Intensification, Sustainable Sugarcane Initiative.</li>
<li><b>Eco-friendly agriculture</b> practices (UPSC key): <b>crop diversification</b> (breaks pest cycles, saves water; e.g. moving Punjab away from paddy), <b>legume intensification</b> (fixes nitrogen, cuts urea) and <b>tensiometer use</b> (measures soil moisture so farmers irrigate only when needed). <b>Vertical farming</b> was left out as energy-intensive (debated, since it saves land and water).</li>
<li>Programmes: National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture, Soil Health Cards, Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana, Per Drop More Crop, Integrated Nutrient and Pest Management, Sub-Mission on Agroforestry (2016). See 1.2 and 4.2 on nitrogen.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Water</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Virtual water</b> (J.A. Allan; Stockholm Water Prize 2008) is the <b>volume of water needed to produce a commodity</b>. Water-scarce countries can save water by importing water-intensive goods. About 1,500 litres for 1 kg of wheat, 2,500–3,500 litres for rice, about 15,000 litres for beef. India is a big net <b>exporter</b> of virtual water (rice, cotton, sugar), worrying for Punjab and Haryana. Related: water footprint (Arjen Hoekstra).</li>
<li><b>Groundwater</b>: the UN World Water Development Report 2022 ("Groundwater: Making the invisible visible") says India extracts about 251 km³ a year, <b>more than a quarter of global groundwater withdrawal</b>, the most of any country. But about <b>89% goes to irrigation</b>, not mainly to drinking water and sanitation, so the reason given in Statement II was wrong.</li>
<li>Groundwater supplies 60%+ of irrigation and about 85% of rural drinking water. CGWB's 2024 assessment: recharge about 446 BCM, extraction about 245 BCM (stage about 60%); about 11% of units over-exploited (Punjab extracts about 160%+, Haryana, Rajasthan, Delhi). Causes: free or cheap power for pumps, water-hungry crops, landowners owning groundwater under the Easements Act 1882.</li>
<li>Measures: Atal Bhujal Yojana (2019, community groundwater management in 7 States, World Bank-aided), Jal Shakti Abhiyan "Catch the Rain", Amrit Sarovar (75 ponds per district), Model Groundwater Bill, CGWA guidelines (2020), National Aquifer Mapping, PM-KUSUM solar pumps, Punjab's Preservation of Subsoil Water Act 2009.</li>
<li><b>Tarun Bharat Sangh</b> (Bhikampura, near Alwar, Rajasthan), led by Rajendra Singh ("Waterman of India"; Magsaysay 2001, Stockholm Water Prize 2015), is famous for <b>rainwater harvesting</b>: it revived traditional <b>johads</b> (earthen check dams), recharging groundwater and reviving rivers such as the Arvari.</li>
<li>Traditional water harvesting: johads, baoris, kunds and tankas (Rajasthan), kuls (Himachal), zabo (Nagaland), eri (Tamil Nadu), surangam (Kerala).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Land and sand</h3>
<ul>
<li>Heavy <b>sand mining</b> in riverbeds causes <b>pollution of groundwater</b> (loss of sand's natural filtering; saltwater intrusion near coasts) and <b>lowering of the water table</b> (the deepened channel drains groundwater). It <b>increases</b>, not decreases, salinity in estuaries.</li>
<li>Other impacts: bank erosion, undermined bridges and embankments, loss of fish spawning grounds and turtle and gharial nesting sites, shifting river courses. Sand is the second most used resource after water (UNEP 2019, 2022). Regulation: sand is a minor mineral under the States; Sustainable Sand Mining Guidelines (2016), Enforcement Guidelines (2020). Alternatives: manufactured sand, recycled construction waste, overburden sand from coal mines.</li>
<li>The <b>Miyawaki method</b> (Japanese botanist Akira Miyawaki, 1970s) creates <b>dense mini forests in urban areas</b>: native species planted very densely (3–5 saplings per m²) in canopy, tree, sub-tree and shrub layers on enriched soil; they grow about 10 times faster and are self-sustaining in 2–3 years. Benefits: carbon capture, lower urban heat, biodiversity, groundwater recharge, less noise and dust. Criticism: high cost, cannot replace natural forests or grasslands. Used in Kerala, Hyderabad, Chennai, Mumbai, Prayagraj; under Nagar Van Yojana (2020) and MISHTI (2023). Related: sacred groves, agroforestry, "Ek Ped Maa Ke Naam" (2024), Green Credit Programme (2023).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
