// data/book_science_s1.js
// Science book, Unit 1: Physics.
addBookTopic("science", "s1-1", `
<p>Mechanics deals with motion and the forces that cause it. UPSC tests it through everyday situations (a car on a curve, a balloon rising, a cube floating) that can be solved with a few basic laws.</p>

<h3>Units and measurement</h3>
<ul>
<li>1 metre = 10<sup>9</sup> nanometres. A person 6 feet tall (6 × 30.48 cm ≈ 1.83 m) is about <b>183 × 10<sup>7</sup> nm</b>.</li>
<li><b>Mass</b> is the amount of matter and is the same everywhere. <b>Weight</b> = mass × g changes with g. A 100 kg body has <b>100 kg mass on the Moon</b> too, though its weight falls from about 1,000 N to about 167 N (Moon's g is about one-sixth of Earth's).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Newton's laws and motion</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>First law (inertia)</b>: a body keeps moving at constant velocity unless a net force acts. So a car running at a <b>uniform 60 km/h</b> on a straight road has <b>zero net force</b>: the engine's force exactly balances friction and air resistance.</li>
<li><b>Second law</b>: F = ma. <b>Third law</b>: every action has an equal and opposite reaction.</li>
<li><b>Conservation of momentum</b>: if no external force acts, total momentum cannot change. So a man on a perfectly frictionless surface could, in principle, propel himself by blowing air out (whistling), like a rocket (UPSC key; the effect is tiny, so some disputed it).</li>
<li><b>Uniform circular motion</b>: speed is constant but direction keeps changing, so the body accelerates towards the centre: <b>centripetal acceleration</b> a = v²/r = <b>ω²r</b>.</li>
<li><b>Jet engines vs rockets</b>: air-breathing jet engines (turbojet, turbofan, ramjet, scramjet) take oxygen from the surrounding air, so they <b>cannot work in space</b>. A rocket carries <b>both fuel and oxidiser</b>, so it works without air (usually liquid oxygen or a solid oxidiser rather than gas, but UPSC accepted the statement).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Vehicles in motion</h4>
<ul>
<li>A four-wheeler taking a sharp curve at high speed tends to <b>overturn about its outer wheels</b> (road friction acts inward below the centre of gravity, inertia acts outward through it, so the inner wheels lift) and to <b>skid outwards</b> if friction cannot supply the needed centripetal force (mv²/r). Roads are banked on curves for this reason.</li>
<li>When a car is driven on a level road, the reaction at the <b>front wheels becomes less than R</b> (its value at rest): traction acts at road level while resistance acts higher up, shifting load to the rear (debated key).</li>
<li>In a tanker accelerating forward, the oil surface tilts with <b>greater depth at the rear</b> (tan θ = a/g), because inertia makes the oil lag behind.</li>
<li><b>Fuel-efficient car features</b> (UPSC key): <b>streamlined body</b> (less drag) and <b>multipoint fuel injection</b> (precise fuel metering). Radial tyres also cut rolling resistance (debated omission). A <b>catalytic converter</b> cuts emissions (CO, hydrocarbons, NOx), not fuel use.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Friction</h4>
<ul>
<li>Static friction &gt; sliding friction &gt; rolling friction.</li>
<li><b>Ball bearings</b> replace sliding with rolling and <b>reduce the effective contact area</b> between wheel and axle, cutting friction, heat and wear. Other ways to reduce friction: lubricants, polishing, streamlining, air cushions, magnetic levitation. Friction is useful for walking, braking, writing and tyre grip.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Geometry of motion</h4>
<ul>
<li>A person standing at the middle of a ladder sliding between a wall and the floor traces a <b>circular path</b> (radius half the ladder's length, centred at the wall–floor corner).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Simple harmonic motion: the pendulum</h3>
<ul>
<li>At the <b>mean position</b> the restoring force and (tangential) acceleration are zero and speed is greatest.</li>
<li>In each cycle the bob reaches any given velocity <b>twice</b>.</li>
<li>At the <b>extreme positions</b> velocity is zero but acceleration is <b>maximum</b>, not zero.</li>
<li>An ideal pendulum keeps its amplitude; a real one slowly loses it to air resistance (damping). UPSC treated the pendulum as ideal.</li>
<li>The time period depends on length and g (T = 2π√(L/g)), not on mass or (small) amplitude.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Gravitation</h3>
<ul>
<li>g = GM/R². If Earth's radius shrank by 1% with the same mass, g would <b>rise by about 2%</b> (Δg/g = −2ΔR/R).</li>
<li><b>Weight increases with latitude</b>: g is about 9.78 m/s² at the equator and 9.83 m/s² at the poles, because the Earth is an <b>oblate spheroid</b> (polar radius about 21 km shorter) and because rotation reduces effective g at the equator. So "weight decreases with latitude" is false, though "the Earth is not a perfect sphere" is true.</li>
<li>For orbits and satellites, see 6.2.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pressure, fluids and buoyancy</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Archimedes' principle</b>: a body in a fluid is pushed up by a force equal to the weight of fluid displaced. A body floats if its density is less than the fluid's.</li>
<li>A weightless balloon filled with 200 cc of water has <b>zero weight in water</b>: it displaces exactly its own water.</li>
<li>A cube that sinks fully when 0.2 kg is placed on it, and floats 2 cm above water without it: 2 cm × a² of extra displaced water must weigh 200 g, so a² = 100 and <b>a = 10 cm</b>.</li>
<li><b>Iron floats on mercury but sinks in water</b>, because iron's specific gravity (about 7.8) is <b>less</b> than mercury's (13.6) but more than water's (1.0).</li>
<li>A hydrogen balloon rising through the atmosphere <b>increases in size</b> (outside pressure falls, so the gas expands: Boyle's law) and finally bursts; weather balloons are under-filled for this reason.</li>
<li><b>Torricelli's law</b>: liquid leaves an opening at speed v = √(2gh), depending only on the height of liquid, not its density. UPSC's key said the kerosene vessel empties first (low viscosity), though in theory water, mercury and kerosene would empty almost together.</li>
<li>Drinking through a straw works by <b>atmospheric pressure</b>, not capillarity (see 1.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Elasticity</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under a load, a wire stretches by ΔL = FL / (AY), where Y is Young's modulus. For the same material and load, ΔL ∝ L/d². So a <b>3 m wire of 1.5 mm diameter</b> stretches more (3/2.25 ≈ 1.33) than 1 m × 1 mm (1.0), 2 m × 2 mm (0.5) or 1 m × 2 mm (0.25).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s1-2", `
<p>Heat flows from hot to cold by conduction, convection and radiation, and it changes the state and properties of matter. Many UPSC questions are about why everyday things behave the way they do.</p>

<h3>Ways heat moves</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Mode</th><th>How</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Conduction</td><td>Through a material, particle to particle</td><td>Metals feel hot or cold quickly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Convection</td><td>By movement of a fluid</td><td>A <b>car radiator</b>: coolant circulates (pumped) and air blown through the fins carries heat away; sea breezes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Radiation</td><td>By electromagnetic waves, no medium needed</td><td>Sunlight; a minor part of radiator cooling despite the name</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Copper feels hotter than glass</b> at the same temperature because its <b>thermal conductivity</b> is far higher (about 400 W/m·K against about 1), so it passes heat to the skin faster. Copper is also denser than glass, but density does not explain it.</li>
<li>Bodies cool faster when they have more <b>surface area per unit mass</b> (Newton's law of cooling). A thin <b>circular plate</b> cools faster than a hollow cube or hollow sphere of the same radius; the sphere, with the least surface for its size, cools slowest.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Latent heat and changes of state</h3>
<ul>
<li>Latent heat is absorbed or released during a change of state without a change in temperature.</li>
<li><b>Steam at 100°C</b> holds more heat than boiling water at 100°C: the extra latent heat of vaporisation (about 540 cal/g or 2,260 J/g). That is why steam burns are worse.</li>
<li>The latent heat of <b>fusion</b> of ice (about 80 cal/g) is much smaller than that of <b>vaporisation</b>.</li>
<li>In an <b>air conditioner</b> (or refrigerator), heat is <b>extracted from room air at the evaporator coils</b> (the refrigerant evaporates and absorbs heat) and <b>rejected outside at the condenser coils</b> after compression.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Boiling point and pressure</h3>
<ul>
<li>Higher pressure raises the boiling point; lower pressure lowers it.</li>
<li>So the <b>boiling point falls with altitude</b> because <b>air pressure decreases</b> (not increases) with height: about 90°C at Leh and about 70°C on Everest, so cooking is slow.</li>
<li>In a <b>pressure cooker</b>, food cooks at the boiling point inside, which depends on the pressure, set by the <b>weight on the vent and the area of the hole</b> (pressure = weight/area). A heavier weight or smaller hole means higher pressure (about 120°C at 2 atm). The <b>flame temperature</b> only changes how fast heat is supplied; extra heat just releases steam.</li>
<li>Autoclaves sterilise at about 121°C. Impurities such as salt raise the boiling point and lower the freezing point. Pressure lowers the melting point of ice (regelation; ice skating, glacier sliding).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Anomalous expansion of water</h3>
<ul>
<li>Water <b>contracts from 0°C to 4°C</b>, where it is densest, and <b>expands above 4°C</b>. So heated from 0°C to 10°C, its volume <b>first decreases, then increases</b>. The cause is hydrogen bonding.</li>
<li>In severe winter a lake freezes on top while the bottom stays liquid, because <b>water is densest at 4°C</b>: the 4°C water sinks and stays at the bottom, while colder, lighter water rises and freezes, and the ice insulates the water below. Aquatic life survives.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Surface tension, capillarity and viscosity</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Capillarity</b> (from surface tension and adhesion): liquids rise in narrow tubes, higher in narrower tubes (Jurin's law); mercury shows capillary depression.</li>
<li>Without capillarity, a <b>kerosene lamp</b> (oil rises up the wick), <b>blotting paper</b> and the rise of water in <b>tall trees</b> (with transpiration pull and cohesion) would fail. A <b>drinking straw</b> works by atmospheric pressure instead. Other examples: water rising in soil, towels, damp walls.</li>
<li>Surface tension lets insects walk on water and makes drops spherical; detergents lower it.</li>
<li><b>Viscosity of liquids</b> such as glycerine, honey and oil <b>decreases on heating</b>, because rising kinetic energy helps molecules overcome the forces between them. (Gases become more viscous when heated.)</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s1-3", `
<p>Sound and light are both waves, but sound is a mechanical (longitudinal) wave needing a medium, while light is electromagnetic and travels even through vacuum. This topic covers their behaviour and the everyday effects they produce.</p>

<h3>Sound</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sound travels fastest in <b>solids</b>, slower in liquids, slowest in gases, because solids are stiffer relative to their density.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Medium</th><th>Speed of sound (approx.)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Air at 0°C</td><td>331 m/s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Air at 100°C</td><td>387 m/s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water</td><td>1,490 m/s</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Wood</b> (along the grain)</td><td>3,300–4,000 m/s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steel</td><td>5,900 m/s</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>In air, sound speed rises with <b>temperature</b> (v ≈ 331 + 0.6T m/s) and with <b>humidity</b> (moist air is less dense, since water vapour is lighter than N2 and O2). It does not depend on pressure. So "speed of sound decreases as humidity increases" is the incorrect statement.</li>
<li><b>Mach number</b> = speed ÷ local speed of sound. At 15 km, air is about −56°C, so sound is slower (about 295 m/s against 340 m/s at sea level). A jet at Mach 1 is therefore <b>slower</b> at 15 km than at sea level.</li>
<li>Sound travels through rock mainly as <b>longitudinal</b> compression waves (UPSC key), though solids also carry transverse waves (earthquake S-waves).</li>
<li>Pitch: a <b>shorter flute</b> (shorter air column) gives a <b>higher</b> frequency (f ∝ 1/L).</li>
<li>The <b>diatonic scale</b> has seven notes in an octave (Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni; UPSC key, though some count eight with the upper Sa). If Sa is 256 Hz, the upper Sa is 512 Hz and Ni about 480 Hz.</li>
<li><b>Decibels</b> are logarithmic: +10 dB means 10 times the intensity. About 100 dB is a <b>machine shop</b> or jackhammer.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The electromagnetic spectrum</h3>
<ul>
<li>From long to short wavelength: radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays. All travel at the speed of light in vacuum.</li>
<li>Photon energy E = hc/λ: <b>shorter wavelength means higher energy</b>. Among visible colours, <b>blue (or violet) has the most energy</b> and red the least. So "red light is more energetic than green" is false, though red's wavelength is indeed longer.</li>
<li><b>Radio waves</b> are produced by <b>rapidly oscillating electric currents</b> in antennas. Being uncharged, electromagnetic waves are <b>not bent by magnetic fields</b>; only charged particles are (Lorentz force). Long-distance radio "bending" comes from ionospheric reflection and diffraction.</li>
<li><b>Night vision</b> devices use <b>infrared</b> (thermal imaging) or amplify faint light. <b>TV remotes</b> use <b>infrared light</b> from an LED, so they need a line of sight. Police speed guns use the <b>Doppler effect</b> of reflected short radio waves (microwave radar) or laser (LIDAR).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Reflection, refraction and total internal reflection</h3>
<ul>
<li>When light passes from air into glass, its <b>frequency stays the same</b> but its <b>speed and wavelength</b> decrease (v = c/n). In water, light travels at about 0.75 times its vacuum speed (n ≈ 1.33), not 0.95.</li>
<li>A <b>stick dipped in water looks bent and shorter</b> because of <b>refraction</b> (not scattering).</li>
<li><b>Mirage</b>: hot ground heats the air above it (<b>terrestrial heating</b>), and light from the sky bends upward through layers of different density (<b>refraction</b>, ending in total internal reflection at a grazing angle), so the sky looks like water on the road.</li>
<li><b>Glass beads</b> on traffic signs and road markings glow in headlights because light is refracted and <b>totally internally reflected</b> back towards the source (retroreflection).</li>
<li><b>Rainbow</b>: <b>dispersion, refraction and internal reflection</b> in raindrops. It appears opposite the Sun at about 42°; the primary bow has red outside; a fainter secondary bow (about 51°) has the colours reversed.</li>
<li>Atmospheric refraction makes the Sun visible about two minutes before actual sunrise and after sunset, and makes <b>stars twinkle</b> (planets twinkle less).</li>
<li><b>LASIK</b> eye surgery reshapes the cornea with a laser; it is done only on adults with a stable prescription, not on children.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Scattering and colour of the sky</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Rayleigh scattering</b> by molecules goes as 1/λ⁴, so <b>shorter wavelengths scatter more</b>: the sky is blue, and the Sun looks red at sunrise and sunset because blue light is scattered away along the long path. C.V. Raman's work on light scattering won the 1930 Nobel Prize (Raman effect, 1928).</li>
<li><b>Diffusion</b> of sunlight in the atmosphere is caused by <b>dust particles</b> (and other tiny particles), which spread daylight.</li>
<li><b>Optical illusions</b> (UPSC key): the <b>Sun looking larger at dusk</b>, its <b>colour at dawn</b>, and the <b>twinkling of stars</b>. The Moon visible at dawn and the Pole Star in the sky are real.</li>
<li><b>Albedo</b> is the fraction of light an object reflects; it decides how bright a planet or moon looks. Mercury's albedo (about 0.07–0.14) is <b>much lower</b> than Earth's (about 0.30).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Interference and diffraction</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>CD shows rainbow colours</b> because its closely spaced tracks act as a reflection <b>diffraction grating</b>: light reflects off the metal layer and different wavelengths diffract at different angles.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Colour mixing</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Additive (light)</th><th>Subtractive (pigments, inks)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Primaries red, green, blue; together they make white (screens)</td><td>Primaries cyan, magenta, yellow; together they absorb all (black)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Magenta + yellow = red</b> (magenta absorbs green, yellow absorbs blue). <b>Cyan + magenta = blue</b>. Cyan + yellow = green.</li>
<li><b>CMYK</b> (cyan, magenta, yellow, key = black) is the four-colour process of <b>offset printing</b>.</li>
<li>A tube light labelled <b>6500 K</b> shows its <b>colour temperature</b> ("cool daylight"), not its actual temperature.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Vision</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Persistence of vision</b>: an image stays on the retina for about 1/16 to 1/10 second, so films projected at <b>24 frames per second</b> appear continuous.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s1-4", `
<p>Electricity powers most household devices. This topic covers basic circuit ideas and how familiar devices work.</p>

<h3>Current, resistance and supply</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Resistance</b> R = ρL/A. If two wires have length, diameter and resistivity all in the ratio 1:2, the thicker one has 2ρ, 2L and 4 times the area, so its resistance is the <b>same</b> as the thinner one's (10 Ω).</li>
<li>India's mains supply is about 230 V, <b>50 Hz</b>. In each cycle the current reverses twice, so it changes direction <b>100 times per second</b> (not 60). The USA uses 60 Hz.</li>
<li>The resistance of the dry human body is of the order of <b>10<sup>4</sup> ohms</b> (UPSC key); wet skin can fall to about 1,000 Ω, which is why wet hands make shocks dangerous.</li>
<li>A <b>choke</b> (inductor, ballast) in a fluorescent tube gives a high-voltage surge to strike the arc and then <b>limits the current</b> so the tube does not burn out.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Lighting</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Lamp</th><th>How it makes light</th><th>Efficacy (lumens per watt)</th><th>Life</th></tr>
<tr><td>Incandescent</td><td>Hot filament</td><td>About 15</td><td>About 1,000 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>CFL</b></td><td>Discharge through <b>mercury vapour</b> makes UV, which makes a <b>phosphor</b> glow</td><td>About 60</td><td>8,000–10,000 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sodium vapour</td><td>Discharge through sodium vapour</td><td>About 100–150</td><td>15,000–25,000 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>LED</b></td><td>Electroluminescence in a <b>semiconductor</b> p-n junction</td><td>100–200</td><td>25,000–50,000+ hours</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>So a CFL is <b>less energy-efficient</b> than an LED and does <b>not</b> last longer.</li>
<li><b>Sodium lamps</b> emit light in all directions (360°), needing reflectors; <b>LEDs</b> are directional. Low-pressure sodium gives almost <b>monochromatic</b> yellow light (589 nm), so colours cannot be told apart, while LEDs give white light with good colour rendering. LEDs, not sodium lamps, last longer.</li>
<li>Policies: UJALA (2015, EESL) LED bulbs; Street Lighting National Programme (2015; over 1.3 crore LED street lights). The 2014 Physics Nobel went to Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura for the blue LED, which made white LEDs possible. Blue-rich LEDs add to light pollution; Hanle in Ladakh became a Dark Sky Reserve (2022).</li>
<li>Used fluorescent lamps contain mercury (see 2.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Displays</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>OLEDs</b> (organic light-emitting diodes) use thin organic films that emit their own light, so they need no backlight (unlike LCDs). They can be made on <b>flexible plastic</b>, used for <b>roll-up displays in clothing</b>, and made <b>transparent</b>; they also give true blacks and wide viewing angles. Drawbacks: shorter life of blue OLEDs, burn-in, cost.</li>
<li>Other displays: LCD (backlit; "LED TVs" are LED-backlit LCDs), AMOLED, QLED (quantum dots), MicroLED, e-ink.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Household devices</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Microwave oven</b>: microwaves (about 2.45 GHz) make polar <b>water molecules</b> rotate, generating heat. A potato heats up but a dry paper plate does not, because the <b>potato contains water and the paper does not</b>.</li>
<li><b>CD player</b>: reads microscopic pits with a <b>laser beam</b> (780 nm infrared). Blu-ray uses a 405 nm blue-violet laser (see 8.1).</li>
<li><b>Laser printer</b>: a <b>semiconductor laser</b> (laser diode) writes an electrostatic image on a drum that picks up toner (early models used helium–neon lasers).</li>
<li><b>Dolby B and Dolby C</b>: <b>noise reduction</b> circuits that cut tape hiss on audio cassettes.</li>
<li>TV remote: infrared light (see 1.3).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s1-5", `
<p>Modern physics deals with the very small (atoms, nuclei, elementary particles) and the very large (relativity). These ideas underpin nuclear energy (7.1), medical imaging and much of astronomy (6.1).</p>

<h3>Radioactivity</h3>
<ul>
<li>Unstable nuclei decay by emitting alpha particles (helium nuclei), beta particles (electrons or positrons) or gamma rays.</li>
<li><b>Half-life</b> is the time for half the nuclei to decay. With a half-life of 4 months, half remains after 4 months and a quarter after 8 months, so <b>three-quarters decays in 8 months</b>.</li>
<li><b>Zirconium is not radioactive</b> (stable isotopes). Its alloy zircaloy clads nuclear fuel because it absorbs few neutrons. Radioactive elements and isotopes include uranium, thorium, radium, plutonium, polonium, radon, <b>astatine</b> and <b>francium</b> (both very rare, short-lived), and <b>tritium</b> (radioactive hydrogen-3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Medical uses of radiation</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>CT scan</b> (computed tomography) uses <b>X-rays</b> taken from many angles and combined by a computer into cross-sectional slices. Invented by Godfrey Hounsfield and Allan Cormack (Nobel 1979).</li>
<li><b>MRI</b> uses magnetic fields and radio waves, not ionising radiation (Nobel 2003; see 10.1). <b>PET scans</b> use positrons (antimatter).</li>
<li><b>CyberKnife</b> is a robotic <b>stereotactic radiosurgery</b> system that delivers precise radiation beams to tumours; it treats tumours but does not map their spread (that is done by PET-CT and MRI).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Elementary particles and forces</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Particle</th></tr>
<tr><td>Zero mass (as once believed)</td><td><b>Neutrino</b>; now known to have a tiny mass</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fractional charge</td><td><b>Quark</b> (+2/3 or −1/3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Half-integer (fractional) spin: fermions</td><td><b>Positron</b>, electron, proton, neutron</td></tr>
<tr><td>Integer spin: bosons</td><td><b>Phonon</b> (quantum of vibration), photon, Higgs boson</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The four <b>fundamental forces</b>, strongest to weakest: <b>strong nuclear &gt; electromagnetic &gt; weak nuclear &gt; gravity</b>. Gravity is the <b>weakest</b> (about 10<sup>−38</sup> of the strong force), but dominates at large scales because it is always attractive and long-range. Strong and weak forces are very short-range. Electromagnetic and weak forces unify as the electroweak force (Nobel 1979); the Standard Model does not include gravity.</li>
<li>The <b>Higgs boson</b> is the particle of the Higgs field, which <b>explains why elementary particles have mass</b>. It was discovered at CERN's Large Hadron Collider (ATLAS and CMS) in July 2012; Peter Higgs and François Englert won the 2013 Nobel. It has nothing to do with "teleporting" matter or better fission fuels. "Boson" honours Satyendra Nath Bose. India became an associate member of CERN in 2017. Peter Higgs died in April 2024.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Neutrinos</h3>
<ul>
<li>Neutrinos are <b>chargeless</b>, travel close to the speed of light, are produced in <b>beta decay</b> (and in the Sun, reactors and supernovae), have a <b>tiny but non-zero mass</b> (shown by neutrino oscillation, Nobel 2015: Kajita and McDonald), and about <b>100 trillion pass through each human body every second</b>. All four statements were correct.</li>
<li><b>IceCube</b> (South Pole, completed 2010) is the <b>world's largest neutrino detector</b>, using a cubic kilometre of ice with 5,160 sensors <b>buried 1,450–2,450 m deep</b>. It also <b>searches for dark matter</b> indirectly. It found the first high-energy neutrino source (blazar TXS 0506+056, 2018) and Milky Way neutrinos (2023). It detects Cherenkov light.</li>
<li><b>India-based Neutrino Observatory</b> (Bodi West Hills, Theni, Tamil Nadu): planned as a mega science project; stalled over environmental concerns.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Antimatter</h3>
<ul>
<li>Antimatter has the same mass as matter but opposite charge. The positron was predicted by Dirac and discovered by Anderson (1932).</li>
<li>The STAR experiment at Brookhaven's Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider (with Indian scientists) created <b>anti-helium-4</b> (2011), then the heaviest antimatter nucleus; in 2024 it reported anti-hyperhydrogen-4.</li>
<li>Its implications: it helps probe whether <b>antimatter stars or galaxies</b> exist and helps understand the <b>evolution of the universe</b> (why matter dominates). It has no use in mineral or oil exploration.</li>
<li>CERN's ALPHA experiment showed antihydrogen falls under gravity like matter (2023).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Relativity</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Special relativity</b> (1905): constant speed of light, E = mc², time dilation, length contraction.</li>
<li><b>General relativity</b> (1915) predicts that <b>light is affected by gravity</b> (confirmed by Eddington, 1919; gravitational lensing), that <b>matter warps space-time</b> (the core idea: curvature is gravity) and a dynamic, <b>expanding universe</b> (Einstein added a cosmological constant to keep it static, later calling it his biggest blunder after Hubble showed expansion in 1929).</li>
<li>Other confirmed predictions: Mercury's perihelion precession; gravitational redshift and time dilation (GPS clocks must be corrected); <b>gravitational waves</b> (LIGO, 2015); black holes (Event Horizon Telescope images of M87*, 2019, and Sagittarius A*, 2022); frame-dragging. See 6.1.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_science_s2.js
// Science book, Unit 2: Chemistry.
addBookTopic("science", "s2-1", `
<p>Chemistry begins with the structure of the atom, which explains the periodic table, how atoms bond, and how reactions happen.</p>

<h3>Atomic structure</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Quantum number</th><th>Describes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Principal (n)</td><td>Size and energy of the shell</td></tr>
<tr><td>Azimuthal (l)</td><td>Shape of the orbital (s, p, d, f)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Magnetic (m<sub>l</sub>)</b></td><td><b>Orientation</b> of the orbital in space (−l to +l)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spin (m<sub>s</sub>)</td><td>Electron spin (+½ or −½)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The order of filling orbitals is governed by the <b>Aufbau principle</b>: electrons fill orbitals in order of rising energy (1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d …; the n + l rule). Pauli's exclusion principle allows two electrons per orbital; Hund's rule spreads electrons singly before pairing.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Periodic trends</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Across a period (left to right)</th><th>Down a group</th></tr>
<tr><td>Atomic radius</td><td>Decreases</td><td><b>Increases</b> (new shells)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ionisation energy</td><td><b>Increases</b> (not decreases)</td><td>Decreases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electron affinity</td><td>Generally increases</td><td><b>Generally decreases</b> (with exceptions: chlorine's is higher than fluorine's)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electronegativity</td><td><b>Increases</b> (fluorine is highest)</td><td>Decreases</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So "electron affinity always increases down a group" is false, though the atomic radius does increase down a group.</p>

<h3>Bonding and compounds</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Ionic compounds</b> dissolve in polar water but are generally <b>insoluble in organic solvents</b> such as alcohol, kerosene or petrol. In the <b>solid state they do not conduct</b>: the ions are fixed in the lattice. They conduct when molten or dissolved.</li>
<li><b>Water is the "universal solvent"</b> because it is <b>dipolar</b>: a bent molecule (about 104.5°) with a partial negative oxygen and partial positive hydrogens, which surround and pull ions apart and form hydrogen bonds with polar molecules; its dielectric constant is about 80. (Water is a poor conductor of heat; its high specific heat explains climate moderation, not solubility.)</li>
<li>Oxidation numbers: Ca in CaO2 (a peroxide, O = −1) is +2; Al in NaAlH4 (H = −1) is +3; Mn in MnO2 is +4; S in H2S2O7 (oleum) is +6.</li>
<li>Alkanes increase in molecular weight by 14 (CH2) each step: methane CH4 (16) &lt; ethane (30) &lt; propane (44) &lt; butane (58).</li>
<li>The elements primarily responsible for the origin of life were <b>carbon, hydrogen and nitrogen</b> (with oxygen, phosphorus and sulphur: CHNOPS). The Miller–Urey experiment (1953) made amino acids from methane, ammonia, hydrogen and water with electric sparks. Amino acids and nucleobases have been found in asteroid samples (Ryugu; Bennu, via NASA's OSIRIS-REx, 2023–25).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Chemical and physical change</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>chemical change</b> makes a new substance and is usually irreversible: <b>souring of milk</b> (bacteria turn lactose into lactic acid), rusting, burning, cooking, digestion, ripening.</li>
<li>A <b>physical change</b> alters state, shape or size only: <b>crystallising salt</b>, <b>melting ice</b>, dissolving sugar, boiling water, magnetising iron.</li>
<li><b>Reactions speed up at higher temperatures</b> because molecules move faster and collide more often and more energetically, so more collisions exceed the activation energy (Arrhenius). A rough rule: the rate doubles for every 10°C.</li>
<li>An aqueous solution of <b>copper sulphate is acidic</b> because the salt (of a weak base and a strong acid) undergoes <b>hydrolysis</b>: Cu²⁺ ions react with water and release H⁺.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Solutions and osmosis</h3>
<ul>
<li>In <b>osmosis</b>, the solvent moves through a semipermeable membrane <b>from the dilute solution to the concentrated</b> one.</li>
<li>In <b>reverse osmosis</b>, pressure greater than the osmotic pressure is applied to the <b>concentrated</b> solution (e.g. seawater), pushing pure water out. RO purifiers and desalination plants use it. Both statements in the 2005 question had these backwards.</li>
<li>Adding solutes <b>lowers</b> water potential (it becomes more negative), so water moves into the solution. Colligative properties (lowering of vapour pressure, elevation of boiling point, depression of freezing point, osmotic pressure) depend on the number of solute particles.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s2-2", `
<p>Chemistry is all around us: in the kitchen, in building materials, in fuels and in the metals and alloys we use. UPSC asks for common names, compositions and uses.</p>

<h3>Common compounds and their uses</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Compound</th><th>Formula</th><th>Uses and facts</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Baking soda</b></td><td>Sodium bicarbonate, NaHCO3</td><td>Baking; <b>fire extinguishers</b> (releases CO2 in soda-acid and dry-chemical types)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Washing soda / soda ash</td><td>Na2CO3·10H2O / anhydrous Na2CO3</td><td>Softening water, glass-making. <b>Anhydrous sodium carbonate is soda ash, not baking soda</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Quicklime</b></td><td>CaO</td><td>Used in <b>making glass</b> (soda-lime glass from sand, soda ash and lime), mortar</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Gypsum</b></td><td>CaSO4·2H2O</td><td>Heated to about 150°C gives <b>Plaster of Paris</b> (CaSO4·½H2O) for casts and statues; <b>added to cement (2–5%) to slow its setting</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bleaching powder</b></td><td>CaOCl2</td><td>Made by passing chlorine over slaked lime in the <b>Hasenclever</b> (or Bachmann) plant; key element <b>chlorine</b>; disinfectant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hypo</td><td>Sodium thiosulphate, Na2S2O3</td><td>Photography (fixer); key element sodium</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Silica gel</b></td><td>Porous SiO2</td><td>A desiccant that <b>absorbs (adsorbs) moisture</b>, kept in medicine bottles and packaging</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Philosopher's wool</b></td><td>Zinc oxide, ZnO</td><td>Fluffy white flakes formed when zinc burns</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calcium carbonate</td><td>CaCO3</td><td>Abrasive in toothpaste; limestone</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Metals, alloys and gems</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Material</th><th>Composition and facts</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>German silver</b></td><td>Copper, zinc and <b>nickel</b> (about 60:20:20): <b>contains no silver</b>; cutlery, keys, instruments</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Solder</b></td><td><b>Tin and lead</b> (60:40 or eutectic 63:37, melting about 183°C); lead-free solders now</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Nichrome</b></td><td>Nickel (80%) and chromium (20%): <b>hard and ductile</b>, drawn into heating-element wires (UPSC key; tungsten is also hard and drawn into filaments)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brass / bronze</td><td>Copper + zinc / copper + tin</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>18-carat gold</b></td><td>18 parts gold and 6 parts other metals (75% gold); pure gold is 24 carat</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Rubies and sapphires</b></td><td><b>Aluminium oxide</b> (corundum, hardness 9); chromium makes ruby red, iron and titanium make sapphire blue</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Lightest metal: lithium</b> (0.53 g/cm³; floats on oil). <b>Heaviest (densest): osmium</b> (22.6 g/cm³), just ahead of iridium.</li>
<li>Densities: <b>gold (19.3) &gt; mercury (13.6) &gt; steel (about 7.8–8.0)</b> g/cm³.</li>
<li><b>Anodising</b> aluminium thickens its natural <b>aluminium oxide</b> layer electrolytically, protecting it and letting it take dyes.</li>
<li><b>Selenium</b> is a non-metal that is <b>not a poor conductor</b>: a semiconductor whose conductivity rises in light (photocells, light meters, photocopier drums). Graphite is the classic conducting non-metal.</li>
<li><b>Xenon</b> is the "stranger gas" (Greek <i>xenos</i>), discovered by Ramsay and Travers (1898).</li>
<li><b>Liquid sodium</b> is a <b>coolant in fast breeder reactors</b> (FBTR and PFBR, Kalpakkam), since it carries heat well without slowing neutrons (see 7.1).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Glass</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Crookes glass</b> contains cerium oxide and <b>cuts off ultraviolet</b> rays (sunglasses, goggles).</li>
<li>Early laminated <b>shatter-proof glass</b> used a cellulose nitrate or acetate interlayer (modern ones use polyvinyl butyral). UPSC accepted that cellulose's insolubility in water explained the use.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fuels</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Fuel gas</th><th>Main constituents</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>CNG</b></td><td><b>Methane</b> (with some ethane), compressed to about 200 bar</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>LPG</b></td><td><b>Butane and propane</b> (ethyl mercaptan added for smell); not methane</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Coal gas</b></td><td>Hydrogen (about 50%), methane (about 35%), CO</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Water gas</b></td><td><b>CO and hydrogen</b> (steam over red-hot coke)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Methane</b> (natural gas) is supplied by <b>pipelines</b> (PNG) for homes and factories.</li>
<li><b>LNG</b>: natural gas cooled to about −162°C, shrinking about 600 times, carried in cryogenic tankers (stored near atmospheric pressure; CNG is the compressed one). India's <b>first LNG terminal</b> was <b>Dahej</b> (Gujarat, Petronet LNG, 2004), not Hassan. <b>Natural gas liquids</b> (ethane, propane, butane, natural gasoline) are separated <b>from natural gas</b>; LPG is one of their products.</li>
<li><b>Mine explosions</b> are caused mostly by <b>methane mixed with air</b> ("firedamp", explosive at 5–15%), sometimes followed by coal-dust explosions.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Explosives</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Nitroglycerine</b> (glyceryl trinitrate) is a powerful, shock-sensitive explosive; Alfred Nobel tamed it as <b>dynamite</b> (1867) by absorbing it in kieselguhr.</li>
<li><b>RDX</b> (Research Department eXplosive) is <b>cyclonite</b>, used in plastic explosives such as C-4 and Semtex. TNT, PETN and HMX are other military explosives (see 9.1).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Detergents, soaps and hard water</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Hard water</b> contains calcium and magnesium salts. With soap it forms insoluble scum, wasting soap; in <b>boilers</b> it forms scale, reducing efficiency. UPSC's key said hard water is unsuitable for drinking, washing with soap, boilers and irrigation (all four; debated, as moderately hard water is fine to drink).</li>
<li><b>Synthetic detergents lather well in hard water</b> because their calcium and magnesium salts are <b>soluble</b>; soaps' are not.</li>
<li>In washing powder, <b>sodium sulphate</b> is a filler that <b>keeps the powder dry</b> and free-flowing, and <b>sodium silicate maintains alkalinity</b> (and protects machines from corrosion).</li>
<li><b>Dry cleaning</b>: the key named <b>carbon tetrachloride</b>, the classic non-flammable solvent (now abandoned: toxic and ozone-depleting). Trichloroethylene and benzene were also used historically (debated); sodium carbonate is for wet washing. Today perchloroethylene is the usual solvent.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Batteries</h3>
<ul>
<li>In the Leclanché <b>dry cell</b> (zinc–carbon, about 1.5 V), the electrolyte is a moist paste of <b>ammonium chloride and zinc chloride</b>; the zinc can is the anode and a carbon rod in MnO2 is the cathode. For lithium-ion and EV batteries, see 7.2.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Industrial processes</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Process</th><th>Industry</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cracking (breaking heavy hydrocarbons)</td><td>Petroleum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Smelting (extracting metal from ore by heat)</td><td>Copper</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hydrogenation (adding hydrogen with a nickel catalyst)</td><td>Edible fats (vanaspati)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vulcanisation (heating rubber with sulphur; Goodyear)</td><td>Rubber</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Refrigerants</h3>
<ul>
<li>Large cold storage plants use <b>ammonia</b> (it liquefies at moderate pressure and has a high latent heat), while domestic refrigerators used <b>CFCs</b> (now HFC-134a and hydrocarbons such as R-600a). Ammonia is kept out of homes mainly because it is toxic. See the Environment book on CFCs and HFCs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fireworks and flame colours</h3>
<ul>
<li>Metal salts give colours: <b>strontium red</b>, <b>barium green</b>, sodium yellow, copper blue, calcium orange, potassium lilac.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Food chemistry</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Aspartame</b> (a dipeptide of aspartic acid and phenylalanine) gives about 4 kcal/g like other proteins, but is about <b>200 times sweeter</b> than sugar, so the tiny amount used adds negligible calories. IARC classed it "possibly carcinogenic" (2023).</li>
<li><b>Colas</b> contain significant <b>caffeine</b> (about 30–40 mg per can), plus sugar, phosphoric acid and CO2.</li>
<li><b>Caffeine</b> is a mild <b>diuretic</b> and stimulant. <b>Citric acid</b> is used in soft drinks (acidulant, preservative E330). <b>Ascorbic acid</b> (vitamin C) is needed for collagen in bones, teeth and gums (scurvy when deficient). <b>Citric acid cannot replace vitamin C</b>; it has no vitamin activity.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s2-3", `
<p>New materials (nanomaterials, graphene, carbon fibre, hydrogels) and strategic minerals (rare earths) are reshaping technology. At the same time, many everyday chemicals raise health and environmental concerns.</p>

<h3>Nanotechnology</h3>
<ul>
<li>"Nano" means 10<sup>−9</sup> m; nanomaterials are 1–100 nm in size. At this scale they behave differently: huge surface area and quantum effects.</li>
<li>Nanoparticles <b>also occur in nature</b>: volcanic ash, sea spray, forest-fire smoke, desert dust, viruses, clays, magnetite in some bacteria. So "only man-made" is wrong.</li>
<li>Nanoparticles of <b>metal oxides</b> (zinc oxide, titanium dioxide) are used in <b>sunscreens and cosmetics</b>.</li>
<li>Concerns: engineered nanoparticles <b>accumulate in the environment</b> (water, soil), <b>enter food chains</b> and <b>trigger free radicals</b> (oxidative stress, inflammation, DNA damage); some are <b>unsafe for humans</b> and can cross the blood–brain barrier.</li>
<li>In health: nanotechnology enables <b>targeted drug delivery</b> (liposomes, dendrimers, gold and polymer nanoparticles to tumours) and contributes to <b>gene therapy</b> (lipid nanoparticles carry mRNA in COVID-19 vaccines and siRNA drugs). Also diagnostics (quantum dots), imaging, antimicrobial silver coatings.</li>
<li>Other uses: nano urea and nano DAP (IFFCO), water purification, electronics. India: Nano Mission (2007, DST); guidelines for nanopharmaceuticals (2019) and nano agri-inputs (2020). The 2023 Chemistry Nobel was for quantum dots.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Carbon materials</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Allotrope</th><th>Structure and use</th></tr>
<tr><td>Diamond</td><td>3D tetrahedral; hardest natural substance; insulator</td></tr>
<tr><td>Graphite</td><td>Layers; conducts electricity; lubricant; pencil "lead"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fullerene (C60)</td><td>Football-shaped "buckyball" (Nobel 1996)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Graphene</b></td><td>A single <b>two-dimensional</b> layer of carbon atoms in a honeycomb lattice</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Carbon nanotubes</b></td><td>Rolled graphene sheets; very strong, light, conductive</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Graphene</b> has excellent electrical conductivity, is one of the <b>thinnest yet strongest</b> materials (about 200 times stronger than steel by weight), and can serve as transparent <b>conducting electrodes</b> for touch screens, LCDs and OLEDs. It is made of <b>carbon, not silicon</b> (though it is highly transparent). Isolated by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov using sticky tape (2004; Nobel 2010).</li>
<li><b>Carbon nanotubes</b> can carry <b>drugs and antigens</b> in the body and serve in <b>biochemical sensors</b>; UPSC's key also treated them as <b>biodegradable</b> (some enzymes break them down; debated). Making artificial blood capillaries was not accepted.</li>
<li><b>Carbon fibre</b> (carbon fibre-reinforced polymer) is strong, stiff and light: used in <b>aircraft and automobiles</b> (Boeing 787 and Airbus A350 are about 50% composites; Tejas; F1 cars), wind turbine blades, satellites, sports goods. It <b>can be recycled</b> (pyrolysis, solvolysis, grinding), though recycling is costly. Made mostly from polyacrylonitrile. India imports most carbon fibre; a Reliance plant at Hazira and the National Technical Textiles Mission aim to build capacity.</li>
<li><b>Activated carbon</b>: a huge surface area (500–3,000 m² per gram) <b>adsorbs pollutants and heavy metals</b>, and it can be <b>made cheaply from carbon-rich wastes</b> (coconut shells, rice husk, sawdust). Both explain why it is a good and attractive tool for treating effluents. Uses: water treatment, air purification, gas masks, mercury capture, gold recovery, poisoning treatment. Other adsorbents: zeolites, silica gel, metal–organic frameworks (2025 Chemistry Nobel). Adsorption is a surface phenomenon; absorption is bulk uptake.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Hydrogels and polymers</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Hydrogels</b> are networks of water-loving polymers that hold large amounts of water without dissolving. UPSC's key accepts only <b>controlled drug delivery</b> (smart insulin release, wound dressings, contact lenses, tissue scaffolds), not mobile air-conditioning or industrial lubricants (research exists on both). Other uses: superabsorbent granules in farm soil (ICAR's Pusa Hydrogel), diapers (sodium polyacrylate).</li>
<li><b>Polyethylene terephthalate (PET)</b>: its fibres (polyester: Terylene, Dacron) are <b>blended with wool and cotton</b> (terywool, terycot), and PET bottles <b>can be recycled</b> into fibre and new bottles (FSSAI allows recycled PET for food contact since 2022). It is <b>not</b> suitable for storing all alcoholic drinks (spirits can leach chemicals; it is slightly permeable), and <b>burning it releases CO2</b>.</li>
<li><b>Bisphenol A</b> is a key building block of <b>polycarbonate</b> plastics (and epoxy can linings). It mimics oestrogen (endocrine disruptor). BIS banned it in baby bottles (2015).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Resin code</th><th>Plastic</th><th>Uses</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>PET</td><td>Bottles, polyester</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>HDPE</td><td>Milk cans, pipes</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>PVC</td><td>Pipes, cables (dioxins when burnt; phthalate plasticisers)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>LDPE</td><td>Carry bags, films</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>PP</td><td>Food containers, straws</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>PS</td><td>Thermocol, cups</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Others</td><td>Polycarbonate (BPA), acrylic, nylon, PLA (bioplastic)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Plastic is in cigarette butts</b> (cellulose acetate filters, the most littered item on Earth), <b>eyeglass lenses</b> (CR-39, polycarbonate, Trivex) and <b>car tyres</b> (synthetic rubbers such as styrene-butadiene, plus nylon or polyester cords; tyre wear is a major microplastic source). All three.</li>
<li>India banned 19 single-use plastic items from 1 July 2022; carry bags must be at least 120 microns (December 2022); EPR for plastic packaging. Global plastics treaty talks (INC-5 at Busan 2024; Geneva 2025) ended without agreement; a short session in February 2026 chose a new chair. Biodegradable alternatives: PLA, PHA.</li>
<li><b>Bioasphalt</b> replaces fossil bitumen with <b>bio-based binders</b> made from <b>renewable</b> sources and <b>organic wastes</b> (vegetable oils, lignin, molasses, used cooking oil, algae); it is <b>eco-friendly</b> for road surfacing. It was given full customs duty exemption in Budget 2011–12. It is not made from non-renewable resources.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Rare earth elements and critical minerals</h3>
<ul>
<li>Rare earth elements are <b>17</b> elements (the 15 lanthanides plus scandium and yttrium), not 13. They are not rare in the crust but rarely concentrated enough to mine.</li>
<li>Uses: permanent magnets (neodymium, praseodymium, dysprosium, terbium) in EV motors, wind turbines, robots, drones, AI data centres, satellites and defence; <b>phosphors</b> in flat screens and monitors (europium, terbium, yttrium), because they are <b>phosphorescent/luminescent</b> (so II explains I); catalysts (cerium), lenses and batteries (lanthanum), fibre-optic amplifiers (erbium), MRI contrast (gadolinium).</li>
<li><b>China</b> dominates mining (about 60–70%) and processing (about 90%) and has <b>restricted exports</b> (2010 quotas; April and October 2025 controls; the October controls were suspended for a year to November 2026 after a US–China deal). Demand is rising fast with electronics.</li>
<li>Rare earths are found in <b>many countries</b>: the USA, Myanmar, Australia, Brazil, Vietnam, Russia, Greenland, South Africa and India. China leads mining, followed by the <b>USA</b> (Mountain Pass), Myanmar and Australia, not India. India has about 6.9 million tonnes of reserves (among the top five) but mines only about 1% of world output.</li>
<li>India: <b>IREL</b> (Department of Atomic Energy) mines monazite beach sands (Chavara in Kerala, Manavalakurichi in Tamil Nadu, OSCOM in Odisha), which also contain thorium, so private mining is restricted. <b>National Critical Mineral Mission</b> (January 2025; ₹16,300 crore plus ₹18,000 crore from PSUs, to 2030–31) for exploration, overseas assets, recycling and stockpiles. A ₹7,280 crore scheme for <b>sintered rare earth permanent magnets</b> (November 2025; 6,000 tonnes a year). India listed 30 critical minerals (2023); KABIL holds lithium blocks in Argentina.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Chemicals of concern in daily products</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Chemical</th><th>Where</th><th>Concern</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Triclosan</b></td><td><b>Toiletries</b>: soaps, hand washes, toothpastes, deodorants</td><td>Hormone disruption, antimicrobial resistance, toxic to algae. The US FDA banned it in consumer hand soaps (2016)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Lead</b></td><td><b>Lipstick</b> (pigments)</td><td>Neurotoxin</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Brominated vegetable oil</b></td><td><b>Soft drinks</b> (emulsifier for citrus flavours)</td><td>Bromine build-up; banned by the US FDA (2024); not permitted by FSSAI</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Monosodium glutamate</b></td><td><b>Chinese fast food</b> (flavour enhancer, umami)</td><td>Controversial, though safe in normal amounts; the 2015 Maggi case involved MSG labelling and lead</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parabens</td><td>Cosmetics</td><td>Oestrogen mimic</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phthalates</td><td>PVC, perfumes</td><td>Endocrine disruptors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Potassium bromate</td><td>Bread improver</td><td>Banned by FSSAI (2016)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calcium carbide</td><td>Illegal fruit ripening</td><td>Arsenic and phosphorus impurities; banned (ethylene is allowed)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Formalin, metanil yellow</td><td>Fish preservation; turmeric and sweets</td><td>Adulterants</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Mercury in fluorescent lamps</b>: when the lamp is on, <b>mercury vapour emits ultraviolet</b> radiation (mainly 254 nm), which the phosphor coating turns into visible light. Careless disposal causes mercury pollution.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_science_s3.js
// Science book, Unit 3: Biology: life and its diversity.
addBookTopic("science", "s3-1", `
<p>All living things are made of cells, carry their instructions in DNA, and have evolved from common ancestors. This topic covers the cell, the basics of inheritance and the course of evolution.</p>

<h3>The cell</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Plant cell</th><th>Animal cell</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cell wall</td><td><b>Cellulose</b></td><td>Absent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plasma membrane</td><td>Present (inside the wall)</td><td>Present</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plastids (chloroplasts)</td><td>Present</td><td>Absent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vacuole</td><td><b>One large central vacuole</b> (tonoplast; keeps the cell turgid)</td><td>Small, many or none</td></tr>
<tr><td>Centrioles</td><td>Absent in higher plants</td><td>Present</td></tr>
<tr><td>Food stored as</td><td>Starch</td><td>Glycogen</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lysosomes</td><td>Rare</td><td>Common</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>All cells have a plasma membrane, so "plant cells lack a plasma membrane" is wrong.</li>
<li>Cell walls: fungi have chitin, bacteria peptidoglycan. Robert Hooke named the cell (1665); cell theory came from Schleiden and Schwann (1838–39); Virchow added "every cell from a cell".</li>
<li><b>Protein synthesis</b> happens on <b>ribosomes</b>; those on the rough <b>endoplasmic reticulum</b> make proteins for export and membranes, which the ER folds and transports.</li>
<li>Besides the nucleus, <b>mitochondria</b> (and chloroplasts) contain their own circular DNA and 70S ribosomes, evidence that they descend from bacteria (endosymbiotic theory, Lynn Margulis).</li>
<li><b>Apoptosis</b> is programmed, gene-controlled cell death, carried out by caspase enzymes; it shapes embryos (separating fingers) and removes damaged cells. Nobel 2002 (Brenner, Horvitz, Sulston). For uncontrolled growth and metastasis, see 4.3.</li>
</ul>

<h3>DNA and inheritance</h3>
<ul>
<li>DNA suits storing and passing on information because of the <b>complementarity of its two strands</b>: A pairs with T, G with C, so each strand is a template for the other (semi-conservative replication, Meselson–Stahl 1958).</li>
<li><b>Pleiotropy</b>: one gene controlling two or more traits (the sickle-cell gene causes anaemia and gives malaria resistance; phenylketonuria). (Polygenic inheritance is the reverse: many genes, one trait.)</li>
<li><b>Sex determination</b>: women are XX, men XY. All eggs carry X; a sperm carries X or Y, so the <b>father decides the child's sex</b>. "Females determine sex" is false.</li>
<li><b>Colour blindness</b> is X-linked recessive. If a colour-blind woman marries a normal man, the <b>son is colour blind</b> (XcY) and the <b>daughter is a carrier but not affected</b> (XcX).</li>
<li><b>Blood group inheritance</b>: an AB parent passes A or B; an O parent passes O. So their biological children can only be A or B. If a couple of AB+ and O− have sons who are A+, B+ and O+, the <b>O+ son is the adopted one</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Evolution</h3>
<ul>
<li>Vertebrate order: fish → amphibians → reptiles → birds and mammals. So <b>shark → tortoise → otter</b>, and <b>salamander → python → kangaroo</b>.</li>
<li>The ultimate source of new heritable variation, and so of new species, is <b>mutation</b> (Hugo de Vries; UPSC key), though reproductive isolation is what actually splits species.</li>
<li>Lamarck's "<b>use and disuse</b>" was the textbook explanation (UPSC key) for <b>snakes losing their limbs</b>; modern biology explains it by natural selection (and changes in a Hox gene enhancer). Lamarckism is rejected today.</li>
<li>Drivers of evolution: natural selection (Darwin), mutation, genetic drift, gene flow, isolation; continental drift and ice ages (see the Environment book, 2.1).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Human evolution</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Species</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Australopithecus</td><td>"Lucy" (Ethiopia, 3.2 million years)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Homo habilis</td><td>First tool maker</td></tr>
<tr><td>Homo erectus</td><td>First to leave Africa; used fire</td></tr>
<tr><td>Neanderthals</td><td>Europe and West Asia; extinct about 40,000 years ago</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Denisovans</b></td><td>An <b>early (archaic) human species</b> of Asia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Homo floresiensis</td><td>"Hobbit", Indonesia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Homo sapiens</td><td>Africa, about 300,000 years ago</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Denisovans were identified in 2010 from DNA in a finger bone from Denisova Cave (Altai, Siberia). They interbred with Neanderthals and modern humans; their DNA survives in Melanesians, Papuans, Aboriginal Australians and some Asians. Tibetans' high-altitude gene EPAS1 came from them. A Tibetan jawbone (Xiahe, 2019) and a 2025 study linking the Harbin skull to Denisovans were in the news.</li>
<li>Svante Pääbo won the 2022 Nobel in Physiology or Medicine for sequencing the Neanderthal genome and discovering Denisovans. India: the Hathnora skull (Narmada valley).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s3-2", `
<p>Plants make their own food, fix nitrogen with the help of microbes, and can be propagated without seeds. UPSC asks about their processes, adaptations and useful products.</p>

<h3>Photosynthesis</h3>
<ul>
<li>Photosynthesis converts <b>light (free) energy into chemical potential energy</b> stored in glucose: 6CO2 + 6H2O + light → C6H12O6 + 6O2. The oxygen released comes from water.</li>
<li>Light reactions (thylakoids) split water and make ATP and NADPH; the Calvin cycle (stroma) fixes CO2 using RuBisCO.</li>
<li>Chlorophyll absorbs <b>blue and red</b> light most strongly and reflects green, so leaves look green.</li>
<li>C3 plants (rice, wheat), C4 plants (maize, sugarcane; efficient in heat), CAM plants (cacti, pineapple; stomata open at night).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Transport, growth forms and adaptations</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Girdling</b> (removing a ring of bark, including phloem) kills a tree because the <b>roots are starved of energy</b>: phloem carries sugars from leaves down to roots.</li>
<li>Many transplanted seedlings fail because <b>most root hairs are lost</b> in transplanting (transplant shock); good practice is to keep a soil ball, transplant in the evening, water well and prune leaves.</li>
<li>Fruits in cold storage last longer because their <b>rate of respiration is decreased</b> (and enzyme and microbial activity slow). Ethylene is the ripening hormone; calcium carbide ripening is banned. Gamma irradiation (BARC) also extends shelf life.</li>
<li><b>Desert plants</b> reduce water loss with <b>hard, waxy leaves</b>, <b>tiny or no leaves</b> and <b>thorns instead of leaves</b>; also sunken stomata, CAM, deep roots and succulent stems. Khejri (<i>Prosopis cineraria</i>) is Rajasthan's State tree, protected by the Bishnois.</li>
<li><b>Epiphytes</b> (orchids, ferns, mosses) depend on other plants only for <b>mechanical support</b>; they make their own food and are not parasites.</li>
<li><b>Insectivorous plants</b> (pitcher plant <i>Nepenthes</i>, sundew <i>Drosera</i>, bladderwort <i>Utricularia</i>, Venus flytrap) grow in <b>nitrogen-deficient</b> soils and digest insects for nitrogen. In the pitcher plant, the <b>leaf</b> is modified into the pitcher (its apex forms the lid).</li>
<li><b>Dichogamy</b>: in a bisexual flower the anthers and stigma mature at different times, preventing self-pollination (protandry, protogyny).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Growth forms</h4>
<ul>
<li>Herb: soft green stem (wheat, mint, ginger). Shrub: woody, branched from the base (rose, cassava). Tree: single woody trunk. Climbers need support; creepers spread on the ground. Annual, biennial (carrot), perennial.</li>
<li>Correct pairs (2025): <b>cassava</b> is a woody shrub; <b>ginger</b> is a herb with a <b>pseudostem</b> of leaf sheaths (its true stem is the rhizome); <b>Malabar spinach</b> is a herbaceous climber. Wrong: <b>mint</b> is a perennial herb, not an annual shrub; <b>papaya</b> is a giant herb with a soft hollow stem, not a woody shrub.</li>
<li>Modified stems: rhizome (ginger, turmeric), tuber (potato), corm (colocasia, saffron), bulb (onion), runner, stolon (mint, strawberry).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Nitrogen fixation and biofertilisers</h3>
<ul>
<li>Legumes (pea family, Fabaceae) carry <i>Rhizobium</i> bacteria in root nodules that fix atmospheric nitrogen using the enzyme <b>nitrogenase</b>, which works only without oxygen; pink <b>leghaemoglobin</b> mops up oxygen.</li>
<li><b>Groundnut, horse-gram and soybean</b> all belong to the pea family. Groundnut pods develop underground (geocarpy); horse-gram (kulthi) is a hardy dryland pulse.</li>
<li>Nitrogen-fixing plants: <b>alfalfa (lucerne), chickpea and clover (berseem)</b>. Not legumes: <b>amaranth, purslane (kulfa) and spinach</b>.</li>
<li>Other legumes: pulses (arhar, lentil, moong, urad, rajma), peas, fenugreek, tamarind, <i>Pongamia</i>, acacias. Non-legume symbiotic fixers: <i>Frankia</i> (Alnus, Casuarina).</li>
<li><b>Blue-green algae</b> (cyanobacteria such as <i>Anabaena</i> and <i>Nostoc</i>) are biofertilisers for <b>rice</b> because they <b>fix atmospheric nitrogen</b> in special cells (heterocysts); <i>Azolla</i> fern hosts <i>Anabaena</i>. They add about 20–30 kg of nitrogen per hectare in paddy.</li>
<li>Of <i>Agaricus</i>, <i>Nostoc</i> and <i>Spirogyra</i>, only <b><i>Nostoc</i></b> is a biofertiliser: <i>Agaricus</i> is the edible button mushroom; <i>Spirogyra</i> is a green alga that does not fix nitrogen.</li>
<li>Free-living fixers: <i>Azotobacter</i>, <i>Clostridium</i>, <i>Azospirillum</i>. <i>Nitrosomonas</i> converts ammonia to nitrite (nitrification), not fixation; <i>Agrobacterium</i> causes crown gall and is the vector of plant genetic engineering. Phosphate solubilisers: <i>Bacillus</i>, <i>Pseudomonas</i>.</li>
<li><b>Mycorrhiza</b> (fungus and root): helps plants <b>resist drought and increases absorptive area</b>, <b>tolerate extremes of pH</b> (and heavy metals) and <b>resist disease</b>, so it is used to rehabilitate degraded and mined land. Types: ectomycorrhiza (pines, oaks) and arbuscular endomycorrhiza (<i>Glomus</i>, most crops).</li>
<li>Schemes: Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana, National Mission on Natural Farming (2024), PM-PRANAM.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Propagation</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Vegetative propagation</b> produces a <b>clonal population</b> and can be practised <b>most of the year</b>. It does <b>not eliminate viruses</b>; it passes them on. Virus-free plants come from <b>meristem (shoot-tip) culture</b>.</li>
<li>Natural methods: runners (strawberry), rhizomes (ginger), tubers (potato), bulbs (onion), leaves (<i>Bryophyllum</i>). Artificial: <b>cuttings</b>, <b>layering</b>, <b>grafting and budding</b>, tissue culture (banana, orchids).</li>
<li><b>Sweet orange</b> is propagated by <b>grafting</b> (onto hardy rootstock); <b>jasmine</b> by <b>layering</b>.</li>
<li><b>Bougainvillea, carnations, cocoa and grapes</b> can all be propagated by <b>stem cuttings</b> (cocoa is more often seeded or grafted).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Plant diseases</h3>
<ul>
<li>Plant diseases are spread by <b>birds, dust, rain and wind</b> (all four), as well as insects (aphids, whiteflies).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Disease</th><th>Pathogen and crop</th></tr>
<tr><td>Late blight</td><td><i>Phytophthora</i>, potato; caused the Irish famine (1845)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rusts (black, brown, yellow)</td><td>Fungi, wheat (Ug99 stem rust)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blast</td><td>Fungus, rice; wheat blast reached Bangladesh (2016)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Red rot</td><td>Fungus, sugarcane</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bacterial leaf blight</td><td>Bacterium, rice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tobacco mosaic</td><td>Virus</td></tr>
</table>
<p>India regulates imports under the Destructive Insects and Pests Act 1914 and the Plant Quarantine Order 2003.</p>

<h3>Useful plants and products</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Neem</b>: its oil (azadirachtin) is a <b>pesticide</b> against some insects and mites and has <b>pharmaceutical uses</b> (skin care, toothpastes, soaps). UPSC did not accept neem seeds as a source of biofuel and hospital detergents. India won the neem patent case at the European Patent Office (2000; Vandana Shiva and others challenged W.R. Grace and USDA), a landmark biopiracy case, as were turmeric (USA, 1997) and basmati; the <b>Traditional Knowledge Digital Library</b> (2001) now blocks such patents.</li>
<li><b>Maize</b> ("queen of cereals", a C4 plant) is used for <b>starch</b>, its <b>oil as biodiesel feedstock</b>, and <b>alcoholic beverages</b> (and ethanol). Poultry feed is its largest use; it became India's biggest ethanol feedstock as E20 was reached in 2025.</li>
<li><b>Camphor</b> (camphor laurel tree), <b>chicory</b> (roasted root blended with coffee) and <b>vanilla</b> (cured pods of the vanilla orchid; second most expensive spice) are all plant products.</li>
<li><b>Saffron</b> is the dried <b>stigma</b> (with style) of <i>Crocus sativus</i>; about 1.5 lakh flowers make 1 kg.</li>
<li>A <b>phytotron</b> is a facility to <b>grow plants under controlled conditions</b> (temperature, light, humidity, CO2); India has one at IARI, New Delhi.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s3-3", `
<p>Animals are classified by body plan into phyla and classes. UPSC asks where a given animal belongs, how it breathes or reproduces, and unusual behaviours.</p>

<h3>Classification</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Group</th><th>Features</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Flatworms (Platyhelminthes)</td><td>Often hermaphrodite</td><td><b>Tapeworm</b> (hermaphrodite), liver fluke</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roundworms (Nematoda)</td><td><b>Separate sexes</b></td><td><i>Ascaris</i>, <b>filarial worm</b> (<i>Wuchereria</i>, causing filariasis, spread by Culex), <b>guinea worm</b> (a nematode, not an annelid)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Annelids</td><td>Segmented worms</td><td>Earthworms, leeches</td></tr>
<tr><td>Molluscs</td><td>Soft body, mantle</td><td>Snails, oysters, <b>octopus</b>, squid, cuttlefish (cephalopods)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Echinoderms</td><td>Mostly external fertilisation, oviparous with free larvae (so "all echinoderms are viviparous" is wrong)</td><td>Starfish, sea urchins, sea cucumbers</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Arthropods (the largest phylum)</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Features</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Insecta</b></td><td>3 body parts, 6 legs, 1 pair of antennae, usually wings</td><td>Bees, ants, butterflies; the <b>cicada, froghopper and pond skater</b> (all Hemiptera, "true bugs")</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Arachnida</b></td><td>8 legs, 2 body parts, no antennae</td><td>Spiders, scorpions, <b>mites and ticks</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Crustacea</b></td><td>Mostly aquatic, 2 pairs of antennae, gills</td><td><b>Crab</b>, prawn, lobster, barnacle, woodlouse</td></tr>
<tr><td>Myriapoda</td><td>Many legs</td><td>Centipedes, millipedes</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Of crab, mite, scorpion and spider, the <b>crab</b> is the odd one out (a crustacean; the others are arachnids).</li>
<li>Insect orders: Coleoptera (beetles, the largest), Lepidoptera (butterflies, moths), Hymenoptera (bees, wasps, ants), Diptera (flies, mosquitoes), Hemiptera (bugs, cicadas, aphids), Orthoptera (grasshoppers, locusts). Periodical cicadas emerge every 13 or 17 years; in 2024 two broods emerged together for the first time since 1803. Pond skaters walk on water using surface tension.</li>
<li>Most <b>insects respire through a tracheal system</b>: air tubes opening through spiracles carry oxygen directly to tissues.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Vertebrates and mammals</h4>
<ul>
<li>Roundworms have <b>no circulatory system</b> (body-cavity fluid moves materials). Among fishes, <b>bony fishes usually have a swim bladder</b> for buoyancy; <b>cartilaginous fishes</b> (sharks, rays) lack one and have <b>internal fertilisation</b>.</li>
<li>Insect metamorphosis: egg → larva → <b>pupa</b> → adult (<b>imago</b>).</li>
<li>Mammals: <b>monotremes</b> lay eggs (<b>echidna</b>, platypus); marsupials (pouch); placentals.</li>
<li>Marine mammals: <b>sea cow</b> (dugong, manatee) and <b>sea lion</b> are mammals; the <b>sea horse is a fish</b> (the male carries the eggs). Others: whales, dolphins, porpoises, seals, walruses, sea otters. Misnamed animals: starfish (echinoderm), jellyfish (cnidarian), silverfish (insect), cuttlefish (mollusc).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Reproduction and life cycles</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Amoeba</b> reproduces by binary fission, but <b>not all unicellular organisms are asexual</b>: <i>Paramecium</i> (conjugation), <i>Chlamydomonas</i>, yeast and <i>Plasmodium</i> also reproduce sexually.</li>
<li>The <b>silkworm</b> larva spins the cocoon, but the commercial fibre is harvested at the <b>pupa</b> (cocoon) stage, when cocoons are boiled and reeled.</li>
<li><b>Sexual cannibalism</b> (the female killing or eating the male after mating) is found in <b>spiders</b> (black widow, some orb-weavers) and praying mantises, not honeybees (the drone dies from mating, but the queen does not kill it).</li>
<li>The <b>wolf</b> is monogamous: a breeding pair leads a pack of its own offspring.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Behaviour</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Ants</b> (leafcutter ants of the Americas) are well-known <b>cultivators of fungi</b>: they grow fungus gardens on chewed leaves and eat the fungus, using antibiotic-producing bacteria to protect it; this farming began about 60 million years ago. Fungus-growing termites (also in India; <i>Termitomyces</i> mushrooms are eaten) and ambrosia beetles also farm fungi; ants also "herd" aphids.</li>
<li>A <b>chameleon's eyes move independently</b>, so it can look ahead with one and behind with the other.</li>
<li><i>Spirogyra</i> and <i>Ulothrix</i> are <b>green</b> algae (not blue-green) and live mainly in fresh water.</li>
<li>For animal behaviour more broadly (waggle dance, tool use, hibernation, defences), see the Environment book, 2.2.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s3-4", `
<p>Microorganisms (bacteria, archaea, fungi, protists and viruses) are everywhere. They cause diseases, but also make soil fertile, ferment food, produce medicines and clean up pollution.</p>

<h3>Microbes compared</h3>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Bacteria</th><th>Fungi</th><th>Viruses</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cell type</td><td>Prokaryote</td><td>Eukaryote</td><td>Not a cell</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wall</td><td>Peptidoglycan</td><td>Chitin</td><td>Protein capsid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Genome</td><td>DNA</td><td>DNA</td><td>DNA <b>or</b> RNA</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grown on artificial media?</td><td><b>Yes</b> (nutrient agar)</td><td><b>Yes</b> (Sabouraud agar)</td><td><b>No</b>: only in living cells (cell cultures, embryonated eggs, animals)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Treated by</td><td>Antibiotics</td><td>Antifungals</td><td>Antivirals, vaccines</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Exceptions: some bacteria cannot be grown on artificial media (<i>Mycobacterium leprae</i>, grown in armadillos; <i>Treponema</i>).</p>

<h3>Viruses</h3>
<ul>
<li>Viruses are obligate intracellular parasites: a DNA or RNA genome in a protein coat, sometimes with a lipid envelope. They <b>lack the enzymes to generate energy</b> and have no ribosomes; they <b>cannot be cultured in synthetic media</b>. They spread by <b>many routes</b> (air, water, food, contact, body fluids), not only by biological vectors. Antibiotics do not work on them.</li>
<li>Viruses infect <b>all forms of life</b>: <b>bacteria</b> (bacteriophages), <b>fungi</b> (mycoviruses), <b>plants</b> (tobacco mosaic virus, the first discovered, 1892), animals, archaea; even viruses can be parasitised by virophages.</li>
<li>The 2025 statements were all false: viruses <b>are abundant in oceans</b> (about 10 million per mL; they kill 20–40% of marine bacteria daily, the "viral shunt"), phages <b>do infect bacteria</b> (phage therapy against resistant bacteria; phages gave biology CRISPR), and viruses <b>do change host transcription</b> (that is how they replicate; HIV, HPV, EBV alter host gene expression).</li>
<li>History: Ivanovsky discovered TMV; Beijerinck coined "virus"; W.M. Stanley crystallised TMV (1935). Giant viruses (Mimivirus) blur the line with cells.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Genome</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Double-stranded DNA</td><td><b>Adenovirus</b>, herpes, smallpox, mpox, HPV, hepatitis B (partly)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Single-stranded DNA</td><td>Parvovirus</td></tr>
<tr><td>(+) single-stranded RNA</td><td>Coronaviruses (SARS-CoV-2), polio, dengue, Zika, hepatitis C</td></tr>
<tr><td>(−) single-stranded RNA</td><td>Influenza, measles, rabies, Ebola, Nipah</td></tr>
<tr><td>Double-stranded RNA</td><td>Rotavirus</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Retroviruses</b> (single-stranded RNA, copied to DNA by reverse transcriptase)</td><td><b>HIV</b> (AIDS), HTLV</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>So adenoviruses have <b>double-stranded DNA</b> and retroviruses <b>single-stranded RNA</b> (the 2021 statement reversed them). <b>Some colds are caused by adenoviruses</b> (most by rhinoviruses), and <b>AIDS by a retrovirus</b>. Adenoviruses serve as vaccine and gene therapy vectors (Covishield); reverse transcriptase is the target of antiretroviral drugs.</li>
<li><b>Viroids</b> are naked, circular <b>single-stranded RNA</b> with no protein coat (T.O. Diener, 1971); they cause plant diseases such as potato spindle tuber. <b>Prions</b> are misfolded proteins (mad cow disease, Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease; Stanley Prusiner). Mycoplasma are bacteria without cell walls; rickettsia are tiny intracellular bacteria (typhus).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fungi</h3>
<ul>
<li>A separate kingdom of heterotrophs with chitin walls: decomposers, parasites and mutualists (mycorrhiza with about 90% of plants; lichens).</li>
<li>Some mushrooms are <b>medicinal</b> (reishi, shiitake, Cordyceps or "keeda jadi", turkey tail), some <b>psychoactive</b> (<i>Psilocybe</i>, fly agaric), some <b>insecticidal</b> (<i>Beauveria bassiana</i>, <i>Metarhizium</i>, used as biopesticides) and some <b>bioluminescent</b> (<i>Mycena</i>; glowing mushrooms in Goa's Mhadei sanctuary and Meghalaya's <i>Roridomyces phyllostachydis</i>, 2018). All four.</li>
<li>Uses: antibiotics (penicillin from <i>Penicillium</i>), fermentation (yeast), cyclosporin. Edible: button (<i>Agaricus</i>), oyster, paddy-straw, morels (gucchi). Directorate of Mushroom Research, Solan.</li>
<li>Diseases: athlete's foot, ringworm, black fungus (mucormycosis), crop rusts and smuts.</li>
<li><b>Aflatoxins</b> are produced by <b>moulds</b> (<i>Aspergillus flavus</i>, <i>A. parasiticus</i>) on badly stored groundnuts, maize, cottonseed and chillies. They are heat-stable (cooking does not destroy them) and aflatoxin B1 is a potent liver carcinogen. FSSAI sets limits; drying grain below about 10% moisture prevents them. Other mycotoxins: ergot, ochratoxin, fumonisin.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Extremophiles</h3>
<p>Some microorganisms grow <b>above the boiling point of water</b> (deep-sea vent microbes at up to 122°C under pressure), <b>below freezing</b> (in sea ice and permafrost) and in <b>highly acidic</b> environments below pH 3 (<i>Picrophilus</i> near pH 0). All three.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Condition</th><th>Example or use</th></tr>
<tr><td>Thermophile</td><td>Heat</td><td><i>Thermus aquaticus</i> → Taq polymerase for PCR (Kary Mullis, Nobel 1993)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Psychrophile</td><td>Cold</td><td>Cold-active enzymes in detergents</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acidophile</td><td>Low pH</td><td><i>Acidithiobacillus</i>: bioleaching of copper and gold; acid mine drainage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Halophile</td><td>Salt</td><td>Salt pans, Dead Sea</td></tr>
<tr><td>Piezophile</td><td>Pressure</td><td>Mariana Trench</td></tr>
<tr><td>Radioresistant</td><td>Radiation</td><td><i>Deinococcus radiodurans</i></td></tr>
</table>
<p>Many extremophiles are Archaea (Carl Woese's third domain). Tardigrades are extremophile animals (sent on Axiom-4, 2025, in an Indian experiment). Extremophiles matter for astrobiology (Mars, Europa, Enceladus).</p>

<h3>Biofilms</h3>
<ul>
<li>A biofilm is a community of microbes on a surface wrapped in a slimy self-made matrix. Biofilms <b>form on medical implants</b> (catheters, heart valves, joint replacements), on <b>food and food-processing surfaces</b> (<i>Listeria</i>, <i>Salmonella</i>) and show <b>antibiotic resistance</b> (10–1,000 times more). All three.</li>
<li>Examples: dental plaque, ship-hull fouling. Cells communicate by quorum sensing. Useful biofilms: wastewater treatment, bioremediation.</li>
<li>For antimicrobial resistance, see 4.3.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Probiotics and useful microbes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Probiotics</b> are live microorganisms that benefit the host: mostly bacteria (<i>Lactobacillus</i>, <i>Bifidobacterium</i>) and some <b>yeasts</b> (<i>Saccharomyces boulardii</i>). They <b>help maintain gut flora</b> and <b>help digest milk sugar</b> (lactase). Many probiotic species <b>do occur naturally in our gut</b>, so that statement was wrong.</li>
<li>Prebiotics are fibres that feed good bacteria (inulin); synbiotics combine both. Sources: curd, buttermilk, idli batter, kimchi, kombucha. FSSAI regulates probiotic foods. The gut microbiome is linked to obesity, diabetes and the gut–brain axis.</li>
<li><b>Oil-eating bacteria</b>: <b><i>Pseudomonas</i></b> engineered by Ananda Mohan Chakrabarty to degrade hydrocarbons; Diamond v. Chakrabarty (1980) was the first US patent on a living organism. India's Oilzapper is a bacterial consortium for oil spills.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_science_s4.js
// Science book, Unit 4: The human body and health.
addBookTopic("science", "s4-1", `
<p>The human body is a set of organ systems that work together and are coordinated by nerves and hormones. UPSC asks where things happen, which organ does what, and how hormones control the body.</p>

<h3>Digestive system</h3>
<p>Digestion is the enzymatic <b>breakdown of large food molecules into absorbable units</b>: proteins into amino acids, carbohydrates into simple sugars, and fats into fatty acids and glycerol.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Site</th><th>Secretion</th><th>Action</th></tr>
<tr><td>Mouth</td><td>Saliva: <b>ptyalin</b> (salivary amylase)</td><td>Starts digesting <b>starch</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Stomach</td><td>HCl, <b>pepsin</b> (from pepsinogen, activated by acid)</td><td>Starts digesting <b>proteins</b> into peptides</td></tr>
<tr><td>Liver → gall bladder</td><td><b>Bile</b>: bile salts, <b>no enzymes</b></td><td>Emulsifies fats into tiny droplets; helps absorb fats and vitamins A, D, E, K</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pancreas</td><td>Trypsin(ogen), chymotrypsin, carboxypeptidase, <b>pancreatic lipase</b>, amylase, bicarbonate</td><td>Proteins, fats (main source of lipase), starch; neutralises acid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Small intestine</td><td>Peptidases, maltase, lactase, sucrase</td><td>Completes digestion; absorption through villi</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>liver has an important role in fat digestion</b> because of bile, but it <b>produces no fat-digesting enzymes</b>. Lipase comes mainly from the pancreas (minor lingual and gastric lipases).</li>
<li>Protein digestion <b>begins in the stomach</b>, so "all proteins are digested in the small intestine only" is false, although the pancreas does release protein-digesting enzymes into the small intestine.</li>
<li>The <b>common bile duct</b> opens into the <b>duodenum</b>, not the stomach. It usually joins the <b>pancreatic duct</b> (which also empties into the duodenum) at the ampulla of Vater, guarded by the sphincter of Oddi.</li>
<li>Small intestine, by decreasing length: <b>ileum</b> (about 3–3.5 m; absorbs vitamin B12 and bile salts; Peyer's patches) → <b>jejunum</b> (about 2–2.5 m; main nutrient absorption) → <b>duodenum</b> (about 25 cm, C-shaped; receives bile and pancreatic juice; iron absorption).</li>
<li>The <b>appendix</b> hangs from the caecum, the first part of the <b>large intestine</b>.</li>
<li>The <b>hepatic portal vein</b> carries nutrient-rich blood from the stomach, intestines, spleen and pancreas to the liver (about 75% of the liver's blood) for processing before it reaches the rest of the body.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Gut hormones</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Gastrin</b> is a <b>hormone</b>, not an enzyme: made in the stomach lining, it stimulates secretion of HCl and pepsinogen.</li>
<li><b>Secretin</b> (the first hormone discovered, Bayliss and Starling, 1902) is released by the <b>duodenum</b> when <b>acid</b> arrives; it makes the pancreas release bicarbonate.</li>
<li><b>Enterogastrone</b> (now understood to include GIP and CCK) is produced in the small intestine in response to <b>fatty acids</b>; it slows stomach emptying and acid secretion. CCK also releases bile and pancreatic enzymes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The liver</h3>
<ul>
<li>The largest gland. It makes <b>bile</b> and <b>urea</b> (it turns toxic ammonia from amino acids into urea through the ornithine cycle; kidneys excrete it).</li>
<li>Other roles: stores glycogen, iron and vitamins A, D and B12; detoxifies drugs and alcohol; makes plasma proteins (albumin) and clotting factors; can regenerate itself.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Blood and circulation</h3>
<ul>
<li>Blood <b>pH is 7.35–7.45</b> (slightly alkaline), held by buffers (bicarbonate), the lungs and the kidneys.</li>
<li>Counts per microlitre: red cells about 45–55 lakh; <b>platelets about 1.5–4.5 lakh</b>; white cells only 4,000–11,000. So there are <b>more platelets than white cells</b>.</li>
<li>All blood cells, including <b>lymphocytes</b>, form in the <b>red bone marrow</b> (ends of long bones, flat bones, vertebrae). B cells mature in the marrow, T cells in the thymus.</li>
<li><b>Carbon monoxide</b> binds haemoglobin about <b>200–250 times more strongly</b> than oxygen, forming carboxyhaemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen. Treatment is oxygen (hyperbaric oxygen in severe cases).</li>
<li><b>Nitric oxide</b> (NO) is made in the body (by endothelial cells, from L-arginine) and <b>dilates blood vessels</b>, increasing blood flow; Nobel 1998 (Furchgott, Ignarro, Murad). Nitroglycerine for angina works by releasing NO. Do not confuse it with:
<ul><li><b>Nitrous oxide</b> (N2O): "laughing gas", an anaesthetic; a strong greenhouse gas and now the largest ozone-depleting emission.</li>
<li><b>Nitrogen dioxide</b> (NO2): a reddish-brown pollutant that causes acid rain and smog; it is in the Air Quality Index.</li>
<li><b>Dinitrogen pentoxide</b> (N2O5): the anhydride of nitric acid.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
<h4>Blood groups</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Group</th><th>Antigens on red cells</th><th>Antibodies in plasma</th></tr>
<tr><td>A</td><td>A</td><td>Anti-B</td></tr>
<tr><td>B</td><td>B</td><td>Anti-A</td></tr>
<tr><td>AB</td><td>A and B</td><td>None (<b>universal recipient</b>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>O</td><td>None</td><td>Anti-A and anti-B</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>O Rh-negative</b> is the <b>universal donor</b>: it has no A, B or D (Rh) antigens. For an emergency patient of unknown group, O negative is safe; O positive could sensitise an Rh-negative recipient.</li>
<li>About 85% of people worldwide (over 90% in India) are <b>Rh positive</b>, not 50%. Rh incompatibility between an Rh-negative mother and an Rh-positive foetus can cause haemolytic disease of the newborn (prevented with anti-D injection).</li>
<li>Landsteiner discovered ABO (1901). Bombay blood group (hh), found in Mumbai in 1952, is very rare.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Kidneys</h3>
<ul>
<li>Blood enters the kidney by the <b>renal artery</b> and filtered blood leaves by the <b>renal vein</b>.</li>
<li>In the nephron, blood is filtered at the glomerulus into Bowman's capsule. In a healthy person <b>all</b> the filtered glucose is reabsorbed (mostly in the proximal tubule) into the capillaries around the tubules. That is why the kidney statements ("cleaner blood leaves through the renal artery"; "much of the glucose is reabsorbed") were both marked wrong.</li>
<li>About 75–80% of <b>kidney stones are calcium oxalate</b>; others are calcium phosphate, uric acid, struvite and cystine.</li>
<li><b>Renin</b> (from the kidney) converts angiotensinogen into angiotensin, which raises blood pressure. (Rennin, a different enzyme, curdles milk in calves.)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Hormones</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Gland</th><th>Hormone</th><th>Role</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pituitary (anterior)</td><td><b>Growth hormone</b>, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, prolactin</td><td>"Master gland"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pituitary (posterior)</td><td><b>Vasopressin (ADH)</b>, <b>oxytocin</b></td><td>ADH: water reabsorption in kidneys. Oxytocin: <b>contracts smooth muscle</b> of the uterus (labour) and breast (milk release)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thyroid</td><td>Thyroxine, calcitonin</td><td>Metabolism; calcitonin lowers blood calcium</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Parathyroid</b> (four, behind the thyroid)</td><td><b>PTH</b></td><td><b>Raises blood calcium and lowers phosphate</b> (from bone, kidney reabsorption, activates vitamin D)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pancreas (islets of Langerhans)</td><td><b>Insulin</b> (beta cells), glucagon (alpha cells)</td><td>Insulin lowers blood glucose</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adrenal cortex</td><td><b>Cortisol and cortisone</b>, aldosterone</td><td>Stress, metabolism, salt balance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adrenal medulla</td><td>Adrenaline</td><td>"Fight or flight"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gonads</td><td><b>Progesterone</b> and oestrogen (ovary); testosterone (testis)</td><td>Reproduction</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Insulin</b> is a <b>peptide</b> (small protein) of 51 amino acids in two chains joined by disulphide bridges (Banting and Best, 1921; Sanger sequenced it). Recombinant insulin was the first genetically engineered drug.</li>
<li><b>Blood glucose</b> is expressed in <b>milligrams per decilitre</b> (mg/dL) in India and the USA: fasting normal is about 70–99; 126 or more means diabetes. Elsewhere it is mmol/L (1 mmol/L is about 18 mg/dL). HbA1c shows average sugar over about three months.</li>
<li><b>Diabetes insipidus</b> comes from a <b>deficiency</b> of vasopressin (or kidneys not responding to it), not an excess. It causes a lot of dilute urine and great <b>thirst</b>.</li>
<li><b>Alcohol suppresses vasopressin</b>, so the kidneys reabsorb less water and urination increases.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Nervous system, bones and muscles</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Medulla oblongata</b> (lowest part of the brainstem) controls involuntary reflexes: <b>swallowing, vomiting</b>, coughing, sneezing, breathing, heart rate and blood pressure. The cerebellum controls balance and coordination; the cerebrum controls thought, memory and voluntary action; the hypothalamus controls temperature, hunger and the pituitary.</li>
<li>Bones: the <b>femur</b> is the longest and strongest; the <b>stapes</b> (ear) is the smallest. An adult has 206 bones.</li>
<li>Common names: <b>breastbone = sternum</b>, <b>collarbone = clavicle</b>, <b>kneecap = patella</b>, <b>shoulder blade = scapula</b>.</li>
<li><b>Muscle fatigue</b> after hard work is classically put down to <b>lactic acid</b>, which builds up when muscles run short of oxygen and switch to anaerobic glycolysis.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Eyes and reproduction</h3>
<ul>
<li>In <b>eye donation</b>, the <b>cornea</b> is transplanted (keratoplasty). The cornea has no blood vessels, so rejection is uncommon.</li>
<li>Fertilisation normally takes place in the <b>fallopian tube</b> (ampulla). The embryo implants in the uterus about a week later.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s4-2", `
<p>Nutrients are carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, water and fibre. UPSC asks about deficiency diseases, which nutrients are essential and which foods supply them.</p>

<h3>Vitamins</h3>
<p><b>Fat-soluble</b>: A, D, E, K (stored in the body; excess can be toxic). <b>Water-soluble</b>: B group and C (not stored; daily intake needed).</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Vitamin</th><th>Deficiency</th><th>Sources</th></tr>
<tr><td>A (retinol)</td><td><b>Night blindness</b>, dry and scaly skin, xerophthalmia (dry eyes, <b>tear glands stop working</b>), Bitot's spots</td><td>Carrots, green leaves, milk, fish oil</td></tr>
<tr><td>B1 (thiamine)</td><td><b>Beriberi</b>: loss of appetite and <b>indigestion</b>, nerve damage, <b>enlarged heart</b> (wet beriberi)</td><td>Whole grains (lost in polishing rice)</td></tr>
<tr><td>B2 (riboflavin)</td><td>Cracked lips and mouth corners</td><td><b>Milk</b>, eggs</td></tr>
<tr><td>B3 (niacin)</td><td>Pellagra</td><td>Meat, groundnuts</td></tr>
<tr><td>B9 (folic acid)</td><td>Megaloblastic anaemia; neural tube defects in babies</td><td>Green leaves</td></tr>
<tr><td>B12 (cobalamin)</td><td>Pernicious anaemia</td><td>Animal foods, <b>milk</b> (made only by bacteria)</td></tr>
<tr><td>C (ascorbic acid)</td><td><b>Scurvy</b>: bleeding gums, poor wound healing, joint (and some muscle) pain</td><td>Citrus, amla, guava</td></tr>
<tr><td>D (calciferol)</td><td><b>Rickets</b> (children), osteomalacia (adults); poor calcium <b>absorption</b> from the gut</td><td>Sunlight on skin, fish, fortified milk</td></tr>
<tr><td>E (tocopherol)</td><td>Nerve and muscle problems, red-cell breakdown; infertility in animals (<b>not night blindness</b>)</td><td>Vegetable oils, nuts</td></tr>
<tr><td>K</td><td>Delayed blood clotting</td><td>Green leaves, gut bacteria</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Milk is a good source of B vitamins</b> (especially B2 and B12), so "milk contains none of the B vitamins" is false. Milk is poor in iron and vitamin C.</li>
<li>Vitamin D deficiency lowers calcium absorption, so urine calcium tends to <b>fall</b>, not rise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Minerals</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Muscle contraction</b> needs <b>calcium</b> (released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, it binds troponin so actin and myosin interact), <b>sodium</b> (with potassium, carries the nerve impulse) and, per UPSC's key, <b>iron</b> (myoglobin and haemoglobin supply oxygen to working muscle). Potassium handles repolarisation (deficiency causes cramps); magnesium helps relaxation; ATP powers the myosin heads.</li>
<li>Calcium sources (mg per 100 g, approximate): <b>ragi</b> about 340–350 (highest among cereals), <b>skimmed milk</b> about 120 (removing fat does not remove calcium), <b>egg</b> about 50–60, <b>rice</b> only about 10. Rice is not a good source of calcium.</li>
<li>Iron: haemoglobin. About 57% of Indian women aged 15–49 are anaemic (NFHS-5); Anaemia Mukt Bharat. Iodine: thyroid (goitre); universal salt iodisation. Zinc: immunity. Fluoride: teeth (excess causes fluorosis).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fats</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Saturated fats</b> have <b>only single bonds</b> between carbon atoms (mostly solid: ghee, butter, coconut oil). <b>Unsaturated fats</b> have one (mono) or more (poly) C=C double bonds, which makes them <b>more reactive</b>: they oxidise (go rancid) and take up hydrogen.</li>
<li><b>Trans fats</b> come mainly from <b>partial hydrogenation</b> of vegetable oils (vanaspati). They raise LDL and lower HDL, harming the heart. A "no trans fat" label says the product is not made with hydrogenated oil and is better for the heart; it says <b>nothing about animal fats</b> (ruminant fats in milk and meat contain small natural trans fats). FSSAI limits industrial trans fat to 2% of total fat (since 2022); WHO's REPLACE package aims to eliminate it.</li>
<li><b>Fatty acids</b> belong in a balanced diet (energy, fat-soluble vitamins), but the body <b>can synthesise most fatty acids</b>. Only the <b>essential fatty acids</b> must come from food: linoleic acid (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Proteins and antioxidants</h3>
<ul>
<li>Of the 20 amino acids, nine are <b>essential</b> and cannot be made by the body: histidine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, valine. <b>Glycine, serine and tyrosine are non-essential</b> (tyrosine is made from phenylalanine), so they need not be in the diet. Cereals are low in lysine and pulses in methionine, so dal-chawal complement each other.</li>
<li>Protein deficiency: kwashiorkor (protein) and marasmus (protein and energy).</li>
<li><b>Antioxidants</b> (vitamins C and E, beta-carotene, flavonoids, polyphenols) <b>neutralise free radicals</b> produced during metabolism, which otherwise damage DNA, proteins and membranes (oxidative stress).</li>
<li>For food fortification and nutrition schemes (POSHAN Abhiyaan, fortified rice), see the Economy book.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s4-3", `
<p>Diseases may be infectious (caused by pathogens) or non-infectious (genetic, hormonal, deficiency, occupational, cancer). The immune system defends the body, and vaccines train it in advance.</p>

<h3>Immunity</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Innate immunity</b> is non-specific and has no memory: skin, mucus, neutrophils, macrophages, natural killer cells, fever, complement.</li>
<li><b>Adaptive immunity</b> uses lymphocytes that recognise specific <b>antigens</b> and remember them, so <b>B and T cells protect the body from diseases caused by pathogens</b>.
<ul><li><b>B cells</b> (mature in bone marrow) make antibodies (humoral immunity); some become memory cells.</li>
<li><b>T cells</b> (mature in the thymus) give cell-mediated immunity. Helper T cells (CD4) coordinate the response; cytotoxic T cells (CD8) kill infected and cancer cells; regulatory T cells prevent autoimmunity (Nobel 2025: Brunkow, Ramsdell, Sakaguchi).</li></ul></li>
<li>An <b>antigen</b> is any substance (usually a protein or polysaccharide) that the body sees as foreign and that <b>stimulates antibody formation</b>.</li>
<li><b>ELISA</b> detects antibodies or antigens. It is the standard first screening test for <b>HIV</b>, and is used for hepatitis, dengue and COVID. It is <b>not</b> the basic test for cancer (diagnosed by biopsy, imaging and tumour markers).</li>
<li><b>CAR-T therapy</b> engineers a patient's T cells to attack cancer. India's first indigenous CAR-T therapy, NexCAR19 (IIT Bombay, Tata Memorial, ImmunoACT), was approved in 2023 at a fraction of the global cost.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Vaccines</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Platform</th><th>How it works</th><th>COVID-19 examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Inactivated</td><td>Killed whole virus</td><td><b>Covaxin</b> (Bharat Biotech with ICMR–NIV), Sinovac, Sinopharm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Viral vector</td><td>A harmless virus carries the spike gene</td><td><b>Covishield</b> (Oxford–AstraZeneca chimpanzee adenovirus ChAdOx1, made by Serum Institute; <b>not mRNA</b>), <b>Sputnik V</b> (human adenoviruses Ad26 and Ad5), J&J, iNCOVACC (Bharat, nasal)</td></tr>
<tr><td>mRNA</td><td>mRNA in lipid nanoparticles makes cells produce spike</td><td>Pfizer–BioNTech, Moderna, Gemcovac (Gennova, India)</td></tr>
<tr><td>DNA</td><td>Plasmid DNA codes for spike</td><td>ZyCoV-D (Zydus): world's first human DNA vaccine, needle-free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protein subunit</td><td>Purified spike pieces</td><td>Corbevax (Biological E), Covovax (Serum/Novavax)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Recombinant vector vaccines</b> use <b>genetic engineering</b> to insert an antigen gene into a harmless carrier, and the vectors can be <b>viruses</b> (adenovirus, vaccinia, measles) or <b>bacteria</b> (attenuated Salmonella, BCG, Listeria).</li>
<li>Nobel 2023: Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman, for the base modifications that made mRNA vaccines possible. India's drive began on 16 January 2021 and crossed 220 crore doses; Vaccine Maitri sent doses abroad.</li>
<li><b>Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine</b> (PCV) protects against <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>, which causes <b>pneumonia, meningitis and sepsis</b>; fewer infections mean <b>less antibiotic use and less resistance</b>. No vaccine is free of side effects. PCV entered the Universal Immunisation Programme in 2017 and went nationwide in 2021; India's first indigenous PCV is Pneumosil (Serum Institute, 2020). "Conjugate" means a polysaccharide is linked to a carrier protein so infants respond (Hib too). SAANS campaign (2019) against childhood pneumonia.</li>
<li>A <b>malaria vaccine is hard to make</b> because malaria is caused by <b>several species of <i>Plasmodium</i></b> (falciparum, vivax, malariae, ovale, knowlesi), each with many life stages and changing antigens. Humans are only the intermediate host; the mosquito is the definitive host (sexual stage). Vaccines now exist: RTS,S/AS01 and R21/Matrix-M (made by Serum Institute); India's ICMR is developing AdFalciVax (2025), a multi-stage vaccine.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Universal Immunisation Programme</h4>
<ul>
<li>UIP (1985) now covers 12 diseases: TB, diphtheria, pertussis, tetanus, polio, measles, rubella, hepatitis B, Hib, rotavirus diarrhoea, pneumococcal pneumonia, Japanese encephalitis (endemic districts). Pentavalent = DPT + hepatitis B + Hib.</li>
<li><b>Mission Indradhanush</b> (December 2014) aims at full <b>immunisation of children under two and pregnant women</b>, targeting left-out areas; the seven rainbow colours stood for the original seven diseases. Intensified rounds followed (IMI 5.0 in 2023). The U-WIN portal (2024) keeps digital records; an HPV vaccine for girls was announced in 2024.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Diseases by cause</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Cause</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Virus</td><td>Dengue, Zika, chikungunya, influenza, hepatitis, HIV/AIDS, measles, chickenpox, COVID-19; <b>foot-and-mouth disease</b> of cloven-hoofed animals</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bacterium</td><td><b>Cholera</b> (<i>Vibrio cholerae</i>; treated with ORS), TB, typhoid, <b>meningococcal meningitis</b>, leprosy, diphtheria</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fungus</td><td><b>Athlete's foot</b> and <b>ringworm</b> (tinea; dermatophytes like <i>Trichophyton</i>), mucormycosis</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protozoan</td><td>Malaria (<i>Plasmodium</i>), kala-azar (<i>Leishmania</i>), amoebic dysentery</td></tr>
<tr><td>Genetic</td><td><b>Haemophilia</b> (X-linked recessive, poor clotting), sickle-cell anaemia, thalassaemia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hormonal</td><td><b>Diabetes</b> (insulin deficiency or resistance), goitre</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deficiency</td><td>Rickets, scurvy, beriberi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Occupational</td><td><b>Pneumoconiosis</b> (lung fibrosis from mineral dust; "black lung" in <b>coal miners</b>), silicosis, asbestosis, byssinosis (cotton); tanneries, distilleries and the glass industry are not the classic pneumoconiosis settings (glass workers may get silicosis from sand)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Mosquito-borne diseases</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Mosquito</th><th>Diseases</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Aedes</b> (day-biting)</td><td>Dengue, chikungunya, <b>Zika</b>, yellow fever</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anopheles (female)</td><td>Malaria</td></tr>
<tr><td>Culex</td><td>Japanese encephalitis, West Nile fever, filariasis</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Dengue</b> is <b>viral</b> (a flavivirus with four serotypes), not protozoan. Symptoms: high fever, <b>pain behind the eyes</b> (retro-orbital), severe joint and muscle pain ("break-bone fever"), <b>rash and bleeding from nose and gums</b> (dengue haemorrhagic fever, low platelets). Vaccines: Qdenga; India's DengiAll is in phase 3 trials.</li>
<li><b>Zika</b> spreads through the <b>same Aedes mosquito as dengue</b>, and also <b>sexually</b>, from mother to foetus and by transfusion. Infection in pregnancy causes microcephaly; in adults it is linked to Guillain-Barré syndrome. First found in Uganda's Zika forest (1947); WHO emergency in 2016; Indian outbreaks in Gujarat (2017), Rajasthan, Kerala and Maharashtra (2024).</li>
<li><b>Chikungunya</b> spreads only by Aedes bites, not by needles or tattooing. The first vaccine, Ixchiq (US FDA, 2023), had its US licence suspended in August 2025 over safety concerns.</li>
<li><b>Wolbachia method</b>: the bacterium <i>Wolbachia</i> occurs naturally in about half of insect species but not in <i>Aedes aegypti</i>. Infected Aedes are much worse at transmitting <b>dengue, Zika, chikungunya and yellow fever</b>, and pass the bacterium through their eggs. The World Mosquito Program cut dengue by about 77% in Yogyakarta, Indonesia. ICMR-VCRC (Puducherry) developed Indian Wolbachia strains in 2023–24.</li>
<li>Other control methods: sterile insect technique, Oxitec's self-limiting GM mosquitoes, gene drives (research), bed nets, indoor spraying, larvivorous fish (<i>Gambusia</i>, guppy), removing breeding sites. India's malaria elimination target is 2030 (no indigenous cases by 2027); India left WHO's "High Burden to High Impact" group in 2024.</li>
<li>Non-mosquito spread: <b>meningococcal meningitis</b> spreads through <b>respiratory droplets</b> and close contact, not mosquitoes. Symptoms are high fever, headache, <b>stiff and painful neck, vomiting</b>, sensitivity to light and a purple rash.</li>
</ul>

<h3>HIV, hepatitis and blood-borne infections</h3>
<ul>
<li>Spread through blood: unsterilised <b>tattoo needles can transmit hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV</b>.</li>
<li><b>Hepatitis B</b> is about 50–100 times <b>more infectious than HIV</b>, survives outside the body for a week and can cause cirrhosis and <b>liver cancer</b>. It <b>has a vaccine</b> (since 1982; in the UIP with a birth dose). <b>Hepatitis C has no vaccine</b> but is curable with antivirals (sofosbuvir) in 12 weeks.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Hepatitis</th><th>Spread</th><th>Vaccine</th></tr>
<tr><td>A, E</td><td>Faecal-oral (water, food)</td><td>A yes; E only in China</td></tr>
<tr><td>B</td><td>Blood, sex, mother to child</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>C</td><td>Blood (unsafe injections, transfusions)</td><td>No (curable)</td></tr>
<tr><td>D</td><td>Only with hepatitis B</td><td>Hepatitis B vaccine protects</td></tr>
</table>
<p>World Hepatitis Day is 28 July (Baruch Blumberg's birthday). National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme (2018); WHO aims to eliminate hepatitis as a public health threat by 2030. Nobel 2020: discovery of hepatitis C virus (Alter, Houghton, Rice).</p>
<ul>
<li><b>HIV</b> destroys CD4 helper T cells. <b>Male-to-female transmission is about twice as likely</b> as female-to-male. Symptoms of <b>AIDS</b>: <b>swollen lymph nodes, night sweats, memory loss</b> (HIV-associated dementia) and severe <b>weight loss</b> ("slim disease"), plus opportunistic infections such as TB. Transmission is more likely if a person has other sexually transmitted infections, and an infected mother can pass HIV to her baby in pregnancy, at birth and by breastfeeding (preventable with antiretrovirals). India has about 25 lakh people with HIV; NACO gives free antiretroviral therapy; HIV/AIDS (Prevention and Control) Act 2017.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Influenza and COVID-19</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>H1N1</b> influenza A caused the 2009 <b>swine flu</b> pandemic (genes from pig, bird and human viruses); India had a big outbreak in 2015. Influenza viruses are named by surface proteins haemagglutinin (H) and neuraminidase (N). Small changes (drift) cause seasonal flu; reassortment (shift) causes pandemics. Pandemics: 1918 (H1N1), 1957 (H2N2), 1968 (H3N2), 2009 (H1N1).</li>
<li>Swine flu: <b>not everyone is equally susceptible</b> (pregnant women, young children, the elderly and the chronically ill are at higher risk). <b>Antibiotics have no role in primary treatment</b> (it is viral; antiviral oseltamivir is used; antibiotics only for secondary bacterial infection). <b>Culling pigs is unnecessary</b>, since it spreads person to person.</li>
<li><b>ACE2</b> is a protein on cells of the lungs, heart, kidneys, gut and blood vessels; the <b>SARS-CoV-2 spike binds ACE2</b> to enter cells (the enzyme TMPRSS2 helps). ACE2 normally counters angiotensin II. Other receptors: HIV uses CD4 and CCR5; influenza binds sialic acid.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Antimicrobial resistance (AMR)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Causes of multi-drug resistance in India: <b>incorrect doses and incomplete courses</b> of antibiotics, self-medication and over-the-counter sales, and <b>antibiotics used in livestock</b> as growth promoters. Resistance develops in microbes, so it is <b>not caused by the patient's genes</b> or by having chronic diseases.</li>
<li>NDM-1 ("New Delhi metallo-beta-lactamase") superbug gene was reported in 2008. National Action Plan on AMR (2017; version 2.0 in 2025) follows a One Health approach. The Red Line campaign (2016) marks prescription-only antibiotics. Colistin was banned in animal feed (2019). Kerala has KARSAP and Operation AMRITH. Global: WHO GLASS surveillance; the 2024 UN political declaration aims to cut AMR deaths 10% by 2030.</li>
<li>Biofilms make bacteria far more resistant (see 3.4); phage therapy is an alternative.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Eradication and elimination</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Eradicated</b> means zero cases worldwide. <b>Smallpox</b> is the only human disease eradicated (India smallpox-free in 1977, world in 1980). Rinderpest (cattle) was eradicated in 2011. <b>Diphtheria and chickenpox</b> still occur in India.</li>
<li><b>Eliminated</b> means no local transmission. India: guinea worm (2000), polio-free certification (2014), maternal and neonatal tetanus (2015), yaws (2016), trachoma as a public health problem (2024); kala-azar target met (2023–24).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Cancer</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cancer is uncontrolled cell division, often because apoptosis fails (see 3.1). <b>Metastasis</b> is the spread of cancer cells <b>through the blood or lymphatic system</b> to distant organs.</li>
<li>Diagnosis uses biopsy, imaging and tumour markers; treatment uses surgery, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, immunotherapy and CAR-T.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_science_s5.js
// Science book, Unit 5: Biotechnology.
addBookTopic("science", "s5-1", `
<p>Biotechnology uses living cells and their molecules to make products and treat disease. It rests on recombinant DNA, sequencing, gene editing, stem cells and cloning.</p>

<h3>Recombinant DNA technology</h3>
<ul>
<li>Scientists can <b>cut and paste DNA from any source</b> because <b>restriction endonucleases</b> (the "scissors") cut DNA at specific sequences, leaving sticky ends, and <b>DNA ligase</b> (the "glue") joins fragments. A <b>vector</b> (plasmid or virus) carries the gene into a host. Cohen and Boyer made the first recombinant DNA in 1973.</li>
<li>The genetic code is universal, so genes can move <b>between plant species</b> (resistance genes from wild relatives), <b>from animals to plants</b> (the winter flounder's antifreeze gene tried in tomato and strawberry), and <b>from microbes to higher organisms</b> (Bt cotton's cry genes; herbicide-tolerance genes from <i>Agrobacterium</i>). Human genes put into bacteria make insulin (the first rDNA drug, 1982), growth hormone and the hepatitis B vaccine.</li>
<li>UPSC marked "<b>functional chromosomes can be made by joining DNA segments from different species</b>" as <b>not correct</b>. A working chromosome needs a centromere, telomeres and replication origins that suit the host (artificial chromosomes such as YACs and BACs exist, so this is debated).</li>
<li><b>Synthetic biology</b> engineers microbes to make insulin, vaccines and biofuels. India's <b>BioE3 policy</b> (2024: biotechnology for economy, environment, employment) promotes biomanufacturing. GM crops are regulated by the GEAC under the Environment (Protection) Act 1986 rules of 1989 (see 5.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Genomics and the "-omes"</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Complete set of</th></tr>
<tr><td>Genome</td><td>DNA (fixed)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Transcriptome</b></td><td><b>All RNA transcripts</b> (mainly mRNA) made at a given time; shows which genes are active; studied by RNA-seq</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proteome</td><td>Proteins</td></tr>
<tr><td>Metabolome</td><td>Metabolites</td></tr>
<tr><td>Microbiome</td><td>Microbes in a place (the gut)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Epigenome</td><td>Chemical marks (methylation) on DNA</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Human Genome Project</b> (1990–2003, US-led with the UK, Japan, France, Germany and China; India did not take part): about 3.2 billion base pairs and about 20,000 protein-coding genes. A complete "telomere-to-telomere" genome came in 2022.</li>
<li><b>GenomeIndia</b> (2020) is a <b>national project</b>, not part of the Human Genome Project. It is <b>funded by the Department of Biotechnology</b> and led by IISc's Centre for Brain Research with about 20 institutions. It sequenced <b>10,000 genomes</b> of healthy Indians from 99 population groups to build a <b>catalogue of Indian genetic variation</b>. Data was released in January 2025 and is held at the Indian Biological Data Centre, Faridabad.</li>
<li>Why it matters: India has about 4,600 population groups and much endogamy, so many disease variants are population-specific. Uses include precision medicine, diagnosing rare diseases, drug dosing (pharmacogenomics) and newborn screening. Related: IndiGen (CSIR, 1,029 genomes, 2019), Phenome India. Genomic data falls under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023.</li>
<li><b>Gene mapping</b> (locating genes on chromosomes) lets us check the <b>pedigree of livestock</b> and <b>breed disease-resistant animals</b> (marker-assisted selection). It does <b>not</b> explain the causes of <b>all</b> human diseases, since many are environmental, infectious or multifactorial.</li>
</ul>

<h3>DNA markers: fingerprinting, barcoding, microsatellites, eDNA</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>DNA fingerprinting</b> (Alec Jeffreys, 1984; in India Lalji Singh at CCMB, Hyderabad) uses variable repeats (VNTRs and STRs) to identify individuals. It establishes <b>paternity</b> and identifies offenders, because <b>trace evidence</b> (hair roots, saliva, dried semen, blood), amplified by PCR, is enough. The DNA Technology (Use and Application) Regulation Bill was withdrawn in 2023.</li>
<li><b>Microsatellites</b> (short tandem repeats of 2–6 base pairs, such as CACACA) vary greatly and mutate fast. They are used to study <b>evolutionary relationships</b>, population genetics, kinship and parentage: identifying individual tigers from scat, tracing poached ivory and tiger parts (WII; CCMB's LaCONES lab), measuring inbreeding in Gir lions, and marker-assisted crop breeding. Repeat expansions cause Huntington's disease and fragile X. Other markers: SNPs, mitochondrial DNA (mother's line) and the Y chromosome (father's line).</li>
<li><b>DNA barcoding</b> (Paul Hebert, 2003) identifies <b>species</b> from a short standard gene: COI (mitochondrial) for animals, rbcL and matK for plants, ITS for fungi. It <b>distinguishes look-alike species</b> and <b>detects unwanted animal or plant material in processed foods</b> (fish substitution, pork in mislabelled products, adulterated herbal medicines). It <b>cannot tell the age</b> of an organism (that needs tree rings, radiocarbon dating or epigenetic clocks). Databases: iBOL and BOLD.</li>
<li><b>Environmental DNA (eDNA)</b> is genetic material shed into water, soil or air. <b>Aerial metagenomics</b> means <b>collecting DNA from the air of a habitat at one go</b> and sequencing it all to find which species are present (UK and Danish zoo studies, 2021–22). Uses: biodiversity surveys (Ganga dolphins and fish), invasive species, wastewater surveillance for COVID, polio and AMR.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Gene editing, RNAi and genetic medicine</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Cas9</b> is a bacterial enzyme (from <i>Streptococcus pyogenes</i>) that acts as <b>molecular scissors for targeted gene editing</b>. A guide RNA leads it to the matching DNA, where it makes a double-strand cut; the cell's repair then deletes, changes or inserts genes. Bacteria use CRISPR-Cas naturally against viruses. Nobel Chemistry 2020: Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer Doudna.</li>
<li>Newer tools: Cas12, Cas13 (which cuts RNA), base editing and prime editing (no double-strand break). Indian uses: FELUDA (CRISPR COVID test, CSIR-IGIB, 2020) and two genome-edited rice varieties (2025). Genome edits without foreign DNA (SDN-1, SDN-2) were exempted from GM rules in 2022.</li>
<li><b>RNA interference (RNAi)</b>: small double-stranded RNAs (siRNA, miRNA) destroy a matching mRNA, <b>silencing that gene</b> (Fire and Mello, Nobel 2006; microRNA, Ambros and Ruvkun, Nobel 2024). Uses: <b>gene-silencing drugs</b> (patisiran, the first, 2018; inclisiran for cholesterol), <b>cancer therapy</b> research, and <b>virus-resistant crops</b> (Hawaiian papaya). It switches genes off, so it <b>cannot be used for hormone replacement</b>. Unlike CRISPR, which permanently edits DNA, RNAi acts temporarily on mRNA.</li>
<li><b>Genetic medicines</b> <b>correct or compensate for faulty genes</b>, delivered by <b>engineered viruses</b> (AAV, lentivirus, adenovirus) or <b>lipid nanoparticles</b>. They target specific genes and never <b>alter the entire DNA sequence</b>.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Approach</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Gene addition</td><td>Zolgensma (spinal muscular atrophy), Luxturna (inherited blindness)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gene editing</td><td><b>Casgevy</b>, the first approved CRISPR therapy (2023), for sickle-cell disease and beta-thalassaemia; first personalised CRISPR treatment for an infant (USA, 2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gene silencing</td><td>Patisiran, nusinersen</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cell-based</td><td>CAR-T; India's NexCAR19 (2023)</td></tr>
<tr><td>mRNA</td><td>COVID vaccines</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Somatic</b> editing (not inherited) is allowed for therapy. <b>Germline</b> editing (passed to children) is banned in most countries; India's ICMR gene therapy guidelines (2019) prohibit it for reproduction. Indian work: haemophilia gene therapy trial (CMC Vellore, 2024–25); National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission (2023, target 2047).</li>
<li>What is technically possible: <b>editing the cells that make eggs or sperm</b> (shown in animals), <b>editing a human embryo</b> (He Jiankui's CRISPR twins with edited CCR5 gene, 2018; he was jailed), and <b>injecting human iPS cells into pig embryos</b> to make human–pig chimeras (Salk Institute, 2017) for growing organs. Pig kidneys and hearts have been transplanted into people (USA, 2022–25).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Monoclonal antibodies</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Monoclonal antibodies</b> are <b>man-made proteins</b>, identical copies from one parent cell, that <b>bind one specific antigen</b>. They neutralise pathogens, flag them for destruction or modulate immunity. Names end in "-mab" (-ximab chimeric, -zumab humanised, -umab fully human).</li>
<li>They are made by <b>hybridoma technology</b>: fusing an antibody-producing B cell with a myeloma (cancer) cell, so the hybrid keeps dividing and making one antibody (Köhler and Milstein, Nobel 1984), or by recombinant DNA. Interferons (antiviral proteins) are also now made by recombinant DNA.</li>
<li>Uses: <b>viral infections</b> such as <b>Nipah</b> (India imported m102.4 from Australia during Kerala outbreaks), Ebola (Ebanga, Inmazeb), RSV (nirsevimab), rabies (Rabishield, Serum Institute), COVID cocktails; cancer (trastuzumab, rituximab, checkpoint inhibitors such as pembrolizumab); autoimmune disease (adalimumab); Alzheimer's (lecanemab); pregnancy test kits and ELISA.</li>
<li><b>Nipah</b> is a henipavirus whose reservoir is fruit bats (<i>Pteropus</i>). It was first identified in Malaysia (1998–99, via pigs) and spreads through date-palm sap and between people. Its fatality rate is 40–75%, and it has no approved vaccine or drug. Indian outbreaks: Siliguri (2001), Nadia (2007), then repeatedly in Kerala (2018 onward). NIV Pune does diagnosis.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Stem cells and cloning</h3>
<ul>
<li>Stem cells can renew themselves and turn into other cell types. Types: <b>embryonic</b> (pluripotent, from the blastocyst's inner cell mass), <b>adult</b> (multipotent: bone marrow, fat, umbilical cord blood) and <b>induced pluripotent stem cells</b> (iPSCs: adult cells reprogrammed, Shinya Yamanaka, Nobel 2012).</li>
<li>They are <b>not limited to mammals</b> (plants have meristems; fish, amphibians and insects have stem cells). They are used to <b>screen new drugs</b> and in <b>therapy</b> (bone marrow transplants for leukaemia and thalassaemia, corneal repair).</li>
<li>Embryos are <b>not the only source</b> of human stem cells. Embryonic stem cell lines <b>renew themselves in culture almost indefinitely</b>, and Indian centres (Reliance Life Sciences, NCBS) had derived such lines by 2001–02. In 2002, deriving them meant destroying the blastocyst; embryo-sparing methods came in 2006.</li>
<li>ICMR's stem cell guidelines (2017) allow only approved clinical trials; stem-cell "therapy" for autism and similar conditions is not approved. Cord blood can be banked.</li>
<li><b>Somatic cell nuclear transfer</b> (SCNT) is used for <b>reproductive cloning of animals</b>: a body cell's nucleus is put into an egg whose nucleus has been removed, and the embryo is implanted in a surrogate. <b>Dolly</b> the sheep (1996, Roslin Institute) was the first mammal cloned from an adult cell. India: <b>Garima</b> (2009, NDRI Karnal), the first cloned buffalo by hand-guided cloning; <b>Noori</b> (2012), the first cloned Pashmina goat; a cloned Gir cow (2023). China cloned macaques in 2018. Therapeutic cloning makes patient-matched stem cells; human reproductive cloning is banned.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Mitochondrial replacement</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mitochondria have their own circular DNA (37 genes). The embryo's mitochondria come <b>only from the egg</b> (sperm mitochondria are destroyed), so <b>mitochondrial diseases pass entirely from the mother</b> (Leigh syndrome, MELAS, Leber's optic neuropathy). Mitochondrial DNA traces maternal ancestry ("Mitochondrial Eve").</li>
<li><b>Mitochondrial replacement therapy</b> (MRT) can prevent this, <b>before or after fertilisation</b>: <b>maternal spindle transfer</b> is done before; <b>pronuclear transfer</b> is done after (the parents' pronuclei are moved from the mother's zygote into a donor zygote that has healthy mitochondria). The child has DNA from three people ("three-parent baby").</li>
<li>The UK legalised MRT in 2015; the first spindle-transfer baby was born in Mexico (2016); in 2025 Newcastle researchers reported 8 babies born after pronuclear transfer. India regulates IVF, ICSI and related techniques under the ART (Regulation) Act 2021 and the Surrogacy (Regulation) Act 2021.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s5-2", `
<p>Agricultural biotechnology adds or edits genes in crops to resist pests, tolerate stress or improve nutrition. In India it is tightly regulated, and only one GM crop is grown commercially.</p>

<h3>How transgenic crops are made</h3>
<ul>
<li>A transgenic crop carries DNA from another species, inserted with <i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> (Ti plasmid) or a gene gun.</li>
<li>Techniques used to make transgenic crops include <b>engineered male sterility</b> (GM mustard's barnase-barstar system for hybrid seed; cytoplasmic male sterility itself is natural) and <b>gene silencing</b> (Flavr Savr tomato, non-browning Arctic apple, virus-resistant papaya). <b>Budding and grafting</b> are traditional propagation methods that insert no gene (see 3.2).</li>
<li><b>Genome sequencing</b> helps agriculture by finding <b>markers for disease resistance and drought tolerance</b>, <b>cutting breeding time</b> through marker-assisted selection (from 10–12 years to a few), and revealing <b>host–pathogen relationships</b>. Indian examples: Improved Samba Mahsuri (blight-resistant rice, ICAR-IIRR and CCMB), Swarna Sub1 (flood-tolerant rice, by marker-assisted breeding, not GM), blast-resistant Pusa Basmati 1509; chickpea and pigeonpea genomes (ICRISAT). India helped sequence rice (2005), tomato and wheat.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Traits engineered</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Trait</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pest resistance</td><td><b>Bt cotton</b>, <b>Bt brinjal</b>, <b>MON 863 maize</b> (cry3Bb1 gene against corn rootworm; a disputed 2007 rat-study reanalysis made news)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drought tolerance</td><td>HB4 wheat (Argentina), DroughtGard maize (USA)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nutrition</td><td><b>Golden Rice</b>: grains make <b>beta-carotene (provitamin A)</b> using a maize phytoene synthase gene and a bacterial carotene desaturase gene; approved in the Philippines (2021)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shelf life</td><td>Flavr Savr tomato (1994, first GM food), Arctic apple</td></tr>
<tr><td>Herbicide tolerance</td><td>HT cotton (unapproved in India but widely sown illegally)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Growing plants in spaceships was <b>not</b> a stated aim of GM crops, per UPSC.</p>
<ul>
<li>Other <i>Bacillus</i> species: <i>B. subtilis</i> (a common soil bacterium and probiotic), <i>B. amyloliquefaciens</i> (source of GM mustard's barnase-barstar genes).</li>
<li><b>Canola</b> is rapeseed bred conventionally (in Canada) for oil with <b>very low erucic acid</b> (under 2%) and meal low in glucosinolates.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Bt crops</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bt crops carry <b>cry genes</b> from the soil <b>bacterium <i>Bacillus thuringiensis</i></b>, whose Cry proteins kill specific insect larvae.</li>
<li><b>Bollgard</b> is Monsanto's (now Bayer's) Bt cotton technology. <b>Bollgard I</b> (one gene, Cry1Ac) was approved in India in March <b>2002</b> and is the <b>only GM crop approved for commercial cultivation</b>. <b>Bollgard II</b> (Cry1Ac and Cry2Ab) followed in 2006; pink bollworm has since become resistant. Bollgard II Roundup Ready Flex (herbicide-tolerant) is not approved.</li>
<li><b>Bt brinjal</b> was developed to be <b>pest-resistant</b> against the fruit and shoot borer (cry1Ac, by Mahyco). GEAC approved it in 2009, but Environment Minister Jairam Ramesh imposed a moratorium in February 2010, still in force; Bangladesh has grown it since 2014.
<ul><li>Real objections: fears of <b>health effects</b> from a daily vegetable, and harm to <b>biodiversity</b>, since India is a centre of origin of brinjal with over 2,500 varieties.</li>
<li>Wrong claims: the gene is from a <b>bacterium, not a fungus</b>, and the seeds are <b>not terminator seeds</b> (GURT was never commercialised).</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>GM mustard (DMH-11)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Dhara Mustard Hybrid-11</b> was developed by the <b>Centre for Genetic Manipulation of Crop Plants, University of Delhi</b> (Deepak Pental's team, funded by NDDB and DBT), not IARI with Punjab Agricultural University.</li>
<li>It is <b>not pest-resistant</b>. It uses genes from the soil bacterium <i>Bacillus amyloliquefaciens</i>: <b>barnase</b> (male sterility) and <b>barstar</b> (restores fertility), plus the <i>bar</i> herbicide-tolerance marker. This <b>allows cross-pollination and hybridisation</b> in mustard, which normally self-pollinates, to raise yields.</li>
<li>GEAC approved environmental release in October 2022. The Supreme Court gave a split verdict in July 2024, referred it to a larger bench and asked the Centre for a national GM policy.</li>
<li>Concerns: more glufosinate use, harm to bees, contamination of non-GM mustard and honey exports.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Regulation, patents and biopesticides</h3>
<ul>
<li>Regulators: the <b>GEAC</b> (Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee, MoEFCC) and the RCGM (DBT), under the Environment (Protection) Act 1986 rules of 1989. Genome-edited plants without foreign DNA (SDN-1, SDN-2) were exempted in 2022. India released two genome-edited rice varieties, DRR Dhan 100 (Kamala) and Pusa DST Rice 1, in May 2025.</li>
<li><b>Patents Act 1970, Section 3(j)</b> excludes plants and animals, seeds, varieties, species and <b>essentially biological processes</b> for producing them, so <b>a biological process to create a seed cannot be patented</b>. Microorganisms can be patented.</li>
<li><b>Plant varieties are not patentable</b> in India; they are protected under the <b>Protection of Plant Varieties and Farmers' Rights Act 2001</b>, India's sui generis system under TRIPS Article 27.3(b) (PepsiCo potato case, 2019–24).</li>
<li>The <b>Intellectual Property Appellate Board</b> (2003, Chennai) existed in 2019 but was abolished by the Tribunals Reforms Act 2021; appeals now go to High Courts.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Right</th><th>Law</th></tr>
<tr><td>Patents</td><td>Patents Act 1970 (product patents from 2005; 20 years; Section 3(d) blocks evergreening, as in the Novartis Glivec case, 2013)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Geographical indications</td><td>GI Act 1999 (Darjeeling tea first, 2004)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plant varieties</td><td>PPV&FR Act 2001</td></tr>
<tr><td>Copyright; trade marks</td><td>Copyright Act 1957; Trade Marks Act 1999</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biological resources</td><td>Biological Diversity Act 2002 (amended 2023)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>National IPR Policy 2016. The CGPDTM under DPIIT administers patents, designs, trade marks and GIs.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Biopesticides</b> come from all three groups:
<ul><li><b>Bacteria</b>: <i>Bacillus thuringiensis</i>; <i>B. sphaericus</i> against mosquito larvae.</li>
<li><b>Fungi</b>: <i>Trichoderma</i> against plant pathogens; <i>Beauveria</i> and <i>Metarhizium</i> against insects.</li>
<li><b>Flowering plants</b>: neem (azadirachtin), pyrethrum (from chrysanthemum), tobacco (nicotine).</li></ul>
Viruses (nuclear polyhedrosis virus against <i>Helicoverpa</i>) are used too. Biopesticides are registered under the Insecticides Act 1968; Integrated Pest Management combines them with other methods.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_science_s6.js
// Science book, Unit 6: Space.
addBookTopic("science", "s6-1", `
<p>Astronomy covers the solar system, the life of stars and the history of the universe. UPSC asks about planets, the life cycles of stars, unusual objects and the evidence for an expanding universe.</p>

<h3>Distances in space</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Unit</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Astronomical unit (AU)</td><td>Mean Earth–Sun distance, about <b>149.6 million km (150 × 10<sup>6</sup> km)</b>; sunlight takes about 8 min 20 s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Light-year</td><td>Distance light travels in a year in vacuum, about 9.46 × 10<sup>12</sup> km (about 63,240 AU). A unit of <b>distance</b>, not time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parsec</td><td>About 3.26 light-years</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The light-year works as a unit because the <b>speed of light in vacuum is always the same</b> (about 3 × 10<sup>5</sup> km/s for every observer, per Einstein). The nearest star after the Sun is Proxima Centauri, about 4.24 light-years away.</p>

<h3>The solar system</h3>
<ul>
<li>Order from the Sun: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, (asteroid belt), <b>Jupiter, Saturn</b>, Uranus, Neptune. So Jupiter and Saturn lie between Mars and Uranus.</li>
<li><b>Kepler's third law</b> (T² ∝ a³): the farther a planet, the longer its year. Mars takes about <b>687 days</b>, longer than Earth; its smaller size (diameter about 6,779 km, about half of Earth's) has nothing to do with it. Jupiter takes about 11.9 years, Saturn 29.5.</li>
<li>Earth orbits at about <b>30 km/s</b> (about 1,07,000 km/h).</li>
<li><b>Earth is the densest planet</b> (about 5.51 g/cm³; Mercury 5.43), thanks to its iron-nickel core and compression.</li>
<li><b>Moons</b>: in 2009 Jupiter led (about 63); today <b>Saturn</b> has the most (274 confirmed after 128 were added in March 2025), against about 97 for Jupiter.</li>
<li><b>Venus</b>: a runaway greenhouse from an atmosphere of about <b>96% CO2</b>; surface about 465°C, pressure 92 times Earth's, sulphuric acid clouds; human life there is highly improbable. UPSC's key treated the CO2 as true but not on its own the full explanation.</li>
<li><b>The Moon</b> always shows <b>the same face</b> because it is tidally locked: it rotates once in about <b>27.3 days</b>, equal to its orbital period (not 23 days). The synodic month (new moon to new moon) is 29.5 days. Libration lets us see about 59% of the surface over time.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Asteroids</th><th>Comets</th></tr>
<tr><td>Make-up</td><td>Small <b>rocky or metallic</b> bodies</td><td>"Dirty snowballs" of <b>frozen gases</b>, dust and rock</td></tr>
<tr><td>Where</td><td>Mostly the <b>belt between Mars and Jupiter</b></td><td>Outer solar system: <b>Kuiper Belt and Oort Cloud</b> (not between Venus and Mercury)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tail</td><td>None</td><td>Near the Sun, ices turn to gas, forming a coma and <b>glowing tails</b> pointing away from the Sun</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Life cycle of stars</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Sun-like star</th><th>Massive star (over about 8 solar masses)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Birth</td><td colspan="2">Nebula → protostar → main sequence (hydrogen fuses into helium)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lifespan</td><td>About 10 billion years for the Sun (now about 4.6 billion years old)</td><td>A few million years</td></tr>
<tr><td>Old age</td><td>Red giant</td><td>Red supergiant (fusion up to iron)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Death</td><td>Planetary nebula → <b>white dwarf</b></td><td><b>Supernova</b> → neutron star (pulsar) or black hole</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Giant stars live much shorter lives than dwarf stars</b> because they have hotter, denser cores and a <b>much faster rate of nuclear reactions</b> (luminosity rises roughly as mass to the power 3.5). Red dwarfs can live trillions of years.</li>
<li><b>Chandrasekhar limit</b> (about 1.44 solar masses, 1930): the maximum mass of a white dwarf. Heavier remnants collapse into neutron stars or black holes. S. Chandrasekhar shared the 1983 Nobel in Physics; NASA's <b>Chandra X-ray Observatory</b> (1999) is named after him.</li>
<li>Elements heavier than iron form in supernovae and neutron-star mergers. The Hertzsprung-Russell diagram plots stars' luminosity against temperature.</li>
<li>A <b>black hole</b> traps even light because of its <b>very high density</b> (mass packed into a tiny space). First images: M87* (Event Horizon Telescope, 2019) and Sagittarius A* at the Milky Way's centre (2022).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Object</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Cepheids</b></td><td>Pulsating stars that <b>brighten and dim periodically</b>; their period reveals their true brightness (Henrietta Leavitt, 1912), making them "standard candles" for distance</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Nebulae</b></td><td><b>Giant clouds of dust and gas</b>: star nurseries (Orion) or remains of dead stars (Crab)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Pulsars</b></td><td>Rapidly spinning, magnetised <b>neutron stars formed when massive stars collapse</b>, sweeping radio beams like lighthouses (Jocelyn Bell Burnell, 1967)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quasar</td><td>Extremely bright galaxy centre powered by a supermassive black hole</td></tr>
<tr><td>Magnetar</td><td>Neutron star with an extreme magnetic field; a source of some fast radio bursts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exoplanet</td><td>Planet of another star; over 6,000 confirmed (2025)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The universe</h3>
<ul>
<li>Evidence that the universe is expanding: the <b>cosmic microwave background</b> (Penzias and Wilson, 1965), leftover radiation stretched to microwaves, and the <b>redshift</b> of distant galaxies increasing with distance (Hubble's law, 1929). <b>Asteroid movement</b> says nothing about it. <b>Type Ia supernovae</b> used as distance markers showed the expansion is <b>accelerating</b> (Perlmutter, Schmidt, Riess, Nobel 2011), though UPSC's key did not count "supernova explosions".</li>
<li><b>Dark energy</b> (about 68%) drives the acceleration; <b>dark matter</b> is about 27%; ordinary matter about 5%. The "Hubble tension" means measurements disagree on the expansion rate; JWST and DESI (2024–25) hint that dark energy may weaken over time.</li>
<li>Cosmology terms: the <b>event horizon</b> is a black hole's point of no return; a <b>singularity</b> is a point of infinite density (black hole centre, Big Bang); <b>string theory</b> models particles as vibrating strings to unify gravity with quantum mechanics; the <b>Standard Model</b> covers quarks, leptons, bosons and the Higgs, and three forces but not gravity. Higgs boson found at CERN (2012; Nobel 2013 to Higgs and Englert). Stephen Hawking (died 2018) predicted that black holes slowly evaporate.</li>
<li>Four fundamental forces: gravity, electromagnetism, the weak nuclear force and the strong nuclear force.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Gravitational waves</h3>
<ul>
<li>When <b>black holes merge</b> they send out ripples in spacetime, <b>gravitational waves</b>, predicted by Einstein (1916). LIGO first detected them on 14 September 2015, from a merger about 1.3 billion light-years away (Nobel 2017: Weiss, Barish, Thorne). In 2017 a neutron-star merger was seen in both gravitational waves and light ("multi-messenger astronomy").</li>
<li>Ground detectors: LIGO (USA), Virgo (Italy), KAGRA (Japan). <b>LIGO-India</b> at Aundha, Hingoli district, Maharashtra, was approved in April 2023.</li>
<li><b>eLISA / LISA</b> (Laser Interferometer Space Antenna) is designed <b>to detect gravitational waves</b> in space: <b>three spacecraft in an equilateral triangle</b> with laser links (arms of 1 million km in the eLISA concept; about 2.5 million km in LISA, adopted by ESA with NASA in January 2024, launch about 2035). It senses low-frequency waves from supermassive black holes. LISA Pathfinder (2015–17) tested the technology.</li>
<li>Pulsar timing arrays, including India's InPTA using the upgraded GMRT (Pune), found evidence of a nanohertz gravitational-wave background in 2023. Neutrino observatories: IceCube (Antarctica), Super-Kamiokande (Japan), INO (proposed, India).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Exoplanets and the Goldilocks zone</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Goldilocks zone</b> (habitable zone) is the range of distances from a star where liquid water can exist on a planet's surface, so it guides the <b>search for Earth-like planets</b>.</li>
<li>Missions: Kepler (2009–18), TESS (2018), James Webb Space Telescope (2021), ESA's PLATO (planned 2026). Notable planets: TRAPPIST-1's seven Earth-sized planets, Proxima Centauri b, Kepler-452b (2015), K2-18b (2025 biosignature claims). Nobel 2019: Mayor and Queloz for the first exoplanet around a Sun-like star (1995).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Indian astronomy facilities</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Indian Astronomical Observatory at Hanle, Ladakh</b> (about 4,500 m; Indian Institute of Astrophysics) opened its 2-m Himalayan Chandra Telescope in 2001, then the world's highest optical telescope site. The May 2024 auroras were photographed there.</li>
<li>GMRT (Pune), AstroSat (2015), XPoSat (2024), Aditya-L1 (2023): see 6.2.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s6-2", `
<p>Space technology means getting satellites into the right orbit and using them for communication, navigation, Earth observation and exploration. India's programme has grown from foreign-launched satellites to Moon and Mars missions and an opened private sector.</p>

<h3>Orbits</h3>
<ul>
<li>A satellite does not fall because <b>Earth's gravity supplies exactly the centripetal acceleration</b> its motion needs: it is always falling, but its sideways speed carries it around the Earth.</li>
<li>Orbital velocity near Earth is about <b>7.9 km/s</b>; escape velocity is <b>11.2 km/s</b>.</li>
<li>Most satellites are launched <b>eastward</b> to gain Earth's rotation (about 0.4 km/s at the equator); Sriharikota's east coast lets rockets fly over the sea. This only helps; rotation does not give the satellite escape velocity. "Always" is too strong: polar satellites go north or south (PSLV flies south), and Israel launches westward.</li>
<li><b>Geostationary orbit</b> requires all of the following:
<ul><li>A <b>geosynchronous</b> period of one sidereal day (about 23 h 56 min).</li>
<li>A <b>circular</b> orbit.</li>
<li>An orbit in the plane of the <b>equator</b>.</li>
<li>An altitude of about <b>35,786 km</b>. (22,236 is that height in miles; 10,000 km is a medium Earth orbit.)</li></ul>
Such satellites appear fixed in the sky and suit communications. Geosynchronous satellites with an inclined orbit trace a figure-of-eight.</li>
<li>FM radio sounds cleaner than AM because most noise changes a signal's <b>amplitude</b>, which an FM receiver clips off; FM carries information in frequency changes.</li>
<li><b>Sun-synchronous polar orbits</b> (about 600–900 km) suit Earth observation; these satellites move across the sky.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Indian launch vehicles</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Vehicle</th><th>Feature</th></tr>
<tr><td>SLV-3 (1980)</td><td>Launched Rohini; A.P.J. Abdul Kalam was project director</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>PSLV</b></td><td>The workhorse: <b>four stages, solid (1st, 3rd) and liquid (2nd, 4th)</b>. Launches <b>Earth-observation satellites</b> to polar orbits (all the IRS series). Chandrayaan-1 (2008), Mangalyaan (2013), Aditya-L1 (2023); 104 satellites in one launch (2017). PSLV-C4 (2002) first carried over 1,000 kg (METSAT) towards geosynchronous orbit. Two failures in a row, PSLV-C61 (May 2025) and PSLV-C62 (January 2026), both third-stage problems.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>GSLV Mk II</b></td><td>Mainly for <b>communication satellites</b> to geosynchronous transfer orbit; cryogenic upper stage; NISAR (2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>LVM3</b> (GSLV Mk III)</td><td><b>Three stages</b>: two S200 solid boosters, L110 liquid core, C25 cryogenic upper stage. Chandrayaan-2 and 3, OneWeb; human-rated for Gaganyaan</td></tr>
<tr><td>SSLV</td><td>Small satellites up to 500 kg; technology transferred to HAL (2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td>NGLV (approved 2024)</td><td>Next-generation, partly reusable</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Launch sites: Satish Dhawan Space Centre, Sriharikota; a second spaceport for SSLV is coming up at <b>Kulasekarapattinam</b> (Thoothukudi, Tamil Nadu). Balasore (Odisha) and Thumba (Thiruvananthapuram) are missile and sounding-rocket ranges.</p>
<h4>Cryogenic engines</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cryogenic stages burn liquid hydrogen with liquid oxygen. The <b>first GSLV</b> (2001) used a <b>Russian</b> cryogenic stage. India's own stage was ground-tested at <b>Mahendragiri</b> (ISRO Propulsion Complex, Tirunelveli, Tamil Nadu) in the mid-2000s, including a full 720-second test in November 2007. It failed on its first flight (2010) and succeeded on GSLV-D5 (January 2014).</li>
<li>India was <b>not</b> fourth after the USA, Russia and China: France/ESA (Ariane, 1979) and Japan had cryogenic stages earlier, making India about sixth.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Satellites and their uses</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>INSAT/GSAT</b> (communication, geostationary). Up to 2002 <b>all INSATs were launched abroad</b> (US rockets or the Space Shuttle, or Ariane from Kourou, French Guiana). <b>INSAT-3E</b> (2003) and <b>INSAT-4A</b> (December 2005) went up on Ariane-5 from <b>Kourou</b>, not New Mexico. INSAT-4A's Ku-band carried <b>Tata Sky</b> DTH (2006). GSAT-1 (2001) demonstrated digital broadcasting and internet services. INSAT-3B (2000) was launched when K. Kasturirangan was ISRO chairman (1994–2003).</li>
<li><b>METSAT</b> (September 2002), India's first dedicated weather satellite, was renamed <b>Kalpana-1</b> after Kalpana Chawla died in the Columbia disaster (2003). INSAT-3D, 3DR and 3DS now do weather.</li>
<li><b>Indian Remote Sensing (IRS)</b> satellites (IRS-1A, 1988, the first) are used to assess <b>crop productivity</b> (FASAL, CHAMAN), locate <b>groundwater</b> and explore <b>minerals</b>. They are <b>not</b> for telecommunications (geostationary GSATs do that) or, per UPSC, traffic studies. Series: Resourcesat, Cartosat, Oceansat, RISAT (radar through clouds), EOS.</li>
<li><b>Oceansat-2</b> (2009): its ROSA sounder estimates <b>atmospheric water vapour</b>; its scatterometer measures ocean winds, helping <b>predict monsoon onset</b> and cyclones; its ocean colour monitor maps chlorophyll and fishing zones. UPSC did not count coastal pollution monitoring. Oceansat-3 (EOS-06) followed.</li>
<li><b>Remote sensing</b> measures <b>chlorophyll</b> in vegetation (NDVI: plants reflect near-infrared and absorb red), <b>greenhouse gases from paddies</b> (mapping flooded fields plus methane sensors such as Sentinel-5P, GHGSat, MethaneSAT) and <b>land surface temperature</b> (thermal infrared; urban heat islands).</li>
<li><b>NISAR</b> (NASA-ISRO, launched July 2025 on GSLV-F16): dual-frequency radar tracking land, ice and crops.</li>
<li><b>Bhuvan</b> (2009, NRSC Hyderabad) is ISRO's <b>geoportal</b> for satellite imagery and 3D maps of India.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Navigation</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>System</th><th>Owner</th><th>Type</th></tr>
<tr><td>GPS</td><td>USA</td><td>Global</td></tr>
<tr><td>GLONASS</td><td>Russia</td><td>Global</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Galileo</b></td><td><b>European Union</b> (with ESA); civilian; services from 2016; India joined in 2005 but turned to NavIC</td><td>Global</td></tr>
<tr><td>BeiDou</td><td>China</td><td>Global (2020)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>NavIC (IRNSS)</b></td><td>India</td><td>Regional</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>QZSS ("Michibiki")</b></td><td><b>Japan</b></td><td>Regional; Australia, Canada and Israel have no own constellation</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>NavIC</b> has <b>7 satellites: 3 geostationary and 4 geosynchronous</b> (inclined). It covers India and about <b>1,500 km</b> beyond, and is <b>regional, not global</b>. Accuracy better than 20 m; a restricted encrypted service serves the military. Named by PM Modi in 2016; recognised by 3GPP and the IMO (2020).</li>
<li>Second-generation NVS satellites carry an indigenous atomic clock and the L1 band (NVS-01, 2023). NVS-02 (January 2025, the 100th launch from Sriharikota) failed to reach its orbit. Atomic clocks failed on older satellites, and service has been degraded since March 2026; NVS-03 was being readied for launch to restore four working satellites.</li>
<li><b>GPS timing</b> (from onboard atomic clocks) is used in <b>mobile networks</b> (synchronising base stations), <b>banking</b> (time-stamping transactions) and <b>power grids</b> (synchronising phasor measurement units).</li>
<li><b>GAGAN</b> (ISRO with the Airports Authority of India) is India's satellite-based augmentation system. Ground stations measure GPS errors, and corrections are broadcast from GSAT-8, 10 and 15. It gives <b>more accuracy and integrity</b> (about 1.5 m horizontal) and <b>better air traffic management</b>. Its benefits are <b>not limited to aviation</b>: railways (level-crossing alerts), shipping, roads, agriculture, surveying and fishermen's alerts (GEMINI) also use it. DGCA certified it in 2015, making India the fourth with an SBAS after the USA (WAAS), EU (EGNOS) and Japan (MSAS); others are SDCM (Russia) and SouthPAN (Australia–NZ).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Space weather</h3>
<ul>
<li>A major <b>solar storm</b> (flares and coronal mass ejections) can:
<ul><li>disrupt <b>GPS</b> (ionospheric distortion);</li>
<li>damage <b>power grids</b> (induced currents; Quebec blackout, 1989);</li>
<li>bring <b>auroras</b> far from the poles (May 2024: a G5 storm, with auroras seen in Ladakh);</li>
<li>disturb <b>satellite orbits</b> through extra drag (about 40 Starlink satellites lost, February 2022);</li>
<li>black out <b>HF radio</b> on polar flights.</li></ul>
It does <b>not</b> cause <b>tsunamis or forest fires</b>.</li>
<li>The Carrington Event (1859) was the strongest recorded storm. The solar cycle lasts about 11 years; Cycle 25 peaked around 2024–25.</li>
<li>Monitors: India's <b>Aditya-L1</b> (September 2023, at the L1 point about 1.5 million km away, 7 payloads including VELC and SUIT), NASA's Parker Solar Probe (closest pass December 2024), SOHO, ESA's Proba-3. NASA's <b>THEMIS</b> (2007) studied the substorms that make <b>auroras</b> brighten.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Space power</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Radioisotope thermoelectric generators</b> (RTGs) are <b>not reactors</b>: there is no fission. They turn heat from natural radioactive decay into electricity with thermocouples (Seebeck effect).</li>
<li>They <b>power spacecraft</b> where sunlight is weak: Voyager, Cassini, New Horizons, Curiosity, Perseverance.</li>
<li>Their fuel is <b>plutonium-238</b> (half-life about 88 years, an alpha emitter), historically a <b>by-product of US weapons-related reactors</b>.</li>
<li>Radioisotope heater units keep instruments warm. Chandrayaan-3's propulsion module carried two made by BARC, India's first use of nuclear power in space. Space fission reactors: SNAP-10A (1965), Soviet RORSATs, NASA plans for a lunar reactor by about 2030.</li>
<li>On Earth: the Nuclear Energy Mission (100 GW by 2047, small modular reactors). The <b>SHANTI Act 2025</b> replaced the Atomic Energy Act 1962 and the nuclear liability law of 2010, allowing licensed private companies to run nuclear plants.</li>
</ul>

<h3>India's missions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Chandrayaan-1</b> (2008, PSLV): its <b>Moon Impact Probe</b> hit the south pole region on 14 November 2008. Japan's <b>Hiten</b> had crashed on the Moon earlier (1993); China's Chang'e-1 impacted later (2009). Japan's <b>SELENE (Kaguya)</b> lunar orbiter was launched in 2007. Chandrayaan-3 landed near the lunar south pole (August 2023).</li>
<li><b>Mangalyaan</b> (Mars Orbiter Mission): launched 5 November 2013 on PSLV-C25, reached Mars orbit on 24 September 2014. India was the <b>first country to succeed on its first attempt</b>, the fourth agency after the Soviet Union, NASA and ESA (so not "second after the USA"), and the first in Asia. It cost about ₹450 crore and lost contact in April 2022 after about 8 years. Other Mars orbiters: UAE's Hope, China's Tianwen-1 (2021).</li>
<li><b>AstroSat</b> (28 September 2015, PSLV-C30), India's first multi-wavelength space observatory: about <b>1,513 kg</b> at about <b>650 km</b> (not 2,000 kg at 1,650 km). India was not the only country after the USA and Russia to launch one (ESA and Japan had). Five payloads: UVIT, LAXPC, SXT, CZTI, SSM. Successor XPoSat (January 2024) measures X-ray polarisation; only NASA's IXPE did this before.</li>
<li>Planned: Shukrayaan (Venus Orbiter Mission, approved 2024), Mangalyaan-2, Gaganyaan.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Other agencies' missions</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Mission</th><th>Target</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Cassini-Huygens</b> (NASA–ESA–ASI, 1997)</td><td><b>Saturn</b> (not Venus) and its moons; Huygens landed on Titan (2005); "Grand Finale" plunge (2017)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>MESSENGER</b> (2004)</td><td>First to orbit and map <b>Mercury</b> (2011–15); BepiColombo (ESA–JAXA) arrives in 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Voyager 1 and 2</b> (1977)</td><td><b>Outer solar system</b>; now in interstellar space; carry the Golden Record</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Deep Impact</b> (2005)</td><td>Fired an impactor into comet <b>Tempel 1</b> (not Halley's, Hale-Bopp or Hyakutake; 3I/ATLAS is the third interstellar object, found in 2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2001 Mars Odyssey</td><td>Mars; found subsurface ice; longest-serving Mars spacecraft</td></tr>
<tr><td>New Horizons (2006)</td><td>Pluto flyby (2015), Arrokoth (2019)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Juno, JUICE, Europa Clipper</td><td>Jupiter and its moons</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spitzer; TecSAR</td><td>Infrared space telescope; Israeli radar satellite launched by PSLV (2008)</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Atlantis</b> was a NASA <b>Space Shuttle</b> that flew the last shuttle mission (2011), not a space station. Discovery was another shuttle; COBE (1989) mapped the cosmic microwave background. India plans its own Bharatiya Antariksh Station (first module by 2028).</p>

<h3>Space reforms and the private sector</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>IN-SPACe</b> (2020, Ahmedabad) is an <b>autonomous single-window body</b> in the Department of Space that authorises and promotes private space activity. <b>NSIL</b> (2019) is ISRO's commercial arm. The Indian Space Policy came in 2023. FDI was eased in 2024: 74% automatic for satellites, 49% for launch vehicles, 100% for components. A ₹1,000 crore venture fund for space start-ups followed.</li>
<li><b>Agnikul Cosmos</b> (IIT Madras) launched Agnibaan SOrTeD on 30 May 2024 from India's first private launchpad at Sriharikota. It used <b>Agnilet</b>, the world's first <b>single-piece 3D-printed</b> semi-cryogenic engine.</li>
<li><b>Skyroot Aerospace</b> (Hyderabad) flew <b>Vikram-S</b>, India's first private rocket (November 2022), and is building Vikram-1. It did <b>not</b> develop GSLV fuel: GSLV uses Vikas engines (UDMH and N2O4) and ISRO's own cryogenic stage.</li>
<li>Others: Pixxel (hyperspectral imaging), Dhruva Space, Digantara (space situational awareness), Tata's TSAT-1A.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
