// data/book_science_s10.js
// Science book, Unit 10: Science in society.
addBookTopic("science", "s10-1", `
<p>UPSC asks who discovered what, which institution is where, and how Indian science is organised. This topic gathers the scientists, inventors, prizes and institutions that recur in questions.</p>

<h3>Scientists and discoveries</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Scientist</th><th>Contribution</th></tr>
<tr><td>Albert Einstein</td><td>Explained the <b>photoelectric effect</b> with light quanta (1905); his 1921 Nobel was for this, not relativity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Alfred Wegener</td><td><b>Continental drift</b> (1912; Pangaea)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Edwin Hubble</td><td><b>Expansion of the universe</b> (1929, galaxy redshift), building on Lemaître</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harold Urey</td><td>Discovered deuterium (1931; Nobel Chemistry 1934), hence <b>heavy water</b>; G.N. Lewis first isolated D2O (1933)</td></tr>
<tr><td>W.H. Bragg and W.L. Bragg</td><td><b>Father and son</b> who shared the 1915 Physics Nobel for X-ray crystallography (nλ = 2d sin θ); the son, at 25, is still the youngest physics laureate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Theodore Maiman</td><td>First working <b>laser</b> (ruby, 1960); built on Einstein's stimulated emission and the maser (Townes, Basov, Prokhorov)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Watson and Crick</td><td>DNA <b>double helix</b> (1953, using Rosalind Franklin's and Maurice Wilkins's X-ray data; Nobel 1962 with Wilkins). James Watson's field is <b>genetics</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Francis Collins</td><td>Led the public <b>Human Genome Project</b>; later directed the NIH</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ian Wilmut</td><td>Created <b>Dolly</b>, the first cloned sheep (with Keith Campbell, Roslin Institute, 1996)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Robert Webster</td><td><b>Influenza virus</b>: showed pandemic flu arises from bird flu strains mixing with human ones (H5N1 clade 2.3.4.4b is the bird flu strain spreading to US dairy cattle since 2024)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eric Kandel</td><td>Neuroscientist (not literature): how synapses change in memory (Nobel 2000, using the sea slug <i>Aplysia</i>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nicholas Stern</td><td>Economist (not construction technology): the <b>Stern Review</b> on the economics of climate change (2006)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reinhold Messner</td><td>Mountaineer (not computing): first to climb Everest without bottled oxygen (1978) and all fourteen 8,000-m peaks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Others in matching questions</td><td>Harlow Shapley (astronomy: the Sun's position in the Milky Way); Gregor Mendel (laws of heredity); Godfrey Hounsfield (CT scan); William Dickson (motion picture camera, with Edison); Charles Babbage (programmable computer); Brian Greene (string theory); Sigmund Freud (psychoanalysis); Anna Freud (child psychoanalysis); Milton Friedman (monetarist economics)</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Molecular biology pioneers</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Scientist</th><th>Achievement</th></tr>
<tr><td>Werner Arber and Hamilton Smith</td><td>Restriction <b>endonucleases</b> (Nobel 1978, with Daniel Nathans)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kenneth Feldmann</td><td>Transgenic plants (Arabidopsis) using <i>Agrobacterium</i> T-DNA</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kary Mullis</td><td><b>Polymerase chain reaction</b> (PCR, 1983; Nobel 1993)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Howard Temin and David Baltimore</td><td><b>Reverse transcriptase</b> (1970; Nobel 1975)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Joshua Lederberg</td><td>Transduction and conjugation in bacteria (Nobel 1958)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thomas Hunt Morgan</td><td><b>Sex-linked inheritance</b> in fruit flies (Nobel 1933)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Arthur Kornberg</td><td>Isolated <b>DNA polymerase</b> from <i>E. coli</i> (Nobel 1959, with Severo Ochoa)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Har Gobind Khorana</td><td>Completed the <b>genetic code</b> (Nobel 1968, with Nirenberg and Holley)</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Nobel Prizes in Physiology or Medicine often asked</h4>
<ul>
<li>2003: Paul Lauterbur and Peter Mansfield, for <b>magnetic resonance imaging</b>.</li>
<li>2006: Andrew Fire and Craig Mello, for RNA interference, which governs the <b>flow of genetic information</b> (DNA to mRNA to protein). Roger Kornberg won Chemistry the same year for the basis of transcription.</li>
<li>Later prizes: see 3.1 (2022, Pääbo), 4.3 (2023 mRNA; 2025 regulatory T cells), 5.1 (2024 microRNA).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Inventors and technology names</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Christopher Cockerell</b>: hovercraft (1955). <b>David Bushnell</b>: the <i>Turtle</i> (1775), the first submarine used in war. <b>J.C. Périer</b>: an early steamboat (1775, France).</li>
<li><b>Denis Papin</b>: the steam digester (1679), forerunner of the pressure cooker. <b>Max Planck</b>: quantum theory (Nobel 1918). Ether anaesthesia was first shown publicly by William Morton (1846).</li>
<li><b>Adam Osborne</b>: the Osborne 1 (1981), the first commercially successful portable computer.</li>
<li><b>Sergey Brin</b> co-founded Google with Larry Page (1998); <b>Jimmy Wales</b> co-founded Wikipedia (2001) with Larry Sanger.</li>
<li>The first human embryo cloning claims came from <b>South Korea</b> (Hwang Woo-suk, later exposed as fraud) and the <b>UK</b> (Newcastle, 2005); <b>Germany</b> bans embryo cloning under its Embryo Protection Act (1990).</li>
<li><b>Belle II</b> is a <b>particle physics</b> experiment (SuperKEKB, Japan) on matter–antimatter asymmetry, not AI. CRISPR-Cas9 belongs to biotechnology; blockchain to digital currency.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Indian scientists</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Person</th><th>Field</th></tr>
<tr><td>C.N.R. Rao</td><td>Solid-state and materials chemistry; Bharat Ratna 2014</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jagdish Bhagwati</td><td>Trade economics (free-trade advocate)</td></tr>
<tr><td>G.N. Ramachandran</td><td>Physics/biophysics: Ramachandran plot, collagen triple helix</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ashok Jhunjhunwala</td><td>Telecommunication (IIT Madras TeNeT group)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Midas Communication Technologies</b> (Chennai, with IIT Madras's TeNeT) won the <b>CSIR Diamond Jubilee Technology Award 2004</b> for corDECT wireless-in-local-loop and optiMA fibre access for cheap rural telephony.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Big science projects with India</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Project</th><th>Place</th><th>India's role</th></tr>
<tr><td>CERN / Large Hadron Collider</td><td>Geneva</td><td>Associate member (2017)</td></tr>
<tr><td>ITER</td><td>France</td><td>Full member (see 7.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>LIGO-India</td><td>Hingoli, Maharashtra</td><td>Host (see 6.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thirty Meter Telescope</td><td>Hawaii (planned)</td><td>Partner</td></tr>
<tr><td>Square Kilometre Array</td><td>South Africa, Australia</td><td>Joining approved (2023)</td></tr>
<tr><td>India-based Neutrino Observatory</td><td>Theni, Tamil Nadu</td><td>Stalled</td></tr>
<tr><td>FAIR</td><td>Germany</td><td>Partner</td></tr>
<tr><td>Belle II</td><td>Japan</td><td>Participating institutions</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Indian science institutions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>CSIR</b> (1942) is an autonomous society under the Department of Scientific and Industrial Research. Its <b>President is the Prime Minister</b>, the Science and Technology Minister is Vice-President, and the Director-General runs it.</li>
<li><b>National Innovation Foundation</b> (2000, Ahmedabad, now Gandhinagar) is an <b>autonomous body of DST</b> supporting <b>grassroots innovation</b> and traditional knowledge (from Anil Gupta's Honey Bee Network; examples such as the Mitticool clay fridge), not elite research with foreign institutions. Other bodies: Atal Innovation Mission (NITI Aayog), BIRAC (DBT), NIDHI (DST), Anusandhan National Research Foundation (2023 Act).</li>
<li><b>Vigyan Prasar</b> (1989) is under <b>DST</b>, not HRD. <b>Vigyan Rail</b> (2003–04, a science exhibition train) was a DST initiative, not CSIR's. <b>EDUSAT</b> (GSAT-3), India's first education satellite, was launched from <b>Sriharikota</b> by GSLV (20 September 2004), not French Guiana.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Institute</th><th>Location</th></tr>
<tr><td>National Centre for Cell Science (DBT)</td><td><b>Pune</b> (Mysore has CFTRI, food technology, and the Defence Food Research Laboratory)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Centre for DNA Fingerprinting and Diagnostics (DBT)</td><td>Hyderabad</td></tr>
<tr><td>National Brain Research Centre (DBT)</td><td>Manesar, Gurgaon</td></tr>
<tr><td>Central Institute of Medicinal and Aromatic Plants (CSIR)</td><td>Lucknow</td></tr>
<tr><td>Institute of Microbial Technology (CSIR)</td><td>Chandigarh</td></tr>
<tr><td>National Institute of Immunology (DBT)</td><td>New Delhi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indian Institute of Geomagnetism (DST)</td><td>Navi Mumbai (Colaba observatory, 1841)</td></tr>
<tr><td>ARCI, powder metallurgy and new materials (DST)</td><td>Hyderabad</td></tr>
<tr><td>Salim Ali Centre for Ornithology and Natural History</td><td>Coimbatore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tropical Forest Research Institute (ICFRE)</td><td>Jabalpur</td></tr>
<tr><td>Central Water and Power Research Station (1916)</td><td><b>Khadakwasla</b>, Pune (hydraulic models for dams, rivers, ports)</td></tr>
</table>
`);

addBookTopic("science", "s10-2", `
<p>Applied technology questions ask how everyday and strategic systems work: water treatment, weather forecasting, sensors, radar, drones, 3D printing, standards and ocean exploration.</p>

<h3>Water treatment</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Chlorination</b> disinfects mainly through <b>hypochlorous acid</b> (Cl2 + H2O → HOCl + HCl). Uncharged HOCl enters microbes and oxidises their enzymes, and it is far more germicidal than the hypochlorite ion. The action is not due to hydrochloric acid, "nascent oxygen" or hydrogen. It leaves a residual disinfectant in the pipes.</li>
<li><b>UV</b> (UV-C, about 254 nm) <b>kills or inactivates microorganisms</b> by damaging their DNA and RNA, without chemicals. It does <b>not remove odours</b> (activated carbon does) and does <b>not settle particles or reduce turbidity</b>; cloudy water actually weakens UV, so water is filtered first.</li>
<li><b>Reverse osmosis</b> removes dissolved salts and heavy metals; the NGT asked to restrict RO where TDS is below 500 mg/L because it wastes water (2019). Ozonation disinfects and removes odour. The BIS drinking water standard is IS 10500.</li>
<li><b>Membrane bioreactors</b> are <b>wastewater treatment</b> technology: the activated sludge process (microbes eat organic pollutants) plus membrane filtration, giving reusable water in a small footprint.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Sewage treatment stage</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
<tr><td>Preliminary</td><td>Screening, grit removal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Primary</td><td>Settling of suspended solids</td></tr>
<tr><td>Secondary</td><td>Microbes consume dissolved organics: activated sludge, trickling filters, SBR, MBBR, MBR, UASB (anaerobic, yields biogas)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tertiary</td><td>Nutrient removal, disinfection (chlorine, UV, ozone), RO</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Urban India treats only about 40% of its sewage (CPCB 2021). Programmes: Namami Gange, AMRUT 2.0, National Framework on Safe Reuse of Treated Water (2022). Constructed wetlands are a nature-based alternative.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Railway bio-toilets</b> (Railways with DRDO; all coaches fitted by 2019): waste is digested by an <b>anaerobic bacterial inoculum</b> (from cow dung, first developed for soldiers in Siachen), not fungi. The end products are <b>methane, CO2 and water</b>, not just ammonia and water vapour. Clogging by plastic is the main problem.</li>
<li><b>Low Temperature Thermal Desalination</b>: the first plant of 1 lakh litres a day was commissioned at <b>Kavaratti</b>, Lakshadweep (2005, NIOT). It uses warm surface seawater evaporated at low pressure and condensed with cold deep water, the same temperature difference that OTEC (ocean thermal energy conversion) uses to make power.</li>
<li><b>Recirculating aquaculture</b>: water is filtered and reused (90–99%). The <b>biofilter</b> hosts nitrifying bacteria that turn <b>toxic ammonia into nitrate</b> (via nitrite: <i>Nitrosomonas</i>, then <i>Nitrobacter</i>), and per UPSC's key helps treat waste such as uneaten feed. It does <b>not add phosphorus</b>, which is a pollutant to be removed. Related: biofloc, aquaponics; PM Matsya Sampada Yojana (2020) funds them. India is the second-largest fish producer.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Weather and oceans</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Cloud seeding</b> adds particles that act as ice or condensation nuclei: <b>silver iodide</b> (its crystal is like ice) and <b>potassium iodide</b>, dry ice for cold clouds, and salts (sodium or calcium chloride) for warm clouds. It needs moisture-laden clouds and cannot create them, which is why Delhi's 2025 trials for pollution (IIT Kanpur) were hard. IITM's CAIPEEX found 18% more rain in the Solapur rain-shadow area. Concerns: silver toxicity, uncertain effect, "stealing" rain from downwind areas.</li>
<li><b>Bharat Forecast System</b> (May 2025) was developed by <b>IITM Pune</b>, not IIT Delhi. It runs at about 6 km resolution, the finest of any operational global model, to forecast at the level of <b>clusters of panchayats</b>, especially for extreme rain. It runs on the Arka supercomputer (11.77 petaflops, 2025).</li>
<li>Ministry of Earth Sciences bodies: IMD (1875), IITM Pune, NCMRWF Noida, INCOIS Hyderabad (tsunami warning), NCPOR Goa (polar research), NIOT Chennai. Mission Mausam (September 2024, ₹2,000 crore) adds radars, AI forecasting and a cloud chamber. IMD warnings are colour-coded green, yellow, orange, red.</li>
<li><b>Deep Ocean Mission</b> (June 2021, about ₹4,077 crore) is run by the <b>Ministry of Earth Sciences</b>, not the Ministry of Ports. Its project <b>Samudrayaan</b> uses <b>Matsya-6000</b> (NIOT), a submersible designed to carry <b>3 people</b> to 6,000 m in a titanium sphere (12 hours normal, 96 hours emergency endurance); shallow trials were held in 2025. Other pillars: deep-sea mining, ocean climate advice, biodiversity, survey of hydrothermal sulphides, ocean energy and freshwater, and a marine biology station. India holds International Seabed Authority contracts for polymetallic nodules in the Central Indian Ocean Basin (since 1987) and sulphides on the Indian Ocean ridge.</li>
<li><b>IndARC</b> (2014) is India's first <b>underwater moored observatory</b> in the <b>Arctic</b> (Kongsfjorden, Svalbard), studying the Arctic–monsoon link. Other polar facts: Himadri Arctic station (2008), Arctic Council observer (2013), Arctic Policy (2022), Indian Antarctic Act 2022, Himansh station in Spiti (2016). Antarctic stations: Dakshin Gangotri (1983), <b>Maitri</b> (1989), <b>Bharati</b> (2012). The <b>South Asia Satellite</b> (GSAT-9, 2017) serves neighbouring countries.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Sensors and radar</h3>
<ul>
<li>An <b>accelerometer</b> (a tiny MEMS chip) measures acceleration and the direction of gravity. It <b>triggers airbags</b> on sudden deceleration, detects a laptop's <b>free fall</b> and parks the hard disk head, and senses <b>tilt</b> to rotate a phone's screen.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Sensor</th><th>Use</th></tr>
<tr><td>Accelerometer</td><td>Acceleration, tilt, steps, falls</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gyroscope</td><td>Rotation: gaming, image stabilisation, VR</td></tr>
<tr><td>Magnetometer</td><td>Compass</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proximity sensor</td><td>Turns off the screen during calls</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ambient light sensor</td><td>Auto-brightness</td></tr>
<tr><td>Barometer</td><td>Air pressure, altitude</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Radar</b> sends radio pulses and reads echoes. It is used to <b>monitor precipitation</b> (Doppler weather radar) and <b>track animal migration</b> (birds, bats, locust swarms), as well as for air traffic control, ship navigation, speed guns and synthetic aperture imaging from satellites (RISAT, NISAR). <b>Narcotics on passengers</b> are found by X-ray and millimetre-wave scanners, trace detectors and sniffer dogs, <b>not radar</b>. LiDAR uses lasers; sonar uses sound underwater.</li>
<li><b>Black boxes</b> (painted bright orange) are the <b>cockpit voice recorder and flight data recorder</b>. Their memory units are encased in <b>stainless steel or titanium</b>, built to survive 3,400 g and 1,100°C for an hour. Their underwater locator beacon sends <b>ultrasonic pings at 37.5 kHz</b>, not red light. They sit in the tail. They were invented by David Warren (Australia); ICAO raised voice recording to 25 hours for new aircraft (2021). India's AAIB opened a recorder lab in Delhi (2025) and read the recorders of the Air India AI171 crash (Ahmedabad, June 2025) at home.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Drones and 3D printing</h3>
<ul>
<li>Drones can <b>spray pesticides</b> (Namo Drone Didi, 2023, gave drones to women's self-help groups), <b>inspect active volcano craters</b>, and <b>collect whale breath</b> for DNA ("SnotBot").</li>
<li><b>Drone Rules 2021</b> created green, yellow and red zones on the Digital Sky platform. Weight classes: nano (under 250 g), micro (to 2 kg), small (to 25 kg), medium (to 150 kg), large. No remote pilot certificate is needed for non-commercial drones up to 2 kg. Foreign drone imports were banned in 2022, with a PLI scheme for domestic makers. SVAMITVA maps village land by drone.</li>
<li><b>3D printing</b> (additive manufacturing) builds objects layer by layer. It is used for <b>confectionery</b>, <b>bionic ears</b> and bioprinted tissue, <b>automotive</b> and aerospace parts (ISRO's 3D-printed PS4 engine, 2024; Agnikul's single-piece engine), <b>reconstructive surgery</b> implants, and, per UPSC, <b>data processing</b> (printed electronics). India's National Strategy for Additive Manufacturing dates from 2022; Bengaluru got a 3D-printed post office (2023). 4D-printed objects change shape over time. Concerns: printed guns, IP theft.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Standards</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>BIS</b> (Bureau of Indian Standards Act 2016; Ministry of Consumer Affairs; successor of the Indian Standards Institution, 1947) issues the ISI mark, gold and silver Hallmark (HUID mandatory since 2021), Eco Mark and compulsory registration for electronics. Quality Control Orders make certification compulsory for notified goods; some were rolled back in 2025. India is a founding member of ISO.</li>
<li><b>IS 19445:2025</b> is India's first standard for <b>testing bomb disposal systems</b> (containment vessels, suits, robots). It makes equipment comparable and <b>improves interoperability</b> across police, CAPFs and the forces. It was developed with DRDO's <b>Terminal Ballistics Research Laboratory</b> (Chandigarh), with no Russian partner. Other DRDO labs: HEMRL (Pune), DRDL (Hyderabad), CVRDE (Chennai), ADE (Bengaluru).</li>
<li><b>Paper sizes</b>: A0 has an area of <b>1 square metre</b> (841 × 1,189 mm). Each step halves the area, so A4 (210 × 297 mm) is <b>1/16</b> of A0 (A3 is 1/8).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
