// Modern History book, Unit 1: The coming of the Europeans and British expansion.
addBookTopic("modern", "m1-1", `
<p>In the 18th century the Mughal empire broke up into regional states, while European trading companies competed along the coasts. The English came out on top by 1760 and then turned trade into territorial rule.</p>

<h3>Decline of the Mughals and rise of regional powers</h3>
<ul>
<li>After Aurangzeb (died 1707), weak successors, court factions, the jagirdari crisis, Nadir Shah's raid (1739, which took the Peacock Throne) and Ahmad Shah Abdali's invasions broke central power.</li>
<li>Successor states: Bengal (Murshid Quli Khan), Awadh (Saadat Khan), Hyderabad (Nizam-ul-Mulk). New states: Marathas, Sikhs, Jats, Rohillas, Mysore (Haidar Ali).</li>
<li><b>Marathas</b>: after Sambhaji's execution (1689), his brother Raja Ram and then Raja Ram's widow Tarabai kept up resistance from Jinji and Kolhapur. After and the long war with Aurangzeb, Shahu was released (1707) and won the civil war against Tarabai. <b>Balaji Vishwanath</b>, made Peshwa in 1713, <b>streamlined the Maratha administration</b> and revenue, made the Peshwa's office powerful, and won Mughal recognition of <b>chauth</b> (one-fourth of revenue) and <b>sardeshmukhi</b> (an extra tenth) in 1719. Under Baji Rao I and Balaji Baji Rao (Nana Saheb) the Marathas became the <b>strongest Indian power</b>, collecting dues from Delhi to Tanjore.</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b> The Marathas were the strongest power after the Mughals, but they were a confederacy (Peshwa, Scindia, Holkar, Gaekwad, Bhonsle) pursuing regional interests. They did not have a clear idea of a united Indian nation; that idea came with 19th-century nationalism.</div>
<ul>
<li><b>Third Battle of Panipat</b> (14 January 1761): Ahmad Shah Abdali defeated the Marathas under Sadashivrao Bhau. It checked Maratha expansion north and helped the English. (The First Battle of Panipat, 1526, was Babur against Ibrahim Lodi; the Second, 1556, was Akbar against Hemu.)</li>
</ul>

<h3>The European companies</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Company</th><th>Key dates and settlements</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Portuguese</b></td><td>Vasco da Gama at Calicut (1498); Cochin factory (1500). <b>Albuquerque captured Goa from the Bijapur Sultanate</b> (Yusuf Adil Shah) in 1510, and Goa became their capital in the East. Bombay went to the English as Catherine of Braganza's dowry (1661). Hooghly was lost to Shah Jahan (1632)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dutch</b> (VOC, 1602)</td><td>Masulipatnam (1605, with permission from the <b>Golconda</b> sultan), Pulicat (1610, with consent of the Vijayanagara ruler at Chandragiri), Surat, Chinsura, Nagapattinam. Not on land from the Gajapatis, whose dynasty had ended about 1541. Defeated by Travancore at <b>Colachel</b> (1741) and by the English at <b>Bedara</b> (1759)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>English</b> (1600)</td><td>Masulipatnam (1611); Surat (1613, after Jahangir's farman; Thomas Roe's embassy 1615). <b>Madras</b> (1639): Francis Day got the grant from Damarla Venkatadri Nayaka, a chief under the <b>Vijayanagara</b> king at Chandragiri; Fort St George was built there. Bombay from the Crown (1668). Calcutta (1690, Job Charnock; Fort William)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Danish (1616)</td><td>Tranquebar (1620), Serampore (1755); sold to Britain in 1845</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>French</b> (1664, Colbert)</td><td>Surat (1668), Masulipatnam (1669), Pondicherry (1674), Chandernagore</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Pondicherry</b>: the <b>Portuguese</b> were the first Europeans to trade there (early 16th century). The Danes and Dutch had interests before the French settlement of 1674 under François Martin, and the Dutch captured it in 1693. The <b>English captured it several times</b> (1761 under Eyre Coote, 1778, 1793) before returning it to France in 1816. It joined India de facto in 1954.</li>
<li>Farrukhsiyar's <b>farman of 1717</b> gave the English duty-free trade in Bengal for ₹3,000 a year; misuse of these passes (dastaks) by Company servants caused conflict with the Nawabs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Anglo-French rivalry and the conquest of Bengal</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Battle</th><th>Year</th><th>Significance</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Ambur</b></td><td>1749</td><td>Opened the Second Carnatic War; Anwaruddin of the Carnatic killed by Chanda Sahib and Muzaffar Jang with French help</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Plassey</b></td><td>23 June 1757</td><td>Clive defeated Siraj-ud-Daulah after <b>Mir Jafar</b>, Siraj's commander, conspired with the English (with Jagat Seth and Omichand) and held back; Mir Jafar became Nawab</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Wandiwash</b></td><td>1760</td><td>Eyre Coote defeated the French under Lally; ended French power in India</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Buxar</b></td><td>22 October 1764</td><td>Hector Munro defeated Mir Qasim, Shuja-ud-Daulah of Awadh and Shah Alam II. Treaty of Allahabad (1765) gave the Company the <b>Diwani</b> of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So the order is Ambur, Plassey, Wandiwash, Buxar. The three Carnatic Wars ran from 1746 to 1763; Dupleix pioneered using European troops to back Indian rulers. Clive's "dual government" in Bengal (1765–72) gave the Company revenue without responsibility and was followed by the Bengal famine of 1770.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m1-2", `
<p>Between 1765 and 1856 the Company extended its rule through wars, alliances and annexations, until it controlled most of the subcontinent directly or through dependent princes.</p>

<h3>Wars of expansion</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>War</th><th>Dates</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
<tr><td>First Anglo-Mysore</td><td><b>1767–69</b></td><td>Haidar Ali forced a treaty of mutual help (Madras, 1769)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second Anglo-Mysore</td><td>1780–84</td><td>Treaty of Mangalore</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Third Anglo-Mysore</b></td><td><b>1790–92</b></td><td>Cornwallis; Treaty of Seringapatam: Tipu lost half his kingdom</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fourth Anglo-Mysore</td><td>1799</td><td>Wellesley; <b>Tipu killed</b> defending Srirangapatna; Wodeyars restored under subsidiary alliance</td></tr>
<tr><td>First Anglo-Maratha</td><td><b>1775–82</b></td><td>Treaty of Salbai</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second Anglo-Maratha</td><td>1803–05</td><td>After the Treaty of Bassein (1802)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third Anglo-Maratha</td><td>1817–18</td><td>Hastings; Peshwa's office abolished</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>First Anglo-Burmese</b></td><td><b>1824–26</b></td><td>Treaty of Yandabo; Assam, Arakan, Tenasserim ceded</td></tr>
<tr><td>First Anglo-Sikh</td><td><b>1845–46</b></td><td>Treaty of Lahore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second Anglo-Sikh</td><td>1848–49</td><td>Punjab annexed by Dalhousie</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third Anglo-Burmese</td><td>1885</td><td><b>Upper Burma annexed</b> (1 January 1886) under <b>Lord Dufferin</b>: the last major extension of British Indian territory</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>After the Diwani (1765), control of Sylhet brought the British into contact first with the <b>Khasis</b>, who traded limestone and oranges with Sylhet's plains (per UPSC's key; some histories say the Garos at about the same time). Tirot Sing's Khasi uprising (1829–33) opposed a road through the hills from the Brahmaputra valley to Sylhet.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Subsidiary Alliance</h3>
<p>Lord Wellesley's system (1798) required an Indian ruler to:</p>
<ul>
<li>keep a British force in his territory and pay for it, in cash or by ceding land;</li>
<li>accept a British Resident at his court;</li>
<li>expel other Europeans, especially the French;</li>
<li>conduct foreign relations only through the Company.</li>
</ul>
<p>Its aims were to keep a large army at others' expense, shut out Napoleonic France and make the Company paramount. <b>Securing a fixed income for the Company was not its purpose</b>; the payments covered troop costs, often through ceded territory.</p>
<ul>
<li>Signatories: Hyderabad (1798, first), Mysore (1799), Tanjore (1799), Awadh (1801, ceding Rohilkhand and the lower Doab), the Peshwa (Treaty of Bassein, 1802), Scindia and Bhonsle (1803), Gaekwad, Travancore.</li>
<li>Precedents: Dupleix's loans of troops; Warren Hastings' "ring fence" policy of buffer states.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Doctrine of Lapse and annexation for misrule</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under Dalhousie's (1848–56) <b>Doctrine of Lapse</b>, a dependent state whose ruler died without a natural heir passed to the Company; adopted heirs were not recognised. In order: <b>Satara (1848)</b>, Jaitpur and <b>Sambalpur (1849)</b>, Baghat (1850), Udaipur (1852), <b>Jhansi (1853</b>; Rani Lakshmibai's adopted son Damodar Rao was refused) and Nagpur (1854).</li>
<li><b>Awadh</b> was annexed in February 1856 not by lapse but on the <b>pretext of misgovernance</b>: Nawab Wajid Ali Shah was deposed and exiled to Calcutta, although Awadh had been a loyal ally. It angered the many sepoys from Awadh and fed the 1857 revolt (see 4.2).</li>
<li>Dalhousie also annexed Punjab (1849) and Lower Burma (1852), and launched railways (1853), the telegraph and the postal system.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Person</th><th>Associated with</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dalhousie</b></td><td>Doctrine of Lapse (1848–56)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Chelmsford</b></td><td>Dyarchy in the provinces (1919 Act)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Ramsay MacDonald</b> (British PM)</td><td>Communal Award (August 1932)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Linlithgow</b> (Viceroy)</td><td>August Offer (1940)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The Queen's Proclamation of 1858 ended the policy of annexation (see 4.2). British policy towards princely states moved from ring fence (to 1813), to subordinate isolation (to 1857), to subordinate union, and to proposed federation in 1935 (see 2.3).</p>
`);
;
// data/book_modern_m2.js
// Modern History book, Unit 2: Colonial state and administration.
addBookTopic("modern", "m2-1", `
<p>Parliament steadily took control of the East India Company's Indian territories, first by regulating it and finally, after 1857, by transferring power to the Crown.</p>

<h3>The Acts in order</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Act</th><th>Key provisions</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Regulating Act 1773</b></td><td>First step of parliamentary control. The Governor of Bengal became <b>Governor-General of Bengal</b> (<b>Warren Hastings</b> first) with a council of four; Bombay and Madras made subordinate. <b>Supreme Court at Calcutta</b> (1774, Chief Justice Elijah Impey). Directors had to <b>place revenue, civil and military correspondence before the British government</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Pitt's India Act 1784</b></td><td>A <b>Board of Control</b> in Britain to regulate the Company's political affairs, alongside the Court of Directors (commercial): "double government". Territories called "British possessions in India"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Charter Act 1793</td><td>Charter renewed for 20 years; Board of Control members paid from Indian revenue</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Charter Act 1813</b></td><td><b>Ended the Company's trade monopoly</b> in India <b>except tea and trade with China</b>. <b>Asserted the Crown's sovereignty</b> over the Company's territories. ₹1 lakh a year for education (see 5.2). Missionaries allowed. Revenues stayed with the Company (not Parliament)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Charter Act 1833</b></td><td>The Governor-General of Bengal became <b>Governor-General of India</b> (<b>William Bentinck</b> first). Company's commercial functions ended entirely; it became purely administrative. A fourth (law) member added to the council: <b>Macaulay</b>, not an Indian. Law Commission for codification. Said no Indian should be barred from office by religion or birth (not implemented). Ended the separate legislative powers of Bombay and Madras</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Charter Act 1853</b></td><td>The last Charter Act. Separated legislative and executive functions of the council (a 12-member legislative council). <b>Open competition</b> for the civil services (Macaulay Committee, 1854)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Government of India Act 1858</b></td><td>After 1857, Parliament ended Company rule and the Crown ruled directly. A <b>Secretary of State for India</b> with a 15-member India Council; Board of Control and Court of Directors abolished; the Governor-General also became <b>Viceroy</b> (Canning first). The Company itself was formally dissolved in 1874</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b> Clive was <b>Governor</b> of Bengal (1757–60, 1765–67), not Governor-General. The first Governor-General of Bengal was Warren Hastings (1773); the first Governor-General of India was Bentinck (1833); the first Viceroy was Canning (1858). The monopoly of Indian trade ended in 1813, not 1853.</div>

<h3>Governors-General and Viceroys</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Ruler</th><th>Tenure</th><th>Associated with</th></tr>
<tr><td>Warren Hastings</td><td>1773–85</td><td>Regulating Act, <b>Pitt's India Act</b>, Asiatic Society (1784)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cornwallis</td><td>1786–93</td><td>Permanent Settlement, civil service, police, Cornwallis Code (see 2.3, 3.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wellesley</td><td>1798–1805</td><td>Subsidiary Alliance, Fort William College</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bentinck</td><td>1828–35</td><td>Sati abolished (1829), English education (1835)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dalhousie</td><td>1848–56</td><td>Doctrine of Lapse, railways, telegraph</td></tr>
<tr><td>Canning</td><td>1856–62</td><td>1857, first Viceroy, portfolio system</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lytton</td><td>1876–80</td><td><b>Vernacular Press Act</b> (1878), Delhi Durbar 1877, Arms Act</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ripon</td><td>1880–84</td><td><b>Ilbert Bill</b>, local self-government, repeal of the Press Act, Factories Act 1881</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dufferin</td><td>1884–88</td><td>Congress founded; Upper Burma annexed</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Curzon</b></td><td>1899–1905</td><td>Partition of Bengal (1905), Universities Act 1904</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minto II</td><td>1905–10</td><td>Morley-Minto Reforms (1909)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Hardinge II</b></td><td>1910–16</td><td>Delhi Durbar 1911, Bengal partition annulled, capital to Delhi; bomb attack (1912)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Chelmsford</b></td><td>1916–21</td><td>Montford Reforms, Rowlatt Act, Jallianwala Bagh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reading</td><td>1921–26</td><td>Chauri Chaura, Swarajists</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Irwin</b></td><td>1926–31</td><td>Simon Commission, Dandi March, Gandhi–Irwin Pact</td></tr>
<tr><td>Willingdon</td><td>1931–36</td><td>Communal Award, Poona Pact</td></tr>
<tr><td>Linlithgow</td><td>1936–43</td><td>August Offer, Cripps, Quit India</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wavell</td><td>1943–47</td><td>Simla Conference, Cabinet Mission</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mountbatten</td><td>1947–48</td><td>Partition plan</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So Curzon, Hardinge, Chelmsford, Irwin is the correct chronological order.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m2-2", `
<p>After 1858 the British slowly brought Indians into legislatures, first as nominees and then through limited elections, while keeping real power. Each step answered nationalist pressure.</p>

<h3>The Acts of 1861, 1892 and 1909</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Indian Councils Act 1861</b>:
<ul><li>It gave legal backing to Lord Canning's <b>portfolio system</b>: each council member headed a department and could issue orders, which <b>strengthened the Viceroy over his council</b>.</li>
<li>The Viceroy could issue ordinances.</li>
<li>He could nominate Indians as non-official members of the legislative council (Raja of Benares, Maharaja of Patiala, Dinkar Rao, 1862).</li>
<li>Legislative powers were restored to Bombay and Madras.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Indian Councils Act 1892</b>: enlarged councils, indirect election (nomination on recommendation of bodies), and members could discuss the budget and ask questions.</li>
<li><b>Indian Councils Act 1909 (Morley-Minto)</b>: enlarged councils with an elected element and introduced <b>separate electorates for Muslims</b>. S.P. Sinha became the first Indian on the Viceroy's Executive Council (Law Member). Lord Minto is called the "father of communal electorates" (see 6.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Government of India Act 1919</h3>
<p>Montagu's <b>August Declaration (1917)</b> promised the "progressive realisation of responsible government". The <b>Montagu–Chelmsford Report</b> (1918), by Secretary of State Edwin Montagu and Viceroy Chelmsford, was about <b>constitutional reforms</b> and formed the basis of the 1919 Act.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Devolution</b>: the Devolution Rules <b>clearly defined the jurisdiction of the central and provincial governments</b> by dividing subjects into central and provincial lists; provincial budgets were separated.</li>
<li><b>Dyarchy</b> in the provinces (scheme credited to Lionel Curtis) meant <b>dividing provincial subjects into two categories</b>:
<ul><li><b>Reserved</b> subjects were run by the Governor and his executive council, not responsible to the legislature: <b>police, justice, land revenue</b>, irrigation, finance, prisons, famine relief.</li>
<li><b>Transferred</b> subjects were run by Indian ministers responsible to the legislature: <b>local self-government</b>, education, public health, agriculture, industries, public works, excise.</li></ul></li>
<li>A <b>bicameral</b> central legislature (Council of State and Legislative Assembly), with a majority elected directly on a limited franchise (property, tax, education; about 10% of adults).</li>
<li>Communal electorates <b>extended</b> to Sikhs, Indian Christians, Anglo-Indians and Europeans; separate electorates for Muslims were not new, since they came in 1909.</li>
<li>Three of the Viceroy's six councillors (other than the Commander-in-Chief) were to be Indians. The Act also created a High Commissioner for India in London and provided for a Public Service Commission (set up in 1926 after the Lee Commission). A statutory commission was to review it after ten years, which became the Simon Commission (1927).</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b> Dyarchy was introduced in the <b>provinces</b> in 1919; the 1935 Act abolished it there and proposed it at the <b>Centre</b>, where it was never implemented.</div>
<ul>
<li>Congress called the reforms "disappointing and unsatisfactory". Moderates who welcomed them left to form the <b>Indian Liberal Federation</b> (1918; see 7.2). The Chamber of Princes was set up in 1921.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Women's franchise</h3>
<ul>
<li>The 1919 Act <b>left women's vote to provincial legislatures</b>; it did not give every woman over 21 the vote. <b>Madras (1921)</b> was the first province to let women vote, on the same property qualifications as men. In 1917 Sarojini Naidu had led a women's delegation (Women's Indian Association, Annie Besant, Margaret Cousins) to Montagu.</li>
<li>Muthulakshmi Reddy became the first woman legislator in British India (Madras, 1927).</li>
<li>The <b>1935 Act</b> gave women <b>reserved seats</b> in the legislatures and widened female franchise (about 60 lakh women voters).</li>
<li>Universal adult franchise came with the Constitution (Article 326; voting age cut from 21 to 18 by the 61st Amendment, 1988). The 106th Amendment (2023) reserves one-third of Lok Sabha and Assembly seats for women after the next census and delimitation.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m2-3", `
<p>The British built a centralised bureaucracy, police and court system, largely in Cornwallis's time, and governed the princely states indirectly through paramountcy.</p>

<h3>Civil service, police and courts</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Cornwallis</b> (1786–93), the "father of the civil services in India", created the <b>Covenanted Civil Service</b> (later the Indian Civil Service): well paid, rule-bound, banned from private trade and gifts, with higher posts reserved for Europeans.</li>
<li>Cornwallis, not Warren Hastings, organised a <b>regular police force</b> on the British pattern (thanas under daroghas, 1791–93).</li>
<li>The <b>Cornwallis Code (1793)</b> <b>stripped the District Collector of judicial powers</b> and created a separate District Judge, because <b>concentrating absolute power in one person</b> (both assessing revenue and judging disputes about it) was undesirable: the separation of powers.</li>
<li>The <b>Supreme Court at Calcutta</b> was set up under the Regulating Act 1773 (1774). The <b>Indian Penal Code</b>, drafted by Macaulay's First Law Commission, was enacted in 1860 (it came into force on 1 January 1862); the Criminal Procedure Code followed in 1861 and High Courts in 1861–62. The IPC was replaced by the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023 from 1 July 2024.</li>
<li>Indianisation: the 1853 Act opened the civil service to open competition. <b>Satyendranath Tagore</b> became the first Indian to pass the ICS (1863). The maximum age was cut to 19 under Lytton (1878), which led to protests; the Lee Commission (1924) led to the Public Service Commission (1926).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Ilbert Bill</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under Viceroy <b>Ripon</b>, Law Member C.P. Ilbert's Bill (1883) sought to <b>put Indians and Europeans on par in criminal jurisdiction</b> by <b>removing the disqualification on Indian magistrates and judges trying Europeans</b>.</li>
<li>A fierce European agitation (the "white mutiny") forced a compromise (1884): a European could demand a jury at least half European. Indians learned how racial British rule was, and the episode spurred organised nationalism (Congress, 1885).</li>
<li>The Bill is often confused with Lytton's measures: the <b>Vernacular Press Act</b> (1878) restricted Indian-language newspapers, and the <b>Arms Act</b> (1878) barred Indians from carrying arms without a licence. Ripon is also called the "father of local self-government" (1882 resolution). He repealed the Vernacular Press Act (1882), passed the first Factories Act (1881), set up the Hunter Education Commission (1882) and held the first regular census (1881).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Princely states</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Phase</th><th>Policy</th></tr>
<tr><td>1757–1813</td><td>Ring fence (Warren Hastings)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1813–1857</td><td>Subordinate isolation: Subsidiary Alliance, Doctrine of Lapse (see 1.2)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1858–1935</td><td>Subordinate union: annexation ended; Chamber of Princes (1921)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1935–1947</td><td>Equal federation proposed (see 8.1)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Butler Committee</b> (Indian States Committee, 1927–29, Sir Harcourt Butler) was set up <b>to improve relations between the Government of India and the Indian States</b>, including financial relations. Its report said paramountcy must remain paramount, that the princes' relationship was with the Crown directly, and that no state could be handed to a responsible Indian government without its consent.</li>
<li>About 565 princely states covered two-fifths of India. Popular movements in them were led by the All India States People's Conference (1927; see 8.1). They were integrated after 1947 (see 9.1).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
