// Modern History book, Unit 3: Colonial economy.
addBookTopic("modern", "m3-1", `
<p>Land revenue was the Company's main income. Each of its three settlements fixed who paid the state, and each reshaped rural society, mostly to the peasant's cost.</p>

<h3>The three settlements</h3>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Permanent Settlement (Zamindari)</th><th>Ryotwari</th><th>Mahalwari</th></tr>
<tr><td>Introduced by</td><td><b>Lord Cornwallis</b>, 1793 (with John Shore)</td><td><b>Alexander Read</b> (Baramahal, Salem, 1792) and <b>Thomas Munro</b> (Madras, 1820); Elphinstone in Bombay</td><td>Holt Mackenzie (1822); revised by R.M. Bird and Thomason (1833) under Bentinck</td></tr>
<tr><td>Area</td><td>Bengal, Bihar, Odisha, Benares (about 19% of British India)</td><td>Madras, Bombay, Assam, Coorg (about 51%)</td><td>North-West Provinces, Punjab, Central Provinces (about 30%)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who paid</td><td>Zamindar, made <b>owner</b> of the land (a revenue farmer turned landlord)</td><td><b>The ryot directly</b>, given a <b>patta</b> (title deed) with rights to sell, mortgage, sublet or gift</td><td>The <b>village community</b> (mahal) jointly, through the lambardar</td></tr>
<tr><td>Demand</td><td>Fixed <b>for ever</b> (10/11 of rent to the state)</td><td>Revised every 20–30 years after <b>survey and assessment by soil</b>; often 45–55% of produce</td><td>Periodically revised</td></tr>
<tr><td>Effects</td><td>Fixed state income; absentee landlords; subinfeudation; peasants rack-rented</td><td>High, rigid demand; moneylender debt; Deccan Riots (1875)</td><td>Village elites dominated</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Jagirdari</b>: land assigned to big feudal lords (a Mughal-era system).</li>
<li>Under the Permanent Settlement's "<b>sunset law</b>", a zamindar who failed to pay by sunset on the due date had his estate auctioned. Many old zamindari families were ruined and replaced by urban buyers.</li>
<li>Zamindars were supposed to issue <b>pattas</b> to cultivators but many did not, because <b>there was no official check on the zamindars</b>. The peasants' rights were left undefined.</li>
<li>Litigation increased after 1793 because tenancy rights were not defined and estates were sold for arrears in the new civil courts. UPSC's key gave "none of the above" to the options offered (debated).</li>
<li>Ryotwari did not exempt peasants from revenue in bad harvests; the demand was rigid. A 2024 question was dropped because it wrongly linked Ryotwari to Cornwallis.</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b> Cornwallis brought the Permanent Settlement, not Ryotwari. Revenue assessment by soil quality and crops was not a British innovation either: Sher Shah and Todar Mal (Akbar's zabt/dahsala) had done it.</div>
<ul>
<li>Not introduced by the English: mobile field artillery (Babur used it at Panipat, 1526) and <b>tobacco and red chillies</b> (brought by the Portuguese from the Americas, along with potato, tomato, maize, pineapple, papaya, cashew, guava and groundnut; the Portuguese also brought the printing press to Goa in 1556).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Commercial crops and indigo</h3>
<ul>
<li>Indigo was grown under the <b>ryoti</b> system (peasants' own land, with advances) and the <b>nij</b> system (planters' own land).</li>
<li><b>Indigo Revolt (1859–60)</b>, Nadia, Bengal: led by Digambar and Bishnu Biswas. Dinabandhu Mitra's play <i>Nil Darpan</i> (1860) was translated by Michael Madhusudan Dutt and published by Rev. James Long, who was fined and jailed. Harish Chandra Mukherjee's <i>Hindu Patriot</i> backed the ryots. An Indigo Commission followed (1860), and indigo ended in Bengal.</li>
<li>Cultivation moved to Bihar (Champaran's tinkathia system; see 7.1).</li>
<li>Indigo declined everywhere by the early 20th century because <b>synthetic indigo</b> (Adolf von Baeyer; marketed by BASF in 1897) made it <b>unprofitable on the world market</b>.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m3-2", `
<p>Colonial rule turned India from an exporter of manufactures into a supplier of raw materials and a market for British goods. Nationalists measured the cost through the "drain of wealth".</p>

<h3>Company trade</h3>
<ul>
<li>In the mid-18th century the Company's staple exports from Bengal were <b>cotton textiles</b> (muslins, calicoes), <b>raw silk</b>, <b>saltpetre</b> (for gunpowder, from Bihar) and <b>opium</b> (a Company monopoly from 1773, sent to China; Opium Wars 1839–42, 1856–60). Tea came mainly from China then; Assam tea began in the 1830s–40s.</li>
<li>Before Plassey the Company imported bullion to buy Indian goods; after the Diwani (1765) it bought them with Bengal's own revenue, and the drain began.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Aurang</b></td><td>A <b>warehouse or workshop</b> where goods (textiles) were collected; not a treasury official</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Banian</b></td><td><b>Indian agent</b>, broker or moneylender of the Company and its officials in Bengal</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mirasidar</b></td><td>Hereditary landholder in Madras who became the <b>registered revenue payer</b> under Ryotwari</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gomastha</td><td>Paid Indian agent who supervised weavers for the Company</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dadni</td><td>Advance paid to artisans</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dastak</td><td>Duty-free trade pass</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jotedar</td><td>Rich peasant in Bengal</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Deindustrialisation and commercialisation</h3>
<ul>
<li>In the first half of the 19th century the Industrial Revolution <b>ruined Indian handicrafts</b>. After the Charter Act of 1813, cheap machine-made British cloth flooded India under one-way free trade (low duties on British imports, high duties on Indian goods in Britain). Dhaka, Murshidabad and Surat declined.</li>
<li>A major 19th-century result was the <b>commercialisation of agriculture</b>: cash crops (cotton, jute, indigo, opium, tea, sugarcane, groundnut) for export, pushed by cash revenue demands and railways. Food crops were displaced, peasants fell into debt, and famines followed: Orissa (1866), the Great Famine (1876–78), 1896–97, 1899–1900, Bengal (1943). Famine Commissions: Strachey (1880), Lyall (1898), MacDonnell (1901). The Deccan Riots (1875) led to the Deccan Agriculturists' Relief Act (1879).</li>
<li>Modern industry: first cotton mill in Bombay (1854), first jute mill at Rishra (1855), TISCO at Jamshedpur (1907). The <b>first telegraph line</b> ran from <b>Calcutta to Diamond Harbour</b> (1851, W.B. O'Shaughnessy) and spread under Dalhousie; railways began in 1853 (Bombay–Thane). (After independence, Asia's first export processing zone opened at Kandla, 1965.)</li>
<li>Banking: the <b>Oudh Commercial Bank</b> (Faizabad, 1881) was the first limited-liability bank managed by Indians; Punjab National Bank followed (1894).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The drain of wealth</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Dadabhai Naoroji</b> (1825–1917), the "Grand Old Man of India", gave the drain theory in <i>Poverty and Un-British Rule in India</i> (1901; first papers 1867–76). He called British rule "<b>un-British</b>" for violating Britain's own principles, and was the first to estimate India's <b>per capita income: about ₹20 a year for 1867–68</b>.</li>
<li>His most effective contribution was <b>exposing the economic exploitation of India</b>. He did not reinterpret ancient texts (that was Vivekananda and Dayanand) or put social reform before everything.</li>
<li>Other <b>economic critics of colonialism</b>: <b>R.C. Dutt</b> (<i>The Economic History of India</i>, 1901–03), <b>G. Subramania Iyer</b> (founder of <i>The Hindu</i> and <i>Swadesamitran</i>; <i>Some Economic Aspects of British Rule in India</i>, 1903), M.G. Ranade ("father of Indian economics"), G.V. Joshi, Prithwis Chandra Ray, and G.K. Gokhale (budget speeches). This economic critique was the Moderates' greatest contribution (see 6.1).</li>
<li><b>Home Charges</b> were spent in Britain from Indian revenue: <b>India Office</b> costs, <b>salaries and pensions of British personnel</b>, interest on India's sterling debt and stores. UPSC did not count funds for British wars outside India, which were charged to Indian revenue separately.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Currency</h3>
<ul>
<li>Chronology: a silver standard (1835); the Fowler Committee (1898) set a gold exchange standard with the rupee at 1s 4d; the Chamberlain Commission (1913, with Keynes as a member); Babington Smith (1919).</li>
<li>The <b>Hilton-Young Commission</b> (1926) fixed the rupee at <b>1s 6d</b> and recommended a central bank, which became the RBI (Act 1934, set up 1 April 1935).</li>
<li>The overvalued ratio <b>eased remittances</b> of Home Charges to London and <b>protected India's creditworthiness</b> for its sterling debt. Nationalists attacked it as a drain device, as they did the 1931 export of distress gold.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Labour laws and the labour movement</h3>
<ul>
<li>The first <b>Factories Act (1881)</b>, under <b>Lord Ripon</b>, dealt with <b>child labour</b>:
<ul><li>no children under 7;</li>
<li>nine hours for children aged 7–12;</li>
<li>fencing of dangerous machinery;</li>
<li>provincial governments could make rules.</li></ul>
It did <b>not fix wages or allow unions</b>; Lancashire mill-owners had pressed for it to curb Indian competition. The 1891 Act limited women's hours to 11 and added a weekly holiday. Later Acts: 1911 (12-hour day for men), 1934 (10 hours), and the Factories Act 1948, now folded into the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code 2020 (labour codes in force from 21 November 2025).</li>
<li><b>N.M. Lokhande</b>, an associate of Jyotiba Phule, was a <b>pioneer of the labour movement</b>. He founded the Bombay Mill Hands Association (1890), won a weekly Sunday holiday, and edited <i>Deenbandhu</i>. Earlier, Sasipada Banerjee had set up a Working Men's Club (1870).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>Event</th></tr>
<tr><td>1918</td><td>Madras Labour Union (B.P. Wadia); Ahmedabad mill strike (Gandhi; see 7.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1920</td><td>AITUC founded (Lala Lajpat Rai president)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1926</td><td>Trade Unions Act (registration, legal immunity)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1928</td><td>Girni Kamgar Union, Bombay (communist)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1929</td><td><b>Trade Disputes Act</b>: courts of inquiry and boards of conciliation (<b>tribunals</b>), and a <b>ban on strikes</b> in public utilities without notice or to coerce the government. Also the Meerut Conspiracy Case against union leaders and the <b>Royal Commission on Labour</b> under J.H. <b>Whitley</b> (1929–31), set up <b>to report on labour conditions and recommend measures</b>; it led to the Factories Act 1934</td></tr>
<tr><td>1947</td><td>Industrial Disputes Act; INTUC founded (see 8.1 for HMS)</td></tr>
</table>
`);
;
// data/book_modern_m4.js
// Modern History book, Unit 4: Resistance before 1885.
addBookTopic("modern", "m4-1", `
<p>Long before organised nationalism, peasants, tribes and displaced groups rose against the Company, landlords and moneylenders. These uprisings were local and were crushed, but they showed how deeply colonial rule disturbed rural life.</p>

<h3>Civil and religious uprisings</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Sanyasi (and Fakir) rebellion</b> (about 1763–1800) in Bengal followed the 1770 famine; wandering ascetics attacked Company and zamindari treasuries. Bankim Chandra Chatterjee's novel <b><i>Anandamath</i></b> (1882), which contains "Vande Mataram", is set against it.</li>
<li>Others: the Paika rebellion (Odisha, 1817, Bakshi Jagabandhu), the Kittur revolt (Rani Chennamma, 1824), the Wahabi movement (Syed Ahmad of Rae Bareli), the Faraizi movement (Haji Shariatullah, Dudu Miyan), the Kuka movement (Punjab), the Pagal Panthis (Karam Shah), and Velu Thampi in Travancore (1809).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tribal uprisings</h3>
<p>The <b>common factor</b> behind 19th-century tribal revolts was the <b>complete disruption of the old agrarian order</b>: colonial land laws, forest restrictions, outsiders (<i>dikus</i>: moneylenders, traders, landlords) and the end of communal landholding.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Revolt</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>Kol (1831–32)</td><td>Chota Nagpur, against land transfer to outsiders</td></tr>
<tr><td>Khasi (1829–33)</td><td>Tirot Sing, against a British road through the hills</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Santhal Hul</b> (1855–56)</td><td>Brothers <b>Sidhu and Kanhu Murmu</b> (with Chand, Bhairav, Phulo and Jhano) at Bhognadih, Rajmahal hills, 30 June 1855 ("Hul Diwas"); against dikus, high rents, forced labour and corrupt police. The Santhals had settled the Damin-i-koh area. Crushed under martial law in 1856</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Munda Ulgulan</b> ("great tumult", 1899–1900)</td><td><b>Birsa Munda</b> in Chota Nagpur (Ranchi, Khunti), against the breakdown of <b>khuntkatti</b> (joint ownership), dikus, forced labour (beth begari) and missionaries. He founded the Birsait faith and died in Ranchi jail in June 1900</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tana Bhagat (1914)</td><td>Jatra Oraon</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rampa (1922–24)</td><td>Alluri Sitarama Raju</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>After the Santhal Hul, the British <b>created the Santhal Parganas</b> (Act XXXVII of 1855), a separate non-regulation district run through headmen (manjhis, parganaits). Later regulations (1872, 1908) made it <b>illegal for a Santhal to transfer land to a non-Santhal</b>; the Santhal Parganas Tenancy Act 1949 continues this.</li>
<li>After the Ulgulan, the <b>Chota Nagpur Tenancy Act 1908</b> restricted transfer of tribal land and recognised khuntkatti rights. Birsa's birthday, 15 November, is Janjatiya Gaurav Divas (since 2021) and Jharkhand's foundation day (2000); his 150th birth anniversary year was 2024–25.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Peasant movements</h3>
<ul>
<li>Indigo Revolt (1859–60; see 3.1); Pabna agrarian leagues (1873–76), which led to the Bengal Tenancy Act 1885; Deccan Riots (1875) against moneylenders; the Mappila uprisings in Malabar (19th century, and the 1921 rebellion linked to Khilafat).</li>
<li>For 20th-century peasant movements, see 7.1, 7.2 and 8.1.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m4-2", `
<p>The Revolt of 1857 was the biggest challenge to British rule in the 19th century: a sepoy mutiny that became a popular revolt across north and central India. It ended Company rule.</p>

<h3>Causes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Political: Doctrine of Lapse; annexation of <b>Awadh</b> (1856); humiliation of the Mughal emperor.</li>
<li>Economic: heavy land revenue, ruin of artisans, dispossessed taluqdars.</li>
<li>Military: low pay, the General Service Enlistment Act (1856) requiring service overseas, and the greased Enfield rifle cartridges (Mangal Pandey at Barrackpore, 29 March 1857).</li>
<li>Social-religious: missionary activity and laws on sati and widow remarriage.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Awadh</h4>
<ul>
<li>Awadh, the "nursery of the Bengal army", was annexed on grounds of misgovernment in February 1856, not by lapse. Dalhousie had called it "a cherry that will drop into our mouth one day".</li>
<li>Immediately afterwards (per NCERT):
<ul><li>The <b>taluqdars were disarmed and their forts destroyed</b>; they were not allowed to keep arms.</li>
<li>The <b>Summary Settlement of 1856</b> treated taluqdars as <b>interlopers</b> (outsiders) with no permanent stake in the land.</li>
<li>The British believed that <b>removing taluqdars</b> would return land to its real owners and let them <b>collect revenue directly from peasants</b>.</li></ul>
Taluqdars' share of villages fell from 67% to 38%. The Nawab's soldiers, retainers and court musicians also lost their livelihood.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Course of the revolt</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Centre</th><th>Leader</th></tr>
<tr><td>Delhi</td><td>Bahadur Shah Zafar (nominal), Bakht Khan</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kanpur</td><td>Nana Sahib, with <b>Tantia Tope</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Lucknow</td><td>Begum Hazrat Mahal (son Birjis Qadr declared Nawab); siege of the Residency (Henry Lawrence killed)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jhansi, Gwalior</td><td>Rani Lakshmibai (died June 1858)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bihar</b> (Jagdishpur, near Arrah)</td><td><b>Kunwar Singh</b>, zamindar aged about 80; died 26 April 1858 after defeating the British near Jagdishpur</td></tr>
<tr><td>Faizabad</td><td>Maulvi Ahmadullah Shah</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bareilly</td><td>Khan Bahadur Khan</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Tantia Tope</b> waged guerrilla war in central India, was <b>betrayed by his friend Raja Man Singh of Narwar</b>, captured in the Paron forest and hanged at Shivpuri on 18 April 1859.</li>
<li>Areas largely unaffected: most of the south, Bengal proper, Punjab (Sikh princes were loyal) and Rajputana. <b>Chittor</b> (Mewar) saw no significant revolt, although there were risings at Kotah, Nasirabad, Neemuch and Auwa.</li>
<li><b>Lord Canning</b> was Governor-General during the revolt ("Clemency Canning" for opposing indiscriminate revenge) and became the first Viceroy.</li>
<li>Hindus and Muslims fought together under Bahadur Shah. Sir Charles Aitchison remarked that "in this instance we could not play off the Mohammedans against the Hindus".</li>
</ul>
<div class="tip"><b>Exam angle</b> Interpretations differ: a sepoy mutiny (Seeley), a national war of independence (Savarkar, 1909), or "neither first, nor national, nor a war of independence" (R.C. Majumdar). Its weaknesses: no unified leadership or programme, and the loyalty of many princes, the educated middle class and the south.</div>

<h3>Aftermath</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Queen Victoria's Proclamation</b>, read by Canning at Allahabad on 1 November 1858:
<ul><li>It <b>disclaimed any intention to annex Indian states</b>, ending the Doctrine of Lapse, and promised to respect princes' rights.</li>
<li>Under the Government of India Act 1858, it <b>placed Indian administration under the Crown</b> (see 2.1).</li>
<li>It promised religious tolerance, equal protection of law, public offices open "irrespective of race or creed", and amnesty for rebels not guilty of murder.</li></ul>
It had nothing to do with regulating Company trade, which had already ended under the 1813 and 1833 Acts.</li>
<li>The army was reorganised (Peel Commission, 1859): more European troops, artillery kept in British hands, and regiments mixed by caste and region to divide and rule.</li>
<li>In Awadh, policy reversed: loyal taluqdars got their lands back and became pillars of the Raj (Oudh Estates Act 1869).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_modern_m5.js
// Modern History book, Unit 5: Social and cultural awakening.
addBookTopic("modern", "m5-1", `
<p>In the 19th century Indian reformers used reason and reinterpreted scripture to attack idolatry, caste and the oppression of women. Later, lower-caste leaders challenged Brahminical dominance from below.</p>

<h3>Raja Ram Mohan Roy and the Brahmo Samaj</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Raja Ram Mohan Roy</b> (1772–1833), the "father of the Indian Renaissance", combined <b>love and respect for the traditional philosophy of the East</b> (he translated the Upanishads to show the original scriptures taught monotheism) with a <b>rational, scientific outlook and belief in human dignity and the equality of men and women</b>. Both are true of him.
<ul><li>Works: <i>Tuhfat-ul-Muwahhidin</i> (1803–04, in Persian, against idolatry). He studied Persian and Arabic at Patna and Sanskrit at Benares.</li>
<li>Organisations: Atmiya Sabha (1814), the <b>Calcutta Unitarian Committee</b> (1821, with William Adam and Dwarkanath Tagore), Brahmo Sabha (1828).</li>
<li>Education: supported Hindu College (1817) and set up Vedanta College (1825). His 1823 letter to Lord Amherst asked for Western science instead of a Sanskrit college.</li>
<li>Reform: fought <b>sati</b>, which Bentinck banned in 1829 (Regulation XVII), as well as polygamy and caste rigidity, and supported women's property rights.</li>
<li>Press: <i>Sambad Kaumudi</i> and <i>Mirat-ul-Akhbar</i> (1822); he protested the 1823 press regulation.</li>
<li>Political demands: separation of executive and judiciary, Indianisation of services, jury trials, protection of ryots.</li>
<li>The Mughal emperor Akbar II gave him the title "Raja" and sent him to England (1830); he died in Bristol in 1833.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Brahmo Samaj</b> beliefs: it <b>opposed idolatry</b> and preached monotheism, and it <b>denied the need for priests</b> to interpret scripture. It did <b>not</b> hold the Vedas to be infallible; Debendranath Tagore formally rejected that. Vedic infallibility was the Arya Samaj's doctrine.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>Brahmo history</th></tr>
<tr><td>1839–43</td><td>Debendranath Tagore revives it (Tattvabodhini Sabha)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1866</td><td><b>Keshab Chandra Sen</b> forms the Brahmo Samaj of India; Tagore's group becomes the Adi Brahmo Samaj</td></tr>
<tr><td>1870</td><td>Keshab founds the <b>Indian Reform Association</b> (women's education, cheap literature, temperance)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1872</td><td>Native Marriage Act (civil marriage, minimum age), which Keshab campaigned for</td></tr>
<tr><td>1878</td><td>Keshab marries his minor daughter to the Maharaja of Cooch Behar; dissidents form the Sadharan Brahmo Samaj (Shivnath Shastri, Anandamohan Bose)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1881</td><td>Keshab founds the <b>Tabernacle of New Dispensation</b> (Nababidhan), blending religions</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Under Keshab, the Brahmo Samaj campaigned for women's education and widow remarriage. Keshab also inspired the Prarthana Samaj in Bombay (1867; Atmaram Pandurang, M.G. Ranade, R.G. Bhandarkar).</p>

<h3>Vidyasagar and women's reform</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar</b> (1820–91), principal of Sanskrit College, opened it to non-Brahmins.
<ul><li>He campaigned for the <b>Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act 1856</b>.</li>
<li>He wrote <b><i>Bahubivah</i></b> (1871–73) against Kulin polygamy, and the primer <i>Barna Parichay</i>.</li>
<li>He set up about 35 girls' schools.</li>
<li>He was <b>secretary of the Hindu Female School</b>, founded in Calcutta in 1849 by J.E.D. Bethune (Law Member) and later called Bethune School. UPSC accepted "Vidyasagar founded the Bethune School" as correct, though strictly Bethune founded it.</li></ul></li>
<li>Sati was ended by Ram Mohan Roy's campaign and Bentinck's law (1829), not by Keshab Chandra Sen.</li>
<li>The <b>Rakhmabai case</b> (1884): married at 11, Rakhmabai refused to live with her husband, who sued for <b>restitution of conjugal rights</b> in the Bombay High Court. She chose jail over submission and the case ended in a settlement (1888). The case, with the death of 11-year-old Phulmoni (1889), led to the <b>Age of Consent Act 1891</b> (raised from 10 to 12; Behramji Malabari for, Tilak against). Education was not the issue, although Rakhmabai later became one of India's first women doctors.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Law</th><th>Year</th></tr>
<tr><td>Bengal Sati Regulation</td><td>1829</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act</td><td>1856</td></tr>
<tr><td>Native Marriage Act</td><td>1872</td></tr>
<tr><td>Age of Consent Act</td><td>1891</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sarda Act (child marriage; girls 14, boys 18)</td><td>1929</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Other pioneers of women's education: Savitribai and Jyotiba Phule (girls' school at Bhide Wada, Pune, 1848), D.K. Karve (SNDT University, 1916), Pandita Ramabai (Sharada Sadan), Begum Rokeya.</p>

<h3>Arya Samaj, Ramakrishna and Vivekananda</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Arya Samaj</b>: founded by Dayanand Saraswati in Bombay in <b>1875</b> (not 1835). <i>Satyarth Prakash</i> (1875), "back to the Vedas", shuddhi. After his death it split into a "College" (DAV) wing, with Lala Lajpat Rai and Hansraj, which favoured modern education and reform on rational grounds (UPSC's key accepted that Lajpat Rai did not rest reform on Vedic authority; debated), and a "Gurukul" wing (Swami Shraddhanand).</li>
<li><b>Swami Vivekananda</b>: Chicago Parliament of Religions (1893), Ramakrishna Mission (1897). <i>Lectures from Colombo to Almora</i> (1897) collects his speeches on returning from the West, from Colombo through south India to Almora.</li>
<li>Theosophical Society (New York 1875; Adyar 1886; see 6.3).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Anti-caste movements</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Movement</th><th>Founder</th><th>Region, year</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Satyashodhak Samaj</b></td><td>Jyotiba Phule</td><td>Pune, 1873: an <b>anti-caste movement in Maharashtra</b>; education for lower castes and women, marriages without priests. <i>Gulamgiri</i> (1873) compared caste with slavery</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aravippuram movement; SNDP Yogam</td><td>Sree Narayana Guru</td><td>Kerala, 1888; 1903 ("One caste, one religion, one God")</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sadhu Jana Paripalana Sangham</td><td>Ayyankali</td><td>Kerala, 1907</td></tr>
<tr><td>Justice Party (South Indian Liberal Federation)</td><td>T.M. Nair, P. Theagaraya Chetty</td><td>Madras, 1916; won elections under dyarchy (1920)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Self-Respect Movement</b></td><td><b>E.V. Ramasamy "Periyar"</b></td><td>Tamil Nadu, <b>1925</b></td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Periyar left the Congress over the Cheranmadevi gurukulam (separate dining for Brahmin students). The Self-Respect Movement attacked caste, superstition and patriarchy and promoted priestless "self-respect marriages" (legalised in Tamil Nadu in 1967). He joined the Vaikom Satyagraha (1924–25), ran <i>Kudi Arasu</i>, opposed compulsory Hindi (1937–40), led the Justice Party from 1938 and renamed it Dravidar Kazhagam (1944). Annadurai founded the DMK (1949).</li>
<li>Maharashtra's non-Brahmin movement: Shahu Maharaj of Kolhapur (reservation, 1902), Bhaskarrao Jadhav, Dinkarrao Javalkar (<i>Deshache Dushman</i>, 1925), Keshavrao Jedhe.</li>
<li><b>Gopal Baba Walangkar</b>, a Mahar ex-soldier influenced by Phule, published <b><i>Vital-Vidhvansak</i></b> (1888), the first journal aimed at the untouchables.</li>
<li>Ambedkar: Bahishkrit Hitakarini Sabha (1924), Mahad Satyagraha (1927), Kalaram temple entry (1930); journals <i>Mooknayak</i> (1920) and <i>Bahishkrit Bharat</i> (1927). See 7.3 and 8.1.</li>
<li>The <b>Indian National Social Conference</b> (1887, M.G. Ranade and Raghunath Rao) was formed because the <b>Congress did not want social reform in its deliberations</b>, to keep all communities united on political demands. It met alongside Congress sessions until Tilak's supporters barred it from the pandal (1895).</li>
</ul>
<h4>Dating 19th-century events</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Event</th><th>Year</th></tr>
<tr><td>Dinabandhu Mitra's <i>Nil Darpan</i></td><td>1860 (earliest)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Satyendranath Tagore passes the ICS</td><td>1863</td></tr>
<tr><td>Arya Samaj founded</td><td>1875</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bankim Chandra's <i>Anandamath</i></td><td>1882</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Sarvodaya Samaj (1948) was formed after Gandhi's death for constructive work, not for refugees; Vinoba Bhave is known for Bhoodan (see 9.1).</p>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m5-2", `
<p>Western education, a vigorous press and new literature created the educated class that led Indian nationalism. The British shaped education to suit administration, while Indians used it to question British rule.</p>

<h3>Education policy</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>Measure</th></tr>
<tr><td>1781</td><td><b>Calcutta Madrasa</b>, founded by <b>Warren Hastings</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>1784</td><td>Asiatic Society of Bengal (William Jones)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1791</td><td><b>Sanskrit College, Benares</b>, founded by <b>Jonathan Duncan</b> (not William Jones)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1800</td><td><b>Fort William College</b>, founded by Lord (<b>Richard</b>) <b>Wellesley</b> (not his brother Arthur, later Duke of Wellington) <b>to train British civilians</b> in Indian languages, law and customs. The Directors disliked it and set up Haileybury College in England (1806). William Carey, John Gilchrist and Lallu Lal taught there</td></tr>
<tr><td>1813</td><td>Charter Act: ₹1 lakh a year for learning and science, the first state responsibility for education</td></tr>
<tr><td>1817</td><td><b>Hindu College</b>, Calcutta (now Presidency University), with <b>Ram Mohan Roy</b>, David Hare and Edward Hyde East. Alexander Duff came in 1830 and started what is now Scottish Church College. Its radical teacher <b>Henry Louis Vivian Derozio</b> inspired the "Young Bengal" movement and was dismissed in 1831</td></tr>
<tr><td>1823</td><td><b>General Committee of Public Instruction</b>, which managed the grant</td></tr>
<tr><td>1835</td><td><b>Orientalist–Anglicist controversy</b> (Prinsep against Macaulay) settled by Macaulay's Minute (2 February) and Bentinck's resolution: English for higher education ("downward filtration")</td></tr>
<tr><td>1854</td><td><b>Wood's Despatch</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>1857</td><td>Universities of Calcutta, Bombay and Madras</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The Charter Act of 1813, the General Committee of Public Instruction and the Orientalist–Anglicist controversy <b>all led to English education</b>.</li>
<li><b>Wood's Despatch</b> (1854), the "Magna Carta of English education", aimed at <b>spreading Western knowledge</b>. It introduced <b>grants-in-aid</b>, recommended <b>universities</b> on the London model, a ladder from vernacular primary schools to colleges, a Department of Public Instruction in each province, teacher training and women's education. It recommended English for higher education but <b>vernaculars at school level</b>, not English at all levels.</li>
<li>Later: Hunter Commission (1882, primary education), Raleigh Commission (1902) leading to Curzon's Universities Act 1904, Sadler Commission (1917), Hartog Committee (1929), Wardha scheme / Nai Talim (1937), Sargent Plan (1944).</li>
<li>Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay (with Jadunath Bose) was among the first graduates of Calcutta University (1858).</li>
<li>Orientalist scholarship: William Jones (translated <i>Shakuntala</i>), Charles Wilkins (<i>Gita</i>, 1785), Max Müller, James Prinsep (deciphered Brahmi, 1837).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The press</h3>
<ul>
<li><b><i>Som Prakash</i></b> (1858), a Bengali weekly started under <b>Vidyasagar</b>'s guidance (editor Dwarkanath Vidyabhushan), was a main target of Lytton's <b>Vernacular Press Act</b> (1878, the "Gagging Act"), which <b>Ripon repealed</b> in 1882. <i>Amrita Bazar Patrika</i> switched overnight to English to escape it.</li>
<li><b><i>Al-Hilal</i></b> (1912) was Maulana <b>Abul Kalam Azad</b>'s Urdu weekly from Calcutta; banned in 1914, it was followed by <i>Al-Balagh</i>.</li>
<li>Other papers: <i>Kesari</i> and <i>Mahratta</i> (Tilak), <i>The Hindu</i> (1878) and <i>Swadesamitran</i> (G. Subramania Iyer), <i>Bengalee</i> (Surendranath Banerjea), <i>Young India</i>, <i>Navajivan</i>, <i>Harijan</i> and <i>Indian Opinion</i> (Gandhi), <i>Comrade</i> (Mohammad Ali), <i>New India</i> (Besant).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Literature and leaders' books</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Author</th><th>Works</th></tr>
<tr><td>Gandhi</td><td><i>Hind Swaraj</i> (1909, written on a ship), <i>The Story of My Experiments with Truth</i>, <i>From Yeravda Mandir</i>, and <b><i>Songs from Prison</i></b> (English translations of Upanishadic hymns and saint-poets made in Yerawada jail, 1930; versified by John Hoyland, 1934)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tilak</td><td><i>Gita Rahasya</i> (Mandalay jail), <i>The Arctic Home in the Vedas</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Nehru</td><td><i>Glimpses of World History</i>, <i>An Autobiography</i>, <i>The Discovery of India</i> (Ahmednagar fort, 1944)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sarojini Naidu</b></td><td><b><i>The Golden Threshold</i></b> (1905), <i>The Bird of Time</i>, <i>The Broken Wing</i>; the "Nightingale of India"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bhagat Singh</td><td><i>Why I am an Atheist</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Savarkar</td><td><i>The Indian War of Independence 1857</i>, <i>Hindutva</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Maulana Azad</td><td><i>Ghubar-e-Khatir</i>, <i>India Wins Freedom</i></td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Lal Bahadur Shastri</b> translated <b>Marie Curie's autobiography</b> into Hindi while in prison.</li>
<li>National symbols:
<ul><li>In February 1919, at the Besant Theosophical College in <b>Madanapalle</b> (Andhra Pradesh), <b>Tagore translated "Jana Gana Mana" into English</b> as "The Morning Song of India", and Margaret Cousins set the tune still used.</li>
<li>"Jana Gana Mana" was first sung at the Calcutta Congress (27 December 1911) and adopted as the anthem on 24 January 1950.</li>
<li>"Vande Mataram" (from <i>Anandamath</i>) was first sung by Tagore at the 1896 Congress; 150 years were marked in 2025.</li>
<li>The flag was adopted on 22 July 1947; Pingali Venkayya designed its forerunner.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_modern_m6.js
// Modern History book, Unit 6: Rise of nationalism, 1885 to 1918.
addBookTopic("modern", "m6-1", `
<p>Before 1885, regional associations of the educated and propertied classes petitioned for reforms. The Indian National Congress brought them onto one platform, and for twenty years the Moderates led it by constitutional methods.</p>

<h3>Pre-Congress associations</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Body</th><th>Founder, year</th></tr>
<tr><td>Landholders' Society</td><td>Dwarkanath Tagore, 1838</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>British Indian Association</b></td><td>1851, Calcutta; <b>Radhakanta Deb first president</b>, Debendranath Tagore secretary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Madras Native Association</td><td><b>Gazulu Lakshminarasu Chetty</b>, 1852 (also the paper <i>Crescent</i>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bombay Association</td><td>Jagannath Shankarseth, 1852</td></tr>
<tr><td>East India Association (London)</td><td>Dadabhai Naoroji, 1866</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Poona Sarvajanik Sabha</b></td><td>M.G. Ranade and others, 1870; sent the <b>1875 petition to the House of Commons demanding direct representation</b> for India</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indian League</td><td>Sisir Kumar Ghosh, 1875</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Indian Association</b></td><td><b>Surendranath Banerjea</b> and Anandamohan Bose, July 1876 (so it could not have sent the 1875 petition); held the Indian National Conference (1883, 1885); protested the cut in ICS age (1876)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Madras Mahajana Sabha</td><td>M. Veeraraghavachariar, G. Subramania Iyer, P. Anandacharlu, 1884</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bombay Presidency Association</td><td>Pherozeshah Mehta, K.T. Telang, Badruddin Tyabji, 1885</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The Indian National Congress</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>first session</b> met in <b>Bombay</b> (Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit College), 28–31 December 1885, with 72 delegates under <b>W.C. Bonnerjee</b>. A.O. Hume was founder-secretary but never president. It had been planned for Poona but moved because of cholera. Theories of its origin: Hume's "safety valve" (Lala Lajpat Rai's view) versus the "lightning conductor" idea (Gokhale).</li>
<li>The <b>second session</b> (Calcutta, 1886) was presided over by <b>Dadabhai Naoroji</b>.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Session</th><th>President and significance</th></tr>
<tr><td>1887 Madras</td><td><b>Badruddin Tyabji</b>, first Muslim president</td></tr>
<tr><td>1888 Allahabad</td><td>George Yule, first British president</td></tr>
<tr><td>1894 Madras</td><td><b>Alfred Webb</b>, Irish nationalist MP</td></tr>
<tr><td>1906 Calcutta</td><td>Naoroji; Swaraj declared the goal (see 6.2)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1917 Calcutta</td><td><b>Annie Besant</b>, first woman president</td></tr>
<tr><td>1921 Ahmedabad</td><td><b>C.R. Das</b>, elected <b>while in prison</b> (Hakim Ajmal Khan acted)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1924 Belgaum</td><td>Gandhi, the only time he presided</td></tr>
<tr><td>1925 Kanpur</td><td><b>Sarojini Naidu</b>, first Indian woman president</td></tr>
<tr><td>1929 Lahore</td><td>Jawaharlal Nehru; Purna Swaraj (see 7.2)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1931 Karachi</td><td>Vallabhbhai Patel; Fundamental Rights (see 7.3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1938 Haripura, 1939 Tripuri</td><td>Subhas Chandra Bose (see 8.1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1946–47</td><td>J.B. Kripalani (see 8.3)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The Moderates</h3>
<ul>
<li>Leaders: Naoroji, Pherozeshah Mehta, Surendranath Banerjea, Gokhale, Ranade, D.E. Wacha, W.C. Bonnerjee. Methods were petitions, speeches and resolutions within the law ("prayer, petition and protest").</li>
<li>Demands: expanded councils, Indianisation of services and simultaneous ICS exams, lower land revenue, cuts in military spending, civil liberties. Their greatest contribution was the <b>economic critique of colonialism</b> (see 3.2).</li>
<li>Their basic weakness was a <b>narrow social base</b> (the urban educated middle class); they did not mobilise the masses. But they did <b>not</b> fight for the narrow interests of their own groups; their programme served broad national interests.</li>
<li><b>Aurobindo Ghosh</b>'s articles "<b>New Lamps for Old</b>" (<i>Indu Prakash</i>, 1893–94) gave a systematic critique of the Moderates' "mendicant" politics.</li>
<li>In Britain, Sir William Wedderburn and W.S. Caine set up the <b>Indian Parliamentary Committee</b> (1893) <b>to press for Indian reforms in the House of Commons</b>. Naoroji became the first Indian elected to the Commons (Finsbury Central, 1892).</li>
<li><b>G.K. Gokhale</b>, Gandhi's political guru, founded the Servants of India Society (1905). He <b>declined a knighthood</b> (1914) and a seat on the Secretary of State's Council to keep his independence.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The Lucknow session, 1916</h4>
<p>In December 1916 the Congress (president A.C. Mazumdar) and the Muslim League (Jinnah) both met at <b>Lucknow</b> and concluded the <b>Lucknow Pact</b>: Congress accepted separate electorates and both made a joint demand for self-government. The Moderates and Extremists also reunited there (see 6.2).</p>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m6-2", `
<p>Curzon's partition of Bengal turned nationalist politics into a mass movement. Swadeshi and boycott spread new methods, the Extremists challenged the Moderates, and the British answered with repression and separate electorates.</p>

<h3>Partition of Bengal</h3>
<ul>
<li>Announced by <b>Curzon</b> in July 1905, it created "Eastern Bengal and Assam" (capital Dacca). The official reason was administrative convenience; the real aim was to divide Hindus and Muslims and weaken Bengali nationalism.</li>
<li>The <b>Swadeshi Movement</b> was formally launched at the Calcutta Town Hall on <b>7 August 1905</b> with the boycott resolution. That date has been <b>National Handloom Day</b> since 2015, chosen because of 1905. The <b>immediate cause</b> of the movement was the partition.</li>
<li>Partition <b>took effect on 16 October 1905</b>, observed as a day of mourning. People fasted, and Tagore organised Raksha Bandhan ceremonies for unity. Tagore wrote <b>"Amar Sonar Bangla"</b> (1905); its first ten lines became <b>Bangladesh's national anthem</b> (1972).</li>
<li>Partition was <b>annulled by King George V at the Delhi Durbar</b> (12 December 1911, Viceroy Hardinge). The capital moved from Calcutta to Delhi, Bihar and Orissa were separated, and Assam became a province again. New Delhi was inaugurated in 1931.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Swadeshi Movement, 1905–08</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Swadeshi and boycott</b> were adopted as methods of struggle <b>for the first time</b> in the agitation against the partition. Earlier economic nationalists had urged swadeshi, but mass adoption began in 1905.</li>
<li>Methods:
<ul><li>Boycott of foreign goods, with bonfires of foreign cloth.</li>
<li>Swadeshi enterprises, which <b>revived indigenous crafts and industries</b>: Bengal Chemicals (P.C. Ray), Bengal Lakshmi Cotton Mills, V.O. Chidambaram Pillai's Swadeshi Steam Navigation Company at Tuticorin.</li>
<li>National education: the <b>National Council of Education</b> (August 1906), Bengal National College (Aurobindo, principal) and the Bengal Technical Institute (later Jadavpur University).</li>
<li>Samitis such as Ashwini Kumar Dutt's Swadesh Bandhab Samiti (Barisal).</li>
<li>Traditional festivals and folk theatre (Mukunda Das's jatras); Abanindranath Tagore's <i>Bharat Mata</i>.</li></ul></li>
<li>Sakharam Ganesh Deuskar's <b><i>Desher Katha</i></b> (1904) warned against the colonial state's "<b>hypnotic conquest of the mind</b>" and <b>inspired swadeshi street plays and songs</b>; it was banned in 1910. Its "desh" meant <b>India as a whole</b>, not just Bengal.</li>
<li>Muslim participation was limited, but <b>Liakat Hossain</b> led Muslim peasants in Barisal. Tagore's atmashakti (self-reliance) programme and the Dawn Society also belong to this period.</li>
<li>Leaders: Lal-Bal-Pal (Lajpat Rai, Tilak, Bipin Chandra Pal), Aurobindo, Surendranath Banerjea, Chidambaram Pillai, Subramania Bharati.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Moderates, Extremists and the Surat split</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Calcutta Congress of 1906</b> (Naoroji) passed four resolutions: <b>Swaraj, Swadeshi, Boycott and National Education</b>. Annulment of the partition was not one of them. The Moderates wanted to drop them; the Extremists wanted them kept.</li>
<li>The <b>Swadeshi Movement</b> deepened the divide over methods and scope: the Extremists wanted boycott across India and in all spheres, the Moderates wanted it limited to Bengal and foreign goods. At <b>Surat (December 1907)</b>, the main reason for the split was the <b>Extremists' lack of faith in the Moderates' ability to negotiate with the British</b>; the trigger was the presidency (the Moderates' Rash Behari Ghosh) and fears the Calcutta resolutions would be dropped.</li>
<li>Afterwards the government repressed the Extremists: Tilak was jailed in Mandalay (1908) and Lajpat Rai deported there earlier (1907). The two wings reunited at Lucknow (1916).</li>
<li><b>Lala Lajpat Rai</b> (1865–1928), "Punjab Kesari":
<ul><li>He wrote biographies of Mazzini, Garibaldi, Shivaji and Krishna.</li>
<li>He lived in the USA (1914–19), founded the Indian Home Rule League of America and wrote <i>Unhappy India</i> in reply to Katherine Mayo's <i>Mother India</i>.</li>
<li>He was elected to the Central Legislative Assembly (1926).</li>
<li>He helped found DAV College, Lahore and Punjab National Bank, presided over the 1920 special Congress session, and was the first president of AITUC.</li>
<li>He died after a police lathi charge during the Simon Commission protest at Lahore (1928).</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>The Muslim League and separate electorates</h3>
<ul>
<li>When the Congress was formed, Sayyid Ahmad Khan opposed it. The Simla Deputation (1906, Aga Khan) asked Minto for separate electorates.</li>
<li>The <b>All-India Muslim League</b> (Dacca, December 1906; Aga Khan, Nawab Salimullah) <b>supported</b> the partition of Bengal and <b>demanded</b> separate electorates. "It vehemently opposed them" is false. Hakim Ajmal Khan, by contrast, was a nationalist, and the Ahrar movement was nationalist and militant.</li>
<li><b>Syed Ameer Ali</b>, author of <i>The Spirit of Islam</i>, set up the League's <b>London branch</b> (1908) to lobby for separate electorates.</li>
<li>Separate electorates came for Muslims in 1909 (Morley-Minto). They were accepted by Congress in the Lucknow Pact (1916), extended to Sikhs and others in 1919, extended to depressed classes by the Communal Award (1932), and modified by the Poona Pact (1932).</li>
<li>The <b>community-based alliances</b> of later politics grew out of the 1919 reforms because:
<ul><li>they <b>retained and extended separate electorates</b>;</li>
<li>separate electorates were meant to <b>counter growing nationalism</b>;</li>
<li><b>deprived classes rallied around</b> the favours that community representation offered (the Justice Party, Muslim and Sikh parties).</li></ul></li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m6-3", `
<p>Frustration with moderate methods and repression produced revolutionary groups in India and abroad. During the First World War, the Home Rule Leagues revived mass politics by constitutional means.</p>

<h3>Revolutionaries in India</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Group</th><th>Leaders</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Anushilan Samiti</b> (Calcutta, 1902)</td><td>P. Mitra, <b>Aurobindo Ghosh</b>, <b>Barindra Kumar Ghosh</b> (Aurobindo's brother; journal <i>Jugantar</i>; convicted in the Alipore Bomb Case, 1908, and sent to the Andamans)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Abhinav Bharat</b> (1904)</td><td><b>V.D. Savarkar</b> and his brother Ganesh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hindustan Republican Association (1924)</td><td>Sachindranath Sanyal, <b>Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee</b> (Kakori case, 1925); became the HSRA (1928) with Bhagat Singh and Chandrashekhar Azad</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indian Republican Army</td><td><b>Surya Sen</b> ("Masterda") led the <b>Chittagong armoury raid</b> (18 April 1930) and the Jalalabad Hill battle; hanged in 1934. <b>Kalpana Dutt</b> and Pritilata Waddedar took part</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kuka</b> (Namdhari) movement</td><td><b>Guru Ram Singh</b> (Punjab), a religious reform movement that turned anti-British</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Bengal revolutionaries (Anushilan, Jugantar) <b>took shelter in Tripura's hills</b>, which drew this princely state into the freedom movement early in the 20th century; the Tripura Rajya Praja Mandal followed.</li>
<li>Other actions: Khudiram Bose and Prafulla Chaki (Muzaffarpur, 1908); Madan Lal Dhingra (London, 1909); Rash Behari Bose's bomb on Viceroy Hardinge (Delhi, 1912); Bhagat Singh's Assembly bomb (1929; see 7.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Revolutionaries abroad</h3>
<ul>
<li>India House (London, Shyamji Krishna Varma, 1905). <b>Bhikaji Cama</b> was born to a wealthy <b>Parsi</b> family in Bombay (1861) and was <b>Dadabhai Naoroji's private secretary</b> in London. She unfurled the "Flag of Indian Independence" at the International Socialist Congress in <b>Stuttgart</b> (22 August 1907), not Paris, though she lived in Paris.</li>
<li>The <b>Ghadar Party</b> was a <b>revolutionary association with headquarters at San Francisco</b> (Yugantar Ashram), founded in 1913 among mostly Punjabi Sikh immigrants on the <b>North American</b> Pacific coast. Leaders: <b>Lala Har Dayal</b>, Sohan Singh Bhakna (president), Kartar Singh Sarabha, Ramchandra, Barkatullah. Its weekly was <i>Ghadar</i> ("revolt").</li>
<li><b>Rash Behari Bose</b> was actively associated with the Ghadar Party: he planned the Ghadar uprising of February 1915, which an informer betrayed. He later fled to Japan, founded the Indian Independence League and handed the INA to Subhas Bose (1943). Barindra Kumar Ghosh and Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee were not Ghadarites.</li>
<li>After the failed rising: the Lahore Conspiracy Case (Kartar Singh Sarabha hanged at 19), the Singapore mutiny (February 1915), and the Provisional Government of India in Kabul (1915; Raja Mahendra Pratap president, Barkatullah prime minister).</li>
<li>The <b>Komagata Maru</b> was a <b>Japanese steamship</b> chartered by Gurdit Singh (1914) to carry 376 mostly Sikh passengers from Hong Kong to Vancouver, challenging Canada's "continuous journey" rule. It was turned back; at Budge Budge near Calcutta, police firing killed about 20 passengers.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Home Rule Leagues, 1916–18</h3>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Tilak's League</th><th>Besant's League</th></tr>
<tr><td>Founded</td><td>April 1916, Belgaum</td><td>September 1916, Madras</td></tr>
<tr><td>Area</td><td>Maharashtra (except Bombay city), Karnataka, Central Provinces, Berar</td><td>Rest of India</td></tr>
<tr><td>Papers</td><td><i>Kesari</i>, <i>Mahratta</i></td><td><i>New India</i>, <i>Commonweal</i></td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Inspired by the Irish Home Rule movement, the Leagues demanded self-government within the Empire. Besant's internment (June 1917) caused protests and helped bring Montagu's August Declaration (1917).</li>
<li><b>Annie Besant</b> <b>started the Home Rule Movement</b> (with Tilak) and was <b>Congress president</b> (Calcutta, 1917). She did <b>not found the Theosophical Society</b>; Madame Blavatsky and Col. Olcott founded it in New York (1875), and it moved to Adyar (1886). Besant joined in 1889 and became its president in 1907. She was earlier a <b>Fabian</b> socialist (Fabian Society, London, 1884, with Shaw and the Webbs) and founded the Central Hindu College, Varanasi (1898).</li>
<li>In 1920, under Gandhi's presidency, the <b>All India Home Rule League was renamed Swarajya Sabha</b> and merged with the Congress.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The road to Rowlatt</h4>
<ul>
<li>In 1916, <b>Mohammad Ali and Abul Kalam Azad</b> were <b>interned</b> for pro-Turkish writings; they did not resign from any council.</li>
<li>It was <b>Jinnah</b> (with Madan Mohan Malaviya and Mazharul Haque) who resigned from the Imperial Legislative Council in 1919 over the <b>Rowlatt Act</b>, which was passed although every elected Indian member opposed it (see 7.1).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
