// Modern History book, Unit 7: The Gandhian era, 1917 to 1934.
addBookTopic("modern", "m7-1", `
<p>Gandhi returned from South Africa on 9 January 1915 (now Pravasi Bharatiya Divas). Three local struggles in 1917–18 tested satyagraha in India, and the Rowlatt agitation of 1919 made him a national leader.</p>

<h3>Gandhi's ideas and early stand</h3>
<ul>
<li>John Ruskin's <i>Unto This Last</i>, read on a train in 1904, taught him that <b>the good of the individual is contained in the good of all</b>, that a lawyer's work has the same value as a barber's, and that the life of labour is the life worth living. He paraphrased it in Gujarati as <i>Sarvodaya</i>. Other influences: Tolstoy, Thoreau, the <i>Gita</i>.</li>
<li>Gandhi was instrumental in <b>abolishing indentured labour</b>: campaigning with C.F. Andrews (1916–17), recruitment stopped in 1917 and the system was formally abolished in 1920.</li>
<li>At Chelmsford's <b>War Conference</b> (Delhi, April 1918) Gandhi <b>supported</b> the resolution on recruiting Indians and toured Kheda as a "recruiting sergeant", hoping for self-government after the war.</li>
<li>He had received the Kaisar-i-Hind medal (1915), which he returned in 1920.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Champaran, Ahmedabad and Kheda</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Struggle</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Champaran</b> (Bihar, 1917)</td><td>Gandhi's <b>first satyagraha in India</b>, against the <b>tinkathia</b> system (indigo on 3/20 of the land for European planters, plus illegal dues). He was persuaded to come by <b>Raj Kumar Shukla</b>, a local cultivator, at the Lucknow Congress (1916), not by Rajendra Prasad. Companions: <b>Rajendra Prasad</b>, <b>J.B. Kripalani</b>, Brajkishore Prasad, Mazharul Haq, Mahadev Desai, Narhari Parikh, Anugrah Narayan Sinha. He defied an order to leave the district; an inquiry committee with Gandhi as member led to the Champaran Agrarian Act 1918 (tinkathia abolished, 25% of dues refunded). Its significance was <b>joining peasant unrest to the national movement</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Ahmedabad mill strike</b> (1918)</td><td>Gandhi's first hunger strike; won a 35% wage rise. Anasuya Sarabhai led the workers and her brother Ambalal led the mill owners (Morarji Desai had no role). Afterwards the <b>Ahmedabad Textile Labour Association</b> (Majoor Mahajan Sangh, 1920) was formed under <b>Gandhi</b>'s guidance on arbitration and non-violence</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kheda</b> (Gujarat, 1918)</td><td>Crops failed (yield below a quarter of normal), so peasants were entitled to remission, but the <b>government would not suspend revenue collection</b>. There was no plan for a Permanent Settlement in Gujarat, which was a Ryotwari area. <b>Vallabhbhai Patel</b> gave up his legal practice to join</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>J.B. Kripalani</b> was a <b>teacher</b> (a professor at G.B.B. College, Muzaffarpur) when he met Gandhi at Champaran; he later taught at Gujarat Vidyapith, hence "Acharya".</li>
<li>The <b>UP Kisan Sabha</b> (February 1918) was formed by Gauri Shankar Misra and Indra Narain Dwivedi with Madan Mohan Malaviya's support, through the Home Rule League. <b>Nehru was not involved</b>; he joined the Awadh peasant movement in 1920 (Pratapgarh, with Baba Ramchandra).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Rowlatt Act and Jallianwala Bagh</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Rowlatt Act</b> (Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act, March 1919), passed under Viceroy <b>Chelmsford</b>, was based on the <b>Sedition Committee</b> of Justice S.A.T. Rowlatt (1918). It aimed at <b>imprisonment without trial</b> (up to two years) and <b>summary trials</b> by special courts without juries or appeal ("no vakil, no daleel, no appeal"). That <b>aroused popular indignation</b>. It was passed although every elected Indian member opposed it; Jinnah, Malaviya and Mazharul Haque resigned. It was repealed in 1922 without being used.</li>
<li>Gandhi's <b>Rowlatt Satyagraha</b> was his first all-India campaign. He formed a Satyagraha Sabha and <b>used the Home Rule League</b> network and pan-Islamic groups; there was a hartal on 6 April 1919. It had nothing to do with the Simon Commission, which came in 1928. Gandhi called it off after violence, calling it his "Himalayan blunder".</li>
<li>In Amritsar, the arrest of Dr Satyapal and Dr Saifuddin Kitchlew led to protests. On <b>13 April 1919</b> (Baisakhi), General Dyer fired on a peaceful crowd at <b>Jallianwala Bagh</b> (379 dead officially; over 1,000 by Congress estimates). The wave of anger against the <b>Rowlatt Act</b> was the background.</li>
<li>The <b>Hunter Committee</b> (Disorders Inquiry Committee, October 1919) was appointed <b>after Jallianwala Bagh</b>. It criticised Dyer mildly; he was relieved of command but not punished. Congress ran its own inquiry.</li>
<li><b>Rabindranath Tagore renounced his knighthood</b> (letter of 30 May 1919) in protest against the atrocities in Punjab.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m7-2", `
<p>Khilafat and Non-Cooperation (1920–22) were the first truly mass movements. After their withdrawal, the 1920s saw the Swarajists, the Simon Commission boycott, the Nehru Report and finally the demand for complete independence.</p>

<h3>The Non-Cooperation Movement, 1920–22</h3>
<ul>
<li>Causes: the Rowlatt Act, Jallianwala Bagh, the Hunter Committee whitewash, the <b>Khilafat</b> grievance over the treatment of the Ottoman Sultan (Ali brothers, Maulana Azad), and post-war hardship.</li>
<li>Gandhi launched it on 1 August 1920, the day Tilak died. It was approved at the Calcutta special session (September 1920, Lala Lajpat Rai) and ratified at <b>Nagpur</b> (December 1920). There the Congress changed its creed to <b>attaining Swaraj by all legitimate and peaceful means</b>, moving beyond constitutional methods; Jinnah, Annie Besant, G.S. Khaparde and B.C. Pal left over it.</li>
<li>Nagpur also reorganised the Congress: a Working Committee of 15, provincial committees on linguistic lines, four-anna membership, and Hindi where possible.</li>
<li>It was to proceed <b>in stages</b>:
<ol><li>surrender of titles;</li>
<li>boycott of government schools, courts, councils and foreign cloth;</li>
<li>resignation from government service;</li>
<li>civil disobedience and <b>non-payment of taxes</b>, held back as a later stage, used only if Swaraj did not come within a year and the government turned to repression.</li></ol>
Gandhi promised "Swaraj within a year".</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Boycott (negative)</th><th>Constructive (positive)</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Surrender</b> (not retention) of titles and honours; boycott of schools, <b>law courts</b>, councils, <b>foreign cloth</b> (bonfires), official functions</td><td>National schools (Jamia Millia Islamia, Kashi Vidyapith, Gujarat Vidyapith, Bihar Vidyapith); <b>panchayats to settle disputes</b>; khadi and charkha; Hindu–Muslim unity; removal of untouchability; Tilak Swaraj Fund; <b>strict non-violence</b></td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Lawyers who gave up practice: C.R. Das, Motilal Nehru, Patel, Rajendra Prasad, Kitchlew. The Prince of Wales's visit (17 November 1921) was met with a hartal. The <b>Mappila (Moplah) rebellion</b> (1921, Malabar) and the <b>Eka movement</b> (1921–22, north Awadh, Madari Pasi) took place at the same time.</li>
<li>At the <b>Ahmedabad session</b> (December 1921; C.R. Das elected while in jail, Hakim Ajmal Khan acting), <b>Maulana Hasrat Mohani</b>, who also coined "Inquilab Zindabad", moved that Swaraj be defined as <b>complete independence free from all foreign control</b>. Gandhi opposed it as premature and it was defeated. Some questions date this session to 1920.</li>
<li><b>Chauri Chaura</b> (5 February 1922, Gorakhpur): after police beat picketers, a crowd burned the police station and 22–23 policemen died. Gandhi withdrew the movement (Bardoli resolution, 12 February 1922). Motilal Nehru, C.R. Das, Subhas Bose and Jawaharlal Nehru criticised the decision. Gandhi was arrested in March 1922 and sentenced to six years for sedition.</li>
<li>A sessions court sentenced 172 to death. <b>Madan Mohan Malaviya</b>, assisted by Krishna Kant Malaviya, argued the appeal in the Allahabad High Court (1923); only 19 death sentences were upheld. Malaviya founded BHU (1916), was Congress president four times and helped found the Hindu Mahasabha (1915); he got the Bharat Ratna in 2015. (C.R. Das had earlier defended Aurobindo in the Alipore Bomb Case; Jinnah defended Tilak in 1908 and 1916.)</li>
</ul>

<h3>The 1920s</h3>
<ul>
<li>When the Congress (Bombay special session, 1918) condemned the Montagu-Chelmsford proposals as "disappointing and unsatisfactory", Moderates led by Surendranath Banerjea left to form the <b>Indian Liberal Federation</b> (1918–19); Tej Bahadur Sapru and V.S. Srinivasa Sastri later led it.</li>
<li>The <b>Swaraj Party</b> (1923, C.R. Das and Motilal Nehru) contested council elections to wreck them from within; the "no-changers" (Patel, Rajagopalachari, Rajendra Prasad) kept to constructive work.</li>
<li>The <b>Simon Commission</b> (Indian Statutory Commission, November 1927, under Irwin, Secretary of State Lord Birkenhead) had seven British MPs, including Clement Attlee, and <b>no Indian member</b>. That is why it was boycotted with "Simon Go Back" when it arrived in February 1928. Lajpat Rai died after a lathi charge in Lahore, which Bhagat Singh and his comrades avenged by killing J.P. Saunders. Its report (1930) recommended <b>replacing dyarchy with responsible government in the provinces</b> (provincial autonomy), which the 1935 Act implemented.</li>
<li>The <b>Nehru Report</b> (1928, Motilal Nehru), the Indian answer to Birkenhead's challenge, asked for <b>dominion status</b>, not complete independence. It proposed <b>joint electorates with reserved seats</b> for minorities and listed <b>19 fundamental rights</b>, including equal rights for women. Jinnah answered with his Fourteen Points (1929). Younger leaders formed the Independence for India League.</li>
<li><b>Bardoli Satyagraha</b> (1928, Surat district): a no-revenue campaign of peasant proprietors against a 30% (later 22%) increase, led by <b>Vallabhbhai Patel</b>, whom the women of Bardoli called "Sardar". The Maxwell–Broomfield inquiry cut the increase to about 6%. (Eka, by contrast, was a tenants' movement against high rents and illegal cesses.)</li>
<li>Revolutionaries: HSRA (1928); Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutt's bombs in the Central Assembly (April 1929); Bhagat Singh, Rajguru and Sukhdev were hanged on 23 March 1931.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Lahore Congress, 1929</h3>
<ul>
<li>Presided over by <b>Jawaharlal Nehru</b>, it adopted <b>Purna Swaraj</b> (complete independence) as the goal. It <b>authorised the Working Committee to launch civil disobedience</b>, including non-payment of taxes, and to boycott the Round Table Conference. Nehru hoisted the tricolour on the Ravi on 31 December 1929, and <b>26 January 1930</b> was celebrated as Independence Day (hence Republic Day in 1950).</li>
<li>It did <b>not</b> heal the Moderate–Extremist rift (that was Lucknow, 1916) and passed no resolution against the two-nation theory, which came to the fore with the League's Lahore Resolution (1940).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Event</th><th>Year</th></tr>
<tr><td>Morley-Minto Reforms</td><td>1909</td></tr>
<tr><td>Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms</td><td>1919</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chauri Chaura</td><td>1922</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dandi March</td><td>1930</td></tr>
</table>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m7-3", `
<p>The Civil Disobedience Movement (1930–34) broke unjust laws openly, starting with the salt law. Alongside it ran three Round Table Conferences, and the dispute over representation of the depressed classes ended in the Poona Pact.</p>

<h3>Salt Satyagraha and Civil Disobedience</h3>
<ul>
<li>Gandhi sent Irwin eleven demands (31 January 1930), then marched with 78 satyagrahis from Sabarmati (12 March 1930) to <b>Dandi</b>, about 385 km, and broke the salt law on 6 April 1930. <b>The Dandi March began the Civil Disobedience Movement.</b> "<b>Sedition has become my religion</b>" was Gandhi's declaration at the time of publicly breaking the salt law at Dandi. At his 1922 trial he had called Section 124A "the prince among the political sections of the IPC".</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Region</th><th>Salt and related satyagraha</th></tr>
<tr><td>Vedaranyam, <b>Tanjore coast</b></td><td><b>C. Rajagopalachari</b> marched from Trichinopoly (April 1930)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payyanur, Malabar</td><td>K. Kelappan</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dharasana, Gujarat</td><td>Sarojini Naidu, Imam Saheb, Manilal Gandhi (reported by Webb Miller)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Balasore coast, Odisha</td><td>Gopabandhu Choudhury</td></tr>
<tr><td>NWFP</td><td>Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan's <b>Khudai Khidmatgars</b> ("Red Shirts", 1929): a non-violent Pathan movement calling for <b>Pathan regional unity and a struggle against colonialism</b>. The Garhwal Rifles refused to fire on crowds in Peshawar</td></tr>
<tr><td>Elsewhere</td><td>Rani Gaidinliu (Nagaland), forest satyagrahas, no-chowkidari-tax campaign in Bihar</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The government <b>declared the Congress Working Committee illegal</b> (June 1930, and again in January 1932) and jailed leaders.</li>
<li><b>Labour participation</b> was weak in the early 1930s because, after the Comintern's 1928 "left turn", communist-led unions called the Congress <b>bourgeois and reactionary</b> and kept workers away; the Meerut Conspiracy Case (1929) had jailed many leaders.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Gandhi–Irwin Pact and Karachi</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Gandhi–Irwin (Delhi) Pact</b>, 5 March 1931:
<ul><li>Gandhi suspended civil disobedience and the Congress agreed to attend the Second Round Table Conference.</li>
<li>The government withdrew its ordinances, released prisoners <b>not charged with violence</b>, remitted uncollected fines, and allowed salt-making for personal use on the coast and peaceful picketing.</li>
<li>Irwin <b>refused</b> Gandhi's demand for an inquiry into police excesses.</li></ul>
UPSC dropped its 2020 question on the pact. The pact was criticised for not saving Bhagat Singh, Rajguru and Sukhdev.</li>
<li>The <b>Karachi Congress</b> (March 1931, presided over by <b>Vallabhbhai Patel</b>) <b>endorsed</b> the Gandhi–Irwin Pact rather than opposing it. It adopted the <b>Resolution on Fundamental Rights and the Economic Programme</b>, drafted by <b>Jawaharlal Nehru</b> (influenced by M.N. Roy): civil liberties, equality, universal adult franchise, free primary education, a living wage, state ownership of key industries, relief from rent and debt.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Round Table Conferences</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Conference</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>First (November 1930–January 1931)</td><td>Congress absent. <b>Ambedkar demanded separate electorates</b> for the depressed classes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second (September–December 1931)</td><td>Gandhi the sole Congress representative; failed over communal representation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third (November–December 1932)</td><td><b>Congress did not take part</b>. Led to the White Paper (1933), a Joint Select Committee and the <b>Government of India Act 1935</b> (see 8.1)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Civil disobedience resumed in January 1932 and was withdrawn in April 1934.</p>

<h3>Communal Award and Poona Pact</h3>
<ul>
<li>British PM <b>Ramsay MacDonald's Communal Award</b> (16 August 1932) gave separate electorates to the depressed classes as well as Muslims, Sikhs, Christians, Anglo-Indians and Europeans. Gandhi, in Yerawada jail, began a <b>fast unto death</b> (20 September 1932) against separating the depressed classes from Hindu society.</li>
<li>The <b>Poona Pact</b> (24 September 1932), signed by Ambedkar and by Malaviya among others, replaced separate electorates with <b>joint electorates and reserved seats</b>: 148 provincial seats instead of 71, and 18% at the Centre. It also promised <b>fair representation in local bodies and public services</b> and funds for education.</li>
<li><b>After the Poona Pact</b> Gandhi put Harijan upliftment at the centre of his programme. He founded the <b>All India Anti-Untouchability League</b> (September 1932; renamed Harijan Sevak Sangh, 1933), started the weekly <i>Harijan</i> (1933), fasted for 21 days (1933) and made a nationwide Harijan tour (1933–34).</li>
<li>Temple-entry struggles: Vaikom (1924–25), Guruvayur (1931–32, Kelappan, A.K. Gopalan); Travancore's Temple Entry Proclamation (1936).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Organisation</th><th>Leader</th></tr>
<tr><td>All India Anti-Untouchability League</td><td>Mahatma Gandhi (1932)</td></tr>
<tr><td>All India Kisan Sabha</td><td>Swami Sahajanand Saraswati (president, 1936; N.G. Ranga secretary)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Self-Respect Movement</td><td>E.V. Ramasamy Naicker (1925)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depressed Classes Mission</td><td>V.R. Shinde (1906)</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="trap"><b>Common trap</b> Of four statements in a 2003 question, the false one was "the Karachi Congress opposed the Gandhi–Irwin Pact". True ones: the Rowlatt Act's indignation led to Jallianwala Bagh; Bose formed the Forward Bloc; Bhagat Singh was a founder of the HSRA.</div>
`);
;
// data/book_modern_m8.js
// Modern History book, Unit 8: Towards freedom, 1935 to 1947.
addBookTopic("modern", "m8-1", `
<p>The 1935 Act gave the provinces responsible government, and Congress ministries ruled most of them from 1937 to 1939. In the same years the Left grew inside and outside the Congress, among peasants, workers and the people of the princely states.</p>

<h3>The Government of India Act 1935</h3>
<ul>
<li>It came out of the Third Round Table Conference, the White Paper (1933) and a Joint Select Committee. It was the longest Act for India: 321 sections and 10 schedules.</li>
<li><b>All-India Federation</b> of British provinces and princely states (states joined voluntarily through Instruments of Accession). It never came into being because too few princes joined.</li>
<li>The British wanted the princes in it to <b>counterbalance the nationalists</b>. Princes were to nominate (not elect) one-third of the federal assembly and two-fifths of the Council of State.</li>
<li><b>Provincial autonomy</b>: dyarchy was <b>abolished in the provinces</b>, which got ministries responsible to legislatures. <b>Dyarchy was introduced at the Centre</b>, never implemented. "Dyarchy at the Centre as well as in the provinces" is therefore not a feature of the Act. At the Centre, <b>defence and foreign affairs</b> (with ecclesiastical and tribal affairs) were <b>reserved subjects under the Governor-General</b>, outside the control of the federal legislature.</li>
<li>Three lists: Federal (59 items), Provincial (54) and Concurrent (36). <b>Residuary powers lay with the Governor-General</b> (Section 104). Today they lie with Parliament (Article 248).</li>
<li>Governors kept wide discretion: they could <b>veto bills and legislate on their own</b> (ordinances and Governor's Acts).</li>
<li><b>Communal representation was extended, not abolished</b>: to depressed classes (as modified by the Poona Pact), women and labour.</li>
<li>Bicameral legislatures in six provinces; franchise for about 10% of people; women got reserved seats (see 2.2).</li>
<li>It created the <b>Federal Court</b> (Delhi, 1937; Chief Justice Maurice Gwyer), federal and provincial Public Service Commissions, and new provinces of Sind and Orissa. It separated Burma (1937) and abolished the India Council. The RBI came under a separate Act (1934).</li>
<li>Nehru called the Act "a machine with strong brakes but no engine". It is the largest single source of the Constitution: the federal scheme, the Governor, emergency provisions, PSCs and the three lists.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Provincial ministries, 1937–39</h3>
<ul>
<li>In the 1937 elections Congress formed ministries in Madras, Bombay, Central Provinces, UP, Bihar, Orissa and NWFP, and later Assam, eight of eleven provinces. In <b>Punjab</b> the <b>Unionist Party</b> (Sikandar Hayat Khan) governed; in Bengal, Fazlul Huq's Krishak Praja Party formed a coalition with the League.</li>
<li>The ministries <b>resigned</b> in October–November 1939 because Viceroy Linlithgow <b>declared India a party to the Second World War without consulting Indians</b>, and Britain refused to state war aims or promise independence. UPSC accepted "Congress did not accept the Viceroy's decision to declare war" as the reason. The League celebrated "Deliverance Day" (22 December 1939).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Left</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Communists</b>: M.N. Roy founded the émigré Communist Party of India at <b>Tashkent</b> (17 October 1920); the Kanpur conference followed (1925). Conspiracy cases: Peshawar (1922), Kanpur Bolshevik (1924), Meerut (1929). <b>P.C. Joshi</b> was the CPI's first general secretary (1935–47). The CPI backed the war after 1941 ("People's War") and opposed Quit India.</li>
<li>Ideas: Karl Marx explained class struggle through <b>dialectical materialism</b> (Hegel's dialectic applied to material conditions; applied to history it is historical materialism). Marx and <b>Friedrich Engels</b> wrote <i>The Communist Manifesto</i> (1848).</li>
<li>The <b>Congress Socialist Party</b> (1934: Acharya Narendra Dev, Jayaprakash Narayan, Minoo Masani, Lohia, Achyut Patwardhan) worked within the Congress for democratic socialism. It did <b>not</b>:
<ul><li>advocate boycott of British goods and evasion of taxes, which were Congress mass-movement tactics;</li>
<li>seek a dictatorship of the proletariat;</li>
<li>favour separate electorates, which it opposed.</li></ul>
It worked in kisan sabhas and unions and left the Congress in 1948.</li>
<li>Nehru's socialist speeches at Lucknow (1936) provoked the <b>Bombay Manifesto</b> (May 1936), in which 21 Bombay businessmen <b>openly opposed the preaching of socialism</b>. It did <b>not</b> win wide all-India business support (G.D. Birla preferred quiet influence).</li>
<li><b>Peasants</b>: the All India Kisan Sabha (Lucknow, April 1936; Swami Sahajanand Saraswati president, N.G. Ranga secretary). The <b>Tebhaga</b> movement (Bengal, 1946–47; Kisan Sabha) demanded that sharecroppers keep two-thirds of the crop, <b>cutting the landlord's share from half to one-third</b>, as the Floud Commission (1940) had recommended. The Telangana armed struggle (1946–51) was against the Nizam and jagirdars.</li>
<li><b>Labour</b>: the <b>Hind Mazdoor Sabha</b> (Calcutta, December 1948) was founded by socialists including <b>Ashok Mehta</b>, Basawon Singh, R.S. Ruikar (first president), Maniben Kara, <b>T.S. Ramanujam</b> and <b>G.G. Mehta</b>, against both AITUC (communist) and INTUC (Congress, 1947). Later: BMS (1955), CITU (1970).</li>
<li><b>Ambedkar</b> founded the <b>Independent Labour Party</b> (1936) and the <b>All India Scheduled Castes Federation</b> (1942). The Peasants and Workers Party (1947) was founded by Keshavrao Jedhe and Shankarrao More, not Ambedkar. He wrote <i>Annihilation of Caste</i> (1936) and converted to Buddhism at Nagpur on 14 October 1956.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Subhas Chandra Bose and the Forward Bloc</h3>
<ul>
<li>At <b>Haripura</b> (1938), as Congress president with Gandhi's backing, Bose set up the National Planning Committee under Nehru and <b>advocated the Roman script for Hindustani</b>.</li>
<li>At <b>Tripuri</b> (1939) he was re-elected, defeating Gandhi's candidate Pattabhi Sitaramayya ("his defeat is my defeat," said Gandhi). The Pant resolution tied his Working Committee to Gandhi's wishes.</li>
<li>Bose formed the <b>Forward Bloc</b> (May 1939) because he <b>failed to win Gandhi's confidence</b>, the <b>Congress Left was disunited</b>, and the <b>Royists and Congress Socialists (JP) preferred Congress unity</b> to backing him. The provisional key did not count the Communists' stand as a cause (debated). He resigned in April 1939; the Bloc was meant as a left platform.</li>
<li>For his escape (1941) and the INA, see 8.2.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Princely states' people</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>All India States People's Conference</b> (1927) campaigned for responsible government in the princely states. <b>Nehru presided</b> over its 1939 Ludhiana session. After Haripura (1938) the Congress backed state struggles more openly; Praja Mandals grew in many states.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m8-2", `
<p>The Second World War forced Britain to bargain with Indian leaders. When the Cripps offer failed, the Congress launched Quit India, while Subhas Bose led the INA from abroad. By 1946 even the armed forces were wavering.</p>

<h3>August Offer and Individual Satyagraha</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>August Offer</b> (1940, Linlithgow): dominion status as the goal, an expanded Executive Council and a war advisory council, and a promise that no constitution would be imposed without minority consent. The Congress rejected it.</li>
<li><b>Individual Satyagraha</b> (October 1940) asserted free speech against the war effort without disrupting it. <b>Vinoba Bhave</b> was the first satyagrahi (17 October 1940), <b>Jawaharlal Nehru the second</b>, Brahma Dutt the third.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Cripps Mission, March 1942</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sent by Prime Minister <b>Winston Churchill</b>'s War Cabinet, under US and Chinese pressure, as Japan advanced (Singapore and Rangoon fell). The Congress's official negotiators were <b>Maulana Azad</b> (then Congress president) <b>and Nehru</b>.</li>
<li>Proposals:
<ul><li>An Indian Union with <b>Dominion status after the war</b>, free to leave the Commonwealth; not full independence.</li>
<li>After the war, a <b>constitution-making body</b>: provincial members <b>elected</b> by provincial assemblies by proportional representation, and princely members <b>nominated</b> by the rulers.</li>
<li><b>Any province could refuse the new constitution and sign a separate agreement with Britain</b>, which opened the door to partition.</li>
<li>Britain would keep defence during the war.</li></ul>
Its advance over the August Offer: Indians would frame their own constitution.</li>
<li>Everyone rejected it: Congress (no immediate transfer of power, the opt-out, princely nomination, defence), the League (no explicit Pakistan), depressed classes and Sikhs. Gandhi called it "a post-dated cheque"; others added "on a crashing bank".</li>
</ul>

<h3>Quit India, 1942</h3>
<ul>
<li>It was launched <b>in response to the failure of the Cripps proposals</b>. The resolution was drafted at Wardha (14 July 1942) and <b>adopted by the AICC</b> at Gowalia Tank, Bombay on <b>8 August 1942</b>, where Gandhi gave the call "<b>Do or Die</b>" (karo ya maro). Leaders were arrested early on 9 August (Operation Zero Hour); 9 August is August Kranti Day.</li>
<li>Gandhi's instructions on the eve:
<ul><li>Government servants should <b>openly declare allegiance to the Congress</b>, not resign.</li>
<li>Soldiers should <b>refuse to fire on their own people</b>, not leave their posts.</li>
<li><b>Princes should accept the sovereignty of their own people.</b></li></ul></li>
<li>With all leaders jailed, the movement was <b>not actually led by Gandhi</b>. It ran leaderless and spontaneous, through students, peasants and underground networks:
<ul><li><b>Aruna Asaf Ali</b> hoisted the flag at Gowalia Tank and organised underground activity.</li>
<li>Jayaprakash Narayan and Ram Manohar Lohia went underground.</li>
<li><b>Usha Mehta</b> ran the secret <b>Congress Radio</b> from Bombay (August–November 1942).</li></ul>
Parallel governments arose in Ballia (Chittu Pande), Tamluk (Jatiya Sarkar; Matangini Hazra) and Satara (Prati Sarkar, the longest; Y.B. Chavan, Nana Patil).</li>
<li><b>Linlithgow</b> called it "by far the most serious rebellion since 1857", because <b>peasants joined in large numbers</b> in Bihar, eastern UP, Midnapore and Satara, cutting rail and telegraph lines. The League, CPI, Hindu Mahasabha and princes stayed away.</li>
<li>Gandhi, held at the Aga Khan Palace in Poona (where Kasturba and Mahadev Desai died), fasted for 21 days in February 1943.</li>
<li><b>C. Rajagopalachari's</b> pamphlet <b><i>The Way Out</i></b> (1944) offered a <b>solution to the constitutional deadlock</b> between the Congress and the League: the C.R. Formula, under which the League would back independence and, after the war, a plebiscite in Muslim-majority areas would decide on separation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Subhas Bose and the INA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bose escaped house arrest (January 1941) via Kabul to Germany. There he raised the <b>Free India Legion</b> (about 3,000 Indian prisoners of war), the Free India Centre and Azad Hind Radio. (Rash Behari Bose led the Indian Independence League in Japan.)</li>
<li>The first INA (Mohan Singh, 1942) collapsed. Subhas Bose took command in <b>Singapore</b> (July 1943), where the INA was revived, and proclaimed the Provisional Government of Azad Hind there (21 October 1943). Slogans: "Delhi Chalo", "Give me blood and I will give you freedom". It had a Rani of Jhansi Regiment (Lakshmi Sahgal) and fought in the Imphal–Kohima campaign (1944). Bose reportedly died in a plane crash in Taipei (August 1945).</li>
<li><b>Shah Nawaz Khan, Prem Kumar Sehgal and Gurbaksh Singh Dhillon</b>, all <b>INA officers</b> (a Muslim, a Hindu and a Sikh), were tried at the Red Fort (November 1945). Bhulabhai Desai led the defence with Tej Bahadur Sapru, K.N. Katju, Asaf Ali and Nehru. Auchinleck remitted their sentences under public pressure (January 1946). Slogan: "Lal Qila se aayi awaaz, Sehgal Dhillon Shahnawaz".</li>
</ul>

<h3>Wavell Plan and the RIN mutiny</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Wavell Plan</b> (June 1945), discussed at the <b>Simla Conference</b> called by Viceroy <b>Wavell</b> (not Mountbatten, who came in March 1947), proposed an all-Indian Executive Council except the Viceroy and Commander-in-Chief, with <b>equal numbers of caste Hindus and Muslims</b>. It was an interim wartime arrangement, not, per the key, a scheme to avoid partition. It failed when Jinnah insisted that the League alone nominate all Muslim members.</li>
<li>The <b>Royal Indian Navy revolt</b> began on 18 February 1946 on HMIS Talwar in <b>Bombay</b> and spread to <b>Karachi</b>, Calcutta and Madras (about 20,000 sailors). Causes were poor food, racial discrimination and the INA trials. The Naval Central Strike Committee was led by M.S. Khan; the CPI backed it, while Patel and Jinnah persuaded the sailors to surrender. It showed Britain could no longer rely on Indian forces.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Event</th><th>Date</th></tr>
<tr><td>Second Round Table Conference</td><td>September–December 1931</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quit India launched</td><td>8 August 1942</td></tr>
<tr><td>RIN mutiny</td><td>February 1946</td></tr>
</table>
`);

addBookTopic("modern", "m8-3", `
<p>In 1946–47 Britain tried to hand over power to a united India, failed, and partitioned the country. The Constituent Assembly, elected under the Cabinet Mission plan, carried on to frame the Constitution.</p>

<h3>Cabinet Mission, 1946</h3>
<ul>
<li>The mission (Pethick-Lawrence, Stafford Cripps, A.V. Alexander; March–June 1946) <b>recommended a federal government</b>:
<ul><li>A loose <b>Union</b> controlling only defence, foreign affairs and communications.</li>
<li>Provinces grouped into sections A, B and C, with residuary powers.</li>
<li>Rejection of a full Pakistan.</li></ul>
It did not enlarge the powers of Indian courts or deal with Indianising the ICS.</li>
<li>It proposed a <b>Constituent Assembly</b> whose provincial members (292) would be <b>elected by the provincial legislative assemblies</b> by single transferable vote, divided among General, Muslim and Sikh seats by population (about one seat per 10 lakh people). Princely states' 93 members would be nominated. Planned strength 389; 299 after Partition.</li>
<li>It suggested reconstituting the Viceroy's Executive Council as an <b>interim government</b> in which <b>all portfolios, including the War Member's, would be held by Indian leaders</b>. The Simla Conference of 1945 had kept War with the Commander-in-Chief.</li>
<li>The League accepted the plan in June but withdrew over grouping disputes and called <b>Direct Action Day</b> (16 August 1946), leading to the Calcutta killings. Its rejection meant the <b>last opportunity to avoid partition</b> was lost.</li>
<li>In the July 1946 elections the Congress won 208 seats and the League 73 of 296.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Interim Government, 1946</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Member</th><th>Portfolio</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Jawaharlal Nehru</b></td><td><b>Vice-President of the Executive Council</b>, in effect head of the interim cabinet; External Affairs and Commonwealth Relations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vallabhbhai Patel</td><td>Home, Information and Broadcasting</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Rajendra Prasad</b></td><td><b>Food and Agriculture</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Baldev Singh</td><td>Defence</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. Rajagopalachari</td><td>Education</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jagjivan Ram</td><td>Labour</td></tr>
<tr><td>Liaquat Ali Khan (League, from October)</td><td>Finance</td></tr>
</table>
<p>It took office on 2 September 1946. Rajendra Prasad was elected President of the Constituent Assembly on 11 December 1946.</p>

<h3>Towards partition</h3>
<ul>
<li>Attlee announced (20 February 1947) that Britain would leave by June 1948. As alternatives to partition:
<ul><li><b>Gandhi</b> suggested to Mountbatten (April 1947) that <b>Jinnah be invited to form the government</b>; the Congress Working Committee rejected the idea.</li>
<li>In <b>Bengal</b>, H.S. Suhrawardy and Sarat Chandra Bose (with Abul Hashim and K.S. Roy) proposed a <b>united, independent Bengal</b>; the Congress and the Hindu Mahasabha rejected it.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Lord Mountbatten</b>'s first draft, "<b>Plan Balkan</b>" (May 1947), would have handed power to the provinces and princely states separately. Nehru rejected it at Simla, and V.P. Menon drafted the two-dominion <b>3 June Plan</b>. The Indian Independence Act followed (July 1947).</li>
<li>The <b>Radcliffe Committee</b> (two Boundary Commissions, Punjab and Bengal, under Sir Cyril Radcliffe, each with two Congress and two League judges) was appointed to <b>delimit the boundaries between India and Pakistan</b>. Radcliffe had never visited India and had about five weeks; the line was announced on 17 August 1947. Controversies: Gurdaspur, Chittagong Hill Tracts, Murshidabad and Khulna.</li>
<li>At Partition the <b>Congress president was J.B. Kripalani</b> (November 1946–47), followed by Pattabhi Sitaramayya (1948).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Constituent Assembly and people of the period</h3>
<ul>
<li>First meeting 9 December 1946 (Sachchidananda Sinha, temporary president). Nehru moved the Objectives Resolution (13 December 1946). The Drafting Committee was formed under Ambedkar (29 August 1947). The Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949 and came into force on 26 January 1950.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Person</th><th>Position</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Tej Bahadur Sapru</b></td><td>President of the <b>All India (National) Liberal Federation</b>; attended all three Round Table Conferences; INA defence</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>K.C. Neogy</b></td><td><b>Member of the Constituent Assembly</b> (West Bengal); chaired the First Finance Commission (1951)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>P.C. Joshi</b></td><td>First <b>General Secretary of the CPI</b> (1935–47)</td></tr>
</table>
`);
;
// data/book_modern_m9.js
// Modern History book, Unit 9: India after independence.
addBookTopic("modern", "m9-1", `
<p>After 1947 India had to integrate over 560 princely states, redraw its internal map, and build new institutions. UPSC asks about this through dates, firsts and the books of those who took part.</p>

<h3>Integration of the princely states</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sardar Patel, as head of the States Ministry, with its secretary <b>V.P. Menon</b>, persuaded almost all states to sign Instruments of Accession (defence, external affairs, communications) before 15 August 1947 and later merger agreements. Holdouts were Junagadh (plebiscite, 1948), Hyderabad (Operation Polo, September 1948) and Jammu and Kashmir (accession October 1947).</li>
<li>V.P. Menon wrote <b><i>The Story of the Integration of the Indian States</i></b> (1956) and <i>The Transfer of Power in India</i>; he also drafted the Instrument of Accession.</li>
<li>French and Portuguese territories: Chandernagore (1950); Puducherry (de facto 1954, de jure 1962); Goa, Daman and Diu (<b>Operation Vijay</b>, 19 December 1961). Goa became a state in 1987, and Konkani entered the Eighth Schedule in 1992. Sikkim became the 22nd state on 16 May 1975 (36th Amendment).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Reorganisation of states</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Andhra State</b> was carved out of Madras State on 1 October 1953 after Potti Sriramulu died fasting (December 1952). Its capital was <b>Kurnool</b>, with the High Court at Guntur. Andhra Pradesh (with Telangana areas) followed in 1956, and Telangana separated in 2014.</li>
<li>The States Reorganisation Commission (Fazl Ali, with K.M. Panikkar and H.N. Kunzru) led to the States Reorganisation Act 1956: 14 states and 6 union territories, largely on linguistic lines.</li>
<li><b>Gujarat and Maharashtra</b> were created from bilingual Bombay State on <b>1 May 1960</b> (after the Mahagujarat and Samyukta Maharashtra movements), not in 1966. <b>Haryana</b> was created in 1966 (Punjab Reorganisation Act). Later: Nagaland (1963), Himachal Pradesh (1971), Meghalaya, Manipur and Tripura (1972), Mizoram and Arunachal Pradesh (1987), Chhattisgarh, Uttarakhand and Jharkhand (2000).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Events in order</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Event</th><th>Date</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Air India nationalised</b> (Air Corporations Act). It began as J.R.D. Tata's Tata Airlines (1932) and returned to the Tata group in January 2022</td><td>1953</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Imperial Bank of India renamed State Bank of India</b> (SBI Act, on the Gorwala Committee's advice). The Imperial Bank was formed in 1921 from the three Presidency banks</td><td>1 July 1955</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>First elected communist government</b>: E.M.S. Namboodiripad in Kerala; dismissed under Article 356 in 1959 after the "liberation struggle"</td><td>April 1957</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Goa liberated</b></td><td>19 December 1961</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bank nationalisation (14 banks; 6 more in 1980)</td><td>1969</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bangladesh formed</b> (Pakistan's surrender in Dhaka)</td><td>16 December 1971</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Pokhran-I</b> ("Smiling Buddha")</td><td>18 May 1974</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sikkim becomes the 22nd state</b></td><td>16 May 1975</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>SLV-3 launch</b> (failed 10 August 1979; first success with Rohini, 18 July 1980)</td><td>1979</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Movements and memoirs</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Bhoodan</b> (land gift) began on 18 April 1951 at <b>Pochampalli</b> (Nalgonda, Telangana), in the area of the Telangana armed struggle, when landlord V. Ramachandra Reddy gave 100 acres to landless Dalits on <b>Vinoba Bhave</b>'s appeal. It grew into Gramdan. (Pochampalli is also known for its ikat sarees, a GI product.) The Sarvodaya Samaj (1948) carried on Gandhi's constructive work.</li>
<li><b><i>Ayodhya: 6 December 1992</i></b> was written by former Prime Minister <b>P.V. Narasimha Rao</b> (published posthumously, 2006). It gives his account of the Babri Masjid demolition, which happened during his government. The Supreme Court settled the title dispute in November 2019; the Ram temple was consecrated in January 2024.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
