// Art & Culture book, Unit 1: Religion and philosophy.
addBookTopic("culture", "c1-1", `
<p>Buddhism and Jainism arose in the 6th–5th century BCE among the <b>shramana</b> traditions that questioned Vedic ritual. Their ideas, sects and symbols shaped Indian art for a thousand years.</p>

<h3>Ascetic traditions and their terms</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Parivrajaka</b></td><td>A <b>wandering renunciant</b> who has left household life</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Shramana</b></td><td>An ascetic of the non-Vedic traditions (Buddhist, Jain, Ajivika) seeking liberation by effort; they rejected priestly authority, so <b>not a high-status priest</b>. Megasthenes called them "Sarmanes"; Ashoka urged respect for both Brahmanas and Shramanas</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Upasaka / Upasika</b></td><td><b>Lay follower of Buddhism</b> (Jains: shravaka)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bhikkhu / Bhikkhuni; Thera / Theri</td><td>Monk / nun; elder monk / nun (<i>Therigatha</i>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ajivika</td><td>Makkhali Gosala's sect, believing in fate (niyati)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Charvaka / Lokayata</td><td>Materialist school (see 1.2)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Buddhism</h3>
<ul>
<li>Three Jewels: Buddha, Dhamma, Sangha. Nirvana is the extinction of craving and rebirth.</li>
<li><b>Mahayana</b> centres on the <b>bodhisattva</b>, who delays nirvana to help others (Avalokiteshvara/Padmapani, Manjushri, Maitreya, Vajrapani). <b>Paramitas</b> are the <b>perfections whose attainment leads along the bodhisattva path</b>. The six are dana (generosity), shila (morality), kshanti (patience), virya (energy), dhyana (meditation) and prajna (wisdom); later lists have ten. The Prajnaparamita sutras (Heart, Diamond) teach the perfection of wisdom.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Symbols and mudras</h4>
<p>In early (aniconic) Buddhist art at Bharhut, Sanchi and early Amaravati, the Buddha is not shown in human form but by symbols:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Event or idea</th><th>Symbol</th></tr>
<tr><td>Birth</td><td>Lotus; Maya's dream of a white elephant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Great Departure</td><td>Riderless horse (Kanthaka)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Enlightenment and <b>meditation</b></td><td>Bodhi tree; <b>empty seat</b> (vajrasana), which NCERT says <b>indicates the Buddha's meditation</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>First Sermon, Sarnath</td><td>Wheel (dharmachakra), deer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mahaparinibbana (death), Kushinagar</td><td>Stupa</td></tr>
<tr><td>His presence</td><td>Footprints, umbrella</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Human images appeared around the 1st century CE under the Kushanas, in the Gandhara and Mathura schools (see 2.1).</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Mudra</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bhumisparsha</b> (right hand touching the earth)</td><td>The Buddha <b>calls the Earth to witness</b> his merit and purity when <b>Mara</b> challenges him at Bodh Gaya, just before enlightenment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dharmachakra</td><td>Turning the wheel of law: the first sermon</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dhyana</td><td>Meditation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Abhaya</td><td>Fearlessness, protection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Varada</td><td>Giving boons</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vitarka</td><td>Teaching, discussion</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Jainism</h3>
<ul>
<li>Twenty-four <b>tirthankaras</b> ("ford-makers"), from Rishabhanatha to Mahavira. Five vows (mahavratas): ahimsa, satya, asteya, aparigraha and brahmacharya (added by Mahavira). Triratna: right faith, right knowledge, right conduct.</li>
<li>Councils: Pataliputra (about 300 BCE, Sthulabhadra) and Valabhi (5th century CE, where the canon was written down).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Svetambara ("white-clad")</th><th>Digambara ("sky-clad")</th></tr>
<tr><td>Followers of Sthulabhadra, who stayed in Magadha during the famine</td><td>Followers of Bhadrabahu, who went south to Shravanabelagola with Chandragupta Maurya</td></tr>
<tr><td>Women can attain liberation; Mallinath is held to be a woman tirthankara</td><td>Women cannot attain moksha directly; monks go naked</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sub-sects: Murtipujaka, <b>Sthanakvasi</b>, Terapanthi</td><td>Sub-sects: Bisapanthi, Terapanthi, Taranpanthi</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Sthanakvasi</b> sect belongs to <b>Jainism</b> (Svetambara). Inspired by Lonka Shah (1450s), they reject idol worship and temples and pray in <i>sthanakas</i> (halls); monks wear a mouth-cloth (muhpatti).</li>
<li><b>Mahamastakabhisheka</b> is the anointing, every 12 years, of the 57-ft monolith of <b>Bahubali</b> (Gommateshwara) at Shravanabelagola, Karnataka, with milk, saffron and other substances. The statue was made about 981–983 CE under Chavundaraya, minister of the Western Gangas. Bahubali was a son of Rishabhanatha, <b>not a tirthankara</b>; the last tirthankara was Mahavira.</li>
<li>Jain holy sites: <b>Palitana</b> (over 800 shrines on Shatrunjaya hill, <b>Bhavnagar</b> district, Gujarat), Dilwara (Mount Abu), Ranakpur, Sammed Shikharji (Jharkhand), Pawapuri (Bihar).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("culture", "c1-2", `
<p>Indian thought developed six orthodox philosophical systems and several heterodox ones. From the 7th century, the Bhakti movement turned religion towards personal devotion, often in the people's own languages.</p>

<h3>The six systems</h3>
<p>The orthodox (<i>astika</i>) darshanas accept Vedic authority and come in three pairs:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Pair</th><th>Founders and ideas</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sankhya</b> – Yoga</td><td>Kapila (Ishvarakrishna's <i>Sankhya Karika</i>) – Patanjali (ashtanga yoga, adds Ishvara)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nyaya – Vaisheshika</td><td>Gautama (logic) – Kanada (atoms)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mimamsa – Vedanta</td><td>Jaimini (ritual) – Badarayana (<i>Brahma Sutras</i>)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Sankhya</b> is a dualism of <b>purusha</b> (consciousness, many) and <b>prakriti</b> (matter, with three gunas: sattva, rajas, tamas). It <b>accepts rebirth</b>: the subtle body carries karmic traces from birth to birth. Liberation (kaivalya) comes from <b>self-knowledge</b>, discriminating purusha from prakriti, <b>not from any external agent</b>. Classical Sankhya is non-theistic.</li>
<li>Heterodox (<i>nastika</i>) schools: <b>Lokayata/Charvaka</b> (materialism, perception only, no rebirth), Buddhism, Jainism, Ajivika. <b>Kapalika</b> is a tantric Shaiva ascetic sect, not a darshana. So "Lokayata and Kapalika" do not form part of the six systems. Other Shaiva sects: Pashupata (Lakulisha), Kalamukha, Lingayat, Kashmir Shaivism.</li>
<li>The national motto <b>"Satyameva Jayate"</b> comes from the <b>Mundaka Upanishad</b> (3.1.6; Atharva Veda); Madan Mohan Malaviya popularised it. The State Emblem (26 January 1950) adapts Ashoka's Sarnath lion capital. Other mottos: "Yogakshemam Vahamyaham" (LIC, from the <i>Gita</i>), "Nabhah Sparsham Deeptam" (IAF, <i>Gita</i>), "Sham No Varunah" (Navy, Taittiriya Upanishad).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Vedanta teachers</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Teacher</th><th>Period</th><th>School</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Adi Shankaracharya</b></td><td>8th century (Kalady, Kerala)</td><td>Advaita (non-dualism); four mathas</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Ramanuja</b></td><td>11th–12th century (Sriperumbudur)</td><td>Vishishtadvaita (qualified non-dualism)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Madhva</td><td>13th century (Udupi)</td><td>Dvaita</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nimbarka; Vallabha</td><td></td><td>Dvaitadvaita; Shuddhadvaita (Pushtimarg)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Chaitanya</b></td><td>1486–1534 (Nabadwip)</td><td>Achintya Bhedabheda; Gaudiya Vaishnavism, sankirtan</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>So the chronological order is Shankaracharya, Ramanuja, Chaitanya.</li>
<li><b>Ramanuja</b> taught that <b>the best means of salvation is devotion</b> (bhakti) and total surrender (prapatti) to Vishnu. The soul and world are real, but are the "body" of Brahman. Works: <i>Sri Bhashya</i>, <i>Gita Bhashya</i>, <i>Vedartha Sangraha</i>. He opened worship to lower castes at Melkote. The 216-ft seated <b>Statue of Equality</b> (panchaloha) at Muchintal near Hyderabad was inaugurated in February 2022, his 1,000th birth anniversary; it is the second-tallest seated statue after Thailand's Great Buddha.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Bhakti and reform sects</h3>
<ul>
<li>Tamil region: Alvars (Vaishnava) and Nayanars (Shaiva). The <b>Siddhars</b> (Thirumoolar, Sivavakkiyar) were <b>monotheists who condemned idolatry</b>, rituals and caste; they founded Siddha medicine.</li>
<li><b>Lingayats / Virashaivas</b> (Basavanna, 12th century, minister of Kalachuri king Bijjala at Kalyani): Shiva worshipped as the ishtalinga worn on the body. They <b>questioned rebirth and rejected caste</b> and Brahminical ritual, supported widow remarriage and buried their dead. Others: Allama Prabhu, Akka Mahadevi; the Anubhava Mantapa assembly; kayaka (work is worship) and dasoha (sharing); <i>vachanas</i> in Kannada.</li>
<li><b>Ramananda</b> (14th–15th century, Varanasi), from Ramanuja's line, was the <b>first Bhakti saint to preach in Hindi</b>. His disciples came from all castes: Kabir, Ravidas, Sena, Dhanna.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Saint</th><th>Occupation</th></tr>
<tr><td>Namdev (Maharashtra, Varkari)</td><td>Tailor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kabir (Varanasi)</td><td>Weaver</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ravidas</td><td>Cobbler</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sena</td><td>Barber</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Christianity in India</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>St Francis Xavier</b> was a <b>founding member of the Jesuits</b> (with Ignatius of Loyola, 1534). He did <b>not die in Goa</b>: he died in 1552 on Shangchuan Island, China. His body lies in the <b>Basilica of Bom Jesus</b>, Old Goa, which is dedicated to the Infant Jesus, not to him. His <b>feast is celebrated in Goa every 3 December</b>; his relics are exposed about once a decade (latest November 2024–January 2025). "Churches and Convents of Goa" is a World Heritage Site.</li>
<li>The Portuguese brought the printing press to Goa (1556) and set up the Goa Inquisition (1560). Goa was liberated on 19 December 1961.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_culture_c2.js
// Art & Culture book, Unit 2: Architecture and sculpture.
addBookTopic("culture", "c2-1", `
<p>The earliest surviving Indian monuments are Buddhist, Jain and Ajivika: stupas, and halls and monasteries cut into rock. Sculpture developed in three great schools.</p>

<h3>Stupas</h3>
<ul>
<li>The stupa is <b>not Buddhist in origin</b>: it grew from ancient burial mounds. Buddhism adopted and popularised it, and Jains built stupas too (Kankali Tila, Mathura).</li>
<li>A stupa was generally a <b>repository of relics</b>. The Buddha's relics were divided among eight claimants, and Ashoka is said to have redistributed them into 84,000 stupas.</li>
<li>Stupas were also <b>votive</b> (built to gain merit) and <b>commemorative</b> (marking events in the Buddha's life).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Part</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>Anda</td><td>Hemispherical dome</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harmika</td><td>Square railing on top, abode of the gods</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yashti, chhatra</td><td>Central mast and umbrellas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Medhi</td><td>Raised terrace</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pradakshina patha</td><td>Path for walking around</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vedika; torana</td><td>Railing; gateways (Sanchi's four toranas, 1st century BCE)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Famous stupas: Sanchi (begun by Ashoka, enlarged by the Shungas), Bharhut, <b>Amaravati</b>, Nagarjunakonda, Dhamek (Sarnath), Piprahwa (relics found 1898; some were put up for auction in 2025 and India got them back), Kesariya (Bihar). In Sri Lanka a stupa is a dagoba, in Tibet a chorten, in East Asia a pagoda.</li>
<li>The <b>Amaravati</b> Mahachaitya stood in the <b>lower Krishna valley</b> (Guntur district, Andhra Pradesh). It was probably <b>the largest stupa in India</b>, bigger than Sanchi, so it was not "next only to Sanchi". Its school <b>shaped later South Indian sculpture</b> and travelled to <b>Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia</b>. Patrons were the Satavahanas, Ikshvakus, merchants, monks and nuns. Colin Mackenzie rediscovered it (1797); much was burned for lime, and Walter Elliot's finds ("Elliot marbles") went to the British Museum.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Gandhara</th><th>Mathura</th><th>Amaravati</th></tr>
<tr><td>Region</td><td>Northwest (Peshawar, Taxila)</td><td>Mathura, UP</td><td>Krishna–Godavari, Andhra</td></tr>
<tr><td>Patrons</td><td><b>Kushanas</b></td><td>Kushanas</td><td>Satavahanas, Ikshvakus</td></tr>
<tr><td>Material</td><td>Grey-blue schist, stucco</td><td>Spotted red sandstone</td><td>White-green limestone ("marble")</td></tr>
<tr><td>Style</td><td>Greco-Roman: wavy hair, heavy drapery</td><td>Indigenous; Buddhist, Jain and Hindu</td><td>Narrative, crowded, figures in motion</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Rock-cut caves</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>chaitya</b> is a <b>place of worship</b>: a hall with a stupa at its apsidal end and a horseshoe window (Karle, the largest; Bhaja; Ajanta 9, 10, 19, 26). A <b>vihara</b> is a <b>dwelling place for monks</b>, with cells around a hall. A mahavihara was a monastic university (Nalanda, Vikramashila).</li>
<li>The <b>oldest rock-cut caves</b> are the <b>Barabar</b> caves in Bihar (3rd century BCE), made for the <b>Ajivikas by Ashoka</b> (not Chandragupta Maurya); the nearby Nagarjuni caves were made by his grandson Dasharatha. The Lomas Rishi cave's doorway imitates timber. Badami's caves are much later (6th century CE).</li>
<li><b>Ellora</b> (5th–10th century) has caves of <b>different faiths</b>: Buddhist (1–12), Hindu (13–29, including the Kailasa temple, Cave 16, by Rashtrakuta Krishna I) and Jain (30–34).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Religion</th><th>Caves</th></tr>
<tr><td>Buddhist</td><td>Ajanta, Karle, <b>Bhaja</b> (near Lonavala, 2nd century BCE), Kanheri, Nashik, Bedsa, Ellora 1–12, Bagh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hindu</td><td>Ellora 13–29, Elephanta, Badami 1–3, Udayagiri (Vidisha), Mahabalipuram mandapas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jain</td><td>Ellora 30–34, Badami Cave 4, Udayagiri–Khandagiri (Odisha, Kharavela), <b>Sittanavasal</b> (Pudukkottai, Tamil Nadu: Jain cave shrine with Pandya-era frescoes)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ajivika</td><td>Barabar, Nagarjuni</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Besnagar</b> (ancient Vidisha) is famous for the <b>Heliodorus pillar</b> (about 113 BCE), set up by the Greek envoy of Antialcidas who called himself a Bhagavata: early evidence of Vaishnavism, not a Shaivite cave.</li>
<li><b>Ajanta</b> lies in a horseshoe gorge of the <b>Waghora</b> river (Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar district). It was rediscovered by John Smith in 1819 and has two phases: Satavahana (2nd–1st century BCE) and Vakataka (5th century CE, under Harishena). Its Cave 26 has a grand reclining <b>Mahaparinirvana</b> Buddha with celestial beings above and mourners below.</li>
<li><b>Ajanta and Mahabalipuram</b> share only <b>rock-cut monuments</b>. They differ in period (Ajanta to the 6th century, Mahabalipuram 7th–8th century, Pallava) and religion (Buddhist against Hindu). Mahabalipuram has the Pancha Rathas, the Shore Temple and "<b>Arjuna's Penance</b>"/"Descent of the Ganga", carved on huge boulders.</li>
<li>The colossal <b>Varaha</b> relief is at the <b>Udayagiri caves near Vidisha</b> (Gupta, Chandragupta II), not Mount Abu, which is known for the Jain Dilwara temples.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Monument</th><th>Date</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Rock-cut elephant at Dhauli</b> (above Ashoka's edicts near Bhubaneswar)</td><td>3rd century BCE (earliest)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Varaha image, Udayagiri</td><td>Early 5th century CE</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mahabalipuram monuments</td><td>7th–8th century CE</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lingaraja temple, Bhubaneswar</td><td>11th century CE</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Himalayan monasteries</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Tabo</b> (Spiti valley, <b>Himachal Pradesh</b>), founded in 996 by the translator Rinchen Zangpo, is called the "Ajanta of the Himalayas".</li>
<li><b>Nako</b> (Lotsava Lhakhang) is in Kinnaur, Himachal Pradesh, not Zanskar.</li>
<li><b>Alchi</b> (11th–12th century, Kashmiri-style murals) is in Ladakh.</li>
<li><b>Key (Kye) monastery</b>, the largest in Spiti, is in Himachal Pradesh, not Arunachal Pradesh.</li>
<li>Others: Hemis, Thiksey, Lamayuru (Ladakh); Phugtal (Zanskar); Rumtek, Pemayangtse (Sikkim); Tawang (Arunachal, the largest in India).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("culture", "c2-2", `
<p>Structural Hindu temples began in the Gupta period and grew into three styles. Regional dynasties then added their own features, from Odisha's Kalinga style to Vijayanagara's great gateways.</p>

<h3>The three styles</h3>
<p><b>Nagara, Dravida and Vesara</b> are the <b>three main styles of Indian temple architecture</b>.</p>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Nagara (north)</th><th>Dravida (south)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Tower</td><td>Curvilinear <b>shikhara</b> topped by an amalaka and kalasha</td><td>Stepped pyramidal <b>vimana</b> with a domed or octagonal cap</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gateways, walls</td><td>Usually no big gateway</td><td>Enclosure walls and huge <b>gopurams</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Tank</td><td>Rare</td><td>Common</td></tr>
<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Odisha (Lingaraja, Konark), Khajuraho, Solanki (Modhera)</td><td>Brihadeeswara (Thanjavur), Meenakshi (Madurai)</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Vesara</b> is a hybrid of the Deccan: Later Chalukya and Hoysala temples.</p>
<ul>
<li>Temple terms: garbhagriha (sanctum), mandapa (hall), antarala (vestibule), jagati (platform). A <b>panchayatana</b> temple is a <b>style of construction</b> with a main shrine and four subsidiary shrines at the corners. Examples: Dashavatara (Deogarh), Kandariya Mahadeva and Lakshmana (Khajuraho), Brahmeswara (Bhubaneswar).</li>
<li>The Badami Chalukyan region was a <b>laboratory</b> where both styles stand side by side:
<ul><li>Nagara shikharas: the <b>Huchimalligudi temple</b> at Aihole (7th century, an early curvilinear tower and one of the first with an antarala) and the Gupta <b>Dashavatara temple</b> at Deogarh (about 500 CE).</li>
<li>Dravida: <b>Malegitti Shivalaya</b> (Badami) and the <b>Virupaksha temple</b> at Pattadakal (740s), built by Queen Lokamahadevi for Vikramaditya II's victory over the Pallavas and modelled on Kanchi's Kailasanatha.</li></ul>
Pattadakal (WHS 1987) also has Nagara temples: Papanatha, Galaganatha and others.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Regional styles</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Style</th><th>Features and examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Kalinga (Odisha)</td><td><b>Lingaraja</b>, <b>Bhubaneswar</b> (11th century, dedicated to Harihara; rekha deul, jagamohana); <b>Rajarani</b> (11th century, no presiding deity); <b>Konark</b> Sun Temple (13th century, Narasimhadeva I; a chariot with 24 wheels and 7 horses). The Konark wheel inspired Indian Airlines' 2005 logo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chandela (Khajuraho)</td><td><b>Kandariya Mahadeva</b> (Madhya Pradesh), Lakshmana</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solanki (Gujarat)</td><td>Modhera Sun Temple; <b>Somnath</b> rebuilt in this style</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hoysala (Karnataka)</td><td><b>Belur</b> (Chennakeshava, 1117), <b>Halebidu</b> (Hoysaleswara, dedicated to Shiva), Somanathapura: star-shaped plans in soapstone; WHS 2023</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vijayanagara</td><td>Tall raya gopurams; yali pillars; <b>kalyana mandapas</b> (pillared halls for the deity's marriage), which were a notable feature of Vijayanagara temples; amman shrines. At <b>Hampi</b>: the <b>Virupaksha</b> temple (still in worship; gopuram by Krishnadevaraya) and the <b>Vittala</b> temple with its <b>56 musical pillars</b> and stone chariot (on the ₹50 note). <b>Vidyashankara</b> temple, Sringeri (Karnataka)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eastern Chalukya, Andhra</td><td><b>Bhimeswara</b> temples (Draksharamam, Samalkot)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Somnath and the Sun temples</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Somnath</b> (Prabhas Patan, near Veraval, Gujarat) is traditionally the <b>first of the 12 Jyotirlingas</b>. <b>Al-Biruni</b>, who came with Mahmud of Ghazni, <b>described it</b> in <i>Kitab-ul-Hind</i>. Mahmud sacked it in 1025–26. The rebuilt temple's <b>pran pratishtha was done by President Rajendra Prasad</b> (11 May 1951), not Radhakrishnan; Patel and K.M. Munshi drove the rebuilding.</li>
<li>The 12 Jyotirlingas: Somnath, Nageshwar (Gujarat); Mallikarjuna (Srisailam); Mahakaleshwar, Omkareshwar (Madhya Pradesh); Kedarnath; Bhimashankar, Trimbakeshwar, Grishneshwar (Maharashtra); Kashi Vishwanath; Vaidyanath (Deoghar); Rameshwaram.</li>
<li><b>Sun temples</b>: <b>Arasavalli</b> (Srikakulam, Andhra Pradesh), Konark, Modhera, Martand (Kashmir, Lalitaditya), Katarmal (Almora), Deo (Bihar), Suryanar Kovil (Tamil Nadu). <b>Amarkantak</b> (source of the Narmada) and <b>Omkareshwar</b> (a Jyotirlinga, with the Statue of Oneness of Shankaracharya, 2023) are not Sun temples.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Yogini temples and Islamic buildings</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Chausath Yogini temple</b> at Mitaoli (Morena, MP) is a <b>circular temple</b> with 64 cells, built about 1323 under the <b>Kachchhapaghata dynasty</b>. Popular belief holds it <b>inspired the old Parliament House</b>, though there is no proof. It is <b>not the only circular temple</b> (Hirapur and Ranipur-Jharial in Odisha, Bheraghat near Jabalpur). Yogini worship is tantric Shaiva-Shakta, not Vaishnava. Nearby are the Bateshwar group and Kakanmath.</li>
<li>Building materials:
<ul><li><b>Buland Darwaza</b> and the Fatehpur Sikri buildings are mainly <b>red and buff sandstone</b>, with marble only as inlay; only Salim Chishti's tomb is white marble.</li>
<li>Lucknow's <b>Bara Imambara and Rumi Darwaza</b> (Asaf-ud-Daula, 1784, a famine relief work) are of <b>lakhauri brick and lime plaster</b>, not sandstone and marble.</li>
<li>Mughal phases: Akbar's red sandstone; Itimad-ud-Daula's tomb (the first fully white-marble building, with pietra dura); Shah Jahan's white marble (Taj Mahal).</li></ul></li>
<li>Site locations often mismatched: the <b>Amaravati stupa</b> is in Andhra Pradesh; <b>Vikramshila</b> monastery is in Bihar (Bhagalpur), not UP; <b>Hemkund Sahib</b> gurudwara is in Uttarakhand, not Himachal; the <b>Udayagiri</b> caves are in Madhya Pradesh (Vidisha) and Odisha (with Khandagiri), not Maharashtra.</li>
<li>Abroad: <b>Angkor Wat</b> (Cambodia), the world's largest religious monument, was begun under the Khmer king <b>Suryavarman II</b> (12th century) as a Vishnu temple; it is on Cambodia's flag.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("culture", "c2-3", `
<p>India protects its heritage through the ASI and international listings. UPSC asks which sites were listed when, and who the early archaeologists were.</p>

<h3>Heritage bodies and archaeologists</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Archaeological Survey of India</b> (1861, Alexander Cunningham, its first Director-General and the "father of Indian archaeology") is an attached office of the <b>Ministry of Culture</b>. It protects about 3,700 monuments under the Ancient Monuments and Archaeological Sites and Remains Act 1958 (100 m prohibited and 200 m regulated zones). Curzon's Ancient Monuments Preservation Act (1904) and John Marshall (DG 1902–28) revived it.</li>
<li>Colonial <b>archaeologists</b> mainly in South India:
<ul><li><b>Alexander Rea</b>: excavated Adichanallur, Amaravati and Bhattiprolu.</li>
<li><b>A.H. Longhurst</b>: excavated Nagarjunakonda and wrote on Hampi.</li>
<li><b>Robert Sewell</b>: <i>A Forgotten Empire</i>, on Vijayanagara.</li>
<li><b>James Burgess</b>: ASI Director-General who documented Ajanta and Ellora and founded <i>Indian Antiquary</i>.</li>
<li><b>Walter Elliot</b>: excavated part of Amaravati (1845).</li></ul></li>
<li>Other pioneers: William Jones (Asiatic Society, 1784), James Prinsep (deciphered Brahmi, 1837), Daya Ram Sahni and R.D. Banerji (Harappa, Mohenjo-daro), Mortimer Wheeler, H.D. Sankalia, B.B. Lal.</li>
</ul>

<h3>World Heritage Sites</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>Inscriptions</th></tr>
<tr><td>1983–89</td><td>Ajanta, Ellora (1983); <b>Mahabalipuram</b>, <b>Konark</b> (1984); Hampi, Fatehpur Sikri (1986); Pattadakal (1987); Sanchi (1989)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1999–2008</td><td>Mountain Railways: Darjeeling (1999), Nilgiri (2005), <b>Kalka–Shimla</b> (2008); Mahabodhi, Bodh Gaya (2002); <b>Champaner-Pavagadh</b> and <b>Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus</b> (2004)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2014</td><td><b>Rani-ki-Vav</b> (Patan), Great Himalayan National Park</td></tr>
<tr><td>2016–21</td><td>Nalanda, Khangchendzonga (first mixed site), Le Corbusier's Chandigarh (2016); Ahmedabad (2017); Mumbai's Victorian Gothic and Art Deco (2018); Jaipur (2019); Ramappa temple, Dholavira (2021)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>2023</b></td><td><b>Santiniketan</b> (41st) and the <b>Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas</b> (42nd)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2024</td><td><b>Moidams</b> of Charaideo, Assam (43rd)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2025</td><td>Maratha Military Landscapes, 12 forts (44th)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2026</td><td>Ancient Buddhist Site of Sarnath (45th)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Moidams</b> are earthen <b>burial mounds of Ahom royalty and nobles</b> at Charaideo, the first Ahom capital: a vaulted chamber under a mound crowned by a pavilion. They were not forts, pleasure grounds or drill grounds. They are the first cultural WHS in the Northeast (Kaziranga and Manas, 1985, are natural sites).</li>
<li>The Ahoms (1228–1826) were founded by Sukaphaa. They used the paik system of service and kept chronicles called Buranjis. Lachit Borphukan won the Battle of Saraighat (1671) against the Mughals. Monuments: Rang Ghar, Talatal Ghar (Sivasagar).</li>
<li>India hosted the 46th World Heritage Committee in New Delhi (July 2024).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Intangible heritage and Memory of the World</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>India's Intangible Cultural Heritage elements</th></tr>
<tr><td>2008</td><td>Kutiyattam, Ramlila, Vedic chanting</td></tr>
<tr><td>2009–10</td><td>Ramman (Garhwal); Mudiyettu, Kalbelia, <b>Chhau</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>2012–14</td><td>Buddhist chanting of Ladakh; <b>Sankirtana</b> (Manipur); Thatheras of Jandiala Guru</td></tr>
<tr><td>2016–21</td><td>Yoga, Nawrouz; <b>Kumbh Mela</b> (2017); <b>Durga Puja</b> in Kolkata (2021)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>2023</b></td><td><b>Garba</b> of Gujarat, the latest of the options in the 2024 question</td></tr>
<tr><td>2025</td><td>Deepavali (at the New Delhi session)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The 2003 UNESCO convention (India ratified 2005) governs these lists; the Sangeet Natak Akademi is the nodal agency.</li>
<li>UNESCO's <b>Memory of the World Register</b> listed 30 <b>Rig-Veda</b> manuscripts of the Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, Pune, in 2007.</li>
<li>Heritage tourism: the <b>Deccan Odyssey</b> (2004) is a <b>luxury train</b> through Maharashtra that includes Goa, modelled on the Palace on Wheels.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_culture_c3.js
// Art & Culture book, Unit 3: Painting and crafts.
addBookTopic("culture", "c3-1", `
<p>Indian painting runs from cave murals through Mughal and Rajput miniatures to modern art. UPSC asks where famous works are and which school or ruler they belong to.</p>

<h3>Murals</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Site</th><th>Period</th><th>Highlights</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Ajanta</b></td><td>2nd century BCE – 6th century CE</td><td>Jatakas; <b>Bodhisattva Padmapani</b> (Avalokiteshvara holding a lotus) in Cave 1 (Vakataka, late 5th century), with Vajrapani opposite; "Dying Princess" (Cave 16)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bagh</b> (Dhar, MP)</td><td>5th–6th century</td><td>The <b>other surviving Gupta-period cave paintings</b> besides Ajanta. Cave 4's <b>Hallisaka</b> (Hallisalasya) mural shows a <b>joyous folk dance</b>: one dancer in a ring of women with drums and cymbals, plus a royal procession. Many panels are now in the Gwalior Museum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sittanavasal</td><td>9th century</td><td>Jain murals: lotus pond, dancers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Badami, Ellora (Kailasa)</td><td>6th, 8th century</td><td>Chalukya and Rashtrakuta murals</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brihadeeswara</td><td>11th century</td><td>Chola frescoes</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Lepakshi</b> (Anantapur, AP)</td><td>16th century</td><td>Vijayanagara ceiling murals in the Veerabhadra temple; hanging pillar, giant Nandi</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Ajanta technique: tempera (fresco-secco) on plaster of clay, cow dung and rice husk, coated with lime.</li>
<li><b>Sanchi</b> is famous for its stupa and carved gateways, <b>not murals</b>.</li>
<li>Other mural traditions: Kerala murals (Mattancherry palace) and Hampi's Virupaksha ceiling.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Miniatures</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Ruler</th><th>Mughal painting</th></tr>
<tr><td>Humayun</td><td>Brought Mir Sayyid Ali and Abdus Samad from Safavid Iran</td></tr>
<tr><td>Akbar</td><td>Large illustrated manuscripts: <i>Hamzanama</i>, <i>Razmnama</i>, <i>Akbarnama</i>; Daswanth, Basawan</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Jahangir</b></td><td><b>Shifted from manuscripts to albums (muraqqa) and individual portraits</b>, court scenes and natural history: Ustad Mansur (birds, animals), Abul Hasan, Bishandas; haloes, European influence. The peak of Mughal painting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shah Jahan; Aurangzeb</td><td>Formal and ornate (<i>Padshahnama</i>); patronage declined and painters moved to Rajput and Pahari courts</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
<tr><th>School</th><th>Features</th></tr>
<tr><td>Mewar</td><td>Earliest Rajasthani school; bright colours, Ragamala (Sahibdin)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bundi, Kota</td><td>Lush nature; hunting scenes</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kishangarh</b></td><td><b>"Bani Thani"</b> (about 1750) by Nihal Chand at Raja Sawant Singh's court: Radha with lotus-petal eyes, the "Mona Lisa of India"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Marwar</td><td>Bold colours, court life</td></tr>
<tr><td>Basohli; Guler; Kangra (Pahari)</td><td>Bold primary colours; Nainsukh; lyrical <i>Gita Govinda</i> (Raja Sansar Chand)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Folk and textile painting</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Kalamkari</b> ("pen-work") is <b>hand-painted or block-printed cotton textile of South India</b> (Andhra Pradesh), using natural dyes. There are two styles: Srikalahasti (freehand with a bamboo pen; epic themes, temple hangings) and Machilipatnam/Pedana (block-printed, Persian motifs, patronised by the Qutb Shahis). Both have GI tags.</li>
<li>Others: Pattachitra (Odisha), Madhubani (Bihar), Warli (Maharashtra), Phad (Rajasthan), Cheriyal (Telangana), Pichwai (Nathdwara).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Modern art</h3>
<ul>
<li>All of these are <b>artists</b>:
<ul><li><b>Amrita Sher-Gil</b> (1913–41), pioneer of modern Indian painting (<i>Three Girls</i>);</li>
<li><b>Bikash Bhattacharjee</b>, Kolkata realist (Doll series);</li>
<li><b>N.S. Bendre</b>, Baroda School;</li>
<li><b>Subodh Gupta</b>, contemporary artist working in steel utensils.</li></ul>
Others: Raja Ravi Varma, Abanindranath Tagore (Bengal School), Nandalal Bose, Jamini Roy, M.F. Husain, S.H. Raza, Tyeb Mehta.</li>
<li><b>Christie's</b> (1766), <b>Sotheby's</b> (1744) and India's <b>Osian's</b> are all <b>auction houses</b>.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("culture", "c3-2", `
<p>India's crafts and handlooms are tied to particular places, and many are now protected by Geographical Indication tags.</p>

<h3>Textiles and embroidery</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Craft</th><th>Home</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Puthukkuli shawls</b></td><td><b>Tamil Nadu</b>: Toda embroidery of the Nilgiris (GI 2013)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sujni embroidery</b></td><td><b>Bihar</b> (Muzaffarpur), not Maharashtra</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Uppada Jamdani saris</b></td><td><b>Andhra Pradesh</b> (East Godavari), not Karnataka</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phulkari; Chikankari; Kantha</td><td>Punjab; Lucknow; West Bengal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kasuti; Pipli appliqué; Kashida</td><td>Karnataka; Odisha; Kashmir</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Saree towns</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Chanderi</b> (Ashoknagar, MP): light silk-cotton with zari.</li>
<li><b>Kancheepuram</b> (Tamil Nadu): heavy mulberry silk with temple borders (GI 2005).</li>
<li><b>Bhadrachalam</b> (temple town) and <b>Karnal</b> (dairy research and basmati) are not saree centres.</li>
<li>Others: Banarasi, Paithani (Maharashtra), Pochampally ikat (Telangana), Patola (Patan, double ikat), Baluchari (Bengal), Sambalpuri (Odisha), Muga silk (Assam), Maheshwari (MP), Kota Doria (Rajasthan), Gadwal, Mysore silk, Kasavu (Kerala).</li>
<li>National Handloom Day is 7 August, the anniversary of the Swadeshi movement's launch in 1905.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Geographical Indications</h3>
<ul>
<li>Law: the Geographical Indications of Goods (Registration and Protection) Act 1999 (in force 2003), under TRIPS. The registry is in Chennai, and registration lasts 10 years, renewable. Darjeeling tea was the first GI (2004). There are over 650 GIs, with Uttar Pradesh and Tamil Nadu leading.</li>
<li><b>Banaras Brocades and Sarees</b> and the <b>Tirupati Laddu</b> got GI status in 2009. <b>Rajasthani Daal-Bati-Churma</b> has none, being a common regional dish not tied to one place.</li>
<li>Food GIs: Bikaneri Bhujia, Rasgulla (both Odisha and West Bengal), Dharwad pedha, Srivilliputtur palkova, Hyderabadi haleem, Kandhamal haldi.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_culture_c4.js
// Art & Culture book, Unit 4: Performing arts.
addBookTopic("culture", "c4-1", `
<p>Indian classical music has two systems: Hindustani in the north and Carnatic in the south. Both are built on ragas (melodic frameworks) and talas (rhythmic cycles), but they grew differently after about the 13th century.</p>

<h3>Ragas and parent scales</h3>
<ul>
<li>Hindustani ragas are grouped under <b>ten thaats</b>, devised by <b>V.N. Bhatkhande</b> (early 20th century). Carnatic ragas come from <b>72 melakartas</b> (parent scales with all seven notes), systematised by Venkatamakhin (17th century, <i>Chaturdandi Prakashika</i>) and named by the Katapayadi scheme.</li>
<li><b>Bilawal</b> uses all seven natural (shuddha) notes, like the Western major scale. Its Carnatic equivalent is <b>Dheera Shankarabharanam</b> (29th melakarta).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Thaat</th><th>Carnatic melakarta</th></tr>
<tr><td>Bilawal</td><td>Dheera Shankarabharanam (29)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kalyan (Yaman)</td><td>Mechakalyani (65)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Khamaj; Kafi</td><td>Harikambhoji (28); Kharaharapriya (22)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Asavari</td><td>Natabhairavi (20), the natural minor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bhairavi</td><td>Hanumatodi (8), all flat notes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bhairav</td><td>Mayamalavagowla (15), the first raga taught in Carnatic music</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purvi; Marwa; Todi</td><td>Kamavardhini (51); Gamanashrama (53); Shubhapantuvarali (45)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>In the Hindustani <b>time theory</b>, Bhairav belongs to dawn and <b>Todi</b> to the morning, so Todi is the morning raga among the usual options.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Hindustani music</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Dhrupad</b> is the oldest surviving form. Its roots are in older temple music, and it was developed at the court of <b>Raja Man Singh Tomar of Gwalior</b> (15th century, before the Mughals), so it did not originate in Rajput kingdoms under the Mughals. It is <b>primarily devotional and spiritual</b>, and its <b>alap uses syllables from Sanskrit mantras</b> ("om, a, na, ta, ri, num"). Four banis: Gauhar, Dagar, Khandar, Nauhar. It is accompanied by the pakhawaj, with rudra veena and surbahar. The Dagar family kept it alive; <b>Umakant and Ramakant Gundecha</b> (Bhopal) are <b>Dhrupad vocalists</b> trained in that line.</li>
<li><b>Khayal</b>, credited to Amir Khusrau and developed by Sadarang and Adarang (18th century), became the main form. <b>Amir Khusrau</b> (1253–1325, disciple of Nizamuddin Auliya) <b>originated the early qawwali</b> and tarana.</li>
<li><b>Tansen</b> (Ramtanu Pandey) got the title <b>"Tansen" from Raja Vikramjit of Gwalior</b>, not Akbar; Akbar called him "Mian" and made him one of his Navaratnas. He trained under Swami Haridas and Muhammad Ghaus; the Tansen Samaroh is held at Gwalior (a UNESCO Creative City of Music, 2023).</li>
<li><b>Ibrahim Adil Shah II</b> of Bijapur (1580–1627) wrote the <b><i>Kitab-i-Nauras</i></b>, songs in Dakhni praising Hindu deities (Saraswati, Ganesha) and Sufi saints; he was called "Jagadguru".</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Khayal gharana</th><th>Exponents and style</th></tr>
<tr><td>Gwalior (oldest)</td><td>V.D. Paluskar, Omkarnath Thakur; clear and simple</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agra</td><td>Faiyaz Khan; forceful, dhrupad influence</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kirana</b></td><td>Abdul Karim Khan; <b>Bhimsen Joshi</b>, <b>Gangubai Hangal</b> (Dharwad, a Hindustani singer), Prabha Atre; slow and note-focused</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Jaipur-Atrauli</b></td><td>Founded by Alladiya Khan; Kesarbai Kerkar, <b>Mallikarjun Mansur</b> (Dharwad), Kishori Amonkar; rare and complex ragas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Patiala</td><td>Bade Ghulam Ali Khan; fast taans, thumri</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mewati; Rampur-Sahaswan</td><td>Pandit Jasraj; Rashid Khan</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Dharwad–Hubli in north Karnataka is a cradle of Hindustani music.</li>
<li>Reformers:
<ul><li><b>V.D. Paluskar</b> took music from courts to the public and founded the first <b>Gandharva Mahavidyalaya at Lahore</b> (5 May 1901; moved to Bombay in 1908). He set "Raghupati Raghava" to tune.</li>
<li><b>V.N. Bhatkhande</b> devised the ten thaats, organised All-India Music Conferences (Baroda, 1916) and founded Marris College, Lucknow (1926; now Bhatkhande Sanskriti Vishwavidyalaya).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Ravi Shankar</b> (sitar, Maihar gharana, disciple of Allauddin Khan) wrote the autobiography <b><i>My Music, My Life</i></b> (1968); Bharat Ratna 1999. <b>Ronu Majumdar</b> plays the <b>flute</b> (bansuri). <b>Nikhil Banerjee</b> played the sitar and <b>Meeta Pandit</b> is a Hindustani vocalist (Gwalior gharana). <b>Bhajan Sopori</b> played the <b>santoor</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Carnatic music</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Purandara Dasa</b> (1484–1564) is the "father" (pitamaha) of Carnatic music; he systematised teaching with Mayamalavagowla.</li>
<li><b>Annamacharya</b> (1408–1503) composed some 32,000 sankirtanas <b>in praise of Lord Venkateswara</b> of Tirumala (about 13,000 survive on copper plates).</li>
<li>The <b>Trinity</b> (18th–19th century, all born at Thiruvarur):
<ul><li><b>Tyagaraja</b> (1767–1847) composed mostly in praise of <b>Rama</b>, not Krishna, in Telugu. He <b>created or revived several ragas</b>, and the Thyagaraja Aradhana is held at Thiruvaiyaru.</li>
<li>Muthuswami Dikshitar composed in Sanskrit, including the Navagraha kritis.</li>
<li>Syama Sastri was a devotee of Kamakshi.</li></ul>
Annamacharya and Tyagaraja were <b>not contemporaries</b>; three centuries separate them.</li>
<li>Artists:
<ul><li><b>M. Balamuralikrishna</b>: Carnatic vocal.</li>
<li><b>A. Kanyakumari</b>: Carnatic violin (UPSC's key matched her with ghatam; debated).</li>
<li><b>T.V. Gopalakrishnan</b>: mridangam (and vocals).</li></ul></li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("culture", "c4-2", `
<p>India's dance and theatre run from classical forms codified by the <i>Natyashastra</i> to regional folk forms, ritual theatre and martial arts.</p>

<h3>Classical dances</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Dance</th><th>State and features</th></tr>
<tr><td>Bharatanatyam</td><td>Tamil Nadu; from the Sadir/devadasi tradition; revived by Rukmini Devi Arundale (Kalakshetra, 1936). Dancers: <b>Priyadarsini Govind</b>, <b>Lakshmi Viswanathan</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kuchipudi</b></td><td>Andhra Pradesh; grew from dance-drama, so dancers <b>occasionally speak dialogue</b>. <b>Dancing on the rim of a brass plate</b> (Tarangam) is a Kuchipudi feature, not Bharatanatyam. Siddhendra Yogi; Vempati Chinna Satyam; Raja and Radha Reddy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kathak</td><td>North India; <b>Birju Maharaj</b> (Lucknow gharana)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kathakali</td><td>Kerala; <b>Kottakkal Sivaraman</b> (famous for female roles)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mohiniattam</td><td><b>Kerala</b>, not Odisha; <b>Kalamandalam Kshemavathy</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Odissi</td><td>Odisha; basic stances chowk and tribhanga</td></tr>
<tr><td>Manipuri</td><td>Manipur; <b>N. Madhabi Devi</b>, <b>Bimbavati Devi</b> (daughter of Guru Bipin Singh)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sattriya</b></td><td>Assam; created by <b>Srimanta Sankardeva</b> (15th–16th century) and kept alive in the sattras (Majuli). It is <b>a combination of music, dance and drama</b> (Ankiya Naat) and <b>a living Vaishnava tradition</b> of over 500 years. It uses <b>Borgeets</b> by Sankardeva and Madhavdeva, not songs of Tulsidas, Kabir or Mirabai. Recognised as classical in 2000</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The Sangeet Natak Akademi recognises eight classical dances; the Ministry of Culture also lists Chhau (nine).</li>
<li><b>Tribhanga</b> ("three bends") is an S-shaped pose: <b>one leg bent, and the body curved in opposite directions at the waist and neck</b>. It is central to Odissi and common in sculpture (Krishna with the flute, the Didarganj Yakshi).</li>
<li>The <i>Natyashastra</i> (Bharata Muni) defines rasa, bhava and four kinds of abhinaya: angika (body), vachika (speech), aharya (costume) and sattvika (emotion).</li>
<li><b>Chandralekha</b> (1928–2006), who reinterpreted Bharatanatyam with yoga and kalaripayattu, received the Sangeet Natak Akademi Ratna (fellowship, announced 2005). Other great dancers: T. Balasaraswati (Bharatanatyam), Yamini Krishnamurthy (Bharatanatyam and Kuchipudi), Uma Sharma (Kathak).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Folk, ritual and devotional forms</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Garba</b>: Gujarat (UNESCO ICH 2023). <b>Yakshagana</b>: coastal Karnataka dance-drama.</li>
<li>Other folk dances: Ghoomar and Kalbelia (Rajasthan), Bhangra and Giddha (Punjab), Lavani (Maharashtra), Bihu (Assam), Gotipua and Chhau (Odisha), Theyyam (Kerala), Dollu Kunitha (Karnataka), Karagattam (Tamil Nadu).</li>
<li><b>Manipuri Sankirtana</b> (UNESCO 2013) is a <b>song and dance</b> performance <b>narrating the life and deeds of Krishna</b>. Cymbals (kartal) are <b>not the only instrument</b>: the pung drum is central.</li>
<li><b>Chakyar Koothu</b> (Kerala) is performed by the <b>Chakyar</b> community in the <b>koothambalam</b> (temple theatre), with the copper drum <b>mizhavu</b> played by Nambiars. It was for higher-caste temple audiences, so "higher castes could not watch it" is wrong. Kutiyattam is a related Sanskrit theatre (UNESCO 2008).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Theatre institutions</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>National School of Drama</b> was set up by the <b>Sangeet Natak Akademi in 1959</b> and became autonomous in 1975. <b>Ebrahim Alkazi</b> (director 1962–77) was eminent in <b>theatre training</b>; he staged <i>Tughlaq</i> and <i>Andha Yug</i>.</li>
<li>The <b>Sahitya Akademi</b>'s highest honour is <b>election as its Fellow</b>, above the annual award.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Martial arts and regional traditions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Kalaripayattu</b> is an <b>ancient martial art and living tradition of South India</b> (Kerala, parts of Tamil Nadu), practised in a kalari, with weapons (including the flexible urumi sword) and marma healing.</li>
<li>Others: Silambam (Tamil Nadu), <b>Gatka</b> (Sikh stick-fighting of <b>Punjab</b>, not Kerala), Thang-Ta (Manipur), Mardani Khel (Maharashtra), Mallakhamb. Several joined the Khelo India Youth Games (2021).</li>
<li><b>Madhubani</b> painting belongs to <b>Bihar</b>. The <b>Sindhu Darshan</b> festival (Singhey Khababs is the Ladakhi name of the Indus) is held at Shey near Leh, which was in Jammu and Kashmir in 2009 and is now in Ladakh.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_culture_c5.js
// Art & Culture book, Unit 5: Language and literature.
addBookTopic("culture", "c5-1", `
<p>India's languages belong to four main families, and the state recognises some through the Eighth Schedule and others as "classical". This topic also covers calendars and number systems.</p>

<h3>Languages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Language families: Indo-Aryan, Dravidian, Austroasiatic (Munda, Khasi) and Tibeto-Burman. The Eighth Schedule lists 22 languages. Article 350A asks for primary instruction in the mother tongue; Article 350B provides a Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities. NEP 2020 prefers the mother tongue up to at least Class 5.</li>
<li><b>Halbi, Ho and Kui</b> are <b>tribal languages</b>:
<ul><li><b>Halbi</b> (Indo-Aryan) is the link language of Bastar.</li>
<li><b>Ho</b> (Munda) is spoken in Kolhan, Jharkhand, and written in the Warang Citi script.</li>
<li><b>Kui</b> (Dravidian) is spoken by the Kandha (Khond) of Kandhamal, Odisha.</li></ul>
<b>Santali</b> is the only tribal language in the Eighth Schedule (92nd Amendment, 2003, which also added Bodo, Dogri and Maithili); its Ol Chiki script (Raghunath Murmu) marked its centenary in 2025. Others: Gondi, Bhili, Kurukh, Mundari, Khasi.</li>
<li><b>Bengali</b> is the Indian language with the most speakers worldwide after Hindi (second in India; the language of most Bangladeshis). It has <b>more speakers than Portuguese</b>, French or Japanese.</li>
<li>World languages by speakers: <b>Spanish, then Arabic, then French</b>.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Classical languages</th><th>Year</th></tr>
<tr><td>Tamil (first)</td><td>2004</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sanskrit</td><td>2005</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Telugu, Kannada</b></td><td>2008</td></tr>
<tr><td>Malayalam</td><td>2013</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Odia</b> (the first Indo-Aryan language after Sanskrit)</td><td>2014</td></tr>
<tr><td>Marathi, Pali, Prakrit, Assamese, Bengali</td><td>October 2024 (11 in all)</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Gujarati</b> is a scheduled language but not classical. The Ministry of Culture decides on the advice of a Linguistic Experts Committee; the 2024 criteria require early texts 1,500–2,000 years old, a heritage literature and knowledge texts. Benefits include awards, centres of excellence and university chairs.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>International Mother Language Day</b> (21 February) was proclaimed by <b>UNESCO</b>, not UNICEF, in 1999 at Bangladesh's initiative. It recalls the Language Movement: on 25 February 1948 Dhirendranath Datta asked in <b>Pakistan's Constituent Assembly</b> for Bengali alongside Urdu, and on 21 February 1952 police fired on students in Dhaka (Shaheed Minar). 2022–32 is the UN Decade of Indigenous Languages.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Calendars and numerals</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>National Calendar</b> (Saka era, adopted from 22 March 1957 on the Calendar Reform Committee under Meghnad Saha) begins on <b>Chaitra 1 = 22 March</b> (21 March in a leap year). The Saka era begins in 78 CE and Vikram Samvat in 57 BCE.</li>
<li>The <b>Islamic (Hijri) calendar</b> is lunar, about <b>11 days</b> (not 12) shorter than the solar year, so festivals move earlier each year. It began with the <b>Hijra in AD 622</b>; AD 632 is the year of the Prophet's death. The <b>Gregorian</b> calendar (Pope Gregory XIII, 1582) is <b>solar</b>.</li>
<li>Roman numerals: I = 1, V = 5, X = 10, L = 50, C = 100, D = 500, M = 1000. So M > D > C > L.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("culture", "c5-2", `
<p>Classical Sanskrit literature includes drama, poetry, treatises and historical biographies. UPSC usually asks who wrote what and what a work is about.</p>

<h3>Sanskrit drama</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Playwright</th><th>Works</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bhasa</b> (before Kalidasa)</td><td>13 plays rediscovered by T. Ganapati Sastri in Kerala (1912): <i>Svapnavasavadattam</i>, <i>Pratijnayaugandharayanam</i>, <i>Urubhangam</i> (a rare tragedy), <i>Karnabharam</i>, <i>Charudattam</i>, <b><i>Madhyama-vyayoga</i></b> (Bhima and his son Ghatotkacha)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kalidasa</td><td><i>Abhijnanashakuntalam</i>, <i>Vikramorvashiyam</i>, <b><i>Malavikagnimitram</i></b>, the <b>love story of Agnimitra</b>, son of <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b> (founder of the Shunga dynasty), and the maid Malavika; poems <i>Meghaduta</i>, <i>Raghuvamsha</i>, <i>Kumarasambhava</i></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Shudraka</b></td><td><b><i>Mrichchhakatika</i></b> ("The Little Clay Cart"): the <b>love of Charudatta</b>, a generous but impoverished merchant-Brahmin of Ujjayini, for the <b>courtesan Vasantasena</b>; unusual for ordinary city life and a revolt</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Vishakhadatta</b></td><td><b><i>Mudrarakshasa</i></b>: <b>court intrigue in Chandragupta Maurya's time</b>, as Chanakya wins over Rakshasa, minister of the Nandas; <b><i>Devichandraguptam</i></b> (Chandragupta II, Ramagupta and Dhruvadevi)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harsha</td><td><i>Ratnavali</i>, <i>Priyadarshika</i>, <i>Nagananda</i></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bhavabhuti</b> (8th century, Kanauj)</td><td><i>Uttararamacharita</i>, <i>Mahaviracharita</i>, <i>Malatimadhava</i></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Hastimalla</b> (13th century, Jain, Karnataka)</td><td><i>Vikrantakaurava</i>, <i>Maithili Kalyana</i></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kshemeshvara</b> (10th century, Kanauj)</td><td><i>Chandakaushika</i> (Harishchandra)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Others</td><td>Bhatta Narayana (<i>Venisamhara</i>), Rajashekhara (<i>Karpuramanjari</i>), Mahendravarman I (<i>Mattavilasa Prahasana</i>)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So Bhavabhuti, Hastimalla and Kshemeshvara were all <b>playwrights</b>. In Sanskrit drama the jester is the vidushaka, and women and lower characters speak Prakrit. Kutiyattam keeps Sanskrit theatre alive in Kerala.</p>
<ul>
<li>Treatises: <i>Natyashastra</i> (Bharata Muni: drama, dance, music, rasa), <i>Kavyalankara</i> (Bhamaha: poetics), <i>Mahabhashya</i> (Patanjali: commentary on Panini's grammar).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Historical works and chronicles</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Work</th><th>Author</th><th>Subject</th></tr>
<tr><td><i>Harshacharita</i></td><td>Banabhatta</td><td>Harshavardhana</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>Vikramankadevacharita</i></td><td><b>Bilhana</b></td><td>Chalukya Vikramaditya VI</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>Rajatarangini</i></td><td>Kalhana (1148)</td><td>Kashmir; the first true history in Sanskrit</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>Ramacharita</i></td><td>Sandhyakaranandi</td><td>Pala king Ramapala</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>Prithvirajavijaya</i>; <i>Prithviraj Raso</i></td><td>Jayanaka; Chand Bardai</td><td>Prithviraj Chauhan</td></tr>
<tr><td><b><i>Hammira Mahakavya</i></b></td><td><b>Nayachandra Suri</b></td><td>Hammira Chauhan of Ranthambore</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>Gaudavaho</i></td><td>Vakpati</td><td>Yashovarman of Kanauj (Prakrit)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b><i>Nitivakyamrita</i></b></td><td><b>Somadeva Suri</b> (10th century, Jain)</td><td>Polity; he also wrote <i>Yashastilaka</i>. Distinct from Somadeva of the <i>Kathasaritsagara</i></td></tr>
<tr><td><b><i>Milinda Panha</i></b></td><td>(Dialogue)</td><td>King Menander and the monk <b>Nagasena</b>. It is not by Nagarjuna, the Madhyamaka philosopher</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Magadhas</b> and Sutas were bards whose profession was <b>memorising chronicles, dynastic histories and epic tales</b> and praising kings; the Sutas are credited with transmitting the <i>Mahabharata</i> and Puranas. Other terms: sreshthi (head of a merchant guild), kshatrapa (Shaka governor), bhandagarika (treasurer).</li>
<li><b>Charles Wilkins</b> made the first English translation of the <i>Bhagavad Gita</i> (1785), with a preface by Warren Hastings.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Modern Indian plays</h3>
<ul>
<li><i>Yayati</i> (1961) was Girish Karnad's first play. <i>Gidhade</i> is by Vijay Tendulkar (who also wrote <i>Ghashiram Kotwal</i>). <b>Jabbar Patel</b> is a director (<i>Ghashiram Kotwal</i> on stage; films <i>Samna</i>, <i>Simhasan</i>, <i>Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar</i>), so "<i>Giddha</i>: Jabbar Patel" is wrong; Giddha is a Punjabi folk dance.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("culture", "c5-3", `
<p>Questions on modern literature match authors with their works and test knowledge of major prizes, both Indian and international.</p>

<h3>Indian literature in Indian languages</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Author</th><th>Works</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay</b></td><td><b><i>Debi Chaudhurani</i></b> (1884), <i>Anandamath</i> (1882), <i>Kapalkundala</i></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dinabandhu Mitra</b></td><td><b><i>Nil Darpan</i></b> (1860), on the oppression of indigo cultivators</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Premchand</b></td><td><b><i>Shatranj Ke Khilari</i></b> (filmed by Satyajit Ray, 1977), <i>Godan</i>, <i>Kafan</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Devaki Nandan Khatri</td><td><i>Chandrakanta</i> (1888)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Faiz Ahmed Faiz</b></td><td><b>"Subh-e-Azadi"</b> (1947), on the "stained dawn" of a freedom that came with Partition</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Rahi Masoom Raza</b></td><td><i>Aadha Gaon</i> (Hindi; also the TV <i>Mahabharat</i> dialogues)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Ramakanta Rath</b></td><td><i>Sri Radha</i> (Odia poems)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Laxman Gaikwad</b></td><td><i>Uchalya</i> (<i>The Pilferer</i>, Marathi autobiography)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suryakant Tripathi "Nirala"</td><td>Chhayavad Hindi poet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vinda Karandikar</td><td>Marathi poet; Jnanpith 2003</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dalip Kaur Tiwana</b></td><td>Punjabi novelist (a <b>litterateur</b>); Sahitya Akademi, Saraswati Samman</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Indian writing in English</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Author</th><th>Works</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Raja Rao</b></td><td>Wrote in <b>English</b>, not Telugu: <i>Kanthapura</i>, <i>The Serpent and the Rope</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Nirad C. Chaudhuri</td><td><i>The Autobiography of an Unknown Indian</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Mulk Raj Anand</td><td><i>Confessions of a Lover</i>, <i>Untouchable</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>R.K. Narayan</td><td><i>The English Teacher</i>, <i>The Guide</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>V.S. Naipaul</td><td><i>India: A Wounded Civilization</i>, <i>In a Free State</i> (Booker 1971); Nobel 2001</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anita Desai</td><td><i>In Custody</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Kiran Desai</td><td><b><i>The Inheritance of Loss</i></b> (Booker 2006; set in Kalimpong and New York)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Amitav Ghosh</td><td><i>Sea of Poppies</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Jhumpa Lahiri</td><td><i>Unaccustomed Earth</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Amartya Sen</td><td><i>The Argumentative Indian</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Bimal Jalan</td><td><i>The Future of India</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Arundhati Roy</td><td><i>An Ordinary Person's Guide to Empire</i></td></tr>
<tr><td>Mani Shankar Aiyar</td><td><i>Confessions of a Secular Fundamentalist</i></td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Booker Prize and world literature</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bookers with Indian themes:
<ul><li>V.S. Naipaul, <i>In a Free State</i> (1971)</li>
<li>J.G. Farrell, <i>The Siege of Krishnapur</i> (1973, set in 1857)</li>
<li>Paul Scott, <i>Staying On</i> (1977)</li>
<li>Salman Rushdie, <i>Midnight's Children</i> (1981; "Booker of Bookers")</li>
<li>Arundhati Roy (1997), Kiran Desai (2006), Aravind Adiga (2008)</li></ul></li>
<li>Until 2014 the Booker was open only to <b>Commonwealth and Irish citizens</b>; now any English-language novel published in the UK or Ireland qualifies. Its sponsor in the 2000s was the <b>Man Group, an investment firm</b>, not a software company. The <b>2004 winner</b> was Alan Hollinghurst (UK), not a South Asian. <b>J.M. Coetzee</b> won twice (1983, 1999; Nobel 2003), as did Peter Carey and, later, Hilary Mantel. Banu Mushtaq's <i>Heart Lamp</i> won the International Booker in 2025.</li>
<li>Nobel laureates in literature: <b>Gabriel García Márquez</b> (Colombia, 1982; magical realism), <b>Wole Soyinka</b> (Nigeria, 1986; the first African), Pearl S. Buck (1938), Tagore (1913).</li>
<li>Books by world leaders: <i>The Struggle is My Life</i> (Nelson Mandela), <i>The Struggle and the Triumph</i> (Lech Wałęsa), <i>Rebirth</i> (Leonid Brezhnev); UPSC's key gave <i>Friends and Foes</i> to Sheikh Mujibur Rahman.</li>
</ul>
`);
