// Art & Culture book, Unit 6: Society and personalities.
addBookTopic("culture", "c6-1", `
<p>India's festivals and communities are tied to particular regions. UPSC asks which festival, tribe or tradition belongs where.</p>

<h3>Northeast</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>State</th><th>Festivals and arts</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mizoram</b></td><td><b>Chapchar Kut</b> (spring festival in March after clearing jhum land; Cheraw bamboo dance), Mim Kut, Pawl Kut</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Manipur</b></td><td><b>Khongjom Parba</b> (a ballad sung with the dholak about the Battle of Khongjom, 1891, and Paona Brajabashi; Khongjom Day 23 April), <b>Thang-Ta</b> (martial art; not Sikkim), Lai Haraoba, Sangai festival, Pung cholom</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assam</td><td>Bihu, Sattriya, Ambubachi Mela</td></tr>
<tr><td>Meghalaya</td><td>Nongkrem (Khasi), Wangala (Garo, "100 drums"), Behdienkhlam (Jaintia)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nagaland</td><td>Hornbill festival (December), Moatsu, Sekrenyi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Arunachal Pradesh</td><td>Losar, Solung, Dree</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sikkim</td><td>Losoong, Saga Dawa, Pang Lhabsol</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tripura</td><td>Kharchi Puja, Hojagiri dance</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Traditions and communities</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Chaliha Sahib</b> is a 40-day festival of <b>Sindhi</b> Hindus for Jhulelal.</li>
<li>The <b>Nanda Devi Raj Jaat Yatra</b> is a pilgrimage held about every 12 years in Garhwal and Kumaon, Uttarakhand; it is not a Gond tradition.</li>
<li>The <b>Wari</b> is the annual walk of <b>Warkaris</b> to Pandharpur, Maharashtra, carrying the palkhis of Dnyaneshwar and Tukaram; it is not a Santhal tradition.</li>
<li>Tribal festivals: Sohrai, Baha and Karam (Santhal); Keslapur Jathra and Madai (Gond); Sarhul and Karma (Oraon, Munda); Sammakka Saralamma (Medaram) Jatara (Koya, Telangana, the largest tribal fair); Bhagoria (Bhil).</li>
<li>The <b>Gond and Korku</b> tribes of central India (Betul, Harda) hold <b>Hari Jiroti</b>, a month-long monsoon festival in which they <b>plant saplings of fruit trees</b>: mango, jamun, bael, jackfruit, mahua.</li>
<li>The <b>Manganiyars</b> are hereditary Muslim folk musicians of the Thar (Jaisalmer, Barmer), famous for their <b>musical tradition in north-west India</b>, playing the kamaicha, khartal and dholak for Rajput patrons. Similar groups: the Langas (sarangi), the Kalbelias (snake charmers; dance on the UNESCO list) and the Bhopas (Phad scroll singers).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tribes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Changpa</b> are semi-nomadic pastoralists of the Changthang plateau in <b>Ladakh</b>, not Uttarakhand. They <b>rear Pashmina goats</b> and are a <b>Scheduled Tribe</b>. Kashmir Pashmina has a GI tag; shahtoosh (from the Tibetan antelope) is banned. Other pastoralists: Gaddis (HP), Bakarwals (J&K), Bhotiyas (Uttarakhand), Raikas (Rajasthan).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Tribe</th><th>State</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Limbu</b></td><td><b>Sikkim</b> (and Darjeeling, eastern Nepal)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Karbi</b></td><td><b>Assam</b> (Karbi Anglong, a Sixth Schedule council), not Himachal</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dongria Kondh</b></td><td><b>Odisha</b> (Niyamgiri hills; their gram sabhas rejected bauxite mining in 2013)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bonda</b></td><td><b>Odisha</b> (Malkangiri), not Tamil Nadu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Toda, Kota, Irula, Kurumba</td><td>Tamil Nadu (Nilgiris)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gaddi, Kinnaura, Lahaula</td><td>Himachal Pradesh</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Odisha has the most PVTGs (13 of 75); PM JANMAN (2023) targets their development. The Gonds are India's largest tribal group.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("culture", "c6-2", `
<p>This topic gathers questions on artists, film, national awards and institutions.</p>

<h3>Artists and their fields</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Person</th><th>Known as</th></tr>
<tr><td>Amrita Sher-Gil</td><td>Painter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bhimsen Joshi</td><td>Hindustani singer (Kirana; Bharat Ratna 2008)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rukmini Devi Arundale</td><td>Bharatanatyam dancer; founded Kalakshetra (1936)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suryakant Tripathi "Nirala"</td><td>Hindi poet</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bhanu Bharti</b></td><td>Theatre director</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mike Pandey</b></td><td>Wildlife film maker (Green Oscars)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mohammed Zahur "Khayyam"</b></td><td>Film music composer (<i>Umrao Jaan</i>)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Vinda Karandikar</b></td><td>Marathi poet</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Hiren Bhattacharya</b></td><td><b>Puppetry</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Malini Rajurkar</b></td><td>Hindustani vocal (Gwalior gharana)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Pratibha Prahlad</b></td><td>Bharatanatyam</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Vempati Chinna Satyam</b></td><td>Kuchipudi</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Raghu Rai</b></td><td><b>Photography</b> (Magnum; Bhopal gas tragedy, 1971 refugees)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Santosh Yadav</b></td><td>Mountaineer: first woman to climb Everest twice</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>P. Sainath</b></td><td>Rural journalist (PARI; Magsaysay 2007)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Oprah Winfrey; Oscar Wilde</td><td>TV host; dramatist and author</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Cinema and awards</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Nargis</b> was the first actress to get the <b>Padma Shri</b> (1958), after <i>Mother India</i> (1957), India's first Oscar nomination for foreign language film.</li>
<li>No actress has received the <b>Bharat Ratna</b>, so "Meena Kumari" is wrong. Film-related recipients: M.G. Ramachandran (1988), Satyajit Ray (1992), Lata Mangeshkar (2001), Bhupen Hazarika (2019). <b>Raj Kapoor</b> did not get it, though he got the Padma Bhushan and the Dadasaheb Phalke Award (1987).</li>
<li>The <b>Dadasaheb Phalke Award</b> went to Raj Kapoor (1987), Dilip Kumar (1994), Dev Anand (2002) and <b>Yash Chopra</b> (for 2001). In 2004 Yash Chopra was not CBFC chairman and Hema Malini did not head the Children's Film Society.</li>
<li>Film firsts: <i>Jhansi Ki Rani</i> (1953, Sohrab Modi) was India's first Technicolor film; <i>My Dear Kuttichathan</i> (1984, Malayalam) was the first 3D film; <i>Taal</i> (1999) was the first insured film.</li>
<li><b>Paheli</b> (2005), directed by <b>Amol Palekar</b> and based on a Vijaydan Detha folk tale, was India's Oscar entry for 2006.</li>
<li><b>Pearl S. Buck</b> (Nobel 1938, <i>The Good Earth</i>) wrote the screenplay of the English version of <b><i>Guide</i></b> (1965), based on R.K. Narayan's novel; Vijay Anand made the Hindi classic with Dev Anand.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Institutions and their locations</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Institute</th><th>Location</th></tr>
<tr><td>Central Institute of Higher Tibetan Studies (now a central university)</td><td>Sarnath, Varanasi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indira Gandhi Institute of Development Research (RBI)</td><td>Mumbai</td></tr>
<tr><td>NIMHANS</td><td>Bengaluru</td></tr>
<tr><td>Central Institute of English and Foreign Languages (now EFL University)</td><td>Hyderabad</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Cultural bodies: Sangeet Natak Akademi (1953; music, dance, drama), Sahitya Akademi (1954; letters), Lalit Kala Akademi (1954; visual arts), National School of Drama (1959). All come under the Ministry of Culture.</p>
`);
;
