// Ancient History book, Units 1–3: Harappans, the Vedic age, Buddhism and Jainism.
addBookTopic("ancient", "a1-1", `
<p>The Harappan (Indus) civilisation (about 2600–1900 BCE in its mature phase) was India's first urban culture: planned cities, standard weights, crafts and long-distance trade, with a script still undeciphered.</p>

<h3>Cities, crafts and society</h3>
<ul>
<li>Town planning: a grid of streets, a raised citadel (west) and a lower town (east), covered drains, and standard bricks in the ratio 1:2:4 (baked at Mohenjo-daro and Harappa, mud-brick at Kalibangan). Houses opened onto side lanes; Mohenjo-daro had about 700 wells.</li>
<li>Inferences from the finds (Irfan Habib):
<ul><li>Many <b>spindle-whorls but no spinning wheel</b> (which came only around the 13th century) show that <b>spinning was laborious work done at home</b>.</li>
<li><b>Graduated scales and standard weights</b> (binary, then decimal: 1, 2, 4, 8 … 160, 320) show their <b>scientific knowledge</b>; Lothal has an ivory scale.</li>
<li>Large baked-brick houses with courtyards, wells and baths beside one-room tenements point to <b>private property and class difference</b>, not common property.</li></ul></li>
<li>The Harappans were among the first to grow and <b>weave cotton</b> (a fragment from Mohenjo-daro; the Greeks called cotton "sindon").</li>
<li>Crafts: beads (Chanhudaro, Lothal; carnelian from Gujarat reached Mesopotamia), shell work, metallurgy, seal-cutting, faience. They knew copper, bronze, gold, silver, lead and tin, but <b>not iron</b>. Copper came from Khetri and Baluchistan, tin from Afghanistan.</li>
<li>The script has about 400 signs, mostly written right to left.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Art and religion</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>"Dancing Girl"</b> of Mohenjo-daro (about 10.5 cm; found by Ernest Mackay, 1926) is <b>bronze</b>, cast by the lost-wax method; she is in the National Museum, New Delhi. Other works: the steatite "Priest-King", a red sandstone torso (Harappa), terracotta mother goddesses and toy carts, and gold jewellery.</li>
<li><b>Seals</b> (mostly square steatite) show the unicorn (most common), humped bull, elephant, rhinoceros, tiger, buffalo, gharial and goat, but <b>not the cow</b>; the horse is absent or doubtful. The "Pashupati" seal shows a horned figure among animals.</li>
<li>Religion: a <b>mother goddess</b> and a <b>male deity</b> ("proto-Shiva"), tree and animal worship, fire altars at Kalibangan and Lothal. No great <b>palaces or temples</b> have been found, and there were <b>no horse-drawn chariots</b>. UPSC accepted that it was a <b>predominantly secular</b> civilisation in which religion did not dominate.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Sites</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Site</th><th>Known for</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Lothal</b> (Gujarat)</td><td><b>Dockyard</b>, bead-making, rice husk; National Maritime Heritage Complex</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kalibangan</b> (Rajasthan, on the Ghaggar)</td><td><b>Ploughed field</b>, fire altars, earthquake evidence</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dholavira</b> (Khadir Bet, Kutch)</td><td><b>Water harvesting</b>: check dams on seasonal streams and connected rock-cut reservoirs; three-part town; a <b>signboard of ten large signs</b>; WHS 2021 (India's 40th, the first Harappan one)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Banawali</b> (Haryana)</td><td><b>Terracotta plough model</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Rakhigarhi (Haryana)</td><td>Largest site in India; DNA study (2019)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mohenjo-daro; Harappa</td><td>Great Bath, granary; granaries, Cemetery R-37</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Chanhudaro</b>; <b>Kot Diji</b> (Sindh)</td><td>Bead factory, no citadel; pre-Harappan fortified settlement</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Desalpur</b>, Surkotada, Rangpur (Gujarat)</td><td>Fortified sites; disputed horse bones at Surkotada</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ropar; Alamgirpur; Sanauli</td><td>Dog buried with man; easternmost site; chariot burials</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shortughai; Sutkagendor</td><td>Northernmost (Afghanistan); westernmost (Makran coast)</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Sohgaura</b> (Gorakhpur) is <b>not Harappan</b>: it has a Mauryan copper-plate inscription on famine relief and storehouses.</p>

<h3>Rivers then and now</h3>
<ul>
<li>The same species of <b>river dolphin</b> lives in both the Indus and Ganga systems. Since freshwater dolphins cannot cross sea or land, this suggests that in the <b>Pleistocene</b> the two systems were linked: the Yamuna once flowed to the Indus, or the Sutlej to the Yamuna. Neither the Rigveda's <b>Nadi Sukta</b> (a later list of rivers) nor <b>Robert Bruce Foote</b> (father of Indian prehistory, who found the first palaeolithic handaxe at Pallavaram, 1863, and worked in the south) is the basis for this.</li>
<li>The Ghaggar-Hakra, often identified with the Vedic Sarasvati, lost its glacial feeders when the Sutlej and Yamuna shifted; many Harappan sites line it (Kalibangan, Banawali, Rakhigarhi). The Ganges dolphin is the National Aquatic Animal (2009), and Project Dolphin began in 2020.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ancient", "a2-1", `
<p>The Vedic age (about 1500–600 BCE) is known from the four Vedas and their later texts. It falls into the pastoral Early (Rigvedic) period and the agricultural Later Vedic period.</p>

<h3>Rigvedic people and the Harappans compared</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Harappan</th><th>Rigvedic</th></tr>
<tr><td>Urban, planned cities, trade with Mesopotamia</td><td>Rural and pastoral; barter with the cow as a unit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Copper, bronze, gold, silver; <b>no iron</b></td><td><i>Ayas</i> (copper or bronze); iron ("krishna ayas") only in the Later Vedic period</td></tr>
<tr><td>No sign of armour</td><td><b>Coat of mail</b> (varman) and <b>helmet</b> (shipra)</td></tr>
<tr><td>No clear evidence of the horse (disputed bones at Surkotada)</td><td><b>Horse</b> central: chariots, Ashvamedha</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mother goddess, Pashupati</td><td>Indra, Agni, Varuna; cattle as wealth</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So "Indus people knew copper and iron" is wrong. UPSC accepted the horse contrast, though it is debated.</p>

<h3>Economy and rivers</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cattle were wealth: <i>gavishti</i> (search for cows) meant war and <i>gopati</i> meant chief. Agriculture existed (plough, furrow, barley/<i>yava</i>), but rice and iron are absent.</li>
<li>Irrigation from <b>wells</b> let farming spread from river margins into the Punjab–Haryana plains, and <b>draught animals</b> drew up the water. The evidence is the Rigveda's mention of the <b>ashma-chakra</b> (stone pulley wheel) and <b>ahava</b> (strapped pails), and the <b>use of oxen</b> for ploughs and carts even before the Rigveda. Mentions of axes and sickles do not show irrigation. Other terms: <i>kulya</i> (channel), <i>avata</i> (well), <i>urvara</i> (fertile field).</li>
<li>Polity: rajan, sabha, samiti, vidatha; <i>bali</i> was voluntary tribute. In the Battle of Ten Kings on the Parushni (Ravi), Sudas of the Bharatas won.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Vedic name</th><th>Modern river</th><th>Greek name</th></tr>
<tr><td>Sindhu</td><td>Indus</td><td>Indos</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Vitasta</b></td><td><b>Jhelum</b></td><td>Hydaspes (Alexander against Porus)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Asikni</b></td><td><b>Chenab</b></td><td>Akesines</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Parushni</b></td><td><b>Ravi</b></td><td>Hydraotes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vipas</td><td><b>Beas</b></td><td>Hyphasis (where Alexander's army stopped)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shutudri</td><td>Sutlej</td><td>Zaradros</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Yavyavati</b></td><td>Usually the <b>Zhob</b> (not the Beas)</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Kubha, Krumu, Gomati, Suvastu</td><td>Kabul, Kurram, Gomal, Swat</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Sadanira</td><td>Gandak</td><td></td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Religion and ideas</h3>
<ul>
<li>Early Vedic religion was mainly <b>worship of nature and yajnas</b> (sacrifices): Indra (rain, war; the most hymns), Agni (fire), Varuna (guardian of cosmic order), Surya, Ushas, Soma. In the Later Vedic period Prajapati, Vishnu and Rudra rose, and rituals such as the Rajasuya and Ashvamedha became elaborate.</li>
<li><b>Rita</b> is the <b>fundamental moral and cosmic law</b> of the universe; <b>dharma</b> is the idea of <b>duties and obligations</b> to oneself and others.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Texts</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Veda</th><th>Content</th><th>Priest</th></tr>
<tr><td>Rigveda</td><td>10 mandalas, 1,028 hymns; Purusha Sukta (first mention of the four varnas), Nasadiya Sukta, Nadi Sukta, Gayatri mantra; <i>kshetrapati</i> ("lord of the field")</td><td>Hotri</td></tr>
<tr><td>Samaveda</td><td>Rigvedic verses set to melody; origin of Indian music</td><td>Udgatri</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yajurveda</td><td>Sacrificial formulas; Shukla and Krishna</td><td>Adhvaryu</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Atharvaveda</b></td><td><b>Magical charms and spells</b>, healing, farming and daily life; the term <b><i>kshetra-patni</i></b> ("mistress of the field") originates here; calls sabha and samiti the daughters of Prajapati</td><td>Brahman</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Each Veda has Samhita, Brahmanas, Aranyakas and Upanishads. The Vedangas are Shiksha, Kalpa (including the Shulba sutras on geometry), Vyakarana (Panini), Nirukta (Yaska), Chhanda (Pingala) and Jyotisha.</li>
<li>The <b>Upanishads</b> (principal ones about 800–500 BCE) are <b>full of parables</b>: Nachiketa and Yama, Satyakama Jabala, Svetaketu's salt in water ("Tat tvam asi"), the chariot. They were <b>composed earlier than the Puranas</b> (compiled about 300–1000 CE). Core ideas: Brahman, Atman, karma, moksha. Dara Shikoh translated 50 into Persian (1657). "Satyameva Jayate" is from the Mundaka Upanishad.</li>
<li>The 18 Mahapuranas have five marks (creation, re-creation, genealogies, manvantaras, dynasties) and are sources for royal lineages.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ancient", "a3-1", `
<p>Gautama Buddha (about 563–483 BCE by the traditional dating) taught a middle way to end suffering. His order, the Sangha, split into schools that spread across Asia.</p>

<h3>The Buddha's life and names</h3>
<ul>
<li>Born at Lumbini (Nepal) to Shuddhodana of the Shakya republic and Mahamaya (a Koliyan princess). He left home at 29, was enlightened at Bodh Gaya on the Niranjana, gave his first sermon at Sarnath, and died at Kushinagar at 80.</li>
<li>Epithets of the Buddha: <b>Shakyamuni</b> (sage of the Shakyas), <b>Tathagata</b> ("thus-gone", his own term), Sugata, Bhagavat. <b>Nayaputta/Nataputta</b> is <b>Mahavira</b> (Nigantha Nataputta in Buddhist texts).</li>
<li>The <b>kingdoms associated with his life</b> (per UPSC's key) were <b>Kosala</b> (Shravasti, Jetavana; King Prasenajit) and <b>Magadha</b> (Bodh Gaya, Rajagriha; Bimbisara, Ajatashatru). Avanti came to Buddhism through his disciple Mahakachchana (debated), and Gandhara became Buddhist much later.</li>
<li><b>Milinda</b> (Indo-Greek Menander, about 165–130 BCE) was not his contemporary; Bimbisara, Prasenajit and Mahavira were.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teaching and the Sangha</h3>
<ul>
<li>Four Noble Truths, the Eightfold Path and the Middle Way. Three marks of existence: impermanence, suffering, no-self. <b>Nirvana</b> means "blowing out": <b>the extinction of the flame of desire</b>, which ends rebirth.</li>
<li><b>Pavarana</b> ended the rainy-season retreat (vassa): each monk <b>invited others to point out his faults</b> and confessed offences committed during the retreat. Other monastic terms: <i>pabbajja</i> (first ordination as a novice), <i>upasampada</i> (full ordination), <i>vassavasa</i> (the rainy-season retreat), <i>uposatha</i> (fortnightly assembly to recite the rules).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Council</th><th>Place, patron, president</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
<tr><td>First (about 483 BCE)</td><td>Rajagriha; Ajatashatru; Mahakassapa</td><td>Sutta (Ananda) and Vinaya (Upali)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second (about 383 BCE)</td><td>Vaishali; Kalashoka; Sabakami</td><td>Split into <b>Sthaviravadins</b> and <b>Mahasanghikas</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Third (about 250 BCE)</td><td>Pataliputra; Ashoka; Moggaliputta Tissa</td><td>Abhidhamma; missions abroad</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fourth (1st century CE)</td><td>Kundalvana, Kashmir; Kanishka; <b>Vasumitra</b> (with Ashvaghosha)</td><td>Mahavibhasha; Mahayana–Hinayana split</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Schools</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Sthaviravadins</b> (the elders) led to <b>Theravada</b>, not Mahayana. The <b>Lokottaravada</b> school branched from the <b>Mahasanghikas</b>, whose <b>deification of the Buddha fostered Mahayana</b>.</li>
<li><b>Sautrantika and Sammitiya</b> were schools of <b>Buddhism</b>, not Jainism. The <b>Sarvastivadins</b> ("all exists") held that the constituents of phenomena are <b>not wholly momentary but exist always in latent form</b>; they were strong in Kashmir and Gandhara.</li>
<li><b>Mahayana</b> features: <b>deification of the Buddha</b>, the <b>bodhisattva path</b>, and <b>image worship and ritual</b>.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Hinayana / Theravada</th><th>Mahayana</th></tr>
<tr><td>Buddha a teacher, shown by symbols</td><td>Buddha deified; images (Gandhara, Mathura)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ideal: arhat (own liberation)</td><td>Ideal: bodhisattva (liberation for all)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pali texts; Sri Lanka, Southeast Asia</td><td>Sanskrit texts; China, Japan, Korea, Tibet</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>A <b>bodhisattva</b> is a <b>compassionate being on the way to enlightenment</b> who <b>delays his own salvation</b> to help all beings. The idea is central to <b>Mahayana</b>, not Hinayana.
<ul><li>Avalokiteshvara/Padmapani: compassion.</li>
<li>Manjushri: wisdom.</li>
<li>Vajrapani: protection.</li>
<li><b>Maitreya</b>: the <b>future Buddha</b>.</li>
<li>Kshitigarbha: guardian of beings in hell.</li></ul></li>
<li>Mahayana philosophy: Madhyamaka (Nagarjuna, emptiness) and Yogachara (Asanga, Vasubandhu). Vajrayana grew later in eastern India and Tibet.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Centres and spread</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Dhanyakataka</b> (Dharanikota, near Amaravati) in <b>Andhra</b> was a Satavahana capital and a major <b>Mahasanghika</b> centre. Other Andhra sites: Nagarjunakonda, Bhattiprolu, Ghantasala, Salihundam. Elsewhere: Nalanda and Vikramashila (Magadha); Taxila (Gandhara); Ratnagiri, Udayagiri, Lalitgiri (Odisha); Sanchi, Bharhut; Karle, Kanheri.</li>
<li>To China: Kashyapa Matanga (67 CE, White Horse Temple), <b>Sanghabhuti</b> (from Kashmir, about 381–384 CE; wrote a commentary on the <b>Sarvastivada Vinaya</b>), Kumarajiva (Lotus Sutra), Bodhidharma (Chan/Zen). Other texts: the Prajnaparamita sutras, Buddhaghosa's <i>Visuddhimagga</i> (Theravada) and the <i>Lalitavistara</i> (a life of the Buddha).</li>
<li><b>Decline</b> in the early medieval period: the <b>Buddha was absorbed as an avatar of Vishnu</b>. Invaders as a whole did not persecute Buddhism (the Hun Mihirakula did; Indo-Greeks and Kushanas patronised it), and the <b>Guptas were tolerant</b> (Kumaragupta I founded Nalanda).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ancient", "a3-2", `
<p>Jainism, reorganised by Vardhamana Mahavira (24th tirthankara), stresses strict non-violence, asceticism and the liberation of the soul from karma.</p>

<h3>Mahavira and the tirthankaras</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mahavira's mother Trishala was the <b>sister</b> (not daughter) of the Lichchhavi chief Chetaka of Vaishali; Chetaka's daughter Chellana married Bimbisara. The Buddha's mother Mahamaya was a Koliyan princess, though UPSC's key accepted "Kosalan" (debated).</li>
<li><b>Parshvanatha</b>, the 23rd tirthankara (symbol: snake), was born in <b>Varanasi</b>, son of King Ashvasena, and taught four vows. Mahavira (symbol: lion) added brahmacharya. Rishabhanatha was the first.</li>
<li>Mahavira's epithets: Jina ("conqueror", hence Jain), Nigantha Nataputta, Kevalin, Vira.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Doctrine</h3>
<ul>
<li>The universe consists of <b>jiva</b> (souls) and <b>ajiva</b> (matter, space, time, motion, rest). <b>Every object, even rocks and running water, has a soul</b>, which is why Jains practise extreme ahimsa. Beings are classed by senses, from one-sensed (earth, water, fire, air, plants) to five-sensed (animals, humans).</li>
<li><b>Karma</b> is subtle matter clinging to the soul. It is the <b>bane of the soul and must be ended</b>, and <b>penance</b> (tapas, even fasting unto death, sallekhana) is the surest way to destroy it.</li>
<li>Four destinies (<b>gatis</b>): deva (heavenly), manushya (human), tiryancha (animals and plants) and naraki (hell beings). <b>Yakshas</b> are attendant deities within the deva class, not a separate gati.</li>
<li>The world is <b>uncreated</b>, maintained by <b>universal law</b>; there is no creator god.</li>
<li><b>Anekantavada</b> ("many-sidedness" of truth, like the blind men and the elephant) and syadvada (sevenfold "maybe") are core Jain theories. Triratna: right faith, knowledge, conduct.</li>
<li>Strict ahimsa kept <b>farmers away</b> from Jainism, since ploughing kills insects; Jains turned to trade.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Common to Buddhism and Jainism</th><th>Different</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Indifference to Vedic authority</b>; <b>denial of the efficacy of rituals</b>; Kshatriya founders; Prakrit and Pali; monastic orders; ahimsa and karma</td><td><b>Avoiding extremes</b> of penance and pleasure is Buddhist only (Jainism stresses severe penance); Jains believe in a soul in everything, Buddhists deny a permanent self</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Sects, councils and texts</h3>
<ul>
<li>During the famine in Chandragupta Maurya's time, <b>Bhadrabahu</b> led monks south to Shravanabelagola, and his followers became the <b>Digambaras</b>. Those who stayed in Magadha with <b>Sthulabhadra</b> became the <b>Svetambaras</b>. Councils: Pataliputra (about 300 BCE) and Valabhi (5th century CE, canon written in Ardhamagadhi).</li>
<li><b>Kharavela</b> of Kalinga (1st century BCE) patronised Jainism; his Hathigumpha inscription at Udayagiri opens with a salute to the Arhats. Other patrons: Chandragupta Maurya, Amoghavarsha, Kumarapala.</li>
<li><b>Jain texts</b>: Hemachandra's <b><i>Parishishtaparvan</i></b> (source of the story of Chandragupta's Jain end) and Jinasena–Gunabhadra's <b><i>Trishashtilakshana Mahapurana</i></b> (the 63 great men). <b>Buddhist texts</b>: the <i>Nettipakarana</i> (a Pali interpretive manual) and the <i>Avadanashataka</i> (stories).</li>
<li>Other Jain works: <i>Kalpasutra</i> (Bhadrabahu), <i>Tattvartha Sutra</i> (Umasvati), <i>Bhagavati Sutra</i> (lists 16 mahajanapadas); Kannada's Pampa, Ponna and Ranna.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_ancient_a4.js
// Ancient History book, Units 4–6: Mahajanapadas to the regional kingdoms; economy, science and sources.
addBookTopic("ancient", "a4-1", `
<p>From the 6th century BCE, iron tools, rice farming in the Ganga valley, towns, coins and writing created the "second urbanisation". Sixteen great states (mahajanapadas) competed until Magadha absorbed the rest.</p>

<h3>The second urbanisation</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pali texts</b> give the first definite references to <b>coins</b>: kahapana (karshapana), nikkha (nishka), kamsa, kakanika. Archaeology confirms them with <b>punch-marked coins</b>, mostly silver, from many sites. Both point to the <b>emergence of urban life</b> and the <b>transition to a money economy</b>. In Vedic texts, nishka and shatamana were units of value, not definite coins.</li>
<li>Other signs: iron ploughshares, wet rice farming, Northern Black Polished Ware, fortified towns (Rajgir, Kaushambi, Ujjain), guilds, Brahmi writing, and merchants backing Buddhism and Jainism.</li>
<li>Later coinage: Indo-Greek coins with portraits; Kushana gold in quantity; Satavahana lead; Gupta gold dinaras; Roman coins in the south.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The sixteen mahajanapadas</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Mahajanapada</th><th>Capital</th><th>Region and river</th></tr>
<tr><td>Anga</td><td>Champa</td><td>East Bihar</td></tr>
<tr><td>Magadha</td><td>Rajagriha, then Pataliputra</td><td>South Bihar (Ganga–Son)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kashi</td><td>Varanasi</td><td>Varuna–Asi</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kosala</b></td><td>Shravasti, Ayodhya</td><td><b>Sarayu</b>; King Prasenajit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vajji</td><td>Vaishali</td><td>Confederacy of eight clans (Lichchhavis, Jnatrikas)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Malla</td><td>Kushinara, Pava</td><td>Republic; the Buddha died at Kushinara</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chedi; Vatsa</td><td>Shuktimati; Kaushambi</td><td>Bundelkhand; Yamuna (King Udayana)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kuru; Panchala; Matsya; Surasena</td><td>Indraprastha; Ahichhatra, Kampilya; Viratnagar; Mathura</td><td>Upper Ganga–Yamuna and Rajasthan</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Asmaka</b></td><td>Potana</td><td><b>Godavari</b>; the only one south of the Vindhyas</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Avanti</b></td><td>Ujjayini, Mahishmati</td><td>Malwa: Kshipra and <b>Narmada</b> (not the Mahanadi); King Pradyota</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gandhara</td><td>Taxila</td><td>Northwest</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kamboja</b></td><td>Rajapura</td><td>Far northwest (Hindu Kush borders), famous for horses; not on the Vipas (Beas)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Takshashila</b> (Taxila), capital of Gandhara and a great centre of learning (Panini, Chanakya, Jivaka), lay between the <b>Indus and the Jhelum</b>.</li>
<li>Magadha's rise: Bimbisara (Haryanka), Ajatashatru, Udayin (moved the capital to Pataliputra), the Shishunagas (who annexed Avanti), then the <b>Nandas</b> (Mahapadma Nanda). The <b>Nandas ruled North India when Alexander invaded</b> (326 BCE); reports of their huge army helped make his troops refuse to cross the Beas. Chandragupta Maurya took over in about 322 BCE.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ancient", "a4-2", `
<p>The Mauryas (about 322–185 BCE) built India's first large empire. Ashoka's inscriptions are the earliest deciphered records of an Indian ruler.</p>

<h3>Administration</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Level</th><th>Officers</th></tr>
<tr><td>Centre (Pataliputra)</td><td>King, council of ministers (mantriparishad), 18 high functionaries (tirthas) such as the samaharta (chief revenue collector) and sannidhata (treasurer); 27 superintendents (adhyakshas); Ashoka's <b>Dhamma Mahamattas</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Provinces</td><td>Princes as viceroys at Taxila, Ujjain, Suvarnagiri and Tosali</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>District</b> (ahara/vishaya)</td><td><b>Pradeshika</b> (district head), <b>Rajuka</b> (land survey and revenue, later given judicial powers), <b>Yukta</b> (accounts and secretarial). Rock Edict III orders all three to tour every five years</td></tr>
<tr><td>Villages and cities</td><td>Gopa and sthanika; gramika (village head); nagaraka (city), with Megasthenes' six committees for Pataliputra</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Kautilya's <b>Arthashastra</b> on slaves: if a master fathered a son on a female slave, <b>both mother and son became free</b>, and the <b>son had the legal status of the master's son</b>. UPSC did not accept "a person could become a slave as a judicial punishment" (debated: Kangle lists "enslaved by punishment"). Slaves could own and inherit property and buy their freedom; an Arya could not be permanently enslaved. Megasthenes wrote that there were no slaves in India.</li>
<li>Other Arthashastra ideas: the saptanga (seven-limbed) theory of the state, mandala theory of foreign relations, and spies.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ashoka and his inscriptions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ashoka conquered <b>Kalinga</b> (about 261 BCE); Rock Edict XIII describes the slaughter and his remorse. Kalinga controlled land and sea routes to the south, but UPSC's key did not accept that as the stated reason for the conquest (debated).</li>
<li><b>Rock Edict XII</b> preaches <b>tolerance among sects</b>: whoever praises his own sect and blames others "injures his own sect very severely". Other edicts:
<ul><li>I: no animal sacrifice.</li>
<li>II: medical care for people and animals; names the Cholas, Pandyas, Satiyaputras, Keralaputras.</li>
<li>III: officials' tours.</li>
<li>V: Dhamma Mahamattas.</li>
<li>XIII: the Kalinga war and Greek kings.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>James Prinsep</b> first deciphered the edicts' Brahmi (1837). (Not Georg Bühler, Max Müller or William Jones.) The Maski edict (1915) confirmed that "Devanampiya Piyadasi" was Ashoka; his name also appears at Gujarra, Nittur and Udegolam.</li>
<li><b>Kanganahalli</b> (near Sannati, Karnataka) has a relief labelled "<b>Ranyo Ashoka</b>" with his portrait, the only such labelled image.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Major Rock Edict site</th><th>State</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dhauli</b>, <b>Jaugada</b></td><td><b>Odisha</b> (Separate Kalinga Edicts; Dhauli's rock-cut elephant)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Erragudi</b></td><td><b>Andhra Pradesh</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kalsi</b></td><td><b>Uttarakhand</b> (best preserved), not Karnataka</td></tr>
<tr><td>Girnar</td><td>Gujarat (also Rudradaman's and Skandagupta's inscriptions)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sopara</td><td>Maharashtra</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sannati</td><td>Karnataka</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shahbazgarhi, Mansehra; Kandahar</td><td>Pakistan (Kharosthi); Afghanistan (Greek and Aramaic)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>After the Mauryas</h3>
<ul>
<li>The last Mauryan, <b>Brihadratha</b>, was killed by his commander <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b> (about 185 BCE).</li>
<li>The last Shunga, <b>Devabhuti</b>, was killed through his minister <b>Vasudeva Kanva</b> (about 73 BCE), who founded the Kanva dynasty.</li>
<li>The last Kanva was overthrown by the <b>Satavahanas</b> (Andhras), about 28 BCE.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ancient", "a5-1", `
<p>After the Mauryas, foreign dynasties ruled the northwest while the Satavahanas held the Deccan. In the far south, the Sangam age saw the Chera, Chola and Pandya kingdoms and rich trade with Rome.</p>

<h3>Foreign rulers in the northwest</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Invaders</th><th>Period</th><th>Rulers and notes</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Indo-Greeks</b></td><td>From about 190 BCE</td><td>Demetrius, Menander (Milinda). They introduced <b>military governorship</b> (strategoi) and coins with portraits</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Shakas</b></td><td>1st century BCE</td><td>Maues; Western Kshatrapas (Rudradaman's Junagadh inscription, 150 CE)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kushanas</b></td><td>1st–3rd century CE</td><td>Kujula Kadphises; Kanishka (Saka era, 78 CE; Fourth Buddhist Council)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So the order is Greeks, Shakas, Kushanas.</p>
<ul>
<li>In the Krishna valley, the <b>Ikshvakus</b> (3rd century CE, capital Nagarjunakonda) were Brahmanical kings, but their queens richly endowed Buddhist sites, so they were <b>not hostile to Buddhism</b>. The <b>Palas</b> of Bengal (8th–12th century) were great <b>patrons of Buddhism</b>: Vikramashila, Nalanda, Somapura.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Sangam age</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Dynasty</th><th>Emblem</th><th>Capital; port</th><th>Kings</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Chera</b></td><td>Bow</td><td>Vanji (Karur); <b>Muziris</b> (Muchiri), Tondi</td><td><b>Udiyanjeral</b> (the earliest Chera, not a Chola), Nedunjeral Athan, <b>Senguttuvan</b> (began the Kannagi/Pattini cult)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chola</td><td>Tiger</td><td>Uraiyur; <b>Puhar</b> (Poompuhar, Kaveripattinam)</td><td>Karikala (battle of Venni; Grand Anicut on the Kaveri)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Pandya</b></td><td>Fish</td><td>Madurai; <b>Korkai</b> (pearls)</td><td><b>Nedunjeliyan</b> (victory at Talaiyalanganam)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Korkai, Poompuhar and Muchiri</b> were all <b>ports</b>. Exports were pepper ("yavanapriya"), pearls, ivory, muslin and beryl; imports were Roman gold coins, wine and glass. Sources: the <i>Periplus</i>, Pliny, and the Muziris papyrus. Sites: Arikamedu, Pattanam, Keezhadi.</li>
<li><b>Vadakkiruttal</b> ("sitting facing north"): a <b>king or warrior, dishonoured by defeat</b> or a wound in the back, <b>fasted to death</b>. Example: Chera Perunjeral Adan after the battle of Venni. Compare Jain sallekhana.</li>
<li>Sangam poets <b>knew the varna classification</b>, though Tamil society was organised more by occupation and landscape (tinai).</li>
<li>Literature: three Sangams at Madurai (only the third's works survive); Tolkappiyam (grammar), Ettuthogai, Pattuppattu. <b>Akam</b> (love) and <b>puram</b> (war, public life) poems. Five tinais: kurinji (hills), mullai (forest), marutam (farmland), neytal (coast), palai (dry land).</li>
<li>The <b>Patinenkilkanakku</b> (Eighteen Minor Works, including the Tirukkural) carry on akam and puram themes and are classed as <b>post-Sangam</b>; UPSC accepted that as the explanation. The twin epics <i>Silappadikaram</i> (Ilango Adigal) and <i>Manimekalai</i> (Sittalai Sattanar) came later.</li>
<li>Hero stones (nadukal) honoured fallen warriors; bards were the panar and viraliyar.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ancient", "a5-2", `
<p>The Guptas (about 320–550 CE) united north India in its "classical age". After them came Harsha in the north, and the Chalukyas, Pallavas and Rashtrakutas in the Deccan and south.</p>

<h3>The Guptas</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Ruler</th><th>Key facts</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Samudragupta</b> (about 335–375)</td><td>The <b>Allahabad pillar</b> prashasti by Harishena records his conquests: uprooting kings in Aryavarta, capturing and releasing southern kings. Veena-playing coins; "Napoleon of India" (V.A. Smith)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Chandragupta II</b> (about 375–415)</td><td>Defeated the Western Kshatrapas; Vikramaditya; marriage alliance with the Vakatakas; the Mehrauli iron pillar. <b>Faxian (Fa-hien)</b> visited in his reign (about 399–414) and wrote <i>Fo-kuo-chi</i>, describing mild punishments and Chandalas living outside towns. Linked by tradition with <b>Kalidasa</b> and Amarasimha</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kumaragupta I</td><td>Founded Nalanda; performed the Ashvamedha</td></tr>
<tr><td>Skandagupta</td><td>Repelled the Hunas (Bhitari inscription); repaired the Sudarshana lake</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Faxian could <b>not</b> have attended Kanishka's Fourth Council, which was centuries earlier.</li>
<li>Land and revenue terms:
<ul><li><b>Kulyavapa</b> and <b>dronavapa</b> were <b>land measures</b> in Bengal copper plates.</li>
<li>Kshetra: cultivated land; khila: waste land.</li>
<li>Bhaga: the king's share (about one-sixth); bhoga: supplies of fruit and firewood; hiranya: tax in cash.</li>
<li>Agrahara: tax-free land grants to Brahmins.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Vishti</b> (forced labour) was a <b>source of income for the state</b>, a tax paid in labour, common in central and western India.</li>
<li>Indian <b>feudalism</b>: land grants with fiscal and administrative rights expanded in the Gupta period (R.S. Sharma). UPSC's key traced its origin to military campaigns (rewarding chiefs with land), but did not accept the Gupta expansion as the explanation of that origin.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Between the Guptas and Harsha</h3>
<p>From the Guptas' decline to Harsha, northern India was held by the <b>Later Guptas of Magadha</b>, the <b>Pushyabhutis of Thanesar</b>, the <b>Maukharis of Kanauj</b> and the <b>Maitrakas of Valabhi</b>. The Paramaras of Malwa (9th–10th century) and the Yadavas of Devagiri (12th century) came later.</p>

<h3>Harsha (606–647)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sources: Banabhatta's <i>Harshacharita</i>, Hiuen Tsang's <i>Si-yu-ki</i>, the Madhuban and Banskhera plates. Harsha wrote <i>Nagananda</i>, <i>Ratnavali</i> and <i>Priyadarshika</i>.</li>
<li><b>Pulakeshin II</b> (Chalukya of Badami) stopped his southward march <b>on the Narmada</b>, as Ravikirti's Aihole inscription (634) records.</li>
<li><b>Hiuen Tsang</b> (Xuanzang, 629–645) found Harsha a <b>great patron of Buddhism</b>, not hostile to it. He recorded <b>trial by ordeal</b> (fire, water, poison) and <b>duties at ferries and barrier stations</b>; roads were <b>not safe</b>, as he was robbed himself. I-tsing (671–695) came later by sea.</li>
<li>Harsha held the <b>Prayag assembly</b> every five years, an act of charity in which he honoured the Buddha, Surya and Shiva and gave to all sects. It was not "only" to spread Mahayana; the Kanauj assembly (643) honoured Mahayana with Hiuen Tsang.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The south and Deccan</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pallavas</b> of Kanchi:
<ul><li><b>Mahendravarman I</b> (about 600–630) took the titles <b>Mattavilasa</b> (from his farce <i>Mattavilasa Prahasana</i>), <b>Vichitrachitta</b> and <b>Gunabhara</b>, as well as Chitrakarapuli. He began rock-cut temples (Mandagapattu) and was converted from Jainism to Shaivism by Appar.</li>
<li>His son <b>Narasimhavarman I</b> ("Mamalla") took Vatapi (642) and built the Mamallapuram rathas.</li>
<li>His father <b>Simhavishnu</b> founded the line.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Dantidurga</b>, a Chalukya feudatory, founded the <b>Rashtrakuta</b> empire (about 753), whose later capital was Manyakheta. Later kings: Krishna I (built the Kailasa temple, Ellora), Dhruva, and Amoghavarsha I (patron of Jainism; Kannada work <i>Kavirajamarga</i>).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ancient", "a6-1", `
<p>Ancient India's economy rested on agriculture, but crafts organised in guilds and sea trade with Rome and Southeast Asia brought great wealth.</p>

<h3>Guilds</h3>
<ul>
<li>Guilds (<i>shreni</i>, puga, nigama) of weavers, oil-pressers, merchants and others were headed by a shreshthi. They <b>fixed wages, rules of work, standards and prices</b>, and had <b>judicial powers over their members</b>; the king respected guild law (shreni dharma). They were largely autonomous: there is no evidence that every guild was registered with the state or that the king administered them.</li>
<li>Guilds also acted as banks, taking deposits and paying interest (Ushavadata's Nashik inscription), and made donations; the ivory workers of Vidisha carved part of a Sanchi gateway. Gupta-era guild seals come from Vaishali.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ports and sea routes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Gupta-period <b>Ghantasala</b> and <b>Kadura</b> (Andhra coast) and <b>Chaul</b> (Maharashtra) were <b>ports handling foreign trade</b>.</li>
<li>Early links across the <b>Bay of Bengal</b> with Southeast Asia rested on the <b>monsoon winds</b>, which reverse seasonally and allowed predictable return voyages. Odisha's <b>Bali Jatra</b> at Cuttack recalls voyages to Bali; legend makes the Brahmin <b>Kaundinya</b> the founder of Funan (Cambodia).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Coast</th><th>Ports</th></tr>
<tr><td>West</td><td>Barygaza (Bharuch), Sopara, Kalyan, Chaul, Muziris, Barbaricum</td></tr>
<tr><td>East</td><td>Tamralipti, Arikamedu, Puhar, Korkai, Masulipatnam, Ghantasala, Chandraketugarh</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Sources: the <i>Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</i>, Ptolemy and Pliny.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("ancient", "a6-2", `
<p>Ancient Indian scholars made lasting advances in mathematics, astronomy, medicine, grammar and law.</p>

<h3>Mathematics and astronomy</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Baudhayana Sulba Sutra</b> (about 800–600 BCE) states what is now called the Pythagoras theorem, on the <b>sides of a right-angled triangle</b>, for building fire altars.</li>
<li><b>Aryabhata</b> (<i>Aryabhatiya</i>, 499 CE): place-value notation, π ≈ 3.1416, the Earth's rotation and the <b>sine</b> table (known by the 5th century); India's first satellite (1975) is named after him.</li>
<li><b>Brahmagupta</b> (628 CE): rules for zero and negative numbers, and the area of a <b>cyclic quadrilateral</b> (known by the 7th century). Bhaskara II wrote the <i>Lilavati</i> (1150). The Kerala school (Madhava) anticipated calculus.</li>
<li><b>Place value</b> in inscriptions:
<ul><li>Its earliest use in India is on the <b>Mankani plates</b> of Gujarat (595–596 CE).</li>
<li>It became <b>general across India by the 9th century</b>; the Gwalior inscription (876) has a circular zero.</li>
<li>It appears in <b>Southeast Asian Sanskrit inscriptions by the 7th century</b> (Sambor, Cambodia, 683).</li></ul>
The Bakhshali manuscript uses a dot for zero. The Arabs (al-Khwarizmi) and then Europe (Fibonacci, 1202) took up the "Hindu–Arabic" numerals.</li>
<li>Medicine: the <b>Sushruta Samhita</b> describes over 100 <b>surgical instruments</b> (by the 1st century CE), rhinoplasty and cataract surgery. There was <b>no transplant of internal organs</b>. Charaka wrote on medicine.</li>
<li>A UPSC match (key-based, loose): Aryabhata (Earth's orbit), Baudhayana (π), Bhaskaracharya (zero, though Brahmagupta first codified it), <b>Gyandev</b> (Sant Dnyaneshwar) and <b>snakes and ladders</b> (Mokshapat).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Scholars and patrons</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Scholar</th><th>Associated with</th></tr>
<tr><td>Panini (<i>Ashtadhyayi</i>)</td><td>About 5th–4th century BCE, Gandhara; <b>not</b> Pushyamitra Shunga</td></tr>
<tr><td>Patanjali (<i>Mahabhashya</i>)</td><td>Pushyamitra Shunga</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ashvaghosha, Nagarjuna, Charaka, Vasumitra</td><td>Kanishka</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harishena</td><td>Samudragupta</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kalidasa</b>, <b>Amarasimha</b> (<i>Amarakosha</i>), Varahamihira</td><td><b>Chandragupta II</b> (tradition); Amarasimha was not Harsha's</td></tr>
<tr><td>Banabhatta, Mayura</td><td>Harsha</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ravikirti</td><td>Pulakeshin II</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Aryadeva</b> was a <b>Buddhist</b> (Madhyamaka, disciple of Nagarjuna), not a Jain scholar. <b>Dignaga</b> was a <b>Buddhist logician</b> (<i>Pramanasamuccaya</i>; successor Dharmakirti). <b>Nathamuni</b> was a <b>Vaishnava</b> scholar who compiled the 4,000 hymns of the Alvars; his line led to Ramanuja.</li>
<li>Other Buddhist scholars: Asanga and Vasubandhu (Yogachara), Buddhaghosa, Shantideva, Atisha Dipankara.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Law: Mitakshara and Dayabhaga</h3>
<ul>
<li>The two are <b>regional schools</b>, not caste-based. <b>Dayabhaga</b> (Jimutavahana, 12th century) prevailed in Bengal and Assam; <b>Mitakshara</b> (Vijnaneshvara's commentary on the Yajnavalkya Smriti, at Vikramaditya VI's Chalukya court) elsewhere.</li>
<li>Under Mitakshara, <b>sons have a right by birth</b> and can claim during the father's lifetime; under Dayabhaga, <b>only after the father's death</b>. The male-only versus male-and-female distinction is wrong.</li>
<li>The Hindu Succession Act 1956, amended in 2005, made daughters coparceners; Vineeta Sharma (2020) applied this regardless of the father's death date.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ancient", "a6-3", `
<p>Archaeology fills in the long prehistory and early history of India. UPSC tests what each site is known for and which state it is in.</p>

<h3>Prehistoric to early historic cultures</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Culture</th><th>Key sites</th></tr>
<tr><td>Palaeolithic</td><td>Bhimbetka (rock-shelter paintings, WHS), Attirampakkam, Hunsgi, Belan valley, Pallavaram</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mesolithic</td><td>Bagor, Adamgarh, Langhnaj, Sarai Nahar Rai</td></tr>
<tr><td>Neolithic</td><td>Mehrgarh (earliest farming), <b>Burzahom</b> (Kashmir: <b>pit dwellings</b>, dogs buried with masters; <b>no rock-cut shrines</b>), Gufkral, Chirand, Koldihwa (rice), Daojali Hading</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chalcolithic</td><td><b>Inamgaon</b> (Pune; Jorwe culture), Daimabad, Navdatoli, Ahar, Gilund; <b>Ganeshwar</b> (Sikar, near Khetri: <b>copper artefacts</b> that probably supplied the Harappans)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Megalithic (Iron Age)</td><td>Brahmagiri, Adichanallur, Junapani, <b>Mangadu</b> (Kerala), Kodumanal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Early historic</td><td><b>Chandraketugarh</b> (<b>West Bengal</b>, not Odisha): a port town famous for <b>terracotta plaques</b>; Sisupalgarh; Arikamedu</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Salihundam</b> (Srikakulam, AP) is a Buddhist site with stupas and monasteries, <b>not rock-cut cave shrines</b>; Guntupalli is the rock-cut site in Andhra.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Places and their states</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Place</th><th>State</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sisupalgarh</b></td><td>Odisha: early historic fortified city near Bhubaneswar</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Piprahwa</b></td><td>Uttar Pradesh: stupa with the Buddha's relics (1898), identified with Kapilavastu</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Goalpara</b></td><td>Assam: Surya Pahar's Hindu, Buddhist and Jain remains</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bishnupur</b></td><td>Manipur (Vishnu temple); another Bishnupur in West Bengal has terracotta temples</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bhilsa</b></td><td>Madhya Pradesh (old name of Vidisha)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Girinagar</b></td><td>Gujarat (Girnar, near Junagadh)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dwarasamudra</b></td><td>Karnataka (Halebidu, the Hoysala capital), not Maharashtra</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sthanesvara</b></td><td>Haryana (Thanesar), not Uttar Pradesh</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Other old names: Pataliputra (Patna), Kanyakubja (Kannauj), Vatapi (Badami), Manyakheta (Malkhed), Devagiri (Daulatabad), Tosali (near Dhauli).</p>
`);
;
