// Medieval History book, Units 1–3: Early medieval India, the Delhi Sultanate, Vijayanagara and the Deccan.
addBookTopic("medieval", "d1-1", `
<p>Between about 600 and 1200 CE, north India was divided among regional kingdoms that fought over Kannauj, while land grants created a layered, "feudal" society.</p>

<h3>The struggle for Kannauj and the Rajput dynasties</h3>
<ul>
<li>In the 8th century the great powers were the Rashtrakutas (Dantidurga, about 753), the Palas (Gopala, about 750), the Gurjara-Pratiharas (Nagabhata I, about 730), the Pallavas, the Badami Chalukyas and Lalitaditya of Kashmir; the Arabs took Sindh in 712.</li>
<li>The <b>Tripartite Struggle</b> (8th–10th centuries) for Kannauj was fought among the <b>Palas</b> (Dharmapala, Devapala), the <b>Gurjara-Pratiharas</b> (<b>Nagabhata II</b>, who took Kannauj; <b>Mihira Bhoja</b>, 836–885) and the <b>Rashtrakutas</b> (Dhruva, Govinda III, Amoghavarsha).</li>
<li>Chronology: Mahendravarman I (Pallava, about 600–630), then Gopala founding the Palas (about 750), then the Pratiharas under Bhoja (9th century), then Parantaka I establishing Chola power (907–955).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Dynasty</th><th>Region</th><th>Kings and monuments</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Chandelas</b></td><td>Bundelkhand (Khajuraho, Kalinjar)</td><td>Founded by <b>Nannuka</b>; <b>Jayashakti</b> (Jeja), after whom Jejakabhukti is named, was also a Chandela, not a Paramara; Dhanga, Vidyadhara (Kandariya Mahadeva)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Paramaras</td><td>Malwa (Dhar)</td><td>Munja, <b>Raja Bhoja</b> (scholar-king); neither Bhoja was a Rashtrakuta</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chauhans</td><td>Ajmer, Delhi</td><td>Prithviraj III (Tarain, 1191–92)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solankis</td><td>Gujarat</td><td>Bhima I (Modhera), Kumarapala</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gahadavalas</td><td>Kannauj, Varanasi</td><td>Govindachandra; Jaichand (Chandawar, 1194)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kalachuris</td><td>Tripuri</td><td>Gangeyadeva, Lakshmikarna</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Hoysalas</b> (late 10th–11th century; Dwarasamudra), <b>Gahadavalas</b> (late 11th century), <b>Kakatiyas</b> (independent about 1163; Warangal) and <b>Yadavas</b> (about 1187; Devagiri) all rose long after the early 8th century.</li>
<li>Hill states (from Kalhana's <i>Rajatarangini</i> and inscriptions): <b>Durgara</b> was Jammu (hence "Dogra"); <b>Champaka</b> was Chamba and <b>Kuluta</b> was Kullu, both in Himachal (not Central India or Malabar); Trigarta was Kangra.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Feudalism and village life</h3>
<ul>
<li>The essential elements of <b>feudalism</b> are an <b>administration based on control of land</b> and a <b>lord–vassal</b> (overlord–samanta) hierarchy. It meant a <b>weak centre and strong local power</b>, not a strong centralised authority. R.S. Sharma's <i>Indian Feudalism</i> (1965) traced it to land grants; Harbans Mukhia and B.D. Chattopadhyaya disputed the model.</li>
<li><b>Mahattara</b> and <b>Pattakila</b> were <b>village headmen</b> in early medieval inscriptions ("Patel" comes from pattakila). Village heads across periods: gramani (Vedic), gramika (Mauryan), muqaddam and khut (Sultanate), muqaddam and patwari (Mughal), patil and kulkarni (Maratha).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("medieval", "d1-2", `
<p>The imperial Cholas (about 850–1279) built the strongest state in the south, with a navy that reached Southeast Asia and a lively system of village assemblies.</p>

<h3>Chola power</h3>
<ul>
<li>Vijayalaya founded the imperial line (about 850); Parantaka I expanded it.</li>
<li><b>Rajaraja I</b> (985–1014) defeated the Pandyas and Cheras, conquered <b>northern Sri Lanka</b> (making Polonnaruwa a province) and the Maldives, built the Brihadeeswara temple (1010) and surveyed the land.</li>
<li><b>Rajendra I</b> (1014–44) completed the conquest of <b>all of Sri Lanka</b> (UPSC's key credits him with conquering Ceylon), marched to the Ganga (founding Gangaikonda Cholapuram) and about 1025 sent a <b>naval expedition against Srivijaya</b> (the Sailendras, ruling Malaya, Sumatra and Java), sacking Kadaram (Kedah) to control the China trade. So the Cholas both dominated the peninsula and attacked the Sailendra empire.</li>
<li>Kulottunga I sent an embassy to China (1077). Earlier relations with Srivijaya were friendly: its king built the Chudamani Vihara at Nagapattinam with Rajaraja's support.</li>
<li>Contemporaries: <b>Amoghavarsha I</b> (Rashtrakuta, <i>Kavirajamarga</i>), <b>Vishnuvardhana</b> (Hoysala, Belur temple), <b>Prataparudra II</b> (the last Kakatiya, defeated in 1323).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Village and temple terms</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Eripatti</b></td><td>Land whose revenue was set aside to <b>maintain the village tank</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Taniyur</b></td><td>A large village made a separate revenue unit; villages donated to Brahmins were <b>brahmadeyas</b>, not taniyurs</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Ghatika</b></td><td>A <b>college attached to a temple</b> (Kanchi's ghatika)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ur; sabha; nagaram</td><td>Assemblies of ordinary villages; of brahmadeya villages (Uttaramerur inscriptions); of merchants</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nadu</td><td>A group of villages</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vellanvagai; shalabhoga; devadana; pallichchhandam</td><td>Non-Brahmin peasant land; land for a school; temple land; land for Jain institutions</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Kakatiyas and coins</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Kakatiyas (Warangal, about 1163–1323): Rudradeva (Thousand Pillar Temple), Ganapatideva, Rudramadevi and Prataparudra II. Their main seaport was <b>Motupalli</b>, where Ganapatideva's inscription (1244–45) protected merchants and Marco Polo admired Rudramadevi's rule. The Ramappa temple became a WHS in 2021, and Telangana's Mission Kakatiya restores their tanks.</li>
<li>The <b>fanam</b> was a small gold (later silver) <b>coin</b> of South India, used by the Pandyas, Cholas and Vijayanagara and later issued by European companies.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Period</th><th>Coins</th></tr>
<tr><td>Delhi Sultanate</td><td>Iltutmish's silver tanka and copper jital; Muhammad bin Tughlaq's token currency</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vijayanagara</td><td>Gold varaha (pagoda, hun), pratapa, fanam</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sher Shah, Mughals</td><td>Silver rupiya, copper dam, gold mohur</td></tr>
</table>
`);

addBookTopic("medieval", "d2-1", `
<p>The Delhi Sultanate (1206–1526) passed through five dynasties: Mamluk (Slave), Khalji, Tughlaq, Sayyid and Lodi. It built a centralised state on the iqta system while fending off the Mongols.</p>

<h3>Sultans and events</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Qutb-ud-din Aibak</b> (1206–10) <b>died after falling from his horse while playing chaugan</b> (polo) at Lahore.</li>
<li><b>Iltutmish</b> (1211–36): Chengiz Khan <b>reached the Indus in 1221</b> chasing the Khwarazmian prince Jalal-ud-din Mangbarni; Iltutmish refused him refuge and saved Delhi. Iltutmish introduced the <b>iqta</b>, the silver tanka and copper jital, and "the Forty" nobles.</li>
<li>Balban: "blood and iron", frontier forts, the army department (Diwan-i-Arz).</li>
<li><b>Mongols</b>: the first invasion came under Iltutmish, not Jalal-ud-din Khalji. Under <b>Alauddin Khalji</b>, Qutlugh Khwaja (1299) and <b>Targhi (1303) besieged Delhi</b>, which led to the Siri fort, a large standing army and market controls. <b>Muhammad bin Tughlaq</b> repelled Tarmashirin's raid (1327) and <b>lost no north-western territory</b> to the Mongols.</li>
<li><b>Muhammad bin Tughlaq</b> (1325–51):
<ul><li>He issued a heavy <b>gold coin</b> that Ibn Battuta called the <b>dinar</b>.</li>
<li>His <b>token currency</b> (1329–30) was <b>copper/brass</b>, not gold, and failed through forgery.</li>
<li>During the <b>famine</b> (not plague) of about 1334–41 he lived for two years at <b>Swargadwari</b> near Kanauj.</li>
<li>He set up the Diwan-i-Kohi for agriculture.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Firuz Tughlaq</b> (1351–88) set up the <b>Diwan-i-Bandagan</b> (department of slaves, about 1.8 lakh slaves), built canals and charged an irrigation tax.</li>
<li><b>Nasir-ud-din Mahmud</b> (1394–1412) was the <b>last Tughlaq</b>; Timur sacked Delhi in 1398 in his reign, not in Muhammad bin Tughlaq's. The Sayyids (Khizr Khan, 1414) followed.</li>
<li>Afghan rulers in order: <b>Bahlol Lodi</b> (1451), Sikandar Lodi (founded Agra, 1504), <b>Ibrahim Lodi</b> (killed at Panipat, 1526), and later <b>Sikandar Shah Suri</b> (1555, the last Sur).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Event</th><th>Date</th></tr>
<tr><td>Qutb Minar built (Aibak to Iltutmish)</td><td>About 1199–1220</td></tr>
<tr><td>Death of Firuz Tughlaq</td><td>1388</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vasco da Gama at Calicut (in Saluva times, not Deva Raya II's)</td><td>1498</td></tr>
<tr><td>Krishnadeva Raya's reign</td><td>1509–29</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Administration and ideas</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>amil</b> was the revenue collector. The <b>iqta</b> was a Central Asian Islamic institution, <b>not ancient Indian</b>. The <b>Mir Bakhshi</b> was a <b>Mughal</b> office; the Sultanate's military department was the Diwan-i-Arz.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Department</th><th>Function and founder</th></tr>
<tr><td>Diwan-i-Wizarat</td><td>Finance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diwan-i-Arz</td><td>Military (Balban)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diwan-i-Insha; Diwan-i-Risalat</td><td>Correspondence; religious affairs and appeals</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diwan-i-Mustakhraj</td><td>Arrears (Alauddin Khalji)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diwan-i-Kohi</td><td>Agriculture (Muhammad bin Tughlaq)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Diwan-i-Bandagan</b>; Diwan-i-Khairat</td><td>Slaves; charity (both Firuz Tughlaq)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>Barani</b> (<i>Fatawa-i-Jahandari</i>) refused to call the Sultanate truly Islamic because the <b>Sultans supplemented sharia with their own regulations</b> (zawabit).</li>
<li><b>Kulah-daran</b> ("cap-wearers") were the <b>Sayyids</b>, who claimed descent from the Prophet. Other terms: qalandars (wandering dervishes), ulema, bandagan (military slaves), khanqah (Sufi hospice). The Sayyid brothers were later Mughal kingmakers (1713–20).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("medieval", "d3-1", `
<p>Vijayanagara (1336–1646) dominated the south, competing with the Bahmani kingdom and its successor sultanates over the Raichur doab.</p>

<h3>Vijayanagara</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Harihara I</b> and Bukka (Sangama dynasty) founded the city in 1336 on the <b>south bank of the Tungabhadra</b> (a Krishna tributary), ruling as agents of the god <b>Virupaksha</b>; the sage Vidyaranya guided them.</li>
<li>Four dynasties: Sangama, Saluva, Tuluva, Aravidu.
<ul><li><b>Saluva Narasimha</b> ended the Sangama line about 1485 to <b>save the kingdom from disintegration</b>.</li>
<li><b>Vira Narasimha</b> deposed the last Saluva (about 1505).</li>
<li>His younger brother <b>Krishnadeva Raya</b> succeeded him (1509).</li>
<li>Krishnadeva Raya was succeeded by his half-brother <b>Achyuta Raya</b>.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Deva Raya I</b> (1406–22) built a <b>dam on the Tungabhadra and a canal-cum-aqueduct</b> to the capital; Nicolo Conti visited in his time.</li>
<li><b>Krishnadeva Raya</b> (1509–29):
<ul><li>Taxation: land was <b>taxed according to its quality</b>, and <b>workshop owners paid an industries tax</b>; houses, shops, marriages and trade were also taxed.</li>
<li>He let the <b>Portuguese build a fort at Bhatkal</b> in return for horses and help against Bijapur.</li>
<li>He took Raichur (1520) and defeated the Gajapatis.</li>
<li>He wrote <i>Amuktamalyada</i>, and the eight Telugu poets of his court were the Ashtadiggajas.</li>
<li>He built the Vittala and Hazara Rama temples.</li></ul></li>
<li>The <b>Battle of Talikota</b> (1565): the Deccan sultanates defeated Rama Raya and sacked Hampi.</li>
<li><b>Raja Wodeyar</b> took Srirangapatna (1610) and founded Mysore when <b>Venkata II</b> (Aravidu) ruled Vijayanagara. So the <b>Mysore kingdom emerged from Vijayanagara</b>.</li>
<li>Administration: nayankara (amaram lands for nayakas) and the ayagar system of village servants.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Traveller</th><th>Country</th><th>Ruler</th></tr>
<tr><td>Nicolo Conti</td><td>Italy</td><td>Deva Raya I</td></tr>
<tr><td>Abdur Razzaq</td><td>Persia</td><td>Deva Raya II</td></tr>
<tr><td>Afanasy Nikitin</td><td>Russia</td><td>Bahmani kingdom (Muhammad Shah III, with Mahmud Gawan)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Domingo Paes, Duarte Barbosa</td><td>Portugal</td><td>Krishnadeva Raya</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Fernão Nuniz</b></td><td>Portugal</td><td>Achyuta Raya: palace women were <b>wrestlers, astrologers, accountants and soothsayers</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The Deccan sultanates and Gujarat</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Bahmani kingdom (1347) split into Bijapur (Adil Shahi; <b>Yusuf Adil Shah</b> lost Goa to the Portuguese in 1510), Ahmadnagar (Nizam Shahi), Golconda, Bidar and Berar.</li>
<li><b>Ibrahim Adil Shah II</b> of Bijapur (1580–1627), author of the <i>Kitab-i-Nauras</i>, was hailed as <b>"Jagadguru"</b> for his tolerance.</li>
<li>The <b>Nizam Shahis</b> ended when Shah Jahan's forces took Daulatabad (1633): Ahmadnagar was <b>annexed and the boy-king Husain Shah imprisoned for life</b> in Gwalior. Its great minister <b>Malik Ambar</b> (died 1626) had resisted the Mughals for years.</li>
<li>Gujarat: Ahmad Shah I founded Ahmedabad (1411); <b>Mahmud Begarha</b> took Girnar and Champaner and fought the Portuguese at Chaul (1508) and Diu (1509). <b>Bahadur Shah</b>, pressed by Humayun, let the Portuguese build a fort at <b>Diu</b> (1535) and was killed by them in 1537.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_medieval_d4.js
// Medieval History book, Units 4–6: The Mughals, Bhakti and Sufi, economy and the Europeans.
addBookTopic("medieval", "d4-1", `
<p>The Mughals (1526 onwards) built India's largest pre-modern empire on the mansabdari and jagir systems, and their court patronised history-writing, translation and art.</p>

<h3>Babur to Aurangzeb</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Alam Khan Lodi</b>, an <b>uncle of Ibrahim Lodi</b> and a pretender to the throne, was among those who invited Babur, along with <b>Daulat Khan Lodi</b>, the discontented governor of Punjab.</li>
<li><b>Babur</b> (1526–30), descended from Timur and Chengiz Khan, founded the <b>Timurid (Mughal) dynasty</b> in India. He did <b>not introduce gunpowder</b> (Indian states already used cannon) or <b>the arch and dome</b> (the Sultanate's Alai Darwaza, 1311, has the first true dome). His battles:
<ul><li>Panipat (1526), against Ibrahim Lodi.</li>
<li><b>Khanwa</b> (1527), against Rana Sanga. It was <b>more decisive than Panipat</b> because Rana Sanga was a <b>more formidable foe</b>.</li>
<li>Chanderi (1528) and Ghaghra (1529).</li></ul>
He wrote the <i>Baburnama</i>.</li>
<li><b>Akbar</b> (1556–1605):
<ul><li>The <b>Ibadat Khana</b> (1575, Fatehpur Sikri) was the <b>hall where scholars of various religions debated</b>.</li>
<li>He issued the Mahzar (1579) and founded Din-i-Ilahi (1582), and pursued sulh-i-kul.</li>
<li>His translation bureau rendered the <i>Mahabharata</i> as <i>Razmnama</i>. Nizamuddin Panipati translated the <b><i>Yogavasistha</i></b> into Persian (about 1597).</li>
<li>In 1581 he marched to Kabul <b>to crush his half-brother Mirza Hakim</b>, not to reclaim Ferghana.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Shah Jahan</b>'s <b>Balkh campaign</b> aimed to <b>secure a friendly ruler in Balkh and Badakhshan</b>, which bordered Kabul, not to win back Samarqand. <b>Abdul Hamid Lahori</b> was his official historian (<i>Padshahnama</i>). In the war of succession, <b>Dharmat</b> (1658) was fought between <b>Aurangzeb (with Murad) and Dara Shikoh</b>'s imperial army, followed by Samugarh. Dara translated the Upanishads (<i>Sirr-i-Akbar</i>, 1657).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Administration</h3>
<ul>
<li>In ascending order of size: <b>pargana, sarkar, suba</b>. Suba officials were the subedar, diwan, bakhshi, sadr, qazi and kotwal; sarkar officials the faujdar and amalguzar; pargana officials the shiqdar, amil and qanungo; villages had a muqaddam and patwari. Akbar created 12 subas (1580).</li>
<li><b>Jagirdars</b> held revenue assignments in place of salary for military and administrative service (not specifically for judicial and police duties); jagirs were <b>non-hereditary and transferable</b>. <b>Zamindars</b> held <b>hereditary</b> rights and also kept local order and supplied troops. Neither of UPSC's statements on this was correct.</li>
<li>Other terms: mansab (zat and sawar ranks), khalisa (crown land), madad-i-maash (revenue-free grants), zabt/dahsala (Todar Mal's revenue system).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Land assignment</th><th>State</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Iqta</b></td><td>Delhi Sultans</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Jagir</b></td><td>Mughals</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Amaram</b></td><td>Vijayanagara</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mokasa</b></td><td>Marathas</td></tr>
</table>
`);

addBookTopic("medieval", "d4-2", `
<p>After Aurangzeb (died 1707), Mughal power weakened, governors set up successor states, and the Marathas and Afghans competed for the north.</p>

<h3>Later Mughals</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Jahandar Shah</b> (1712–13) was <b>defeated by his nephew Farrukhsiyar</b>, backed by the Sayyid brothers, and later killed.</li>
<li><b>Shah Alam II</b> became emperor in 1759 while in exile. After Buxar (1764) he lived under English protection at Allahabad, returning to Delhi only in 1772. The north-west threat was real, but UPSC's key did not accept it as the explanation: he stayed away mainly because hostile powers controlled Delhi.</li>
<li><b>Murshid Quli Khan</b> introduced revenue farming (ijaradari) in Bengal, not Ali Mardan Khan, who was a noble of Shah Jahan known for canals. True statements from the same question: Ranjit Singh set up cannon foundries at Lahore; Sawai Jai Singh of Amber had Euclid's <i>Elements</i> translated into Sanskrit; Tipu Sultan funded the goddess Sharada's idol at Sringeri.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Successor states</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>State</th><th>Founder</th></tr>
<tr><td>Hyderabad</td><td>Nizam-ul-Mulk Asaf Jah (1724)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bengal</td><td>Murshid Quli Khan</td></tr>
<tr><td>Awadh</td><td>Saadat Khan (1722)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carnatic (Arcot)</td><td>Saadatullah Khan; a Nawab, not a "Nizamat" (UPSC did not accept that it emerged from Hyderabad; debated)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mysore</b></td><td>The Wodeyars, from <b>Vijayanagara</b>; Haidar Ali took power in 1761</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Rohilkhand</b></td><td>Ali Muhammad Khan, from Mughal territory in the 1740s, not from Ahmad Shah Durrani's conquests</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bharatpur (Jats)</td><td>Churaman, Badan Singh, Suraj Mal</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Third Battle of Panipat</b> (14 January 1761): Ahmad Shah Abdali invaded to <b>avenge the Marathas' expulsion of his son Timur Shah from Lahore</b> (1758).</li>
<li><b>Tipu Sultan</b> sent <b>embassies abroad on modern lines</b>: to France, the Ottoman Sultan, Afghanistan and Persia.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("medieval", "d5-1", `
<p>Medieval devotional movements, Bhakti among Hindus and Sufism among Muslims, stressed personal devotion over ritual. Sikhism grew from the teachings of Guru Nanak.</p>

<h3>Bhakti saints</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Saint</th><th>Dates</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Nimbarka</td><td>12th–13th century</td><td>Dvaitadvaita; <b>not</b> Akbar's contemporary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ramananda</td><td>14th–15th century</td><td>Preached in Hindi; Kabir's guru</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Kabir</b></td><td>1440–1518</td><td>Weaver; his teachings are compiled in the <b><i>Bijak</i></b>; lived long before Ahmad Sirhindi</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Guru Nanak</b></td><td>1469–1539</td><td>Was <b>preaching when Babur overthrew the Lodis</b> (Babur Vani)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vallabhacharya</td><td>1479–1531</td><td>Founded <b>Pushti Marg</b> (Shuddhadvaita), not Madhvacharya (Dvaita, Udupi)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chaitanya; Mirabai</td><td>1486–1534; about 1498–1547</td><td>Gaudiya Vaishnavism; Krishna bhakti</td></tr>
<tr><td>Surdas; Tulsidas</td><td>16th century</td><td><i>Sursagar</i>; <i>Ramcharitmanas</i></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dadu Dayal</b></td><td>1544–1603</td><td>Dadu Panth (<i>Dadu Vani</i>); born after 1526</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Tukaram</b></td><td>About 1608–1650</td><td>Varkari saint; <b>contemporary of Jahangir</b> (and Shah Jahan)</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Nagarjuna</b> was a Buddhist philosopher (2nd century), not a Bhakti proponent. <b>Tyagaraja</b> (1767–1847) was a Carnatic composer much later.</p>

<h3>Sufis</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sufi practices: <b>meditation and breath control</b>, <b>severe ascetic exercises in solitude</b> (chilla, a 40-day retreat), and <b>sama</b>, music to arouse ecstasy (especially the Chishtis).</li>
<li>Silsilas:
<ul><li><b>Chishti</b>: Moinuddin (Ajmer), Nizamuddin Auliya, whose disciple was <b>Nasiruddin Chiragh-i-Dehli</b>, and Salim Chishti, a contemporary of <b>Akbar</b> (not Aurangzeb).</li>
<li><b>Suhrawardi</b>: Multan.</li>
<li><b>Qadiri</b>: introduced by <b>Shah Niamatullah and Makhdum Muhammad Jilani</b>; Dara Shikoh followed it.</li>
<li><b>Naqshbandi</b>: Baqi Billah and <b>Shaikh Ahmad Sirhindi</b> (1564–1624), who lived under Akbar and Jahangir, not Ibrahim Lodi.</li></ul></li>
<li>Terms: khanqah, pir and murid, dargah, urs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Sikh Gurus</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Guru Arjan Dev</b> (5th) succeeded his father <b>Guru Ram Das</b> (4th), compiled the Adi Granth (1604) and built the Harmandir Sahib. <b>Gurmukhi</b> was standardised by Guru Angad (UPSC's key credited Arjan Dev; debated).</li>
<li><b>Banda Bahadur</b> was sent to Punjab by <b>Guru Gobind Singh</b> (1708), not Guru Tegh Bahadur.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("medieval", "d6-1", `
<p>Medieval India had a busy economy of irrigated farming, long-distance trade and credit, described in detail by foreign visitors.</p>

<h3>Technology and irrigation</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>araghatta</b> was a <b>water wheel with earthen pots tied to it</b> (a chain of pots), turned by animals to lift water from wells for irrigation. It is similar to the Persian wheel (saqiya), which spread with gearing in the Sultanate period; Babur describes it.</li>
<li>Other devices: the charas or mot (a leather bag pulled by bullocks over a pulley) and the dhenkli (a lever with a counterweight).</li>
<li>Waterworks: the Chola Grand Anicut; Firuz Tughlaq's canals and irrigation tax (haq-i-sharb); Iltutmish's Hauz-i-Shamsi and Alauddin's Hauz Khas; Vijayanagara's tanks.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Trade and credit</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>hundi</b> was a <b>bill of exchange</b> used by merchants and bankers (sarrafs) to move money and give credit over long distances.</li>
<li>The <b>Banjaras</b> were nomadic <b>traders</b> and carriers who moved grain and salt in huge bullock caravans; Alauddin and the Mughals used them. The British wrongly listed them under the Criminal Tribes Act (1871).</li>
<li>Other trading groups: Banias, Marwaris, Chettis, Bohras, Khojas, Multanis; south Indian guilds such as Manigramam and Ayyavole; the Jagat Seth banking house in Bengal.</li>
<li><b>Surat</b> was called <b>Bab-ul-Makka</b> ("Gate of Mecca") because Hajj pilgrims sailed from it; it was also the leading Mughal port.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Travellers and new crops</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Jean-Baptiste Tavernier</b>, a French gem merchant, described India's <b>diamonds and the Golconda mines</b> (Kollur) in detail and sold the "Tavernier Blue" (later the Hope diamond) to Louis XIV.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Traveller</th><th>Country; ruler</th></tr>
<tr><td>Ralph Fitch</td><td>England; Akbar</td></tr>
<tr><td>William Hawkins; Thomas Roe</td><td>England; Jahangir</td></tr>
<tr><td>Francisco Pelsaert</td><td>Dutch; Jahangir</td></tr>
<tr><td>Peter Mundy</td><td>England; Shah Jahan</td></tr>
<tr><td>François Bernier</td><td>France; Shah Jahan and Aurangzeb</td></tr>
<tr><td>Niccolao Manucci</td><td>Italy; Aurangzeb</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The Portuguese brought New World crops in the 16th–17th centuries: <b>papaya, pineapple and guava</b> (all three), as well as custard apple, sapota, cashew, potato, tomato, chilli, maize, groundnut and tobacco.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("medieval", "d6-2", `
<p>European trading companies arrived from 1498, competing for Indian textiles and spices. The English, with Mughal permission, grew from trading posts into a territorial power.</p>

<h3>The companies in order</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Company</th><th>Arrival</th><th>First factories</th></tr>
<tr><td>Portuguese</td><td>1498 (Vasco da Gama at Calicut)</td><td>Cochin (1503); <b>Goa taken in 1510</b> (not 1499)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dutch</td><td>1602</td><td>Masulipatnam (1605); <b>Pulicat</b> (1610), the earliest among common options</td></tr>
<tr><td>English</td><td>1600 charter</td><td>Masulipatnam (1611); <b>Surat (1613)</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Danish</td><td>1616</td><td>Tranquebar (1620)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>French</b></td><td>1664 (Colbert)</td><td>Surat (1668), Pondicherry (1674); the <b>last to come</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The Portuguese</h3>
<ul>
<li>Almeida's "Blue Water" policy and the Battle of Diu (1509); <b>Albuquerque</b> took Goa (1510), Malacca and Hormuz. The cartaz licence system controlled Asian shipping.</li>
<li><b>Bahadur Shah</b> of Gujarat surrendered <b>Diu</b> to the Portuguese (1535). They also held Daman, Bassein and Hooghly (lost to Shah Jahan, 1632); Bombay went to England as a dowry (1661). Goa, Daman and Diu were freed in 1961.</li>
<li>The English opened their first factory in Orissa in 1633 (Hariharpur). In the Carnatic wars, the French under <b>Dupleix</b> took Madras in 1746.</li>
<li>At <b>Kochi</b> the Portuguese came first (Fort Manuel, 1503); the Dutch took it in 1663 and rebuilt the fort; the British took over in 1795.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The English</h3>
<ul>
<li>After Captain Best defeated the Portuguese at Swally (1612), <b>Jahangir</b> issued a farman (1613) allowing the <b>first English factory in Mughal territory at Surat</b>. The first English factory in India, Masulipatnam (1611), was also in Jahangir's reign.</li>
<li>In the first quarter of the 17th century English factories stood at Surat, Ahmedabad, <b>Broach</b> and Agra. <b>Chicacole</b> and <b>Trichinopoly</b> were not English factories then.</li>
<li><b>Fort St George</b>, Madras (1639–44), was the <b>first English fort</b> in India. Later came Hugli (1651), Bombay (1668) and Calcutta's Fort William (1690s).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
