addBookTopic("ir", "r1-1", `
<p>The United Nations (1945) is the central global body for peace, development and human rights. UPSC asks how its organs are composed, how they decide, and what belongs to the UN system.</p>

<h3>Founding and membership</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Charter</b> was signed at <b>San Francisco</b> on 26 June 1945 (not Geneva) and came into force on 24 October 1945 (UN Day). <b>India</b> was one of the <b>51 original members</b> (1945).</li>
<li>There are 193 members; the latest is South Sudan (2011). New members are admitted by the General Assembly on the Security Council's recommendation; the USA vetoed Palestine's bid in April 2024. Many Western countries (France, UK, Canada, Australia) recognised Palestine in September 2025.</li>
<li>The <b>General Assembly can give observer status</b> to <b>non-member states</b> (the Holy See; Palestine, which gained extra rights in 2024) and to <b>intergovernmental organisations</b> (EU, AU, OIC, ASEAN, ICRC). Permanent observers can keep <b>missions at UN headquarters</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Principal organs</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Organ</th><th>Key facts</th></tr>
<tr><td>General Assembly</td><td>One country, one vote. Important questions (peace and security, budget, admission, elections) need two-thirds. "Uniting for Peace" (1950); since 2022 a veto automatically triggers a GA debate. Six main committees (1st Disarmament … 6th Legal)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security Council</td><td>5 permanent members with veto; <b>10 non-permanent members elected for two years</b> by two-thirds of the GA (five a year, by region; no immediate re-election)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>ECOSOC</b></td><td><b>54 members</b> (not 24; 18 in 1945, 27 in 1965, 54 in 1973), elected by the GA by <b>two-thirds for three-year terms</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Trusteeship Council</b></td><td>Set up to supervise trust territories: former mandates and <b>territories detached from Japan and Italy</b> after the war; suspended in 1994 after Palau's independence</td></tr>
<tr><td>ICJ; Secretariat</td><td>The Hague; New York</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Credentials Committee</b> is a <b>General Assembly</b> committee of nine members (not a Security Council body). It meets usually once late in the year (not in March, June and September) and <b>examines the credentials of all members' representatives</b> before reporting to the GA. It has deferred rival claims for Afghanistan and Myanmar since 2021; in 1971 (Resolution 2758) the PRC replaced Taiwan.</li>
<li><b>Kofi Annan</b> (1938–2018) was from <b>Ghana</b>, not Nigeria. He was the <b>first Secretary-General from the UN's own staff</b>, served <b>two terms</b> (1997–2006) and shared the 2001 Nobel with the UN. The next Secretary-General takes office on 1 January 2027; Security Council straw polls began in July 2026.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What is and is not in the UN system</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>IBRD, IFC, IFAD and IMF</b> are all <b>UN specialised agencies</b> (by relationship agreements). The World Bank Group also includes IDA, MIGA (investment guarantees) and ICSID (investment disputes).</li>
<li>The <b>Bank for International Settlements</b> (1930, Basel) is <b>not</b> related to the UN: central banks own it, the RBI joined in 1996, and it hosts the Basel Committee.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ir", "r1-2", `
<p>The UN works through specialised agencies, funds and programmes, each with its own headquarters and mandate. Several have won the Nobel Peace Prize.</p>

<h3>Headquarters</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Body</th><th>Headquarters</th></tr>
<tr><td>UNDP</td><td>New York</td></tr>
<tr><td>UNEP (1972); UN-Habitat</td><td>Nairobi</td></tr>
<tr><td>UNIDO; IAEA</td><td>Vienna</td></tr>
<tr><td>UPU (1874)</td><td>Berne</td></tr>
<tr><td>ITU (1865, oldest); WIPO; ILO; WHO</td><td>Geneva</td></tr>
<tr><td>FAO, WFP, IFAD</td><td>Rome</td></tr>
<tr><td>UNOPS</td><td>Copenhagen</td></tr>
<tr><td>OECD; European Space Agency</td><td>Paris (ESA launches from Kourou)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Council of the EU</td><td>Brussels</td></tr>
<tr><td>ADB</td><td><b>Manila</b> (president always Japanese), not Tokyo</td></tr>
<tr><td>APEC Secretariat</td><td><b>Singapore</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>ASEAN Secretariat</td><td><b>Jakarta</b> (founded at Bangkok, 1967)</td></tr>
<tr><td>OIC</td><td><b>Jeddah</b> (founded at Rabat, 1969; renamed Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, 2011; 57 members)</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
<tr><th>Organisation</th><th>Link</th></tr>
<tr><td>Amnesty International</td><td>Founded by <b>Peter Benenson</b> (1961); London; Nobel 1977</td></tr>
<tr><td>CIS</td><td>Belovezha Accords signed at <b>Viskuli</b>, Belarus (1991); HQ Minsk</td></tr>
<tr><td>Danube Commission</td><td><b>Belgrade Convention</b> (1948); HQ Budapest</td></tr>
<tr><td>European Space Agency</td><td><b>Paris</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Agencies and what they do</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>ILO</b> (1919, Geneva; tripartite; Nobel 1969). Conventions <b>138 and 182</b> concern <b>child labour</b>: C138 is the minimum age and C182 the worst forms (in 2020 the first ILO convention ratified by every member). India ratified both in 2017 after the Child and Adolescent Labour Act 2016; it has not ratified C87 and C98 (freedom of association, collective bargaining).</li>
<li><b>UN-Habitat</b> (1978, Nairobi) is mandated by the General Assembly to promote <b>sustainable towns and cities with adequate shelter for all</b>, and it contributes to <b>poverty reduction, safe water and sanitation</b>. Its partners include NGOs, academia and the private sector, not only governments. Habitat III at Quito (2016) adopted the New Urban Agenda; WUF13 is in Baku (2026).</li>
<li><b>UNOPS</b>' <b>S3i</b> initiative invested in <b>affordable housing, renewable energy and health care</b>, not mass rapid transport. It was wound down after a financial scandal (2022–23).</li>
<li><b>WIPO</b> (1967) is a UN specialised agency (since 1974) based in <b>Geneva</b>, not Rome. <b>TRIPS</b> binds all WTO members; LDCs got 11 years (since extended to 2034), not 20.</li>
<li>The WTO's SPS Agreement uses <b>Codex Alimentarius</b> standards as the reference for food safety in disputes.</li>
<li>The <b>Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action</b> (Fourth World Conference on Women, 1995) is an agenda for women's empowerment with 12 critical areas; Beijing+30 was in 2025. Related: CEDAW (1979; India ratified 1993), UN Women (2010), SDG 5.</li>
</ul>

<h3>UN and the Nobel Peace Prize</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Laureate</th><th>Year(s)</th></tr>
<tr><td>ICRC</td><td>1917, 1944, 1963 (three times)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>UNHCR</b></td><td><b>1954, 1981</b> (the only UN agency to win twice)</td></tr>
<tr><td>UNICEF; ILO</td><td>1965; 1969</td></tr>
<tr><td>UN Peacekeeping; UN and Kofi Annan</td><td>1988; 2001</td></tr>
<tr><td>IAEA (ElBaradei); IPCC (with Al Gore)</td><td>2005; 2007</td></tr>
<tr><td>OPCW; WFP</td><td>2013; 2020</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nihon Hidankyo; María Corina Machado</td><td>2024; 2025</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Peacekeeping</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Mission</th><th>Country</th><th>Period</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>UNMISET</b></td><td>Timor-Leste</td><td>2002–2005</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>UNMIL</b></td><td>Liberia (India's first all-women police unit, 2007)</td><td>2003–2018</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>MINUSTAH</b></td><td>Haiti</td><td>2004–2017</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>MINURCAT</b></td><td>Central African Republic and Chad</td><td>2007–2010</td></tr>
</table>
<p>India is among the largest contributors (about 2.9 lakh personnel since 1950, and the most fatalities). Its first mission was Korea (1950–54); Capt. G.S. Salaria won the Param Vir Chakra in Congo (1961). The Centre for UN Peacekeeping is in New Delhi; Peacekeepers' Day is 29 May.</p>
`);

addBookTopic("ir", "r2-1", `
<p>World trade rules are set at the WTO, but countries also make regional and bilateral deals. The European Union's single market and fiscal rules are a recurring topic.</p>

<h3>The WTO</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>GATT</b> (1947) was replaced by the <b>WTO</b> on 1 January 1995 after the Uruguay Round (Marrakesh, 1994).</li>
<li>The <b>Seattle ministerial</b> (December 1999) failed partly over attempts to link trade with <b>labour</b> and <b>environment</b> standards, not terrorism or debt.</li>
<li>Doha Round terms:
<ul><li><b>NAMA-11</b>: developing countries (India, Brazil, South Africa and others) seeking flexibility in industrial tariff cuts (Non-Agricultural Market Access).</li>
<li><b>Special Safeguard Mechanism</b>: temporary farm tariffs during import surges; the India–US dispute over it collapsed the July 2008 talks.</li></ul></li>
<li>The <b>Trade Facilitation Agreement</b> came from the <b>Bali package</b> (2013). India ratified it in April 2016, and it <b>entered into force on 22 February 2017</b> (not January 2016). It was the first multilateral deal since 1995.</li>
<li>Ministerials: Nairobi (2015, export subsidies banned), Geneva (MC12, 2022, fisheries subsidies), Abu Dhabi (MC13, 2024), Yaoundé (MC14, March 2026: no declaration; the e-commerce duty moratorium lapsed).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Regional trade agreements</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>ASEAN's FTA partners</b> are <b>China, Japan, South Korea, India, and Australia–New Zealand</b>; not Canada (talks began 2021) or the USA.</li>
<li><b>RCEP</b> is centred on <b>ASEAN</b>: the 10 members plus China, Japan, South Korea, Australia and New Zealand. Signed in 2020 and in force in 2022, it is the largest trade bloc. India withdrew in November 2019 over the Chinese import threat, dairy and rules of origin.</li>
<li>The <b>Trans-Pacific Partnership</b> (2016) had only 12 countries, not every Pacific Rim country except China and Russia, and it was a trade agreement, not a maritime alliance. After the USA left (2017) it became the CPTPP (2018); the UK joined in 2024.</li>
<li><b>SAFTA</b> (signed January 2004) came into force on <b>1 January 2006</b>, not December 2005. Non-LDCs had to cut tariffs to 0–5% within seven years (Sri Lanka eight), so lumping India, Pakistan and Sri Lanka together to 2013 was marked wrong.</li>
<li>South Asian trade: <b>Bangladesh</b> is India's largest trading partner in the region (not Nepal), and <b>textiles</b> are a major item of trade with it. India–Sri Lanka trade has fluctuated rather than risen steadily.</li>
<li>The <b>BTIA</b> (Broad-based Trade and Investment Agreement) was the name for India–<b>EU</b> FTA talks (2007–13). Talks were relaunched in 2022 and the FTA was concluded on 27 January 2026.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>India's recent trade deals</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>UAE CEPA</td><td>2022</td></tr>
<tr><td>Australia ECTA</td><td>2022</td></tr>
<tr><td>EFTA TEPA</td><td>Signed 2024; in force October 2025</td></tr>
<tr><td>UK CETA</td><td>Signed July 2025; in force 15 July 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Oman CEPA</td><td>Signed December 2025; in force 1 June 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>EU FTA</td><td>Concluded January 2026</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The European Union's economic rules</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Stability and Growth Pact</b> (1997) <b>limits budget deficits</b> (3% of GDP) and debt (60%), from the Maastricht criteria. It does not deal with sharing infrastructure or technology. It was suspended in 2020–23 and reformed in 2024.</li>
<li>The <b>European Stability Mechanism</b> (2012, Luxembourg) is the eurozone's fund that <b>lends to member states in financial trouble</b> (for example, Greece's 2015 bailout). The eurozone has 20 members, with Bulgaria joining in 2026.</li>
<li>The <b>Digital Single Market Strategy</b> (2015) belongs to the <b>EU</b>. The EU's <b>GDPR</b> was adopted in April 2016 and applied from 25 May 2018 (fines up to 4% of global turnover). Other laws: Digital Services Act and Digital Markets Act (2022), AI Act (2024). India's equivalent is the DPDP Act 2023 (rules notified November 2025).</li>
<li>The <b>European Commission</b> negotiates trade deals and proposes laws. The <b>European Council</b> is the heads of state or government; it sets direction but does not legislate. Laws are made by the Council of the EU and the European Parliament. Both UPSC statements on this were wrong.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ir", "r2-2", `
<p>Beyond the UN, informal groupings (G7, G20, BRICS) and development banks shape the world economy. India is a member of many but not all.</p>

<h3>G7, G20 and BRICS</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>G7</b>: USA, UK, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and Canada (and the EU). <b>Russia</b> was not an original member; it joined in 1997–98 to make the G8 and was suspended in 2014.</li>
<li>The <b>G20</b> began in 1999 as a forum of <b>finance ministers and central bank governors</b>, with leaders' summits since 2008. Members are 19 countries, the EU and the African Union (from 2023), including <b>Argentina, Mexico, South Africa and Turkey</b>; not Malaysia, New Zealand, Iran, Vietnam or Singapore.</li>
<li>India's 2023 presidency ("Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam") had <b>Digital Public Infrastructure</b> among its priorities. Outcomes: AU membership, the Global Biofuels Alliance, the IMEC corridor. The USA holds the 2026 presidency (Miami summit; South Africa excluded) and the UK the 2027 one.</li>
<li><b>BRICS</b>: the first summit was at <b>Yekaterinburg</b> (2009), not Rio. <b>South Africa joined last</b> (2010–11) as of 2014; since 2024 Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, the UAE and Indonesia have joined. The <b>Fortaleza Declaration</b> (2014) created the <b>New Development Bank</b> and the Contingent Reserve Arrangement. India chaired BRICS in 2026 (New Delhi summit, September 2026).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Development banks</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Bank</th><th>HQ, year</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>AIIB</b></td><td>Beijing, 2016</td><td><b>More than 80 members</b> (111 now) including non-Asian ones such as the UK and Germany. <b>China</b> is the largest shareholder (about 26.5% of votes), India second (7.6%) and the largest borrower. Zou Jiayi became president in January 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>New Development Bank</b></td><td><b>Shanghai</b>, 2015</td><td>Set up by <b>BRICS</b>, not APEC; equal founder shares; K.V. Kamath first, Dilma Rousseff now</td></tr>
<tr><td>ADB</td><td>Manila, 1966</td><td>India a founding member</td></tr>
<tr><td>World Bank</td><td>Washington, 1944</td><td>Ajay Banga (2023)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>India's memberships</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Member</th><th>Not a member</th></tr>
<tr><td>AIIB, NDB, <b>ADB</b>, <b>Colombo Plan</b> (1950), IMF, World Bank; <b>MTCR</b> (2016), Wassenaar (2017), Australia Group (2018); <b>SCO</b> (2017), BRICS, G20, Quad, <b>East Asia Summit</b>, ARF, BIMSTEC, IORA</td><td><b>APEC</b>, <b>ASEAN</b> (a dialogue partner only), <b>OECD</b> (a key partner), NSG, NPT, RCEP, CPTPP, the Belt and Road Initiative</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The SCO's 2026 summit was at Bishkek; India again did not endorse the Belt and Road paragraph.</p>

<h3>Development partnership</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Indian-supported project</th><th>Country</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mangdechhu</b> hydroelectric project (720 MW, 2019)</td><td>Bhutan</td></tr>
<tr><td>Restoration of the <b>Stor Palace</b>, Kabul (2016)</td><td>Afghanistan</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>District Hospital, Dickoya</b> (2019)</td><td>Sri Lanka</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Institute of Security and Law Enforcement Studies</b> (2023)</td><td>Maldives</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Tools: lines of credit, grants, ITEC training (1964). Bhutan receives the largest grant share; policies are "Neighbourhood First" and SAGAR/MAHASAGAR.</p>

<h3>Forums and indices</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>World Economic Forum</b> was founded by <b>Klaus Schwab</b> (1971); it is based near Geneva and meets each January at <b>Davos, Switzerland</b>. It gives the <b>Crystal Award</b> to cultural leaders (A.R. Rahman, Shah Rukh Khan, Deepika Padukone).</li>
<li>The <b>Stiglitz Commission</b> (2009), set up by the UN General Assembly President, studied <b>the workings of the global financial system</b> after the crisis.</li>
<li>The <b>Rule of Law Index</b> is published by the <b>World Justice Project</b> (India 79th). Other indices:
<ul><li>Corruption Perceptions Index: Transparency International.</li>
<li>World Press Freedom Index: Reporters Without Borders.</li>
<li>Democracy Index: Economist Intelligence Unit.</li>
<li>Freedom in the World: Freedom House.</li>
<li>Global Peace Index: Institute for Economics and Peace.</li>
<li>Global Hunger Index: Concern Worldwide and Welthungerhilfe.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
`);
;
// data/book_ir_r3.js
// IR book, Units 3–4: regional groupings; security, disarmament and international law.
addBookTopic("ir", "r3-1", `
<p>Asia's regional groupings overlap: ASEAN is at the centre, while South Asia has SAARC and BIMSTEC and the Indian Ocean has its own forums. India uses them for its "Neighbourhood First" and "Act East" policies.</p>

<h3>ASEAN and Southeast Asia</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>ASEAN</b> (Bangkok Declaration, 1967; secretariat Jakarta). Brunei joined in 1984, and Laos and Myanmar in 1997. <b>Timor-Leste</b> became the 11th member in October 2025; it was not a member in 2009. <b>Bangladesh</b> and <b>China</b> are not members: China is a dialogue partner (ASEAN+3, EAS, RCEP), and Bangladesh is in the ASEAN Regional Forum.</li>
<li><b>Mekong-Ganga Cooperation</b> (Vientiane, 2000) began as a <b>joint tourism (and cultural) initiative</b>. It has six members: India, Thailand, Myanmar, Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam. <b>Bangladesh</b> and <b>China</b> are not members; China leads the separate Lancang-Mekong Cooperation (2016).</li>
</ul>

<h3>South Asia: SAARC and BIMSTEC</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>SAARC</b> (1985, Dhaka; secretariat Kathmandu; 8 members). China (with Japan) became an <b>observer</b> in 2005; other observers are Australia, the EU, Iran, Mauritius, Myanmar, South Korea and the USA. No summit has been held since 2014.</li>
<li><b>BIMSTEC</b>:
<ul><li>It was founded on 6 June 1997 by the <b>Bangkok Declaration</b> (not Dhaka, 1999).</li>
<li>Its founders were Bangladesh, India, Sri Lanka and Thailand; Myanmar joined in 1997, and Nepal and Bhutan in 2004.</li>
<li>It has <b>seven members</b>; its secretariat is in Dhaka and its Charter came into force in 2024.</li>
<li><b>Nepal</b> leads people-to-people contact, which includes <b>tourism</b>; India leads security (including energy and disaster management).</li>
<li>The 6th summit (Bangkok, April 2025) adopted Bangkok Vision 2030.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>BIMSTEC centre</th><th>Location</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cultural Industries Observatory</td><td>Thimphu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Energy Centre</td><td>Bengaluru</td></tr>
<tr><td>Centre for Weather and Climate</td><td>NOIDA (at NCMRWF)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Technology Transfer Facility</td><td>Colombo</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>SCO and the Indian Ocean</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>SCO</b>: in 2007 its members were China, Russia, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan and Uzbekistan; <b>Ukraine</b> was never a member. India was an <b>observer</b> from 2005 and became a full member in 2017; Iran joined in 2023 and Belarus in 2024.</li>
<li><b>IORA</b> (formerly IOR-ARC) was set up in <b>1997</b> (Mauritius) for <b>economic cooperation</b>. It was not a recent response to piracy and is not a security alliance. It has 23 members and its secretariat is at Ebene, Mauritius.</li>
<li><b>IONS</b> (Indian Ocean Naval Symposium) began in <b>2008</b> in New Delhi, not 2015. It is a <b>voluntary forum for navies</b> of Indian Ocean littoral states.</li>
<li>Others: the Colombo Security Conclave, the Information Fusion Centre-IOR (Gurugram), and SAGAR (2015) and MAHASAGAR (2025). Naval exercises: Malabar, MILAN, Varuna, SIMBEX, JIMEX.</li>
<li><b>APMCHUD</b> (Asia Pacific Ministerial Conference on Housing and Urban Development) first met in New Delhi in 2006, but the theme in the question belonged to its 6th meeting (2016). Hosting rotates, so India does not host it every year with ADB, APEC and ASEAN; both statements were wrong.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ir", "r3-2", `
<p>Beyond Asia, UPSC asks about the European Union and its neighbours, the Gulf, the Commonwealth, NATO and the polar regions.</p>

<h3>Europe</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>European Union</b>: from the ECSC (1951) and EEC (Treaty of Rome, 1957) to Maastricht (1992) and Lisbon (2009). It has 27 members since Brexit (2020); Croatia joined last (2013).
<ul><li><b>Germany</b> is a founder and <b>Poland</b> joined in 2004.</li>
<li><b>Malta</b> joined in 2004, and <b>Bulgaria and Romania</b> in January 2007.</li>
<li><b>Switzerland</b> (EFTA and Schengen, but not EU) and <b>Belarus</b> are not members.</li></ul>
The eurozone had 21 members after Bulgaria joined on 1 January 2026. Schengen has 29 members, including non-EU Switzerland and Norway. Institutions: the Commission, Parliament, Council of the EU, European Council, Court of Justice (Luxembourg) and ECB (Frankfurt).</li>
<li>The <b>CIS</b> (1991) never included the <b>Baltic states</b> (Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania), which joined NATO and the EU in 2004.</li>
<li>The <b>Nordic Council</b> (1952): Denmark, Finland, Iceland, Norway and Sweden (with the Faroes, Greenland and Åland); not the UK.</li>
<li><b>NATO</b> (1949) had 12 founders including the <b>USA and UK</b>. The NACC (1991) was a dialogue forum with former Warsaw Pact states; it did not replace NATO (and became the Euro-Atlantic Partnership Council in 1997).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Gulf and OPEC</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Gulf Cooperation Council</b> (1981, Riyadh) has six members: Saudi Arabia, UAE, Qatar, Kuwait, Bahrain and Oman; not <b>Iran</b>. The Qatar blockade ran from 2017 to 2021 (Al-Ula Declaration). About 90 lakh Indians live in the GCC; India–GCC FTA talks are under way.</li>
<li><b>OPEC</b>: in 2009 <b>Ecuador</b> was a South American member besides Venezuela; it left in 2020. The UAE left OPEC on 1 May 2026, leaving 11 members.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Commonwealth</h3>
<ul>
<li>In 2010 the Commonwealth had <b>no charter</b>; one was adopted in 2012. Membership is <b>voluntary</b>, not automatic for former colonies: Myanmar never joined, Ireland left in 1949, and Mozambique, Rwanda, Gabon and Togo joined without British ties.</li>
<li><b>Fiji</b> was suspended after its December 2006 coup and readmitted in 2014.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Other groupings and summits</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>APEC</b> (1989, 21 Pacific Rim economies; India not a member) held its 2001 meeting in <b>Shanghai</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The polar regions</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Arctic Council</b> (1996) has eight members: <b>Canada, Denmark</b> (Greenland), Finland, Iceland, Norway, <b>Russia</b>, Sweden and the <b>USA</b>. <b>Japan</b>, the <b>UK</b> and India (since 2013) are observers. Indigenous organisations are Permanent Participants. Denmark chairs it for 2025–27.</li>
<li>The <b>Polar Code</b> (IMO, in force 2017) is a mandatory code for <b>ships operating in Arctic and Antarctic waters</b>, under SOLAS and MARPOL.</li>
<li>Antarctica: the Antarctic Treaty (1959) and Madrid Protocol (1991, mining ban); India joined in 1983 and passed the Indian Antarctic Act 2022. Its stations are Maitri and Bharati, and it hosted the 2024 consultative meeting in Kochi.</li>
<li>Arctic: India's Himadri station on Svalbard (2008) and Arctic Policy (2022). The Svalbard Treaty (1920) was signed by British India.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ir", "r4-1", `
<p>Treaties and export-control groups try to stop the spread of nuclear, chemical and biological weapons. India stays outside the NPT but has joined most other regimes.</p>

<h3>The NPT and the IAEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>NPT</b> (1968, in force 1970) recognises as nuclear-weapon states only those that tested before 1 January 1967: <b>USA, Russia, UK, France and China</b>. India, Pakistan and Israel are not parties; North Korea withdrew in 2003. The 2026 Review Conference again failed to adopt a final document.</li>
<li>The <b>IAEA</b> (1957, Vienna; Rafael Grossi; Nobel 2005). By ratifying the <b>Additional Protocol</b> (2014), India placed its <b>civilian reactors under IAEA safeguards</b> with wider access; military facilities stay outside. India is a founding member.</li>
<li>The <b>Nuclear Security Summits</b> (2010–16) were a US initiative, <b>not UN-led</b>. The <b>International Panel on Fissile Materials</b> is an independent expert group at Princeton, <b>not an IAEA organ</b>. Other instruments: CPPNM, the nuclear terrorism convention (2005), UNSC Resolution 1540, CTBT (not in force; India has not signed).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Export-control regimes</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Regime</th><th>Covers</th><th>India</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Nuclear Suppliers Group</b> (1974, after Pokhran; 48 members, not 24)</td><td>Nuclear exports</td><td><b>Not a member</b> (China blocks); one-time waiver in 2008</td></tr>
<tr><td>MTCR</td><td>Missiles and drones</td><td>Joined 2016</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Wassenaar Arrangement</b> (1996)</td><td><b>Conventional arms and dual-use goods</b>; not under the OECD</td><td>Joined 2017</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Australia Group</b> (1985)</td><td><b>Chemical and biological weapons</b>; informal, mainly European and Western members</td><td>Joined 2018</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><b>NSG membership</b> gives <b>access to the latest nuclear technology</b> and trade, but it does <b>not</b> make a country an NPT party.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Other treaties and bodies</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>New START</b> (2010; in force 2011) is a <b>bilateral US–Russia</b> strategic arms treaty: 1,550 deployed warheads and 700 deployed launchers each. Russia suspended its participation in 2023, and the treaty expired on 5 February 2026.</li>
<li>The <b>OPCW</b> (1997, The Hague; Nobel 2013) implements the Chemical Weapons Convention. It is <b>independent, not an EU organ</b>. It <b>monitors the chemical industry</b> and <b>helps states threatened by chemical weapons</b>. All declared stockpiles were destroyed by 2023; India destroyed its own by 2009.</li>
<li><b>Agent Orange</b> was a <b>herbicide and defoliant</b> used by the US military in Vietnam (1962–71), contaminated with dioxin.</li>
<li>The <b>Pugwash Conferences</b> began in 1957 at <b>Pugwash, Nova Scotia</b>, after the Russell–Einstein Manifesto; they shared the 1995 Nobel with Joseph Rotblat.</li>
<li><b>ITER</b> was signed in 2006 by the EU, India, China, Japan, South Korea, Russia and the USA. <b>Canada</b> withdrew in 2003.</li>
<li>India–Russia: the <b>Action Plan for Prioritization and Implementation of Cooperation Areas in the Nuclear Field</b> (2018) covers Kudankulam and new units, and third-country projects such as Rooppur (Bangladesh). Other partners: the USA (123 Agreement, 2008), France (Jaitapur) and Japan (2016).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ir", "r4-2", `
<p>International law covers the sea, crime, migration and human rights. UPSC also asks about non-state bodies that work across borders.</p>

<h3>Law of the Sea</h3>
<p>UNCLOS (Montego Bay, 1982; in force 1994; India ratified in 1995; the USA has not):</p>
<ul>
<li>A coastal state may fix its <b>territorial sea up to 12 nautical miles</b> (Article 3).</li>
<li>Ships of <b>all states, coastal or land-locked</b>, have <b>innocent passage</b> through it (Article 17).</li>
<li>The <b>EEZ may not exceed 200 nautical miles</b> (Article 57).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Zone</th><th>Extent and rights</th></tr>
<tr><td>Territorial sea</td><td>Up to 12 nm: sovereignty, subject to innocent passage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contiguous zone</td><td>Up to 24 nm: customs, fiscal, immigration, sanitary laws</td></tr>
<tr><td>EEZ</td><td>Up to 200 nm: rights over resources; others keep navigation and overflight</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continental shelf</td><td>200 nm, up to 350 nm if the shelf extends</td></tr>
<tr><td>High seas</td><td>Free for all; the seabed is managed by the International Seabed Authority</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Disputes go to ITLOS (Hamburg), the ICJ or arbitration (the 2016 South China Sea award). The BBNJ "High Seas Treaty" entered into force on 17 January 2026; India has signed it.</p>

<h3>Crime and corruption</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>UNCAC</b> (2003, in force 2005; India ratified in 2011) is the <b>first legally binding global anti-corruption instrument</b>, and its <b>asset recovery</b> chapter is a highlight.</li>
<li>The <b>Protocol against the Smuggling of Migrants</b> belongs to <b>UNTOC</b> (Palermo, 2000), not UNCAC.</li>
<li><b>UNODC</b> (Vienna) assists with both conventions. FATF placed India in "regular follow-up" (2024).</li>
<li><b>INTERPOL</b> (1923; Lyon; 196 members; India joined in 1949, and the CBI is India's National Central Bureau with the Bharatpol portal):</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Notice</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td>Red</td><td>Locate and provisionally arrest a wanted person</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yellow</td><td>Missing persons</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Blue</b></td><td><b>Collect information</b> on a person's identity, location or activities</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Black</b></td><td><b>Unidentified bodies</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Green</b></td><td><b>Warn</b> about a person who is a possible threat to public safety</td></tr>
<tr><td>Orange; Purple</td><td>Imminent threat; criminals' methods</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Silver</b> (2025)</td><td><b>Trace criminal assets</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Migration</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Global Compact for Safe, Orderly and Regular Migration</b> (Marrakech, 2018) was <b>not adopted by all countries</b> (152 for, 5 against), is <b>non-binding</b>, and covers <b>only international</b> migration, not internal displacement. India voted for it. A separate Global Compact on Refugees was also adopted; India is not a party to the 1951 Refugee Convention.</li>
<li>Forums:
<ul><li>The <b>Colombo Process</b> (2003) is a voluntary, <b>non-binding</b> forum of Asian origin countries, so its decisions are not binding.</li>
<li>The <b>Abu Dhabi Dialogue</b> (2008) is a voluntary, non-binding forum of origin and destination countries for contractual labour.</li>
<li>The <b>Global Forum on Migration and Development</b> (2007) was proposed by Kofi Annan and is voluntary and non-binding.</li></ul>
India has the world's largest diaspora and remittances (about $135 billion in 2024–25). The Emigration Act 1983 is to be replaced; Pravasi Bharatiya Divas is on 9 January.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Conventions India has and has not ratified</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Ratified</th><th>Not ratified</th></tr>
<tr><td>ICCPR, ICESCR, CEDAW, CRC, CRPD, Genocide Convention, the four <b>Geneva Conventions</b> (1950), UNCAC, UNTOC; ILO C29, <b>C105</b> (Abolition of Forced Labour), C100, C111, C138, C182, <b>C122</b> (Employment Policy)</td><td>1951 Refugee Convention; <b>Statelessness</b> Conventions (1954, 1961); <b>Migrant Workers</b> Convention (1990); Convention against Torture (signed only); Rome Statute; Ottawa landmine treaty; NPT, CTBT; Geneva Additional Protocols; ILO C87, C98</td></tr>
</table>
<p>UPSC dropped its 2026 question because the correct combination (the migrant workers and statelessness conventions) was not among the options.</p>
<ul>
<li>Agreements and their subjects:
<ul><li>The <b>Alma-Ata Declaration</b> (1978) is about <b>health care</b> ("Health for All").</li>
<li>The <b>Talanoa Dialogue</b> (2018) is about <b>climate change</b>.</li>
<li>The <b>Hague Conventions</b> dealt with the laws of war and later child abduction, not biological and chemical weapons, which come under the BWC and CWC.</li>
<li>The <b>Under2 Coalition</b> is a climate group of sub-national governments, not about child rights.</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Non-state and other bodies</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Médecins Sans Frontières</b> (Doctors Without Borders; France, 1971; Nobel 1999) is a <b>non-governmental organisation</b> for humanitarian medical aid. (Not to be confused with WADA, the anti-doping agency set up by the IOC.)</li>
<li><b>Amnesty International</b> (London, 1961, Peter Benenson; Nobel 1977) is a <b>global human rights movement</b>; Amnesty India stopped work in 2020.</li>
<li>The <b>World Toilet Organization</b> (Singapore, 2001, Jack Sim) is a non-profit, <b>not a UN agency</b>, and it does not fund countries. It runs the <b>World Toilet Summit, World Toilet Day</b> (19 November, adopted by the UN in 2013) and the <b>World Toilet College</b>. India declared itself open-defecation-free in 2019 under the Swachh Bharat Mission.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Intelligence agency</th><th>Country</th></tr>
<tr><td>Foreign Intelligence Service (SVR)</td><td>Russia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ministry of State Security</td><td>China</td></tr>
<tr><td>Secret Intelligence Service (MI6)</td><td>Britain</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mossad</td><td>Israel</td></tr>
</table>
`);
;
// data/book_ir_r5.js
// IR book, Units 5–6: India's foreign relations; world regions and events.
addBookTopic("ir", "r5-1", `
<p>India's foreign policy rests on "Neighbourhood First", "Act East" and connectivity projects that bypass hostile routes. Many questions test where a project runs and whom it links.</p>

<h3>Non-alignment and early policy</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Bandung</b> Asian–African Conference (April 1955, <b>Indonesia</b>; Sukarno, Nehru, Nasser, Zhou Enlai) drew on Panchsheel and led to NAM (Belgrade, 1961).</li>
<li>The NAM chair is the head of the host country. After the 7th summit in New Delhi (1983), President <b>Zail Singh</b> served as NAM chair ("Secretary-General") from 1983 to 1986.</li>
<li>Vajpayee's <i>New Dimensions of India's Foreign Policy</i> (1979) collects his speeches as External Affairs Minister.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Neighbours</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pakistan</b>:
<ul><li>Under the <b>Simla Agreement</b> (1972), Indira Gandhi and Zulfikar Bhutto agreed to respect the <b>LoC</b>.</li>
<li>The <b>Lahore Summit</b> (Vajpayee–Nawaz Sharif) was in <b>1999</b>, not 1997.</li>
<li>Rajiv Gandhi met <b>Benazir Bhutto</b>, not Nawaz Sharif, in Islamabad (1988).</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Sri Lanka</b>: <b>Elephant Pass</b> links the Jaffna peninsula to the mainland and was a battle site in the war with the LTTE (recaptured in 2009).</li>
<li><b>China</b>:
<ul><li><b>Nathu La</b> (Sikkim) reopened for border trade in July 2006, 44 years after 1962.</li>
<li><b>Hand-in-Hand</b> is the India–China counter-terror army exercise (first held at Kunming, 2007).</li>
<li>Vajpayee offered (2003) to build a Buddhist temple at the White Horse Temple, Luoyang; it opened in 2010.</li>
<li>The <b>Stilwell Road</b> (1940s) runs from <b>Ledo (Assam) through Myanmar to Kunming</b>.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Russia</b>: the <b>Delhi Declaration</b> (December 2002) was signed with <b>President Putin</b>.</li>
<li><b>Israel</b>: India <b>established full diplomatic relations in 1992</b> under Narasimha Rao, having recognised Israel in 1950. <b>Ariel Sharon</b> (2003) was the <b>first</b> Israeli PM to visit India, not the second. Modi visited Israel in 2017.</li>
<li>Elsewhere: India gave Tajikistan $5 million for <b>drought relief</b> (2001). ONGC Mittal Energy's $6 billion "infrastructure-for-oil" deal was with <b>Nigeria</b>. <b>Operation Sukoon</b> (2006) <b>evacuated Indians from Lebanon</b> by sea to Cyprus.</li>
<li>Recent change in the neighbourhood (2026): the BNP won Bangladesh's February election and Tarique Rahman became PM. In Nepal, after the 2025 Gen-Z uprising, Balendra Shah became PM in March 2026.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Cross-border link</th><th>Connects</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Maitri Setu</b> (Feni river, 2021)</td><td><b>Sabroom (Tripura)</b> with <b>Ramgarh (Bangladesh)</b>, not the reverse; gives access to Chittagong</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Jhulaghat</b> suspension bridge (Mahakali)</td><td>Pithoragarh with <b>Nepal</b>, not Myanmar</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mechi bridge</b></td><td><b>Panitanki</b> (West Bengal) with <b>Kakarvitta</b> (Nepal)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Agartala–Akhaura</b> rail (2023)</td><td>India with <b>Bangladesh</b> (not an ASEAN link)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Motihari–Amlekhgunj pipeline (2019); Jaynagar–Kurtha rail (2022)</td><td>India with Nepal</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Act East and Southeast Asia</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Look East</b> (1991, Narasimha Rao) aimed to make India a <b>regional player in East Asia</b> and to <b>restore historical and cultural ties</b>, not to fill the post-Cold War vacuum. It became <b>Act East</b> in 2014. India–ASEAN ties became a Comprehensive Strategic Partnership in 2022.</li>
<li>Southeast Asia's lasting importance comes from its <b>location between the Pacific and Indian Oceans</b> and its maritime character (the Malacca, Sunda and Lombok straits).</li>
<li>Projects with ASEAN members:
<ul><li>The <b>Kaladan Multi-Modal</b> project: Kolkata to Sittwe (Myanmar) to Paletwa to Mizoram.</li>
<li>The <b>India–Myanmar–Thailand Trilateral Highway</b>: <b>Moreh to Mae Sot</b>, not Chiang Mai.</li></ul></li>
<li>Other corridors:
<ul><li>The <b>East-West Corridor</b> runs <b>Silchar to Porbandar</b> under the NHDP; it is not part of the Golden Quadrilateral and does not join Dibrugarh and Surat.</li>
<li>The stalled <b>BCIM</b> corridor would link <b>Kolkata</b>, not Varanasi, with Kunming.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Vietnam</b> is one of the <b>fastest-growing economies</b>, driven by <b>global supply chains and exports</b>, <b>low labour costs and stable exchange rates</b>. It is a <b>one-party</b> state, and the claim about the "most productive e-services" was wrong. India–Vietnam is a Comprehensive Strategic Partnership (2016), and India helped restore the My Son temples.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Westward connectivity</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>INSTC</b> (2000; founders India, Iran and Russia) connects <b>India to Central Asia and Europe via Iran</b>: Mumbai to Bandar Abbas or Chabahar, the Caspian, Russia. It has 13 members.</li>
<li><b>Chabahar</b> (Iran, Gulf of Oman) means India <b>need not depend on Pakistan</b> to reach Afghanistan and Central Asia. IPGL signed a 10-year terminal contract in 2024; the US sanctions waiver lapsed in April 2026. It is a counter to Gwadar.</li>
<li>China's <b>Belt and Road Initiative</b> (2013) is opposed by India because CPEC passes through PoK, and because of debt traps (Hambantota). Alternatives: IMEC (2023), INSTC, the Ashgabat Agreement (India joined 2018), and the Chennai–Vladivostok corridor.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ir", "r6-1", `
<p>West Asia's conflicts and Africa's civil wars and crises appear often in older and recent papers.</p>

<h3>Israel, Palestine and the Arab world</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Israel has diplomatic ties with some Arab states</b>: Egypt (1979), Jordan (1994), and the UAE, Bahrain, Morocco and Sudan under the Abraham Accords (2020). The <b>Arab Peace Initiative</b> (Saudi-proposed, Arab League, Beirut 2002) was an Arab offer <b>never signed by Israel</b>.</li>
<li>The <b>two-state solution</b> concerns <b>Israel and Palestine</b>: a Palestinian state on the 1967 lines with East Jerusalem as its capital (Oslo Accords, 1993). After 7 October 2023 came the Gaza war, the ICJ's 2024 opinion on the occupation, wide recognition of Palestine (September 2025) and a ceasefire plan (October 2025). India recognised Palestine in 1988 and supports two states.</li>
<li>The <b>Yom Kippur War</b> (October 1973) was fought between Israel and Arab states led by Egypt and Syria; it led to the oil embargo.</li>
<li>The <b>Golan Heights</b> (Syria) was taken by Israel in 1967 and annexed in 1981 (recognised only by the USA). UNDOF patrols there, with Indian troops.</li>
<li>The <b>Arab Spring</b> began in <b>Tunisia</b> (December 2010, Mohamed Bouazizi). The Tunisian Quartet won the Nobel in 2015.</li>
<li>Towns:
<ul><li><b>Aleppo</b> is in Syria.</li>
<li><b>Mazar-i-Sharif</b> is in Afghanistan.</li>
<li><b>Kirkuk</b> and <b>Mosul</b> are in <b>Iraq</b>, not Yemen or Palestine.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Sheikh Zayed</b> was the founding President of the <b>UAE</b> (1971–2004).</li>
<li>In 2026 a US–Israel war with Iran (from February) reshaped the region, and Saudi–Israel normalisation stayed stalled.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Africa</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Ogaden</b> region caused wars between <b>Ethiopia and Somalia</b> (1964, 1977–78).</li>
<li><b>Liberia</b>'s long civil wars (1989–2003) killed some 2.5 lakh people; Charles Taylor went into exile and UNMIL followed.</li>
<li>The <b>Hema–Lendu</b> violence took place in Ituri, <b>DR Congo</b>.</li>
<li><b>Zimbabwe</b>'s unrest came from Mugabe's <b>land reforms</b> (from 2000), followed by hyperinflation.</li>
<li><b>Niger</b> faced <b>famine</b> in 2005 after drought and locusts.</li>
<li>Around 2002, <b>Angola, Colombia, Congo and Sudan</b> all had civil wars.</li>
<li><b>South Africa</b> sent troops to <b>Burundi</b> (2001) to <b>protect the power-sharing deal</b> mediated by Nelson Mandela (Arusha Accords).</li>
<li><b>Berbers</b> (Amazigh) of <b>Algeria</b> protested in 2001–02; Tamazight became official in 2016.</li>
<li>Presidents in 2002: Joseph Kabila (DR Congo), Olusegun Obasanjo (Nigeria), Yoweri Museveni (Uganda), Levy Mwanawasa (Zambia).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ir", "r6-2", `
<p>This topic gathers questions on regions in the news, disputes and political events across Europe, Asia and the Americas.</p>

<h3>Regions and disputes</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Region</th><th>Country</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Catalonia</b></td><td>Spain (2017 referendum)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mindanao</b></td><td>Philippines (Bangsamoro, 2019)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Crimea</b></td><td>Ukraine, annexed by Russia in 2014 (not Hungary)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Oromia</b></td><td>Ethiopia (not Nigeria)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Chechnya</b></td><td>Russia; <b>separatist rebellion</b> (wars 1994–96, 1999–2009)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Darfur</b></td><td>Sudan (not Mali)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Swat valley</b></td><td>Pakistan (not Iraq)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Basque</b> separatism</td><td><b>Spain</b> (ETA, dissolved 2018)</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Communities:
<ul><li><b>Madhesis</b>: Nepal's Terai, who protested the 2015 constitution.</li>
<li><b>Rohingya</b>: Myanmar's Rakhine, about 7 lakh of whom fled to Bangladesh in 2017.</li>
<li><b>Kurds</b>: Turkey, Iraq, Syria and Iran, not Bangladesh.</li></ul></li>
<li>The <b>Senkaku</b> (Diaoyu) islands in the East China Sea are administered by <b>Japan</b> and claimed by China and Taiwan. Other disputes: the Kurils (Russia–Japan), Dokdo/Takeshima (Korea–Japan), and the Spratlys and Paracels (South China Sea; the 2016 arbitral award rejected China's nine-dash line).</li>
<li><b>Falun Gong</b> is a <b>spiritual movement in China</b>, banned in 1999. <b>Hong Kong</b>'s 2003 march of about 5 lakh people opposed the proposed <b>internal security (Article 23) law</b>, which was withdrawn. Beijing later imposed a National Security Law (2020), and Hong Kong passed its own Article 23 law in March 2024.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The Balkans</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Kosovo</b> declared independence from <b>Serbia</b> (2008). In Kosovo, Serbs are a minority (Albanians are about 90%), while in Macedonia <b>ethnic Albanians are a minority</b>.</li>
<li>Historical <b>Macedonia</b> was contested between <b>Bulgaria and Greece</b>; the 2018 Prespa Agreement renamed the country North Macedonia.</li>
<li>Montenegro and Serbia formed a looser State Union (2003; Montenegro left in 2006). <b>Croatia</b> was under Hungarian administration until 1918. <b>Slovenia</b> left <b>Yugoslavia</b> (1991), not Czechoslovakia.</li>
<li>Serbian PM <b>Zoran Đinđić</b> was assassinated in 2003.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Asia-Pacific and the Americas</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Japan</b> upgraded its Defense Agency to a <b>Ministry of Defense</b> (2007). <b>Thailand</b> had a coup in September 2006. <b>Kim Dae-jung</b> (South Korea) won the 2000 Nobel for his Sunshine Policy. The UN ran <b>East Timor</b> from 1999 to prepare its independence (UNTAET).</li>
<li><b>Cristina Kirchner</b> succeeded her husband as President of <b>Argentina</b> (2007). <b>Michelle Bachelet</b> was Chile's first woman President (2006). <b>Argentina</b>'s 2001 unrest came from <b>huge public debt</b> and default. In 2004 <b>Fidel Castro</b> of <b>Cuba</b> was the longest-ruling head of government. <b>Nicaragua</b> has long planned a canal to rival Panama's.</li>
<li>Presidents in 2001: Andrés Pastrana (Colombia), Gloria Arroyo (Philippines), Vicente Fox (Mexico), Hugo Chávez (Venezuela).</li>
<li>USA: <b>George W. Bush</b> came from <b>Texas</b>. In the 2006 mid-terms the <b>Democrats</b> won the House, and Republican <b>Bobby Jindal</b> was re-elected (later the first Indian-American governor).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Events and people of the 2000s</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Event</th><th>Place</th></tr>
<tr><td>NAM summit 2006</td><td>Havana</td></tr>
<tr><td>EU–India summit 2006</td><td>Helsinki</td></tr>
<tr><td>APEC 2006</td><td>Hanoi (not Bangkok)</td></tr>
<tr><td>UN climate conference 2006</td><td>Nairobi (not Geneva)</td></tr>
<tr><td>St Petersburg's 300th anniversary (May 2003)</td><td>Putin hosted 40+ leaders</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Tshwane Declaration</b> (2006)</td><td>India and South Africa (centenary of Gandhi's satyagraha)</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
<tr><th>Person (2007)</th><th>Position</th></tr>
<tr><td>Nancy Pelosi</td><td>Speaker, US House</td></tr>
<tr><td>Margaret Chan</td><td>WHO</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pascal Lamy</td><td>WTO</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steve Ballmer</td><td>Microsoft</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Today: Tedros (WHO), Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala (WTO, second term from 2025), Satya Nadella (Microsoft).</p>
`);

addBookTopic("ir", "r6-3", `
<p>A few questions test how other countries' political systems are named and organised, and historical firsts.</p>

<h3>Parliaments and constitutions</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Country</th><th>Parliament</th></tr>
<tr><td>Netherlands</td><td>States General</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ukraine</td><td>Verkhovna Rada (Supreme Council)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Poland</td><td>Sejm (with the Senate)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Japan</td><td>Diet</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Russia</b></td><td><b>Federal Assembly</b>: the <b>State Duma</b> (450) is the <b>lower</b> house and the <b>Federation Council</b> the <b>upper</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Kuwait</td><td>National Assembly, which rejected women's suffrage 32–30 in 1999; women got the vote in 2005</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li>The <b>US Constitution</b> came into force in <b>1789</b> (not 1810). All <b>revenue bills originate in the House of Representatives</b>. George W. Bush was <b>not</b> the only president whose father was also president: John Quincy Adams was the son of John Adams.</li>
<li>Britain: <b>Harold Wilson</b> was <b>Labour</b>, not Conservative.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Firsts and laureates</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>New Zealand</b> (1893) was the first country to give women the vote.</li>
<li>The <b>Netherlands</b> was the first to legalise euthanasia (law in force 2002); Belgium followed. India allows only <b>passive</b> euthanasia with living wills (Common Cause v. Union of India, 2018).</li>
<li>Nobel Peace laureates: <b>Desmond Tutu</b> (1984), <b>Lech Wałęsa</b> (1983), and <b>Shimon Peres and Yasser Arafat</b> (1994, with Rabin).</li>
</ul>
`);
