addBookTopic("ca", "k1-1", `
<p>Recent papers ask about flagship infrastructure, schemes and economic trends; older papers asked about single news items. This topic groups them by theme.</p>

<h3>Ports and maritime policy</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Vizhinjam International Seaport</b> (Thiruvananthapuram), commissioned on 2 May 2025, is India's first deep-water container <b>trans-shipment</b> port. Its natural 18–20 m draft and location near the East–West shipping route let India <b>reduce dependence on foreign trans-shipment ports</b> (Colombo, Singapore, Salalah), keep revenue and reposition itself in regional trade. It is not a domestic-only or cruise port. It was built as a PPP (Adani with Kerala, with central viability gap funding). Other projects: Vadhavan (Maharashtra), Galathea Bay (Great Nicobar), Vallarpadam.</li>
<li><b>Sagarmala</b> (2015) aims at <b>port-led development</b>. Its four pillars are port modernisation, connectivity, port-led industrialisation (Coastal Economic Zones) and coastal communities.
<ul><li>Its <b>results</b> (coastal cargo up about 118%, inland waterway cargo up about 700%, better port rankings) <b>validate the strategy</b>.</li>
<li><b>Sagarmala 2.0</b> (2025, with a ₹25,000 crore Maritime Development Fund and shipbuilding goals) <b>extends</b> it.</li>
<li>The two statements do not contradict each other.</li></ul>
New maritime laws came in 2025 (Indian Ports Act, Merchant Shipping Act, Coastal Shipping Act).</li>
<li>Earlier news: <b>Krishnapatnam</b> (Andhra Pradesh, 2008) was then India's largest private port. <b>Rajasthan</b> explored an artificial inland port at Jalore linked to the Gulf of Kutch by a long channel (2015–16). The National Waterways Act (2016) declared 111 waterways.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Transport</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Greenfield airports</b> include <b>Donyi Polo</b> (Itanagar, 2022) and <b>Kushinagar</b> (2021). <b>Vijayawada</b> is an older airport that was upgraded. Others: Mopa (Goa), Navi Mumbai (2025), Noida (Jewar); the UDAN regional scheme began in 2016.</li>
<li><b>National Rail Plan</b>: a "future ready" railway by <b>2030</b>, not 2028. <b>Kavach</b> is an <b>indigenous</b> train protection system from RDSO and Indian firms, not German. It uses <b>RFID tags on the track</b>, station units, loco units and radio. Version 4.0 covered about 2,633 route km of the Delhi–Mumbai and Delhi–Howrah routes by July 2026.</li>
<li>A National Flying Training Institute was set up at Gondia, <b>Maharashtra</b>, around 2008.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Economy, finance and digital public goods</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Critical minerals</b>:
<ul><li>India joined the US-led <b>Minerals Security Partnership</b> (June 2023).</li>
<li>The <b>MMDR Amendment 2023</b> lets the <b>Centre alone auction</b> 24 critical and strategic minerals, and removed lithium and five others from the atomic minerals list.</li>
<li>India is <b>not</b> rich in all of its 30 critical minerals: it is import-dependent for lithium, cobalt, nickel and others.</li>
<li>The National Critical Mineral Mission (2025) and KABIL acquire assets abroad.</li></ul></li>
<li>Stock market:
<ul><li>India accounts for a <b>very large share of the world's equity options</b> traded (NSE is the largest derivatives exchange by contracts).</li>
<li>Its market cap <b>overtook Hong Kong's</b> in January 2024.</li>
<li><b>SEBI</b> does warn investors (about 9 in 10 retail F&O traders lose money), regulates investment advisers and acts against unregistered "finfluencers", so "no regulator" is false. F&O curbs came in November 2024.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>UPI abroad</b> (merchant payments): the <b>UAE, France and Singapore</b> accept it, along with Bhutan, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Mauritius and others; not Germany or Bangladesh as of 2025. UPI–PayNow linked with Singapore in 2023. UPI handles over 23 billion transactions a month.</li>
<li><b>Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission</b> (2021) is <b>voluntary</b> for hospitals and citizens; it offers <b>portable</b> digital health records through the ABHA ID.</li>
<li><b>Smart India Hackathon</b> (2017) is a contest to find <b>digital solutions to national problems</b>; it is not about Smart Cities or making all transactions digital.</li>
<li>In 2006 <b>Shahgarh</b> (Jaisalmer) was in the news for <b>high-quality natural gas</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Energy and textiles</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>PM Surya Ghar Muft Bijli Yojana</b> (February 2024, ₹75,021 crore) targets <b>1 crore rooftop solar households</b> with up to 300 free units a month, <b>grassroots training</b> by MNRE and over <b>3 lakh skilled workers</b>. It gives 60% subsidy up to 2 kW and collateral-free loans.</li>
<li>Solar firsts:
<ul><li><b>Cochin airport</b> (Kerala) was the world's first fully solar-powered airport (2015).</li>
<li>The largest solar park in 2022 was Bhadla (<b>Rajasthan</b>), not Gujarat, though Khavda in Gujarat will be bigger.</li>
<li>The largest floating plant was Ramagundam (Telangana), later Omkareshwar (MP), not Goa.</li></ul>
Non-fossil sources were about 52.6% of installed capacity (February 2026); solar is about 165 GW (2026).</li>
<li><b>Eri silk</b> ("ahimsa silk", mainly Assam) received <b>OEKO-TEX</b> certification (2025), so it can <b>compete in high-end, chemical-free markets</b> and meets international safety and environmental standards. India produces all four commercial silks (mulberry, tasar, muga, eri) and is the second-largest producer.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("ca", "k1-2", `
<p>Environment, science and technology news often overlaps with the Science and Environment books. This topic collects what the questions asked about recent conferences, missions and discoveries.</p>

<h3>Conservation and geoheritage</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Madhav National Park</b> (Shivpuri, Madhya Pradesh) became India's <b>58th tiger reserve</b> in March 2025, and its <b>Sakhya Sagar</b> lake is a Ramsar site (2022). It lies wholly in MP, not shared with Rajasthan. MP has nine tiger reserves; India reached 100 Ramsar sites in June 2026.</li>
<li><b>Tungurahua</b> volcano, a UNESCO Global Geopark in 2025, is in <b>Ecuador</b>. India has no UNESCO Global Geopark yet; candidates include Lonar crater.</li>
<li><b>Hyderabad</b> was named a 2020 <b>Tree City of the World</b> by the <b>FAO and the Arbor Day Foundation</b>, not UNCDF, for one year for its commitment to urban forests.</li>
<li>The <b>EU</b> enacted the <b>Nature Restoration Law</b> (2024): restore 20% of land and sea by 2030. Related EU laws:
<ul><li>CBAM, the carbon border levy, paid from 2026.</li>
<li>EUDR, deforestation-free supply chains, from December 2026.</li>
<li>The <b>Net-Zero Industry Act</b>, approved by Parliament in 2024, aims for <b>40%</b> of clean-tech manufacturing at home by 2030. The EU's neutrality target is <b>2050</b>, not 2040, and it does not aim to make all its own clean tech.</li></ul></li>
<li>The <b>ADB</b> launched the <b>Nature Solutions Finance Hub</b> for Asia and the Pacific (COP28, 2023), aiming to mobilise $2 billion.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Climate and oceans</h3>
<ul>
<li>At <b>COP28</b> (Dubai, 2023) <b>India did not sign</b> the Declaration on Climate and Health. That declaration is <b>non-binding</b>; India's concern was that <b>decarbonising health care</b> (cold chains) could <b>weaken its resilience</b>, which explains why it did not sign. COP28 also produced the first Global Stocktake ("transitioning away from fossil fuels") and the Loss and Damage Fund. Later COPs: COP29 Baku (finance goal of $300 billion), COP30 Belém (2025), COP31 Antalya (November 2026).</li>
<li>At the <b>UN Ocean Conference</b> (Nice, June 2025) the FAO's "<b>Four Betters</b>" for Blue Transformation were <b>better production, better nutrition, better environment and better life</b>. The BBNJ High Seas Treaty entered into force in January 2026.</li>
<li><b>Earth's rotation and axis are shifting</b> partly because <b>melting polar ice moves water toward the equator</b>, which lengthens the day and moves the axis; groundwater pumping also matters. The May 2024 solar storm was real but does not explain the shift.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Technology and defence</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Majorana 1</b> (February 2025) is <b>Microsoft's</b> topological quantum chip, not AWS's (AWS has Ocelot, Google has Willow). <b>Deep learning is a subset of machine learning</b>.</li>
<li>AI summits:
<ul><li>The <b>AI Action Summit</b> (Paris, February 2025) was <b>co-chaired by India</b> and built on Bletchley (2023) and Seoul (2024). The <b>USA and UK did not sign</b> its declaration.</li>
<li>The <b>AI Impact Summit</b> (New Delhi, February 2026) rested on three Sutras, <b>People, Planet and Progress</b> (not "Planning"). Its Charter for Democratic Diffusion of AI is <b>non-binding</b>. The <b>New Delhi Declaration</b> has <b>seven Chakras</b>, including access for social empowerment, AI for science and secure and trusted AI.</li></ul></li>
<li><b>Mission Sudarshan Chakra</b> (announced 15 August 2025) aims at a national <b>air and missile defence shield with counter-strike capability</b>, for <b>rapid, precise responses</b>, covering <b>all important public places by 2035</b>.</li>
<li>Space:
<ul><li><b>Axiom-4</b> (2025; Shubhanshu Shukla, the first Indian on the ISS) and <b>Gaganyaan</b> support <b>microgravity research</b>.</li>
<li><b>SpaDeX</b> (docking achieved January 2025; India the fourth country) is a docking demonstration, per UPSC's key.</li>
<li>Gaganyaan's uncrewed flight was targeted for end-2026, and the Bharatiya Antariksh Station's first module for 2028.</li></ul></li>
<li>Older news: <b>INS Astradharini</b> (2015) is a <b>torpedo launch and recovery vessel</b>; NASA's <b>GL-10 Greased Lightning</b> is a battery-powered tilt-wing aircraft; <b>CERN</b>'s Large Hadron Collider began in 2008 and found the Higgs boson in 2012.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Health and disasters in the news</h3>
<ul>
<li>The 2014–16 <b>Ebola</b> epidemic hit <b>Guinea, Sierra Leone and Liberia</b>.</li>
<li>The <b>Commission on AIDS in Asia</b> was chaired by <b>C. Rangarajan</b> (report 2008). The 2004 International AIDS Conference was in <b>Bangkok</b>.</li>
<li>Hurricanes <b>Ike and Gustav</b> struck in 2008; Kate was older. Tropical Storm <b>Jeanne</b> devastated <b>Haiti</b> (2004). The January 2001 earthquake caused a landslide near <b>San Salvador</b>.</li>
</ul>
`);
;
