addBookTopic("ca", "k3-1", `
<p>Award questions ask who won, which body gives the award, or who sits on the jury.</p>

<h3>Nobel Prizes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Prizes are presented on <b>10 December</b> (Nobel's death anniversary). The <b>Peace Prize</b> is chosen by the <b>Norwegian Nobel Committee</b> (appointed by the Storting) and presented in Oslo; the rest are presented in Stockholm. <b>Literature</b> was one of the original five; <b>Economics</b> was added later (from 1969).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>2025 field</th><th>Laureates</th></tr>
<tr><td>Physics</td><td><b>John Clarke</b> (born in the UK, professor at UC Berkeley), Michel Devoret, John Martinis: macroscopic quantum tunnelling in circuits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chemistry</td><td>Susumu Kitagawa, Richard Robson (UK-born but at Melbourne), Omar Yaghi: metal–organic frameworks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Medicine</td><td>Brunkow, Ramsdell, Sakaguchi: regulatory T cells</td></tr>
<tr><td>Literature; Peace</td><td>László Krasznahorkai; María Corina Machado</td></tr>
<tr><td>Economics</td><td>Joel Mokyr, Philippe Aghion, Peter Howitt: innovation-driven growth</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Shirin Ebadi</b> (Peace, 2003) is from <b>Iran</b>; she was its first woman judge and the first Muslim woman laureate.</p>

<h3>Indian national awards</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Gandhi Peace Prize</b> (1995; ₹1 crore): its jury is chaired by the <b>Prime Minister</b> and includes the <b>Chief Justice</b>, the <b>Leader of the largest opposition party</b> in the Lok Sabha and eminent persons. The <b>President</b> presents it but is not on the jury. Winners include Julius Nyerere (first), <b>Nelson Mandela</b> (2000), ISRO (2014) and Gita Press (2021).</li>
<li><b>Indira Gandhi Prize for Peace, Disarmament and Development</b> (1986):
<ul><li><b>M.S. Swaminathan</b> (1999).</li>
<li><b>Sadako Ogata</b>: the 2001 prize, presented in 2002.</li>
<li><b>ISRO</b> (2014), for the Mars Orbiter Mission.</li>
<li>Others: Gorbachev, MSF, Angela Merkel.</li></ul></li>
<li>The <b>Shanti Swarup Bhatnagar Prize</b> (CSIR, 1958) is for <b>science</b>. The <b>Lal Bahadur Shastri National Award</b> for public administration went to <b>N.R. Narayana Murthy</b> (presented 2002) and <b>Ela Bhatt</b>. The CSIR award for S&T innovation for rural development (2006) went to the <b>CLRI</b>, per UPSC's key.</li>
<li>Stree Shakti Puraskar (1999): per the key, the Devi Ahilya Bai Holkar award honours <b>courage and valour</b>.</li>
<li>Bharat Ratna: up to three a year (five in 2024). Padma awards are chosen by a committee under the Cabinet Secretary.</li>
</ul>

<h3>International and literary awards</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>Pulitzer Prize</b> (Columbia University, 1917) is mainly for <b>American journalism</b>, letters and music; it is not given by the Commonwealth Foundation. The <b>Orange Prize</b> (now the Women's Prize) is for fiction in English by a woman.</li>
<li>The <b>Wolf Prize</b> in agriculture went to <b>Gurdev Khush</b> (2000). The <b>Jamnalal Bajaj</b> international award went to <b>Desmond Tutu</b>.</li>
<li>The <b>Goldman Environmental Prize</b> (2004) went to <b>Champa Devi Shukla</b> and Rashida Bee for Bhopal survivors. The <b>Vachaspati Puraskar</b> (Sanskrit) went to P. Sri Ramachandrudu. Upamanyu Chatterjee is a novelist, so he did not win the Lalit Kala Ratna.</li>
<li>Ramon Magsaysay laureates:
<ul><li><b>Shantha Sinha</b>, an <b>anti-child-labour activist</b> (MV Foundation), who later chaired the NCPCR.</li>
<li><b>Sandeep Pandey</b>, for <b>education and livelihoods for Dalits</b> (Asha for Education).</li></ul></li>
<li>The first <b>V.K. Krishna Menon Award</b> (2008) went to CJI <b>K.G. Balakrishnan</b>; the foundation is in London, not New Delhi.</li>
<li>Cinema: <b>Boong</b>, a Manipuri film directed by <b>Lakshmipriya Devi</b>, won the <b>BAFTA</b> for Children's and Family Film (2026), the <b>first Indian film</b> to do so. Other milestones: Bhanu Athaiya's Oscar (1983), "Naatu Naatu" (2023), and Payal Kapadia's Cannes Grand Prix (2024).</li>
</ul>
`);
;
